const glossaryData = [{"name":"A.E.F.","definition":"American Expeditionary Forces, the American army sent to Europe in World War I. Duffey claims that before joining the army in 1917 he drove ambulances with Hemingway in Italy and E. E. Cummings in France. The narrator doubts even his brief presence in an American army camp and suggests that he borrowed some of these experiences from Sebastian Hilton, while allowing that he lived borrowed scenes fully [MTM B2:72].","citations":["MTM B2:72"],"tags":["World War I","Duffey","Historical Figures","Literary Figures","Italy","France"]},{"name":"A.M.","definition":"Loy Larkin, in a letter to Finnegan from Cambeltown, New South Wales, dated Thursday, February 11, 1943, set a rendezvous time of 7:45 A.M. for the next day, acknowledging Finnegan might find it decadent to wake so early [A:27].","citations":["A:27"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","World War II","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Aansen, Swede","definition":"One of the resident regulars at the Old Wooden Ship, in the town where Finnegan first met Seaworthy, the Devil, and Anastasia [D:215-216]. Aansen belongs to a group of five men called Swede, none of whom is Swedish: their origins are Norse, Danish, Icelandic, German, and Orcadian. The passage does not assign a particular origin to Aansen [D:217].","citations":["D:215-216","D:217"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Aaron","definition":"Former owner of the marbles in Phoebe Anne Ankerson’s suitcase, which Finnegan steals. Her label says Aaron is too old for them and that they are to be given to Harold Michael; some are agates [D:171-172].","citations":["D:171-172"],"tags":["Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Aaron, Avril","definition":"One of the women living at the Old Wooden Ship who are called “salt water widows,” most of them married to seamen [D:221].","citations":["D:221"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Aaron, Casper Stone","definition":"One of the Princes of the Ekklesia at the conclave in Babylonia Bagascia. First called in Conrad Stone Hackenschmidt’s armed roll call, he votes for the straw man, speaking surlily as though under compulsion [MB:566–567].","citations":["MB:566–567"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Ecclesiastical","Conclave"]},{"name":"Ab Salom","definition":"Name by which Teresa Piccone Stranahan identifies Absalom Stein when she recounts the company’s earlier lives aboard the <em>Argo</em>. She calls him Ab-Salom, the Father of Peace, and says that he was a ship’s chandler and provisioner on the Euxine who joined the voyage to look after his investments [MTM B7:233-234]. See <strong>Stein, Absalom</strong>.","citations":["MTM B7:233-234"],"tags":["Argonautica","Ships","Seas & Oceans","Identity"]},{"name":"Abbott, Catherine Stone","definition":"One of the Princes of the Ekklesia at the conclave in Babylonia Bagascia. Called after Casper Stone Aaron in Conrad Stone Hackenschmidt’s armed roll call, she votes for the straw man [MB:566–567].","citations":["MB:566–567"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Ecclesiastical","Conclave","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Abd Aliah of the Sea","definition":"Abd-Aliah of the Sea, former owner of the <em>Argo</em> and father of a famously beautiful daughter with a tail. He sold the ship to Sindbad of El-Basrah [MTM B13:404].","citations":["MTM B13:404"],"tags":["Ships","Nymphs & Mermaids","Family"]},{"name":"Abdo, Adrian","definition":"Actor playing the title role in <em>Goat Boy</em> at the Castle Theatre when Casey, acting on a bet, walks uninvited onto the stage. Casey reads seven names from a Greater Chicago telephone directory and provokes an ecstatic ovation; afterward nobody remembers why the crowd reacted so strongly [PG:543].","citations":["PG:543"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Theater","Casey"]},{"name":"Abdo, James","definition":"James Gate Abdo, one of the Princes of the Ekklesia at the Last Conclave in Babylonia Bagascia [MB:561] [MB:566-567]. He votes “Ego eligo effigiem” for the straw-man after Conrad Stone Hackenschmidt orders that response while covering the assembled Princes with a Browning Automatic Rifle. Six dissenters vote for Joseph Cardinal Hedayat and are killed; the straw-man is elected and burned [MB:567-568]. Abdo’s vote belongs to this coerced ceremony intended to terminate the Church [MB:567-568].","citations":["MB:561","MB:566-567","MB:567-568"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Abomination of Desolation","definition":"Prophetic phrase that Mr. X applies to 'the world that God so loved, becoming trivial and narrow.' In his letter to Absalom Stein, X predicts that they will see this desolation and immediately rebukes Stein for having read Teilhard without laughing [A:265] [A:266].","citations":["A:265","A:266"],"tags":["Scripture","Prophecy & Visions","Theology"]},{"name":"Absalom (Son of King David)","definition":"Biblical son of King David. His supposed appearance in Finnegan’s discussion of dynastic succession remains unverified; the passage previously cited concerns Absalom Stein [MTM B5:169].","citations":["MTM B5:169"],"tags":["Old Testament","Family"]},{"name":"Absinthe","definition":"Ingredient in the “Green Ladies” drunk in the Quarter during the appearance of the Royal Pop People. Margaret Stone describes the drink to Mary Virginia as “like Peppermint Schnapps, except with absinthe instead of the schnapps” [MTM B11:309].","citations":["MTM B11:309"],"tags":["New Orleans","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Absolute Minimum","definition":"Finnegan's reserve against going completely broke. His last hundred-dollar bill is Miguel; reaching that reserve tells him he must find work. Earlier bills included Jose-Ramires, Hernandez, Aloysius, and Gottfried von Guggenheim [D:19]. The narrator calls this the economic law of the cushion and the absolute minimum [D:20].","citations":["D:19","D:20"],"tags":["Finnegan","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Academy (U.S. Military)","definition":"The <a href=\"https://www.westpoint.edu/about/history-of-west-point\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">United States Military Academy at West Point</a>, to which Private Gregory says he gained an appointment about a year after deserting and changing his identity in 1907. After more than a century as a sergeant, he passed for a man of twenty, cultivated political patrons, and entered the Academy. He was later commissioned and became an army historian and archivist [A:77] [A:78].","citations":["A:77","A:78"],"tags":["Soldiers","Identity","Immortality","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Acastus","definition":"Acastus of Iolcus, who with his unnamed wife subjects Peleus to what Melchisedech Duffey calls a “badger-game.” In his commentary in “Promontory Goats,” Duffey identifies Peleus as Casey’s primordial <em>Argo</em> role [PG:534-535]. Hilary Hilton and Mary Jean occupy the corresponding positions in Casey’s twentieth-century life, though Duffey says their game is played in reverse. He suggests that Casey may have sought death at Hilary’s hands and received a more painful derision instead. Duffey admits that he does not know what either Hilton wanted from Casey and wonders whether the original game was also reversed [PG:536].","citations":["PG:534-535","PG:536"],"tags":["Argonautica","Greek Mythology","Duffey"]},{"name":"Acme Pump Company","definition":"Words cast on iron pump handles, among the household inscriptions Duffey remembers from his childhood. He gathers these particulars through close observation and by appropriating other people’s memories [MTM B1:16-17].","citations":["MTM B1:16-17"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Acme Royal Top, The","definition":"Earlier erroneous heading, which inserted Royal into the carriage’s name. See <em>Acme Top, The</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["New Orleans","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Acme Top, The","definition":"Horse-drawn carriage named “The Acme Top” in a Royal Pop Historian’s account of the memories preserved in a Decatur Street flagstone. He lists it with the Acme Open, the Southern Beauty, and other old vehicles during a nostalgic address at Duffey’s place [MTM B11:329].","citations":["MTM B11:329"],"tags":["New Orleans","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Acorns","definition":"Food eaten by Gautier, the goat-man, and his goats during their wandering life in New Jersey. The narrator associates this part of Gautier's diet with his old-world stock [A:196] [A:197].","citations":["A:196","A:197"],"tags":["Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Action","definition":"Finnegan’s term for what his paintings of the thirteen murdered cardinals portray. When Biloxi Brannagan exclaims over their passion, Finnegan contrasts acting with passively undergoing and insists that action does not require exterior motion. The exchange takes place aboard the <em>Argo</em>, where the paintings cover the bulkheads of the Bread and Wine Room [MTM B13:452-453]. His explanation breaks off after “Action is...” when Brannagan interrupts him [MTM B13:453].","citations":["MTM B13:452-453","MTM B13:453"],"tags":["Finnegan","Metaphysics","Visual Arts","Ships"]},{"name":"Actium","definition":"Site of the naval battle of 31 BC. In the history spoken by the talking oak in its wheel, the <em>Argo</em> had been the <em>Anthony Ship</em> at Actium and had been shamed there [MTM B13:404].","citations":["MTM B13:404"],"tags":["Ships","Naval Warfare","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Adam","definition":"Finnegan's counterpart 'in a variant account' of creation. In the opening scene Finnegan is the first man, drinking the first beer; when he raises his eyes from his drink, the Garden is gone and he is in the World. Both he and Adam are said to be 'a little more than half Italian and a little more than half Irish' [A:1].","citations":["A:1"],"tags":["Old Testament","Finnegan","Creation"]},{"name":"Adelphos","definition":"Word X says Anastasia used for Finnegan. He explains it as meaning cousin as well as brother in Old Greek, and takes it as evidence of their kinship through the ancestor he identifies as the Devil [D:279].","citations":["D:279"],"tags":["Greece","Finnegan","Family","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Aden","definition":"Market in Biloxi Brannagan’s stories where a shrewd trader can supposedly make a fortune in Circassian slave-girl futures. Finnegan is willing to credit this part of his account [D:206].","citations":["D:206"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Middle East","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Admiror audiens","definition":"Duffey's Latin reply to Mr. X's description of the nested black, gray, and brown boxes. Duffey gives his own loose translation, 'I am surprised to hear it,' then asks whether he has seen the box before. Mr. X says he has brought it from Chicago after stealing it from Casey [A:136].","citations":["A:136"],"tags":["Duffey","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Adrian the Dutchman","definition":"One of the figures aboard the <em>Navicula Petri</em> in Henry's dream, named beside Hildebrand among apostles and figures from the history of the Church [A:44] [A:45]. The epithet probably identifies <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/vatican/en/holy-father/adriano-vi.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Pope Adrian VI</a>, Adriaan Florensz of Utrecht, whose pontificate lasted from 1522 to 1523. This is an identification inferred from the epithet; the passage does not give his papal number.","citations":["A:44","A:45"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Church History","Dreams","Popes"]},{"name":"Adriatic Sea","definition":"Sea on which Ragusa lies. Duffey tells a New Orleans hull dealer that the <em>Argo</em>’s keel was laid at Ragusa and its timbers came from the Dinaric Alps; he qualifies the whole encounter as symbolically rather than ordinarily accurate [MTM B13:412].","citations":["MTM B13:412"],"tags":["Ships","Seas & Oceans","Duffey"]},{"name":"Adversaries","definition":"Formidable opponents of the Argonauts, including the Evil Prince, the Purple Prince, the Mocking Prince, and the Laughing Prince. The narrator credits them with mystery, personality, and courage, and allows that they sometimes defeat and kill great Ship Masters. The Laughing Prince of Tartary may be the strongest and trickiest; reports call him the Prince of the Third Way, claimed by neither God nor the Devil [MTM B13:437].","citations":["MTM B13:437"],"tags":["Argonautica","Argonauts (Characters)","Cosmic Struggle","The Devil"]},{"name":"Adversary","definition":"The Devil as opponent of Duffey and the fiefdoms of Invisible Neustria [MTM B8:245-246]. The group is said to have known him before his thousand-year imprisonment [MTM B7:236]. In Cleo Mahoney’s account of the sixth possible future, the Adversary and the Patriarch are the only persons left in the world, and the Adversary presses him to sign a covenant that would unmake everyone else [MTM B10:294] [MTM B10:296].","citations":["MTM B8:245-246","MTM B7:236","MTM B10:294","MTM B10:296"],"tags":["The Devil","Cosmic Struggle","Duffey"]},{"name":"Aeetes","definition":"King of Colchis and father of Medea in Greek myth. Rosemary Riorden tells Finnegan in Sydney that he will be loved by Aeetes’ daughter in several forms, including Rosemary herself, as part of his renewed Argonautic story [A:88-89].","citations":["A:88-89"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Argonautica","Australia","Finnegan","Family"]},{"name":"Aegean Sea","definition":"Sea whose islands enter Finnegan’s account of creation: a shattered electrum cup becomes the islands, and the first five-island group comprises Andros, Tinos, Paros, Amargos, and Naxos [A:35]. Anastasia locates the mermaids’ original home in the Cyclades, with an early extension to the Dodecanese and Kos; the survivors have largely retreated to Naxos and nearby rocks [D:35-36]. Naxos is her own home, and she claims descent from its fishtail people [D:115].","citations":["A:35","D:35-36","D:115"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Islands","Greece","Nymphs & Mermaids","Folklore","Finnegan","Creation"]},{"name":"Aerie (geographical)","definition":"High refuge to which Sergeant Santee climbs for the night in Don Lewis’s account of Willy Jones Island. Casper takes an opposite crest, while Lewis remains in the valley with Yokipoki’s body [D:46].","citations":["D:46"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Aerie (poetic)","definition":"Symbol of a bright, solitary, high home mentioned in Hans Schultz's unwritten poem (\"Bright is my Aerie. / Aloft and alone.\") [A:210].","citations":["A:210"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"Aeson","definition":"Father of Jason in Greek myth. Rosemary Riorden tells Finnegan that he is the son of “Aeson a demiurge,” although he believes his father’s name was Giulio and regards him as a slob. She makes this identification while explaining his Argonautic life in Sydney [A:88-89].","citations":["A:88-89"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Argonautica","Australia","Finnegan","Family"]},{"name":"Affidavit","definition":"Document declaring the Aussies superior to the Yanks at drinking and everything else, with obscenities spelled out. Tom Shire inspires it and an unnamed “scribe of genius” writes it. Finnegan, drunk and believing he is signing a roster for beer chits, signs without reading. The narrator says an Aussie non-commissioned officers’ club still keeps it after Shire and Freddy Castle have passed beyond its members’ memory [A:13].","citations":["A:13"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","World War II","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Afghan border","definition":"Setting of military actions in Biloxi Brannagan’s stories. Finnegan does not know enough about these actions, Australian irregulars, or service in the Empire’s forces to test Brannagan’s account [D:206].","citations":["D:206"],"tags":["Soldiers","History & Memory","Australia"]},{"name":"Africa","definition":"Continent on the <em>Brunhilde</em>’s route from Port of Spain to Freetown, Sierra Leone [D:85]. The African Hump is associated with inward, deathlike dreams; Finnegan experiences apprehension on the crossing [D:80] [D:90]. In Freetown Joseph, a Haussa diamond smuggler, calls his people “the Jews of Africa” and proposes a partnership; Brannagan later recognizes Finnegan’s diamond as Sierra Leonean [D:93-94] [D:207]. Don Barnaby explicitly says that Madame Le Mourcheor’s Old Original Juju Joy Pills are made in New Orleans, not Africa [D:249].","citations":["D:85","D:80","D:90","D:93-94","D:207","D:249"],"tags":["Africa","Seas & Oceans","Finnegan","Dreams","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"African Hump","definition":"Destination in the chapter-six epigraph’s imagined geography of sea-dreams: sailors traveling from Port of Spain are said to suffer dreams of deathlike introspection. The epigraph is attributed to Ian McIfreann’s <em>Psychologia Nugarum</em> [D:80-81]. Its image recurs as apprehension enters Finnegan’s dreams [D:90].","citations":["D:80-81","D:90"],"tags":["Sailing","Africa","Dreams","Literature"]},{"name":"African-Extravaganza","definition":"Duffey’s derisive name for claims of extreme human antiquity in the African fossil evidence discussed with a Pattenite. He calls the claims fraudulent and likens the “African Extravaganza” to a pop-rock hymn, yet accepts evidence from elsewhere for a human antiquity greater than the Fundamentalers allow [MTM B9:282-283].","citations":["MTM B9:282-283"],"tags":["Africa","Duffey","Language & Words","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Afrikaans","definition":"Language Joseph knows, unlike “proper Dutch,” which he says he still needs to learn for his diamond business. He makes the distinction while describing his linguistic qualifications to Finnegan [D:94].","citations":["D:94"],"tags":["Language & Words","Crime","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Agadir","definition":"Port in the <em>Brunhilde</em>’s itinerary after Arrecife on Lanzarote and Ifni, and before Magadar, Safti, Mazagan, Casablanca, and Rabat [D:101].","citations":["D:101"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Ports & Harbors","Africa"]},{"name":"Agape (Love)","definition":"Hot purple flower in the image of underground New Orleans sunshine bursting through broken pavement. With Philos and Eros, Agape gives the decade’s warmth the form of flowering love; the narrator calls it the theme flower of the hundred-and-twenty-month late summertime shared by Duffey and Letitia [MTM B7:211].","citations":["MTM B7:211"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey"]},{"name":"Age of giants","definition":"Patrick Stranahan’s name for the period of spiritual greatness he believes has just ended in the Church, giving way to an “age of pygmies” [A:109-110].","citations":["A:109-110"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Church History","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Age of pygmies","definition":"Patrick Stranahan’s name for the diminished age he believes is beginning in the Church after its “age of giants.” He calls the arrival of the common Hell and attacks secular liberalism in the Church; Father McGuigan protests that he has gone too far [A:109-110].","citations":["A:109-110"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Heaven & Hell","Church History"]},{"name":"Ageing","definition":"Age is unstable across the cycle. Private Gregory says he never seems to age and changes identities to conceal his longevity; an army doctor’s interest forces another escape [A:77-78]. Finnegan recalls a young hobo who became forty years older overnight, while Saxon Seaworthy appears to age forty years in three seconds at the sight of Papa Devil [D:14] [D:22]. In Don Lewis’s Big House story, the inhabitants remain unaged by killing and consuming visitors; without a visitor for a year they age one year [D:48]. Members of the other recension are said to deepen rather than age [D:161-162]. Duffey looks younger than he did twenty years earlier [MTM B8:261-262], and Stein later describes his progressive rejuvenation, which Duffey had thought no one had mentioned aloud [MTM B11:317].","citations":["A:77-78","D:14","D:22","D:48","D:161-162","MTM B8:261-262","MTM B11:317"],"tags":["Immortality","Time & Eternity","The Devil","Finnegan","Duffey","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Agency, Royal Imperial Dominion Detective","definition":"Employer of Shamus McGregor (Marie Monaghan's uncle 'Shaw'), who worked as a night watchman for the agency [A:28].","citations":["A:28"],"tags":["Occupations","Family"]},{"name":"Aghri Dagh","definition":"Name that Zabotski says the mountain bore before an Armenian king renamed it Ararat. His account questions whether the king correctly identified the biblical mountain. The gloss “Mountain” in the passage belongs to Ararat, not to Aghri Dagh [MTM B9:280-281].","citations":["MTM B9:280-281"],"tags":["Scripture","Middle East","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Ahriman","definition":"The evil spirit of Zoroastrian tradition. In the epigraph attributed to Arpad Arutinov’s <em>The Back-Door of History</em>, “the children of Ahriman” return like primordial monsters or frightening mutations. The passage places them among its claims about overlapping species, mingled spirit and flesh, and demonic possession [D:26].","citations":["D:26"],"tags":["Other Mythologies","Cosmic Struggle","Metaphysics","Angels & Demons"]},{"name":"Aion","definition":"Jung’s work invoked in the Postscript for the Ego, Self, Shadow, Anima, and Animus. Lafferty counts these as five persons within the human person and uses the account to introduce his claim that inner multiplicity can sometimes become physically visible [PS:477].","citations":["PS:477"],"tags":["Literature","Metaphysics","Identity","Ghosts & Spirits"]},{"name":"Air","definition":"Medium from which Finnegan says he learned Doppio di Pinne's name: “I read it in the air.” He also knows that Dopey was killed; Joe Cross asks whether he read that in the air too [D:63]. A fuller counterpart appears in <em>The Elliptical Grave</em>, where the Pioneer Reventlo Expedition in Calabria seeks human origins and mutations by excavating earth, haunted air, and the higher noosphere. Joseph Abramswell invents air excavation, startling hidden presences into activity and using detection and amplification equipment. The air yields human and animal ghosts, particularly recent ones, and still more ghost plants; reconstructed trees supply landscape history. Joseph calls haunted air “recording air”: its records repeatedly lose clarity and need amplification. He admits that he does not understand their survival in the wind, but describes immaterial pens and baffles and anomalously cohesive clumps. Joseph and Dorothy Blue-Ice also extract the outlines of grumpus fish. Jeffery Wind instead leads the uncertain work of excavating the noosphere, chiefly from a tethered balloon. Cecelia Calca infers a thirty-meter limit for haunted-air ghosts from her own inability to rise higher. She identifies the Invisible Man, whom Jeffery meets in his balloon, as a more tenuous ghost, but the narrator questions that classification. Later, Jeffery experiments with recycling air, and Anabella conjectures that their cocoons are made of air. The book also shows a Phoenix leaking air in Fritz Otto's projected film and gives a verse about shoveling air. (<em>The Elliptical Grave</em>, PDF pp. 26, 42-44, 47, 50, 56, 73, 79, 85).","citations":["D:63"],"tags":["Finnegan","Ghosts & Spirits","Literature","History & Memory","Metaphysics","Italy"]},{"name":"Akroreia","definition":"One of the three Oreads whom Finnegan meets in dreams on the mountain at Naxos, with Anemotrephes and Oreibates. They call Anastasia their sister, warn Finnegan to flee after the coming night’s events, and forbid him to join their climb. When he recognizes their kinship with the teras, Akroreia sadly asks how he knew; Oreibates calls it their “destitution and destruction.” The sisters push Finnegan from the dream-mountain [D:136-139].","citations":["D:136-139"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Women Characters","Family","Dreams"]},{"name":"Al-Raschid, Haroun","definition":"Abbasid caliph who ruled from 786 to 809. Anastasia uses his reign as a marker in her mermaid history, saying that mermaids had reached the Persian Gulf by his time [D:35]. In Lafferty's <em>Sindbad, the Thirteenth Voyage</em>, Harun al-Rashid is a recurring Golden Boy or Boy-Caliph, associated with Baghdad Mirage, masks, magic, and cruel practical jokes. Reports of his deaths and rebirths conflict in chronology. Redcrown Charnel, his father in a previous Kentauron Mikron life, receives a coded message announcing his rebirth on Gaea-Earth. Harun appears about eleven, though described as fifty in his present life. Citizen Heifritz says he ruled for three days twenty-seven years earlier, in Moslem year 164, before his sons Al-Amin and Mamun made him Caliph-Emeritus. The current action also has him install Al-Amin as caliph. These are the novel's accounts, not historical chronology. Harun builds towers by magic; Mamun calls him the chief creator of the present Baghdad Mirage, and Essindibad's ship suggests that he could have begun its creation before birth. Legends link his strong hands to Hercules-like snake strangling and to killing his own twin. He can escape recognition, although his many masks also paradoxically leave him recognizable to everyone. John Thunderson resembles him, calls him a friend, reports his boyish “Ifrit treble,” and receives a promise of a vizierate contingent on Harun remaining caliph. Harun claims invisibility and friendship with devils, with a foot in Earth, heaven, and hell. His delightful aura later turns to revulsion when his mask falls. Spy Alexander describes him as a potentially good creature of God who actually serves evil as Satan's recurrent pawn. Harun collects Christian blood for Al-Amin's installation, mocks and repudiates Al-Amin, calls Mamun his only son, and seeks Al-Amin's corpse for further jokes. He wears green clown motley and a dog mask in Dog Country, where victims protest his permanent transformation of humans into dogs. Harun himself insists that a caliph's bones are holy. Near the Fountain of Life-Bubbles, a murdered man's widow pours a dog-attracting essence over him, and savage dogs kill him. In his final seconds he receives two surrogate ninety-year dogs' lives. The Dog Magician extracts his still-living, transparent heart and gives it to Scheherazade, identified as his widow as well as Al-Amin's. Its apparent goldfish identifies itself as a bottled, miniaturized golden whale; Scheherazade has it installed beside her own heart. The Dog Magician thinks this is Harun's seventh and last life, and explains his bodies as contraptions inhabited by devils. He and the Dog Surgeon later record the death and burial in the Royal Archives. (<em>Sindbad, the Thirteenth Voyage</em>, PDF pp. 10-11, 16, 20, 27, 34-36, 38-40, 46, 66, 70, 73, 117, 120, 128, 133, 136, 138-142).","citations":["D:35"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Nymphs & Mermaids","Literature","Middle East","Magic & Sorcery","Shapeshifting","Immortality","Identity","Death & Mortality","Family","Angels & Demons"]},{"name":"Alabama","definition":"One of three possible settings, with Louisiana and Mississippi, for the rural scenes in the first chamber of Elroy Redheart’s four-chambered heart artwork. Its fields, working people, and young dancers also contain the disturbing scene of the dead Jill of Hearts [MTM B7:221].","citations":["MTM B7:221"],"tags":["Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Alaric","definition":"Gothic leader of about AD 395-410, named by Mr. X among kings and heroes whose followers await their awakening from enchanted sleep. X uses their legends as a comparison for the Devil's imprisonment [MTM B6:204-205]. Alaric is central to Lafferty's <em>The Fall of Rome</em>. That book places his birth in 376, as a Roman and a member of the Balthi, while qualifying the tradition of his birth on Peuce island and its fulfillment of a Gothic prophecy. His father's name Alareidar comes from a doubtful source; his father dies in a horse accident, and his mother's fate has conflicting accounts. Raised by male tutors and his older cousin Stairnon, later his wife and formative oracle, Alaric grows into a “boy giant” with a powerful capacity for learning. Athaulf, Sarus, and Singerich are his close kindred, though their precise family relationships remain uncertain. Their affinities diverge: Athaulf remains Gothic, Sarus commits himself to Rome, and Singerich becomes Greek. Alaric attends the Imperial School, hears Theodosius and Stilicho, and recognizes in Stilicho the equal who will be his ally, enemy, and eventual friend. He excels at transporting armies and aspires to become Master General, while Stairnon insists on his Gothic kingship. The book follows his command in the costly battle of Frigidus in 394; his approach to, negotiations with, and withdrawal from Constantinople in 395; his appointment as Master General of Illyricum and elevation as Gothic king in 397; and his Italian campaign beginning late in 401 and ending in defeat in 403. He then sincerely pledges himself to the Empire and helps maintain its eastern peace until 408. Secretly summoned to the Italian border by the generals' council, especially Sarus, he is drawn into the conflict after Stilicho's murder. The narrative places his first siege of Rome in 409, acknowledges uncertainty over siege details, and subsequently has him secure Attalus's elevation as emperor. Negotiations at Ravenna collapse after Sarus attacks under a truce. Under pressure from Stairnon, Athaulf, and the Gothic elders, Alaric abandons his Roman allegiance before taking Rome in 410. The narrator calls this a werewolf-like change: the Empire has become a cruel joke to Alaric, and he would replace it. The ensuing events enter an explicitly legendary register. A tempest destroys the Goths' fleet; Alaric dies of fever three months after taking Rome; and his burial beneath the diverted River Busento is a legend whose literal truth the narrator doubts. (<em>The Fall of Rome</em>, PDF pp. 32-35, 38, 40, 51, 60, 62, 101, 114, 120, 143-144, 176-177, 221, 232, 246, 251, 272, 278, 287, 300, 302, 304-305, 308-310).","citations":["MTM B6:204-205"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Folklore","History & Memory","Identity","Soldiers","Combat","Family","Italy","Literature","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Alaric's Sword","definition":"Relic listed among the <em>Argo</em> Masters' recoveries [MTM B13:422]. In <em>The Fall of Rome</em>, a related Alaric sword is a broken-hafted, nearly rusted-through blade brought from Shade Island, a Gothic cemetery in the Danube. In Alaric's reported encounter, a towering ancestral figure who speaks with the authority of the dead gives him the sword and says that no other man can wield it. Useless in battle, it becomes an impressive talisman and recognized symbol of authority, held by Alaric when the Goths raise him as king. The narrator preserves uncertainty: Stairnon and Athaulf might have contrived the encounter, and the old Goths either recognized the sword's signs or came to believe that they did. (<em>The Fall of Rome</em>, PDF pp. 99, 101-104).","citations":["MTM B13:422"],"tags":["Argonautica","Folklore","Weapons","History & Memory","Literature","The Dead"]},{"name":"Alarm (Clock)","definition":"Hans Schultz’s alarm clock, pictured as a chrome-colored dragon with an “ivory grin.” It gives a clattering roar until he throttles it; when he releases the lever it can only go “tunkle tunkle” [A:16-17].","citations":["A:16-17"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Alarm (Mind-Alarm)","definition":"Nonphysical alarm that Charlotte Garfield believes she triggered while robbing a walk-in vault containing Aga Gonof’s stash. She claims a small share of the stash is hers [MTM B3:84] [MTM B3:86]. The alarm detects her temporary name Mullens but not her appearance [MTM B3:93]. After Gloria and Manolo are murdered, Letitia says the killers acquired the Mullens name and hotel from this alarm but never knew their target was a little girl or midget [MTM B3:97].","citations":["MTM B3:84","MTM B3:86","MTM B3:93","MTM B3:97"],"tags":["Crime","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Albanians","definition":"People whom others claim are related to the Transylvanians, according to Countess Margaret. She lists them with Slavs, Greeks, Armenians, and Goths, then rejects all these proposed origins while asserting Transylvanian separateness [MTM B8:255].","citations":["MTM B8:255"],"tags":["Eastern Europe","Identity"]},{"name":"Albertus Magnus (Albert the Great) (c. 1200--1280)","definition":"German Dominican, bishop, philosopher, and natural investigator; teacher of Thomas Aquinas and commentator on Aristotle. His studies included zoology in <em>De animalibus</em>, and the breadth of his learning earned him the title <em>Doctor universalis</em>. He was canonized and declared a Doctor of the Church in 1931. John William Schultz insists that fourth-graders should already have mastered the catechism and should be studying Aquinas and Albert [A:208-209].","citations":["A:208-209"],"tags":["Saints","Religious Orders","Theology","Germany","Historical Figures","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Albion of the White Cliffs","definition":"Britain or England under an ancient literary name. In Henry Salvatore’s dream, Albion of the White Cliffs is one of the shattered masts of the <em>Navicula Petri</em>, alongside Moscovy and Gaul. The dream’s account describes Albion’s apostasy as foretold in the Apocalypse [A:44-45].","citations":["A:44-45"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Apocalyptic","Prophecy & Visions","Dreams"]},{"name":"Alcohol","definition":"Recurring element of the characters’ social life and Finnegan’s dependence. While cleaning bars and washing dishes he drinks during breaks and in the evenings [D:19]. The narrator later calls him the most addicted of the four friends on Basse-Terre, though Johnny Duckwalk can drink more [D:236]. At the Old Wooden Ship, John Indelecio and John Jereboam bear the nicknames Little Alcohol and Big Alcohol [D:215].","citations":["D:19","D:236","D:215"],"tags":["Alcohol","Bars & Taverns","Finnegan","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Alcove","definition":"Storage nook Duffey builds for the hundred bushels of barley bought to supply the Big Pot. The barley fills the alcove six feet deep [A:133].","citations":["A:133"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Alexander the Great","definition":"Historical ruler used as a chronological marker in Anastasia’s account of mermaids. She says that they had reached the Red Sea by his time, though how they arrived is unknown [D:34-35].","citations":["D:34-35"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Nymphs & Mermaids","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Alexandrian","definition":"Earlier heading that misspelled the proposed vehicle model. See <em>Ford Alexandrine</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Ali","definition":"Boy in Don Lewis’s Big House tale. Its inhabitants stay young by killing visitors and eating their organs. Ali brings two ta’a nuts, plants one before entering, and keeps the other in his pocket, which prevents the household from killing him. He snaps his fingers to bring morning; the planted nut has become a tree with a branch at his window, and he climbs down to escape. The household consequently ages a year [D:48-49].","citations":["D:48-49"],"tags":["Folklore","Magic & Sorcery","Immortality"]},{"name":"Alice","definition":"Town named by Demetrio among the places he visited in his youth, in his account of having traveled throughout the world and the United States [D:58].","citations":["D:58"],"tags":["History & Memory"]},{"name":"Alkies","definition":"Captain Guiterrez’s term for the Dirty Fivers as heavy drinkers. At the Buffalo Bar he calls them “a bunch of Alkies,” including himself when he is with them [A:66].","citations":["A:66"],"tags":["Alcohol","Soldiers","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Allentown","definition":"Town on the Pittsburgh bus route described to Finnegan. The bus goes through Allentown and Reading rather than Philadelphia [D:170].","citations":["D:170"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Alligator Calls","definition":"Animal-call instruments Giulio Solli whittles from tupelo-gum for Finnegan to blow while they drift through the Cajun swamps. The set calls alligators, panthers, devil-fish, swamp boas, hairy men, and white sharks; Finnegan says he never knew what would answer from the water or trees [MTM B5:174].","citations":["MTM B5:174"],"tags":["Louisiana","Finnegan","Family"]},{"name":"Alligator Tail Soup","definition":"Alligator-tail stew mixed into Duffey’s barley soup among the changing ingredients of the Big Pot [A:134].","citations":["A:134"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Almonds","definition":"Food Finnegan eats while wandering Tangier during the hunt that ends in Don Lewis’s death [D:103-105]. Almond also appears among the skin-color comparisons applied to the island women pursued by Don Barnaby and Johnny Duckwalk [D:236].","citations":["D:103-105","D:236"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Africa","Finnegan","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Aloysia, Linus","definition":"Linus Aloysia, one of Duffey’s musical friends during the Chicago years. He is named in a group spanning opera, ragtime, Chicago hot music, and string bands, with composers, players, and stagers among them; the passage does not assign an individual specialty to him [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Music"]},{"name":"Aloysius (Currency)","definition":"One of the hundred-dollar bills Finnegan has named and kept as the reserve at the bottom of an adventure. Aloysius is listed among the predecessors of Miguel [D:19].","citations":["D:19"],"tags":["Finnegan","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Aloysius McGivern Basket Weaver","definition":"Regular patron at the Old Wooden Ship tavern; receives a stipend from his affluent (\"red-dog rich\") family to stay away and avoid causing them embarrassment, with the condition that they won't return him to a sanitarium [D:225]; known for complaining about being compelled into basket weaving (\"'The Devil finds work for idle hands to do,' they say... Why the devil don't they put the Devil to basket weaving...?\") [D:225]; one of the diverse group of fifty regulars at the tavern [D:216].","citations":["D:225","D:216"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Money & Trade","Family"]},{"name":"Aloysius, Brother","definition":"Unverified identification. The supplied citations do not establish a Brother Aloysius, his alleged mathematical blessing, his clothing, his journey to Rome, or his connection with Finnegan.","citations":[],"tags":[]},{"name":"Aloysius, Linus","definition":"Earlier heading that misspelled the musician’s surname. See <em>Aloysia, Linus</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Music"]},{"name":"Alpha (Plan)","definition":"Earlier phase of Casey Szymansky’s plan to become world leader. He tells Letitia that Alpha will become past history when he mounts the white horse and begins Beta, first taking Chicago, then America, then the world [MTM B4:117].","citations":["MTM B4:117"],"tags":["Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Alpha Centauri","definition":"One of the stars whose right ascension Hans learns to calculate under Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni. The Professor treats such calculations as only the childhood of astronomy [A:211] [A:214].","citations":["A:211","A:214"],"tags":[]},{"name":"Alpine","definition":"One of the racial categories, alongside Mediterranean, that the narrator says anthropologists impose on the Cajuns. The passage mocks these classifications and insists on the Cajuns as a people apart [A:123].","citations":["A:123"],"tags":["Louisiana","Identity"]},{"name":"Altar stone","definition":"Consecrated stone used as a portable altar for Mass. In Ignacio’s story, the eighth-century Benedictine Joseph of Maintz packs one into the chamber he fires to the moon, with bread, wine, grape cuttings, and wheat for continued celebration of Mass. His books include the Missal, Scriptures, writings of Augustine and Jerome, and Pliny’s <em>Natural History</em> [A:271]. The stone belongs to the tradition of consecrated altars containing saints’ relics; Aquinas explains the stone altar as signifying Christ and his stone tomb.","citations":["A:271"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Saints","Sacraments","Religious Orders","Liturgy","Patristics"]},{"name":"Alternate reality","definition":"The alternative present that the closing directions in <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> ask the reader to approach from another side. Enniscorthy Sweeny compares an event to a solid object viewed from past, present, alternative present, and future: no view is to cancel the others [MTM B13:467]. “Great Day in the Morning” offers a different disturbance of shared reality when Duffy sees other people pass through walls that still bruise and bloody him; he wonders whether his own subjectivity is at fault [GD:498].","citations":["MTM B13:467","GD:498"],"tags":["Duffey","Metaphysics","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Amargos","definition":"Amargos is the form in Archipelago; Amorgos is used in <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>. See <em>Amorgos</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Folklore","Seas & Oceans","Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Creation"]},{"name":"Amateur Drunks","definition":"Finnegan’s name for the drinkers who “blossom at receptions.” At Vincent and Theresa’s wedding reception in May 1946, he says he becomes an amateur himself for the occasion, “for sheer love of it,” then performs his familiar antics among the guests [A:95] [A:113].","citations":["A:95","A:113"],"tags":["Finnegan","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Amazons","definition":"Mythical warrior women; featured in Casey Szymansky's second erotic dream world fantasy, alongside female gladiators, Cretan girl bull-fighters, and circus girl acrobats [A:157].","citations":["A:157"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Folklore","Women Characters","Combat","Dreams"]},{"name":"Ambulance Driver","definition":"Wartime occupation Duffey claims to have held with Hemingway in Italy and Cummings in France before joining the American army. The narrator questions Duffey’s service and suggests he may have borrowed experiences from Sebastian Hilton, whom the account identifies as an ambulance driver in both countries [MTM B2:72].","citations":["MTM B2:72"],"tags":["Italy","France","Duffey","Historical Figures","World War I","Occupations"]},{"name":"America","definition":"Destination reached by St. Brandon in the <em>Argo</em>’s legendary history [MTM B13:404], and by some of Anastasia Grandmother’s emigrant granddaughters [D:125]. Anastasia’s mermaid account denies New World varieties [D:35]. Finnegan tells Brannagan that he has known beer served in pitchers only as an Australian custom [D:199]. Widow Waldo is said to have been Miss America [MTM B9:275].","citations":["MTM B13:404","D:125","D:35","D:199","MTM B9:275"],"tags":["Saints","Nymphs & Mermaids","Ships","Sailing","Ireland","Women Characters","Alcohol"]},{"name":"American (Nationality)","definition":"Nationality of Santee, Casper, and Lorrigan, the three soldiers in Don Lewis’s account of the disappearance on Willy Jones Island [D:44]. In Tangier it is Don Lewis, not Finnegan, whose Spanish sounds like an American imitating an Arab; Lewis also offers five hundred dollars American for a hiding place [D:107]. Manuel is separately described as South American [D:145].","citations":["D:44","D:107","D:145"],"tags":["Soldiers","World War II","Language & Words"]},{"name":"American Balzac","definition":"Title Casey imagines earning in his fourth dream world, the literary one. He will write ten thousand words daily and become so great that Balzac will eventually be called the French Szymansky [A:157-158].","citations":["A:157-158"],"tags":["France","Literary Figures","Literature","Dreams"]},{"name":"American Blood and Thunder (Old)","definition":"The sensational Western and crime romances that survive in the Harlow House library after Gautier’s goats have eaten the books they are allowed. The narrator lists “Old Wild West,” “Train Robber,” “Buffalo Hunter and Indian Scalper,” and “Bank Robber and Brigand” as descriptions of the surviving reading matter. Gautier, the goat-man, loves these books in both paper and hard covers [A:196-198].","citations":["A:196-198"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"American Colonel (elderly)","definition":"Missing elderly officer whose fingerprints match those of the apparently twenty-year-old Australian Private Gregory. Gregory offers the discovery as the reason the authorities detained and questioned him after his latest change of identity [A:78-79].","citations":["A:78-79"],"tags":["Australia","Soldiers","Identity","Immortality"]},{"name":"American counterpart","definition":"Unnamed American counterpart of the Australian brigadier whom a raiding party embarrasses during the Tania episode. Unaware of the scheme, he demands an explanation and then goes through the jungle seeking someone to blame [A:54]. The later account attributes a further layer of the trick to Stein [A:55].","citations":["A:54","A:55"],"tags":["Australia","World War II","Soldiers"]},{"name":"American Fig","definition":"Zabotski’s name for the sycamore he compares to Bryan Blackstone. He curses Blackstone’s tree to wither so that it will await its owner on the blessed shore, but expressly denies that he can command a man to die [MTM B9:270].","citations":["MTM B9:270"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"American History","definition":"Subject that John William Schultz would have children read through on the second night of his five-evening preparatory curriculum before serious education begins [A:209]. In Duffey’s first high-school year, American History is one of the subjects at which Sebastian Hilton leads the class [MTM B1:25].","citations":["A:209","MTM B1:25"],"tags":["History & Memory"]},{"name":"American Journalism","definition":"Field that Casey says Stein, Gabrielovitch, and Duffey have disgraced in his <em>Crock</em> attack on the newly launched <em>Bark</em>. He denounces their politics and qualifications in an extended polemic against the rival Catholic magazine [A:128-130].","citations":["A:128-130"],"tags":["Duffey","Literature","Journalism"]},{"name":"American Mediterranean","definition":"Casey’s name for the Great Lakes in his high-school notebook. He praises their shores and hinterland over the European Mediterranean and makes Chicago the center of the world [A:166].","citations":["A:166"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Literature"]},{"name":"American style cabaret","definition":"Cabaret that Falo and Nauti are building on Luzon after the war. They declare it open when Vincent and Finnegan arrive, using a large box as a table and two smaller boxes as chairs while construction continues [A:63-64].","citations":["A:63-64"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","World War II","Bars & Taverns","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"American Values ('Reaffirmation of')","definition":"Rehabilitation class Casey must attend three times weekly after four weeks in the stockade and demotion from sergeant. Its teacher is Stein the Red, whose influence Casey resents [A:50].","citations":["A:50"],"tags":["World War II","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Amnesia","definition":"Finnegan describes memory loss when moving between his upper and lower lives; Seaworthy answers that all their kind indulge in amnesia [D:11]. Finnegan claims its advantages when refusing to recognize Papa-D [D:15], while Anastasia challenges him with “Which will it be, amnesia or me?” [D:16]. The narrator recalls the gaps in his Green Islands identity and his personification of Amnesia as an old companion [D:50] [D:75]. Duffey’s Sea of the Seven Lost Years connects by a hidden strait with the Sea of Amnesia [MTM B13:406]. A counterpart in <em>Fourth Mansions</em> concerns Freddy Foley's disputed memory and identity during confinement in “the Bug.” Doctor Decker says that he was found wandering and amnesiac, identified as Julius Smith, and needs to recover his past as part of his cure. Foley denies that Smith is his name and accuses the authorities of hiding him under it. Richard Bencher reports that the official record instead says Foley insisted that he was Julius Smith and did not know or remember Fred Foley. Foley calls the record wrong and alleges “dirty work.” (<em>Fourth Mansions</em>, PDF pp. 161, 183, 186-187).","citations":["D:11","D:15","D:16","D:50","D:75","MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Seas & Oceans","Literature","Metaphysics","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Amnesia's Woods","definition":"Image for Finnegan’s gaps in memory. As he wonders whether he belongs to the “other human race,” the narrative recalls his passage through Amnesia’s woods and his meeting with Amnesia in the clearing [D:50].","citations":["D:50"],"tags":["Finnegan","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Amoral Hypnosis","definition":"Don Barnaby’s name for the condition induced by Madame Le Mourcheor’s Joy Pill, in which sleep becomes temporary death, then a transported and detached existence. He claims to move among distant places and many lives within the garden where he took the pill [D:250-251].","citations":["D:250-251"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Amorality","definition":"The Laughing Prince’s supposed freedom from moral allegiance. The narrator first calls total amorality impossible, then describes the Prince’s closed world of television images and hallucinations as the first massive instance of it. The desert and figures in which Duffey is murdered are called amoral, though his death in that unreal scene is real [MTM B13:438] [MTM B13:441-442].","citations":["MTM B13:438","MTM B13:441-442"],"tags":["Duffey","Metaphysics","Sin & Redemption","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Amorgos","definition":"Island named in Finnegan’s account of the original Aegean five-island group [A:35]. In Anastasia’s mermaid history, surviving mermaids inhabit Naxos and the rocks between it and Amorgos [D:35-36].","citations":["A:35","D:35-36"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Folklore","Seas & Oceans","Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Creation"]},{"name":"Amos","definition":"Prophet quoted in the narrator’s introduction to Casey’s problem of evil: “Shall there be evil in a city which the Lord hath not done?” The wording comes from Amos 3:6 and is presented as a puzzle before Casey declares evil the only problem in the world [A:154].","citations":["A:154"],"tags":["Old Testament","Theology","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Amoy","definition":"Sick parakeet visiting Ward Fourteen, whose stories Ignatius Ti expands from a few words of Tagalog-Parakeet. In Ti’s account, Amoy was once a red-winged blackbird trained by thieves, then a jewel thief in Karachi. After marrying an uncommon magpie of the common species, he withheld a blue diamond intended for her. His master Ali ben Taife, also called Lord Peter Petrof, County von Vinger, Sinhor Dedos, and Mr. Fingers, cut his throat to recover it. Amoy died and became a fish before becoming a parakeet [A:79-81]. A sadistic medico poisons him, and an official order calls for his death. He continues visiting friends, tries to be cheerful despite genuine persecution, and may be the ward’s best-balanced resident [A:81-82]. He later dies in terrible agony [A:88].","citations":["A:79-81","A:81-82","A:88"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Crime","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Amphora of rum","definition":"Quantity of rum Finnegan and Anastasia take past a cigar factory to the ruins and old walls above an unnamed harbor. The narrator places the episode possibly at Paraiso, Campeche, or another stop; it is not the later visit to Naxos [D:67-68].","citations":["D:67-68"],"tags":["Finnegan","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Amraphel","definition":"One of the Three Slant-Faced Men whom Duffey recognizes by names printed in the comic strip <em>Flame Man</em>. The narrative identifies Amraphel as king of Shinar or Upper Babylonia and groups him with Arioch and Thadal as opposite kings or anti-magi without magic. It further identifies them as followers of Chodorlahomor, king of Elam, while noting that this detail is absent from the strip [MTM B7:227-228].","citations":["MTM B7:227-228"],"tags":["Old Testament","Magic & Sorcery","Middle East","Duffey","Identity"]},{"name":"Amusement park rides","definition":"Amusement parks are among the crowded places Herman Hercules says he needs for his well-being. His list includes arenas, opera houses, circuses, fairs, music halls, and other places that remain alive with people [MB:585].","citations":["MB:585"],"tags":["Carnival","Theater"]},{"name":"Amyl acetate","definition":"One of Askandanakandrian’s seven “sovereigns,” the small set of substances he says can rehabilitate distressed merchandise ranging from fish to furniture [A:257]. Amyl acetates have a banana-like odor and are associated with the commercial name banana oil.","citations":["A:257"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Occupations"]},{"name":"Anachronism","definition":"Term Finnegan mistakenly applies to the redundancy he hears in “saltcellar.” When Doll questions “anachronism,” he corrects himself to “that other thing” meaning saying the same thing twice, then derives cellar from saliere and sal [D:189]. Duffey calls the arrival of his own cremated ashes while he is still alive an anachronism; Dotty replies that Duffey himself is one [MTM B6:203].","citations":["D:189","MTM B6:203"],"tags":["Language & Words","Finnegan","Duffey","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Anamnesis","definition":"Recollection, the principal function Teresa Piccone attributes to the <em>Argo</em> talismans. She describes them as gold filigree laid over pieces of the Talking Oak Dordogne, with gold from the Great Fleece. Their function is less to create the companions than to awaken them and restore their remembering, enabling them to recognize one another after renewed birth and sleep. Their reunion prefigures the Resurrection [MTM B5:178-179].","citations":["MTM B5:178-179"],"tags":["Argonautica","History & Memory","Identity","Resurrection","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Anas Paludosa","definition":"Duck species named by Biloxi Brannagan in his account of handling diamonds larger than ducks’ eggs. When Finnegan suggests the ducks must be small, Brannagan answers that they are large for their kind [D:207].","citations":["D:207"],"tags":["Language & Words","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Anastasia","definition":"Name Finnegan interprets as “the resurrection” when Anastasia Demetriades restores his respectability with an electric shaver and tends his wounds [D:16]. On Naxos, she and her grandmother share the name: Anastasia Gyiagyia is the grandmother, and Anastasia Engone is the visiting granddaughter, Demetriades herself [D:125-126].","citations":["D:16","D:125-126"],"tags":["Women Characters","Family","Resurrection","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Anastasia Demetriades","definition":"See <em>Demetriades, Anastasia</em>, the name she gives Finnegan when he asks who she is [D:16].","citations":["D:16"],"tags":["Women Characters","Nymphs & Mermaids"]},{"name":"Anastasia Engone","definition":"<em>Anastasia Demetriades</em> under the designation “Anastasia Engone,” glossed as granddaughter during her visit to Anastasia Gyiagyia on Naxos [D:125-126]. She is the granddaughter who jokes that the morning when Prometheus brought fire was cold [D:127].","citations":["D:125-126","D:127"],"tags":["Women Characters","Family","Greece"]},{"name":"Anastasia Grandmother (Gyiagyia)","definition":"Anastasia Demetriades' grandmother on the mountain road of Naxos. Black-haired and black-eyed, she immediately recognizes her granddaughter but pretends otherwise as a game. She speaks only Romaic and lives alone in a diminished house with fire-damaged rooms boarded off. Though poor, she remains vital and pretty, and was once prettier than her granddaughter [D:125-126]. She cooks the kid Finnegan and Anastasia buy and prepare. At dinner, she jokes that her granddaughter was too little to remember the morning of Prometheus [D:126-127]. She calls Finnegan one of the violent ones of the other blood, says that she shares it, and that her granddaughter has a little of it [D:128].","citations":["D:125-126","D:126-127","D:128"],"tags":["Women Characters","Family","Greece","Islands","Identity"]},{"name":"Anatolia","definition":"Origin of the <em>Seabach</em> when it approaches the blind side of Naxos with six men aboard [D:135]. Earlier, Finnegan includes Anatolia among the directions from which Anastasia's awaited boat might come; she declines his help watching [D:131]. Turkish Anatolia is also the source of the turbaned, laughing marble Christ in Duffey's collection [MTM B3:104].","citations":["D:135","D:131","MTM B3:104"],"tags":["Middle East","Ships","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Ancient World","definition":"The books' varied accounts of prehistory, classical antiquity and the medieval centuries, often given by speakers with conflicting histories. Captain Sammy Guiterrez calls the West old and the East young, dismissing Chinese and Irish claims as “toy history” [A:66]. Zabotski speculates that a thousand years of supposed dark ages might have originated in a sick boy's chronicle in 1348 [MTM B9:281-282]. A Pattenite challenges the Olduvai evidence, and Duffey calls it fraudulent while accepting other evidence for human antiquity beyond the Pattenite's allowance [MTM B9:282-283]. Zabotski describes the Pristine World as real, essential and intuitively inhabited. It occupies the ordinary Molasses World's space differently, excludes duplicated and invalid persons and situations, and lets him live years within an ordinary day or two [MTM B9:277] [MTM B9:279].\n\nThe Royal Pop Historians claim new ways of recovering history from stone patinas and transparencies, maintain that they were present at ancient events, and announce the obliteration of history's former subject matter [MTM B11:323-325]. Duffey examines their goatskin proclamation with violet light and finds older writing beneath it, including a Fourth Gospel with marginal pagona shorthand [MTM B11:326-327]. Hugh de Turenne announces the Black Sea's obliteration at Himself's command, partly to erase the memory of his imprisonment and undermine Colchis and the Fleece [MTM B11:336]. Cyrus Roundhead reduces Rome to an Etruscan neighborhood, calls the Etruscans a pop people, and says the Macedonians invented heroic Greece, Athens and its romances. He describes Jerusalem as a failed modern property venture and asserts that most Jews were and remain in Babylon. These are the Royal Pop revisionists' assertions within the conflict, not a settled history shared by all the characters [MTM B11:337].\n\nOther accounts supply different foundations. Behind Duffey's twentieth-century childhoods stands his more real life of thousands of years as the Boy King, with dromedary-hide tents, flocks, pastures and silver rivers [MTM B1:17]. The opening applies the phrase “without father, without mother” to Duffey with explicit uncertainty about its fit [MTM B1:6]. His childhood also includes invisible giants commanded to ring a bell [MTM B1:15]. Ignacio Ti's story places Joseph of Maintz, Bishop Turpin and Charlemagne in an eighth-century “noon-time of the World”; Joseph takes Augustine's writings, among other books, to the moon [A:271]. An epigraph attributed to G. K. Chesterton's The Everlasting Man presents the Creator's personal appearance in the Roman Empire as a unique claim [MTM B5:176]. Absalom Stein's notes call 946-1946 the Devil's imprisonment and the empire of Neustria or Christendom [MTM B6:185]. Henri Salvatore's unsent letter says Otto I first bolted the door at Aachen in 946 and transferred the prisoner that year to the Crimean Chersonese [MTM B5:180]. X's account calls the peninsula Tauric or Euxine Chersonese, dates the transfer 946 or 947, and says its names Phylake and Carcer meant Prison for more than a thousand years before the actual imprisonment [MTM B6:204]. Henri is identified as the former Euphemus, later Emperor Henry of Neustria and now emperor of Invisible Neustria [MTM B8:245].\n\nAncient geography also enters poems, journeys and arguments. Hans imagines a southern voyage farther than Ulysses went and beyond Ophir, to which the men of Solomon and Hiram made their three-year voyage [A:16]. Finnegan calls the Aegean the original Pentanesia, naming Andros, Tinos, Paros and Amargos, with Hans supplying Naxos. In the same island reverie Finn invokes Strabo's sense of islands, St. Paul and Xavier's greater archipelagos [A:35]. The <em>Argo</em>'s later history includes carrying St. Paul [MTM B13:404]. The Fin-Iason poem proposes climbing Sinai to recover what Moses forgot and asks whether the burning bush is at Hebron or Hest [A:171]. Its next page separates Argonauts mired in Friesian bogs from the conjecture that Illyrian fleets vanished in Illyrian fogs [A:172]. Casey's notebook records claims that the Romanies founded and named Rome and that Hinnegan's Milesians were in Ireland before the Flood [A:164-165]. San Simeon's forgotten ancient name is Babylon, and its rulers are the mad Balbo dynasty [MB:558].\n\nRosemary Riorden tells Finn that he and his friends began in Illyria: he is the son of the demiurge Aeson although he believes his father to be Giulio, and his companions include Orpheus, Peleus, Euphemus and Meleager. She forecasts his later company with the Dioscuri and the love of Aeetes' daughter in several forms, including her own, and says he will find the Fleece without knowing what he has found [A:89]. Teresa's later catalogue names Finnegan as Iason, Hans as Orpheus and subsequently Faust, Vincent as Meleager, Henry as Euphemus, Casey as Peleus, Dotty as Medea, Marie as Eurydice, Mary Virginia as Laonome, herself as Atalanta, Mary Catherine as Antigone and Stein as Ab-Salom. She calls their Colchis voyage the prototype of quests, says they passed the Devil's future Chersonese prison, and insists that they still hide the Golden Fleece from him [MTM B7:234]. Duffey's commentary traces Casey from Peleus through Kasmir Gorshok and Prince Casimir to his modern identity. His Peleus is Achilles' father and the victim of Acastus of Iolcus and his wife's badger game [PG:534-536]. Dotty says the <em>Argo</em> voyages continue as the company's other life [MTM B5:180]. The ship's logs make the finding of the Fleece of Colchis one of its early triumphs, though other quests were equally or more important [MTM B13:421]. Gorshok later makes the fleshless Duffey a mask, scarf and gauntlets from the Fleece's combings [MTM B13:443].\n\nPatrick Stranahan's ancient standards include Livy as history and the advice to read Augustine once in youth [A:109]. He polemically compares contemporary opponents to Sadducees who reject resurrection [A:110]. He calls St. Patrick Roman rather than Irish, his own wife Monica returned, Dante a rimester and Petrarch a sonnet writer, and prefers the anonymity he associates with better medieval artists [A:111]. Patricia Solli calls her father Giulio literally antediluvian and reports that an important man called him the Monster Forgotten [A:174-175]. Duffey recognizes Finnegan as the son of the Monster Giulio, whom he himself knew [MTM B5:174].\n\nIn an uncertain ocean dream, Marie calls herself Demeter and Persephone and explains a Demeter-Pontos grouping as a precursor of the pietà form [D:91-92]. Finn's story of Galen's lunar measurements uses a giant goat-sucker passing behind the moon [D:132]. A chapter epigraph attributed in the novel to Galen describes daemons begetting Tityroi or Satyroi and renewing their race, including a night's procreation with thirteen women at Calchedon. The narrator follows it by asking about Galen's teacher Satyrus and the source of his information [D:160-161]. Ewaglouwshkoul, the Neanderthal Eve, is awakened from the ice by the magus Duffey. She guesses she was her tribe's last woman, but allows that males and other bands may have survived; she says her people invented ships and seamen [MTM B13:399-401]. The Laughing Prince of Tartary claims enmity with both God and the Devil, although reports question his independence. Duffey eventually recognizes him as the Giggling Prince [MTM B13:437-438] [MTM B13:441]. An unlabeled speaker in “Promontory Goats” recalls Justinian's skull giving judgments to chieftains through a snake emerging from its right eye-socket for yes and left for no, then uses the image to disown the judgments his own body may make after death [PG:545].\n\nTeresa explains the talismans as <em>Argo</em> gold filigree over Talking Oak Dordogne, with the gold derived from the Fleece. Their work is Anamnesis or Recollection, awakening the company to recognition, and their reunion prefigures Resurrection [MTM B5:178-179]. Henri describes Paradise and the completed quest as an already possessed foundation from which the world must be rebuilt [MTM B5:179-180]. <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> locates the Terrestrial Paradise in the Antilles [D:234]; its narrator connects the Haggada's wormy forbidden fruit with a possible hint in Augustine [D:264]. Angela's father exploits the Caribbean's true and false passages in his Master Map and half-map trade [D:260-261].","citations":["A:66","MTM B9:281-282","MTM B9:282-283","MTM B9:277","MTM B9:279","MTM B11:323-325","MTM B11:326-327","MTM B11:336","MTM B11:337","MTM B1:17","MTM B1:6","MTM B1:15","A:271","MTM B5:176","MTM B6:185","MTM B5:180","MTM B6:204","MTM B8:245","A:16","A:35","MTM B13:404","A:171","A:172","A:164-165","MB:558","A:89","MTM B7:234","PG:534-536","MTM B13:421","MTM B13:443","A:109","A:110","A:111","A:174-175","MTM B5:174","D:91-92","D:132","D:160-161","MTM B13:399-401","MTM B13:437-438","MTM B13:441","PG:545","MTM B5:178-179","MTM B5:179-180","D:234","D:264","D:260-261"],"tags":["Saints","Old Testament","New Testament","Apocalyptic","Heaven & Hell","Patristics","Argonautica","Magic & Sorcery","Monsters & Creatures","Folklore","The Devil","Giants & Titans","Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans","Islands","Ireland","Italy","Greece","Middle East","Finnegan","Duffey","Argonauts (Characters)","Women Characters","Historical Figures","The Dead","Visual Arts","Literature","Family","Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","History & Memory","Dreams","Identity","Resurrection","Creation"]},{"name":"Andaman Sea","definition":"Sea included in the catalogue of waters sailed by the <em>Argo</em> and remembered by Melchisedech Duffey [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Sailing","Argonautica"]},{"name":"Andros","definition":"Island Finnegan names in the Aegean Pentanesia, the original five-island cluster in his account of archipelago origins. He also names Tinos, Paros, and Amargos; Hans supplies the forgotten fifth name, Naxos [A:35].","citations":["A:35"],"tags":["Greece","Islands","Folklore"]},{"name":"Anegada Passage","definition":"One of the seven true passages through the Antilles in Angela Cosquin's account of her father's treasure-map trade. She contrasts these with seven false or shoal passages, where a logical ship ought to have gone down [D:260].","citations":["D:260"],"tags":["Caribbean","Seas & Oceans","Shipwrecks","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Anemotrephes","definition":"One of the three Oreads Finnegan encounters in his Naxos mountain dreams, with Akroreia and Oreibates. They call Anastasia their sister and warn him to leave after the coming night's events [D:137]. Anemotrephes denies being a teras, while her sisters' answers concede the affinity; Anastasia later confirms that they share it a little. The three push Finnegan off the dream mountain [D:139].","citations":["D:137","D:139"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Greece","Dreams","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Angela Cosquin","definition":"Barmaid and factotum at Joseph Chastaigne's establishment on Basse-Terre, called the Imperial or Royal Bar and nicknamed Sloppy Joe's [D:235] [D:258] [D:266]. Angela is beautiful in an old island fashion, dazzlingly dressed, musical and talkative; her ancestry includes French, Carib, English, noble and enslaved forebears. She can carry two hundred pounds on her head and launch a six-man boat alone [D:259]. She says that her stories are true, unlike those of the four topers. Her father was a skilled cartographer and antiquarian whose decorative maps developed into a trade in fabricated pirate treasure maps and supporting papers; Angela retains some for sale [D:259-261]. She denies the nearby <em>St. Marc</em> and <em>Sangre del Cristo</em> wrecks, but the narrator reveals that she knows them well and dives among their real treasure. After attracting suspicion by spending old coins and wearing diamonds, she returns her loot and deposits her own money in a submerged copper box marked “Angela Cosquin sa Caisse.” Every month Johnny Duckwalk steals the sovereign she has just added [D:262-263]. Her seven accurate maps of Finnegan's hidden stashes, marked S X S and robado por C F, alarm him; he infers that she has read his mind, and she says she will keep the maps as a memory. She is the first to call him Count Finnegan [D:263]. She deplores Barnaby's and Le Marin's departures and predicts that those who leave never return [D:265-266]. She offers Finnegan two ships full of gold and everything else if he stays. They promise to protect the poor, and he sits on her strong knees to say goodbye. He never returns [D:268].","citations":["D:235","D:258","D:266","D:259","D:259-261","D:262-263","D:263","D:265-266","D:268"],"tags":["Women Characters","Caribbean","Bars & Taverns","Shipwrecks","Money & Trade","Family","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Angels","definition":"Lazarus Reilly offers newcomers lodging, credit and loans at the Old Wooden Ship lest he slight an angel in disguise. The narrator identifies Soft-Talk Susie Kutz as the only actual angel among the regulars; her favorable reports help the tavern prosper [D:216]. In the debate about the Ark, Fundamentalers ask whether angels guard it in the Armenian mountains. Zabotski says that angels guard things beyond human reach, including wooden castles even if they are not the Ark [MTM B9:280-281].","citations":["D:216","MTM B9:280-281"],"tags":["Angels & Demons","Bars & Taverns","Theology"]},{"name":"Anger","definition":"Seaworthy's response to the Texas beer served after his and Finnegan's awakening: “There should be a punishment to fit the crime” [D:12]. The narrator also describes Papa Devil as often on the edge of anger, making him someone to fear [D:87].","citations":["D:12","D:87"],"tags":["Finnegan","Devils (Characters)","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Angry League For Style In The Universe, The Unbelievers'","definition":"The Unbelievers' Angry League For Style In The Universe, whose members hate <em>The Resurrection of Count Finnegan</em> although none has seen it [MB:554]. At the close of How Many Miles to Babylon, the League condemns the happy ending and its supposed lack of true style, calling it “shameful corn” [MB:598-599].","citations":["MB:554","MB:598-599"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","Visual Arts","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Anima","definition":"In Jung's terminology, the feminine aspect of the male psyche. The Postscript names Anima and Animus as opposing twins alongside Ego, Self and Shadow in its account of Jung's Aion, using this plurality to discuss the several persons within a person [PS:477].","citations":["PS:477"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Identity","Literature"]},{"name":"Animal Form","definition":"Finnegan tells the Capuchin that an old animal is taking him over, linking this to his new memories of the Devil's kindred and different blood [D:119]. Later the narrator describes him as feral, a living gargoyle and an ape-cat while he climbs buildings, attacks a man and escapes with Seaworthy's treasure. The passage says he becomes a man again after the escape, while leaving the precise nature of the transformation elusive [D:162] [D:164-165]. A local legend calls the climber an ape or monstrous man [D:162]. Joe Cross compares Dopey's scream to that of a terrified animal [D:64], and Doll repeats someone else's description of Finnegan as a hooked-nosed ape during a night of rough amusement [D:173].","citations":["D:119","D:162","D:164-165","D:64","D:173"],"tags":["Finnegan","Shapeshifting","Monsters & Creatures","Identity"]},{"name":"Animality","definition":"Quality whose denigration Finnegan challenges in his visionary account of the older people. He calls them brothers of the great animals and asks how animality became “a dirty thing” [D:212-213]. Duffey uses the word adversely in a different context, listing animality among the degradations the Devil will bring and have mistaken for holy poverty [MTM B6:194].","citations":["D:212-213","MTM B6:194"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Monsters & Creatures","The Devil","Theology"]},{"name":"Animated Marvels","definition":"Duffey's extraordinary company of talismanic creations, called Animated Marvels, magnificent animations and splendid animations. At the Rounders' Club they have “Unreality Fringes,” a smokey halo, yet are real; their extravagant conversations cannot later be recalled [MTM B5:158-161]. Duffey says he literally helped form twelve people in his present life, with an additional uncounted creation and others of mixed status, while conceding that he did not do everything [MTM B5:151]. The talismans are described as small engraved flat gold sticks distributed during his hidden years, including one for Giulio's unborn child; Letitia's rib-shaped talisman makes her a bonus outside the twelve [MTM B4:120-121] [MTM B3:90] [MTM B3:93]. Duffey had begun giving talismans away as a child [MTM B1:18]. He says that he put more than ordinary Teras blood into his creations [MTM B5:170]. Dotty's letter gives another account: through Finnegan they share a Teras intellectual stratum between conscious and unconscious thought, an elusive power expressed in his paintings and their conversations [MTM B6:189].\n\nTheir origin remains contested. Marie Monaghan Schultz says Duffey collected their forgotten souls, gave them settings and sparkle, and displayed them, rather than making them; she invokes his <em>Razzle Daz</em> comic strip [MTM B5:159]. Stein's Diminishing Theory alternates groups making Melchisedechs and Melchisedechs making groups, each somewhat smaller than its predecessor [MTM B4:121]. In his notebook Stein believes on one day that the company and Duffey's earlier acquaintances made Duffey; on other days he believes Duffey evoked them from clay. They love him despite his unlovely qualities [MTM B10:304]. Teresa describes talismans of <em>Argo</em> gold filigree and Talking Oak Dordogne, their principal work being recollection and awakening rather than creation; reunion prefigures Resurrection [MTM B5:178-179]. Patrick Plunket likewise distinguishes Duffey's animated clays and quick-earths from the reality they became, and says he only woke them [MTM B12:390-391]. The dung, blood and lightning in the Muck of Animation produce a later dozen, which Duffey calls better than his first dozen [MTM B12:394].\n\nThe formal list names Finnegan/John Solli, son of Monster Giulio; Hans/John Schultz; Henri Salvatore; Vincent Stranahan; Casey Szymansky; Dotty Yekouris; Mary Monaghan Schultz; Mary Virginia Schaeffer; Teresa Piccone; Absalom Stein; and Mr. X. Although called twelve, the enumeration supplies eleven names [MTM B5:161-162]. Mary Catherine Carruthers is already recognized as one of the creations in the preceding scene [MTM B5:158]. Teresa later supplies the Argonaut identities: Finnegan is Iason/Jason; Hans Orpheus and later Faust; Henry Euphemus; Vincent Meleager; Casey Peleus; Dotty Medea; Marie Eurydice; Mary Virginia Laonome; Teresa Atalanta; Mary Catherine Antigone; and Stein Ab-Salom. She withholds Margaret Stone's identity and does not identify X [MTM B7:234]. Duffey calls Finnegan the original Finn McCool and places his Marvels in the intersecting Argo Legend and Finnegan Cycle [MTM B5:169]. He also describes Casey as the medieval Prince Casimir and his own twentieth-century creation [PG:536]. The Countess is said to be someone's animation, but whose remains unclear [MTM B7:215].\n\nHenri describes their condition as Quest Completed, Fleece found and Grail Abundant, with rebuilding the world still to do [MTM B5:179-180]. The narrative says the Splendid Animations have sailed, are sailing and will continue to sail on <em>Argo</em> [MTM B10:300]. Most are <em>Argo</em> Masters, probably nearly as old as Duffey, forming an interdependent living corporation; Teresa's role is probably animating, while Duffey's is creative. Brannagan belongs to this larger circle [MTM B13:446]. Their Land, also called the Archipelago or Greater Archipelago, is a scattered recuperative island concentrated in Chicago, St. Louis and New Orleans. It offers the ship water, provisions, healing and virtues; the temporal relations remain uncorrelated [MTM B13:448].\n\nRelated groups should be distinguished. Cyrus Roundhead's Royal Pop company resembles Duffey's creations but goes further in a treacherous direction; its presence produces the Geometry of Shining Space [MTM B11:321]. Its young performers supply the Horse Opera's splendor before the slaughter [MTM B11:333]. Roundhead's claim that the final persons have no human chromosomes, blood, hormones or mental processes describes his own group and recruits [MTM B11:350]. The shining bodies that move through matter and apparently time belong to the Casey Machine's broader description of the splendid moment [CM:511]. The Devil is Dead offers parallels through the other human race, Non-people, Teras, double blood and Other Flesh [D:50] [D:121] [D:139] [D:209] [D:237] [D:285]; its attributed Overlark passage connects Neanderthal inheritance with imagination and ghostliness [D:270-272]. These parallels do not establish that every such figure is one of Duffey's twelve. Papa Devil I and II are grandchildren of the Devil through Ifreann; Doll resists X's account of primordial control and insists that people can act against it [D:278-279] [D:285] [D:292]. Herman, his primary Cardinal Erculo, and the remembered Saul are other long-lived figures, without being identified there as Duffey's creations [MB:584-585].","citations":["MTM B5:158-161","MTM B5:151","MTM B4:120-121","MTM B3:90","MTM B3:93","MTM B1:18","MTM B5:170","MTM B6:189","MTM B5:159","MTM B4:121","MTM B10:304","MTM B5:178-179","MTM B12:390-391","MTM B12:394","MTM B5:161-162","MTM B5:158","MTM B7:234","MTM B5:169","PG:536","MTM B7:215","MTM B5:179-180","MTM B10:300","MTM B13:446","MTM B13:448","MTM B11:321","MTM B11:333","MTM B11:350","CM:511","D:50","D:121","D:139","D:209","D:237","D:285","D:270-272","D:278-279","D:292","MB:584-585"],"tags":["Argonautica","Magic & Sorcery","Monsters & Creatures","Ships","Sailing","Islands","Finnegan","Duffey","Family","Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","History & Memory","Identity","Creation","Resurrection"]},{"name":"Animosity","definition":"Efram Gate Gratz's declared attitude at the Last Conclave, where the leaders of the institution now called the Crowd assemble to dismantle it. He proclaims “total animosity” and vows to bury Peter and prevent the institution's return [MB:561].","citations":["MB:561"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Apocalyptic","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Animus","definition":"In Jung's terminology, the masculine aspect of the female psyche. The Postscript names Animus and Anima as opposing twins alongside Ego, Self and Shadow in its account of Jung's Aion, using this plurality to discuss the several persons within a person [PS:477].","citations":["PS:477"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Identity","Literature"]},{"name":"Anne","definition":"Radio broadcaster for the Japanese from Java during the Pacific war. Her intimate voice, homesickness-inducing songs and understanding of the soldiers give her an advantage that Stein's Anopheles Network cannot match in some respects. She signs off, “This is your best enemy, Anne, Good Night” [A:46].","citations":["A:46"],"tags":["Minor Characters","World War II","Pacific Theater","Music"]},{"name":"Annina","definition":"Sister of Dominic Vinobianco, whom Casey records thinking about constantly in his notebook at St. Bonaventure's boarding school [A:161-162] [A:166]. She is later named Annina Vinobianco among the girls he goes out with [A:168].","citations":["A:161-162","A:166","A:168"],"tags":["Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Annunciation Church of Sydney, Australia","definition":"Church where John Gottfried Schultz (Hans) and Marie Anna Monaghan marry on Monday, February 15, 1943. Because Hans lacks army permission, the marriage is to remain secret for a while; twenty or thirty attendees are all Marie's relatives [A:31].","citations":["A:31"],"tags":["Australia","Sacraments","Family","World War II"]},{"name":"Anopheles Network","definition":"Small G.I. radio station Stein establishes as part of his Special Service duties during the Pacific war. It occupies one of his two pyramidal tents. Casey writes squibs for its broadcasts, which also feature comic sponsors, records, volunteer speakers and jokes read by Stein [A:46].","citations":["A:46"],"tags":["World War II","Pacific Theater","Music","Literature"]},{"name":"Antagonist","definition":"Papa Devil warns that their clever antagonist may turn the Naxos trap into a double ambush [D:123]. Finnegan later believes he has killed his principal prey at the Pirate Hotel, but Seaworthy’s sour laugh shows that he has escaped again [D:231]. In the town of the final rendezvous Finnegan senses his old antagonist’s presence. The left-footed tracker is also there, with his employer; the tracker is not simply another name for the antagonist [D:275].","citations":["D:123","D:231","D:275"],"tags":["Finnegan","Crime","Combat"]},{"name":"Antarctica","definition":"Casey’s fifth imaginary world, that of the explorer, requires a cartographic error that makes Earth fifty percent larger and melon-shaped. In it Antarctica is an ice ring around a southern sea and sunny continent, probably Atlantis after continental dispersal. Casey imagines taking modern techniques there and bringing its Higher Ethic home [A:158].","citations":["A:158"],"tags":["Dreams","Fictional Places","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Antebellum period","definition":"A period before a war. In the account of superimposed <em>Brunhilde</em> voyages, X says that some incidents attributed to Finnegan in the first decade of the “second antebellum period” actually happened to Giulio Solli in the decade before World War I, and that Finnegan remembers them filially. The commentator immediately cautions that X cannot be depended upon [MTM B6:187].","citations":["MTM B6:187"],"tags":["History & Memory","World War I","Finnegan","Family"]},{"name":"Antecedent Worlds","definition":"Nine discarded but still animated and populated worlds in a fancy Mary Virginia Schaeffer records in her Duffey Book. She explicitly says that the idea of divine false starts has no authority in Scripture or revelation, though legends support it. Our world is the tenth and might itself be discarded. She speculates that Duffey has wandered in from one of the earlier worlds, whose different conditions explain his circular continuity and lack of parents [MTM B10:291-292].","citations":["MTM B10:291-292"],"tags":["Creation","Metaphysics","Duffey","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Antediluvian","definition":"Patti Solli’s description of her father Giulio, whose antiquity embarrasses her. She insists that she means the word literally: he was “from before the beginning,” and Finnegan and she look a little that way too [A:174].","citations":["A:174"],"tags":["Family","Finnegan","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Anthony (Saint)","definition":"Saint invoked when the narrator compares the beautiful, treacherous women in Casey’s second imaginary world to St. Anthony’s temptations [A:157], an allusion to Anthony the Abbot’s legendary struggle. An Anthony also appears beside Francis among the saints aboard the <em>Navicula Petri</em> in Henry’s dream, though that passage does not specify which Anthony [A:45].","citations":["A:157","A:45"],"tags":["Saints","Dreams","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Anthony Ship","definition":"Former name of <em>Argo</em> at Actium, where the ship was shamed, according to the history told by the talking oak in its wheel [MTM B13:404].","citations":["MTM B13:404"],"tags":["Ships","Argonautica","Naval Warfare"]},{"name":"Anthropomorphic Colts (Strong Experience of)","definition":"Phrase that rises half-consciously in Duffey’s mind during the Great Day upheaval. As façades and whole buildings topple, the noisy, airish activity outside seems like a clattering experience of anthropomorphic colts. Duffey then exclaims over the phrase itself [MTM B11:311].","citations":["MTM B11:311"],"tags":["Duffey","Language & Words","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Anti-Christus","definition":"Title Casey publicly claims as Casey of the Zodiac [MTM B12:382]. Mary Catherine says that his followers hail the Antichrist role as the highest human status, while she is unsure how fully Casey believes it himself [MTM B12:377]. Hilary Hilton equates him with Rolo Danovitz, the Antichrist of a quasi-fictional cycle [MTM B12:378]. Casey’s projection of his face and voice over television programs has helped make him known as Anti-Christus [MTM B12:380]. Teresa quotes a latter-day prophecy that ghostly Duffey will carry Prince Casimir on <em>Argo</em> to the coast nearest Armageddon, where he will reign for a while as Anti-Christ [MTM B12:388]. Finnegan likewise foretells <em>Argo</em>’s transport of Antichrist to Megiddo, while Duffey and the other Masters seek to prevent his coming aboard [MTM B13:453-454]. Duffey’s later commentary allows that Casey may instead be one of the false, premature antichrists [PG:535].","citations":["MTM B12:382","MTM B12:377","MTM B12:378","MTM B12:380","MTM B12:388","MTM B13:453-454","PG:535"],"tags":["Apocalyptic","The Devil","Prophecy & Visions","Identity","Argonautica"]},{"name":"Anti-Corn-Law League","definition":"British organization founded in 1839 to campaign for repeal of the Corn Laws, which were repealed in 1846. Its encyclopedia article runs into the defective ANTICHRIST entry that gives Casey’s birth details: after “His manifestation as the Antichrist was,” the text switches to the League’s founding and purpose [PG:528-529].","citations":["PG:528-529"],"tags":["History & Memory","Literature","Paradox"]},{"name":"Anti-Prize","definition":"The Sword and Scabbard of Saint Sécaire, a peril to the world that <em>Argo</em>’s Masters want to lose rather than recover. They lose it three times; on the last occasion they weld sword to scabbard with molten fillers and bury it deep in the Nine Mile Depth. Four hundred years later Cyril and Cyrus Dimbeau accidentally recover it while taking bathysphere core samples [MTM B13:422].","citations":["MTM B13:422"],"tags":["Weapons","Magic & Sorcery","Argonautica","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Antichrist","definition":"Eschatological role claimed by Casey, who calls himself Casey of the Zodiac, the Anti-Christus [MTM B12:382]. Finnegan foretells <em>Argo</em> carrying Antichrist to the Plains of Megiddo [MTM B13:453]; Duffey’s commentary nevertheless leaves open whether Casey is the final Antichrist or a premature, false one [PG:535].","citations":["MTM B12:382","MTM B13:453","PG:535"],"tags":["Apocalyptic","Prophecy & Visions","Identity","Argonautica"]},{"name":"ANTICHRIST (encyclopedia entry)","definition":"Defective entry in about one percent of copies of the A-C volume of a cheap Chicago encyclopedia, reported by Silas and Maud Whiterice. It identifies the Antichrist as Kasmir (Casey) W. Szymansky, born in Chicago on October 7, 1921, to pawnbroker and antique dealer Gabriel Szymansky and Miriam Lessing, then runs into the Anti-Corn-Law League article. The commentators call it a hoax, wonder whether Casey or his printery colleagues inserted it, and confirm the biographical particulars [PG:528-529].","citations":["PG:528-529"],"tags":["Literature","Paradox","Identity","Family"]},{"name":"Antilles","definition":"Island region in which the narrator locates the Terrestrial Paradise at precisely sixteen degrees north and sixty-one degrees, forty-four minutes, forty-two seconds west [D:234]. The following scene identifies the setting as Basse-Terre [D:235-236]. Angela Cosquin describes her father’s maps of seven true and seven false passages through the Antilles [D:260]. The narrator also uses the region to measure Johnny Duckwalk’s drinking: fewer than a dozen men in all the Antilles can outdrink him [D:236].","citations":["D:234","D:235-236","D:260","D:236"],"tags":["Caribbean","Islands","Heaven & Hell","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Antipodal Ghost","definition":"Finnegan’s phrase for Mr. X after a disguised-voice telephone warning that he must flee. Asked by Brannagan who called, Finnegan says “My antipodal ghost” and explains that they are alike but a world apart [D:209].","citations":["D:209"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Antipodes","definition":"The far southern world in Hans’s thoughts about Australia, a place beyond the voyages of Ulysses and the men of Solomon and Hiram, to which old legends are relegated and where griffins and dragons seem naturally at home [A:16]. Theresa Piccone teasingly asks Vincent for a fuller account of “the Rover Boys at the Antipodes” in her letter [A:49].","citations":["A:16","A:49"],"tags":["Australia","Folklore","Monsters & Creatures","Literature"]},{"name":"Antiquarian","definition":"One of Angela Cosquin’s father’s occupations, alongside cartography. His skill with old scripts, seals, inks, papers, parchment and documents helps him make convincing maps and supporting records for an increasingly fraudulent treasure-map trade [D:259-261].","citations":["D:259-261"],"tags":["Occupations","Money & Trade","Crime"]},{"name":"Antique Dealer","definition":"Gabriel Szymansky’s occupation on the rich-street side of his business. His two shops face different streets and connect through a passage beneath the alley: antique dealer to the rich, pawnbroker to the poor. He prospers but lets Casey think him poor [A:160].","citations":["A:160"],"tags":["Occupations","Money & Trade","Family","Social Class"]},{"name":"Anvils","definition":"Objects among the household “iron words” Duffey remembers and appropriates from other minds. The anvils bear the names S.R. & Co. or Peter Wright [MTM B1:17].","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Apathy (Goat)","definition":"One of the goats that understands Finnegan, named with Hermione, Cecille and Diocletian as the smartest of Gautier’s flock [A:199-200]. Gautier and his goats now live year-round in New Jersey and frequent the Barrens [A:196-197].","citations":["A:199-200","A:196-197"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Ape","definition":"One of the animal semblances assigned to Tityroi or Satyrs in the chapter epigraph attributed to Galen [D:160-161]. Finnegan becomes a feral climber, living gargoyle and ape-cat during the Pirate Hotel raid; the narration says he could not have performed the escape as a man and becomes a man again afterward. The climber also gives rise to a local ape or monstrous-man legend [D:162] [D:164-165]. Doll later echoes someone else’s description of him as a hooked-nosed ape [D:173].\n\nA related treatment appears in Lafferty’s Serpent’s Egg. Its Axel’s Apes, also called Smithy, Golden and Blue-Eyed Apes, are the Unfallen People of Second Eden in Ethiopia. Their gargoyle faces connect them with the novel’s legends of cathedral builders; adult height averages just over two meters, and a later passage raises a possible affinity with the old Titans. Ansel Grindstone interprets their human chromosome count as proof that they are men. A separate speaker reports that the boy Axel’s X-rays show a human skeleton beneath his fleshy hump, face and feet. The explicit human/ape/mechanical-hybrid speculation chiefly concerns the Dolophonoi-Assassins, with all three species proposed as their possible components.\n\nThe Apes are extraordinary smiths, stoneworkers and mathematicians, and speak slowly and thoughtfully. They possess bi-location or trans-location, exceptional strength, animal and bird communication, and the ability to smell and appraise metal deposits as far as two hundred kilometers away. The experimental child Axel’s intelligence is off the scale. The group appears somewhat childish to humans and computers, enjoys meditation, puzzles and animal stories, and has a weakness for wine, including the mushroom wine of its underground taverns. The narrator says the Apes are afraid of nothing and incapable of anger. Their refusal to give testimony is specifically a refusal to testify about human origins, not a general refusal of self-description.\n\nThe novel’s legends connect the Unfallen People with an Ape Bridge, which Axel Grindstone believes should be called the Angel Bridge, and with teaching smithcraft to the Romanies. Ansel proposes that God has not yet fully activated them. Their discovery by the human Axel Grindstone leads raiders to Second Eden. The reigning sleepy one calls the raiders unwitting divine agents and directs the Apes to go with them. Five hundred are taken away and sold to zoos and institutions; the newborn Axel goes to the Lynn-Randal experiment in America. About a hundred of the dispersed Apes are consumed by lightning within five minutes, which the narrator says is God taking them to himself.\n\nThe infant’s father, Alpha, follows, gathers companions from American zoos and founds Apes’ Alley in limestone caverns near the experiment. The community has shops, smithies, a mint, clubs, a silent-film theater and meditation halls, with old gas wells supplying light and power. Alpha and Satrap Saint Ledger are close friends. Inneall says that her own circulating blood is partly human and partly Axel’s, secretly taken from the two boys while they slept. The caverns are eventually closed, and a giant hand extinguishes the holy fire as a voice imposes three nights and days of Holy Sleep. The expected worldwide awakening is to begin when Axel wakes at midnight cockcrow, but he is stabbed in the throat in the instant before he can awaken the others. His friends place him in an ocean-floor cenotaph. Sebastian Lazar later suggests treating him and the other murdered children as Leaders Asleep who may return. The sea-louse epilog calls their condition an undead coma and says neither the Apes nor the Whales were promised succession to humanity, while also boasting of its own ability to outdo Inneall’s lies. (<em>Serpent's Egg</em>, PDF pp. 13, 17, 21-23, 25, 27-32, 52, 54-61, 77-78, 152-153, 157-158, 171, 176).","citations":["D:160-161","D:162","D:164-165","D:173"],"tags":["Finnegan","Shapeshifting","Monsters & Creatures","Literature","Creation","Identity","Resurrection","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Ape-cat","definition":"Narratorial name for the form Finnegan takes when climbing, attacking and escaping the Pirate Hotel. The passage says that as a man he could not have done it; only as the ape-cat could he accomplish the raid, and afterward he becomes a man again [D:164-165].","citations":["D:164-165"],"tags":["Finnegan","Shapeshifting","Monsters & Creatures","Crime"]},{"name":"APO Oceans","definition":"Humorous name for the waters separating Casey’s overseas army service from home, using APO, Army Post Office. At a <em>Crock</em> stockholders’ meeting a stranger presents a new signed proxy from Casey, still “across the APO Oceans”; it supersedes the earlier proxy Casey gave Duffey before going overseas [MTM B4:144].","citations":["MTM B4:144"],"tags":["World War II","Soldiers","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Apocalypse","definition":"The Book of Revelation, named in the account of Henry Salvatore’s <em>Navicula Petri</em> dream. Among the shattered masts is Albion of the White Cliffs, whose apostasy the narration says is foretold in the Apocalypse [A:45].","citations":["A:45"],"tags":["Scripture","New Testament","Apocalyptic","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Apocryphal","definition":"Status of some Finnegan adventures that the narrator says cannot be separated from the canonical accounts. His travels invite questions about simultaneous worlds and people; his friends remember different versions, and both Finnegan and X are unreliable tellers [A:187].","citations":["A:187"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Apollo","definition":"Greek god of music and prophecy, one of the identities assigned to Hans Schultz alongside Dr. Faustus and Aquinas Redevivus [A:39]. Hans’s dream of climbing an iron ladder toward the sun culminates in the statement that he was Apollo [A:62]. The narrator later again calls Hans Faust and Apollo while describing Casey’s place among the Dirty Five; it says Casey comes close to Hans in compassion [A:169].","citations":["A:39","A:62","A:169"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Dreams","Identity","Argonauts (Characters)"]},{"name":"Apollonius of Rhodes (c. 3rd century BC)","definition":"Hellenistic Greek poet of the third century BCE, author of the <em>Argonautica</em>, a four-book epic in hexameters about Jason and the Argonauts’ expedition for the Golden Fleece. His learned Alexandrian poem draws on Homeric epic and other earlier accounts of the voyage. Ancient biographical traditions associate him with Rhodes and the headship of the Alexandrian Library, though the details and chronology of his career are uncertain.","citations":[],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Argonautica","Greece"]},{"name":"Apopka","definition":"One of the Florida towns through which Finnegan, Doll and X drive in the evenings, along with Orlando, Kissimee, Pine Castle and sometimes Daytona, passing through citrus and scrub [D:183].","citations":["D:183"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Apostasy","definition":"Renunciation of religious faith, represented in Henry’s <em>Navicula Petri</em> dream by a shattered mast named Albion of the White Cliffs. The narration says Albion’s apostasy is foretold in the Apocalypse [A:45].","citations":["A:45"],"tags":["Theology","Prophecy & Visions","Dreams"]},{"name":"Apostate","definition":"The narrator’s description of W., one of four initialled figures whose episodes affect the Pelican and <em>Bark</em> circle. W. and Y. are omitted from the chronicle for reasons of space, and the narrator says little has been missed by omitting W. [A:132].","citations":["A:132"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Theology","Literature"]},{"name":"Apostle to the greater Archipelagos","definition":"Finnegan’s title for Francis Xavier in his meditation on ocean islands as constellations that prefigure the skies [A:35]. Xavier was a Jesuit missionary whose work extended from India through the Maluku islands to Japan.","citations":["A:35"],"tags":["Saints","Islands","Religious Orders"]},{"name":"Apostles","definition":"Christ’s apostles, present among the saintly crew of Henry’s <em>Navicula Petri</em> dream [A:45]. Salvation Sally imagines the Apostles accepting rest and kindness from flippant unbelievers, as she has received from Hans and Marie [A:8]. In a letter, Show Boat repeats Papa Piccone’s Italian legend of an unnamed tippling apostle, perhaps Thomas, Bartholomew or Jude, who finishes wine left in cups and wineskins but nevertheless converts tens of thousands [A:262]. Dotty calls Jude the patron of printers and little magazines; Finnegan supplies his association with impossible causes [A:115].","citations":["A:45","A:8","A:262","A:115"],"tags":["New Testament","Saints","Dreams","Alcohol","Journalism"]},{"name":"Apostolo, Evelyn","definition":"Member of Duffey’s bookish and literary North Chicago circle, listed with Tony Apostolo. The narrator calls 1925-1935 its golden age, followed by an electrum age of mixed gold and silver lasting until about 1946 [MTM B3:98].","citations":["MTM B3:98"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Literature","Duffey"]},{"name":"Apostolo, Tony","definition":"Reporter among Duffey’s literary Chicago friends [MTM B3:98]. Tony’s extravagant partisan opinions are frequently opposed by Rollo McSorley. He challenges the story that the Depression began with the 1929 stock-market crash, appealing to his own work for the International News Service at the time [MTM B4:122]. He bets one hundred dollars against Rollo that Duffey can move a mule by magic, points out nearby street-department mules, and calls a photographer to record the test; Rollo and Elena O’Higgins call their own photographers [MTM B4:129-130].","citations":["MTM B3:98","MTM B4:122","MTM B4:129-130"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Journalism","Money & Trade","Duffey"]},{"name":"Apostolus Potator Incognitus, Sanctus","definition":"Show Boat’s Latin title for the unknown drinking apostle of Papa Piccone’s Italian legend, approximately “Holy Apostle, Unknown Drinker.” In her letter to Dotty she says she prays to him, using Sanctus Apostolus Potator Incognitus, while defending Finnegan despite his failings [A:262].","citations":["A:262"],"tags":["Saints","Alcohol","Language & Words","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Apparition (Feast of the)","definition":"Marie Monaghan’s name for the feast on the day she becomes engaged to Hans. She calls it the second Thursday in February [A:28]; the marriage date places this in 1943, making the engagement February11 [A:31].","citations":["A:28","A:31"],"tags":["Family","Sacraments","Australia"]},{"name":"Apparition (Ghostly manifestation)","definition":"Papa Devil’s silent arrival on ruined walls is likened to a spook; the narrator leaves the port uncertain, perhaps Paraiso or Campeche [D:67-70]. In a later possible future, Casey appears as the Apparition, Man of Mystery and Antichristus aboard <em>Argo</em>, his glory made of tampered-with light [MTM B13:462].","citations":["D:67-70","MTM B13:462"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Devils (Characters)","Apocalyptic","Identity"]},{"name":"Apparitions","definition":"One of the phenomena Royce Rollins attributes to his supposed Neanderthal aliens in The Return of the Neanderthal: The Alien Within. The chapter epigraph presents this through Charles O. A. Harrington’s hostile review in Science for Today, which reports Rollins’s claim that apparitions, fetches, second sight and related manifestations are normal aspects of the species [D:240-242].","citations":["D:240-242"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Monsters & Creatures","Literature"]},{"name":"Apple Butter","definition":"One of the foods Duffey buys when provisioning his first St. Louis establishment, later Rounders’: a five-gallon jar, alongside pigs’ feet, spiced sausage, cheese, bread, potatoes and drink [MTM B2:41].","citations":["MTM B2:41"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Aquilae congregabunt dumque In nostram memoriam quotiescumque","definition":"Latin couplet announced as the company’s motto in the poem opening Stranahan’s chapter: “Aquilae congregabunt dumque / In nostram memoriam quotiescumque” [A:91]. It associates gathering eagles with remembrance. Henry’s dream ship bears the different, related inscription Ubicumque fuerit corpus illic congregabuntur et aquilas, which the narration calls an echo of the Dirty Five’s high motto [A:44]. The imagery recalls the Gospel’s gathering eagles, while the remembrance language suggests the Latin Eucharistic formula.","citations":["A:91","A:44"],"tags":["Language & Words","Scripture","Liturgy","Argonauts (Characters)","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Aquinas in a T Shirt","definition":"Finnegan’s caption for Hans Schultz among the Seven Pillars of Righteousness in his illustrated masthead for the <em>Bark</em> [A:121-122]. It combines Hans’s Thomistic identity with an ordinary T-shirt.","citations":["A:121-122"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Journalism","Identity"]},{"name":"Aquinas Redevivus","definition":"“Aquinas revived,” one of the learned identities assigned to Hans Schultz alongside Apollo and Dr. Faustus in the account of the Dirty Five’s earlier lives [A:39].","citations":["A:39"],"tags":["Identity","Saints","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Aquinas, Thomas","definition":"Dominican philosopher and theologian, born about 1225 and died 1274, author of the Summa Theologiae and Summa Contra Gentiles. His theology brings Aristotelian reasoning into conversation with Christian revelation; the Summa Theologiae includes the Five Ways concerning God’s existence and extensive treatments of ethics, law and sacraments. Canonized in 1323 and declared a Doctor of the Church in 1567, he has been repeatedly commended by the Church as a model of theological and philosophical inquiry. In the novels, the Answer incorporates material from Aquinas alongside Augustine and Pope Benedict [MTM B4:114]. John William Schultz urges that Aquinas and Albert be taught to fourth-graders [A:208-209]. The narrator’s notes on time quote Thomas’s distinction between eternity as an instantaneous whole and time as the measure of change [MTM B13:468-469]. The chapter epigraph attributed to Carmody Overlark calls him the angelic ox and imagines his massive back-brain [D:271-272]. Another epigraph attributes to his Summa Contra Gentiles a defense of divine knowledge of singulars [D:43].","citations":["MTM B4:114","A:208-209","MTM B13:468-469","D:271-272","D:43"],"tags":["Saints","Theology","Religious Orders","Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","Literature"]},{"name":"Arabic","definition":"Language Joseph says he learned while working as a caravan boy, alongside several others [D:94]. Finnegan has studied an O’Leary book of colloquial Arabic during the Atlantic crossing, but people in Tangier, especially Arabs, cannot recognize the language when he speaks it [D:104].","citations":["D:94","D:104"],"tags":["Language & Words","Finnegan","Africa"]},{"name":"Arabs","definition":"Among the people Finnegan encounters in Tangier. They fail to understand his book-learned Arabic, but a little Arab boy shares his finger-count game, pledges lifelong friendship and organizes runners to keep him informed [D:104].","citations":["D:104"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Finnegan","Africa","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Arachnia","definition":"Earlier heading misread the word arachnid. See <em>Arachnid</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Language & Words","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Arachnid","definition":"During his display of miscellaneous learning in Orlando’s Marine Room, Finnegan asks Doll how many legs an arachnid has [D:190].","citations":["D:190"],"tags":["Language & Words","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Arafura Sea","definition":"Sea between Australia and New Guinea, included with the Timor and Savu seas in the catalogue of waters sailed by <em>Argo</em> and remembered by Duffey [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Australia","Argonautica"]},{"name":"Aragh","definition":"One of the names the Malay boy gives his canoe, glossing it as “the direction,” alongside <em>Aral</em> and <em>Bumi</em>. These names lead Finnegan to recognize the canoe as <em>Argo</em> [A:89].","citations":["A:89"],"tags":["Ships","Argonautica","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Aral","definition":"Name the Malay boy gives his canoe, glossed as “hindrance.” He also names it <em>Bumi</em> and <em>Aragh</em>, and Finnegan recognizes the Ship <em>Argo</em> in it [A:89].","citations":["A:89"],"tags":["Ships","Argonautica","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Aransas Pass","definition":"One of <em>Brunhilde</em>’s nightly coastal ports, listed after Port O’Conner and before Corpus; the ship next anchors off Padre Island for a beach party and fish fry [D:34].","citations":["D:34"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Ships"]},{"name":"Ararat (Mt.)","definition":"Mountain in eastern Turkey traditionally associated with Noah’s Ark. A Cummings-clan Fundamentaler treats belief in the Ark’s survival, snowbound and guarded by angels in Armenia, as a test of faith. Zabotski accepts angelic guardians but questions the identification of the biblical country and mountain; Duffey thinks the wooden remains may be castles [MTM B9:280-281]. Zabotski later calls the structures on Ararat discarded models and his own flood-going contraption the original [MTM B9:287]. Askandanakandrian identifies Mt. Ararat in his “own Armenia” as the present secluded center of the other blood [A:258].","citations":["MTM B9:280-281","MTM B9:287","A:258"],"tags":["Old Testament","Angels & Demons","Ships","Middle East","Duffey","Family"]},{"name":"Arbuthnot","definition":"One of the aliases X lists for Gregory McColum Papadiabolous, the Irish Papadiabolous II. His other surnames include Noonan, Flaherty, Christy, Clarenden, Cobbett, Malory and Ingraham; both he and his Asiatic twin use variants of John with Gregory [D:285]. The name may allude to John Arbuthnot (1667-1735), the Scottish physician and literary friend of Jonathan Swift and Alexander Pope.","citations":["D:285"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Ireland","Family","Identity","Historical Figures","Literary Figures"]},{"name":"Arca","definition":"Spanish word for a chest. In Angela Cosquin’s account, her father marks his fabricated land-treasure maps with a baul or arca beneath crossed pickaxe and shovel. Brands such as T, K and BP suggest Teach, Kidd and Bartolomo Portuguese to credulous buyers [D:259-260].","citations":["D:259-260"],"tags":["Crime","Visual Arts","Money & Trade","Language & Words","Family"]},{"name":"Arcades","definition":"Passageways among the shops and bars on the street floor of Sydney’s Plaza, where Finnegan and his companions gather [A:3]. Arcades are also among the amenities of the newly appearing Bayougoula Park in New Orleans [MTM B11:331-332].","citations":["A:3","MTM B11:331-332"],"tags":["Australia","New Orleans","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Arcadia","definition":"Greek region idealized in pastoral literature. Don Barnaby says Madame Le Mourcheor uses honey from bees in the meadows around Megapolis in “Arcady” for her Juju Joy Pills [D:248-249].","citations":["D:248-249"],"tags":["Greece","Folklore","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Arcadian motif","definition":"An old pattern of pastoral symbolism Duffey thinks he recognizes in the landscape of the Presence. Patrick Plunket corrects his habit of treating it as contrived art: it is real, and their ascent is of the invisible, interior mountain [MTM B12:390].","citations":["MTM B12:390"],"tags":["Duffey","Mysticism","Metaphysics","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Archangel Statues","definition":"Three spread-winged archangels on the south front of St. Malachy’s Church in Chicago. Duffey sees the stone as quivering with wings; each side of the church also bears nine large-winged angels [MTM B3:87].","citations":["MTM B3:87"],"tags":["Angels & Demons","Visual Arts","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Archbishop","definition":"Church official to whom Bascom Bagby reports supposed heresies in New Orleans parishes during a visit [MTM B7:227]. Henry Salvatore visits a bishop and an archbishop while arranging entry into the seminary in Louisiana [A:124-125].","citations":["MTM B7:227","A:124-125"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Louisiana","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Archipelago","definition":"Scattered homeland of the Animated Marvels, also called the Greater Archipelago, with much of its population in Chicago, St. Louis and New Orleans. It supplies <em>Argo</em> with provisions, healing and virtues; many of its people are <em>Argo</em> Masters, though their relations across time and place remain incompletely understood [MTM B13:448]. The poem opening Stranahan’s chapter describes the Fivers themselves as islands in a weird Archipelago, discovering valleys and skerries in sky-lands [A:91]. Finnegan tells creation stories of island groups formed from a broken electrum cup, a shattered bronze bowl and a mat torn by sea turtles; Hans observes that archipelago originally meant a great sea rather than its islands [A:35-36].","citations":["MTM B13:448","A:91","A:35-36"],"tags":["Islands","Argonautica","Argonauts (Characters)","Folklore","Seas & Oceans","Language & Words","Creation","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Archipelago (The Greater)","definition":"Another name for the Land of the Animated Marvels, the scattered recuperative island whose people chiefly live in Chicago, St. Louis and New Orleans and provide for <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:448]. Finnegan also calls Francis Xavier the Apostle to the greater Archipelagos, imagining ocean islands as constellations that prefigure the skies [A:35].","citations":["MTM B13:448","A:35"],"tags":["Islands","Argonautica","Saints","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Archipelagoes","definition":"Island groups whose origins Finnegan describes through creation legends: an electrum cup broken into the Aegean islands, a bronze bowl shattered into the long islands off Malaya, and a woven mat torn by sea turtles into the green islands, with Guinea retaining its stitched center. He says each began as a five-island cluster or pentanesia, and calls Xavier the Apostle to the greater Archipelagos [A:35].","citations":["A:35"],"tags":["Folklore","Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Creation","Saints"]},{"name":"Architects of all the new realms","definition":"X’s description of the influential notables who gather in Crimea to adore the released Devil and receive their status in the new order. His account includes clergy, political advisers, intellectuals and artists [MTM B6:204-205]. The narrative dates the release to the last Saturday of May 1946, while also questioning whether such releases recur [MTM B5:184].","citations":["MTM B6:204-205","MTM B5:184"],"tags":["The Devil","Cosmic Struggle","Social Class","Eastern Europe"]},{"name":"Archive, Leonard","definition":"One of three hospitable men living by a Louisiana swamp landing, with Oliver Greenflag and Harry A. “Honeybucket” Kincaid. He welcomes Duffey and Brannagan and promises anything the travelers need [MTM B13:416-417]. Archive explains that varying voltages, frequencies and electrodes lets them collect fish, animals, plants, chemicals, minerals, salts, alcohols, petroleums and sugars from water. Selective polarization and differences of potential provide power, and he says they have given these techniques to the world [MTM B13:418]. He claims frequency modulation and broadcasting can also draw feelings, weather, notions, ideas, emotions and people. After Honeybucket summons three young women, Archive asks them whether they want anything beyond the pleasure of the men’s company [MTM B13:419].","citations":["MTM B13:416-417","MTM B13:418","MTM B13:419"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Louisiana","Fishing","Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Archivist","definition":"Army-history occupation in which Private Gregory says he spent years after admission to the U.S. Military Academy and his eventual commission. He sought obscurity but became a leading expert and teacher, drawing on his centuries of personal experience [A:77-78].","citations":["A:77-78"],"tags":["Soldiers","Occupations","Immortality","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Ardor","definition":"Passion, especially sexual passion, in Hillary Hilton’s joke that cold snow acting on Casey’s ardor produced a lasting coolness toward him. Hilton tells Finnegan that he caught Casey with Mary Jean, dragged him naked from bed into the snowy road, and left him there [A:251-252].","citations":["A:251-252"],"tags":["Language & Words","Family","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Arena del Mar","definition":"One of the nine projected volumes into which Endymeon Ellenbogen says he has classified his poetry. The poems are uncollected and unpublished, though he recites them readily [A:237].","citations":["A:237"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Argo","definition":"The ancient Quest Ship around which the company’s overlapping lives and legends gather. The Dirty Five are identified as Iason/Finnegan, Orpheus/Hans, Meleager/Vincent, Peleus/Casey and Euphemus/Henry [A:38-39]. Teresa’s later account adds Hans’s Faust identity, Marie/Eurydice, Dotty/Medea, Mary Virginia/Laonome, Teresa/Atalanta, Mary Catherine/Antigone and Stein/Ab-Salom. She calls their voyage older than Greek myth, invokes Homer as witness to their antiquity, and says their quest was the prototype of all quests; they sailed to Colchis and still possess the Golden Fleece [MTM B7:233-234]. Casey calls the Five’s transport <em>Argo</em> “for this night at least” near the Talaud Islands [A:63], and the poem opening Stranahan’s chapter recalls sailing the ship anciently called <em>Argo</em> [A:91].\n\nIts identities include the <em>Navicula Petri</em> or <em>Peter Ship</em>, which Henry dreams as a galleon-like vessel of pennants, saints and shattered masts, then recognizes as the <em>Argo</em> whose quest has been sanctified [A:44-46]. Henry’s dream supplies the name and Finnegan’s storm-battered masthead for the magazine Bark, also spelled Barque [A:121] [A:128]; Hans likewise calls the remembered ship the <em>Bark</em> [MTM B5:178]. The talking oak in its wheel recounts appearances as the <em>Anthony Ship</em> at Actium, the flagship of Abd-Aliah of the Sea and then Sindbad’s ship; as St. Brandon’s <em>Land of Behest</em>, the <em>Salle du Roi</em> of Robert of Namur, Willy Jones’s <em>Flying Serpent</em> and Dana Coscuin’s <em>Catherine</em>. It has carried St. Paul, Godfrey of the Gate, Mark Twain and Judas, and has repeatedly come into infidel ownership [MTM B13:404-405]. As <em>Brunhilde</em> it belongs to Saxon Seaworthy and carries Finnegan among dangerous associates [D:21] [D:30]; Seaworthy is identified with Sindbad when commissioning the lounge paintings [D:59]. X calls Finnegan’s disputed ship <em>Delay</em>, <em>World</em> or <em>Argo</em>, while the narrator questions both its registration and the reliability of the voyage’s accounts, including Seaworthy’s threat to have Finnegan killed [A:187].\n\n<em>Argo</em> is a Hope Ship and, in its primary sense, a show boat. It goes where other ships cannot, carries dangerous passengers and cargo, opens dark lands and ports, breaks conspiracies, and brings joy, grace and entertainment [MTM B13:400] [MTM B13:405]. It is above all a Quest Ship: the Golden Fleece is an early triumph, and its later recoveries include the Holy Cross. The Sword and Scabbard of Saint Sécaire are an anti-prize that its Masters have repeatedly tried to lose; the most recent discovery is made by the Dimbeau brothers against the Masters’ wishes [MTM B13:421-422]. In another account it delivers the Peter-Bell to the mooring rock north of San Simeon [MB:577-578]. Duffey and Brannagan also root out future evils before they can flow into the present; their most complete successes erase the evils so thoroughly that the achievements cannot be remembered [MTM B13:416] [MTM B13:420-421].\n\nIt knows seas, islands, shores and promontories, including migrating islands and seas. An old log even claims a voyage on the moon’s Mare Nectaris [MTM B13:405-406]. The Sea of the Seven Lost Years has scattered shores and channels amid other waters; Duffey’s years on it are not consecutive [MTM B13:406]. The ship also sails outside the cosmos and within minds and persons [MTM B13:437]. It ordinarily moves smoothly and rapidly against wind and waves [MTM B13:402]. A voyage is a cycle or dynasty of adventures, interrupted by hiatus, mutation or a death of the ship, not simply one trip between ports. <em>Argo Twelve</em> is already active when Duffey names his balloon, and the later adventure cycle is called the climactic Fourteenth Voyage [MTM B2:64] [MTM B13:410-412]. Duffey describes its log as a dating clock covering nine thousand past years and sixty-seven future years, with some later futures recorded in a different, elusive ledger. The talking oak can announce an artifact’s date, though it sometimes gives geographical bearings instead [MTM B13:411].\n\n<em>Argo</em>’s dimensions and fittings vary: she has as many masts as a voyage needs, sometimes funnels and engines, innumerable decks and unfamiliar rooms, animal stalls, unknown families, shops and cottage industries, yet can fit a small boat slip. Some suppose the present vessel is only the boat of a greater Ship. It retains the talking-oak wheel, Noah’s broken molar in a plank, the Special Dove’s cote, grist mills, log books and a lantern that shines around headlands and corners [MTM B13:403-404]. Duffey recounts rediscovering her as an old hull in New Orleans, built at Ragusa of Dinaric oak with unusual spring and able to regain her fittings by remembering them; the account is expressly called symbolically rather than ordinarily accurate [MTM B13:412]. She can be frozen solid in blue ice and released for sailing [MTM B13:398-402]. A later account shows constant emergency repairs and poor ordinary seaworthiness, while declaring that she cannot sink forever [MB:590].\n\nThe Masters form an interdependent society, including Duffey, Brannagan, Casey, Finnegan, Henry and others of the Animated Marvels [MTM B13:446]. Melchisedech is an extraordinary pilot [MTM B13:402]; X is admitted as a servitor with half shares, rather than a full Master [MTM B13:434-435]. The mechanical, ghostlike seamen are materialized residues or place tokens of absent travelers; arriving Masters don their own effigies. Effigies of respected companions also exist, and Casey mars those of Finnegan and two companions. The ship carries more coffins than there are Masters [MTM B13:415-416] [MTM B13:449-450]. After Duffey’s killing, his bones are laid in one; God comes aboard and tells him that his duties do not require flesh. Gorshok supplies the golden mask, scarf and gauntlets with which Duffey resumes sailing [MTM B13:443-444]. Other passengers include the rescued Neo-Neanderthal Eva and the returning Monsignor X, who brings the flayed skin attributed to Cardinal Artemis [MTM B13:400-407] [MTM B13:451-452]. Coryphaena fish play a salute when a present or future Pope rides aboard [MTM B13:450-451].\n\nThe Sindbad Lounge contains Finnegan’s oceanic paintings, made in his youth when the ship was <em>Brunhilde</em> [MTM B13:404]; their commission and prophetic grave scenes are recounted in the earlier cruise [D:59-61] [D:85-86]. The Bread and Wine Room hosts meaningful gatherings and later bears his thirteen Deaths of the Cardinals paintings directly on its bulkheads [MTM B13:403] [MTM B13:452]. The chart room preserves moral instruction even during evil ownership [MTM B13:437]. The scattered Land of the Animated Marvels, or Greater Archipelago, is a recuperative island for <em>Argo</em>, supplying water, provisions, healing and virtues, although its relation to the ship across time and place remains unresolved [MTM B13:448]. Teresa identifies the birth talismans as pieces of <em>Argo</em>’s gold filigree over talking oak, their gold from the Fleece and their function anamnesis or recollection; Hans describes the people as stranded on a mundane shore awaiting their return [MTM B5:178-179].\n\nFinnegan foretells that <em>Argo</em> will carry Antichrist to Megiddo [MTM B13:453]. Duffey, Brannagan and Gorshok nevertheless try to prevent the mysterious passenger’s entry, seeking detectives’ advice and installing alarms, firedrakes and Gunboat Smith as guards [MTM B13:454-458]. An entourage, altered sea, magnetic wind and growing disturbance surround the ship; the winds now carry her instead of opposing her [MTM B13:459-460]. Her mirrors shatter into cubistic reflections, and the Man of Mystery emerges from below decks as Peleus, Gorshok, Prince Casimir, Casey and Antichristus. <em>Argo</em> lands him at Habonim by the Plains of Megiddo [MTM B13:461-462]. These events are increasingly marked as a probable but unsettled future. The ship disappears while Duffey, Brannagan and Gunboat fade out of that world; rediscovering her becomes their next task, and the whole coming of Antichrist subsequently recedes with the scene [MTM B13:451] [MTM B13:460] [MTM B13:463] [MTM B13:468].\n\nThe <em>Brunhilde</em> itinerary includes Freeport, Palacios, Port O’Conner, Aransas Pass and Corpus, then an anchorage off Padre Island [D:34]; Port Isabel and Brownsville [D:55]; Mexican coastal anchorages, with one episode uncertainly placed at Paraiso or Campeche [D:59] [D:67]; Campeche, Progreso, Cozumel, Ciudad Chetumal, Belize, Puerto Barrios, La Ceiba, Trujillo, Cabo Gracias a Dios, Blue Fields, Limon and San Cristobal [D:81]; Portobello, Manana, Cartagena, Santa Maria, Willemsted, Caracas, Cumana and Port of Spain [D:85]; Cayenne, Belem, Sao Luis, Camocim, Fortaleza and Natal [D:86]; Freetown [D:89]; Bissau, Bathurst, Dakar, St. Louis, Memrhar, Port Etienne, Villa Cisneros, Las Palmas and Grand Canary [D:96]; Arrecife on Lanzarote, Ifni, Agadir, Magadar, Safti, Mazagan, Casablanca, Rabat and Tangier [D:101]; Palermo, Messina, Zakinthos and Naxos [D:114-115]. <em>Argo</em>’s later or differently ordered journeys include Biloxi [MTM B13:408] and Habonim [MTM B13:462].","citations":["A:38-39","MTM B7:233-234","A:63","A:91","A:44-46","A:121","A:128","MTM B5:178","MTM B13:404-405","D:21","D:30","D:59","A:187","MTM B13:400","MTM B13:405","MTM B13:421-422","MB:577-578","MTM B13:416","MTM B13:420-421","MTM B13:405-406","MTM B13:406","MTM B13:437","MTM B13:402","MTM B2:64","MTM B13:410-412","MTM B13:411","MTM B13:403-404","MTM B13:412","MTM B13:398-402","MB:590","MTM B13:446","MTM B13:434-435","MTM B13:415-416","MTM B13:449-450","MTM B13:443-444","MTM B13:400-407","MTM B13:451-452","MTM B13:450-451","MTM B13:404","D:59-61","D:85-86","MTM B13:403","MTM B13:452","MTM B13:448","MTM B5:178-179","MTM B13:453","MTM B13:454-458","MTM B13:459-460","MTM B13:461-462","MTM B13:451","MTM B13:460","MTM B13:463","MTM B13:468","D:34","D:55","D:67","D:81","D:85","D:86","D:89","D:96","D:101","D:114-115","MTM B13:408","MTM B13:462"],"tags":["Argonautica","Greek Mythology","Ships","Sailing","Crew & Sailors","Ports & Harbors","Seas & Oceans","Islands","Duffey","Finnegan","Argonauts (Characters)","Ghosts & Spirits","Prophecy & Visions","Apocalyptic","Cosmic Struggle","Time & Eternity","History & Memory","Identity","Creation","Resurrection","Catholic Church","Saints","Popes","Old Testament","New Testament","Magic & Sorcery","Visual Arts","Bars & Taverns","Greece","Africa","Louisiana"]},{"name":"Argo Company (Teresa's account)","definition":"Teresa Piccone Stranahan’s account of the old company awakened by Duffey. She says he assembled and awakened them rather than created them, causing them to be born again to oppose the newly loosed Devil. She explicitly denies that they were the first crew, pilots or voyagers, although they sailed on the first ship, the <em>Argo</em>, identified with the Ark, and added its sails and rudder [MTM B7:233-234].\n\nHer identifications are Finnegan as Iason, later Jason; Hans as Orpheus and later Faust; Henry as Euphemus; Vincent as Meleager; Casey as Peleus; Dotty as Medea; Marie as Eurydice; Mary Virginia as Laonome; Teresa herself as Atalanta; and Mary Catherine as Antigone. Duffey is the original Melchisedech, but his Argonaut name was something else. Stein is Ab-Salom, Father of Peace, a Euxine ship’s chandler who joined to protect his investments. Teresa says Henry learned to walk on water from his ocean-father, rejects Dotty’s evil reputation, and substitutes Marie’s journey to Australia and motherhood of its peoples for Eurydice’s descent to the underworld. Gabrielovitch, Zabotski and Salvation Sally may belong to an older company; Teresa does not know about X, and knows Margaret’s company but refuses to disclose it [MTM B7:234].","citations":["MTM B7:233-234","MTM B7:234"],"tags":["Argonauts (Characters)","Argonautica","Creation","Identity","Duffey","The Devil","Resurrection"]},{"name":"Argo Twelve","definition":"Duffey’s first name for his custom-made, hydrogen-filled silk-bag balloon during his early St. Louis years, the period beginning in 1915 [MTM B2:38] [MTM B2:63-64]. He takes friends including Bagby, Mary Louise, Charley Murray and Beth Keegan on ascents. A powerful misty visitor tells him <em>Argo Twelve</em> is already active, so he renames the balloon <em>Argo Twelve and Half</em> [MTM B2:64].","citations":["MTM B2:38","MTM B2:63-64","MTM B2:64"],"tags":["Duffey","Ships","Clubs & Societies","Identity"]},{"name":"Argo Twelve and Half","definition":"Duffey’s revised name for his balloon after a misty person of great power tells him the actual <em>Argo Twelve</em> is currently active and its name unavailable. The visitor permits the balloon sometimes to serve as a pinnace or ship’s boat for <em>Argo</em>’s Twelfth Voyage [MTM B2:64]. The same page also calls it <em>Argo Twelve and a Half</em>.","citations":["MTM B2:64"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Duffey","Identity"]},{"name":"Argonauts","definition":"The people of <em>Argo</em>, including its interdependent society of Masters such as Duffey, Brannagan and Casey [MTM B13:446] [MTM B13:454]. The narrator portrays them as commando experts against evil, able to distinguish right from wrong apart from a short interlude at Wien, with guidance preserved in the chart room even during evil ownership of the ship [MTM B13:437]. In his Notes on the Finnegan Cycle, Absalom Stein says that everyone must first have existed in legend before living in life [MB:549-550].","citations":["MTM B13:446","MTM B13:454","MTM B13:437","MB:549-550"],"tags":["Argonautica","Crew & Sailors","Argonauts (Characters)","Duffey","Cosmic Struggle","Folklore"]},{"name":"Ariadne","definition":"Daughter of King Minos of Crete in Greek myth, associated with her abandonment by Theseus on Naxos. The novel calls Naxos the home of Ariadne and white wine, as well as the home of Anastasia Demetriades [D:115].","citations":["D:115"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Greece","Islands","Women Characters","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Arioch","definition":"One of the Three Slant-Faced Men whose names Duffey finds in <em>Flame Man</em>, with Amraphel and Thadal. The narration identifies him as king of Ellasar, Pontus or Lower Babylonia and describes the three as crooked kings or anti-magi who have no magic [MTM B7:227].","citations":["MTM B7:227"],"tags":["Old Testament","Middle East","Duffey","Minor Characters","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Aristotle (384--322 BC)","definition":"Greek philosopher (384-322 BCE), student of Plato, tutor of Alexander and founder of the Lyceum in Athens. His works range across logic, ethics, politics, biology and metaphysics; his systematic treatment of inference helped establish logic as a discipline. The Nicomachean Ethics and Politics examine human flourishing and political life. Known to medieval scholars as the Philosopher, he profoundly influenced later philosophy and Christian theology. Casey’s school notebook mocks an attributed statement about lynx urine and signets [A:162] [A:165]. The narrator of <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> treats the Unities of Aristotle, alongside Plutarch’s Heroics, as conventions from which Finnegan’s shadow life is freed [D:161].","citations":["A:162","A:165","D:161"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Literature","Metaphysics","Theology","Greece"]},{"name":"Ark (Noah's)","definition":"Noah’s biblical vessel. In Casey’s school notebook, Hinnegan claims the Milesians were in Ireland before the Flood; Casey retorts that knowledge of this would require Noah to have taken two Irishmen aboard the Ark [A:162] [A:164-165]. Fundamentalers ask Zabotski and Duffey whether the Ark survives in Armenia under angelic guard. Their debate distinguishes belief in the Ark from the disputed identification of Ararat and from Duffey’s suggestion that the wooden remains were castles [MTM B9:280-281].","citations":["A:162","A:164-165","MTM B9:280-281"],"tags":["Old Testament","Ships","Ireland","Duffey","Folklore"]},{"name":"Ark-advocate","definition":"Participant in the Fundamentalers’ discussion who insists that one wooden structure in Ararat’s snow is the true Ark. He says people have entered it and brought objects back as proof [MTM B9:283].","citations":["MTM B9:283"],"tags":["Old Testament","Ships","Minor Characters","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Armageddon (Plains of Megiddo)","definition":"In Finnegan’s prophecy, the destination to which <em>Argo</em> will carry the Antichrist for the last battle [MTM B13:453]. The culminating voyage reaches the seaport of Habonim, said to serve the Plains of Megiddo; the emerging Antichrist recognizes Casey as Prince Casimir [MTM B13:462]. These events belong to a shimmering possible future, and that future recedes again [MTM B13:451] [MTM B13:463] [MTM B13:468].","citations":["MTM B13:453","MTM B13:462","MTM B13:451","MTM B13:463","MTM B13:468"],"tags":["Apocalyptic","Prophecy & Visions","Ships","Combat"]},{"name":"Armenia","definition":"Country and older regional name invoked in the debate about the Ark. A Fundamentaler locates the Ark in Armenia’s high mountains, while Zabotski questions whether the present place names identify the biblical country and mountain correctly [MTM B9:280-281]. Askandan calls Mount Ararat in his “own Armenia” the secluded center of the other blood [A:258]. These are the speakers’ identifications; Mount Ararat lies in modern Turkey, not in the present Republic of Armenia.","citations":["MTM B9:280-281","A:258"],"tags":["Old Testament","Family","History & Memory","Middle East"]},{"name":"Armenian","definition":"Ethnic designation applied to Askandanakandrian, or Asking Dan, the ancient comic dealer in distressed or “nervous” merchandise, associated with Chicago [A:256-258]. Margaret Stone is described as his niece and as embodying many ethnic lineages at once [MTM B7:212].","citations":["A:256-258","MTM B7:212"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Armstrong's","definition":"Name on cheese presses in the catalogue of household inscriptions remembered and appropriated by Melchisedech Duffey. Many of these “iron words” are stolen from other people’s minds; the surrounding memories range across Iowa, St. Louis and Boston [MTM B1:17].","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory","Identity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Arracht","definition":"Irish word meaning a spectre or monster. In the account of the Dirty Five’s earlier lives, Finnegan is called Dionysus or Ulysses but also a Terras or Arracht [A:39].","citations":["A:39"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Ireland","Finnegan","Identity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Arrecife","definition":"Port city on Lanzarote in the Canary Islands. <em>Brunhilde</em> calls there after Grand Canary and before Ifni, on the African coast [D:96] [D:101].","citations":["D:96","D:101"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Ports & Harbors","Islands","Africa"]},{"name":"Arson","definition":"Casey jokingly offers arson and mayhem as pleasanter topics than Absalom Stein [A:67]. Henry’s subsequent “No arson, no mayhem” praises Sammy’s successful confidence trick after the boy obtains money and whisky; it is a comment on that performance, not a general account of the Dirty Five’s conduct [A:68].","citations":["A:67","A:68"],"tags":["Crime"]},{"name":"Art Bijou (Duffey's Walk-In)","definition":"Duffey’s New Orleans art business, established in the Pelican Press building after his move south in May 1946. The building also contains his pawn shop and the press’s common workroom [MTM B5:184] [MTM B6:190]. The related establishment includes a second-hand book and antique business; the press premises initially have cots and a cook stove for living quarters [A:116] [A:143]. Letitia’s affectionate catalogue joins the Art Bijou, bookstore, pawn shop, press, institute and soup kitchen [MTM B7:211]. Duffey conceives the Bijou as a flexible fortress defending the arts, while his creative stasis allows him to move back and forth through the years [MTM B6:199].\n\nThe painting <em>The Resurrection of Count Finnegan</em> arrives around the middle of the 1950s [MB:550]. Stein views it [MB:551]; Hilary Hilton buys it for twenty thousand dollars but leaves it hanging in the Bijou, visiting it at least annually [MB:553]. It remains on the walls for at least thirty years [MB:554]. Duffey also contemplates his own ashes on a table in the Bijou [MTM B1:6].","citations":["MTM B5:184","MTM B6:190","A:116","A:143","MTM B7:211","MTM B6:199","MB:550","MB:551","MB:553","MB:554","MTM B1:6"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Finnegan","Visual Arts","Literature","Resurrection","Money & Trade","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Art Dealer","definition":"Profession represented by buyers seeking Finnegan’s pictures [A:182]. A reputable dealer gives Finnegan van Ghi a standing commission for as many pictures as he can paint [A:191]. Duffey sees art dealing, together with pawnbroking, as his own vocation and a means of rebuilding the world [MTM B6:199].","citations":["A:182","A:191","MTM B6:199"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Occupations","Visual Arts","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Art-and-Elegance Shop","definition":"Lily Koch’s Chicago shop, where Duffey encounters Lily and her sister Letitia. Lily identifies Letitia as her partner in the establishment [MTM B3:90-91].","citations":["MTM B3:90-91"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Artaguette Park","definition":"Park beside Sarkis Popotov’s residence in the changed New Orleans landscape. Margaret reports that Sarkis had lived beside buildings for forty years but can no longer remember what they were; now Artaguette Park occupies the neighboring space. The others tell him it will soon seem familiar [MTM B11:309].","citations":["MTM B11:309"],"tags":["New Orleans","Fictional Places","History & Memory","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Artemis (Diana)","definition":"Greek goddess of the hunt, identified with the Roman Diana, whose imagery also associates her with the moon. The chapter “DIANA ARTEMIS” places Finnegan and Anastasia on Naxos [D:130]. By moonlight, Anastasia’s head resembles Diana’s [D:132]. The narrator specifies the light-bringing Artemis, comparing her head with a painted-marble image at Messene in Asclepius’s sanctuary, said to have been seen by Pausanias, and with a little cypress-wood Artemis on a temple step at Delos [D:133].","citations":["D:130","D:132","D:133"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Islands","Greece","Women Characters","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Artemis, Carlos Cardinal","definition":"Carlos Cardinal Artemis of Santa Cruz, one of the thirteen Cardinals pursued by John Mogul’s Track and Total. His designated double is Gilberto Levine y O’Brien of Rio [MB:579]. After Cardinal Ti’s death, Mogul’s next predicted killing is Artemis’s flaying [MB:583]. The account describes an arranged accident in Portugal: a cork-cutting machine, which the Cardinal has come to bless, strips off his skin; he dies shortly afterward [MB:591].\n\nThe identity of the victim and surviving double remains uncertain. Gilberto has copied Artemis’s natural skin markings with tattoos, adding personal marks. Monsignor X shows Duffey a purportedly cardinal’s skin aboard <em>Argo</em>, but neither can determine whose skin it is [MTM B13:451-452]. Separately, Finnegan has radiation-imprinted a masterwork on the inside of Artemis’s skin while he was alive and unaware. The account asks whether this picture is now inside the trophy skin cherished by Mogul or inside the living Gilberto’s skin [MB:591].","citations":["MB:579","MB:583","MB:591","MTM B13:451-452"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Crime","Finnegan","Duffey","Ships","Visual Arts","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Arthur (King)","definition":"Legendary British king. X includes Arthur among figures such as Barbarossa, Alaric, Brian Boru and the Cid who await awakening or release from an enchanted trance-sleep [MTM B6:204-205].","citations":["MTM B6:204-205"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Folklore","Time & Eternity","Dreams"]},{"name":"Artillery","definition":"In Casey’s ninth proposed world, a workers’ revolution uses artillery in streets and parks to overthrow skyscrapers and factory smokestacks [A:160].","citations":["A:160"],"tags":["Weapons","Dreams","Combat"]},{"name":"Artist","definition":"Finnegan is explicitly identified as a painter [D:37], and paints the voyages of Sindbad on <em>Brunhilde</em>’s barroom panels [D:59] [D:62]. Demetrio, the cantina proprietor, is himself an artist who makes Finnegan a belt and gives each of the two girls a silver ring [D:59]. Angela Cosquin’s father is praised as an exquisite artist and meticulous mapmaker [D:259]. X has also worked as a stage magician and Escape Artist under the name Great X-Capo [D:276].","citations":["D:37","D:59","D:62","D:259","D:276"],"tags":["Finnegan","Visual Arts","Theater","Occupations","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Artist's tie","definition":"Tie, accompanied by a beret, that Duffey wears “for the trade” after opening a second-hand bookstore, art and antique shop, and hock-shop in New Orleans [A:143].","citations":["A:143"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Visual Arts","Money & Trade","Occupations"]},{"name":"Artistic Degradation","definition":"One of the seven heads of the modern enemy named by Bascom Bagby [MTM B5:169].","citations":["MTM B5:169"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Arutinov, Arpad","definition":"Name credited as the author of <em>The Back-Door of History</em> in the epigraph to the second chapter of <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>. The passage attacks abstraction and describes the intermingling of human and inhuman blood [D:26].","citations":["D:26"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Ascend into the city","definition":"Duffey’s command to Finnegan after joining him in the burial cave: “Ascend into the city. There are doings that only you can do.” Their surrounding exchange combines lament, biblical prayer and calls to rise and act [MTM B12:374-375].","citations":["MTM B12:374-375"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan","Resurrection","Prayer & Devotion"]},{"name":"Ascendere","definition":"Italian verb meaning to ascend or go up, sung by Finnegan in the fragment beginning “Vecchia zimarra senti” [A:31]. The song is Colline’s farewell to his coat in Puccini’s <em>La Bohème</em>. Finnegan sings during the Australian Bay Hotel picnic: because the hotel forbids a lawn bonfire, the company gathers around a plate of burning charcoal [A:29-31].","citations":["A:31","A:29-31"],"tags":["Australia","Italy","Finnegan","Music","Theater","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Asclepius","definition":"Greek god of medicine and healing. The narrator compares Anastasia’s head with an image of Diana Artemis said to have been seen by Pausanias in Asclepius’s sanctuary at Messene: a little statue of painted marble with a natural expression [D:133].","citations":["D:133"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Greece","Women Characters","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Ashes","definition":"Duffey’s ashes exist alongside his surviving bones. After the slant-faced killers strip his flesh, the slack-faced men burn the flesh and viscera and place the ashes in a cigar canister formerly owned by a King of Spain. X takes custody, intending to return them to a living Duffey [MTM B13:442]. X shows them to Duffey in another encounter [MTM B6:203], and they stand on his table in the New Orleans Art Bijou [MTM B1:6]. They flame when Teresa touches them [MTM B7:233]. Their coexistence with Duffey alive exemplifies his disrupted sequence of death and life [MTM B13:445].\n\nFresh human ashes are also used for the test at the Decatur Street Opera House. They burn human hands and distinguish the participants by the resulting flames; these ashes are not identified as Duffey’s [MTM B11:347-348].","citations":["MTM B13:442","MTM B6:203","MTM B1:6","MTM B7:233","MTM B13:445","MTM B11:347-348"],"tags":["New Orleans","Spain","Duffey","Death & Mortality","Resurrection","Theater","Paradox"]},{"name":"Asia","definition":"The continental setting invoked when X calls Smyrna-born Papadiabolous I Asiatic [D:285]. Smyrna is modern İzmir on the Anatolian peninsula in Turkey. In Seaworthy’s dream of an Indian city, a wheel repeatedly falls from a cart carrying corpses; he calls this a defect of small Asian carts [D:72]. Duffey’s familiarity with Asian deserts helps him recognize that the Laughing Prince’s amoral desert is artificial [MTM B13:439].","citations":["D:285","D:72","MTM B13:439"],"tags":["Middle East","Devils (Characters)","Dreams","Duffey"]},{"name":"Asiatic","definition":"X’s description of Papadiabolous I, Gregory McIfreann, who was born in Smyrna. His twin, Papadiabolous II, was born in Cork and is called an Irishman. The distinction in this account turns on their different birthplaces, not an independently established difference in ancestry [D:285].","citations":["D:285"],"tags":["Ireland","Middle East","Devils (Characters)","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Asimov, Isaac","definition":"Science-fiction writer and biochemist (1920-1992). The first chapter’s epigraph is credited to Asimov and imagines a groomed, well-dressed Neanderthal passing unnoticed on Fifth Avenue [D:9]. It introduces the question of ancient identities beneath ordinary appearances. During Finnegan’s transport about the other blood, Asimov is named with Mendel, Morgan, Galton and Painter in a rhetorical challenge about primordial ancestors reappearing after a thousand generations [D:214].","citations":["D:9","D:214"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","History & Memory","Identity","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Askandanakandrian","definition":"Askandan, or Asking Dan, the ancient comic Armenian dealer in distressed merchandise in Chicago and uncle of Margaret Stone [MTM B7:212]. He joins Finnegan and Hilary Hilton at dinner, bargaining over green fish sticks and unusable soap base. His habitual “I was just asking,” together with his name, explains the nickname [A:256]. He describes himself as a specialist in rehabilitating nervous merchandise and weevily grain, then tells Finnegan that a man he has crossed will have him followed and murdered [A:257]. After Finnegan names that man, Askandan says he knows him and knows about the other blood, whose secluded center he locates at Mount Ararat. He affirms that Finnegan is a Teras and has a soul. The narrator calls him a reader of souls and master of dialects and roles [A:258].\n\nHe joins Finnegan’s plot against Casey. When Casey finds himself naked on the road, Asking Dan offers a zoot suit, pointed shoes and an enormous hat, finally selling them on a promise of payment the next morning. Finnegan has bought the old suit for nine dollars to supply the stunt [A:258-261].","citations":["MTM B7:212","A:256","A:257","A:258","A:258-261"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Finnegan","Crime","Money & Trade","Family","Identity","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Asking Dan","definition":"Nickname of Askandanakandrian, the little old dealer in distressed merchandise. It comes from his habitual “I was just asking” and the sound of his name [A:256]. He supplies the naked Casey with a zoot suit, pointed shoes and a large hat on credit [A:259-261]. See Askandanakandrian.","citations":["A:256","A:259-261"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Aspects (1)","definition":"In the passage attributed to Bascom Bagby’s Letters After I Am Dead, beings or manifestations inhabiting Iron Meadows. Bagby says that some aspects of the Argo Legend occupy one such meadow, unknown to either Duffey or God [MTM B5:177].","citations":["MTM B5:177"],"tags":["Argonautica","Metaphysics","Paradox"]},{"name":"Aspects (2)","definition":"Alternative persons or manifestations of a self. Doctor Saul Rafelson’s notes propose that Duffey’s other selves may be projections, captured independent people, or living images split off from him. Rafelson speculates that Duffey’s twelve independent creations express twelve aspects of him and explicitly distinguishes them from his shadows [MTM B4:146-147]. On Naxos, after Finnegan confesses his fear of the animal or other kindred within him, the Capuchin absolves his present person “in all its aspects” [D:119-120].","citations":["MTM B4:146-147","D:119-120"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Finnegan","Duffey","Creation","Identity","Sacraments"]},{"name":"Aspirins","definition":"Tablets mentioned in Dotty’s letter complaining to Marie about Salvation Sally’s arrival in New Orleans. Dotty says she has had the flu and has been taking aspirins; Belonki now wants her to try his joy pills [A:147].","citations":["A:147"],"tags":["New Orleans","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Ass","definition":"Donkey, in Joe’s comparison of Dopey’s second scream to that of an ass seized by a lion. Joe is recounting Dopey’s disappearance after Papa Devil entered the cabin [D:64].","citations":["D:64"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Crime","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Assassins of Jebel Shammar","definition":"Self-described sect whose members, according to Margaret Stone, threaten to kill her for preaching the Real Presence at night. They brandish knives and claim that their jealous Djinn is the only nocturnal Real Presence. Margaret answers with sweet talk and a claim that New Orleans is a truce city. She doubts whether such a sect exists: the three local Arabs attend Tulane and may merely be teasing her. She fears the Slant-Faced Men much more [MTM B7:225].","citations":["MTM B7:225"],"tags":["Theology","New Orleans","Crime","Women Characters","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Assignment","definition":"The leftfooted man’s mission to kill Finnegan. In Florida, Finnegan tells Doll that this pursuer does not belong to the latest trouble or the people X has been discussing: he is on “an earlier assignment” [D:182-183].","citations":["D:182-183"],"tags":["Crime","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Astute Florentine father","definition":"Allusion to Niccolò Machiavelli. Niccolo Crotolus says he takes his first name from this Florentine “father,” whom he resembles in mind, and invokes the discussion of a prince’s faithfulness. He applies the distinction between appearing faithful and actually obeying to his employer’s contradictory instructions about a killing [A:279]. The passage echoes chapter XVIII of The Prince.","citations":["A:279"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Crime","Identity"]},{"name":"Atalanta","definition":"Swift-footed huntress of Greek mythology. Teresa Piccone names Atalanta as one of her identities while recounting the ancient <em>Argo</em> company, adding that it is only a small part of who she is [MTM B7:234].","citations":["MTM B7:234"],"tags":["Argonautica","Greek Mythology","Argonauts (Characters)","Women Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Atheistic Communism","definition":"One of the seven contemporary heads of the enemy named by Bascom Bagby [MTM B5:169]. A later epigraph, credited to Pius XI’s Atheistic Communism, calls it an imminent danger [MTM B5:177]. The source is the encyclical Divini Redemptoris, dated 19 March 1937, whose third paragraph identifies bolshevistic and atheistic Communism in these terms.","citations":["MTM B5:169","MTM B5:177"],"tags":["Popes","Theology","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Athens","definition":"Greek city whose historical existence Cyrus Roundhead denies in a Royal Pop historical discourse. He calls Athens a Macedonian invention designed to supply a prestigious culture and past. John the Greek objects that he comes from there and lived there until thirteen [MTM B11:337].","citations":["MTM B11:337"],"tags":["Greece","History & Memory","Creation"]},{"name":"Atlantic Ocean","definition":"Ocean crossed eastward by <em>Brunhilde</em> from Natal, Brazil, on January 18 to Freetown, Sierra Leone, on January 26. The narrator calls westward travel easier; Papa Devil enlarges this into a story of ancient Atlantic crossings whose eastward returns were almost impossible [D:89]. Anastasia’s account of mermaids denies that true mermaids live in the Atlantic, distinguishing northern sea-cows from them [D:34-35]. During the Atlantic crossing, Finnegan’s uncertain waking dream with Marie concerns immortality and her identification with Demeter and Persephone. Although they should be aboard ship in mid-ocean, the scene places them on a jungle beach [D:91-92].","citations":["D:89","D:34-35","D:91-92"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Immortality","Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans","Africa","Finnegan","Dreams"]},{"name":"Atlantic Port","definition":"Landing Finnegan had expected at the end of the obscure return from his interrupted round-the-world voyage. Instead, he is paid off in San Francisco. He explains that he worked below in the engine room and passed free time with drink and old comic magazines, uncertain whether he had actually circumnavigated the globe [A:145].","citations":["A:145"],"tags":["Sailing","Ports & Harbors","Finnegan","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Atlantic States","definition":"Eastern American states traversed by Gautier the goat-man and his herd over forty years. By the time Finnegan meets him, he remains in New Jersey throughout the year because age makes travel harder [A:196].","citations":["A:196"],"tags":["Minor Characters","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Atlantis","definition":"Legendary lost island or continent. In Casey’s fifth imagined world, Antarctica is an ice ring surrounding a warm southern sea and continent, which he supposes to be Atlantis after continental migration. He dreams of exchanging modern techniques for its Higher Ethic [A:158].","citations":["A:158"],"tags":["Folklore","Dreams","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Atlas","definition":"Reference book of maps supplied with the dictionary, Bible, missal and <em>Rule of St. Benedict</em> in Casey’s six-boy chamber at St. Martin’s Hall, St. Bonaventure’s boarding school [A:161-162].","citations":["A:161-162"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"Attic","definition":"Two distinct upper rooms in Duffey’s early life. His childhood domain in a large town house is an attic room; the Prisoner John Window is in a little closet or cell opening off it [MTM B1:12]. Later, Lily leads eight young people away from an unwanted formal dance to a soundproof attic billiard room, where they play pool, music and informal dances and share food and drink [MTM B1:33].","citations":["MTM B1:12","MTM B1:33"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory","Music"]},{"name":"Attic Ephebic Inscriptions","definition":"Inscriptions invoked by X when <em>Argo</em>’s talking oak challenges his right to board. X claims that the initial X stands for Xenoi in Attic ephebic inscriptions; the oak supplies the meaning “Strangers.” This is his argument for being present in Inscription, since he admits no other scriptural or inscriptional credentials. Duffey eventually admits him as a servitor with half shares, not as a Master Argonaut [MTM B13:435].","citations":["MTM B13:435"],"tags":["Ships","Greece","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Attila the Hun","definition":"Fifth-century king of the Huns. The epigraph attributed to Carmody Overlark’s Studies in the Imagination includes fifth-century representations of Attila among its examples of a Neanderthal-type head and enlarged back-brain. Overlark associates this type with ghostliness and imagination [D:270-272].","citations":["D:270-272"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Ghosts & Spirits","Literature"]},{"name":"Attorney","definition":"Profession Manuel claims for himself. He says that he is fairly rich and a lawyer among his own people but travels in poverty to infiltrate the apparatus turning men into devils. His stated mission serves himself and an otherwise ineffective society in a country south of Mexico [D:56-57].","citations":["D:56-57"],"tags":["Occupations","Crime","Devils (Characters)","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Auditorium","definition":"The Pelican group’s auditorium begins in a warehouse and loft next to the print shop, acquired when Duffey, Dotty and Stein put Henry’s work into action [A:126]. In the later Duffey buildings it is the principal and largest room, used for the Royal Pop meeting [MTM B11:327]. A different auditorium, at Ursuline Academy, is the venue for <em>Seven Roads</em>, announced at the Ghost Coffee Shop and later shown in performance with Duffey playing his own role [MTM B10:293] [MTM B12:366].","citations":["A:126","MTM B11:327","MTM B10:293","MTM B12:366"],"tags":["Duffey","Theater","Journalism","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Aura","definition":"An apprehended presence or emanation. Marie Courtois, like Papa Devil, has an aura of great power that makes Finnegan feel his signature for the ship might mortgage his soul [D:32]. When Papa-D enters the club early in the story, Finnegan recalls the aura although he cannot clearly remember the person [D:15]. Later the Descalced priest says Papa’s aura exceeds the ordinary evils a priest encounters [D:70].","citations":["D:32","D:15","D:70"],"tags":["Finnegan","Devils (Characters)","Identity","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Auriferous cargo","definition":"Gold-bearing freight, the object of the coastwise quest in the poem opening “Stranahan, Who Is Meleager.” Its speaker remembers sailing with the companions on the ship anciently called <em>Argo</em> [A:91].","citations":["A:91"],"tags":["Argonautica","Ships","Sailing","Maritime Trade","Money & Trade","Literature"]},{"name":"Aurignacian people","definition":"People associated with the Aurignacian culture of the early Upper Paleolithic. The epigraph credited to Carmody Overlark’s Studies in the Imagination includes Aurignacian and Grimaldi types in its imagined back-brain lineage, associating that lineage with ghostliness and imagination [D:270-272].","citations":["D:270-272"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","History & Memory","Literature"]},{"name":"Aurora, Sister Mary (Rittenhouse)","definition":"One of the ecclesial or “vineyard” people in Duffey and Letitia’s Chicago golden-age circle [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Aussie","definition":"Colloquial Australian identification. Biloxi Brannagan calls himself an Australian while recalling his life as an awkward seaman and his courtship of Gertrude [D:203].","citations":["D:203"],"tags":["Australia","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Aussie Brigadier","definition":"Australian officer targeted by the disastrous raid Casey induces Stein and the American officers to arrange. Stein has been deceived into believing that Tania, actually Tommy Trouncer performing as a woman, is being kept by the brigadier [A:51-54]. The brigadier initially says that an unauthorized woman’s presence would merit punishment, then becomes furious when he realizes he is the accused man. He summons the island personnel to explain the intrusion; the resulting scandal damages Stein, Colonel Laycheck and the three majors [A:54].","citations":["A:51-54","A:54"],"tags":["Australia","Islands","Pacific Theater","World War II","Soldiers","Theater","Identity"]},{"name":"Aussie non-com club","definition":"Australian noncommissioned officers’ club that preserves Finnegan’s humiliating affidavit on the inferiority of Yanks at drinking and almost everything else. Tom Shire inspires the document; the intoxicated Finnegan signs it at the wet canteen believing he is signing an outfit roster to obtain beer chits. Later club members are too young to have known Shire and Freddy Castle but still cherish the affidavit [A:11-13].","citations":["A:11-13"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","World War II","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Aussie Private","definition":"The young Australian identity adopted by Private Gregory after escaping his long American officer career. In 1907 he had abandoned his century-long sergeancy, made himself about twenty, and obtained an appointment to the U.S. Military Academy. Years later, unable to appear convincingly older, he escaped again as an Australian private. Investigators matched this young private’s fingerprints to those of the vanished elderly American colonel and kept him under observation [A:78-79].","citations":["A:78-79"],"tags":["Australia","Soldiers","Identity","Immortality"]},{"name":"Austin, Blessed","definition":"Saint Augustine of Hippo (354-430), called Blessed Austin and an old <em>Argo</em> Master by the narrator. In discussing the strange and inadequate sustenance of the futures, the narrator quotes his image of being served the sun and moon while hungering for God [MTM B13:447]. The wording comes from Confessions, Book III, chapter 6, where Augustine contrasts created things and deceptive imaginings with the divine truth he seeks.","citations":["MTM B13:447"],"tags":["Saints","Patristics","Ships","Food & Dining","Time & Eternity","Theology"]},{"name":"Australia","definition":"Country of origin named for Marie Monaghan, Hans’s wife [MTM B5:158], and by Biloxi Brannagan in his own account [D:203]. At the Brannagans’ kitchen garden, Finnegan says he has known beer served in pitchers only as an Australian custom [D:199]. Teresa’s <em>Argo</em> account identifies Marie as Eurydice, who went to the down-under world of Australia instead of the underworld [MTM B7:234]. Salvation Sally is first encountered in the Australian episode, and Dotty later recalls that Stein knew her in Australia before she came to New Orleans [A:8-9] [A:147].","citations":["MTM B5:158","D:203","D:199","MTM B7:234","A:8-9","A:147"],"tags":["Australia","Underworld","Greek Mythology","Finnegan","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Australian custom","definition":"Serving beer in a pitcher, which Finnegan says he has known only in Australia. He makes the remark at the Brannagans’ home; Gertrude corrects his assumption that he has found a beer garden, explaining that it is their kitchen garden and her husband is drinking his breakfast [D:199].","citations":["D:199"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","Alcohol","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Australian Wine","definition":"The narrator compares Australian air and sunset with Australian wine: it may be better or worse than northern wine, but it is different [A:30].","citations":["A:30"],"tags":["Australia","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Austria","definition":"One of the countries in which Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch lives after fleeing the Krakow region, according to X’s family history [D:278-280]. X identifies Ifreann as the father of both Papadiabolous I and II [D:285]. His account connects the departure with Krakow’s return to Austrian rule [D:280]. Historically, the Free City of Krakow lasted from 1815 to 1846 and was then incorporated into the Habsburg monarchy.","citations":["D:278-280","D:285","D:280"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Family","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Austrian Manner","definition":"The narrator’s description of the way Carmelo Mendoza rolls his r’s while speaking [MTM B12:369].","citations":["MTM B12:369"],"tags":["Language & Words","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Auto Ball Bearing","definition":"Name inscribed on grindstones in Duffey’s catalogue of remembered household “iron words.” Many such memories are appropriated from other people’s minds; the surrounding account ranges across Iowa, St. Louis and Boston [MTM B1:17].","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory","Identity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Automatic Members","definition":"Status promised on the Rounders’ Club sign: patrons automatically become members of the Famous Steeplechase Club, among the establishment’s many advertised attractions [MTM B2:49].","citations":["MTM B2:49"],"tags":["Duffey","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Automobile","definition":"Transport used throughout the narratives. X’s eight-colored car carries him, Finnegan and Doll; Finnegan calls it a celestial omnibus and a purring panther [D:179]. Earlier Finnegan hot-wires George Brubaker’s car, compels the girl with him to drive, tells her to pose as Mrs. George Brubaker, and claims temporary possession under a “Lien of Extreme Need” [D:167-169]. When X warns him to leave Biloxi, Brannagan offers Gertrude’s car and she arranges the title [D:209]. Philip Manford’s overland automobile carries Duffey’s party on St. Louis outings [MTM B2:58].","citations":["D:179","D:167-169","D:209","MTM B2:58"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Crime","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Aventine hill","definition":"One of Rome’s seven hills. In Marie Courtois’s dreamlike discourse, it is the site of the thirteenth station in the old Roman Way of the Cross and of an earlier Demeter-and-Pontos grouping. She connects this Pietà image with the earth as mother and her own lap [D:91-92].","citations":["D:91-92"],"tags":["Italy","Greek Mythology","Women Characters","Dreams"]},{"name":"Avoca","definition":"Town in Pottawattamie County, Iowa, one of seven places disputing the honor of being Duffey’s birthplace in one of his possible childhoods [MTM B1:7].","citations":["MTM B1:7"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Avram","definition":"One of the Chicago Stone cousins living near Duffey’s bookstore. Margaret says that Hugo Stone was an invented name used by several boys, sometimes Nathan or Avram but most often the twins Myron and Efram. Duffey rejects her explanation and insists that Hugo was the same person as Absalom Stein [MTM B4:112].","citations":["MTM B4:112"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Duffey","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"B. Flat, Benny","definition":"Name Dotty gives successively to several suitors associated with the <em>Jazz Magazine</em>. The narrator explains their differing descriptions by the fact that they are different young men, all nice and swinging [MTM B7:212].","citations":["MTM B7:212"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Music","Identity","Journalism"]},{"name":"Babel theory","definition":"Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni’s literal belief that the divergence of languages occurred at Babel in historical time. He tells Hans that this position caused his expulsion from a dozen academies and learned societies in Christian countries [A:213].","citations":["A:213"],"tags":["Old Testament","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Babel, Tower of","definition":"Biblical tower whose language division Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni accepts literally. He describes the event to Hans as a catastrophic umlaut shift with vowel breaking and the shattering of sonants, applying philological terminology to the birth of dialects [A:213].","citations":["A:213"],"tags":["Old Testament","Language & Words","Creation"]},{"name":"Babylon","definition":"Ancient city invoked by Absalom Stein when he challenges Duffey’s right to enter Jewish historical experience: he asks whether Duffey was in the desert, followed the Pillar and Cloud, and was in Babylon [A:131]. In How Many Miles to Babylon, the name also belongs to the small country San Simeon; the narrator says nobody remembers why it bears that ancient name [MB:558]. How Many Miles to Babylon is the title of the supplementary narrative [MB:548].","citations":["A:131","MB:558","MB:548"],"tags":["Middle East","Duffey","Literature","History & Memory","Identity","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Babylonia Bagascia","definition":"Town hosting the declared Last Conclave, whose assembled Gates and Stones intend to dismantle the Ekklesia. It was once mistress of the world under another name, but its former holy element is now hidden or in flight [MB:561]. At the Voltaire Festival on the night before the Conclave, Hiram III is strangled with the entrails of Paul the Eleventh [MB:563-565].","citations":["MB:561","MB:563-565"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia","Apocalyptic","Fictional Places","Crime","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Babylonia Oceania","definition":"Oceanic Babylon, the former name of the Mooring-Stone land. As the submerged country rises, John Giwa blesses it and changes its name to Babylonia Sancta, Holy Babylon [MB:597].","citations":["MB:597"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Fictional Places","Identity","Miracles"]},{"name":"Babylonia Sancta","definition":"Holy Babylon, the name John Giwa gives the newly risen Mooring-Stone land, formerly Babylonia Oceania or Oceanic Babylon. Its emergence combines volcanic activity with an effusion of grace in the account, and the conclave there elects Finnegan Pope [MB:597].","citations":["MB:597"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Fictional Places","Identity","Miracles","Popes","Ekklesia"]},{"name":"Back-Brain","definition":"In the epigraph attributed to Carmody Overlark’s Studies in the Imagination, an enlarged basal region of the skull associated with unconscious and subliminal activity, psychical phenomena, parapsychology and ghostliness. Overlark claims that the Neanderthal back-brain was six times the modern size and invokes Plato’s broad head, Homer’s bust and Aquinas’s massive back-brain as later examples [D:270-271]. His speculative lineage also includes Grimaldi and Aurignacian types and connects this anatomy with imagination [D:272].","citations":["D:270-271","D:272"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Historical Figures","Metaphysics","Literature"]},{"name":"Baconian Turgidity","definition":"A Francis Bacon allusion in Finnegan’s rejected outline of an ordinary human life: Wordsworthian intimations at the beginning, Baconian turgidity in the middle, and Bierceian irony at the end. He asks why he should follow that pattern and declares that he is not constituted that way [D:237].","citations":["D:237"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literary Figures","Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Badger Game","definition":"A staged compromising encounter used for extortion. Charlotte Mullens recalls that her mother Gloria involved her, while still a child, in such schemes against male callers, fetching witnesses and threatening imprisonment to extract payment [MTM B3:87]. In Past Graduate, Duffey says that Acastus of Iolcus used a badger game against Peleus and discusses Hilary and Mary Jean’s attempted reverse badger game against Casey. He suggests that the old Acastus incident may itself have been the reverse variety [PG:535-536].","citations":["MTM B3:87","PG:535-536"],"tags":["Argonautica","Money & Trade","Family","Crime","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Bagby, Bascom","definition":"Duffey’s St. Louis friend and business associate, later his brother-in-law through marriage to Mary Louise [MTM B2:62] [MTM B2:73]. A cabinetmaker with a shop next to Duffey’s premises, Bagby proposes that they work together [MTM B2:46]. He also fights professionally and introduces Duffey to boxing managers [MTM B2:50]. He is a humorously rough-looking man with beetling brows and belly, a “baroque” or flawed pearl, and a slob whose shanty mind contains the accumulated life of whole nations [MTM B2:43] [MTM B2:61-62]. He frequents Chestnut Street fleshpots and fights brutally, at times leaving others or himself for dead. His violent political, economic and religious opinions include condemnation of most local clergy as Judas Priests; he attends Mass across town at St. Malachy’s, which he considers nearest to the true faith [MTM B2:62-63]. He composes and whistles the Gadarene Swine Song [MTM B2:48]. Duffey calls him his dark object, uncleansed stables and another part of himself [MTM B2:73] [MTM B5:155]. His weekly letters offer Duffey intelligence and compassion, even during Duffey’s seven hidden years [MTM B4:145]. After Duffey and Letitia attend his St. Louis funeral, letters continue arriving every Monday for many years, by a means the narrative leaves mysterious [MTM B8:237]. Later letters discuss life outside ordinary time and give Duffey advice from beyond death [MTM B8:248] [MTM B10:302].","citations":["MTM B2:62","MTM B2:73","MTM B2:46","MTM B2:50","MTM B2:43","MTM B2:61-62","MTM B2:62-63","MTM B2:48","MTM B5:155","MTM B4:145","MTM B8:237","MTM B8:248","MTM B10:302"],"tags":["Duffey","The Dead","Bars & Taverns","Music","Money & Trade","Clubs & Societies","Family","Death & Mortality","Occupations","Combat","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Bagby, Mary Louise (Byrne)","definition":"Duffey’s sister, almost certainly his stepsister, two years older than he is [MTM B2:54]. She bears the surname Byrne from the kindred or supposed kindred who raised her. Red-haired and blue-eyed, with large strong hands and swinging shoulders, she is described as his anima made animate and the flame-red part of his soul. The narrative calls her intelligent, proud, friendly and ghostly, and associates her strength with the royal bovine or sacred cow of chthonic goddesses [MTM B2:55]. Duffey has appropriated much of her St. Louis experience into his own childhood [MTM B1:16] [MTM B2:54]. She says that her own attributes and talismans arrange coincidences, including his meeting with Bagby and the old horse barn, and that she created Beth Keegan, though creating persons is less easy for her than for Duffey [MTM B2:61]. She likes Bagby despite his failings and eventually marries him [MTM B2:63] [MTM B2:73]. After Letitia’s death she flies from St. Louis to the wake in New Orleans [MTM B8:254]. In Duffey’s final St. Louis visit she is called Dame Bagby, weighty and ponderous but still merry. She is present at his death and anticipates their reunion [MTM B12:384-385] [MTM B12:388].","citations":["MTM B2:54","MTM B2:55","MTM B1:16","MTM B2:61","MTM B2:63","MTM B2:73","MTM B8:254","MTM B12:384-385","MTM B12:388"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Magic & Sorcery","New Orleans","Duffey","Women Characters","Family","Death & Mortality","Creation","Identity"]},{"name":"Bagby, Mary Louise (née Byrne)","definition":"Earlier heading treated Byrne as a demonstrated birth surname, although the novel says it came from those who raised her. See <em>Bagby, Mary Louise (Byrne)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Magic & Sorcery","New Orleans","Duffey","Women Characters","Family","Death & Mortality","Creation","Identity"]},{"name":"Baghdad","definition":"City in Iraq. The epigraph attributed to Moisha El-Gazma calls Bassorah the port of Baghdad and questions whether Bassorah could have been a port or even a town [D:53]. In the narrator’s account of Hans Schultz’s encounter with Cosmopolis, New York exceeds the combined imaginative promise of Baghdad, Paris, Heidelberg and Lyonesse [A:224]. The Bassorah epigraph also opens Sindbad, the Thirteenth Voyage, whose Baghdad is an elaborate, internally conflicting magical city.\n\nIn Sindbad, Baghdad is a mirage or cloud city built in a single night, its name meaning the Last City Built by Magic in Old Kentauron. Essindibad’s account places it on the east bank of the Tigris, the seventh city on a site previously occupied by Babel, Gade, Babylon, Burj Aqarquf, Seleucia and Cispon. Its thousand gates lead to a thousand suburbs, each with a fair, circus and trade bazaar; a hundred suburbs have slave markets, and each has numerous theatres and nightclubs. Central Baghdad reserves such large communal entertainments to its Grand Bazaar, Grand Fair and Grand Circus. Its roof-gardens, salons and musical clubs coexist with high-altitude dives and subterranean meeting places in cellars, cisterns and sewers, which Harun is rumored to frequent. Its citizens are said to love novelties.\n\nMuch of the city’s original magic has become substantial, though breaks in its surfaces expose depths and even blue sky below. Trams and taxis are anachronistic survivals, and a partly credible railroad runs almost to Constantinople; the citizen who describes it doubts the Boy-Caliph’s complete belief in his own creation. An inserted Kerney quotation describes square brick-and-bitumen walls with twenty-five brass gates per side, parenthetically suggesting two hundred and fifty instead; an adjacent Arabian Nights quotation evokes the City of Brass. The narrator describes bedrock under Baghdad and an artificial electrum sky, which Al-Amin later removes. Mamun, however, says that its stones and splendor are illusions: beneath Lady Narkos’s narcotic enchantment lie mud, poor bricks, wattle, oversized mud palaces and sand-and-pebble roads continually sinking into mud. Harun, in Mamun’s account, is its principal maker of illusion.\n\nNamed places include Blacksmith Street, Cork and Bottle Street, the botanical gardens and their weeping olive groves, the High Place of the Hanging Gibbets, the Royal Archives, the hundred-door main treasury and the Fountain of Life-Bubbles. The bubbles can give a condemned person a full and pleasant ninety-year life in ten seconds, and uneasy observers call them Devil’s Gifts. Scheherazade calls Baghdad a weak place in Gaea-Earth, where the imprisoned devils of other worlds can emerge through thin earth or rivers. She regards its magic as black and tainted; Blue Moon disagrees about the taint. Lady Narkos inhabits narcotic cookies, smoke and powders, while size-changing Ifrits move through the city. Rival spies dispute the nature and location of the gates of hell, including the supposed main underground gates near Baghdad. Dragons carry swallowed demons out of confinement, accounting in the Spy-Bird’s report for their fire-breathing, and deliver them to spacecraft. Harun defends these devils as his friends in the Three Worlds; his delightful aura briefly reverses into horror. He also claims that the citizens he transformed into dogs can never be changed back.\n\nCitizen Heifritz says that Harun’s sons moved the capital to Merv and left Baghdad an innocuous ornament housing their Caliph-Emeritus father. The novel calls its present Moslem Year 191 and Christian Year 813, the Year of Three Caliphs. Al-Amin’s brief reign brings public works, irrigation and navigation projects, musicians’ guild recognition, a performing-arts institution, slave-freeing laws, public distribution of treasury wealth and removal of the artificial sky. His sanitation programme targets foul sewers and a stranded whale whose odor reaches Baghdad. Mamun succeeds after killing him in their duel and bears the Mark of Cain at his throat. He defends the continuing need for Narkos’s illusions, orders the construction of spacecraft to pursue the escaping devils, and welcomes the prospect of their occupying themselves in Carouse Town east of Baghdad. Scheherazade’s account of that town mixes events already happening with her anticipated fiction. She later imprisons Mamun in a bottle; their negotiated arrangement allows time for rule, marriage, entertainment and soldierly carousing, leaving three hours of daily confinement. This negotiated account differs from Mamun’s opening claim that he leaves his cell for only one hour a day. (<em>Sindbad, the Thirteenth Voyage</em>, PDF pp. 5, 10, 22-25, 27, 29, 56, 65, 76-78, 84-89, 112, 116-117, 120, 126-128, 132-133, 136, 138, 142-148).","citations":["D:53","A:224"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Angels & Demons","Magic & Sorcery","Shapeshifting","Monsters & Creatures","Folklore","Ports & Harbors","Middle East","Bars & Taverns","Literature","Theater","Carnival","Crime","Occupations","Metaphysics","Identity","Creation","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Bagpipes","definition":"One of the instruments played by Gautier the goat-man, who is described as collateral with Pan. He also plays the syrinx, making ghost music in the wastelands and swamps [A:197].","citations":["A:197"],"tags":["Music","Greek Mythology"]},{"name":"Bags (Skin)","definition":"Containers, alternatively jars, in which Gautier the goat-man stores strips of dried goat meat chopped fine with melted fat and honey [A:197].","citations":["A:197"],"tags":["Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Bahr El Ghazal","definition":"Region now in South Sudan, called Sudan in the novel. A freestanding, life-size tufa statue from Bahr El Ghazal depicts a Black Christ with a cattle herder’s crooked rod [MTM B3:104].","citations":["MTM B3:104"],"tags":["Africa","New Testament","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Baile","definition":"One of the beaches named in Finnegan’s opening boat poem, alongside Clarence and Mona. The poem promises to drive a boat through wind and cloud to these destinations [A:171].","citations":["A:171"],"tags":["Ships","Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Bait","definition":"Lure for fish. Oliver Greenflag explains that the bait for Puff Fish is a frequency and that frequency itself would have to be altered to make the fish unavailable [MTM B13:418-419]. Honeybucket Kincaid operates the apparatus that can attract them [MTM B13:420].","citations":["MTM B13:418-419","MTM B13:420"],"tags":["Fishing","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Bait Box","definition":"Metaphor for Sebastian Hilton’s repertoire of calculated come-ons that lure bullies into attacking him. His apparent weakness, tears and simpering conceal his ability to defeat them [MTM B1:22].","citations":["MTM B1:22"],"tags":["Combat","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Bait Bucket","definition":"Duffey’s metaphor for the frequency that attracts Puff Fish. He enters the Main Frequency Modulator while Biloxi prevents interference, then declares that he has abolished the frequency in past, present and future. He insists that the apparatus still works perfectly; it is the possibility of attracting Puff Fish that he has destroyed [MTM B13:420].","citations":["MTM B13:420"],"tags":["Fishing","Duffey","Time & Eternity","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Bait House","definition":"The pleasant, shady building where Finnegan and Doll Delancy drink beer after fishing in Florida. It is long and low, with whitewashed rafters and water moving beneath and sometimes through its floor planks. Photographs of unnamed notable anglers and their catches form a comic caste hierarchy on its walls. There a man from Minneapolis tells Finnegan that a left-footed man has been asking for him [D:182].","citations":["D:182"],"tags":["Fishing","Finnegan","Women Characters","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Bakelite","definition":"Synthetic resin named in Casey’s high-school notebook XVI, which says that it is made from “formaldehide and gum arabic” [A:163]. That is the notebook’s formulation; Bakelite is actually a phenol-formaldehyde resin.","citations":["A:163"],"tags":["Literature","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Balbo VII, Gaetan","definition":"Earlier heading shortened the printed name Gaetano to Gaetan. See <em>Balbo VII, Gaetano</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Family","Social Class","Death & Mortality","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Balbo VII, Gaetano","definition":"The heir expected to become San Simeon’s Mad King Gaetano VII. In the Balbo dynasty a prince leaves home at nineteen and returns at thirty-nine as Mad King, allowing his father to become Mad King Emeritus and his grandfather to die. Gaetano VII instead becomes a high churchman at thirty-nine and does not return. His father must continue as Mad King and his grandfather cannot obtain the relief of death [MB:558-559].","citations":["MB:558-559"],"tags":["Family","Social Class","Death & Mortality","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Bali","definition":"Earlier heading obscured the fact that the cited voyage catalogue names a sea. See <em>Bali Sea</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Bali Sea","definition":"The Bali Sea, included in the catalogue of seas sailed by the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Balinese Room, The","definition":"Historic Galveston nightclub on a pier. Dutch Duquesne boasts of earning a hundred dollars a night in tips as a waiter at the Balinese Room, or possibly another swank place at the end of a pier. Mary, who works the clubs, exposes him as a kitchen boy who cleans fish and opens oysters [D:221].","citations":["D:221"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Fishing","Women Characters","Occupations","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Balkans","definition":"Region of southeastern Europe, printed “Balkins” in Casey’s editorial attack on <em>The Bark</em>. He accuses the magazine of supporting clerical-reactionary elements in Poland, Hungary and the Balkans [A:130].","citations":["A:130"],"tags":["Eastern Europe","Journalism"]},{"name":"Bally Alley","definition":"Place where Finnegan proposes a midnight stroll, only for Herman Hercules to warn that their enemies are there too. Hercules recommends the concealed harbor express, a route under buildings and through sewers, cisterns and piers, to reach the <em>Argo</em> [MB:587].","citations":["MB:587"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Finnegan","Fictional Places","Crime"]},{"name":"Balm","definition":"Ointment the Devil reportedly uses on the galls left by his neck, wrist and ankle shackles. In Zabotski’s radio news, the newly released Devil buys ten kilograms of balm from a nearby pharmacy [MTM B6:191].","citations":["MTM B6:191"],"tags":["The Devil"]},{"name":"Baltimore","definition":"City where Joe Cross once worked in a raw bar [D:40]. Finnegan and Doll Delancy check the heavy suitcase through to Baltimore by bus, but leave the bus at Philadelphia, cross town by taxi and complete their own journey by train [D:171-172]. Separately, Charlotte Mullens says that she saw the Devil at the Royal Rogues and Graceful Swindlers Convention in Baltimore [MTM B7:217].","citations":["D:40","D:171-172","MTM B7:217"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Bars & Taverns","Occupations","The Devil"]},{"name":"Baltimore bus","definition":"Bus boarded by Doll Delancy and Finnegan in Newark, with the heavy suitcase checked through to Baltimore. They leave the bus at Philadelphia and continue to Baltimore by train, leaving the luggage to complete the bus journey [D:169] [D:171-172].","citations":["D:169","D:171-172"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Balzac, Honoré de","definition":"French novelist whom Casey Szymansky intends to surpass as the American Balzac, making Balzac in turn the French Szymansky [A:158]. Gautier the goat-man reads <em>Les Contes Drolatiques</em> to his goats and says that Balzac appeals to goats and to the goat in man [A:198].","citations":["A:158","A:198"],"tags":["France","Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Bam (palm wine)","definition":"Palm wine drunk by Finnegan and Joseph, the Haussa boy, in Freetown; the available edition prints its name as “barn.” Finnegan becomes drunk, while Joseph remains unaffected. Joseph proposes moving on to gin and then discussing business [D:93]. Their subsequent conversation leads to the diamond-smuggling arrangement [D:94-95].","citations":["D:93","D:94-95"],"tags":["Africa","Finnegan","Alcohol","Crime","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Banana Nose","definition":"Finnegan’s conspicuous nose, described as banana-shaped when he first studies his reflection [A:1] and as a bugle nose in his comic-mask face [A:6]. In the painting of his future Count persona, the same banana nose has acquired nobility and distinction [MTM B12:368].","citations":["A:1","A:6","MTM B12:368"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Banana-nosed brat","definition":"Dotty Yekouris’s name for Finnegan as she watches him join Vincent, Hans and Casey after their difficult conversation. Her affection is mixed with frustration at his failure to understand what she offers him [A:93].","citations":["A:93"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Bananas only","definition":"The only food Falo and Nauti can offer Finnegan and Vincent at their newly opened cabaret. They charge a peso a dozen because they have no change, although the bananas cost them nothing. Boxes serve as table and chairs, with chunks of wood providing additional seats [A:64-65].","citations":["A:64-65"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","Food & Dining","Women Characters","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Bandicoot Enterprises Limited","definition":"Company whose president is Bandicoot Blackstone. In the accelerating family succession, he says that he will remain chairman of the various boards while his son John, as First Executive, runs the businesses [MTM B9:287-288].","citations":["MTM B9:287-288"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Family","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Bandits","definition":"The supposed thieves of a bottle of Finnegan’s rum while he sleeps near the buffalo’s capital. The narrator offers bandits as the explanation only because the alternative would be to believe that the buffalo stole it [A:206].","citations":["A:206"],"tags":["Finnegan","Alcohol","Crime"]},{"name":"Bandolier","definition":"Part of Casey’s elaborate military costume for his planned appearance as the Leader. He is belted and bandoliered in a scarlet tunic and shako when Letitia intercepts him [MTM B4:117].","citations":["MTM B4:117"],"tags":["Soldiers","Theater","Identity"]},{"name":"Bands","definition":"Musical ensembles include the three bands playing military and inspirational music for the Leader’s promised appearance in Henry Horner Park [MTM B4:116-117], and the Rounders’ String Band, in which Duffey takes over the banjo [MTM B2:51-52]. String, horn and flute bands also appear in the narrator’s catalogue of popular arts damaged by the new degradation of culture [MTM B6:207].","citations":["MTM B4:116-117","MTM B2:51-52","MTM B6:207"],"tags":["Duffey","Music","Theater"]},{"name":"Bands (Rubber)","definition":"Rubber bands binding the paper in Finnegan’s chrysus suitcase. The thirty-six thousand pieces are packed in bundles of one hundred, arranged three bundles by six and stacked twenty bundles deep [D:166].","citations":["D:166"],"tags":["Finnegan","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Bang-heads","definition":"The sophisticated headaches suggested by the drinks depicted in the third room of Elroy Redheart’s miniature artwork. Glinty hints evoke the drinks, while hot artificial colors and cheap plastic figures express the artificial sins and feverish opulence of the scenes [MTM B7:223].","citations":["MTM B7:223"],"tags":["Alcohol","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Banjo","definition":"Instrument Duffey takes up in the Rounders’ String Band and continues playing throughout his life [MTM B2:51-52]. He carries a banjo and straw hat to the Duffeys’ puppet shows, using local mice as living performers and ending the show with tunes [MTM B4:134]. When asked whether a banjo suits his role as a Patriarch, he offers to play his recorder flute instead [MTM B9:266].","citations":["MTM B2:51-52","MTM B4:134","MTM B9:266"],"tags":["Duffey","Music","Theater"]},{"name":"Bank","definition":"Financial institution invoked in several episodes. Saxon Seaworthy proposes finding a bank after he and Finnegan recover enough over drinks, promising to buy Finn shoes when he has funds [D:10]; he later cites his bank balance as evidence of his philosophical success [D:82]. Finnegan makes a close-out bank call before leaving his New York portraitist career [A:193]. Hans visits banks among his business errands [A:97], and Adrian Hilton is identified as a banker [MTM B4:123]. Duffey must discover the location and number of the bank’s safety-deposit box so that Charlotte can recover the Gonof stash [MTM B3:84-86]. Mike Melchiades tells Duffey that, after his wife leaves, he empties their joint account and refuses her a withdrawal despite knowing that she supplied much of the money [MTM B4:142-143].","citations":["D:10","D:82","A:193","A:97","MTM B4:123","MTM B3:84-86","MTM B4:142-143"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Women Characters","Money & Trade","Family","Crime"]},{"name":"Bank Building","definition":"Building containing the safety-deposit box, described as a walk-in, holding the Gonof stash sought by Charlotte Mullens. Duffey first recovers the key’s form and later the bank and box details during the episode Charlotte calls a psychosomatic trance [MTM B3:84-86].","citations":["MTM B3:84-86"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Crime","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Bank Robber and Brigand","definition":"One of the adventure subjects listed among Gautier’s surviving books at Harlow House. His goats have eaten much of the library, leaving the old American “Blood and Thunder” romances he loves [A:198].","citations":["A:198"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"Banner","definition":"Pennants and flags on the <em>Navicula Petri</em> in Henry’s dream. Their mottoes include In hoc signo vincet (printed “Ih hoc signo vincet”); Finnegan’s unfinished Nisi esses sollicitus, scratched out and replaced with “If you can’t be careful, be good”; Ubicumque fuerit corpus illic congregabuntur et aquilas; and the sequence Tu es Petrus, Portae inferi non prevalebunt, tibi dabo claves regni. Their devices include a Lamb, Greek Cross, Fish, Six-Pointed Star, Woman, Serpent and Crescent Moon [A:44-45].","citations":["A:44-45"],"tags":["Ships","Dreams","Catholic Church","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Bannon, Terry","definition":"One of the Forty Scribbling Seamen who succeed Terry Cork in supplying The Plimsoll Line for <em>The Bark</em> and work for the Seaman’s Paper during their intervals ashore. The other named Terrys are Anderson, Jamil and Renier; the collective name also belongs to a song composed by Dotty [MTM B7:213].","citations":["MTM B7:213"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Journalism","Music","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Bantry","definition":"Town in County Cork, Ireland, where Finnegan says he once spent a day and a half unloading cargo. He offers this brief visit as a rebuttal when Doll says that he has never been in Ireland [D:192].","citations":["D:192"],"tags":["Maritime Trade","Ireland","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Baptism","definition":"The sacramental rite and several less literal uses of its language. Finnegan teases Theresa that the baptismal salt flamed against her brimstone nature and that the font cracked; she replies that any of a dozen witches could have cracked it [A:108]. In their uncertain alternate life, Theresa remembers baptizing Chiara, Rafaello and Theresa Anna in a known parish church; Archipelago explicitly calls them her children by Finnegan, distinct from her children by Vincent [A:203-204]. A later <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> passage gives the same three names to Theresa and Vincent’s younger children [MTM B12:385]. X says that Katie Noonan’s son was baptized Columkill and received the surname Gregorovitch through his adoptive father [D:279]. The talking oak in the <em>Argo</em>’s wheel claims to have been baptized in the spirit and to be speaking in tongues [MTM B13:460].","citations":["A:108","A:203-204","MTM B12:385","D:279","MTM B13:460"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Sacraments","Ships","Finnegan","Family","Identity","Dreams"]},{"name":"Baptist","definition":"John the Baptist appears among the sacred figures aboard the <em>Navicula Petri</em> in Henry’s dream, alongside the Apostles, Stephen, Paul, Linus, Clement and Cletus [A:45]. Separately, George Koran’s account of the Majesty’s speaking itinerary includes a Baptist audience in Waco [MTM B8:244].","citations":["A:45","MTM B8:244"],"tags":["New Testament","Dreams","Ships","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Baptist's Head","definition":"Relic included among the <em>Argo</em> Masters’ discoveries: John the Baptist’s head remains in good flesh, with uncut, growing hair and beard each more than a hundred feet long [MTM B13:421-422].","citations":["MTM B13:421-422"],"tags":["Ships","New Testament","Miracles"]},{"name":"Bar","definition":"Drinking establishments recur throughout the narratives. Finnegan and Seaworthy wait outside a bar opposite a graveyard, then enter for beer [D:10] [D:12]. Anastasia works at the adjoining club and later tends the tiny four-stool bar on the <em>Brunhilde</em> [D:13] [D:16] [D:22]. The Cadillac Bar is dingy outside but rich within and favored by Texans [D:58]; Finnegan’s friends nickname a clean Antillean establishment Sloppy Joe’s although its actual name is uncertain between Imperial Bar and Royal [D:235]. Finnegan has frequented New Orleans oyster bars [D:40], meets Francine in a Chicago bar [A:181], and provokes near-riots with his dog-housebreaking product in Buffalo taverns [A:189-190]. The Dirty Five drink with Captain Guiterrez at the Buffalo Bar [A:66]. Duffey’s youthful enterprise includes a whisky bar operated by John Rattigan [MTM B1:27], and his later Rounders’ premises also sell whisky [MTM B2:44]. Kate’s Neanderthal Bar in Biloxi interests Eva as a place to meet her own people [MTM B13:407]. In Trinali’s poem, the Brimstone Combo plays at a saloon on Beelzebub and Hell-Raiser Streets near Iniquity Square [A:235].","citations":["D:10","D:12","D:13","D:16","D:22","D:58","D:235","D:40","A:181","A:189-190","A:66","MTM B1:27","MTM B2:44","MTM B13:407","A:235"],"tags":["Ships","New Orleans","Finnegan","Duffey","Women Characters","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Music","Literature","Money & Trade","Clubs & Societies","Occupations"]},{"name":"BAR (Browning Automatic Rifle)","definition":"Browning Automatic Rifle. Conrad Stone Hackenschmidt uses a BAR to coerce the Conclave into voting for a straw-man and kills Stockton Stone Crocker when he votes for Cardinal Hedayat instead [MB:567]. BAR fire is heard during the Naxos violence, and the boy guiding Finnegan warns that the men aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em> have such weapons and could shoot his head off if he approaches [D:143-145].","citations":["MB:567","D:143-145"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Islands","Greece","Ekklesia","Weapons","Crime"]},{"name":"Bar-boys","definition":"The Cadillac Bar staff, dressed in white jackets among chrome and crystal furnishings. Most of the customers there are Texans [D:58].","citations":["D:58"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Occupations"]},{"name":"Bar-fly","definition":"One of the kinds of acquaintances Finnegan recruits, along with show-girls, to populate his Manhattan art exhibition as Gregory van Ghi. He coaches them in arty remarks so that the show will appear well attended [A:191].","citations":["A:191"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Bars & Taverns","Visual Arts","Identity"]},{"name":"Barataria Bay","definition":"Louisiana coastal bay where the young Henry Salvatore spends weeks trapping muskrats. The novel prints the name Baratarria Bay [A:124].","citations":["A:124"],"tags":["Louisiana","Occupations"]},{"name":"Barbara","definition":"Saint Barbara, appearing with Catherine as a pair of sea-urchin-like figures aboard the <em>Navicula Petri</em> in Henry’s dream [A:45]. In her traditional martyr legend, her wealthy pagan father Dioscorus confines her in a tower; she rejects marriage, receives Christian baptism, refuses to abandon her faith under torture and is beheaded by her father, who is then killed by lightning. The tradition also recounts miraculous aid during her suffering. Venerated among the Fourteen Holy Helpers, she is invoked against lightning and sudden death and as patron of artillerymen, miners and others exposed to explosive danger. Her art commonly includes a tower, sometimes with three windows, and her customary feast is December 4. Her life lacks secure early historical documentation, though veneration is attested from the seventh century. Her feast was removed from the General Roman Calendar in 1969, while devotion to her continued.","citations":["A:45"],"tags":["Saints","Sacraments","Prayer & Devotion","Miracles","Folklore","Women Characters","Dreams","Death & Mortality","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Barbarians","definition":"Trinali Peterson’s derisive name for people who dine under electric light. Her insistence on candlelight also masks the fact that her own electricity has been cut off for nonpayment [A:228]. The <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> narrator uses a separate comic ethnic contrast at the wedding, describing distinguished Irish and Italian guests together with a sprinkling of people of barbarian races [MTM B5:183].","citations":["A:228","MTM B5:183"],"tags":["Italy","Food & Dining","Language & Words","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Barbarossa","definition":"Frederick I, the Holy Roman Emperor. Mr. X includes Barbarossa among the leaders supposedly sleeping in enchantment or imprisonment while their subjects await their return [MTM B6:204-205]. Duffey claims to have attended Kings’ Conclaves with him, Solomon, Saul, Ptolemy, Chandragupta, Nebuchadnezzar, Hsien, Stephen of Hungary and Conrad the Second [MTM B5:166].","citations":["MTM B6:204-205","MTM B5:166"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Germany","Folklore","Duffey","Magic & Sorcery","Dreams"]},{"name":"Barbary Ape","definition":"One of the waterfront establishments in Melchisedech’s catalogue of memories aboard the <em>Argo</em>. It is listed near the Fanged Fish, Octopus Joe’s, the Sulu Ritz and other places of maritime hospitality [MTM B13:407].","citations":["MTM B13:407"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Duffey","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Barbary Flames","definition":"Flames in which the speaker proposes to scorch himself in the travel poem opening Finnegan’s chapter: “And get myself scorched in the Barbary flames” [A:171-172].","citations":["A:171-172"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Barbuda","definition":"Island Ignacio calls good for nothing in this world. In his account it belongs to a group of five Leeward islands including St. Kitts, prompting Finnegan’s theory that each five-island group has a rogue island. Finnegan, not Ignacio, responds “Barbuda, my brother!” [A:272].","citations":["A:272"],"tags":["Islands","Caribbean","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Barbuda Island","definition":"See <em>Barbuda</em>: the island Ignacio identifies as the useless member of his five-island Leeward group [A:272].","citations":["A:272"],"tags":["Islands","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Barge","definition":"A Pattenite Fundamentaler’s name for Noah’s vessel while comparing his faction’s chronology with Zabotski’s Pristine History of the World. He places its flotation and the Floodtide Catastrophes about 2800 BC, while noting that Zabotski does not quite call it Noah’s barge [MTM B9:278]. Barges also carry paper in the H and H H Magazine editor’s promised continuation of the Zabotski-house account [MTM B9:288]. Finnegan says he has worked on river barge-trains [A:99].","citations":["MTM B9:278","MTM B9:288","A:99"],"tags":["Old Testament","Ships","Finnegan","Journalism","Occupations"]},{"name":"Bargirl","definition":"Anastasia Demetriades, who seeks the bartender’s berth and then opens the bar aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em> [D:16] [D:22]. An unnamed bargirl serves Finnegan and Seaworthy Texas beer at the first bar they enter; it is the only beer cold enough to be available, although Seaworthy dislikes it [D:12].","citations":["D:16","D:22","D:12"],"tags":["Ships","Finnegan","Women Characters","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Occupations"]},{"name":"Bark, The","definition":"Journal printed by the Pelican Press in New Orleans as the fellowship’s work of rebuilding the world. Paid printing subsidizes a publication intended to reach roughly twenty-five thousand people who influence public opinion [A:118]. Henry proposes the name from his dream of an endangered boat, and Finnegan draws that boat for the masthead. Its Seven Pillars of Righteousness are Henry, Duffey, Stein, Dotty, Hans, Gabrielovitch, and Finnegan [A:121–122]. Casey attacks the journal and its personnel in <em>The Crock</em>, including a complaint about the ship’s rigging [A:128–131].<br><br>Dotty and Mary Virginia largely run the journal after Mary leaves her father’s trucking firm; Duffey contributes to both it and the separate <em>Investigator</em> [A:143]. After Dotty disappears, Letitia becomes editor, loses some paying print accounts, and keeps <em>The Bark</em> going with private funds [MTM B8:238–239]. In “Great Day in the Morning” it is scheduled for Thursday or whenever it appears, and Duffey’s earlier article has anticipated the disastrous new condition [GD:490] [GD:492–493].","citations":["A:118","A:121–122","A:128–131","A:143","MTM B8:238–239","GD:490","GD:492–493"],"tags":["Journalism","New Orleans","Ships","Dreams"]},{"name":"Barkers' Mallet","definition":"Hochstapler’s own small mallet or gavel, used by the Wool-Hat to kill him over less than three hundred dollars. The killer says he meant only to strike him and receives a year and a day. Hochstapler is Betty’s father [A:217].","citations":["A:217"],"tags":["Carnival","Family","Crime","Weapons"]},{"name":"Barley","definition":"Grain Duffey buys for the Big Pot: a hundred bushels, filling a storage alcove six feet deep [A:133]. The opening creation conceit says barley and hops were made on the third day, so beer is already available [A:1]; Toohey’s, Tooth’s and K.B. Lager are named on the next page [A:2].","citations":["A:133","A:1","A:2"],"tags":["Duffey","Alcohol","Creation","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Barley and hops","definition":"Beer ingredients already available in Archipelago’s opening creation conceit because, the narrator says, barley and hops were made on the third day [A:1].","citations":["A:1"],"tags":["Alcohol","Creation"]},{"name":"Barley soup","definition":"Soup served from Duffey’s Big Pot, which he intends to keep boiling forever and which the narrator says has already boiled for at least a quarter-century. He begins with water and barley and offers bowls to the poor at a cent apiece [A:133]. Additions include donated salt and onions, rough fish, seasonal produce from Henry’s Cajun cousins, X’s dead horse, an occasional bony old cow, game, alligator-tail stew, potatoes, greens, turnips, nine kinds of fish, leeks and garlic. Finnegan and Dotty once contribute seventy pounds of honey from four bee trees; Regal brewery supplies a keg of beer weekly. The operation reaches about three hundred meals daily, with someone on duty around the clock [A:134].","citations":["A:133","A:134"],"tags":["Fishing","Louisiana","Finnegan","Duffey","Women Characters","Alcohol","Food & Dining","Social Class"]},{"name":"Barm","definition":"Yeast foam from fermenting malt liquor. Mr. X tells Doll that, after Finnegan’s crisis and a briefing, their instructions will work in him like a barrel of barm, and they will meet him again the following year. The fighting binge follows this prediction [D:190-191].","citations":["D:190-191"],"tags":["Finnegan","Alcohol","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Barmaid","definition":"Dotty Yekouris worked as a barmaid before the war, with Finnegan as her customer [A:96]; Casey’s hostile editorial calls her the Beautiful Barmaid and ridicules the supposed limits of her drink repertoire [A:129]. Anastasia Demetriades becomes bargirl aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em> [D:16] [D:22], while an unnamed bargirl serves Finnegan and Seaworthy Texas beer in the opening bar [D:12]. Soft-Talk Susie Kutz tends the Old Wooden Ship’s bar and is, the narrator says, its sole actual angel unaware, submitting kindly exaggerated reports to the Lord [D:216].","citations":["A:96","A:129","D:16","D:22","D:12","D:216"],"tags":["Angels & Demons","Ships","Finnegan","Women Characters","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Occupations"]},{"name":"Barn","definition":"Finnegan and Howland stay in an abandoned barn during their northward travels; Howland chants his five-gophers song as they lie in a hay loft [A:179]. Duffey buys a rickety horse barn or livery stable on Walnut Street in St. Louis [MTM B2:40], provides beds, food, drink and horseshoe pitching there, and eventually develops it into the Rounders’ Club [MTM B2:47] [MTM B2:49]. Giulio receives hospitality and a talisman there [MTM B2:42-43] [MTM B2:47]. In Finnegan’s playful account of Greek astronomy, a barn is one imagined measure of the sun’s or moon’s size [D:132].","citations":["A:179","MTM B2:40","MTM B2:47","MTM B2:49","MTM B2:42-43","D:132"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Finnegan","Duffey","Bars & Taverns","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Barn Owl (Giant)","definition":"Finnegan’s comparison for the buffalo seen from the front in their ancestral city. He comically insists on a precise species distinction, though no listener is present. The narrator tentatively locates the ancestral city in Frio County [A:205-206].","citations":["A:205-206"],"tags":["Finnegan","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Barnaby, Don (Duke of Moule)","definition":"One of Finnegan’s three companions in the Basse-Terre terrestrial Paradise, with Charles Le Marin and Johnny Duckwalk [D:233-236]. Duke of Moule is only his nickname. He and Duckwalk chase the island girls while Finnegan and Le Marin do so less enthusiastically [D:236]. His arrival seems to Finnegan like a third presence gradually coalescing around him and Le Marin, but the narrator calls this an impression [D:258-259]. He reminds Finnegan of another Don, apparently Don Lewis, yet is larger, rougher and fearless; Finnegan regards him as a lecher, a fugitive for homicide and a genuine poet who might have ghostly extensions [D:249]. He tells the extravagant story of Madame Le Mourcheor’s Juju Joy Pills and the many lives he experiences in her garden [D:248-252]. A voice other than his twice speaks from his hammock while his even breathing continues: first asking Finnegan to repay Anastasia fifty dollars, then pleading for rescue after three years dead in the sand [D:238] [D:256]. He slips into memories of the <em>Brunhilde</em> and Manuel, then denies that he was there [D:253] [D:265]. Restless, he leaves Paradise promising to return; Angela says he will not, and reports his claim that he has not been the other Don for several years [D:265].","citations":["D:233-236","D:236","D:258-259","D:249","D:248-252","D:238","D:256","D:253","D:265"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Islands","Caribbean","Finnegan","Minor Characters","The Dead","Literature","Ghosts & Spirits","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Barnacle (Holy)","definition":"The Holy Barnacle and the Pearl Beyond Price, one of the announced topics of the Society for Creative History, also called the Royal Pop Historians. Margaret Stone reports the program and decides to attend lest the group discard something she wants to preserve [MTM B11:307].","citations":["MTM B11:307"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","History & Memory","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Barnacle Remover (Dr. Stein's)","definition":"Dr. Stein’s Barnacle Remover, one of the drinks Absalom Stein mixes at the Bay Hotel picnic with gin and ice. His other inventions are the Flying Steinmetz, Steinheim Stinger, Stein Zombie, Stein Collins, Ein-Stein and Stein Julip [A:29-30].","citations":["A:29-30"],"tags":["Alcohol","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Barnacles","definition":"Image of accreted material obscuring history in the Royal Pop Historians’ opening speech. The speaker claims that misplaced times and incidents can be recovered from barrows or from beneath the cement of barnacles, freed from their incrustations and revised as events still happening in the present [MTM B11:323-324].","citations":["MTM B11:323-324"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","History & Memory","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Barnard","definition":"One of the twenty Stone boys whom Margaret Stone identifies as first cousins in four families, all living within three blocks of Duffey’s former Chicago bookstore [MTM B4:112].","citations":["MTM B4:112"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Barney","definition":"Commercial fisherman and resident regular at the Old Wooden Ship [D:215-216]. He, Shrimp Boat Gordon and Tom inspect the sea and sky and smell the air each morning, each asking what the others think; more often than not they declare the day unsuitable for fishing and remain to drink [D:220].","citations":["D:215-216","D:220"],"tags":["Fishing","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Occupations"]},{"name":"Baron","definition":"Title Finnegan assigns to Gottfried von Guggenheim, one of the named hundred-dollar bills he keeps as the financial cushion beneath his adventures. The sequence also includes Aloysius, Hernandez, Jose-Ramires and the current Miguel [D:19-20].","citations":["D:19-20"],"tags":["Finnegan","Money & Trade","Social Class","Identity"]},{"name":"Baroque","definition":"Description of Bascom Bagby as a flawed pearl, combining extraordinary mental depth and scope with slovenliness and turbulent habits [MTM B2:62]. In the transformed world, Cyrus Roundhead says that the people of the thunder dimension woke to consciousness when the world was already in its baroque phase [MTM B11:350].","citations":["MTM B2:62","MTM B11:350"],"tags":["History & Memory","Metaphysics","Identity"]},{"name":"Barracaded","definition":"The source’s spelling of barricaded in Trinali Peterson’s account of resisting eviction. She had burned her stock of driftwood after the heat was cut off; while barricaded inside, she received food from Lupido in a basket lowered from her window [A:229].","citations":["A:229"],"tags":["Women Characters","Social Class"]},{"name":"Barracks","definition":"Tom Shire and Freddy Castle’s military quarters, with a wet canteen. Their elaborate hoax makes Finnegan and Vincent believe Americans are barred: they disguise the two in winter tunics and digger hats, cut Vincent’s hair, rehearse an Australian accent and take them over back fences. At the canteen, orderly sergeant Bushmaster recognizes and welcomes them, nearly spoiling the joke [A:10-12].","citations":["A:10-12"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","Soldiers","Bars & Taverns","Identity"]},{"name":"Barracuda Bar (Biddy's)","definition":"Biddy’s Barracuda Bar, one of the waterfront establishments in Melchisedech’s catalogue of memories aboard the <em>Argo</em>, alongside the Fanged Fish, Salty Dog’s Shack-Up House and many others [MTM B13:407].","citations":["MTM B13:407"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Duffey","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Barrel House","definition":"A kind of drinking establishment in the narrator’s account of New Orleans nightlife continuing into daylight: a party can go straight from Breakfast at Brennan’s to a barrel house or night club and find entertainment [MTM B7:219].","citations":["MTM B7:219"],"tags":["New Orleans","Bars & Taverns","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Barrels of Wine","definition":"Three barrels, with additional jugs, that Papa Piccone plans to provide for Vincent and Theresa’s wedding reception. Maurine tells Monica that he refuses to let people remember a shortage two thousand years later, as at Cana [A:105].","citations":["A:105"],"tags":["Alcohol","Food & Dining","Family","New Testament"]},{"name":"Barrens","definition":"Part of the wild New Jersey back country where Finnegan meets Gautier the goat-man. The narrator describes low mountains, high seas, hard sands, soft rocks and unusually isolated inhabitants [A:193-194]. Gautier and his goats often occupy the abandoned Harlow House; although many Barrens shacks are haunted, the narrator explicitly leaves that house’s haunting uncertain, and Finnegan never visits it [A:196-197].","citations":["A:193-194","A:196-197"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Finnegan","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Barrier","definition":"Finnegan’s name for the division he feels between himself and humanity, despite the affection of his friends; he says he touched his father only briefly and years after his father’s death [D:90]. Duffey encounters a barrier to a valid future on the normal track of time [MTM B10:290]. On Dumaine Street he later meets the sign “The Future Begins Right Here,” solid to him though others pass through it; calling on his giants and his qualified lordship of time, he breaks through the barrier and figurative breakers into clear water, jumping a year ahead while the world also advances [MTM B12:358].","citations":["D:90","MTM B10:290","MTM B12:358"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","Giants & Titans"]},{"name":"Barroom","definition":"The <em>Brunhilde</em>’s small bar, where Anastasia serves drinks until Papa Devil orders it closed [D:22]. Seaworthy commissions Finnegan to paint its panels with the Voyages of Sindbad [D:59-60]. Anastasia becomes the dead wife of the fourth voyage and Marie the wife of the seventh; Papa Devil is proposed as the man-eating ogre and William Gerecke as the Old Man of the Sea [D:61-62]. Unable to draw Papa Devil’s apparent face convincingly, Finnegan discovers that it is a living mask and paints the concealed face; the picture later matches the face of the dead man whom the others cannot identify [D:62-63]. After remembering the burial, he paints the buried man’s face together with another [D:77]. The later explanation distinguishes the dead Papadiabolous painted atop another corpse from a separate dead figure with the current Papa Devil’s true face; their meanings are understood by different observers [D:85-86].","citations":["D:22","D:59-60","D:61-62","D:62-63","D:77","D:85-86"],"tags":["The Devil","Ships","Finnegan","Women Characters","The Dead","Bars & Taverns","Visual Arts","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Bartender","definition":"See <em>Barmaid</em>, for Dotty Yekouris, Anastasia Demetriades, the unnamed woman serving Texas beer, and Soft-Talk Susie Kutz [A:96] [D:12] [D:16] [D:22] [D:216].","citations":["A:96","D:12","D:16","D:22","D:216"],"tags":["Angels & Demons","Ships","Finnegan","Women Characters","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Occupations"]},{"name":"Bartholomew","definition":"One of three possible names, with Thomas and Jude, for the tippling Apostle in the old Italian legend Show Boat says her father remembers imperfectly. The Apostle finishes leftover wine, bottles of wine are found changed to water, and he nevertheless converts tens of thousands. Show Boat uses the tale to defend Finnegan in a letter to Dotty [A:262].","citations":["A:262"],"tags":["New Testament","Folklore","Italy","Finnegan","Alcohol","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Bartolomo","definition":"Bartolomo Portuguese, one of the old pirates whom Angela Cosquin says customers might imagine behind initials on her father’s fabricated treasure maps. BP on a depicted treasure chest suggests his name, as T suggests Teach and K suggests Kidd [D:259-260].","citations":["D:259-260"],"tags":["Crime","Maritime Trade","History & Memory","Family"]},{"name":"Basal bulge","definition":"Feature of the Neanderthal-type head in the chapter-eighteen epigraph attributed to Carmody Overlark’s <em>Studies in the Imagination</em>. The epigraph links a large back-brain with ghostliness and imagination [D:270-272].","citations":["D:270-272"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Literature"]},{"name":"Baseline","definition":"Sebastian Hilton’s image for the danger of being with Duffey: the three slant-faced pursuers can use the two targets as a baseline and triangulate on them. He says two of their rare kind together register too strongly on the hunters’ receptors, yet insists that they will continue to associate despite the danger [MTM B1:36].","citations":["MTM B1:36"],"tags":["Duffey","Cosmic Struggle","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Bashfulness","definition":"Mr. X’s stated reason for staying away from his wife in Milano. A promised week’s absence first becomes three weeks; fear of her irony delays him further, events send him abroad, and his youthful shyness returns so strongly that he fears he can never face her again, though he loves her and hears that she waits faithfully [A:140-141].","citations":["A:140-141"],"tags":["Italy","Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Basic","definition":"Reduced enlisted status held by Casey Szymansky and Hans Schultz. After four weeks in the stockade, Casey attends rehabilitation classes three times a week and works as a basic during the rest of his time [A:50]. Hans loses his sergeant’s rank for marrying without permission; the later account explicitly calls him a basic in the battery where he had been staff sergeant [A:50] [A:58].","citations":["A:50","A:58"],"tags":["Soldiers","World War II"]},{"name":"Basil","definition":"One of Henry Salvatore’s older cousins who follow his lead, with Sebastian, Fabian and Adrian; Peter, a younger cousin, also follows him [A:123].","citations":["A:123"],"tags":["Family","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Basin Street","definition":"Nickname of George Becaud, an Old Wooden Ship regular [D:216]. An orchestral bull-fiddle player, he knows Shot Gun Schaeffer’s story and uses his large instrument case to pretend he is a professional killer owed the five thousand dollars Schaeffer received by mistake [D:223].","citations":["D:216","D:223"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Music","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Basket","definition":"Trinali Peterson lowers a basket from her window so Lupido can supply food while she is barricaded against eviction. She compares herself with St. Paul, then clarifies that Paul escaped in a basket [A:229], as in Acts 9:25. Hillary Hilton separately expands the eggs-in-several-baskets metaphor into his trading philosophy: eggs and baskets may be rotated, sold or given away with one another [A:255].","citations":["A:229","A:255"],"tags":["New Testament","Women Characters","Food & Dining","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Basket Weaver","definition":"Nickname of Aloysius McGivern, a resident regular at the Old Wooden Ship [D:216]. His wealthy family pays him to remain there peacefully without embarrassing them and promises not to send him to a sanitarium again. He especially resents the compulsory basket weaving there and asks why the Devil, concerned with idle hands, is not made to do it himself [D:225].","citations":["D:216","D:225"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Money & Trade","Family"]},{"name":"Basque","definition":"Language Casey Szymansky speaks intuitively with a shepherd on a mountaintop in a night dream, although he has never seen or heard it. Such dreams realize his ambition to know all languages [A:159].","citations":["A:159"],"tags":["Dreams","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Bassa Accompaniment","definition":"Earlier heading altered the printed phrase basso accompanamento. See <em>Basso accompanamento</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Duffey","Women Characters","Music"]},{"name":"Basse-Terre","definition":"The Basse-Terre of Finnegan’s terrestrial Paradise in the Antilles, distinguished by the narrator from Basseterre on St. Kitts, which Finnegan originally meant to visit. The narrative gives precise coordinates while Finnegan himself remains confused about the names [D:234-235]. His friends’ Sloppy Joe’s is actually named either the Imperial Bar or the Royal [D:235]. The narrator’s requirements for paradise include French-speaking Black islanders and English-speaking Frenchmen [D:237]. Finnegan later learns that a leftfooted man has hired a local friend to kill him; he pays more for protection, supplies clothes that turn up on a murdered lookalike, and begins rationalizing his departure [D:267]. In Archipelago he cites Basse Terre among the pleasure resorts he has sacrificed to help Casey [A:252].","citations":["D:234-235","D:235","D:237","D:267","A:252"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","France","Caribbean","Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","Crime","Identity"]},{"name":"Basseterre","definition":"Capital on St. Kitts, the Basseterre Finnegan meant to reach before arriving instead at the other Basse-Terre [D:235]. In Angela Cosquin’s account of her father’s treasure-map business, the passage between Basseterre and Nevis is one of seven false or shoal passages where wrecks accumulated, or would have if ships had followed his imaginative logic [D:260].","citations":["D:235","D:260"],"tags":["Islands","Caribbean","Shipwrecks","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Basso accompanamento","definition":"Giulio the Monster’s howled bass part in Hi, Ho! The Gadarene Swine! Sebastian, Countess Margaret, Letitia and Melchisedech sing the ballad in four voices; Giulio cannot sing and howls along [MTM B3:107].","citations":["MTM B3:107"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Duffey","Women Characters","Music"]},{"name":"Bassorah","definition":"Port of Sindbad’s stories. The Moisha El-Gazma epigraph calls it Baghdad’s folkloric port, then contradicts ordinary geography by describing a scorched rock slab in mud shallows and imagining it as an ancient launch pad for space fleets [D:53]. In the <em>Argo</em>’s history as told by the talking oak, Abd-Aliah of the Sea sells the ship to Sindbad of El-Basrah; much later it becomes the <em>Brunhilde</em>, whose Sindbad Lounge Finnegan paints [MTM B13:404]. Marie says Seaworthy has sailed from more Bassorahs than Sindbad would understand, as she imagines herself his final-voyage wife [D:61-62].","citations":["D:53","MTM B13:404","D:61-62"],"tags":["Folklore","Ships","Ports & Harbors","Finnegan","Women Characters","Visual Arts","Literature"]},{"name":"Bastos","definition":"Tagalog word for rude or impolite, used in Amoy’s angry protest “Hindi bastos. Pinakamabuti” when Ti calls the magpie he loved common. Ti freely renders the protest as a denial that she was common and calls her an uncommon member of the common species [A:80]. Hindi is the Tagalog negative, meaning no or not; pinakamabuti means best.","citations":["A:80"],"tags":["Language & Words","Folklore"]},{"name":"Bat","definition":"Bat wings carry the deformed counterparts of the bright arts from the Devil’s reopened pit in the narrator’s account; Duffey repeats the image while describing the Company of One Thousand and its corruption of the world [MTM B6:207-208]. A separate baseball bat appears in Vincent’s Theory of the Uncreated Creation: newly created batters could already be in slumps without having seen a bat [A:57].","citations":["MTM B6:207-208","A:57"],"tags":["The Devil","Duffey","Metaphysics","Creation","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Batangas","definition":"Philippine place from which an uncle of one of the cabaret girls is expected to bring a piano on Saturday, if he can obtain a cart. Nauti then identifies herself as the player, saying Falo lacks the talent [A:64-65]. Batangas names both a province and its capital city; the dialogue does not specify which is meant.","citations":["A:64-65"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","Music","Family","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Bath","definition":"Service advertised by Duffey at his new St. Louis establishment [MTM B2:41]. He heats water for Giulio in a horse trough, and Giulio removes his clothes and scapulars and bathes with relief [MTM B2:42-43]. Finnegan takes a bath after renting a room on the morning of his encounter with Seaworthy [D:17].","citations":["MTM B2:41","MTM B2:42-43","D:17"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Finnegan","Duffey","Occupations"]},{"name":"Bathildes","definition":"One of the women’s names assigned to the bottles drunk by Finnegan and his three friends in Paradise. In the day’s alphabetical progression, Bathildes follows Agatha and precedes Clotilda [D:243].","citations":["D:243"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Caribbean","Finnegan","Alcohol","Identity"]},{"name":"Bathurst","definition":"West African port, now Banjul in The Gambia, visited by the <em>Brunhilde</em> after Bissau and before Dakar as it moves north from February 1 [D:96].","citations":["D:96"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","Africa"]},{"name":"Bathysphere","definition":"Submersible used by the French brothers Cyril and Cyrus Dimbeau at Nine Mile Depth. Their hundred-meter seabed cores strike the Sword and Scabbard of Saint Sécaire, which they bring into the vessel with grapples and raise to their attending boat [MTM B13:422]. After the brothers begin drawing the sword, Duffey and Brannagan reseal it, drop it back to its former hiding place and cast a Forgetfulness Mesh. They leave the bathysphere party sampling ocean fleas at middle depths, unable to remember the sword [MTM B13:423-424].","citations":["MTM B13:422","MTM B13:423-424"],"tags":["Saints","Duffey","Weapons","Ships","Seas & Oceans","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Batters","definition":"Baseball players already in slumps despite never having seen a bat, one of Vincent’s examples of an apparent past created with the world in his Theory of the Uncreated Creation [A:57].","citations":["A:57"],"tags":["Creation","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Battling Siki","definition":"Boxer whom Finnegan names, with Harry Wills, when describing his own style after his fighting binge. Finnegan says he fights somewhat like Siki [D:192].","citations":["D:192"],"tags":["Finnegan","Combat","Historical Figures"]},{"name":"Baul","definition":"Treasure-chest emblem, also called an arca, drawn below a crossed pickaxe and shovel on the maps fabricated by Angela Cosquin’s father. Initials such as T, K or BP suggest old pirates [D:259-260]. Angela’s separate seven maps of Finnegan’s buried stashes use the maleta, or suitcase, instead of the baul [D:263].","citations":["D:259-260","D:263"],"tags":["Crime","Money & Trade","Family","Visual Arts","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Bavaria","definition":"Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni’s approximate birthplace: the narrator places his birth around 1860 in Bavaria, but allows a slightly different place and time [A:211].","citations":["A:211"],"tags":["Germany","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Bavarian Club","definition":"St. Louis drinking and singing club visited by Duffey and Bagby on the first night of Duffey’s new business. Costumed young women give patrons rides on their backs, and Duffey befriends Helen [MTM B2:47]. Later outings identify her as Helen Platner [MTM B2:58].","citations":["MTM B2:47","MTM B2:58"],"tags":["Duffey","Bars & Taverns","Clubs & Societies","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Baxter","definition":"Baxter Blackstone, Bryan Blackstone’s young son, nicknamed Bandicoot by the neighborhood boys. He begs Zabotski not to kill his father and fears the family will starve if he dies [MTM B9:271].","citations":["MTM B9:271"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Bay Hotel","definition":"Site of the group picnic where a double table is placed on the terrace, the company plays softball on the lawn, swims, drinks Stein’s invented gin mixtures and eats large seafood platters. The party includes Hans and Marie, Finnegan, Loy, Tom Shire, Freddy Castle, Virginia, Dorothy and Stein; Casey refuses to attend with Stein and leaves with one of the new girls [A:29-30].","citations":["A:29-30"],"tags":["Australia","Food & Dining","Alcohol","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Bay Rum","definition":"Nickname of Benny Boerum, one of the resident regulars at the Old Wooden Ship [D:216]. The narrator groups him with Raviola and Saddle Sore as unkempt and uncouth, supposedly falling below the other residents’ standards [D:224].","citations":["D:216","D:224"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Bayougoula Park","definition":"Park suddenly present beside Duffey’s buildings in the transformed New Orleans of the Fifth Contingency. It replaces buildings Zabotski half-remembers owning, although Stein finds records giving the park a history of more than a century [MTM B11:331]. Zabotski continues to suspect he owns its land [MTM B11:333]. After Duffey returns from that contingency, the park is gone and some of Zabotski’s buildings stand there again [MTM B12:356].","citations":["MTM B11:331","MTM B11:333","MTM B12:356"],"tags":["New Orleans","Louisiana","Duffey","Fictional Places","History & Memory","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Bazaar (Parish)","definition":"Parish bazaaring, included among the everyday popular arts that the narrator says lose their style and vitality after the Devil’s release [MTM B6:207-208].","citations":["MTM B6:207-208"],"tags":["Catholic Church","The Devil","Social Class"]},{"name":"Beacon Club","definition":"One of the remembered waterfront establishments in Melchisedech’s catalogue aboard the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:407].","citations":["MTM B13:407"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Duffey","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Beak","definition":"The Pelican magazine is figured as a bony bird with an empty beak, surviving on scant nourishment [A:132]. In the seventh future outlined by Cleo Mahoney, an ominous bird carries in its beak the pennant “Whatever can go wrong will go wrong” [MTM B10:296-297]. Buzzards’ sharp beaks tear at Duffey in his post-fall suffering [MTM B12:393].","citations":["A:132","MTM B10:296-297","MTM B12:393"],"tags":["Duffey","Journalism","Prophecy & Visions","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Beans","definition":"One of San Simeon’s crops, contrasted with the gold that accounts for its exceptional wealth [MB:558]. In Adrian Hilton’s banking analogy, a comic-strip detective stops people eating poisoned beans by shooting them; Adrian compares that destructive rescue with the bank moratorium [MTM B4:123].","citations":["MB:558","MTM B4:123"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Money & Trade","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Beanstalk (Giant of the)","definition":"The Giant of the Beanstalk, one of Henry Salvatore’s mythic identities in the narrator’s catalogue, alongside Friar Tuck and Pantegruel [A:39].","citations":["A:39"],"tags":["Folklore","Giants & Titans","Identity"]},{"name":"Bear","definition":"Mabel, the trained bear who walks with Hans and Betty Hochstapler and assists their comic hypnosis act, acting as an audience volunteer and imitating animals [A:216] [A:219]. Elena Angelino claims a bear great-grandfather, and Professor Kirol says he knew the ancestral couple and confirms her story [A:218]. Eva is released from the ice wearing a blond female cave-bear skin [MTM B13:399]; the crew later kills cave bears for grease to soften frozen lines and for a replacement cloak [MTM B13:402]. In his transport concerning the older people, Finnegan calls them kin to the cave bear and other great animals [D:212-213].","citations":["A:216","A:219","A:218","MTM B13:399","MTM B13:402","D:212-213"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Carnival","Family","Women Characters","Folklore"]},{"name":"Beard","definition":"Duffey’s full, pointed beard is part of Casey’s description of him [A:42], and accompanies his arrival from Chicago [A:97]. As a young proprietor he has a growing red or sorrel beard [MTM B2:41]; later the narrative contrasts his red beard with Bagby’s black whiskers [MTM B2:61], and in New Orleans he keeps it white and bright for his art-dealing persona [A:143]. Giulio’s beard hosts possums in the comic Gadarene Swine ballad [MTM B3:107]. The Baptist’s preserved head has a still-growing beard exceeding a hundred feet [MTM B13:422]. Gautier’s resemblance to an aged Pan includes a dirty-white beard and gray tangled hair [A:194]. Joe Cross’s youthful face is encircled by a red beard on the voyage [D:100].","citations":["A:42","A:97","MTM B2:41","MTM B2:61","A:143","MTM B3:107","MTM B13:422","A:194","D:100"],"tags":["New Testament","Duffey","Minor Characters","Carnival","Visual Arts","Identity"]},{"name":"Bearings","definition":"Latitude-and-longitude coordinates that the <em>Argo</em>’s talking oak sometimes gives when asked to date an artifact, even when they appear unrelated to the object [MTM B13:411]. Lost bearings also describe the Argonauts’ temporary confusion on waking from their hectic lives [MTM B13:398]. The narrator gives the terrestrial Paradise’s location as 61° 44′ 42″ W, 16° N [D:234].","citations":["MTM B13:411","MTM B13:398","D:234"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Argonautica","Ships","Sailing","Argonauts (Characters)","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Beast","definition":"Animal and bestial imagery includes the lions and leopards eating Nimrod Flesh Plant at Zabotski Flats [MTM B12:362], Papa Devil’s claim that he was once a tiger and will regain his stripes [D:122], and Finnegan, Vincent and Stein as the metaphorical hyenas sabotaging Show Boat’s preaching [MTM B7:231-232]. The white shark waiting for Finnegan above his underwater cave is a preternatural sea predator [MB:555]. In “Promontory Goats,” the Sonnet to Pained Victory invokes beasts and scapegoats carrying burdens [PG:529]; later, human celebrants stage the saying about wild beasts devouring entrails in holy places [MB:565].","citations":["MTM B12:362","D:122","MTM B7:231-232","MB:555","PG:529","MB:565"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Seas & Oceans","The Devil","Finnegan","Folklore","Literature","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Beatific Vision","definition":"In Catholic theology, the direct vision of God in heavenly glory. Professor Kirol compares Heaven to a tapestry whose face is the Beatific Vision, while embodied observers see only its tangled reverse; he tells Hans that they already inhabit the spiritual universe without yet perceiving it fully [A:214]. The account of Hell in “Promontory Goats” imagines portions possessing natural happiness while lacking the happiness of the Beatific Vision [PG:532].","citations":["A:214","PG:532"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Mysticism","Theology"]},{"name":"Beatitudes","definition":"Happiness associated with the terrestrial Paradise, though the narrator warns that snakes remain and eventually drive the friends out [D:235]. The fourth contingency for Duffey, the <em>Argo</em>’s Fourteenth Voyage within the Seven Lost or Golden Years, concerns ongoing beatitudes and the promise of Final Beatitude, with the completed quest’s great moment abiding [MTM B10:300].","citations":["D:235","MTM B10:300"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Ships","Caribbean","Duffey","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Beautiful Barmaid (Dotty the)","definition":"Casey’s epithet for Dotty Yekouris in his hostile editorial about the Pelican Press group. He professes affection for her beauty while belittling her skills as barmaid and journalist and regretting her investment in the venture [A:129].","citations":["A:129"],"tags":["Women Characters","Bars & Taverns","Journalism"]},{"name":"Becaud, George (Basin Street)","definition":"George Becaud, called Basin Street, is a regular at the Old Wooden Ship [D:216]. He plays bull fiddle in an orchestra and carries it in a large case. Knowing Shot Gun Schaeffer’s story about a mistaken five-thousand-dollar payment, he pretends the case holds an 800-gauge shotgun that could decapitate a bull elephant, then claims the money was meant for one of his own jobs. He repeats the intimidating joke nightly, sending Schaeffer scurrying upstairs to lock himself in; the narrator emphasizes Becaud’s sinister appearance and Schaeffer’s terror [D:223-224].","citations":["D:216","D:223-224"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Weapons","Bars & Taverns","Music","Money & Trade","Occupations"]},{"name":"Bed","definition":"Duffey buys three cheap iron beds and mattresses when fitting out his St. Louis establishment [MTM B2:40-41]. Finnegan seldom sleeps in beds, although he does sleep in his rented room after meeting Seaworthy [D:18-19]. Marie Courtois lies across a hotel bed in catalepsy [D:112], and X speaks from bed while Finnegan sits by the window [D:157]. Aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em>, Finnegan shares a cabin with Don Lewis and Joe Cross [D:31]; an envelope hidden above a balk can be seen from his bunk [D:50]. Joe says Dopey previously occupied that bunk and describes the frightening circumstances of his disappearance [D:63-64]; Finnegan later feels Dopey’s presence there [D:78]. Casey’s school furnishings include a black-blanketed bed [A:161], while Finnegan sets up a cot behind the print shop during his stay with Casey [A:183].","citations":["MTM B2:40-41","D:18-19","D:112","D:157","D:31","D:50","D:63-64","D:78","A:161","A:183"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan","Ships","Ghosts & Spirits","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Bed Pan","definition":"In Ignatius Ti’s warning against turning one’s back on a corpse, a falling bed pan is one of the accidents a frightened ward boy may attribute to the dead man [A:85-86].","citations":["A:85-86"],"tags":["The Dead","Death & Mortality","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Bee Tree","definition":"Honey source raided by Finnegan and Dotty, who bring seventy pounds from four bee trees to sweeten Duffey’s soup [A:134]. Gautier the goat-man taps a score of bee trees along his route every year [A:197].","citations":["A:134","A:197"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Beebee Town","definition":"One of seven Iowa communities said to dispute the honor of being Duffey’s birthplace, within the account of one of his possible childhoods [MTM B1:7].","citations":["MTM B1:7"],"tags":["Duffey","Identity"]},{"name":"Beef","definition":"Served in many varieties at the Harvesters’ Dinner in Chicago’s Colony Club [MTM B3:94]. Zabotski also has two heavy steers barbecued whole for Duffey’s supposed four-thousandth birthday party [MTM B8:257-258].","citations":["MTM B3:94","MTM B8:257-258"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Beer","definition":"The Sydney beers Toohey’s, Tooth’s and K. B. Lager remind Finnegan of Paradise [A:2]. Beer drives the wet-canteen hoax played on Finnegan and Vincent [A:11-14], followed by Hans Schultz’s victorious contest with Joe Bushmaster [A:15-23]. Patrick Stranahan’s recommended four brews are American, Danish, Bohemian and Guiness [A:110]. Gautier makes corn beer [A:196], while the Regal brewery sends Duffey a weekly keg for his soup [A:134]. Finnegan orders two glasses when he believes he has met his father again [A:244]. With Seaworthy he drinks Texas beer because nothing else is cold [D:12]; he drinks between bar-cleaning jobs [D:19] and with Doll at the bait house [D:182]. Biloxi Brannagan drinks beer from a pitcher in his kitchen garden [D:199], where Finnegan finds his long retirement spent drinking and watching ships [D:201].","citations":["A:2","A:11-14","A:15-23","A:110","A:196","A:134","A:244","D:12","D:19","D:182","D:199","D:201"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","Duffey","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Family","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Beer Chit","definition":"Supposed authorization to buy beer at the Australian wet canteen. As part of Tom Shire and Freddy Castle’s hoax, Sergeant Bushmaster tells Finnegan to sign for beer chits; the document actually insults American drinking prowess. The bartender explains that no chit is required, only sixpence [A:13-14].","citations":["A:13-14"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","Soldiers","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Beer Garden","definition":"Finnegan’s mistaken identification of Biloxi Brannagan’s private kitchen garden. Seeking cover from pursuers, he vaults its fence and joins Brannagan at a table; Gertrude serves a pitcher of beer before correcting his assumption that the place is open for business [D:198-199]. Brannagan later claims to have spent thirty-five years sitting by the sea, ten in an old chair and twenty-five in its replacement [D:204].","citations":["D:198-199","D:204"],"tags":["Finnegan","Alcohol","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Beetles (Sexton)","definition":"Sexton beetles in an as-yet-unlocated Paean passage, described in the original glossary as consuming dead flesh so that it cannot remain to remember. The supplied Archipelago edition does not contain the cited poem.","citations":[],"tags":["The Dead","History & Memory","Death & Mortality","Literature"]},{"name":"Beetling Belly","definition":"Bascom Bagby’s conspicuous belly, paired with his beetling brows when he arrives for a shave and haircut at young Duffey’s establishment [MTM B2:43].","citations":["MTM B2:43"],"tags":["Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Beetling Brows","definition":"Bascom Bagby’s projecting brows, paired with his beetling belly at his first visit to Duffey’s establishment [MTM B2:43].","citations":["MTM B2:43"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Duffey"]},{"name":"Bells","definition":"Young Melchisedech summons a giant hand to help ring a church bell [MTM B1:14-15]; Bagby suggests a service bell linking Duffey’s shop with his own cabinet work [MTM B2:46]. Tower bells toll in Casey’s imagined Fortress Major [A:157]. Naxos has animal, boat, buoy and farmyard dinner bells [D:131]. Off San Simeon, the Sea-Bell, Martyr-Bell and Peter-Bell summon a hidden assembly despite official efforts to silence them [MB:560]. Hackenschmidt orders all bells broken and melted lest they awaken the supposedly dead Church [MB:568]. The novel’s prophecy attributed to Nostradamus calls Finnegan the Pope of Bells and Bay [MB:598].","citations":["MTM B1:14-15","MTM B2:46","A:157","D:131","MB:560","MB:568","MB:598"],"tags":["Popes","Giants & Titans","Ships","Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Duffey","Dreams","Ekklesia","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"BEMs (Bug-Eyed Monsters)","definition":"Bug-eyed monsters, a pulp science-fiction term. Bagby says the Slant-Faced Men receive the same comic treatment as BEMs and HLPs in pulp-magazine letter columns, although he and the others know the Slant-Faced Men are real and dangerous [MTM B7:225].","citations":["MTM B7:225"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Literature"]},{"name":"Bench (Park)","definition":"The park-bench sleeper device, one of four basic practical-joke formulas for the dog-housebreaking product Finnegan sells in Buffalo. Its variant is the bus-waiter trap [A:189-190].","citations":["A:189-190"],"tags":["Finnegan","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Benedictine","definition":"Religious affiliation of Joseph of Maintz in Ignacio’s story of an eighth-century priest who shoots himself to the moon to escape worldly temptations without committing suicide. Ignacio attributes the tale to Turpin of Rheims and professes belief in it [A:271].","citations":["A:271"],"tags":["Religious Orders","Folklore","Saints"]},{"name":"Benediction","definition":"Church service at which Casey served one winter evening before seeing rich parishioners enter the monsignor’s study. The meeting actually concerned fundraising, but Casey imagined a higher religion reserved for the wealthy. This fed the esoteric dream-world whose library he had begun with material from a magazine advertisement [A:158-159].","citations":["A:158-159"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Liturgy","Money & Trade","Dreams"]},{"name":"Beneficient","definition":"One side of the opposition Papa Devil gives Finnegan when asked to explain the plot: monsters and men, Policia y Ladrones, the Beneficient and the Maleficent [D:120].","citations":["D:120"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Devils (Characters)","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Benevolent Shark at Maule","definition":"Waterfront establishment at Maule remembered in Melchisedech’s catalogue aboard the <em>Argo</em>, alongside the Fanged Fish at Ogopo and other seafaring haunts [MTM B13:407].","citations":["MTM B13:407"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Duffey"]},{"name":"Benignity","definition":"Don Barnaby’s “bedamned benignity” during his experience with Madame Le Mourcheor’s Juju Joy Pill. Pitying the supposedly miserable John Sourwine, or Sour John, he tries to bring him across the street for a pill so that they can share the pleasure [D:249-251].","citations":["D:249-251"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Benning, Martin","definition":"Soldier in Ward Fourteen whom the Sergeant considers sound except on a few points [A:75]. Benning demands proof of Green’s theory that God sustains the universe from moment to moment; George Buckram, rather than Benning, then asks whether Green is God [A:76]. Benning leaves the ward after a few weeks, before the Sergeant’s departure [A:88].","citations":["A:75","A:76","A:88"],"tags":["Soldiers","Minor Characters","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Bera","definition":"B-flat trumpet player in the Brimstone Combo in Trinali’s poem about the old saloon at Beelzebub and Hell-Raiser Streets [A:235]. His name echoes Bera, king of Sodom in Genesis 14:2; the combo’s other names also adapt names of kings and places from that chapter.","citations":["A:235"],"tags":["Old Testament","Heaven & Hell","Bars & Taverns","Music","Literature"]},{"name":"Beret","definition":"Part of Duffey’s art-dealer appearance in New Orleans: he keeps a bright white beard and adds an artist’s tie and beret while running his second-hand book, art and antique shops [A:143].","citations":["A:143"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Visual Arts","Literature","Money & Trade","Occupations"]},{"name":"Bergen (Swede)","definition":"One of five men called Swede at the Old Wooden Ship, although the group consists of a Norwegian, a Dane, an Icelander, a German and an Orkneyman, with no Swede among them [D:215] [D:217]. Unlike his seafaring companions, Bergen has sailed only once and was so seasick that he vowed never to leave land again. His wife also refuses to sail after childhood seasickness. For twenty years each has written asking the other to cross the Atlantic; the narrator says they will remain apart unless someone builds a bridge [D:217].","citations":["D:215","D:217"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Sailing","Seas & Oceans","Family","Bars & Taverns","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Bering Sea","definition":"One of the seas named in the catalogue of the <em>Argo</em>’s voyages and Melchisedech’s maritime memories [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans","Duffey"]},{"name":"Berlitz","definition":"Language manuals on Duffey’s shelves, available to Casey in his pursuit of every language. The list also includes Thimms, Hugos, Marlboroughs, Cortina, Langenscheidt and Metoula [A:159].","citations":["A:159"],"tags":["Duffey","Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Berny Cacciatore","definition":"See <em>Cacciatore, Berny.</em>","citations":[],"tags":["Journalism"]},{"name":"Berry Jelly","definition":"One of Gautier the goat-man’s homemade provisions, alongside berry wine, corn beer and potato whisky [A:196-197].","citations":["A:196-197"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Berry Wine","definition":"Drink made by Gautier the goat-man alongside his berry jelly, corn beer and other provisions [A:196].","citations":["A:196"],"tags":["Alcohol","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Best enemy","definition":"Anne’s affectionate phrase in her sign-off from the Japanese radio station in Java. Her intimate voice and homesick songs give her broadcasts an appeal that Stein’s Anopheles Network cannot match [A:46].","citations":["A:46"],"tags":["World War II","Pacific Theater","Women Characters","Music"]},{"name":"Betelgeuse","definition":"One of the stars whose right ascension Hans learns to calculate for a given date while studying with Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni [A:214].","citations":["A:214"],"tags":[]},{"name":"Beth Keegan","definition":"See <em>Keegan, Beth.</em>","citations":[],"tags":["Women Characters"]},{"name":"Betrayal of Giotto","definition":"Giotto painting cited by Van Ghi in Finnegan’s story about accidentally painting a devil. Van Ghi claims that the same sinister figure recurs in the backgrounds of the Betrayal and other artworks, and tells Finnegan he has drawn the Joker instead of the Queen [D:255].","citations":["D:255"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Finnegan","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Better Life League","definition":"Organization that recognizes Zabotski as an Entrance Person, although he thinks little of the organization. Duffey’s account nevertheless says its classification of Zabotski is correct [MTM B9:268].","citations":["MTM B9:268"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Bevo","definition":"Anheuser-Busch’s non-alcoholic near beer. In the novel it is sold with snacks and sweets by vendors at the Chicago park gathering where ten-year-old Casey’s planned appearance as the Leader is thwarted by Letitia [MTM B4:118].","citations":["MTM B4:118"],"tags":["Alcohol","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Bible","definition":"Biblical quotations frame several episodes: Genesis 14:18 on Melchisedech’s bread and wine opens Book Two [MTM B2:38], and Hebrews 7:9-10 on Levi paying tithes through Abraham opens Book Seven [MTM B7:210]. Genesis 6:4 supplies the giants epigraph [D:212], and excerpts from Numbers 13-14 introduce Angela Cosquin’s chapter [D:258]. Duffey’s expectation that he has no end invokes scriptural Melchisedech [MTM B10:290], recalling Hebrews 7:3. A Bible is among the books supplied in Casey’s schoolroom, although its translation is unspecified [A:161-162]. The cited passages do not establish that all these Bibles and quotations are Douay-Rheims; some wording differs from that translation.","citations":["MTM B2:38","MTM B7:210","D:212","D:258","MTM B10:290","A:161-162"],"tags":["Old Testament","New Testament","Scripture","Duffey","Literature"]},{"name":"Bible (Douay-Rheims)","definition":"Earlier heading assigned the Douay-Rheims translation to passages and a school Bible whose translation is not established. See <em>Bible</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Old Testament","New Testament","Scripture","Duffey","Literature"]},{"name":"Bicycle riding","definition":"One of the popular arts said to lose its style and vitality in the widespread degradation following the Devil’s release [MTM B6:207].","citations":["MTM B6:207"],"tags":["The Devil","Social Class"]},{"name":"Biddy's Barracuda Bar","definition":"Waterfront establishment in Duffey’s catalogue of maritime memories aboard the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:407].","citations":["MTM B13:407"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Duffey","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Bierceian Irony","definition":"Finnegan’s phrase for an ironic ending in the manner of Ambrose Bierce. In his supposed life after death, he questions why his existence should follow a human literary pattern of Wordsworthian beginning, Baconian middle and Bierceian end; he insists that he has reached something, but not the end [D:237].","citations":["D:237"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literary Figures","Literature","Identity"]},{"name":"Big Bird","definition":"The giant huracan or hurricane bird said to nest in San Simeon’s limestone crags. The narrative offers alternative accounts of one repeatedly flying bird or several newly hatched birds, and says its twisting flights tear away pieces of the country that form at least thirteen Antillean islands [MB:557-558].","citations":["MB:557-558"],"tags":["Islands","Monsters & Creatures","Folklore","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Big Boy","definition":"Unlocated expression attributed in the original glossary to Josephine McSorely’s <em>Underground Eagle</em> material. The cited Casey Machine page does not contain it.","citations":[],"tags":["Journalism"]},{"name":"Big Fat-Head","definition":"One of the familiar valley names Don Lewis lists when recounting how three American soldiers became lost on Willy Jones Island after pursuing and killing Yokipoki. Big Fat-Head is paired with Little Fat-Head; the valley where they are stranded is one they do not recognize [D:44-46].","citations":["D:44-46"],"tags":["Soldiers","Islands","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Big House","definition":"House on Willy Jones Island in one of Don Lewis’s supposedly happy island stories. Its inhabitants kill first-night visitors and make broth from their organs to preserve their own youth; a year without a visitor makes everyone a year older [D:48]. Ali carries one protective ta’a nut and plants another before entering. When the inhabitants come to kill him, he brings morning by snapping his fingers, finds the planted nut grown into a tree reaching his window, and climbs down to escape. That year the household ages [D:48-49].","citations":["D:48","D:48-49"],"tags":["Immortality","Islands","Folklore","Magic & Sorcery","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Big Pot","definition":"Zabotski’s huge glass-lined experimental crock, given to Duffey [A:132]. Duffey sets the thousand-gallon vessel over gas burners and establishes his continuing barley-soup service [A:133]. In the later account, razzers and jazzers mock the Big Pot; three medical students apparently plant the human bones they then fish out, and their Cannibal Duffey sign is generally received as a joke [MTM B6:197].","citations":["A:132","A:133","MTM B6:197"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Big Red Barn on Pristine Cove","definition":"The neighbors’ name for Zabotski and Waldo’s floating boat palace on Lake Borgne, shared with many children and animals [MTM B10:290]. After floating out of Dumaine Street, it lies at Zabotski’s lake lot on a small kedge anchor [MTM B9:287].","citations":["MTM B10:290","MTM B9:287"],"tags":["Ships","Louisiana"]},{"name":"Big Swede Swanson","definition":"One of the five regulars called Swede at the Old Wooden Ship [D:215]. The group contains no actual Swede, though the passage does not assign Swanson a particular nationality [D:217].","citations":["D:215","D:217"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Big Triangle","definition":"Region containing the undersea nest of the young Saucerite who joins the <em>Argo</em> near the Mooring-Stone. He studies among human children, takes classmates to his nest, and talks with the passengers and the fleshless Melchisedech [MB:591-592].","citations":["MB:591-592"],"tags":["Ships","Duffey","Fictional Places","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Bill (Buffalo Chips) Dugan","definition":"Bill Buffalo Chips Dugan, one of the Old Wooden Ship’s regulars [D:215]. The bar is in Galveston [MTM B13:458]. He belongs to the closely associated group of Russ, Smokehouse, Bob and Charleyhorse Heckel. Dugan and Smokehouse exploit their employer’s confusion by sharing one garage job on alternate days and splitting the wage. Both Dotty Danvers and Dotty Hulme have been married to Dugan [D:220].","citations":["D:215","MTM B13:458","D:220"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Occupations","Family"]},{"name":"Bill Collectors","definition":"Dotty’s persecutors during the Pelican group’s lean period. Their pursuit appears in a comic parallel with the ideological enemies shooting at Stein, Gabby and Duffey [A:132].","citations":["A:132"],"tags":["Women Characters","Money & Trade","Journalism"]},{"name":"Bill O'Shivaree","definition":"Client defended by Patrick Stranahan. Father McGuigan asks whether O’Shivaree will hang, and Stranahan replies that he will not unless Stranahan loses the case [MTM B5:169].","citations":["MTM B5:169"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Crime"]},{"name":"Billiard Balls","definition":"Beth Keegan’s joke when Duffey and Mary Louise discuss her as an ivory doll or artifact: she says he could become rich by cutting her into billiard balls [MTM B2:57].","citations":["MTM B2:57"],"tags":["Women Characters","Duffey","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Billiousness","definition":"Finnegan’s term for the unpleasant character he attributes to ballads in a letter to Dotty. It completes his comic catalogue of supposed defects in musical forms, including fugue, opera, pop, waltz, hillbilly, swing and downbeat [A:263-264].","citations":["A:263-264"],"tags":["Finnegan","Music","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Bilocation","definition":"Being in two places at once. The narrator considers this as a possible explanation of Finnegan and Theresa’s otherwise impossible twelve years together, citing saints and ecstatics but contrasting their brief episodes with the couple’s long interval [A:202]. Later, when Pope Paul the Eleventh suggests that Finnegan has the gift, Finnegan replies that the greatest devils have it too and denies being either saint or devil [MB:598].","citations":["A:202","MB:598"],"tags":["Saints","Miracles","Devils (Characters)","Finnegan","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Biloxi","definition":"Mississippi port where Brannagan and Gertrude live. Marie’s story explains Brannagan’s nickname by his having gone ashore there and never caught his ship again [A:25]. Finnegan finds refuge with the couple after eluding pursuers [D:198-201]. The <em>Argo</em> sails against winds and currents to Biloxi [MTM B13:402] and docks there amid preparations for celebration [MTM B13:408].","citations":["A:25","D:198-201","MTM B13:402","MTM B13:408"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Ports & Harbors","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Biloxi Girl","definition":"Marie’s Aunt Gertrude in the comic story Hans hears in his mind while waiting for Marie. Gertrude lets Brannagan mistake an old piling for his ship’s mast, so he believes he need not hurry back aboard [A:25].","citations":["A:25"],"tags":["Ships","Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Bimini","definition":"Point of departure for the luxury yacht carrying the shadow-men Bolo Manolo, Douglas McAfee and Lloyd Cardigan-Pembroke toward the Mooring-Stone [MB:594].","citations":["MB:594"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing"]},{"name":"Binnacle","definition":"Housing for a ship’s compass. Anastasia’s signaling lantern, made with reflecting mirrors rather than a projector, is enclosed in a binnacle-like case from an old boat [D:131]. The name also belongs to a Sydney bar [A:19].","citations":["D:131","A:19"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Women Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Binnacle (Bar)","definition":"Sydney bar visited during the preparations for Hans Schultz’s beer-drinking contest. Stein joins the group there and hears about the coming event; Marie joins them later at the Harbor House [A:19].","citations":["A:19"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Australia"]},{"name":"Bird of Paradise","definition":"In Ignatius Ti’s freely translated account of Amoy’s former life as a jewel-stealing blackbird, Birds of Paradise are dismissed as poor judges of gems [A:80]. Duffey later tells Beth Keegan a dream in which he creates Paradise Birds from clay, but rough men shoot them down and they become clay pigeons again; Beth interprets the dream as doubt about his powers and creations [MTM B2:67].","citations":["A:80","MTM B2:67"],"tags":["Duffey","Crime","Dreams","Creation"]},{"name":"Birds","definition":"The narrative gives Black Papua an extravagant abundance of birds among its caves and cliffs [A:36]. Melchisedech can turn stones into birds [MTM B1:18]. Hillary Hilton includes tropical birds among the distressed merchandise he trades [A:254-255]. In Finnegan’s painting, an apparent smaller bird is really a bird-cry written in a speech balloon; its message predicts the meat to come into Duffey’s pot [MTM B6:196]. A rowdy bird carries the ominous pennant in Cleo’s seventh contingency [MTM B10:297]. Trinali’s poem calls the birds over the Dead Sea Devil Birds [A:235-236], while the frozen world around the <em>Argo</em> contains motionless birds suspended in the air [MTM B13:398].","citations":["A:36","MTM B1:18","A:254-255","MTM B6:196","MTM B10:297","A:235-236","MTM B13:398"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Finnegan","Duffey","Visual Arts","Literature","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Birsha","definition":"Diatonic-harp player in the Brimstone Combo in Trinali’s poem about the saloon at Beelzebub and Hell-Raiser Streets [A:235].","citations":["A:235"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Bars & Taverns","Music","Literature"]},{"name":"Bissau","definition":"Port in Guinea-Bissau, first named stop on the <em>Brunhilde</em>’s northward coastal itinerary beginning on February 1 [D:96].","citations":["D:96"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","Africa"]},{"name":"Bjonk (Swede)","definition":"Swede Bjonk, one of the Old Wooden Ship’s resident regulars [D:215]. The five men called Swede are collectively Norwegian, Danish, Icelandic, German and Orkney-born, with no actual Swede among them; Bjonk’s individual nationality is not specified [D:217].","citations":["D:215","D:217"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Black Papua","definition":"The narrative’s name for the densely wooded Papua landscape reached by the returning soldiers: closely spaced creeks, steep bluffs, thorny growth, ravines, waterfalls, deep pools and miles of caves. Within an exaggeratedly crowded square mile it places three small language communities and a possible fourth whose existence is doubtful [A:36]. Its ensuing account presents the region as full of ghosts and its people as nightly visitors to the dead [A:37].","citations":["A:36","A:37"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Language & Words","Pacific Theater"]},{"name":"Black Rooster","definition":"One of the varieties of joy pills Don Barnaby names before praising Madame Le Mourcheor’s preparation [D:248-249].","citations":["D:248-249"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Black Sea","definition":"Sea on whose shore Teresa locates Colchis and the Devil’s preordained prison in her account of the <em>Argo</em> voyage [MTM B7:233-235]. Henri’s letter names the Crimean peninsula as the prison site [MTM B5:180], while Stein dates the imprisonment and release to 946-1946 [MTM B6:185]. In the Royal Pop history, Margaret announces the sea’s obliteration [MTM B11:308], Zabotski asks about its disasters [MTM B11:333], and Hugh de Turenne explains the destruction as an attack on the Devil’s former prison and Colchis’s support for the <em>Argo</em> and Fleece [MTM B11:336]. Duffey’s account of finding the <em>Argo</em> says that a Black Sea-built hull would have been cedar; her actual oak is from the Dinaric Alps and her keel was laid at Ragusa [MTM B13:412]. The ship’s mysterious bell-bearer later supplies Black Sea ship-biscuit to the people at the Mooring-Stone [MB:578].","citations":["MTM B7:233-235","MTM B5:180","MTM B6:185","MTM B11:308","MTM B11:333","MTM B11:336","MTM B13:412","MB:578"],"tags":["Argonautica","The Devil","Ships","Seas & Oceans","History & Memory","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Black-blanketed bed","definition":"Bed provided for each boy in the six-boy rooms of St. Martin’s Hall at Casey’s boarding school, St. Bonaventure’s [A:161].","citations":["A:161"],"tags":[]},{"name":"Blackbirds","definition":"With crows, birds that come from the nearby woods to Trinali’s driftwood graveyard and taunt the seabirds [A:233]. Finnegan’s scattered shipmates in Tangier are compared to a flock of blackbirds while he tries to watch them on the day of the hunt [D:103].","citations":["A:233","D:103"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Africa","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Blackmail","definition":"Duffey says that blackmail began Casey’s political change, but that Casey now believes the ideology; Mary Catherine says he receives a subsidy and will turn the <em>Crock</em> into a vehicle for its propaganda [A:98]. Duffey himself rejects a portfolio man’s attempt to use compromising photographs to gain control of his magazine, insisting that Letitia knows his past and that confessed guilt is forgiven [MTM B4:140].","citations":["A:98","MTM B4:140"],"tags":["Duffey","Crime","Journalism","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Blackstone, Bandicoot","definition":"Baxter Blackstone, Bryan’s son, nicknamed Bandicoot by the neighborhood boys. At five he begs Zabotski not to kill his father and fears having to work in the mines or starve [MTM B9:271]. On what Duffey experiences as the next morning, Bandicoot is starting high school, the dead sycamore has long been removed, and Bryan complains of the withers of middle age; a neighbor says eight years have passed [MTM B9:276]. Later Bandicoot is president of Bandicoot Enterprises Limited and reports that his father is dying after a long life. He will remain chairman while his own son John runs the businesses [MTM B9:288].","citations":["MTM B9:271","MTM B9:276","MTM B9:288"],"tags":["Family","Time & Eternity","Money & Trade","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Blackstone, Bryan","definition":"Zabotski’s neighbor, two doors away but with their properties adjoining at the back. During their dispute over Zabotski’s building, Zabotski predicts Bryan’s decline and death within five days while denying any power or intention to kill him [MTM B9:269-270]. He does command Bryan’s sycamore to wither [MTM B9:270]. What appears to Duffey as the next morning comes eight years later: Bandicoot reports that the tree died and was cut down and that Bryan has the withers of middle age [MTM B9:276]. Later, Bryan is dying after a long life, while Zabotski remarks that he lasted only five days [MTM B9:288].","citations":["MTM B9:269-270","MTM B9:270","MTM B9:276","MTM B9:288"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Family","Time & Eternity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Blade-thin slit","definition":"Narrow opening between sixth-floor curtains through which Finnegan, perched on exterior stone ornamentation, watches Seaworthy, Gerecke, Wirt, Gonof and seven other Devils at their meeting [D:163].","citations":["D:163"],"tags":["Finnegan","Devils (Characters)"]},{"name":"Blaireau, The","definition":"French ship among the treasure-laden wrecks shown on Angela Cosquin’s father’s master map. Customers choose ships and even new locations for the fabricated treasure maps he sells to confidence men [D:261]. The name means badger in French.","citations":["D:261"],"tags":["Ships","France","Family","Crime"]},{"name":"Blanket","definition":"Anastasia brings a blanket and shawl from her grandmother’s for the Naxos vigil [D:131], and Finnegan later awakens alone on the blanket [D:140]. Papa Devil spreads blankets for the beach supper on Padre Island [D:40]. Casey’s school bedding includes black blankets [A:161]. Duffey uses horse blankets around Giulio’s makeshift bath and on his bed [MTM B2:42]. Dotty offers X the sofa but says he will not need a blanket that night [A:136].","citations":["D:131","D:140","D:40","A:161","MTM B2:42","A:136"],"tags":["Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Devils (Characters)","Women Characters","Duffey"]},{"name":"Blarney's","definition":"One of the Sydney pubs visited by the four Dirty Fivers, Marie, Tom Shire and Freddy Castle after Hans defeats Bushmaster in the drinking contest [A:23].","citations":["A:23"],"tags":["Australia","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Blatt-brained","definition":"Duffey’s derisive description of the idea of traveling backward in time. He and Brannagan say their voyages move forward or sideways and distinguish changing possible futures from altering an already completed past [MTM B13:413].","citations":["MTM B13:413"],"tags":["Duffey","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Blessed Austin","definition":"Saint Augustine of Hippo, called an old <em>Argo</em> Master in the narrator’s account of the strange sustenance offered by the futures. The quoted image of sun and moon served as dishes while the speaker hungers for God comes from Augustine’s Confessions, Book III, chapter 6 [MTM B13:447].","citations":["MTM B13:447"],"tags":["Saints","Patristics","Ships","Historical Figures","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Blessed Margaret Herself","definition":"Margaret Stone as named in Duffey’s proposed <em>Argo</em> pennant: This Ship is Salted by Blessed Margaret Herself. The promise answers Margaret’s challenge that the masters may have lost their savor; Duffey says she and her companions reassure and replenish them [MTM B13:447].","citations":["MTM B13:447"],"tags":["Ships","Duffey","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Blessed Virgin","definition":"In the meditation on Marie’s green eyes, the Archipelago narrative says that early Flemish Annunciations give the Blessed Virgin red hair and green eyes [A:24]. The later discussion of holy wealth cites the Marian litany’s titles Tower of Ivory and House of Gold as literal holy and joyous wealth, rather than mere allegory [MTM B4:125].","citations":["A:24","MTM B4:125"],"tags":["Prayer & Devotion","Visual Arts","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Blevins, Father","definition":"Chaplain quoted in Letitia’s Oh Murder! scrapbook, which Duffey reads in distress. Blevins boasts of sexual activity with students, including children, presents it as education he would make compulsory, rejects the concept of wrong, and dismisses his bishop’s authority to object [MTM B8:251]. The surrounding satirical clippings attack deliberately lowered literacy and pornography in teaching, ideological standards for judging wars, dehumanization and murder of opponents, selective recognition of Christ in others, and a priests’ senate leader’s claims of supremacy over diocesan authorities [MTM B8:251-252].","citations":["MTM B8:251","MTM B8:251-252"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Duffey","Journalism","Crime"]},{"name":"Blind Man","definition":"Joe Cross’s comparison for Papa Devil entering the cabin where Dopey disappears: he seems blind yet knows exactly where he is going, and his manner strikes Joe as chillingly insane [D:64].","citations":["D:64"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Devils (Characters)"]},{"name":"Blind Side","definition":"The side of Naxos opposite the harbor, approached by the <em>Seabach</em> from Anatolia [D:135]. It arrives after dark and signals once; after midnight five men land, while the young sixth crewman takes the boat around for a diversionary attack from the sea [D:136].","citations":["D:135","D:136"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Ships","Islands","Greece"]},{"name":"Blinding flash","definition":"The sudden idea that rescues Finnegan’s dog-housebreaking-product business: market it to practical jokers rather than housewives, turning its immediate effect on dogs into a source of disruptive pranks [A:189-190].","citations":["A:189-190"],"tags":["Finnegan","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Blindness","definition":"Among the ailments the Boy King Melchisedech helps cure by placing his golden hands on the affected parts, enabling the physician to effect the cure [MTM B1:18]. Henri later calls Duffey’s St. Louis week a stay in Damascus with an opportunity for blindness and recovery, while saying he has noticed neither occurring [MTM B5:184]. This recalls Saul’s loss and restoration of sight in Acts 9.","citations":["MTM B1:18","MTM B5:184"],"tags":["New Testament","Duffey","Miracles"]},{"name":"Blister Beetles","definition":"Among the creatures afflicting Duffey after his fall into the dung heap, with horse leeches, latrine snakes, scorpions, midges and crab cooties [MTM B12:392-393].","citations":["MTM B12:392-393"],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Bloke","definition":"One of the <em>Seabach</em> raiders, identified by the doggerel crew roster and his greasy digger hat [D:135]. He goes ashore with the Black, Clerk, Hack and Haberdasher while the young Buck keeps the boat [D:136]. After the raid he lies dead on the Naxos cobbles, his bloodstained hat beside him [D:147].","citations":["D:135","D:136","D:147"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Islands","Australia","Greece","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Blood","definition":"Bodily blood, ancestry and violent imagery recur throughout the cycle. Finnegan wakes in a dump with his own dried blood in his hair [A:177], while the Captain metaphorically smells blood as he breaks the amnesiac Sergeant [A:74]. Sebastian Hilton sheds other boys’ blood in calculated fights [MTM B1:23]; Melchisedech keeps jars of blood, and Charles Hart says he once did likewise [MTM B1:18].\n\nThe <em>Brunhilde</em>’s peculiar people are described as full of old black blood [D:50]. Anastasia Grandmother claims the other blood for herself and a little for her granddaughter [D:128]. Papa Devil places himself, Anastasia and Finnegan among the Non-people and calls Dopey a seaman of strange blood [D:121]. Brannagan says he and Finnegan each have a little double blood, in differing recensions [D:209-210]; Finnegan later calls all four Paradise friends people of double blood [D:237]. Casey calls the Dirty Five brothers who are not blood relatives [A:154]. X’s genealogy makes the earlier Ifreann a son of the Devil [D:278], and attributes the Devil’s dark power to Papadiabolous I [D:286].\n\nDuffey explains the Pelican’s Christ symbolism through the traditional belief that it feeds its young with its blood [A:121]. After Dopey vanishes, Papa Devil dismisses the remaining dribble as bug blood [D:64]. Don Lewis’s body has only a trickle at the mouth [D:110], and the dead Bloke’s hat is bloodied [D:147]. Finnegan escapes through broken glass with cuts to face and hands [D:165-166]. Marie boasts of the pleasure of blood on her hands [D:92], and Patrick Stranahan’s crossroads image has fresh, old and placental blood running down its signpost [MTM B5:164].","citations":["A:177","A:74","MTM B1:23","MTM B1:18","D:50","D:128","D:121","D:209-210","D:237","A:154","D:278","D:286","A:121","D:64","D:110","D:147","D:165-166","D:92","MTM B5:164"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Devils (Characters)","Family","Identity","Combat","Death & Mortality","New Testament"]},{"name":"Blood Roar","definition":"The crowd’s bloodthirsty roar when Zabotski fails the ashes test at the Opera House: the ashes burst into flame on his hands, exposing him as stubbornly human to the supposedly bright people, who drag him away [MTM B11:347].","citations":["MTM B11:347"],"tags":["New Orleans","Theater","Combat","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Blood Snakes","definition":"Creatures that dart toward the scent of Duffey’s blood while he suffers in the dung heap after his fall [MTM B12:392-393].","citations":["MTM B12:392-393"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Duffey"]},{"name":"Blood-of-Martyrs Grass","definition":"Herba Cruor Martyrum, the grass with extraordinarily long roots and a red tinge in its green that had grown only on Vatican Hill. It is destroyed there with blowtorches after the Last Conclave [MB:570]. Later an ancestral dolphin tells the approaching shadow-men that the grass has returned, rising through the water to cover the Mooring-Stone with red-flecked green. Its return accompanies warming water and rising land [MB:594-595].","citations":["MB:570","MB:594-595"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Ekklesia","Miracles"]},{"name":"Bloody Idyl of Anastasia","definition":"The Anastasia narrative also told by X, whose Grecian enchantment is set against the unreliability of both X and Finnegan. The passage questions the separation of canonical and apocryphal adventures and reports X’s claim that Finnegan imagined much of the strange voyage [A:187].","citations":["A:187"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Literature","Identity"]},{"name":"Bloody Mary","definition":"Cocktail Casey says Dotty cannot distinguish from a Screwdriver in his hostile editorial about the <em>Bark</em> group [A:129]. Dotty is particularly angered by the false insult to her professional mixing skills; she has attended bartenders’ school [A:131].","citations":["A:129","A:131"],"tags":["Women Characters","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Journalism"]},{"name":"Bloody Mulligans","definition":"People said to have lived in the Chosky Bottoms, within Finnegan’s catalogue of the place’s spooks and tall tales. The Bottoms are associated with a man-killing panther, swamp ghost, masked riders, giant coons and fish, and enormous bull snakes; Finnegan treats the name as something that can belong to many mysterious places [D:274].","citations":["D:274"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Folklore","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Blooper Fish","definition":"Fish requested by Duffey at the futuristic Louisiana canal landing. Honeybucket Kincaid adjusts electrical frequencies to summon fourteen-pound Blooper Fish, selects one and places it in the eviscerator. Duffey expects fourteen pounds of head steak from it [MTM B13:416-417].","citations":["MTM B13:416-417"],"tags":["Fishing","Louisiana","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Blow Brass","definition":"One of the fashionable Fleshpot Row establishments Shirley Israel praises over Duffey’s preferred places, saying that these are where the real people go [MTM B4:126].","citations":["MTM B4:126"],"tags":["Duffey","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Blow Fish Ball Room, Harold's","definition":"Waterfront establishment in Melchisedech’s catalogue of remembered haunts aboard the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:407].","citations":["MTM B13:407"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Duffey","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Blow-mouths","definition":"Mary Lightfoot’s name for the clamorous troublemakers whom she says must be allowed to grow among the good until the final harvest. Countess Margaret then reminds the company that an enemy sowed them; both remarks adapt the wheat-and-weeds parable [MTM B4:130].","citations":["MTM B4:130"],"tags":["New Testament","Women Characters","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Blue","definition":"Other uses include the sky-blue Mediterranean scenes Finnegan paints [D:114], the blue mountain and haze on Naxos [D:127], the blue-dream sea of Paradise [D:233], and the blue deep water beyond green shallows [D:37]. Blue Fields is a port of call [D:81]. Blue-birds appear in the verse about Li Tai Po [A:222], and Mary Catherine serves blue cheese [A:247]. Finnegan remembers blue sparks from Anastasia’s hair [D:207-208]; Eva’s veins and eyelids are sky blue within the ice [MTM B13:398]. Gautier likes blue-jowled romances [A:198]. Chris McAbney is called a blue-nosed sinner [D:31], Teresa Piccone a blue-moon person [MTM B5:163], and Finnegan, mistaking another city for New Orleans, says the people are getting bluer [A:270].","citations":["D:114","D:127","D:233","D:37","D:81","A:222","A:247","D:207-208","MTM B13:398","A:198","D:31","MTM B5:163","A:270"],"tags":["Finnegan","Seas & Oceans","Women Characters","Visual Arts","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Blue Book (Official)","definition":"Casey’s imaginary record of his own future sporting greatness, supposedly issued in 2001 for his eightieth birthday. His mother finds the notebooks, mocks the Official Blue Book language and tears them up [A:157].","citations":["A:157"],"tags":["Dreams","Family","Literature"]},{"name":"Blue Cheese","definition":"One of the foods Mary Catherine Carruthers sets out for Finnegan when he visits her in Chicago, with beer, pigs’ feet and small smoked sausages [A:247].","citations":["A:247"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Blue Diamond","definition":"Almond-sized gem that Amoy swallows intending it as a gift for his pregnant magpie wife, in Ignatius Ti’s freely translated story of Amoy’s earlier life as a blackbird thief in Karachi [A:80]. Amoy cannot bring himself to surrender the diamond even when his master Ali ben Taife, also Lord Peter Petrof, cuts his throat to obtain it. Amoy becomes a fish; the master dies a year later and becomes a donkey hauling rocks in the diamond’s mine [A:81].","citations":["A:80","A:81"],"tags":["Folklore","Crime","Shapeshifting","Family","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Blue Eyes","definition":"Hans seems to read minds with his blue eyes [A:5]; Mary Jean has black hair and blue eyes [A:168].","citations":["A:5","A:168"],"tags":["Women Characters","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Blue Fields","definition":"Port of call listed between Cabo Gracias a Dios and Limon on the <em>Brunhilde</em>’s southward coastal itinerary [D:81]. The location and name identify it most plausibly with Bluefields, Nicaragua.","citations":["D:81"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors"]},{"name":"Blue-birds","definition":"Birds that fly like darts when Li Tai Po plays his flute in the novel’s verse narrative about him [A:221-222].","citations":["A:221-222"],"tags":["Music","Literature"]},{"name":"Blue-jowled romances","definition":"Gautier’s favored American adventure reading in the Harlow House library: blood-and-thunder tales of the Wild West, train robbers, buffalo hunters, Indian scalpers, bank robbers and brigands [A:198]. After reading French works aloud to his goats, he turns to these American books silently once the goats sleep [A:199].","citations":["A:198","A:199"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"Blue-moon person","definition":"Description of Teresa Piccone as a person encountered only once in a lifetime. The surrounding passage exaggerates the danger of looking too long at her brilliant beauty [MTM B5:163].","citations":["MTM B5:163"],"tags":["Women Characters"]},{"name":"Blue-nosed sinner","definition":"The narrator’s description of Chris McAbney, a hostile <em>Brunhilde</em> crewman. Anastasia mocks his Scottish identity with her “Scotch and shoder” pun, while the narrator calls him the worst kind of sinner [D:31].","citations":["D:31"],"tags":["Sin & Redemption","Ships","Crew & Sailors","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Bob","definition":"Resident regular at the Old Wooden Ship [D:215–216]. With Russ and Charleyhorse Heckel, he seeks out men willing to buy drinks for everyone and steers them to the tavern. The three also rob customers outside after they have finished spending but still have money [D:220].","citations":["D:215–216","D:220"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Crime"]},{"name":"Boerum, Benny (Bay Rum)","definition":"Benny Boerum, nicknamed Bay Rum, is one of the resident regulars at the Old Wooden Ship [D:216]. The narrator groups him with Raviola and Saddle Sore as unkempt and uncouth exceptions to the establishment’s supposedly high standards [D:224].","citations":["D:216","D:224"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Boethius (c. 480--524/525 AD)","definition":"Roman philosopher, Christian theologian and senator, apparently intended by the printed “Boëthus” in the narrator’s list of people with seven hidden years, beside Caesar, Diocletian, Carl the Great, Wellington, Lincoln, Sam Houston, George Barrow and Hans Schultz [MTM B2:72]. The proposed seven-year parallel belongs to the novel’s account of Duffey’s missing years.\n\nBoethius wrote <em>The Consolation of Philosophy</em> in prison before his execution. Its prose-and-verse dialogue with personified Philosophy draws on ancient philosophy and became influential in medieval thought; its relationship to Christianity is debated, since it makes no explicit Christian appeal. His Latin translations and commentaries transmitted Aristotelian logic, and his mathematical writings supplied material for the quadrivium of arithmetic, geometry, astronomy and music. Dates are approximate: standard accounts often give about 480 to 524, while other scholarship argues for about 475-477 to 526. The short notes on time in <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> also attribute to Boethius the distinction between the flowing instant that produces time and the abiding instant that produces eternity [MTM B13:469].","citations":["MTM B2:72","MTM B13:469"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Literary Figures","Italy","Theology","Music","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Bogovitch, I.N.C.G.P.","definition":"Inscription on the old gravestone that Finnegan shows Doll and X in the cemetery across from the club [D:275-277]. X interprets Bogovitch as “son of God,” a euphemism for Chortovitch, “son of the Devil,” and reconstructs the earlier occupant’s name as Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch Papadiabolous Chortovitch [D:277-278]. This is the old Ifreann, father of the two later Papadiabolous brothers, not simply another name for Papadiabolous I [D:285-286]. The grave is also Gregory McIfreann’s, the son whom X calls Papadiabolous I [D:284-285]. Finnegan’s earlier burial account explains the shared grave: he and Seaworthy place a new corpse above the bones of an older occupant [D:76].","citations":["D:275-277","D:277-278","D:285-286","D:284-285","D:76"],"tags":["The Devil","Devils (Characters)","The Dead","Family","Identity","Language & Words","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Bogs","definition":"Part of the wild New Jersey country through which Finnegan wanders before meeting Gautier the goat-man. The description begins with bogs and bog-men, then names the Barrens, mountains, and sea [A:193-194].","citations":["A:193-194"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Bohemian","definition":"A Bohemian brew is one of the four beers Patrick Stranahan considers sufficient, with an American, a Danish and Guinness [A:110]. Bohemian is also one strain of the Angelino clan, whose members are described as predominantly Italian and German, with Mexican and Irish Tinker ancestry too [A:218]. In the account of the artistic and political patrons who might support Finnegan, he can be a Bohemian with Bohemians as readily as a drinker among drinkers [A:184].","citations":["A:110","A:218","A:184"],"tags":["Alcohol","Eastern Europe","Family","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Bohemians","definition":"In Sally’s argument with Marie, Bohemia supplies the disputed heresy that Sally says Marie does not understand; she asks who the true heretics are [A:8]. In a different sense, the Bohemes are the unconventional artistic circle Hans enters in New York: James Fitzjames and Trinali Peterson are the two Bohemes who dine with him, and he subsequently accompanies the wider group [A:225-226] [A:236].","citations":["A:8","A:225-226","A:236"],"tags":["Theology","Eastern Europe","Literature"]},{"name":"Boil","definition":"Honeybucket Kincaid lets the water, described as boiling with fish activity, settle for ten seconds between electrically summoning the gar and summoning Blooper Fish [MTM B13:417]. Doll Delancy jokingly wishes to boil Finnegan in oil when he proposes painting her instead of continuing their boisterous evening [D:173].","citations":["MTM B13:417","D:173"],"tags":["Fishing","Finnegan","Women Characters","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Bolo Manolo","definition":"Named as the double of Ignatius Cardinal Ti of Manila in Track and Total’s list of thirteen shadow-men [MB:578-579]. He travels from Bimini with Douglas McAfee and Lloyd Cardigan-Pembroke on a luxury yacht, speaks to the dolphin about the vanished Blood-of-Martyrs Grass, and reaches Mooring-Stone with the gathering shadow-men [MB:594-595]. The later passage inconsistently calls Lloyd, previously paired with Cardinal Lloyd Spencer, the double of the dead Ti [MB:579] [MB:594].","citations":["MB:578-579","MB:594-595","MB:579","MB:594"],"tags":["Identity","Ecclesiastical","Ships","Crime"]},{"name":"Bologna","definition":"Finnegan’s comparison for an inside-out tiger, a huge mass of bologna encased in its own intestinal membrane. He says it would resemble Papa Devil, who replies that he is speaking of himself and intends to become a tiger again [D:122].","citations":["D:122"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Finnegan","Food & Dining","Shapeshifting"]},{"name":"Bolshevestic and Atheistic Communism","definition":"The danger named in an epigraph attributed to Pius XI’s <em>Atheistic Communism</em>, which sets modern revolution within the continuing struggle between good and evil after the Fall [MTM B5:177]. The passage comes from sections 2-3 of the 1937 encyclical <em>Divini Redemptoris</em>; the novel’s “Bolshevestic” differs from the official English “bolshevistic.”","citations":["MTM B5:177"],"tags":["Popes","Catholic Church","Sin & Redemption","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Bonaventure's (St.)","definition":"Casey’s boarding high school, forty miles from his home; he lives in St. Martin’s Hall [A:161]. Its school paper, <em>The Towers</em>, is dominated by Gregory Hall, while the St. Martin’s boys produce the manuscript rival <em>Chambers</em>. Their individually drawn chamber-pot mastheads parody Gregory’s towers. This rivalry supplies the earlier model for Casey’s later <em>Crock</em> magazines [A:167].","citations":["A:161","A:167"],"tags":["Literature","Journalism"]},{"name":"Bond","definition":"The restraints Don Lewis ties around the wrists and ankles of the dead Yokipoki before burying him. Awakened by noises, Don imagines the corpse cutting its bonds with the buried butcher knife; the disturbance and apparent attack prove to be rats [D:46-47].","citations":["D:46-47"],"tags":["Death & Mortality","Weapons"]},{"name":"Bone","definition":"Hans’s joking argument contrasts supposedly scarce ancient fingers with commonly found jawbones, brainpans and pelvises [A:4-5]. Finnegan feels terror melt his bones at Papa Devil’s presence [D:15], and feels an electric shock from Anastasia’s bones [D:20]. He identifies shapes by touch among the older remains in the grave where he and Seaworthy bury a new corpse [D:76]. X says the Papadiabolous brothers have indistinguishable skeletons and facial bones despite their differing fleshed appearances [D:286].\n\nDuffey’s killers strip his bones and separately burn his flesh to ashes. X and Casey recover both, and the bones are placed aboard the <em>Argo</em>. God subsequently confirms that Duffey may continue as a Master without flesh [MTM B13:442-443].","citations":["A:4-5","D:15","D:20","D:76","D:286","MTM B13:442-443"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Devils (Characters)","Women Characters","Death & Mortality","Argonautica"]},{"name":"Bone-Bleached World","definition":"The exclamation of an imagined future professor who recognizes an Australian femur found in Turkestan thirty thousand years later. The narrator uses the fancy to exaggerate Tom Shire and Freddy Castle’s unmistakably Australian appearance [A:5].","citations":["A:5"],"tags":["Australia","Time & Eternity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Bone-meal","definition":"One of the products John Mogul says his murder business makes from victims’ bodies when practicable, alongside soap and essential oils. He describes himself as a knacker and euthanasiast, and specifies bone-meal for farmers [MB:573].","citations":["MB:573"],"tags":["Crime","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Bones","definition":"Hans, not Finnegan, jokes that prehistoric fingers are harder to find than jawbones, brainpans and pelvises [A:4-5]. Trinali imagines the driftwood shore as an elephants’ graveyard, with larger pieces becoming the bones of mammoths, behemoths and Leviathan; these are images for timber, not excavated animal remains [A:232].\n\nFinnegan feels Anastasia’s bones like an electric shock [D:20], handles an older skeleton while digging the grave for the first Papa Devil [D:76], and later hears X explain that the Papadiabolous twins have the same skeletal and facial-bone structure [D:286]. X brings living Duffey his own future ashes in an urn [MTM B6:203]. In the later account of their recovery, X and Casey identify Duffey’s bones and ashes separately; X keeps the ashes, and both men carry the bones to the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:442-443].","citations":["A:4-5","A:232","D:20","D:76","D:286","MTM B6:203","MTM B13:442-443"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Devils (Characters)","Women Characters","Death & Mortality","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Bonfire","definition":"In Ignacio’s account of Joseph of Maintz, the eighth-century priest fires himself to the moon and promises a bonfire every full moon as proof that he still lives; the fires continue for ninety years [A:271]. At the Bay Hotel picnic the management forbids a lawn bonfire, so a waitress lights charcoal briquettes on a broad trencher-plate for the party’s singing [A:29-31].","citations":["A:271","A:29-31"],"tags":["Folklore","Music"]},{"name":"Bong","definition":"The sound or action of Dotty ringing a bell to draw people to Duffey’s great soup crock. A small kettle nearby receives contributions [A:133].","citations":["A:133"],"tags":["Women Characters","Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Bonifacients, True","definition":"The narrator’s name for the seven original trustees and directors of the <em>Bark</em>: Henri Salvatore, Melchisedech Duffey, Absalom Stein, Dotty Yekouris, Hans Schultz, Draja Gabrielovitch and John Solli (Finnegan). They are also the Seven Pillars of Righteousness; later deaths require replacements [MTM B7:214].","citations":["MTM B7:214"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Women Characters","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Bonner, Calvin","definition":"One of the sporty friends in Duffey and Letitia’s North Chicago golden-age circle, listed beside golfers Mike and Peggy Conner and tennis players Peter and Jenny Reid [MTM B3:98-99].","citations":["MTM B3:98-99"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Booby-hatch","definition":"Private Gregory’s disparaging name for the army psychiatric ward in which he is already confined. He fears years of observation after authorities link his young Australian identity to the fingerprints and papers of a vanished elderly American colonel [A:79].","citations":["A:79"],"tags":["Soldiers","Identity"]},{"name":"Boodle","definition":"X’s term for the suitcase money. After retrieving it from the locker and leaving a decoy, he divides it among three bags: Finnegan’s sturdy cowhide bag gets the heaviest share, Doll’s chic overnight bag a smaller share, and X keeps the nondescript travel bag [D:178-180].","citations":["D:178-180"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Money & Trade","Crime"]},{"name":"Booger","definition":"One of the Teras types Duffey lists with Gargoyles, Neanderthals, Vagaries and Variants. He says everyone has some variant blood and that he put more than usual into his own creations [MTM B5:169-170].","citations":["MTM B5:169-170"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Duffey","Creation"]},{"name":"Boogerme","definition":"Earlier heading truncated the source word boogermen. See <em>Boogermen</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Finnegan","Monsters & Creatures","Identity"]},{"name":"Boogermen","definition":"The source reads “boogermen”: frightening figures into which Finnegan asks how his ancient people, once the inventors of childhood, have been transformed in others’ judgment [D:213].","citations":["D:213"],"tags":["Finnegan","Monsters & Creatures","Identity"]},{"name":"Book Clubs","definition":"Mary Catherine Carruthers tells Finnegan that she belongs to three book clubs, among the cultural and social pursuits she has taken up while living alone and unable to get over Casey. She describes herself as improving her mind [A:249].","citations":["A:249"],"tags":["Women Characters","Literature","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Book Dealers","definition":"Duffey and John Rattigan buy and sell about a hundred books a week, new as well as used, during their boarding-school trading days. Sebastian Hilton is their best book customer [MTM B1:25].","citations":["MTM B1:25"],"tags":["Duffey","Literature","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Book of Life","definition":"Finnegan invokes the biblical register of the saved when he asks Duffey to strike out diners who refuse his gifts of oysters at the Broadway Oyster House. Duffey plays along and says he has done so [MTM B5:172-174]. The image of erasing a name recalls Revelation 3:5.","citations":["MTM B5:172-174"],"tags":["New Testament","Scripture","Finnegan","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Book Store","definition":"Duffey adds a book and art business to Gabriel Szymansky’s Chicago shops, and prepares to live with Letitia above the new bookstore [MTM B3:81-82] [MTM B3:91-92]. Casey later prints the last of his early <em>Crock</em> magazines in its back room [A:168]. Mary Catherine Carruthers is among the young visitors associated with the store [MTM B3:99]. In New Orleans Duffey again runs a secondhand bookstore, together with art, antique and pawn businesses [A:143].","citations":["MTM B3:81-82","MTM B3:91-92","A:168","MTM B3:99","A:143"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Women Characters","Literature","Occupations"]},{"name":"Bookies","definition":"The bookmakers taking bets on Hans Schultz’s beer-drinking contest with Joe Bushmaster. The early odds favor Bushmaster eight to five; as Hans establishes a lead, betting shifts to him [A:18] [A:21].","citations":["A:18","A:21"],"tags":["Alcohol","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Booklet (Instruction)","definition":"Zabotski’s description of a rock drawing and accompanying writing that he believes to be the oldest building manual. He has obtained several copies, thinks it explains a great barn or castle, and uses it for the contraption in his yard. He identifies the undeciphered language as Hazh-Khazh and says he must decipher it while building [MTM B9:272].","citations":["MTM B9:272"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Books Abroad","definition":"A section of the <em>Crock</em> written by Polly Polyglot, the pen name of Mary Frances Rattigan [A:41].","citations":["A:41"],"tags":["Women Characters","Literature","Journalism"]},{"name":"Boondocks","definition":"Marie’s term for the extreme backcountry where her uncle Shakes Pearson supposedly writes tobacco-contest verses on bark with oak-ball juice because paper is unavailable [A:25]. Henry Salvatore’s later parish offers a parallel image of remoteness: it lies so deep in the swamps that he numbers muskrats among his parishioners [MTM B7:214].","citations":["A:25","MTM B7:214"],"tags":["Folklore","Louisiana","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Boots","definition":"Don Lewis removes Yokipoki’s boots before burial so that the dead man may rest comfortably [D:46]. Finnegan and Doll wear rubber boots and swimsuits during their shallow-lake fishing holiday [D:181]. Tommy Cow-Town Borger and Jake-Leg Lewis retain cowboy boots, spurs, chaps and gauntlets although their work is aboard cattle boats [D:222]. Related shoe references show Seaworthy promising new footwear to the ragged Finnegan, whose feet have been bleeding, and Finnegan subsequently buying shoes [D:10-11] [D:17].","citations":["D:46","D:181","D:222","D:10-11","D:17"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Fishing","Crew & Sailors","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Boots and Boot-jacks","definition":"Ingredients in one of Shakes Pearson’s unfinished tobacco-contest limericks, along with old sacks. In Marie’s tale he repeatedly leaves off writing to get whisky, and squirrels eat the bark on which he writes [A:25-26].","citations":["A:25-26"],"tags":["Folklore","Literature","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Borg (Surname)","definition":"One of the Angelino clan’s family names, listed with Obermeyer, Viskochil, Trevino and Larrigan [A:218].","citations":["A:218"],"tags":["Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Borger, Tommy (Cow Town)","definition":"Tommy Borger, nicknamed Cow Town, is a resident regular of the Old Wooden Ship [D:216]. With Jake-Leg Lewis he is a saltwater cowboy who rides cattle boats rather than horses. The two keep wearing boots, spurs, chaps and gauntlets through the long intervals between increasingly rare cattle voyages [D:222].","citations":["D:216","D:222"],"tags":["Ships","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Borgne (Lake)","definition":"Lake on whose Pristine Cove Zabotski and Wife Waldo establish their enormous house [MTM B9:267]. Zabotski calls it open-mouthed because it leads into the Gulf [MTM B9:289]. His two-hundred-acre property, Zabotski Flats, is about two-thirds freshwater and one-third salt meadows, with the boat-castle anchored there [MTM B12:362].","citations":["MTM B9:267","MTM B9:289","MTM B12:362"],"tags":["Louisiana","Ships"]},{"name":"Boston","definition":"One of the cities where Duffey lives with relatives in the Iowa-based version of his childhood, alongside Dubuque, Sioux City, Council Bluffs, Omaha, Kansas City, St. Louis and Chicago. He later often claims to come from Boston [MTM B1:7] [MTM B1:9]. PFC Timothy Lorrigan is from Boston [D:44], as is Emmet Collins, Cardinal Merry’s double [MB:579]. Casey says infiltrates come from Boston to heckle Duffey at communion breakfasts [A:42-43]. Finnegan’s puzzlement at being called a Yankee includes his lack of the supposed Bostonian speech affliction [A:273].","citations":["MTM B1:7","MTM B1:9","D:44","MB:579","A:42-43","A:273"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan","History & Memory","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Boston Irish","definition":"The narrator describes Duffey’s arresting voice as Boston Irish with a slight admixture of stage Yiddish during the St. Louis reunion [A:97].","citations":["A:97"],"tags":["Ireland","Duffey","Theater","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Bostonian affliction","definition":"An unspecified supposed fault of Boston speech that the narrator says Finnegan lacks, among the reasons he is puzzled by Ignacio calling him a Yankee [A:273].","citations":["A:273"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Bottle","definition":"Finnegan credits Italians with inventing the bottle and the fork for extracting olives; Vincent prefers an Irish claim [A:5]. Before the <em>Brunhilde</em> sails, Finnegan figuratively hides in a bottle to escape a real threat to his life [D:27]. Empty beer bottles become gasoline lamps in the army camp [A:39]. Other bottles hold Black Label at the Buffalo Bar [A:66], or the Canadian whisky Duffey carries to Chicago during Prohibition [MTM B3:77].\n\nOn Naxos, Finnegan and Anastasia buy a wineskin and propose leaving it in a forked bush on the mountain [D:126]. Finnegan later takes gin uphill as protection against forebodings, carries the bottle through the disaster and Anastasia’s death, and cannot bring himself to discard it [D:130] [D:148]. In Paradise, fifty cents buys a bottle [D:235]. The four friends name successive bottles Agatha, Bathildes, Clotilda, Delphina, Etheldreda, Faustina, Georgina and Hildegarde, eventually opening Idabel before sleeping [D:243] [D:256].\n\nIgnatius Ti says a falling pill bottle near a corpse can make a frightened attendant blame the dead man [A:85-86]. Show Boat’s story of an unidentified tippling Apostle describes wine bottles mysteriously replaced with water [A:262]. An epigraph credited to Belloc’s “Heroic Poem in Praise of Wine” imagines bottling God the Father in a flask [MTM B5:176].","citations":["A:5","D:27","A:39","A:66","MTM B3:77","D:126","D:130","D:148","D:235","D:243","D:256","A:85-86","A:262","MTM B5:176"],"tags":["Alcohol","Finnegan","Duffey","Women Characters","Ships","Folklore","Literature","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Bottomless Unconscious","definition":"In the narrator’s fantastic account of Black Papua, the Papuans are the Well of the World: other peoples and places exist within their bottomless unconscious and depend on its continued support [A:37].","citations":["A:37"],"tags":["Dreams","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Boulders","definition":"Henry and Casey sit on two boulders after swimming in a waterfall pool in the New Guinea jungle, continuing their conversation about Duffey [A:42].","citations":["A:42"],"tags":["Pacific Theater"]},{"name":"Boule, Dan","definition":"Daniel Jean Boule, also spelled Boulle, is named as the double of Henri Cardinal Salvatore of New Orleans. Henri quotes a prophetic account in which this double travels up the Mexican coast by tramp steamer after the Cardinal’s death; Henri instead proposes to survive and travel as the double of his dead cousin Dan [MTM B12:387-388]. The Mooring-Stone narrative names Boulle among the thirteen doubles and describes that steamer journey to the conclave [MB:579] [MB:592-593] [MB:596].","citations":["MTM B12:387-388","MB:579","MB:592-593","MB:596"],"tags":["Identity","Ecclesiastical","Ships","The Dead"]},{"name":"Boundary","definition":"Finnegan describes his personal boundaries as indefinite when Dopey’s face persists in his shaving mirror [D:188]. Elsewhere he laments a barrier between himself and human beings despite their friendship and love [D:90]. In the Great Day Contingency, the abolition of boundaries threatens individuality itself and results in the obliteration of persons [MTM B10:298].","citations":["D:188","D:90","MTM B10:298"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","Apocalyptic","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Bounty Hunters","definition":"After killing the thirteen principal Cardinals in hiding, Track and Total offers bounties for their surviving doubles. The shadow-men converge on the waters around Mooring-Stone, where hunters operate on land and sea [MB:589-590]. The hunters try to close their trap after the <em>Argo</em> and other vessels enter, but changing shoals wreck their attack [MB:595].","citations":["MB:589-590","MB:595"],"tags":["Crime","Seas & Oceans","Finnegan","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Bow","definition":"Each diner on Zabotski’s ark has a small bow and arrows for attacking the dangerous creatures in the slumgullion. One child has trained midget octopuses to shoot back, with lethal poisoned arrows of their own [MTM B12:364-365]. In the epigraph credited to Hiram Hunt’s <em>The Death of the Species</em>, the taut bow represents a balance between competing elements of humanity; destroying either wood or string destroys the whole [D:283-284].","citations":["MTM B12:364-365","D:283-284"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Ships","Weapons","Death & Mortality","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Bownee","definition":"One of Gautier’s seven youngest goat kids, listed with Luin, Mairt, Keadin, Ordin, Haoine and Sahirn. The goat-man gives this group hebdomadal, or weekly, names; Luin is subsequently glossed as Monday [A:194-195].","citations":["A:194-195"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Box","definition":"Boxes serve as objects, receptacles and metaphors across the cycle. The roadhouse music box plays “Seems Like Old Times” and “It’s a Great Big World” [A:99]. Falo and Nauti furnish their new cabaret with one large box as a table and two small ones as chairs [A:64]; Captain Sammy borrows a shoeshine box without its owners noticing, earns money and returns it [A:67]. Askandan keeps his foul, sticky soap base in a boxcar [A:256]. Childhood Duffey knows which households keep box-elder or elm in their wood boxes [MTM B1:17]. Sebastian Hilton’s bait box is figurative, his collection of tricks for provoking bullies into fights [MTM B1:22].\n\nX brings Duffey nested black, gray and brown boxes. The brown one holds the <em>Crock</em>’s infiltrate cards, which X admits stealing; Duffey introduces an apparently real version of X’s old disappearing-red-box trick, then replaces it with roses [MTM B6:201-203]. X also brings the cigar container or urn holding Duffey’s own ashes [MTM B6:185] [MTM B6:203]. The safety-deposit box sought by Charlotte and Duffey is the Chicago stash associated with Aga Gonof, and Charlotte later calls it a walk-in [MTM B3:84-86]. Duffey notices the theft of his box of war medals during the coming-leader affair [MTM B4:115-116].\n\nThe bony Duffey regains flesh and a working voice box on voyages that fall earlier than his death [MTM B13:444]. Stein has requested burial in a plain pine box with a lighted cigar [GD:493]. Mail is supposedly waiting for Finnegan in a call box [D:267]. Advertisements in the <em>Crock</em> give box numbers for ordering consecrated hosts or stolen dead bodies [PG:524] [PG:546]. Angela Cosquin has a copper cash box inscribed “Angela Cosquin sa Caisse” and keeps it with the sunken treasure ships, adding a saved sovereign each month [D:262].","citations":["A:99","A:64","A:67","A:256","MTM B1:17","MTM B1:22","MTM B6:201-203","MTM B6:185","MTM B6:203","MTM B3:84-86","MTM B4:115-116","MTM B13:444","GD:493","D:267","PG:524","PG:546","D:262"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Finnegan","Music","Money & Trade","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Box Car","definition":"Freight car holding Asking Dan’s eighty-three thousand pounds of unscented soap base. The substance becomes sticky and foul-smelling when wet; Hillary Hilton offers twenty cents per hundred pounds, but Dan refuses to sell [A:256].","citations":["A:256"],"tags":["Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Box-elder wood","definition":"One household’s firewood, contrasted with another’s elm, in the account of young Duffey’s extraordinarily detailed awareness of other homes. The narrator says such knowledge combines observation and memory with theft from other minds [MTM B1:17].","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Duffey","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Boxer","definition":"The Stranahans are a sporting family of amateur boxers, and old boxers are among Patrick’s visitors [A:103-104]. Finnegan has boxed and habitually carried his left low to draw fire [D:172]. His extravagant fighting reminiscences include Harry Greb, Joe Gans and Tom Sharky, followed by comparisons with Harry Wills and Battling Siki [D:191-192]. Bascom Bagby fights professional bouts, and introduces young Duffey to a preliminary contest with Dandy Dan Dillard [MTM B2:50]. Berny Cacciatore is a boxing and sports historian among the Royal Pop Historians [MTM B11:322].","citations":["A:103-104","D:172","D:191-192","MTM B2:50","MTM B11:322"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Combat","Family"]},{"name":"Boy (Anastasia's cousin)","definition":"Anastasia’s young cousin on Naxos. He intercepts the noisy Finnegan descending the mountain after the shooting, tells him Anastasia has instructed him to hide, and warns that hunters mean to kill him [D:144]. He reports the armed men, the dead in the streets, and the danger to Finnegan, the big man and the South American; he later identifies his relationship to Anastasia, says she is dead, and describes the sunken smaller boat and Manuel’s search for Papa Devil [D:145-146].","citations":["D:144","D:145-146"],"tags":["Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Combat","Family","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Boy (Malayan)","definition":"The unnamed Malayan boy who guides the Sergeant, Finnegan, through the shifting journey after his ward experience. He says the Sergeant has spent long enough in the Green Islands and must grow up, brings him to Sydney, and directs him to the Plaza Hotel while he visits the Malayan and Indonesian Club [A:88]. Later, back in the islands, they travel by canoe. The boy supplies the names <em>Aral</em>, <em>Bumi</em> and <em>Aragh</em>, which the Sergeant takes to mean the <em>Argo</em>; they dive into extraordinarily wet water by the coral reefs [A:89].","citations":["A:88","A:89"],"tags":["Ships","Islands","Australia","Finnegan","Time & Eternity","Identity"]},{"name":"Boy King, The","definition":"Melchisedech Duffey’s primordial childhood identity. The narrator calls the Boy King’s thousands of years the solid basis of Duffey’s other lives, and his tents, flocks, rivers and groves more real than the landscapes of his later childhood [MTM B1:17]. The golden-handed king feeds visitors with bread and wine, summons visible and invisible giants to release waters or overthrow rival kings, helps physicians cure the sick, and turns stones into birds. Usually concealed by an “urchin disguise,” he distributes replenishing talismans that confer gifts and help create the Duffey Nation [MTM B1:18]. As an eight-year-old he commands a giant to ring a church bell [MTM B1:15]. His later displays include golden sparks and gold coins [MTM B2:38] [MTM B5:167]. In a dream he calls himself the Boy King or Boy Magician and makes clay Paradise Birds; their destruction leads Beth to interpret the dream as doubt in his powers and creations [MTM B2:67].","citations":["MTM B1:17","MTM B1:18","MTM B1:15","MTM B2:38","MTM B5:167","MTM B2:67"],"tags":["Miracles","Magic & Sorcery","Giants & Titans","Duffey","Identity","Creation","Dreams"]},{"name":"Boy Magician","definition":"Duffey’s name for himself, alongside Boy King, in a dream he relates to Beth. He makes clay birds, turns them to living gold and sends them flying as Paradise Birds. When rough men shoot them down, they fall as clay pigeons. Beth interprets the dream as doubt in his powers and creations [MTM B2:67].","citations":["MTM B2:67"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Dreams","Creation","Identity"]},{"name":"Brace of steins","definition":"A pair of beer steins, in the mock Australian request Tom Shire coaches Finnegan to use at the wet canteen: “Hoy soy, dyg, me moit and oi need a broice of stoins.” It forms part of the attempt to pass as Australians [A:12].","citations":["A:12"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Bracelets","definition":"Golden bracelets and jangles in Finnegan’s song for Elena, whose sound he compares to the Tambuls of Spain as she tends his battered face [A:274].","citations":["A:274"],"tags":["Spain","Finnegan","Music"]},{"name":"Brain","definition":"Brain and mind imagery ranges from anatomy to psychic power and damaged identity. Hans’s comic argument about ancient forks cites surviving jawbones, brain pans and pelvises rather than fingers [A:5]. An epigraph credited to Carmody Overlark’s <em>Studies in the Imagination</em> contrasts the modern forebrain with a supposedly greater Neanderthal back-brain, associates the latter with unconscious and ghostly powers, and adduces Plato, Homer and Aquinas among its examples [D:270-272]. The narrator’s fantastic account of Papua similarly makes other peoples dependent on the Papuans’ bottomless unconscious [A:37]. Sulem distinguishes the tujuan, or personal directions, supposedly located under a brain lobe, in the liver or in the diaphragm, from the semangat, or soul; he places both in the diaphragm himself [A:60]. In Don Lewis’s Big House tale, the inhabitants consume visitors’ brains and other organs [D:48].\n\nDuffey enters Lily Koch’s mind, where she recognizes him and invites a reciprocal intimacy of minds [MTM B1:29]. Newton Prescott may require brain surgery if writing and burning his journal fails to free him from compulsive memories [CM:503]. Stein explains his supposed soul exchange with Casey as a trade of interior goblins, kabouters, that share their former owners’ secrets [PG:537-538]. In the Great Day, Stein’s once plentiful brains have metaphorically evaporated [GD:493]. Finnegan’s resurrected brain retains his old identity despite the new assignment and modified identity he receives [MB:554]. Duffey’s returning life appears to him as life in the past, an effect of the <em>Argo</em>’s ambiguous relation to time [MTM B13:445].\n\nHillary Hilton deals figuratively in distressed brains [A:255]. Seaworthy’s shovel spills part of the first Papa Devil’s brain [D:75]. Finnegan resumes using his human brain after the ape-cat escape [D:165], and describes his own indefinite boundaries after seeing Dopey in his mirror [D:188]. Brannagan calls the world’s selective idiocy an epidemic spread by a man-sized germ [D:202].","citations":["A:5","D:270-272","A:37","A:60","D:48","MTM B1:29","CM:503","PG:537-538","GD:493","MB:554","MTM B13:445","A:255","D:75","D:165","D:188","D:202"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Finnegan","Duffey","History & Memory","Dreams","Resurrection","Identity","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Brain Damage","definition":"Biloxi Brannagan’s diagnosis of the world’s selective idiocy. He describes a recurring infection carried by a man-sized germ and calls the parasite “the alien within” [D:202-203].","citations":["D:202-203"],"tags":["Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Brambles","definition":"The brambles on the sheer Naxos mountainside through which Finnegan makes his hazardous descent, and the bushes he bursts through while fleeing downhill after the shots [D:141] [D:144].","citations":["D:141","D:144"],"tags":["Islands","Greece","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Branch","definition":"The Frio is named as a river branch in Finnegan’s confused account of his travels [A:202]. In Don Lewis’s tale a ta’a nut tree grows a branch to Ali’s window and enables his escape from the Big House [D:49]; old gnarled branches are among the features of the Naxos mountaintop [D:132]. Margaret Stone uses branch for the Stein side of the family [MTM B4:112], while Charlotte Garfield says she and Duffey belong to closely related branches of the old people [MTM B8:260]. The child hero proposes a forked peach branch for reading an object’s history, and Duffey has such a red-peach dowser’s stick [MTM B11:326]. In their shared prayer Duffey responds to Finnegan’s shaken-earth verse with “Heal its branches, for it quakes” [MTM B12:375].","citations":["A:202","D:49","D:132","MTM B4:112","MTM B8:260","MTM B11:326","MTM B12:375"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Family","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Brannagan, Gertrude","definition":"Biloxi Brannagan’s hospitable wife. Finnegan meets her at their Biloxi house, mistaking the private kitchen garden for a beer garden, and she adopts him on sight [D:199-200]. A full-built, dark, slightly Frenchy woman of forty-five, give or take fifteen years, she is described as a fortress and “Living Gothic” [D:200]. She supplies rice ribby, huckleberry and greenberry wine, perry, pecan pancakes, roast pork and turtle chowder [D:208], and arranges the transfer of Finnegan’s impounded car in exchange for her nearly worthless one [D:209]. Finnegan kisses her goodbye once, then four or five times more [D:210]. Duffey recalls her expansive kisses to both himself and Biloxi [MTM B13:410].","citations":["D:199-200","D:200","D:208","D:209","D:210","MTM B13:410"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Women Characters","Alcohol","Food & Dining","Family"]},{"name":"Brannagan, John Joseph Biloxi","definition":"John Joseph is Biloxi Brannagan’s proper given name, as Gertrude reveals while speaking to Finnegan [D:201].","citations":["D:201"],"tags":["Identity","Family"]},{"name":"Brass","definition":"Metal used for the bright stops and frets of the Coryphaena fishes’ shell horns [MTM B13:450], and for the stop-keys and filigree of Duffey’s handmade recorder [MTM B6:190]. Brass is also part of the figurative alloy of Betty Hochstapler’s heart, along with vanadium steel, phosphor bronze and a warmer glowing metal [A:216]. Duffey initially mistakes the nail holding the Royal Pop Historians’ parchment for brass or bronze; it is copper-iron, of old Macedonian workmanship [MTM B11:313].","citations":["MTM B13:450","MTM B6:190","A:216","MTM B11:313"],"tags":["Music","Duffey","Women Characters","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Brave","definition":"Finnegan speaks “bravely” when making his rehearsed request for brews to Sergeant Bushmaster [A:13]. The Old Wooden Ship’s fifty regulars are called a brave company [D:216]. X says brave men blanch at “flogging a dead horse,” his figure for pursuing an unfashionable conspiracy inquiry [A:139]. A darker related word appears when ladies send their bravos to take gruesome souvenirs from the severed head of Paul the Eleventh [MB:562].","citations":["A:13","D:216","A:139","MB:562"],"tags":["Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","The Dead","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Brawler","definition":"Henry Salvatore calls himself a lecher, drunkard, glutton and brawler [A:38]. Duffey arrives from Chicago as a “high-sounding brawler,” amid the company’s pre-wedding gatherings [A:97]. Bagby brawls throughout St. Louis, including vicious fights in which he has left a man for dead and been left for dead himself [MTM B2:62].","citations":["A:38","A:97","MTM B2:62"],"tags":["Duffey","Alcohol","Combat","Identity"]},{"name":"Brazil","definition":"Brazil is the western end of the Atlantic crossing discussed aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em>. After sailing from Natal to Freetown, the narrator says a ship should go from Africa to Brazil, where westward drift assists it; Papadiabolous claims many earlier peoples made that westward passage and that returning was much harder [D:89]. X includes Brazil among countries where Irish teachers could give English an Irish accent [D:279]. It is also the surname of Cicero Brazil among Duffey’s Chicago acquaintances [MTM B3:99].","citations":["D:89","D:279","MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Ships","Seas & Oceans","Africa","Ireland","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Brazil, Cicero","definition":"One of the college and university people in Duffey’s Chicago circle during its golden age [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Bread","definition":"Bread and wine introduce Melchisedech in the Genesis 14:18 epigraph to Book Two [MTM B2:38]. Young Duffey stocks ten loaves of black bread for his new establishment [MTM B2:41]. Among his Seven Christs is a wheat-and-rye Danish bread head, baked and varnished, which the narrator connects with Corpus Christi loaves [MTM B3:103]. In Ignacio’s moon-voyage story Father Joseph packs bread and wine, wheat and grape cuttings for celebrating Mass [A:271]. The Bohemes eat tough fish with old bread from Lupido’s, and Trinali claims she could feed a multitude [A:234]. Finnegan unloads bread and other provisions at Anastasia’s grandmother’s house [D:126]. Duffey orders pond-scum bread at the extraordinary marine restaurant [MTM B13:417]. Gautier, by contrast, does not bother with bread but boils wheat and eats it with milk and honey [A:197].","citations":["MTM B2:38","MTM B2:41","MTM B3:103","A:271","A:234","D:126","MTM B13:417","A:197"],"tags":["Old Testament","Liturgy","Food & Dining","Duffey","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Bread and Wine Room","definition":"An intimate room aboard the <em>Argo</em> where meaningful gatherings take place [MTM B13:403]. It later houses the recovered Lord’s Table from the Cenaculum in Jerusalem [MTM B13:422]. Its name recalls the bread and wine of the Melchisedech epigraph [MTM B2:38]. Duffey also has an earlier small Bread and Wine Room at his St. Louis establishment, where he dines with friends [MTM B2:58].","citations":["MTM B13:403","MTM B13:422","MTM B2:38","MTM B2:58"],"tags":["Ships","Duffey","Liturgy","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Breakwater","definition":"The Naxos breakwater has a cafe where Finnegan breakfasts with Anastasia and Papadiabolous after confession and Mass [D:120]. Later, someone tells Finnegan that Anastasia fell from the breakwater when shot and was already dead when pulled from the water [D:147].","citations":["D:120","D:147"],"tags":["Liturgy","Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Women Characters","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Breathing","definition":"Finnegan hears Marie Courtois’s ecstatic breathing through her hotel-room door before finding her in a cataleptic rapture [D:112]. In the island hammocks, a voice from the sleeping Don Barnaby asks Finnegan to repay Anastasia fifty dollars, and later says it has been dead in the sand for three years; Barnaby’s vigorous, regular breathing is uninterrupted on both occasions [D:238] [D:256]. On the <em>Argo</em> all breathing stops immediately before the Great One appears, then resumes at a different pace [MTM B13:461-462].","citations":["D:112","D:238","D:256","MTM B13:461-462"],"tags":["Ships","Finnegan","Ghosts & Spirits","Dreams","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Breeze","definition":"A breeze and a bloom are among the narrator’s comic requirements for an earthly paradise [D:237]. At the bar Finnegan and his companions call Sloppy Joe’s, a light blue breeze ruffles golden air [D:235]. In New Orleans the new breeze of the coming Splendor carries putridity and the smell of change [MTM B11:313]; after the Opera House killings, it blows beneath a jeweled sky, described as new and unlegacied [MTM B11:351].","citations":["D:237","D:235","MTM B11:313","MTM B11:351"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","New Orleans","Bars & Taverns","Theater"]},{"name":"Brendon, Saint (Brendan the Navigator)","definition":"Brendan the Navigator, the sixth-century Irish abbot associated with legendary Atlantic voyages. The Chapter Nine epigraph in <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> attributes an account of unredeemable, long-lived species to “Saint Brendon, Trachtas” [D:130]. The title means a treatise, rather than voyages. The <em>Argo</em>’s talking-oak history identifies its <em>Land of Behest</em> phase with St. Brandon’s voyage from Ireland to America, the great fish Jascoyne and the transport of Judas [MTM B13:404]. Biloxi Brannagan identifies himself as an <em>Argo</em> Master by producing a red-gold coin bearing a Celtic cross, a coracle and the name “Naomh Brandon” [MTM B13:425]. These are the cycle’s transformations of the Brendan tradition, not proof of a historical voyage to America.","citations":["D:130","MTM B13:404","MTM B13:425"],"tags":["Saints","Folklore","Ships","Sailing","Ireland","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Brewery horses","definition":"Edward Ranwick’s disparaging comparison for the sturdy girls of the Bavarian Club. The narrator rejects it, calling them powerful but trim; Helen Platner is one of the girls with whom Duffey is friendly [MTM B2:58].","citations":["MTM B2:58"],"tags":["Germany","Women Characters","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Brews","definition":"Finnegan asks Sergeant Bushmaster for a “clutch of brews” while trying to pass as an Australian [A:13]. Patrick Stranahan’s compressed philosophy allows four brews: an American, a Danish, a Bohemian and Guinness [A:110].","citations":["A:13","A:110"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Brhzhlozh","definition":"The intuitive music-writing machine credited with the Bear Ballet at the Decatur Street Opera House. Mary Virginia says Brhzhlozh is only a machine, and Cyrus Roundhead agrees; he explains that the splendid people rejected even keeping a few unmutated humans as composers [MTM B11:349-350]. The machine’s music accompanies the cruel spectacle of bears, net wielders and human victims.","citations":["MTM B11:349-350"],"tags":["New Orleans","Music","Theater","Creation"]},{"name":"Bride","definition":"Theresa Piccone, Show Boat, is Vincent Stranahan’s bride; Finnegan sits on her lap at their wedding reception [A:113]. The earlier Bride and Bridegroom remark in the Lotus Eaters belongs to Marie Monaghan’s engagement celebration with Hans Schultz [A:28-29].","citations":["A:113","A:28-29"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Family","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Bridegroom","definition":"Vincent Stranahan is the bridegroom at his wedding to Theresa Piccone, Show Boat [A:113]. In the earlier Lotus Eaters scene, Marie Monaghan speaks of Bride and Bridegroom while celebrating her engagement to Hans Schultz [A:28-29].","citations":["A:113","A:28-29"],"tags":["Family","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Bridge","definition":"Duffey emerges from the Sea of Lost Years onto the Mississippi shore below Eads Bridge in St. Louis [MTM B13:465]. In Chicago he identifies himself with the Pont du Sable Traffic Trestle, whose supposedly ornamental support is knocked out; its loss carries weight for him [MTM B3:104-105]. Theresa compares the performer Maria Tornabuoni’s overhang to a Renaissance bridge [A:49], while Hillary Hilton buys an actual bridge in Grundy County with Finnegan accompanying him [A:254]. Hugh Stranahan’s friends play bridge, the card game [A:104].\n\nIn Finnegan’s uncertain dream-conversation with Marie Courtois, crossing a bridge figures death and immortality; Marie insists it has only one shore, a belief Finnegan later recalls as she dies [D:91-92] [D:157]. Another dream-bridge takes him from the Naxos mountaintop to a higher, internally lit, translucent mountain, where he pursues the Oreads [D:138].","citations":["MTM B13:465","MTM B3:104-105","A:49","A:254","A:104","D:91-92","D:157","D:138"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Finnegan","Duffey","Dreams","Death & Mortality","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Bridge-playing","definition":"Hugh Stranahan’s bridge-playing friends form one of the many circles of visitors to Patrick and Monica’s house [A:104].","citations":["A:104"],"tags":["Family"]},{"name":"Brig","definition":"One of the vessels in Terry Cork’s comic verse about Dotty: her face launches tugs, tramps, a brig, a coracle and the Bark [MTM B7:214].","citations":["MTM B7:214"],"tags":["Ships","Literature"]},{"name":"Brigadier","definition":"The unnamed Australian brigadier falsely implicated in the Tania hoax. The conspirators tell Stein that the officer has smuggled a woman onto the island for sexual purposes; Tania is actually Tommy Trouncer in costume [A:51]. Casey urges Stein to enlist officers for a raid and a charge of moral turpitude. The brigadier first thinks an unauthorized woman must be punished, then becomes furious on learning that he is the accused [A:53-54]. Stein’s general access to unusual colonels and brigadiers is mentioned earlier [A:19].","citations":["A:51","A:53-54","A:19"],"tags":["Islands","Australia","Soldiers","Identity"]},{"name":"Brigand","definition":"Brigands belong to the Old American Blood and Thunder adventure material Gautier loves in the Harlow House library, alongside train robbers, buffalo hunters, Indian scalpers and bank robbers [A:198].","citations":["A:198"],"tags":["Crime","Literature"]},{"name":"Bright Spot","definition":"Dotty’s name for the few remaining sources of good in a world filling with spiritual trash: she says they are mostly themselves, and Duffey agrees that the world still contains many good people. Bagby’s death immediately follows this exchange [MTM B7:236].","citations":["MTM B7:236"],"tags":["Women Characters","Duffey","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Brilliant young man with a stutter, The","definition":"The unnamed brilliant man whom the <em>Argo</em> Masters encounter at Weinsburg on the Nechar River. His mind is compared to a burning sphere, and his frustrated fury makes him a dangerous overturner. Duffey says “Ephphatha,” opening his lips and tongue and curing his stutter [MTM B13:430]. The man objects that he never asked for the cure: frustration supplies the anger needed for his destructive mission. Duffey refuses to restore it and leaves with Brannagan. Casey lingers, and the narrator pointedly asks why anyone would give the man his stutter back [MTM B13:431].","citations":["MTM B13:430","MTM B13:431"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Minor Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Brimstone","definition":"Sulfur, associated with diabolic fire. Finnegan tells Theresa Piccone that her baptismal salt flamed on contact with real brimstone and that the font cracked; she answers his witchcraft teasing in kind [A:108]. Brimstone is among the boiling materials used to seal Saint Sécaire’s sword inside its scabbard [MTM B13:422]. Doll’s “Diabolique” calls for salting the buried Devil with sulphur [D:293].","citations":["A:108","MTM B13:422","D:293"],"tags":["Saints","Sacraments","The Devil","Weapons","Literature"]},{"name":"Brimstone Combo","definition":"The band in Trinali’s poem about an old saloon by Beelzebub and Hell-Raiser Streets near Iniquity Square. Birsha plays diatonic harp, Shinar a nine-stringed kithara, Bera a B-flat trumpet, Zoar drums, and Schemember a ram’s horn. The Seven Just Men visit nightly for toddies [A:235]. The names adapt Genesis 14: Bera, Birsha and Shemeber are kings, whereas Shinar is a land and Zoar a city.","citations":["A:235"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Literature","Bars & Taverns","Music"]},{"name":"Briquettes (Charcoal)","definition":"Charcoal briquettes in a two-foot trencher-plate supplied and lit by the hotel waitress when a lawn bonfire is forbidden. The picnic party sits around the small fire singing childhood camp songs [A:30-31].","citations":["A:30-31"],"tags":["Music","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Brisbane, Gilbert","definition":"One of the artists whose work Duffey sells and whose creative ideas contribute to the Rebuilding Thesis. He appears in the roster with Rita Tinder and Crystal O’Boyle [MTM B6:198-199].","citations":["MTM B6:198-199"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"British","definition":"Seaworthy claims that a Hindu belief in India as Purgatory has been interpreted as a criticism of life under British rule [D:72]. The Naxos raiders carry Enfield rifles of old British issue [D:136]. The narrator distinguishes the British Basseterre from the French Basse-Terre while stressing Finnegan’s confusion about which island he has reached [D:235].","citations":["D:72","D:136","D:235"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Weapons","Islands"]},{"name":"Brokebolt, Paul Cardinal","definition":"Paul Cardinal Brokenbolt of Freemantle, Australia, whose double is Cecil Octavian of England [MB:579]. The name is printed Brokebolt in the “Deaths of the Cardinals” painting list [MTM B13:452]. He is among the thirteen primaries killed in the Track and Total campaign; his burial stone recalls the Lord walking by the Sea of Galilee and seeing two brothers [MB:588-589].","citations":["MB:579","MTM B13:452","MB:588-589"],"tags":["Australia","Ecclesiastical","The Dead","Identity"]},{"name":"Brokenbolt, Cecil Octavian","definition":"Earlier heading attached the primary’s surname to his double. See <em>Octavian, Cecil (of England)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ships","Identity"]},{"name":"Brood","definition":"A collective word for offspring or creations. X says the perversity in Katie Noonan’s family brood cannot, in his view, be explained by the kind man who adopted Ifreann [D:279]. In the talisman discussion, Duffey is credited with a brood of beautiful, bumptious people whose creation is compared with biological parenthood [MTM B5:165].","citations":["D:279","MTM B5:165"],"tags":["Duffey","Family","Creation"]},{"name":"Brook","definition":"Finnegan and Howland wade several brooks or creeks flowing west or southwest during their early northward wanderings toward Chicago [A:178-179].","citations":["A:178-179"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Brooms (Family)","definition":"One of the four so-called families, alongside the Darts, Oakleys and Leakys, attacked by a Pattenite in the debate with Duffey. The speaker claims their fossil discoveries support one another circularly and that rift faults invalidate the evidence’s original location [MTM B9:282-283]. The name likely alludes to Robert Broom, the South African palaeontologist associated with important hominin finds. The family grouping and attack are the speaker’s polemic.","citations":["MTM B9:282-283"],"tags":["Africa","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Brotherhood","definition":"The Dirty Five form a mystic brotherhood, compared with the secret societies of the Papuan men around them; its members are Henry, Hans, Vincent, Casey and Finnegan [A:38-39]. The word is used ironically for the psychiatric professionals who cannot appreciate Howell’s humor [A:82-83]. In the <em>Argo</em> episode, the unrecognized Casey Gorshok warns Duffey and Brannagan that failure to recognize a fellow Master could break the Brotherhood [MTM B13:425-426].","citations":["A:38-39","A:82-83","MTM B13:425-426"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Clubs & Societies","Identity"]},{"name":"Brown Box","definition":"<em>The Crock</em>’s archive of cards on infiltrators, brought by X from Chicago inside a gray box nested in a black one. Duffey recognizes the archive as the <em>Crock</em>’s property, and X admits stealing it [MTM B6:201-203]. Most cards are in Duffey’s hand; others are by X and fellow contributors. X adds an unnaturally large orange-red card that nevertheless fits inside the small box [MTM B6:203].","citations":["MTM B6:201-203","MTM B6:203"],"tags":["Duffey","Crime","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Brown Stone Statues of Sjahrir","definition":"The brown stone statues in Brannagan’s extraordinary stories, said to be people petrified upright by Seti darts dipped in quick-acting juices. Finnegan is willing to accept this, alongside tales of trading Circassian slave-girl futures at Aden and of Prester John in Madagascar, while remaining uncertain how far to trust Brannagan [D:206].","citations":["D:206"],"tags":["Folklore","Magic & Sorcery","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Brown, Father (Code Name)","definition":"One of the expert advisers summoned by the <em>Argo</em> Masters, under a name borrowed from G. K. Chesterton’s priest-detective. The narrative allows that such advisers may be fictional persons or actual persons under disguises [MTM B13:455]. Father Brown warns that the hardest intruder to exclude is already inside, then recommends a trusted English bulldog to detect the familiar turning strange and wrong. Duffey accordingly brings Gunboat Smith aboard [MTM B13:457-458].","citations":["MTM B13:455","MTM B13:457-458"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Ships","Identity"]},{"name":"Browne's Prelude","definition":"The music whose first eight bars Finnegan plays softly on his harmonica while urging X to continue the graveyard history [D:285]. The group is at the shared grave of Ifreann and Gregory McIfreann, whom X identifies as Papadiabolous I [D:284-285].","citations":["D:285","D:284-285"],"tags":["Music","Finnegan","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Brownie","definition":"A helpful supernatural being, traditionally an elf that works at night. Charley Murray asks whether brownies make Duffey’s otherwise impossible magic props. The narrator calls the term vague but potentially apt for the very large helpers involved, then identifies them as ebony faber-giants working under Duffey’s orders [MTM B1:21].","citations":["MTM B1:21"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Magic & Sorcery","Giants & Titans","Duffey","Creation"]},{"name":"Brownsville","definition":"The port visited by the <em>Brunhilde</em> the day after Port Isabel. Seaworthy has associates at each stop [D:55].","citations":["D:55"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors"]},{"name":"Brubaker, George","definition":"The Newark resident whose unlocked car Finnegan takes while fleeing New York with Doll Delancy. From the car’s papers Finn supplies Doll with the identity Mrs. George Brubaker and says he may briefly have to be George himself [D:167-168]. After returning the car to Brubaker’s house, Finn leaves a note signed Roving Magistrate Number Sixteen, admonishing him for leaving it unlocked and ordering him to Room 101 of the Epimetheos Building. Finn does not know whether that building exists [D:169]. He later guesses that the borrowed glasses he wears also belong to Brubaker [D:170].","citations":["D:167-168","D:169","D:170"],"tags":["Finnegan","Crime","Identity"]},{"name":"Brubaker, Mrs. George","definition":"The identity Finnegan assigns Doll Delancy while she drives George Brubaker’s stolen car from New York toward its owner’s Newark address [D:167-168]. Half asleep, Finn believes they are stopped and hears her tell a questioner with a flashlight that she is Mrs. George Brubaker and is driving home [D:169].","citations":["D:167-168","D:169"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Brunhilde","definition":"Saxon X. Seaworthy’s ship, formerly a millionaire’s steam yacht and now a diesel vessel. The narrator calls her a rakish, welcoming old ship despite the killers and cutthroats she carries [D:21] [D:55]. Captain Orestes Gonof commands a crew including Harry Scott, Don Lewis, Joe Cross, Art Emery and Chris McAbney; Finnegan is also carried as a crewman, under John Solli [D:30] [D:39]. Anastasia Demetriades opens the ship’s small bar [D:22]. Manuel first appears as a stowaway and self-described spy; Papa Devil obtains papers that make him a crew member [D:54-55] [D:59].\n\nFinnegan decorates the bar with Sindbad-voyage panels and works out the living face concealed beneath Papa Devil’s assumed appearance [D:59] [D:62]. Anastasia calls the <em>Brunhilde</em> a plague ship that infects wherever it touches [D:69]. Finnegan, rather than Anastasia explicitly speaking of herself, comes to understand that the water-witch’s true form resembles the ship [D:31]. Joe Cross reports Dopey’s screams and unexplained disappearance from a cabin after Papa Devil enters [D:64]; Papa later leaves open whether he or Finnegan killed Dopey, or whether the double was absorbed into Finn [D:121-122]. Don Lewis is murdered by Marie Courtois ashore at Tangier, and Finnegan buries him in sand [D:110-114]. Later Finn says Marie is dying aboard after Joe Cross shot her during the Naxos battle [D:157].\n\nThe coastwise voyage calls at Freeport, Palacios, Port O’Conner, Aransas Pass, Corpus and Padre Island, then Port Isabel and Brownsville [D:34] [D:55]. It continues down Mexico and Central America through Campeche, Progreso, Cozumel, Ciudad Chetumal, Belize, Puerto Barrios, La Ceiba, Trujillo, Cabo Gracias a Dios, Blue Fields, Limon and San Cristobal [D:81]. South American stops include Cayenne, Belem, Sao Luis, Camocim, Fortaleza and Natal [D:86]. Leaving Natal on January 18, the ship reaches Freetown on January 26 [D:89], then goes by Bissau, Bathurst, Dakar, St. Louis, Memrhar, Port Etienne, Villa Cisneros and Las Palmas to the Grand Canary [D:96]. It proceeds through Arrecife on Lanzarote, Ifni, Agadir, Magadar, Safti, Mazagan, Casablanca, Rabat and Tangier [D:101], and onward through Palermo, Messina and Zakinthos to Naxos [D:114-115].\n\nThe <em>Argo</em>’s later history explicitly recalls the <em>Brunhilde</em> as a name the ship bore under evil owners, with Finnegan’s paintings still in the Sindbad Lounge [MTM B13:404]. Stein’s notes pose further questions about its place in the sequence of <em>Argo</em> and <em>Barque</em> and the overlap of Finnegan’s experience with Giulio’s [MB:549-550]. Those notes say Finn did not die in the early cabin encounter, while recording his hazy memory of Papa Devil bending over to kill him before his first boarding [MB:548].","citations":["D:21","D:55","D:30","D:39","D:22","D:54-55","D:59","D:62","D:69","D:31","D:64","D:121-122","D:110-114","D:157","D:34","D:81","D:86","D:89","D:96","D:101","D:114-115","MTM B13:404","MB:549-550","MB:548"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Crew & Sailors","Ports & Harbors","Islands","Greece","Africa","Finnegan","Women Characters","The Dead","Visual Arts","Crime","Time & Eternity","History & Memory","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Brunissende","definition":"The heroine named in the Occitan romance <em>Jaufre and Brunissende</em>. Hans meets a poor scholar asking for a quarter to buy it; Hans funds the purchase but buys <em>Les Cents Novelles Nouvelles</em> for himself, and the two read together in a New York park [A:224].\n\nThe medieval <em>Jaufre</em>, the sole surviving Arthurian romance in Occitan, follows its knight through combats and courtly trials toward union with Brunissende, lady of Montbrun. Their courtship and eventual marriage combine the Arthurian quest with the culture of southern French romance. Its treatment of chivalry includes comic and critical elements as well as idealized love.","citations":["A:224"],"tags":["Women Characters","Literature"]},{"name":"Brush","definition":"Brush and bushes provide cover and uncertain boundaries. Finnegan and Anastasia walk through dunes and brush at Padre Island [D:34] [D:38]; at a stop uncertainly identified as Paraiso, Campeche or another port, luxuriant growth over the old walls shelters lizards, snakes and birds [D:67-68]. At Tangier, Finn follows his companions into the weeds and sparse shanties [D:104], then buries Don Lewis in cleaner sand beneath aromatic bushes [D:110]. On Naxos, wine is left in a bush fork [D:126], Oreads emerge from bushes in Finn’s dream [D:137], and Finn and Manuel hide in a thicket after the battle [D:148].\n\nDuffey deliberately chooses thorn hedges in the Delectable Place and discovers a dangerous-looking mechanical lever in the thicket despite Patrick’s warning [MTM B12:391]. The suit-selling Old Dan emerges from bushes, appearing to the naked young customer as perhaps a man or a gnome [A:259]. Ghosts haunt the Barrens and brush in Gautier’s account [A:197], and Elena Musquiz finds the exhausted Finnegan in scrub brush after his buffalo vigil [A:206].","citations":["D:34","D:38","D:67-68","D:104","D:110","D:126","D:137","D:148","MTM B12:391","A:259","A:197","A:206"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Finnegan","Duffey","Ghosts & Spirits","Dreams"]},{"name":"Buffalo (American Bison)","definition":"The old buffalo bulls of an ancestral city, believed to lie in Frio County just above the fork of two creeks. Finnegan recognizes their distinctive smell and real dung but sees through them, as they seem to see through him, in the altered vision associated with his drinking and life-travel [A:205]. Most of the herd has gone north for summer, leaving sore-footed elders to guard the Capital. Finn sleeps within their circle and feels kinship with their ancient remnant stock; the next morning Elena Musquiz rescues him from the scrub [A:206].","citations":["A:205","A:206"],"tags":["Finnegan","Time & Eternity","Identity"]},{"name":"Buffalo Bar","definition":"The bar on Luzon where the Dirty Five drink with their friend Captain Guiterrez, nicknamed Sammy, a young-looking Tagalog officer [A:63] [A:66].","citations":["A:63","A:66"],"tags":["Soldiers","Bars & Taverns","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Buffalo Hunter and Indian Scalper","definition":"One of the adventure subjects listed among Gautier’s old American “Blood and Thunder” romances, the books left after his goats have thinned the Harlow House library [A:198].","citations":["A:198"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"Buffoon","definition":"According to Hans, Stein understood and helped devise the practical joke played on him, enjoying the part of the buffoon [A:55]. After shooting Stockton Stone Crocker, Hackenschmidt says <em>Sic simper zannionibus</em>, glossed in the text as “thus ever to buffoons or zanies” [MB:567].","citations":["A:55","MB:567"],"tags":["Language & Words","Ekklesia","Crime"]},{"name":"Bug","definition":"Finnegan tells the Muses who visit him in a dream, “Go away, girls, you bug me” [A:181]. In Joe Cross’s account of Dopey’s disappearance, Papa Devil dismisses the blood in the cabin as “Bug blood, I guess” [D:64].","citations":["A:181","D:64"],"tags":["Finnegan","Dreams","Devils (Characters)","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Bugger","definition":"Don Lewis calls the apparent Japanese soldier whom his patrol pursues “the little bugger” [D:44]. In his account of confronting Castleman, Le Marin substitutes “You’re a bugger and a Turk” for the words he actually used [D:245-246].","citations":["D:44","D:245-246"],"tags":["Soldiers","Crew & Sailors","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Buggernosed","definition":"Doll Delancy calls Finnegan and X “loop-eared, buggernosed bums” while challenging their dismissal of ordinary people’s power to save themselves [D:291-292].","citations":["D:291-292"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Buggy","definition":"Buggies appear in the comic account of Cajun antiquity [A:124] and among Duffey’s private vehicles for outings with Mary Louise and Beth [MTM B2:58]. The Royal Pop History catalogue includes Johnson’s Jump-Seat Buggy [MTM B11:329]. An old horse that pulled a buggy around the French Quarter becomes the first large animal put into the Pot [MTM B6:196]. Tommy Katt calls his vehicle a “buggy” when he helps the wounded Finnegan escape [D:232].","citations":["A:124","MTM B2:58","MTM B11:329","MTM B6:196","D:232"],"tags":["Louisiana","Duffey","Finnegan","New Orleans","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Bugle Nose","definition":"Finnegan’s face is described as a comic mask with a bugle nose [A:6]. Papa Devil calls attention to it [D:23], Anastasia calls Finnegan “buglenosed” [D:24], and Dopey’s writing asks why “bugle nose” is not dead [D:78]. Dotty’s related phrase is “banana-nosed brat” [A:93].","citations":["A:6","D:23","D:24","D:78","A:93"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Building","definition":"Buildings include the Plaza and its many bars [A:3] [A:88]; the Cat Castle, Patrick Stranahan’s family home [A:102]; the warehouse and loft adjoining the New Orleans print shop, fitted out for a library, institute, auditorium, and living quarters [A:126]; the Federal Buildings where Finnegan might obtain mural commissions [A:184]; and the abandoned Harlow House, sometimes occupied by the goat-man and his goats [A:197]. In <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>, Don Lewis seeks protection at the consulate and at a paid hiding place in Tangier [D:106-108]. Finnegan climbs six stories up a Gothic hotel [D:162], invents an appointment at the possibly nonexistent Epimetheos Building [D:169], drinks at the shady bait house [D:182], and attacks the Pirate Hotel [D:231]. The Old Wooden Ship is a four-story, unpainted, pungent building on a garlic block, with a raked-shell sidewalk, many doors, interior and exterior stairs, loose slat shutters, and a swaying, creaking motion [D:215]. Zabotski’s boat palace on Pristine Cove is called the Big Red Barn [MTM B10:290].","citations":["A:3","A:88","A:102","A:126","A:184","A:197","D:106-108","D:162","D:169","D:182","D:231","D:215","MTM B10:290"],"tags":["New Orleans","Bars & Taverns","Finnegan","Visual Arts","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Bulfinch's Mythology","definition":"The mythology book from which Duffey says he knows the <em>Argo</em>, when Teresa asks whether he remembers sailing on the ship [MTM B12:386].","citations":["MTM B12:386"],"tags":["Literature","Argonautica","Duffey"]},{"name":"Bull","definition":"Finnegan is compared to a yearling bull [A:1], and Count Finnegan in the painting retains the strength and speed of a yearling bullock [MTM B12:368] [MB:551]. Mary Louise is figured as the royal bovine or sacred cow [MTM B2:55]. Casey’s imagined women include Cretan girl bull-fighters [A:157], and his school notebook defines a Free Martin as a sterile heifer born twin to a bull calf [A:164]. The Cretan Bull Drama at the Decatur Street Opera House combines athletic displays with the killing of human victims [MTM B11:349]. Bull snakes occur at the Nursery and in the lore of Chosky Bottoms [A:208] [D:274]. Basin Street Becaud plays a bull fiddle and pretends its case holds a shotgun capable of decapitating a bull elephant [D:223]. The bull-sinew bowstring figures the tension between the elements composing humanity [D:283].","citations":["A:1","MTM B12:368","MB:551","MTM B2:55","A:157","A:164","MTM B11:349","A:208","D:274","D:223","D:283"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Theater","Cosmic Struggle","Music","Folklore"]},{"name":"Bull snakes","definition":"Snakes among the wildlife along Hans’s mile-long running route through the Nursery [A:208]. Eighteen-foot bull snakes are also part of the lore of Chosky Bottoms [D:274].","citations":["A:208","D:274"],"tags":["Folklore"]},{"name":"Bull-fighters (Cretan Girl)","definition":"Women in Casey Szymansky’s second imaginary world, an erotic fantasy populated by figures from history and legend [A:157].","citations":["A:157"],"tags":["Women Characters","Dreams","Combat"]},{"name":"Bulldog (English)","definition":"Father Brown recommends an English bulldog to detect the moment when something familiar becomes strange and wrong, as protection against the Man of Mystery. Duffey chooses Gunboat Smith, whom he trusts despite Kasmir Gorshok’s objections [MTM B13:458].","citations":["MTM B13:458"],"tags":["Duffey","Argonautica","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Bulldog Courage","definition":"Courage attributed to Joe Bushmaster’s forebears by his supporters during the drinking contest. Finnegan jokes that some of those forebears were bulldogs [A:23].","citations":["A:23"],"tags":["Finnegan","Alcohol","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Bully Boys","definition":"The Slant Faced Men appear as the Laughing Prince’s bully boys in Duffey’s desert hallucination [MTM B13:440].","citations":["MTM B13:440"],"tags":["Duffey","Dreams"]},{"name":"Bum","definition":"Finnegan tells Hillary Hilton that the Shaman diagnosed him as a “natural born bum,” and Hilton agrees [A:255]. Giulio Solli, Finnegan’s father, is called a poor bum and is recalled as a dock worker [A:244]. Vincent’s parable of two bums in a gutter ridicules refusing a remedy because it has been tried before [A:57]. Finnegan gives a pursuing beggar his pocket change while escaping with the suitcase [D:166]. See <em>Bumi</em> for the canoe’s name.","citations":["A:255","A:244","A:57","D:166"],"tags":["Finnegan","Family","Occupations","Argonautica","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Bumi","definition":"One of the names of the canoe in the Malayan boy’s account, glossed by him as “of the world.” Its other names are <em>Aral</em> and <em>Aragh</em>, and the Sergeant recognizes it as the <em>Argo</em> [A:89].","citations":["A:89"],"tags":["Ships","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Bunk","definition":"Finnegan occupies the bunk from which Dopey disappeared and shares the cabin with Don Lewis and Joe Cross [D:31] [D:64]. He senses Dopey’s dead but lingering presence there [D:78]. The edge of Dopey’s envelope is visible behind an upper balk only to someone lying in Finnegan’s bunk [D:50-51] [D:78].","citations":["D:31","D:64","D:78","D:50-51"],"tags":["Ships","Crew & Sailors","Finnegan","The Dead"]},{"name":"Buonorroti","definition":"Michelangelo Buonarroti (1475–1564), named “Buonorroti” by Papa Piccone alongside Sancho and Cellini. Patrick Stranahan replies that such artists should have remained anonymous like their medieval predecessors [A:111]. See <em>Michaelangelo</em>.","citations":["A:111"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Historical Figures"]},{"name":"Buoys","definition":"Some buoys along the Naxos shipways carry bells that clang as they roll with the waves [D:131].","citations":["D:131"],"tags":["Greece","Islands","Sailing"]},{"name":"Burden","definition":"In Bascom Bagby’s commentary from Purgatory, prayers supposedly offered for the Poor Souls often transfer the petitioners’ own burdens to them. These arrive on caravans of laden scapegoats. Casey cares for the exhausted animals and repairs their injuries [PG:546].","citations":["PG:546"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Prayer & Devotion","The Dead"]},{"name":"Bureau (Weather)","definition":"The weather bureau, or “some bureau anyhow,” provides Casey’s analogy for testing political phrases by sending up balloons to see which way the wind blows. He then replaces the balloon comparison with an echo-cave analogy [A:185].","citations":["A:185"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Burgandy, Horatio","definition":"One of the scientifically inclined people in Duffey and Letitia’s Chicago circle, listed together with Mildred Burgandy [MTM B3:98-99].","citations":["MTM B3:98-99"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Burgandy, Mildred","definition":"One of the scientifically inclined people in Duffey and Letitia’s Chicago circle, listed together with Horatio Burgandy [MTM B3:98-99].","citations":["MTM B3:98-99"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Burglars","definition":"John (Little Alcohol) Indelecio and John (Big Alcohol) Jereboam, among the Old Wooden Ship’s characters, work as burglars: Little Alcohol climbs to high windows, while Big Alcohol handles drilling and prying [D:218]. X describes Papadiabolous II, also known as Noonan, as a gentleman burglar before he entered police work [D:285-287].","citations":["D:218","D:285-287"],"tags":["Crime","Occupations","Crew & Sailors","Devils (Characters)"]},{"name":"Burglary (Upper-story)","definition":"Finnegan has recently committed several upper-story burglaries to obtain evidence, rather than money, before he perches like a living gargoyle on a Gothic hotel [D:162].","citations":["D:162"],"tags":["Finnegan","Crime"]},{"name":"Burial","definition":"Finnegan helps Saxon Seaworthy bury Papadiabolous I in an already occupied grave [D:75-76] [D:84] [D:153]. X later identifies the older occupant as their father, called Ifreann, buried in 1930 [D:285] [D:288]; the stone is inscribed I.N.C.G.P. Bogovitch [D:277]. The ground has split since the second burial [D:293]. Finnegan buries Don Lewis in clean sand near Tangier [D:110-111], recalling Don’s account of burying Yokipoki in mud [D:46]. Anastasia receives a lavish funeral, which X thinks Seaworthy arranged [D:157].","citations":["D:75-76","D:84","D:153","D:285","D:288","D:277","D:293","D:110-111","D:46","D:157"],"tags":["Finnegan","Devils (Characters)","The Dead","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Burlesque","definition":"Papa Piccone defends the Star and Garter’s shows as burlesque in the original sense, despite threats to close the theatre unless it drops the designation [A:48]. Finnegan’s cigar-store Indian carvings also have a touch of burlesque [A:183], and Anastasia can appear as a “burlesque of the pretty” [D:20]. Piccone’s account of Oscar Oliver Omygosh turns on the failure of a stage caricature when its original appears in person [MTM B5:168]. Burlesque is also among the popular arts described as being systematically degraded [MTM B6:207]. These uses connect theatrical entertainment with comic distortion.","citations":["A:48","A:183","D:20","MTM B5:168","MTM B6:207"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Theater","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Burning Bush","definition":"Image X applies to Absalom Stein in a letter: Stein has seen the burning bush and must now wander forty years in the desert. X follows it with an appeal for intelligence and serenity [A:265-266].","citations":["A:265-266"],"tags":["Old Testament","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Burr Oak","definition":"Unverified comparison. The earlier glossary says Duffey likens his voice to a burr-oak branch scratching a wooden wall, but a supporting passage has not been located.","citations":[],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Burro","definition":"Finnegan and Anastasia rent the donkey Papapaleologus for their visit to her grandmother on Naxos [D:124-125].","citations":["D:124-125"],"tags":["Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Bursar","definition":"In Bagby’s account, Duffey acts as North American Bursar of the World-Wide Argonauts Argo Balloon Club, collecting subscriptions intended for a nonexistent headquarters in Geneva [MTM B2:63].","citations":["MTM B2:63"],"tags":["Duffey","Money & Trade","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Burton","definition":"Richard Francis Burton, translator of the <em>Arabian Nights</em>. The introduction invokes his warning to keep the <em>Nights</em> under lock and key before warning of its own narrative precipices [D:7]. See <em>Burton, Sir Richard Francis (1821--1890)</em>.","citations":["D:7"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Burton, Sir Richard Francis (1821--1890)","definition":"English explorer, soldier, linguist, diplomat, and translator (1821-1890). The introduction to <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> invokes “Old Burton” and his warning that subscribers should keep their copies of the <em>Nights</em> under lock and key [D:7]. His <em>Book of the Thousand Nights and a Night</em>, privately published under the Kama Shastra Society imprint from 1885, is unexpurgated, uses deliberately archaic language, and carries extensive notes on customs and sexuality. It also draws heavily on John Payne’s earlier English translation. Burton’s other publishing ventures included the <em>Kama Sutra</em> and <em>The Perfumed Garden</em>. He visited Mecca in disguise in 1853, explored East Africa with John Hanning Speke, and held consular appointments including Fernando Po, Damascus, and Trieste. He died in Trieste.","citations":["D:7"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Africa","Middle East","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Bush","definition":"X tells Absalom Stein that he has seen the burning bush and must wander forty years in the desert [A:265-266]. Other settings include the scrub brush where Elena Musquiz rescues Finnegan [A:206], the haunted brush near the Harlow House [A:197], and the bushes from which the suit-selling, gnome-like stranger emerges [A:259]. Finnegan and Anastasia walk through dunes and brush [D:38]; on Naxos they leave wine in the fork of a bush [D:126], Oreads emerge from bushes in Finnegan’s dream [D:137], and Finnegan and Manuel later hide in a thicket [D:148]. Aromatic bushes cover the place Finnegan chooses for Don Lewis’s grave [D:110]. Duffey deliberately leaves the green way to enter thorn hedges in the Delectable Place [MTM B12:391-392]. See <em>Brush</em>.","citations":["A:265-266","A:206","A:197","A:259","D:38","D:126","D:137","D:148","D:110","MTM B12:391-392"],"tags":["Old Testament","Nymphs & Mermaids","Finnegan","Duffey","Dreams","Greece"]},{"name":"Bushmaster, Joe","definition":"Australian sergeant on orderly duty at the wet canteen, described as larger and fiercer-looking than a gorilla [A:12]. He joins the joke played on Finnegan and Vincent, including the fictitious beer-chit requirement [A:13-14], but stops the torment because he dislikes seeing innocents suffer [A:14]. Calling himself the world’s heavyweight drinking champion, he agrees to face Hans Schultz [A:15]; Jimmy Hansen advertises him as the “Lion of the South” [A:19]. His confidence gives way to worry and panic as Hans drinks steadily ahead of him [A:20-21]. He finally fails to finish a glass after four hours and twenty minutes [A:23].","citations":["A:12","A:13-14","A:14","A:15","A:19","A:20-21","A:23"],"tags":["Australia","Soldiers","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Busker (Street)","definition":"Finnegan plays the concertina in bars and streets during the Ignacio and Elena episode. Ignacio thinks it undignified for a Yankee to play in the streets. Elena accompanies the instrument she sold him, and later Finnegan refuses to play for rude visitors [A:272-273]. The passage describes street performance but does not establish that busking was his youthful occupation.","citations":["A:272-273"],"tags":["Finnegan","Music"]},{"name":"Bust","definition":"The Ambrosian bust of Homer appears in the chapter-opening discussion of supposedly Neanderthal features and capacities [D:270-271].","citations":["D:270-271"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Literary Figures"]},{"name":"Buster","definition":"Unverified term. The earlier glossary attributes “Buster” to Casey and Seaworthy as a name for Finnegan; neither claimed use has been located.","citations":[],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Busybody","definition":"Unverified attribution. No supporting passage has been located for Casey calling Duffey a busybody because of his interference.","citations":[],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Butcher","definition":"In Don Lewis’s story, Yokipoki has a double-edged butcher knife, which Don uses while digging his grave [D:46]. Don imagines that the dead man is cutting his bonds and attacking him with it, then discovers rats at the body [D:47]. A butcher also buys Homer Hochheimer’s purple cow in Marie’s anecdote [A:24].","citations":["D:46","D:47","A:24"],"tags":["Weapons","Food & Dining","The Dead"]},{"name":"Butler","definition":"Surname of Buttercup Butler, the perennial senior who serves as room captain in Casey’s chamber at St. Bonaventure’s [A:161-162]. See <em>Butler, Buttercup</em>.","citations":["A:161-162"],"tags":["Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Butler, Buttercup","definition":"Perennial senior and room captain in Casey Szymansky’s six-boy chamber at St. Bonaventure’s. Smallest of the boys, he looks like an owl, has some wit, and gives little help with lessons [A:161-162].","citations":["A:161-162"],"tags":["Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Butterfly","definition":"X’s conspicuous eight-toned car is called a “butterfly.” He argues that its very conspicuousness will confound pursuers, then admits Doll is right that he simply fell in love with it [D:179-180]. Butterflies gather at the old wood graveyard [A:233]. Gloria Mullens calls her supposedly dead husband a grasshopper and a butterfly, describing his improvident character rather than a literal transformation [MTM B3:79].","citations":["D:179-180","A:233","MTM B3:79"],"tags":["Language & Words","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Buzzards","definition":"Buzzards attack Duffey with “knifie beaks” after he falls into the dung heap [MTM B12:392-393]. Casey’s attack on the <em>Bark</em> remarks that “even buzzards sometimes gag,” referring to Finnegan’s departure [A:128-129]. Zabotski compares the strange episode hanging over the town to a fancy-Dan buzzard with peacock feathers [MTM B11:306-307].","citations":["MTM B12:392-393","A:128-129","MTM B11:306-307"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan","Journalism","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Byrne, Mary Louise","definition":"See <em>Bagby, Mary Louise (Byrne)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Byzantine Empire/style","definition":"Unverified composite entry. The earlier glossary connects Byzantine history and iconoclasm with Professor Kirol and compares Anastasia’s story to a Byzantine puzzle. Supporting passages for these connections have not been located.","citations":[],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Literature","Paradox"]},{"name":"Cabala","definition":"The Jewish mystical tradition also called Kabbalah. The novels use “cabalistic” more loosely for mysterious signs: Finnegan invents meaningless markings on a lead weight [D:164], and teasingly tells Theresa that witches have marks on their fingernails which appear on Fridays [A:107-108]. These passages do not identify particular symbols from Jewish mysticism.","citations":["D:164","A:107-108"],"tags":["Mysticism","Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cabalistic Markings","definition":"Meaningless signs invented by Finnegan and marked on the lead weight he uses to break a window during his attack on the meeting in the Gothic hotel [D:162-164].","citations":["D:162-164"],"tags":["Finnegan","Combat","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cabbage","definition":"One of the foods served at the Colony Club’s Harvesters’ Dinner, alongside kale and sauerkraut [MTM B3:94].","citations":["MTM B3:94"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Cabby","definition":"Joe Sorrell, who drives Finnegan and Anastasia across to the bay side to visit the <em>Brunhilde</em> [D:21].","citations":["D:21"],"tags":["Ships","Finnegan","Occupations"]},{"name":"Cabin","definition":"Finnegan shares a cabin on the <em>Brunhilde</em> with Don Lewis and Joe Cross [D:31]. In Joe’s account, Papa Devil enters while Dopey is alone in the bunk; Joe hears two screams, then a third cut short. When he enters, only Papa Devil and a little blood remain, although Dopey could not have escaped [D:64]. Finnegan later senses Dopey’s dead presence there [D:78].","citations":["D:31","D:64","D:78"],"tags":["Ships","Crew & Sailors","Finnegan","Devils (Characters)","The Dead"]},{"name":"Cabinet head","definition":"An unnamed minister imagined as losing sleep over Seaworthy and Papa Devil’s conspicuous company while they dine on the Grand Canary [D:99-100].","citations":["D:99-100"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Cabinet Maker","definition":"One of Duffey’s natural crafts, alongside carpentry and joinery [MTM B1:21]. Bagby also has a cabinet shop adjoining Duffey’s premises; both buildings are made of fine walnut [MTM B2:46].","citations":["MTM B1:21","MTM B2:46"],"tags":["Duffey","Occupations"]},{"name":"Cable exercises","definition":"Part of Hans Schultz’s physical training, drawn from mail-order muscle builders as he strives to match his father John William’s strength [A:208].","citations":["A:208"],"tags":["Family"]},{"name":"Cables","definition":"The giant spider’s web in Hans’s climbing dream has cable-like threads eight inches thick [A:61]. Duffey climbs through hidden cables and struts above a stage to investigate the Majesty [MTM B8:240]. In the Great Day’s disordered streets, severed electrical feeders leave wires dangling and sparking above a dead child [GD:488].","citations":["A:61","MTM B8:240","GD:488"],"tags":["Dreams","Duffey","Theater","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Cabo Gracias a Dios","definition":"Cape on the Caribbean coast at the Honduras-Nicaragua boundary. Cabo Gracias a Dios appears among the <em>Brunhilde</em>’s stops, after Trujillo and before Blue Fields [D:81].","citations":["D:81"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Cabo Rojo","definition":"One of the seven false or shoal passages through the Antilles in Angela Cosquin’s account of her father’s shipwreck maps. Her list also names Jardines Banks, Vieques, St. Barthelemy, the Basseterre-Nevis passage, the Basse-Terre-Marie Galante passage, and the Grenadines [D:259-260].","citations":["D:259-260"],"tags":["Shipwrecks","Caribbean","Sailing"]},{"name":"Cabriolar","definition":"One of the Spanish “goatish verbs” offered by Gautier in his discussion of Finnegan’s “cut capers” pun; it names the capering side of the joke, paired with <em>capar</em> [A:196] [A:199].","citations":["A:196","A:199"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cabron","definition":"Spanish for a male goat, given as <em>cabron</em> by Gautier in his explanation of Finnegan’s goat pun [A:196] [A:199]. The usual Spanish spelling is <em>cabrón</em>.","citations":["A:196","A:199"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cacciatore, Berny","definition":"Writer for the <em>Sporting News Sheet</em> and companion of Gabrielovitch and Bulo Belonki on their early-morning rounds of the bars [A:126]. He tells the Pelican group that Finnegan boxed before the war under the name Solli and was good at it, a fact even Dotty had not known [A:127]. He is later described as the finest historian of boxing and other sports [MTM B11:322].","citations":["A:126","A:127","MTM B11:322"],"tags":["Journalism","Minor Characters","Finnegan","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Cactus kingdom","definition":"The noon-day devils’ offer to Finnegan in the heat of the chaparral. He sees that their promised kingdom is only the cactus country already available to him [A:205].","citations":["A:205"],"tags":["Finnegan","The Devil"]},{"name":"Cadensus, Catherine","definition":"Described as the prettiest girl staying at the Old Wooden Ship. She goes about with Vivian Gilligan, Ouida Garrard, and Mercedes Morrero; the four draw admiring attention in the bar. Finnegan comes increasingly often to see Catherine, although he stays to look at Mercedes [D:224].","citations":["D:224"],"tags":["Women Characters","Finnegan","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Cades","definition":"Biblical Kadesh, named in Melchisedech’s psalm exchange with Finnegan: “The Lord shakes the desert of Cades” [MTM B12:375]. The passage corresponds to Psalm 29:8, numbered Psalm 28:8 in the Douay-Rheims Bible.","citations":["MTM B12:375"],"tags":["Prayer & Devotion","Old Testament","Scripture","Finnegan","Duffey"]},{"name":"Cai","definition":"One of the places where summer is eternal in the verse opening the Finnegan chapter of <em>Archipelago</em> [A:171].","citations":["A:171"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Cain","definition":"Son of Adam and Eve, introduced before his brother Abel in Genesis 4. During his Transport, Finnegan asks whether Cain belonged to his own older people and links the question to the facial mark or protuberance “like another nose” [D:212-213].","citations":["D:212-213"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Cajun","definition":"The south-Louisiana people whose history includes the settlement of Acadian exiles. Henry Salvatore is explicitly identified as Cajun [A:123]. His cousins send seasonal produce for Duffey’s soup by intercoastal-canal boatmen [A:134]. Ouida Garrard is also Cajun [D:224], and Cajun French is one ingredient of Finnegan’s speech [A:6]. A Cajun riverman receives the talisman from which Henri Salvatore is said to originate [MTM B5:152]; “Henri” is pronounced “Onree” in the Cajun fashion [MTM B6:195]. In “Great Day in the Morning,” Duffy supposes that drunken Cajuns must have assembled the disorderly newspaper [GD:486].","citations":["A:123","A:134","D:224","A:6","MTM B5:152","MTM B6:195","GD:486"],"tags":["Louisiana","France","Finnegan","Duffey","Magic & Sorcery","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cajun French","definition":"One of the elements of Finnegan’s mixed New Orleans dialect. The narrator describes it as a “shapeless drift of Cajun French come to town” [A:6].","citations":["A:6"],"tags":["New Orleans","Louisiana","Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cake","definition":"Finnegan and Doll have coffee and cakes at the station before leaving Newark [D:169-170]. Doll also brings cakes to the graveyard picnic; when X calls them devil’s food, she corrects him that they are gingerbread [D:277] [D:284]. The Widow Waldo brings freshly made cakes with coffee [MTM B6:196]. Duffey’s first talisman goes to an Italian pushcart seller of little cakes when Duffey is no more than three [MTM B1:18]. Lily orders a sixty-four-inch-square cake, about three feet high and nine hundred pounds, for the birthday she declares Duffey’s four-thousandth [MTM B8:257]. In the Majesty’s speech, hatred is “both the cake and the frosting on the cake” [MTM B8:242].","citations":["D:169-170","D:277","D:284","MTM B6:196","MTM B1:18","MTM B8:257","MTM B8:242"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Duffey","Finnegan","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Calf","definition":"Finnegan’s psalm prayer makes Lebanon skip like a calf [MTM B12:375], corresponding to Psalm 29:6. Casey’s notebook defines a Free Martin as a sterile heifer born twin to a bull calf [A:164]. Dotty compares Finnegan to a wandering calf her family eventually butchered [A:95-96]. Loy writes that her father killed the fatted calf for his returning prodigal daughter [A:27].","citations":["MTM B12:375","A:164","A:95-96","A:27"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Finnegan","Women Characters"]},{"name":"California","definition":"The location of an unpublished <em>Man from Uncle</em> manuscript mentioned by Rick Norwood in the publisher’s back matter to <em>Archipelago</em> [A:285]. The name also occurs in California Street [MTM B1:25], California Fruit chewing gum [MTM B1:26], and California Bucking Rolls [MTM B2:45-46]. These names alone do not place Duffey’s school or establish where the products were made.","citations":["A:285","MTM B1:25","MTM B1:26","MTM B2:45-46"],"tags":["Literature","Duffey","Language & Words"]},{"name":"California Bucking Rolls","definition":"One explanation offered for the padded leather straps found in Duffey’s former livery stable: fittings that can be added to an ordinary saddle to make it suitable for breaking horses [MTM B2:45-46].","citations":["MTM B2:45-46"],"tags":["Duffey","Occupations"]},{"name":"California Fruit Chewing Gum","definition":"One of the goods sold during the boys’ secret nighttime sales at school, alongside drinks, sweets, and pigs’ feet [MTM B1:25-26].","citations":["MTM B1:25-26"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"California Street","definition":"Street named in the account of Duffey and his friends returning to school after ten at night. The narrator immediately qualifies it as whatever street, in whatever town, provided the scene that year [MTM B1:25].","citations":["MTM B1:25"],"tags":["Duffey","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Call box","definition":"The supposed place where unforwarded letters await Finnegan, one of the reasons he offers himself for leaving Paradise [D:267].","citations":["D:267"],"tags":["Finnegan","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Callagy","definition":"One of the leaders who try to rally the old-line people against the Royal Pop People, alongside Fitzherbert, Chastellain, Faucher, Panebianco, Cassady, and Loubet. The leaders either join the new people or perish, but the text does not specify which fate belongs to Callagy [MTM B11:335].","citations":["MTM B11:335"],"tags":["Minor Characters","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Calumet, Alice","definition":"One of the pleasant, competent, interesting, but “slippery” people in Duffey’s Chicago circle, printed as “Alive Calumet” in that list [MTM B3:100]. With Olivia Hallahan, Shirley Israel, and Josephine McSorley, Alice hisses at Demetrio Glauch when he rejects their entertainment and leaves [MTM B4:127].","citations":["MTM B3:100","MTM B4:127"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Calumet, John","definition":"Member of Duffey’s Chicago circle, listed with Alice Calumet among its “slippery” people [MTM B3:100] [MTM B4:127]. He stays at the supper after Demetrio Glauch leaves; the Calumets belong to the group that later seeks to keep Duffey close and use his magic [MTM B4:127] [MTM B4:137].","citations":["MTM B3:100","MTM B4:127","MTM B4:137"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Calumets","definition":"John and Alice Calumet belong to the social circle that seeks to keep Duffey close and exploit his supposed magic, while promoting the reputation of salons allegedly superior to his [MTM B4:127] [MTM B4:137].","citations":["MTM B4:127","MTM B4:137"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Calvados, Dry Gulch","definition":"Earlier heading that reversed letters in the surname. See <em>Cavaldos, Dry Gulch</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Cambeltown","definition":"New South Wales location named in the heading of Loy Larkin’s letter to Finnegan, dated Thursday, February 11, 1943 [A:27].","citations":["A:27"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","World War II"]},{"name":"Camel","definition":"Honeybucket owns a camel left over from an unsuccessful amusement-park ride; it eats weeds behind the Old Wooden Ship. He claims to have twenty milk camels and a plankton-feed business, with camels the first animals willing to eat the feed, but the narrator explains that he has never tried the scheme or even seen plankton [D:219]. Camel-hair tents belong to Duffey’s remote childhood memories, and his Boy King realm includes silk from camel, ass, ewe, and gazelle [MTM B1:6] [MTM B1:17]. Piccone calls camels natural burlesques that cannot be burlesqued further [MTM B5:167].","citations":["D:219","MTM B1:6","MTM B1:17","MTM B5:167"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Duffey","Food & Dining","Carnival"]},{"name":"Campeche","definition":"A stop on the <em>Brunhilde</em>’s voyage down the Mexican coast [D:81]. Campeche, Paraiso, or another stop may be the setting of <em>The Unaccountable Corpse</em>, and either may also be where Don Lewis had a seaman knifed and left behind to divert suspicion from himself [D:67] [D:105]. In the geography of San Simeon, Campeche is one of five more powerful neighboring countries to which San Simeon pays gold tribute [MB:559].","citations":["D:81","D:67","D:105","MB:559"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","Crime","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Campeche Bank","definition":"The Campeche Banks mark the far reach of the intermittent and sometimes ghostly shoal waters north of San Simeon, more than a hundred kilometers from its north coast [MB:559]. After two bells arrive at the mooring rock, the people install about a hundred more along the coast and shoals as far as “Campche Bank” and Perez Island [MB:577-578].","citations":["MB:559","MB:577-578"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Seas & Oceans","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Campfire songs","definition":"Childhood songs sung by the picnic party at the Bay Hotel around a charcoal-filled trencher: “Clementine,” “Ivan Scravinski Scravar,” “Red Wing,” “Casey Jones,” “Camp Town Race Track,” “Rye Whisky,” “Trail of the Lonesome Pine,” “I Wonder Who’s Kissing Her Now,” “Frankie and Johnny,” “Stardust,” “Waltzing Matilda,” and “There’s a Long Long Trail A-Winding” [A:29-31].","citations":["A:29-31"],"tags":["Music","Australia"]},{"name":"Cana","definition":"Site of the wedding at which Jesus turns water into wine in John 2:1-11. Maurine tells Mamma Monica that Papa Piccone is supplying three barrels and additional jugs for the reception because he refuses to be remembered for running out of wine as the hosts at Cana were [A:105].","citations":["A:105"],"tags":["Miracles","Scripture","New Testament","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Canada","definition":"Source of the liquor that, in Charlotte Mullens’s account, Aga Gonof’s boats bring to Chicago, Detroit, and Cleveland [MTM B3:83-84].","citations":["MTM B3:83-84"],"tags":["Maritime Trade","Alcohol","Crime"]},{"name":"Canal","definition":"Henry’s cousins send produce from Cajun country for Duffey’s soup by the intercoastal-canal boatmen [A:134]. In a separate anatomical usage, “linkage patterns of Volkman canals” appear among the purported tests establishing Duffey’s great age in an excerpt from <em>The Eighteenth Book of Strange Encounters</em> [MTM B8:250].","citations":["A:134","MTM B8:250"],"tags":["Louisiana","Duffey","Food & Dining","Literature"]},{"name":"Canary (bird)","definition":"Hans’s stage command makes Mabel the bear imitate a canary by squealing and flapping her legs like wings [A:219]. Le Marin compares the disappearance of the <em>Sarcophagus</em> crew to a cat reporting an empty canary cage, with the incriminating signs still visible, suggesting that the predator’s own account reveals the answer [D:245].","citations":["A:219","D:245"],"tags":["Theater","Crime","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Canary (Grand)","definition":"Island where the <em>Brunhilde</em> party stops for two days [D:96]. At its luxurious night restaurant, Papa Devil dons a monocle and resembles Count von Baummenbauch; other distinguished diners stare at Seaworthy’s party [D:99-100]. Finnegan’s brandy drinking there is recalled in <em>Archipelago</em> [A:188]. Absalom Stein later says he watched the party dine there in “death-like glitter” but could not cross the room to them [MB:549-550]. Don Lewis tells Finnegan that the world is on fire and that they have been lighting the fuses [D:99].","citations":["D:96","D:99-100","A:188","MB:549-550","D:99"],"tags":["Islands","Ships","Finnegan","Devils (Characters)","Food & Dining","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Candlelight","definition":"Lupido favors candlelight in his cafe and gives Trinali his candle stubs; she also steals the long candles [A:228]. Duffey’s formal balloon dinner is held by candlelight high above the city in the “late-ish darkness” [MTM B2:65].","citations":["A:228","MTM B2:65"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Duffey"]},{"name":"Candles","definition":"Candlelight or kerosene lamps illuminate Duffey’s school-store sales from ten-thirty to midnight and help Charley Murray’s magic tricks [MTM B1:26]. In Finnegan’s night-dune imaginary, people have candle-lit pumpkin heads [D:50]. Trinali uses candles after her electricity is cut off, receiving stubs and stealing longer candles from Lupido’s cafe [A:228]. Four thousand candles are lit on the cake for Duffey’s birthday [MTM B8:257]. Duffey blows out the candles at the end of his balloon dinner before descending [MTM B2:65-66].","citations":["MTM B1:26","D:50","A:228","MTM B8:257","MTM B2:65-66"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Finnegan","Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Candy","definition":"Sold nightly with sandwiches in Duffey’s school store, whose larger stock includes drinks and pigs’ feet [MTM B1:26]. In Elroy Redheart’s miniature art tableau, an opened candy box beside a dead girl contains her removed heart as an oversized red chocolate; a Jill of Hearts card is pinned beside the scene [MTM B7:221].","citations":["MTM B1:26","MTM B7:221"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining","Visual Arts","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Canned Goods (Doubtful)","definition":"Uncertain merchandise used by Hillary Hilton as a comparison in discussing distressed stock. He would rather deal in distressed cigars than doubtful canned goods; distressed eggs are worse still [A:253].","citations":["A:253"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Cannibal","definition":"Finnegan’s response to Hans’s hints about unfamiliar meat invokes the cannibal story he attributes to Melville and calls partly phony [A:58]. Hans objects that “cannibal” is too common and says that initiates use “necrophagists”; the exchange leaves the nature of their meal uncertain [A:59].","citations":["A:58","A:59"],"tags":["Finnegan","Food & Dining","Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cannibal Duffey's Irish Restaurant","definition":"Garish sign painted on the Big Pot by three medical-student jazzers, tightly enough to remain bright almost forever. The same men are suspected of planting the human bones they fish out of the stew; the sign is generally taken in good humor [MTM B6:197].","citations":["MTM B6:197"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Cannibalism","definition":"One of the activities an unnamed mother predicts people will seek out through the Casey Machine. Rejecting her daughter’s hope that users will choose the best of others’ lives, she expects scandal, cruelty, and knowledge useful for personal ruin [CM:505].","citations":["CM:505"],"tags":["Family","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Cannon, Orville","definition":"See <em>Howland, Orville Cannon</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Minor Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Canoe","definition":"Vessel from which the Sergeant and the Malayan boy dive among the islands. The boy names it <em>Aral</em>, <em>Bumi</em>, and <em>Aragh</em>, glossing these as “hindrance,” “of the world,” and “the direction”; the Sergeant then recognizes it as the Ship <em>Argo</em> [A:89].","citations":["A:89"],"tags":["Ships","Argonautica","Identity"]},{"name":"Canonical","definition":"Used for the established account of Finnegan’s adventures, which the narrator says cannot be separated from their apocrypha [A:187]. Duffey’s possible deaths in different times prompt the question whether there is a canonical impediment to dying twice [MTM B12:361]. The narrative’s “canonical ratio” is one corrupt priest in twelve, one devil-serving nun in twelve, and one lay participant in the abomination in twelve [MTM B8:245].","citations":["A:187","MTM B12:361","MTM B8:245"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Death & Mortality","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Cant (Riming)","definition":"Rhyming patter invoked in Finnegan’s “Antipodal Ode to Loy Larkin”: “And cut your teeth on riming cant” [A:32].","citations":["A:32"],"tags":["Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cantina","definition":"Drinking place on the corner near the Mogul’s quarters, from which several of his guards return after a late drink, according to X [MB:573].","citations":["MB:573"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Cap (Hat)","definition":"Finnegan wears an oversized digger hat while pretending to be Australian and later regrets giving it up [A:13] [A:15]. Sammy leaves his cap on a table while Finnegan prepares to distract him [A:67]. In Bagby’s image of the seven-headed enemy, one head wears a red stocking cap [MTM B5:169].","citations":["A:13","A:15","A:67","MTM B5:169"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Cape (Clothing)","definition":"Duffey imagines Absalom Stein in a purple cape with scarlet lining, although Stein actually wears an unfigured sports shirt [MTM B5:161]. Monsignor X returns secretly to the <em>Argo</em> in red robes and a red-piped cape [MTM B13:451]. At the graveyard meeting, X wears his old magician’s cloak, full of pockets and concealed drops, from his career as X-Capo [D:276].","citations":["MTM B5:161","MTM B13:451","D:276"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Identity"]},{"name":"Cape (Geographical)","definition":"Coastal headland. The Cape of Delgan appears in a travel poem as a place where gold lies unclaimed on the ground [A:172]. “The Horn” appears in Finnegan’s joke about Hans and Marie swimming by the long route when the canal would be shorter [A:30].","citations":["A:172","A:30"],"tags":["Sailing","Literature"]},{"name":"Cape buffalo","definition":"Comparison for Henry Salvatore’s strength during his years as a merchant seaman: his apparent fat is deceptive, and he has never been beaten in a fight [A:124].","citations":["A:124"],"tags":["Combat","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Capella","definition":"Unverified reference. The earlier glossary identifies Capella as a star by which Gomez sails, but the cited passage does not establish this.","citations":[],"tags":["Sailing"]},{"name":"Capers","definition":"Dancing and playing tricks, in Finnegan’s explanation of his joke about Castrador-Capros, who “cut capers.” He offers <em>fare delle capriole</em> and <em>castrare becchi</em> as the two senses, while the goat-man criticizes the pun’s language [A:199].","citations":["A:199"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Capital (Buffalo)","definition":"The buffalo herd’s center, guarded in high summer by old bulls while the rest of the herd has moved hundreds of miles north. Finnegan sleeps within their circle [A:206].","citations":["A:206"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Capital (City)","definition":"New York is Patrick Stranahan’s “Capital (as it were)” in his list of gracious American cities [A:110]. Separately, X’s newly purchased car bears a D.C. plate [D:177].","citations":["A:110","D:177"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Capital (Financial)","definition":"Casey mistakes his prosperous father Gabriel Szymansky for a poor man because Gabriel bargains tightly and is stingy; Gabriel runs adjoining antique and pawn shops [A:160]. Casey later inherits money from his father’s estate, spends much of it, keeps careful accounts, and never runs out [A:168].","citations":["A:160","A:168"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Family"]},{"name":"Capital (Goat)","definition":"Earlier mislabel for the buffalo herd’s capital. See <em>Capital (Buffalo)</em> [A:206].","citations":["A:206"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Capitalism","definition":"Unverified attribution. No passage has been located for the earlier glossary’s claim that Papadiabolous practices State Capitalism.","citations":[],"tags":["The Devil","Devils (Characters)"]},{"name":"Capo, X- (alias)","definition":"X-Capo, Mr. X’s former stage name as a magician and escape artist. His many-pocketed cloak at the graveyard is the same one he used in that career [D:276]. See <em>Great X-Capo, The</em>.","citations":["D:276"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Theater","Identity"]},{"name":"Capote","definition":"Unverified entry. The earlier glossary combines a cape or cloak with a person called El Capote, but neither intended source reference has been established.","citations":[],"tags":[]},{"name":"Capricorn","definition":"Zodiac sign singled out by an absurd new traffic signal during the Great Day: “Capricorns should not cross any streets today.” Another signal permanently forbids Leos to cross [GD:488].","citations":["GD:488"],"tags":["Apocalyptic","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Captain","definition":"Several figures bear the title. Captain Guiterrez, nicknamed Sammy, is a youthful-looking Tagalog friend of the Dirty Fivers; he borrows a shoeshine box without its owners’ knowledge to earn more drinking money, and joins the singing and night in the horse pasture [A:66-69]. Orestes Gonof is the “forgotten captain” of the <em>Brunhilde</em>, imagined as an ancient Phoenician at the Grand Canary dinner [D:100]. An unnamed army captain cruelly interrogates the amnesiac Sergeant until he breaks; the Sergeant then promises to find and torment him in Hell [A:73-74]. The haberdasher is acting captain and pilot of the <em>Seabach</em> [D:135], and the fleshless Melchisedech is pilot and captain aboard the <em>Argo</em> [MB:591-592].","citations":["A:66-69","D:100","A:73-74","D:135","MB:591-592"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Ships","Finnegan","Duffey","Soldiers","Alcohol","Heaven & Hell"]},{"name":"Captain Cook (Bar)","definition":"Pub visited by the four Dirty Fivers, Marie, Tom, and Freddy after Hans defeats Bushmaster in the drinking contest. Their crawl continues to Blarney’s, the Dutchman’s, and the Commodore before they take Hans home to bed [A:23].","citations":["A:23"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Australia"]},{"name":"Captain Justice","definition":"One of three comic strips in which Dotty Yekouris has seen the Slant-Faced Men, alongside <em>Flame Man</em> and <em>The White Avenger</em>. Dotty says she could find them comic if she did not know they were real; Margaret Stone stresses that they actually kill people [MTM B7:225].","citations":["MTM B7:225"],"tags":["Literature","Visual Arts","Crime"]},{"name":"Capuchin priest, The","definition":"Young Greek Capuchin whom Finnegan finds on Naxos on Passion Sunday to hear his confession before Mass. Finnegan initially assumes he is Italian; the priest speaks Italian but reproaches Solli for not knowing better Greek [D:118-119]. When Finnegan describes another person or kindred within him and doubts that he is human, the priest accepts that such another kindred exists and assumes salvation is available to it. He absolves Finnegan’s present person “in all its aspects” [D:119-120].","citations":["D:118-119","D:119-120"],"tags":["Sacraments","Sin & Redemption","Greece","Islands","Finnegan","Identity","Theology"]},{"name":"Caravan boy","definition":"One of Joseph’s former occupations. He tells Finnegan that his caravan journeys taught him Arabic and French [D:93-94].","citations":["D:93-94"],"tags":["Occupations","Language & Words","Africa"]},{"name":"Carbine","definition":"A short, light rifle. The narrator describes Nick the Rattle’s weapon during the beach shooting as “greater than a carbine and less than a rifle” [A:282].","citations":["A:282"],"tags":["Weapons"]},{"name":"Carbon balances","definition":"One of the apparent records of the earth’s age in Vincent’s speculation that the universe might have been created ten minutes ago. Carbon balances, partly exhausted atomic clocks, and ancient sediments would then have been created with it [A:56].","citations":["A:56"],"tags":["Creation","Time & Eternity","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Carbon Fourteen","definition":"Gertrude Brannagan’s comparison for the phrase “Argo Fourteen,” which sounds to her like a cosmic dating method such as Carbon Fourteen. Duffey replies that the <em>Argo</em> really is a dating clock, with logs extending far into the past and partly into the future [MTM B13:410-411].","citations":["MTM B13:410-411"],"tags":["Ships","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Card sharps","definition":"Art (One Shot) Yodel and Art (Two Shot) Welch, unsuccessful card sharps and tricksters at the Old Wooden Ship who are repeatedly beaten by more skillful sharpies [D:221]. The earlier resident list gives the first name as Ant (One Shot) Yoder [D:216].","citations":["D:221","D:216"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Crime"]},{"name":"Card-carrying Communist","definition":"Casey’s accusation against Absalom Stein in the <em>Crock</em> attack on the <em>Bark</em>: he calls Stein a card-carrying Communist under the name Hugo Stone [A:128-129].","citations":["A:128-129"],"tags":["Identity","Journalism"]},{"name":"Cardigan-Pembroke, Lloyd","definition":"Welsh pig butcher of Tywyn listed as the double of David Cardinal Lloyd Spencer of Cardiff [MB:579]. He travels from Bimini with Bolo Manolo and Douglas McAfee on a luxury yacht to the Mooring-Stone gathering [MB:594-595], and is among the thirteen who assemble for the conclave that elects Finnegan [MB:596-597]. A later sentence inconsistently calls him the double of Cardinal Ti, whose double in the earlier list is Bolo Manolo [MB:579] [MB:594].","citations":["MB:579","MB:594-595","MB:596-597","MB:594"],"tags":["Popes","Ecclesiastical","Ships","Ekklesia","Identity"]},{"name":"Cardinal","definition":"A prince of the Church. Manuel is described as a man accustomed to dining with cardinals and ministers of state [D:100]. In X's account of the Devil's release from imprisonment in the Crimea, Catholic cardinals are disproportionately represented among the spectators; several prostrate themselves before the Devil [MTM B6:205-206].<br><br>In Book Thirteen, X brings Duffey a flayed skin said to be Cardinal Artemis's. Its markings cannot securely distinguish Artemis from his double, Gilberto [MTM B13:451-452]. Finnegan paints thirteen executions or murders in the <em>Deaths of the Cardinals</em> series on the bulkheads of the <em>Argo</em>'s Bread and Wine Room; Salvatore, one of the subjects, is an <em>Argo</em> Master [MTM B13:452]. Finnegan subsequently says that the thirteen supposed doubles include one surviving holy man, whose presence preserves the line [MTM B13:453].<br><br>In <em>How Many Miles to Babylon</em>, John Mogul's Track and Total hunts the thirteen cardinals and their doubles [MB:578-580]. The list names Hedayat, Brokenbolt, Ti, Merry, Leviathan, Artemis, Lloyd Spencer, Salvatore, Gregorio, Doki, Gabrailovitch, Erculo, and Runosake; some names differ in spelling or form from the Book Thirteen list. Roughly five hundred abdicated cardinals are also still alive. Mogul solicits methods of murder through a public contest, to which the hunted cardinals and their doubles themselves contribute [MB:579-580].","citations":["D:100","MTM B6:205-206","MTM B13:451-452","MTM B13:452","MTM B13:453","MB:578-580","MB:579-580"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Ecclesiastical","The Devil","Finnegan","Visual Arts","Crime","Death & Mortality","Identity"]},{"name":"Cardines","definition":"Latin “hinges,” from <em>cardo</em>, a hinge or pivot. One of the names grouped with “Gates,” “Swingers,” and “Stones” for the Princes of the Crowd at the Last Conclave in Babylonia Bagascia [MB:561].","citations":["MB:561"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cards","definition":"Finnegan compares the construction of his ode to Loy to building a house of cards [A:32]. Vincent plays solitaire with a pack whose missing four of clubs and six of diamonds are replaced by labeled paper slips [A:65]. George Koran’s press card is rejected with a death threat by the Slant-Faced Men [MTM B8:240]. The brown box brought by X contains index cards on suspected infiltrates, including annotations by Duffey and X [A:137] [MTM B6:202-203]. Elroy Redheart’s artwork includes a Jill of Hearts card daggered to a wall beside a dead girl and a Queen of Hearts card pinned into a woman’s own removed heart [MTM B7:221] [MTM B7:224]. A beach fortune-card weighing machine gives Anastasia’s weight as 130 pounds [D:20].","citations":["A:32","A:65","MTM B8:240","A:137","MTM B6:202-203","MTM B7:221","MTM B7:224","D:20"],"tags":["Finnegan","Visual Arts","Journalism","Identity"]},{"name":"Care (Perpetual)","definition":"Perpetual maintenance promised for every Casey Machine site after Newton Prescott obtains its designation as a shrine. Perpetual Guardian Payments are to go to a responsible group, which he identifies as his own [CM:503] [CM:513].","citations":["CM:503","CM:513"],"tags":["Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Cargo","definition":"The Stranahan chapter’s opening verse describes a quest for “auriferous cargo” aboard <em>Argo</em> [A:91], and the <em>Argo</em>’s logs emphasize the finding of the Golden Fleece among its early quests [MTM B13:421]. Another voyage poem has the Dutchman carrying cargo from the Gulf of Oman to Cutch [A:172]. Anastasia says Seaworthy transports unusual cargoes worldwide, often people [D:55]. Le Marin describes Castleman’s theft of both recorded and unmanifested cargo from the <em>Sarcophagus</em>, observing that illegal cargo is easiest to seize [D:243-244]. Finnegan recalls helping unload a cargo during a brief stay at Bantry [D:192].","citations":["A:91","MTM B13:421","A:172","D:55","D:243-244","D:192"],"tags":["Argonautica","Ships","Maritime Trade","Finnegan","Crime"]},{"name":"Carib Indian","definition":"Indigenous people of the Lesser Antilles, also known as Kalinago. Carib ancestry forms part of Angela Cosquin’s island heritage, alongside French and English ancestry and forebears who were noblemen or slaves [D:259].","citations":["D:259"],"tags":["Caribbean","Islands","Family","Social Class"]},{"name":"Caribbean","definition":"Conflagrations, insurrections, and slaughters in this region lead Finnegan to plan to intercept the <em>Brunhilde</em> there. Before he can leave, he learns that the ship is already in his own port and that Seaworthy has anticipated him [D:230].","citations":["D:230"],"tags":["Caribbean","Ships","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Carl the Great (Charlemagne, c. 748--814, King of the Franks, Holy Roman Emperor)","definition":"See <em>Charlemagne</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Historical Figures","Duffey","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Carlson-Shipman Press","definition":"Carlson-Shipman Press, named as publisher of Royce Rollins’s <em>The Return of the Neanderthal</em>, subtitled <em>The Alien Within</em>, in Charles O.A. Harrington’s review for <em>Science for Today</em> [D:240-242].","citations":["D:240-242"],"tags":["Literature","Journalism"]},{"name":"Carnality","definition":"Charlotte Mullens says she always has much carnality to confess, and the narrator remarks on the pleasure and carnality accompanying her presence [MTM B3:87] [MTM B3:84]. In the Devil’s speech, hatred is described as the whole catalogue of carnalities, the ultimate lust, and the perfect perversion [MTM B8:242].","citations":["MTM B3:87","MTM B3:84","MTM B8:242"],"tags":["Women Characters","The Devil","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Carnival","definition":"Small traveling show that Hans and Professor Kirol join at Wisconsin Rapids. The Professor owns some equipment, rents or leases additional equipment and acts as needed, and performs as a mind reader, hypnotist, magician, and answer man [A:215-216]. Betty Hochstapler and the multiethnic Angelino family belong to the carnival world [A:216-218]. Hans spends five or six years there, travels several continents, learns fortune-telling and Deep Romany, reads minds, performs strongman feats including the Donkey Walk, develops hypnotism, and reads hundreds of books [A:218-220].","citations":["A:215-216","A:216-218","A:218-220"],"tags":["Carnival","Magic & Sorcery","Occupations","Family"]},{"name":"Carnival Business","definition":"Professor Kirol says the carnival business makes it possible to hire a performer of almost any nationality, providing the native speaker required for his method of learning a language [A:213].","citations":["A:213"],"tags":["Carnival","Occupations","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Carnivals","definition":"Traveling shows in which Hans spends five or six formative years [A:218]. Finnegan has also worked with a carnival for six weeks, attending to horses with shovel and pitchfork [A:220]. Carnivals appear among the lively arts said to be undergoing deliberate degradation after the Devil’s release [MTM B6:207].","citations":["A:218","A:220","MTM B6:207"],"tags":["Carnival","Finnegan","Occupations","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Carpathia","definition":"Region where X says he has seen Sebastian Hilton and the Countess, hoping that they can improve the situation there [MTM B6:201]. The name apparently alludes to the Carpathian region of Central and Eastern Europe; no precise locality is specified.","citations":["MTM B6:201"],"tags":["Eastern Europe"]},{"name":"Carpenter","definition":"One of Duffey’s natural crafts, alongside joinery and cabinetmaking [MTM B1:21]. Duffey and Bagby each claim to be the best carpenter, joiner, and cabinet man in St. Louis [MTM B2:46]. Duffey hires four fast carpenters to shorten a cafe table’s legs during the Vienna argument [MTM B13:433]; his own establishment contains rooms of his characteristic “intrepid and inexact carpentry,” including more than he remembers making [MTM B11:321]. Parts of the <em>Seabach</em> really have been cobbled together by drunken Irish carpenters from Hogan’s alley [D:135].","citations":["MTM B1:21","MTM B2:46","MTM B13:433","MTM B11:321","D:135"],"tags":["Duffey","Occupations","Ships"]},{"name":"Carpet (on the)","definition":"Called before superiors for reprimand. Casey is “on the carpet” before Colonel Laycheck and Majors Meyerhofer, Twicherby, and Terwilliger after challenging Stein’s lectures; Laycheck threatens punishment and forbids further interference [A:47].","citations":["A:47"],"tags":["Soldiers","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Carrados, Max","definition":"Name used by one of the experts consulted by Duffey, Biloxi Brannagan and Kasmir Gorshok about keeping the Antichrist off the <em>Argo</em>. The group includes fictional detectives and actual persons concealed under fictional names [MTM B13:454–455]. The person using the name Max Carrados, the Blind Detective, advises counting entries and exits, checking everyone, including oneself, and sounding the alarm when an arrival has no matching departure [MTM B13:456].","citations":["MTM B13:454–455","MTM B13:456"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Argo","Antichrist","Detection"]},{"name":"Carrion birds","definition":"Joseph says that even carrion birds would refuse the bodies of the evil people with whom Finnegan travels. Joseph then adds that he knows what those people are [D:95].","citations":["D:95"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Carrozza","definition":"Italian for a carriage. Finnegan, answering X’s literal-minded response to “crate,” includes <em>carrozza</em> among extravagant names for their eight-toned automobile, his “celestial omnibus” and “purring panther” [D:179].","citations":["D:179"],"tags":["Italy","Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Carruthers, Johnny (Johnny)","definition":"Chicago friend and schoolmate of Casey Szymansky, sharing his chamber at St. Bonaventure’s boarding school [A:161-162]. Mary Catherine Carruthers is his sister [A:168].","citations":["A:161-162","A:168"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Carruthers, Mary Catherine","definition":"Johnny Carruthers’s sister and Casey Szymansky’s repeatedly engaged but never married sweetheart [A:168] [MTM B8:261]. One of Duffey’s true talisman-created people, she frequents his Chicago bookstore from childhood [MTM B3:99]. His conduct with her on the bookstore sofa is photographed for later blackmail; the narrator censures it, although she reassures him and declares her love [MTM B4:111] [MTM B4:139] [MTM B8:261]. She joins the St. Louis reunion and remains an affectionate friend of Finnegan, who later visits her in Chicago and hears of her continuing attachment to Casey [A:97] [A:247-249]. On a later visit to New Orleans, she is about forty or fifty, having made substantial money working for Hilary Hilton. Though she seems the least of Duffey’s Splendid Animations, they are close, she calms his worries, and he senses her entanglement in his future [MTM B8:261-262]. Duffey especially enjoys her company at his final Chicago party before his death [MTM B12:383].","citations":["A:168","MTM B8:261","MTM B3:99","MTM B4:111","MTM B4:139","A:97","A:247-249","MTM B8:261-262","MTM B12:383"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Family","Creation","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Carson-Shipman Press","definition":"Earlier heading omitted the letter l from Carlson. See <em>Carlson-Shipman Press</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Literature","Journalism"]},{"name":"Cart","definition":"Duffey gives his first talisman to an Italian cake seller with a hokey-pokey pushcart [MTM B1:18]. In Ignatius Ti’s story, Lord Peter or Ali hen Taife becomes a donkey hauling a rock cart at the diamond mine [A:81]. Seaworthy dreams of carrying an unwanted corpse through an Indian city on a faulty cart; people repeatedly prevent him from abandoning it [D:72-73]. Gautier owns three small carts, each drawn by four goats [A:196]. Ramires and Elena Musquiz take the exhausted Finnegan to D’Hannis in their cart [A:203] [A:206]. Nauti expects her uncle to bring a piano from Batangas if he can obtain a cart [A:64-65]. A Royal Pop reminiscence names the Fulton Road Cart and Hallock’s Grocery Cart among old horse vehicles [MTM B11:329].","citations":["MTM B1:18","A:81","D:72-73","A:196","A:203","A:206","A:64-65","MTM B11:329"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan","Dreams","History & Memory","Shapeshifting"]},{"name":"Cart (Hokey-pokey pushcart)","definition":"Pushcart from which an Italian man sells little cakes. Duffey, no more than three years old, gives this man the first talisman he gives away [MTM B1:18].","citations":["MTM B1:18"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey"]},{"name":"Cartagena","definition":"Colombian port on the Caribbean coast, included in the <em>Brunhilde</em>’s itinerary after Portobello and Manana and before Santa Maria [D:85].","citations":["D:85"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Caribbean","Ships"]},{"name":"Cartographer","definition":"Angela Cosquin’s father, also an antiquarian, artist, and imitator. He first makes decorative antique-style maps on old leather, vellum, and parchment, then supplies confidence men with invented land-treasure and shipwreck-treasure maps, complete with archaic writing and purported affidavits [D:259-260].","citations":["D:259-260"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Occupations","Family","Crime"]},{"name":"Cartoonist-style","definition":"Finnegan’s description of what others take for birds or butterflies beside the mouths of figures in prehistoric rock paintings. He calls them speech-and-song balloons written both inside and outside, in an original language richer and more complex than modern speech [MTM B6:193].","citations":["MTM B6:193"],"tags":["Finnegan","Visual Arts","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Carvings","definition":"Finnegan’s low-round wall carvings remain in his burial cave on the Marianao Coast [MTM B12:371-374]. His scrimshaw pieces are offered to Duffey and would be recognized by seamen as Count Finnegan’s work [MTM B5:173]. He also carves four life-size cigar-store figures and sells them for a hundred dollars each [A:183]. The Devil’s gravestone bears the inscription I.N.C.G.P. Bogovitch [D:277]. In the thorn enclosure, Duffey admires the gnarled fruitwood carving of a lever handle before turning it and falling [MTM B12:392].","citations":["MTM B12:371-374","MTM B5:173","A:183","D:277","MTM B12:392"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Visual Arts","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Casa del Oro","definition":"“House of Gold,” one of the Spanish treasure ships represented on Angela Cosquin’s father’s master map of real or imaginary wrecks. Its name belongs to the group she compares with the litany of the Blessed Virgin [D:260-261].","citations":["D:260-261"],"tags":["Ships","Spain","Shipwrecks","Money & Trade","Prayer & Devotion"]},{"name":"Casetta","definition":"Italian diminutive meaning a little house. X offers <em>casetta</em>, alongside <em>cesto</em>, <em>paniera</em>, and <em>scatola</em>, when he takes Finnegan’s word “crate” literally rather than as a nickname for their eight-toned car [D:179].","citations":["D:179"],"tags":["Italy","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Casey Machine","definition":"An “Achronological Eaves-Dropping Machine,” encountered in coin parlors, tea rooms, and bars, through which users gain access to other people’s lives and thoughts. Newton Prescott recounts its history in a journal intended to treat his compulsion to forget it [CM:503-504]. Josephine McSorely’s account traces its origin to a gathering that performs its own Particular Judgment; Casey supplies the impulse of invasive curiosity but denies the machine’s existence and tries to withdraw his contribution [CM:506-507]. Prescott credits Casey, his own electronics, and the promoters together. He gives the devices charges collected after clinical death and, for added effect, dead people’s eyes [CM:507-509].<br><br>The machine develops within the wider Casey Condition. According to Prescott, everyone can read the minds, bodies, memories, and unconscious lives of the living, the dead, and the unborn, because all time is simultaneously accessible. The physical machines become unnecessary, though their trade remains profitable [CM:510-512]. The initial demand is largely for sexual exposure, slander, and blackmailing knowledge. But others find that fuller knowledge increases their love for people. Their appeal to grace and good will disputes Prescott’s assumption that degradation must prevail [CM:505] [CM:511-512].<br><br>The Condition occupies an abiding moment which, Prescott says, can remain eternal although people enter and leave it. Anticipating collective forgetfulness, he arranges perpetual federal payments and has a memory capsule implanted in his brain [CM:512-513]. The mania is forgotten; its emotional and historical contents are said to enter the earth’s forces. Prescott has the capsule removed and can no longer understand most of his own journal, though the money continues to arrive [CM:514-515].<br><br>The Addenda complicate this account. Prescott identifies himself as a ghostly fragment of Casey’s personality, moving through time as Casey’s eyes and ears. He describes a subscription device that hooks wealthy listeners and claims that Casey transfers his guilt to him. These are Prescott’s assertions, including his suspicion that the transfer has deceived God [CM:515-516].","citations":["CM:503-504","CM:506-507","CM:507-509","CM:510-512","CM:505","CM:511-512","CM:512-513","CM:514-515","CM:515-516"],"tags":["Time & Eternity","History & Memory","The Dead","Sin & Redemption","Money & Trade","Identity","Ghosts & Spirits"]},{"name":"Casey the Crock","definition":"Kasmir Gorshok, the ninth-century scholar and necromancer named as an earlier identity of Casey Szymansky [A:39]. Duffey’s commentary distinguishes this earlier medieval role from Casey’s later role as Prince Casimir [PG:534-535]. See <em>Crock (4) (Casey the)</em>.","citations":["A:39","PG:534-535"],"tags":["Identity","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Casimir (Prince)","definition":"Prince Casimir of Poland, identified in Duffey’s commentary as Casey’s later medieval identity and as Saint Casimir [PG:534-536]. Teresa quotes a prophecy that Duffey will carry Prince Casimir on the <em>Argo</em> to the coast nearest Armageddon to reign for a time as Antichrist [MTM B12:388]. The later voyage presents Casey, Gorshok, Peleus, and Prince Casimir as the same Antichristus at Habonim, while the growing shimmer marks the future’s uncertainty [MTM B13:454] [MTM B13:462]. Duffey’s commentary also allows that Casey may instead be a false or premature antichrist [PG:535].","citations":["PG:534-536","MTM B12:388","MTM B13:454","MTM B13:462","PG:535"],"tags":["Saints","Apocalyptic","Ships","Identity","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Casper, Robert","definition":"Corporal from Gobey in Morgan County, Tennessee, one of three American soldiers missing on Pulau Petir or Willy Jones Island on October 1, 1944 in Don Lewis’s story [D:44]. He twice outflanks the elusive Japanese soldier, then goes with Sergeant Santee to kill the wounded man [D:44-45]. Lost and quarrelling afterward, Casper sleeps on one ridge while Santee takes another and the third soldier remains below [D:46]. Don reports conflicting rumors that the other two were killed or returned separately with implausible stories; he does not know their fate [D:48].","citations":["D:44","D:44-45","D:46","D:48"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Islands","Pacific Theater","World War II","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Caspian Sea","definition":"Inland sea onto which the <em>Argo</em> must make a difficult passage in order to investigate reports about the Laughing Prince in Tartary [MTM B13:438].","citations":["MTM B13:438"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Cassady","definition":"One of the surnames given for leaders of the old-line humans’ resistance to the Royal Pop People. The leaders as a group fail because some defect and others die; the passage assigns no particular fate to Cassady [MTM B11:335].","citations":["MTM B11:335"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Cassandra","definition":"Trojan prophetess whose warnings are not believed. In a letter to Absalom Stein, X calls himself and Stein “Cassandras in pants”: their revelations provoke ridicule, though X says the leaders of the mockery know they are true [A:265-266].","citations":["A:265-266"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Cassino","definition":"Robber Cassino, played by Falo, Nauti, Vincent, and Finnegan with a pack whose missing four of clubs and six of diamonds are replaced by labeled paper. Falo always wins and attributes her advantage to having known the same cards since childhood [A:65].","citations":["A:65"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Castagnetta","definition":"Niccolo Crotolo says that castagnetta, meaning a little chestnut, is the newer word for a castanet, replacing the old word Crotolo which he gives as his family name [A:279].","citations":["A:279"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Caster (Mold)","definition":"One of Duffey’s practical skills, alongside metal spinning and wood turning, used while making props for Charley Murray’s magic acts at boarding school [MTM B1:21].","citations":["MTM B1:21"],"tags":["Duffey","Occupations"]},{"name":"Castitas","definition":"Chastity. An epigraph attributed to St. Francis de Sales identifies it with honesty and integrity, opposed to corruption. The narrator then calls a peculiar chastity of mind necessary to the highest intelligence, and says Duffey’s school friends retain these definitions even when Duffey himself falls into corruption [MTM B1:27].","citations":["MTM B1:27"],"tags":["Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Castle, Freddy","definition":"Australian soldier and companion of Tom Shire, smaller than Tom and with a rusty tinge. The narrator jokes that both men would remain unmistakably Australian in desert dress or even as ancient bones [A:5]. They befriend Finnegan, Vincent, Hans, and their party at the races, win their bets, drink beer, and dine together; Freddy contributes “Fingers were made before forks” [A:3-5]. Back at the barracks, he and Tom stage a hoax: they dress Finnegan and Vincent in hot winter tunics and digger hats, exaggerate the danger of entering the wet canteen, and enlist Sergeant Bushmaster in the joke when Freddy’s signal saves it from exposure [A:7] [A:10-14]. Freddy and Tom officiate at the subsequent drinking contest between Hans and Bushmaster, then join the pub crawl and later the Bay Hotel picnic and softball game [A:15] [A:20] [A:23] [A:29]. Finnegan later counts Freddy among those friendly to him in his normal life, while lamenting a barrier between himself and humanity [D:90].","citations":["A:5","A:3-5","A:7","A:10-14","A:15","A:20","A:23","A:29","D:90"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","Soldiers","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Castleman","definition":"“The last of the grand pirates,” still active in Charles Le Marin’s story. Le Marin says Castleman began as a rumrunner in the twenties and then preyed on rumrunners [D:242-243]. He infiltrates the <em>Sarcophagus</em> with dockside replacements indistinguishable from the departing crew, seizes its recorded and unrecorded cargo, and has the captain and remaining loyal men thrown overboard bound and weighted. The ship is left to furnish the familiar mystery of a perfectly ordered, apparently deserted vessel [D:244-245]. Le Marin’s own role becomes ambiguous when he claims to be his replacement and to have helped the takeover. He says he later insults the large, dangerous Castleman in a stateside bar, then leaves him alone rather than pushing his courage further; Castleman no longer uses that name. Johnny Duckwalk challenges the whole story as a version of the <em>Mary Celeste</em> tale [D:245-246].","citations":["D:242-243","D:244-245","D:245-246"],"tags":["Ships","Crew & Sailors","Maritime Trade","Crime","Bars & Taverns","Identity"]},{"name":"Castrador-Capros","definition":"Joker and dancer invented for Finnegan’s goat pun: he is called Castrador-Capros because he “cut capers.” Gautier finds the joke wanting, although several older goats appreciate it [A:199].","citations":["A:199"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Castrare becchi","definition":"Italian for castrating male goats, one of the meanings Finnegan invokes in his “cut capers” pun, alongside dancing and playing tricks [A:199].","citations":["A:199"],"tags":["Italy","Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Casual bottle of pills","definition":"In ward boy Ignatius Ti’s story, an ordinary bottle of pills falling near a dead man can seem to have been thrown by the corpse. It illustrates the fear aroused by the familiar body after death [A:85-86].","citations":["A:85-86"],"tags":["The Dead","Death & Mortality","Folklore"]},{"name":"Cat","definition":"Finnegan’s quiet climb through a window is compared to a cat’s [D:164]. The old woman associated with Patrick Stranahan’s Cat Castle kept eight or nine cats, not the legendary fifty [A:102]. Don Barnaby’s tall tale includes black-cat urine among the ingredients of Juju Joy Pills, with a cat afflicted by glanders in the particular recipe he describes [D:248-249]. X’s family account says Ifreann killed cats and tried to kill the children [D:280]. Biloxi Brannagan says he has watched the Deer Horn Ship on Cat Island without visiting it [D:204]. Cats also figure among the imagined carriers of contagious killing in the story-writing competition [MB:580].","citations":["D:164","A:102","D:248-249","D:280","D:204","MB:580"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Islands","Crime"]},{"name":"Cat Woman","definition":"Old woman who leaves the Cat Castle to her lawyer, Patrick Stranahan. The narrator says that she was old and cranky rather than exceptionally eccentric, and kept eight or nine cats, not fifty [A:102].","citations":["A:102"],"tags":["Women Characters"]},{"name":"Cat-calls","definition":"Mocking responses to the revelations of X and Absalom Stein, whom X calls “Cassandras in pants” in his letter. X says the leaders of the ridicule know the revelations are true [A:265-266].","citations":["A:265-266"],"tags":["Language & Words","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Catastrophe (Cosmic)","definition":"Biloxi Brannagan expects a cosmic catastrophe to sink the continents while leaving the islands secure; a cosmic student counters that the islands would follow the continents down [D:203]. Separately, the effect of Shirley Israel’s new drink on the company is compared to a natural catastrophe during the quarrel over moving a mule [MTM B4:130].","citations":["D:203","MTM B4:130"],"tags":["Cosmic Struggle","Islands","Duffey","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Catbirds","definition":"Birds rising from the low bushes along Hans’s running course through John William Schultz’s nursery [A:208]. Beth Keegan says a catbird in a draft corner of Duffey’s fireplace chimney is singing that the year is almost over; its song prompts her distress at the passing of youth [MTM B2:59].","citations":["A:208","MTM B2:59"],"tags":["Music","Duffey","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Catch-phrases","definition":"Set expressions in which Sally often speaks. The narrator contrasts their familiar wording with the electric directness of Sally herself [A:8].","citations":["A:8"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Catch-words","definition":"Slogans on which people metaphorically graze like sheep before going to slaughter, in X’s pessimistic reply to Doll about the people’s ability to save themselves [D:290].","citations":["D:290"],"tags":["Language & Words","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Catedratico","definition":"Spanish for a professor. One of the professional categories, with <em>abogado</em> and <em>medico</em>, in the Spanish-speaking town that Finnegan initially mistakes for a transformed New Orleans. His friend Ignacio is an abogado [A:269].","citations":["A:269"],"tags":["Finnegan","Occupations","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Catfish (Mad)","definition":"One of the fantastical murder devices proposed in the story-writing competition: mad catfish pour from every water to pursue the designated victim in a relentless frenzy [MB:580-581].","citations":["MB:580-581"],"tags":["Crime","Literature"]},{"name":"Catfish (Mississippi)","definition":"Anastasia says that some giant Mississippi catfish have human eyes but no further resemblance to true mermaids [D:34-35]. The narrator also jokingly includes in Finnegan’s mixed speech a mud-cat dialect unintelligible to other catfish [A:6].","citations":["D:34-35","A:6"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Catherine (Saint)","definition":"Saint seen beside Barbara aboard the <em>Navicula Petri</em> in Henry’s dream; both look like sea-urchins. The passage names her only Catherine, so identification with Alexandria or Siena remains uncertain [A:44-45]. Catherine of Siena lived in 1347-1380. The martyr legend belongs instead to Catherine of Alexandria: a learned noblewoman traditionally placed in the early fourth century, who defends Christianity before the emperor’s philosophers and converts them, survives the miraculous breaking of the wheel intended for her execution, and is beheaded. Traditions also associate her with the conversion of the empress and with the Fourteen Holy Helpers. Her book and wheel mark her learning and martyrdom; she is commemorated on November 25 in Catholic and Greek usage. The details of her life are historically uncertain, and her Roman-calendar observance, removed in 1969, was restored in 2002 as an optional memorial.","citations":["A:44-45"],"tags":["Saints","Dreams","Women Characters","Miracles","Church History"]},{"name":"Catherine, The","definition":"Name of the <em>Argo</em> when Dana Coscuin takes her around the Horn [MTM B13:405]. In <em>Half a Sky</em>, the ship is first <em>La Catalina</em>, encountered repeatedly at Guayaquil. Before buying her, Dana places a splinter of his murdered wife Catherine Dembinska’s skull aboard, joining ship and bride in a ghostly marriage. Valiente Tirana supplies the additional money for purchase and refitting. Dana outfits her with nine brass cannon, a crew including Damisa and Serafino, blue and green sails, and a ghost-green hull; her new name is painted <em>Catherine Dembinska</em>. Sailing against the Humboldt current, she is haunted by the stowaway Ifreann Chortovitch, whose malign influence, theft, and murder provoke an unsuccessful search and a priest’s prayers at Tocopillo. Following a wise woman’s advice, Dana baits Ifreann into the hold; Dana, Damisa, Serafino, and Osborne eventually throw him overboard. Later the crew remember incompatible mundane versions of these events. The near-invisible <em>Catherine</em> breaks the sea-siege of Valparaiso, destroying the revolutionary gunships and altering history and memory. On the voyage around the Horn she damages Ifreann’s pursuing ironclad <em>Brami Piekielne</em>, the Gates of Hell, and escapes to repair at Bahia Blanca. In early 1854 she reaches Basse-Terre. During the earthquake, Angelene, briefly the messenger who orders Dana to Carloforte, then tells him to bury the ship at the shoal called the Grave of Catarina Dama Binación. Dana describes the ship as his wife’s body during her purgatorial stay. With Jack Galopade he deliberately floods her; Catherine’s spirit departs as the ship explodes, and the vessel is destroyed in the encounter with Ifreann’s ship. (<em>Half a Sky</em>, PDF pp. 102, 105-108, 110-111, 116, 120, 122-124, 134, 138-139, 146, 151-153, 184, 198-199, 201-203).","citations":["MTM B13:405"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Crew & Sailors","Naval Warfare","Caribbean","Ghosts & Spirits","Shipwrecks","Heaven & Hell","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Catholic Center Party of America","definition":"Duffey’s political party, of which Casey says he is the sole member: “He is the Party.” Casey, d’Allesandro, Mary Frances, and Demetrio Glauch are prospective recruits rather than enrolled members [A:42].","citations":["A:42"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Duffey"]},{"name":"Catholic Church","definition":"The Church is central to the characters’ religious and political disputes. Casey describes Duffey’s one-man Catholic Center Party and his index of supposed infiltrators in Catholic lay groups [A:42-43]. Henry fears a Secular-Liberal Religion working from inside the Church but containing no Catholic element [A:125]. Casey attacks the <em>Bark</em>’s claim to be a Catholic publication, emphasizing its lack of ecclesiastical sanction [A:128]. Duffey visits Chicago’s stone, angel-covered St. Malachy’s, whose ruddy turrets catch the morning sun [MTM B3:87-88]. X’s family story invokes the parish records of St. Elmo near Krakow and says landed Catholic families employed Irish governesses for religious teaching and manners [D:279]. In X’s account of the Devil’s release, Catholic clergy are among the gathered notables and some cardinals worship the released Devil [MTM B6:204-206]. Duffey also reports X’s unreliable claim that Finnegan was secretly made a papal count [MTM B12:367]. The Peter-Bell is said to have belonged to one of Rome’s many St. Peter’s churches [MB:578]; later Finnegan is elected and crowned Pope Finnegan I in the <em>Argo</em> conclave [MB:597].","citations":["A:42-43","A:125","A:128","MTM B3:87-88","D:279","MTM B6:204-206","MTM B12:367","MB:578","MB:597"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Popes","Duffey","Finnegan","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Catholic Publication","definition":"Description claimed by the <em>Bark</em> and challenged by Casey, who calls it neo-Centrist and anti-Communist and says it has no ecclesiastical sanction [A:128].","citations":["A:128"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Journalism"]},{"name":"Cattle","definition":"Cattle boats are among the vessels on which Henry sails [A:124]; Tommy Cow-Town Borger and Jake-Leg Lewis are similarly called salt-water cowboys because their work is on cattle boats [D:222]. Trinali imagines a piece of driftwood as a former tree shading Georgia cattle [A:233], and Demetrio lists cattlemen among his bar’s customers [D:58]. Cattle graze among the strange fruits at Zabotski Flats [MTM B12:363]. Honeybucket claims cattle can be induced to eat plankton, though the narrator says he has never tried his scheme [D:219]. A Sudanese Christ statue carries a cattle herder’s crooked rod [MTM B3:104]. Mary Louise is described as a royal bovine or sacred cow [MTM B2:55]. The Pyramid Lord calls monkeys, humans, alligators, and whales his people’s cattle, a claim of dominion rather than an inventory of domestic animals [MB:593]. Cow bells sound around the Naxos landing [D:131]. In Anastasia’s mermaid account, the creatures the Norse called mermaids are instead sea-cows [D:34-35].","citations":["A:124","D:222","A:233","D:58","MTM B12:363","D:219","MTM B3:104","MTM B2:55","MB:593","D:131","D:34-35"],"tags":["Ships","Maritime Trade","Occupations","Visual Arts","Nymphs & Mermaids","Other Mythologies"]},{"name":"Cattle Boats","definition":"Vessels among those on which Henry Salvatore signs out from New Orleans, Port Arthur, and Galveston during his seagoing youth [A:124].","citations":["A:124"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Ports & Harbors","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Caucasian","definition":"Racial category invoked in the narrator’s comic complaint that anthropologists classify Cajuns under broad types, then as Alpine or Mediterranean, rather than recognizing them as a distinct people. The passage proceeds into deliberately impossible claims about Cajun blood, heads, language, and antiquity [A:123-124].","citations":["A:123-124"],"tags":["Louisiana","Identity"]},{"name":"Cauldron (Pit or)","definition":"Image of extinction in Absalom Stein’s account of forgotten people. He fears that people hanging by their fingernails at the rim of oblivion will fall into a pit or cauldron and cease ever to have existed unless someone remembers them [MTM B11:315].","citations":["MTM B11:315"],"tags":["Death & Mortality","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Cauliflowers (Prince of the)","definition":"“Prince of the Cauliflowers,” title given to Bello Belonki of the <em>Sporting News</em> among Dotty’s journalistic circle [MTM B7:213].","citations":["MTM B7:213"],"tags":["Journalism"]},{"name":"Causeway","definition":"Road by which Finnegan rides a produce truck onto the island and into the city for his final confrontation. He senses his antagonist there and considers it likely that his old ship is in port [D:275].","citations":["D:275"],"tags":["Islands","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Cavaldos, Dry Gulch","definition":"Frequent visitor to the Old Wooden Ship, called “Dry Gulch Cavaldos” in the text, who comes in twenty times a day. He is distinguished from the fifty regulars who live there [D:216].","citations":["D:216"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Cave","definition":"Gautier and his goats winter in a cave [A:194]; caves also run like spongework through the hollow cliffs of Black Papua [A:36-37]. In Finnegan’s primordial transport, caves are places of ancestral worship and of refuge after defeat [D:213-214]. Anastasia says mermaids live in water-level caves on rocky islands [D:34-35]. Finnegan dreams of sitting with an old teras in a cave or at the foot of a cliff [D:140], while Don Barnaby claims to visit caves in the middle of the earth during his joy-pill experience [D:251]. Finnegan’s sea-burial cave on the Marianao Coast is depicted in <em>The Resurrection of Count Finnegan</em> and is later visited by Duffey [MTM B12:371-374] [MB:550-551]. The cave or room beneath Mooring-Rock, reached from the well shaft, becomes the Room of the Conclave [MB:577] [MB:596]. Casey also uses an echo cave as a metaphor for political slogans amplified by apparently independent voices [A:185-186].","citations":["A:194","A:36-37","D:213-214","D:34-35","D:140","D:251","MTM B12:371-374","MB:550-551","MB:577","MB:596","A:185-186"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Nymphs & Mermaids","Finnegan","Seas & Oceans","Death & Mortality","Resurrection","Visual Arts","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cave Bear","definition":"Prehistoric bear, <em>Ursus spelaeus</em>. During his primordial transport, Finnegan speaks of his people’s brotherhood with the cave bear, giant stag, bison, and woolly rhinoceros [D:213]. Eva emerges from the ice wrapped in the blond skin of a female cave-bear [MTM B13:399-400].","citations":["D:213","MTM B13:399-400"],"tags":["Finnegan","Family"]},{"name":"Cave Bear (Female)","definition":"Animal whose blond skin wraps Eva when Duffey and his simian helpers free her from the ice [MTM B13:399-400]. See Cave Bear.","citations":["MTM B13:399-400"],"tags":["Women Characters"]},{"name":"Cavendish","definition":"Family name under which Charlotte and the Mullens household are known as show people at Chicago’s Colony Club. Manolo is excepted because he is apparently a newly acquired son [MTM B3:93].","citations":["MTM B3:93"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Cavern Spirits","definition":"Beings included with demiurges, angelics, pure intellects, and monsters in the narrator’s comparison: the extraordinary conversations of Duffey’s splendid animations, like theirs, cannot be recalled afterward [MTM B5:160-161].","citations":["MTM B5:160-161"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Creation"]},{"name":"Caverns (Vast)","definition":"Vast caverns of the Papuans’ “under-persons,” the unconscious in which the narrator says all other peoples and lands subsist. The Kiriwinans understand that their existence depends on being maintained there [A:37].","citations":["A:37"],"tags":["Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Cayene","definition":"Earlier heading omitted a letter from the port’s name. See <em>Cayenne</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","France"]},{"name":"Cayenne","definition":"Cayenne, the French Guianan port visited by the <em>Brunhilde</em> before Belem, Sao Luis, Camocim, Fortaleza, and Natal on the outward voyage [D:86].","citations":["D:86"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","France"]},{"name":"Cayman","definition":"Island destination named with Kingston and Moule in the opening voyage stanza of Finnegan’s chapter [A:171].","citations":["A:171"],"tags":["Islands","Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"CC624","definition":"Star designation in the list of positions Hans learns to calculate under the Professor’s instruction [A:214]. The supplied page prints “CC624”; it does not identify the star further.","citations":["A:214"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cecil","definition":"Name Le Marin says a Nigerian acquaintance gave his moustache, in the joke arising from Hildegarde Katt’s description of her husband Tommy [D:229].","citations":["D:229"],"tags":["Language & Words","Identity"]},{"name":"Cedar","definition":"Wood Duffey says would have been used for the <em>Argo</em> had she been built in a Black Sea port at that remote date. In his account she was instead built at Ragusa from Dinaric oak; he qualifies the account as symbolically rather than ordinarily accurate [MTM B13:412].","citations":["MTM B13:412"],"tags":["Ships","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Ceiling","definition":"Bagby’s and Duffey’s adjoining St. Louis buildings have walnut ceilings as well as walnut walls, rafters, and other woodwork [MTM B2:46]. A noose hangs from the ceiling of the vacant fourth room in Elroy Redheart’s heart artwork [MTM B7:224]. Finnegan recalls drifting through the Cajun swamps with Giulio under a ceiling of fronds only three feet above their flatboat [MTM B5:174].","citations":["MTM B2:46","MTM B7:224","MTM B5:174"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Celeste, Marie","definition":"“Marie Celeste,” Johnny Duckwalk’s name for the derelict-ship story he accuses Le Marin of recycling. Le Marin insists on the form “Mary Celeste” and denies the resemblance [D:246].","citations":["D:246"],"tags":["Ships","Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cell (attic)","definition":"Little closet or cell off the main attic room in the large town house where Duffey lives with pretended kindred. It contains the peaked fourth-floor Prisoner John Window, from which he sees the three slant-faced boys [MTM B1:12].","citations":["MTM B1:12"],"tags":["Duffey","Family"]},{"name":"Cellini","definition":"Benvenuto Cellini (1500-1571), Florentine goldsmith, sculptor, and autobiographer. Papa Piccone names him with Buonorroti and Sancho in answer to Patrick Stranahan’s dismissal of later artists; Patrick replies that such craftsmen were good but should have remained anonymous [A:111]. Cellini’s works include the bronze <em>Perseus with the Head of Medusa</em> in Florence and the gold <em>Saliera</em> made for Francis I. His <em>Autobiography</em>, composed late in his life and partly dictated, offers a vivid, self-dramatizing account of Renaissance craftsmanship, patronage, quarrels, and his own exploits.","citations":["A:111"],"tags":["Italy","Literary Figures","Visual Arts","Literature"]},{"name":"Celluloid Cuffs","definition":"Cuffs or gauntlets Lily Koch snaps on as her merchant’s trademark at her Saturday art sales. After the noon closing and departure of the customers, she unsnaps them until the next sale [MTM B1:28-29].","citations":["MTM B1:28-29"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Occupations","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Cenacle","definition":"The Upper Room associated with the Last Supper. The <em>Argo</em>’s recovered Lord’s Table is said to have come from the Cenaculum in Jerusalem and to rest in her Bread and Wine Room [MTM B13:422]. “Cenacle” also names the supper room at Randal’s where Duffey’s party watches the television over-picture of Casey [MTM B12:380].","citations":["MTM B13:422","MTM B12:380"],"tags":["New Testament","Ships","Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Cenotaphs (Cretan)","definition":"Cretan tomb-monuments from which, the narrator says, statuettes of Anastasia have been excavated. The claim accompanies her resemblance to a ship’s carved figurehead and the question of what she really is [D:32].","citations":["D:32"],"tags":["Women Characters","Visual Arts","Identity"]},{"name":"Centaur","definition":"Centaurus, partly visible and labeled at the right edge of the celestial ship illustration on the cover of the supplied <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> PDF [MTM:1].","citations":["MTM:1"],"tags":["Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Centrist","definition":"Political position Casey attributes to Duffey, sole member of the Catholic Center Party of America [A:42]. Henry Salvatore, in conversation with his father, declares “We are the Centrists” and says they stand in the middle and build the world around themselves [A:125].","citations":["A:42","A:125"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Duffey"]},{"name":"Centrists (Neo-)","definition":"Term in Casey’s hostile description of the <em>Bark</em> as a neo-Centrist Catholic publication; he questions whether its hyphenated label has any meaning [A:128]. Stein had proposed the long title <em>Neo-Centrist Review of Evolvate Socio-Geopolitical Motifs in a Disintegrating World</em> for the magazine [A:121].","citations":["A:128","A:121"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Journalism","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Centuries","definition":"Historical periods repeatedly mixed with uncertain memories, symbolic history, and long lives. The Capuchin dates Sicilian Naxos to the seventh century before Christ [D:119]; Casey’s Kasmir Gorshok identity is placed in the ninth century [A:39]; Anastasia says Norse sea-cows were taken as mermaids as late as the tenth [D:35]. Lawrence tells Henry that he cannot restore the thirteenth century, only the sixteenth [A:125]. Scanlon assigns different colors to sixteenth- and eighteenth-century seas [A:234], and Angela’s father imitates sixteenth-, seventeenth-, and eighteenth-century handwriting on his treasure maps [D:260]. The postscript describes Ellis’s Mermaid Series as a revival of seventeenth-century drama [PS:476]. X is described as a mid-nineteenth-century north Italian type [MTM B6:200], and his family account discusses Irish governesses and obscure people of a century earlier [D:279-280]. Duffey remembers three overlapping twentieth-century childhoods [MTM B1:6] and later interprets the Presence through twentieth-century Jungian imagery, which Plunket rebukes as mistaking reality for art [MTM B12:390]. The young Casey invents a Blue Book for 2001 [A:157]. Private Gregory, not Duffey, recalls centuries at sea [A:77]. The narrator pictures Pan as old for twenty-odd centuries [A:194]; Marie Courtois has a beauty forbidden for centuries [D:37]. A passage attributed to Galen says Daemons must return each century to renew their descendants [D:160-161]. Finnegan blames centuries of altered sky-color on smoke [D:127]. The <em>Argo</em> has logbooks of voyages lasting centuries and extraordinarily long-lived pilots [MTM B13:405]; X’s history makes the Devil’s imprisonment similarly span centuries [MTM B6:204-205]. Beth Keegan’s theatrical walk is extravagantly compared with something unseen for thirty centuries, since Tarshish fell [MTM B2:57].","citations":["D:119","A:39","D:35","A:125","A:234","D:260","PS:476","MTM B6:200","D:279-280","MTM B1:6","MTM B12:390","A:157","A:77","A:194","D:37","D:160-161","D:127","MTM B13:405","MTM B6:204-205","MTM B2:57"],"tags":["Time & Eternity","History & Memory","Identity","Ships","Literature"]},{"name":"Cephalic Index","definition":"Term in Casey’s school notebook: he says one adds three to the skull index to obtain the cephalic index, then jokes that there are more living roundheads than dead ones and threatens an unnamed living example [A:162-163].","citations":["A:162-163"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cepheids","definition":"Pulsating variable stars. Professor Kirol places the noonday devil among the Cepheids in his account of time, space, and eternity [A:215].","citations":["A:215"],"tags":["The Devil","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Ceram Sea","definition":"Indonesian sea, also called the Seram Sea, between Seram and northwestern New Guinea. It is among the many seas sailed by the <em>Argo</em> and remembered by Melchisedech [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Cerebral Athleticism","definition":"Professor Kirol’s term for rapid calculation, large additions, squaring, extracting roots, and prodigious memory. He distinguishes these feats from thought, while insisting that a developed memory is an indispensable tool for a thinking person [A:212].","citations":["A:212"],"tags":["History & Memory"]},{"name":"Ceremonies, The","definition":"Ritual proceedings of the Conclave at Babylonia Bagascia. After offerings to the assembled cults, Conrad Stone Hackenschmidt conducts the roll call and coerced election of a straw-man, which Monsignor X burns. The delegates then make their avowals and denials [MB:565-568].","citations":["MB:565-568"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia"]},{"name":"Ceremony","definition":"In Finnegan’s story, a Jesuit gives his Devil painting a short-form exorcism, while saying that it is unnecessary to waste ceremony on the Devil [D:255]. The kindred’s farewell to fifteen-year-old Duffey after he finishes high school is a hand-washing ceremony, concluded with a gift of one hundred dollars [MTM B2:38]. Howland ceremoniously uncorks and replaces Finnegan’s bottle while posing as his valet for Casey [A:182]. The Conclave at Babylonia Bagascia has its own offerings, straw-man election and burning, and avowals and denials [MB:565-568].","citations":["D:255","MTM B2:38","A:182","MB:565-568"],"tags":["Religious Orders","Ecclesiastical","The Devil","Finnegan","Duffey","Ekklesia","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Certainty","definition":"Finnegan asserts that Papa Devil is dead finally, after the deaths of the original and the surrogate [D:150]. He recalls Anastasia’s promise that they will be together again, first professing certainty and then doubting that his own people possess that promise [D:149]. Seaworthy keeps Finnegan as confirmation of his sanity after the man he believes he killed appears alive; X later explains the confusion as a substitution of twins [D:86] [D:289]. A passage attributed to Belloc lists public certainty among the communal supports lost through the Reformation’s isolation of the soul [MTM B5:176]. Finnegan’s conviction that he is the only person in the world persists [A:205]; elsewhere the narrator jokes that certainty of hostile intent develops when someone repeatedly shoots at you [D:197].","citations":["D:150","D:149","D:86","D:289","MTM B5:176","A:205","D:197"],"tags":["Finnegan","The Devil","History & Memory","Metaphysics","Death & Mortality","Resurrection","Church History"]},{"name":"Cerulean saltcellar","definition":"One of Finnegan’s extravagant self-descriptions during his St. Patrick’s Day logorrhea. He calls himself a perfectly spherical “saitcaster,” the grandfather of the saltshakers, and the cerulean saltcellar [D:189].","citations":["D:189"],"tags":["Finnegan","Alcohol","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Chaff-cup","definition":"Term equated with “glume” in Casey’s school notebook, beside the observation that the beard of wheat is called the awn [A:164].","citations":["A:164"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Chain","definition":"Asking Dan says Casey’s new zoot suit needs a jangle chain, which he promises to supply later [A:261]. Truck drivers carry lengths of chain as weapons in Casey’s imagined revolution [A:160]. Finnegan wears Heliogabalus on a neck chain with his Miraculous Medal [A:61]. Zabotski pulls a boat-whistle chain as his ark floats away [MTM B9:287]. A fictional murder-contest proposal describes an exponentially spreading chain reaction of animals driven to attack one victim [MB:580-581].","citations":["A:261","A:160","A:61","MTM B9:287","MB:580-581"],"tags":["Ships","Finnegan","Weapons","Crime","Dreams"]},{"name":"Chains","definition":"Lengths of chain arm the truck drivers in Casey’s imagined revolution [A:160]. Asking Dan promises Casey a jangle chain to complete his zoot-suit outfit [A:261]. See Chain.","citations":["A:160","A:261"],"tags":["Occupations","Dreams"]},{"name":"Chair","definition":"Three huge easy chairs fill much of the <em>Brunhilde</em>’s small bar [D:22]. Finnegan and Anastasia watch the town from a giant’s chair, an eight-foot-wide dished and backed slab among old ruins; the stop may be Paraiso or Campeche [D:67-70]. Finnegan drinks all night in a chair by X’s window [D:157], and slumps in an easy chair while entertaining Casey with lies and Howland’s valet routine [A:182]. Patrick Stranahan shoots his pistol from an easy chair [A:104]; each boy in St. Martin’s six-boy rooms has his own chair [A:161]. Bagby offers Duffey a barber chair [MTM B2:43]. Shot Gun Schaeffer barricades his door with a chair to escape Basin Street’s intimidation [D:223]. At the Voltaire Festival, Voltaire is awakened and seated to watch the spectacle [MB:563]. Casey descends from his Curile Chair in the Zodiac to attend Duffey’s party [MTM B12:383]. Gertrude says Biloxi has occupied the same chair for thirty years [MTM B13:409]; in his own earlier account he gives twenty-five years in that chair and ten in its predecessor [D:204].","citations":["D:22","D:67-70","D:157","A:182","A:104","A:161","MTM B2:43","D:223","MB:563","MTM B12:383","MTM B13:409","D:204"],"tags":["Ships","Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Chaldee","definition":"Older term for Biblical Aramaic. The name <em>Argo</em> appears on the ship in Latin, Greek, Hebrew, and Chaldee letters [MB:577]. Anastasia imagines that the waves write Chaldee on the sand; Finnegan claims that all clairvoyants can read it and that its message frightens him [D:38]. The narrator of “The Casey Machine” sees a warning as handwriting on the wall in archaic Babylonian or Chaldee [CM:512].","citations":["MB:577","D:38","CM:512"],"tags":["Ships","Finnegan","Language & Words","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Chalice","definition":"Liturgical cup. The discussion of holy wealth asks whether it is God’s vanity or joy to be touched only by the gold of the gold-lined chalice [MTM B4:125]. Ignatius Cardinal Ti’s grave bears the words “My chalice indeed you shall drink” [MB:588-589]. The <em>Argo</em>’s recovered treasures include the Holy Grail from Glastonbury, separately from the Lord’s Table from the Jerusalem Cenaculum [MTM B13:422].","citations":["MTM B4:125","MB:588-589","MTM B13:422"],"tags":["Liturgy","Ships","Duffey","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Chalk","definition":"Casey’s school notebook says putty is made of chalk and linseed oil [A:164]. In <em>Promantia</em>, the visionary mathematician uses chalk to demonstrate equations implying a changed universe before graduate students [PG:542].","citations":["A:164","PG:542"],"tags":["Prophecy & Visions","Creation"]},{"name":"Chamber","definition":"St. Martin’s Hall at St. Bonaventure’s has six-boy chambers, which give <em>The Chambers</em> its name and chamber-pot masthead [A:167]. In Ignacio’s tale, Joseph of Maintz fashions a spherical passenger chamber for his moon rocket [A:271]. Finnegan’s comic letter imagines a gas-chamber sentence had he demanded a jury [A:264]. His story of an analyst’s dissection leads to a chamber where the scattered remains glow with a possible diffused essence, complicating the analyst’s conclusion that the man was merely an empty jug wrapped in primordial straw [MTM B5:181-182]. Elroy Redheart’s ceramic heart has four dog-house-sized chambers filled with miniature scenes [MTM B7:221]. A sea-cave burial crypt appears in <em>The Resurrection of Count Finnegan</em> [MB:550].","citations":["A:167","A:271","A:264","MTM B5:181-182","MTM B7:221","MB:550"],"tags":["Finnegan","Visual Arts","Death & Mortality","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Chamber Pot","definition":"The <em>Crock</em>’s unchanging cover depicts an old and ornate chamber pot, engraved by d’Allesandro during his middle Chicago period [A:40]. Its school-paper predecessor, <em>The Chambers</em>, has hand-drawn mastheads showing a row of chamber pots in imitation of the <em>Towers</em> silhouette [A:167].","citations":["A:40","A:167"],"tags":["Journalism","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Chamberings","definition":"Archaic term for wanton or sexual conduct. In Finnegan’s “Antipodal Ode” to Loy, the lovers say they have not indulged in chamberings as worldly people do [A:32].","citations":["A:32"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Chambers, The (school paper)","definition":"Manuscript paper produced by boys in St. Martin’s Hall at St. Bonaventure’s. Audifax O’Hanlon draws each masthead individually as a row of chamber pots whose silhouette imitates the towers on Gregory Hall’s <em>Towers</em>. The name comes from St. Martin’s six-boy rooms [A:167].","citations":["A:167"],"tags":["Journalism","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Chameleon","definition":"Image of changeable appearance and identity. Casey’s second imaginary world is an erotic, incoherent chameleon life [A:157]. To Finnegan, Anastasia seems a chameleon who changes whenever he looks at her [D:13]. In Don Barnaby’s joy-pill story, he marries a chameleon-like woman whose names and appearances vary while he recognizes her as the same person [D:250].","citations":["A:157","D:13","D:250"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Dreams","Identity","Shapeshifting"]},{"name":"Chameleon life","definition":"Casey’s second imaginary world, an erotic fantasy begun before he is eight. Its changing company includes queens, nymphs, Amazons, gladiators, bull-fighters, and acrobats drawn from history and legend [A:157].","citations":["A:157"],"tags":["Dreams"]},{"name":"Chamonix","definition":"Place named by Finnegan with St. Zermatto and Guebwiller in his argument about prehistoric rock paintings. He says supposed butterfly images are actually speech-and-song balloons in an original complex language. Duffey challenges him and calls the painters Neanderthals [MTM B6:193].","citations":["MTM B6:193"],"tags":["Finnegan","Visual Arts","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Champion","definition":"Joe Bushmaster, the Lion of the South, holds the heavyweight beer-drinking championship until Hans Schultz, the Milwaukee Seidel, defeats him [A:17] [A:19] [A:23]. The narrator jokes that a woman in a contestant’s corner once brought down a former champion [A:19]. Johnny Duckwalk is an old champion drinker whom fewer than a hundred men in the world can outdrink [D:236]. Casey’s childhood record books make his imagined sporting self the greatest running back, gaining sixteen and a fraction yards per carry [A:156-157]. Herman Hercules, not Finnegan, says he was the ninety-nine-pound wrestling champion for many years [MB:585].","citations":["A:17","A:19","A:23","D:236","A:156-157","MB:585"],"tags":["Alcohol","Dreams","Identity"]},{"name":"Chance","definition":"Finnegan doubts Hans’s chances against Bushmaster, although Hans confidently agrees to bet [A:17]. Dotty’s hopes for a life with Finnegan are described as still having every chance even as the narrator suggests their failure [A:93-94]. Colonel Laycheck offers Casey a chance to retreat from challenging Stein [A:47]. The amnesiac sergeant asks for a chance to regain reliable memory of his name [A:71-74]. Duffey says he gave Casey’s father a talisman in a chance encounter [MTM B5:151]. Don Lewis briefly thinks he may escape because he has accounted for every man on the <em>Brunhilde</em>; an unexpected killer immediately destroys that hope [D:109]. Finnegan calls to the graveyard dead because he wants to give them a chance to rise [D:284]. Stein hopes to save forgotten people by remembering their faces [MTM B11:315-316]. The seventh contingency of the girls’ play promises the beatific vision with only one chance in a hundred billion of failure, then introduces a contrary omen [MTM B10:296-297].","citations":["A:17","A:93-94","A:47","A:71-74","MTM B5:151","D:109","D:284","MTM B11:315-316","MTM B10:296-297"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","The Dead","History & Memory","Death & Mortality","Theater"]},{"name":"Changeling","definition":"In folklore, a fairy child substituted for a stolen human child. The narrator calls Finnegan a changeling of the other or double blood [A:39]. Patricia Solli says that, despite their shared childhood, he seems a changeling to her [A:175]; <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> repeats her speech [D:18-19]. Seaworthy speaks of changelings like himself and Finnegan coming late to understanding their nature [D:11].","citations":["A:39","A:175","D:18-19","D:11"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","Folklore"]},{"name":"Channel (Irish)","definition":"New Orleans neighborhood from which Patricia Solli says her family came, distinguishing it from the French Quarter [A:175].","citations":["A:175"],"tags":["New Orleans","Family"]},{"name":"Channel (Shipping)","definition":"The <em>Brunhilde</em> reaches the channel as its voyage begins; its lurch accompanies the narrator’s turning wheel of fortune [D:28]. In Zabotski’s flood story, a reporter describes the flooded channel outside his house, humorously called Dumaine Street, as sufficient to carry an ocean-going ship away in a good rain [MTM B9:289].","citations":["D:28","MTM B9:289"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Channels of communication","definition":"Signals and communication arrangements X proposes before he, Doll, and Finnegan scatter and later meet beside Ifreann’s grave. X offers to arrange the plan, though he says they will also know the time and place intuitively [D:186].","citations":["D:186"],"tags":["Finnegan","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Chant","definition":"Howland chants his five-gophers jingle to a hoedown tune while he and Finnegan lie in a hayloft [A:179]. Beth Keegan, now Beth Erlenbaum, greets Duffey’s St. Louis visit with a chant beginning “Kerowl, kerowl!” [MTM B5:154].","citations":["A:179","MTM B5:154"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Music"]},{"name":"Chaos","definition":"Askandanakandrian says nervous-merchandise dealers try to bring confusion out of chaos, because less complexity invites competition and more is intolerable [A:256-257]. The narrator of <em>Promantia</em> posits a dozen false chaoses before true chaos, a dozen false creations before real creation, and twenty false falls before the falls of angels and humanity [PG:530].","citations":["A:256-257","PG:530"],"tags":["Creation","Angels & Demons","Metaphysics","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Chaparral","definition":"Texas brush country where Finnegan spends about 105 hours, from Tuesday evening until Sunday morning, somewhere in Medina or Frio County, probably northeast of the Frio branch. The interval includes delirium, noonday devils, and portions of the twelve-year life he shares with Theresa in another, perhaps valid, existence [A:202-205].","citations":["A:202-205"],"tags":["The Devil","Finnegan","Dreams","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Chapel (Our Lady of the Dolphins)","definition":"Chapel of Christian mermaids on a small island between Patmos and Lipsos, according to Anastasia’s account of mermaid history. She places the community in the second century and says the chapel still stands in poor condition [D:35-36].","citations":["D:35-36"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Islands","Greece"]},{"name":"Character Defect","definition":"Finnegan’s explanation to Doll for his behavior: he has a character defect and is a slob. The exchange follows his suggestion that they fight a few rounds without trying for a knockout, and Doll’s reply that she always fights for one [D:193].","citations":["D:193"],"tags":["Finnegan","Combat"]},{"name":"Charisma","definition":"Show Boat jokingly claims special charisma as the source of her knowledge that the serpent of Eden is the many-armed hydra still active in the world [A:263]. Charisma is also among the fashionable words embellishing the Devil’s public speech, concealing no change in its underlying message [MTM B8:243].","citations":["A:263","MTM B8:243"],"tags":["The Devil","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Charity","definition":"Marie tells Sally that charity is a Latin word for love [A:9]. Duffey’s charity is imperfect but has gained some ground from his natural state [MTM B4:110]. Deeds of kindness and charity join the Law and worship of God among the three requirements for preserving the world [MTM B7:228]. Art as Charity belongs to the synthesis Duffey seeks and recognizes in Teresa [MTM B7:229]. It is Duffey whose charity prevents his phoning Casey at a quarter past three in the morning about the stolen box; X urges the immediate call [A:137].","citations":["A:9","MTM B4:110","MTM B7:228","MTM B7:229","A:137"],"tags":["Duffey","Language & Words","Visual Arts","Theology"]},{"name":"Charlemagne","definition":"Charles or Carl the Great (birth year disputed, traditionally 742 or 747/748; died 814), king of the Franks from 768 and Lombards from 774, crowned emperor by Pope Leo III in 800. His Carolingian empire covered much of western and central Europe, with Aachen a major seat of rule, and revived western Roman imperial authority. His government used royal envoys, the <em>missi dominici</em>, to supervise justice and promoted legal and monetary reform, including new weights and coinage. His patronage of scholars such as Alcuin supported schools, Latin learning, the copying of classical works, Carolingian minuscule, and church and liturgical reform. These political and cultural legacies underlie the later epithet Father of Europe; the designation Holy Roman Emperor is retrospective. In Ignacio’s tale, Archbishop Turpin, a peer of Charlemagne, records Joseph of Maintz’s journey to the moon [A:271]. The Iron Crown of Charlemagne is among the treasures recovered by the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:422].","citations":["A:271","MTM B13:422"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Church History","History & Memory","Literature","Ships"]},{"name":"Charles (Uncle)","definition":"Charles Salvatore, one of Henry Salvatore’s closest uncles, named with Alphonse, Fulgence, George, and Matthew [A:122].","citations":["A:122"],"tags":["Family","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Charnel house","definition":"One destination on the blood-covered crossroads sign in Patrick Stranahan’s ominous account of the postwar world, alongside nowhere, easy house, and crossbar hotel [MTM B5:164].","citations":["MTM B5:164"],"tags":["Death & Mortality","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Charon's (boat)","definition":"Papadiabolous II’s image of death as the alternative to escaping Naxos in his own boat. He tells Finnegan and Anastasia that none of the three will return to the <em>Brunhilde</em>: they will leave in his vessel or Charon’s. His boat is the <em>Seabach</em>, pronounced <em>Shouakh</em> and also called the <em>Hawk</em> [D:121-122].","citations":["D:121-122"],"tags":["Underworld","Ships","Greece","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Chart","definition":"Duffey studies Zabotski’s charts, schematics, and projections while pondering their contradictory sense of historical progress [MTM B9:273]. The <em>Argo</em>’s chart room contains instructions for locating a second log book, although Duffey cannot reliably find the instructions or even the room [MTM B13:411].","citations":["MTM B9:273","MTM B13:411"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Duffey","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Chart Room","definition":"The <em>Argo</em>’s chart room contains instructions for finding the alternate log of dislocated futures, but Duffey cannot always find the directions or even the room [MTM B13:411]. In a separate scene concerning the <em>Brunhilde</em>, X asks Finnegan what tracings he made in its chart room as part of an interrogation that exposes Finnegan’s failure to gather the evidence X expects [D:188-189].","citations":["MTM B13:411","D:188-189"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Finnegan","Duffey"]},{"name":"Charter (carnival acts)","definition":"To hire or engage performers: Professor Kirol sometimes charters acts and forms combinations when assembling his carnival [A:215].","citations":["A:215"],"tags":["Carnival","Occupations"]},{"name":"Charter (of Acts)","definition":"Earlier heading treated the verb charter as the title of a document. See <em>Charter (carnival acts)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Carnival","Occupations"]},{"name":"Chartres Street","definition":"Street tentatively identified as the location of Duffey’s current establishment in New Orleans. The narrator immediately qualifies the location as Chartres Street or whatever street it was [MTM B11:310].","citations":["MTM B11:310"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey"]},{"name":"Chase, The","definition":"The pursuit in a saying Endymeon attributes to the Arabs: days spent in the chase do not count against the length of life. He uses this analogy to claim that translation does not exhaust an author’s allotted creative talent [A:237].","citations":["A:237"],"tags":["Time & Eternity","Literature"]},{"name":"Chasm","definition":"Bottomless fissure in Duffey’s central apocalyptic dream, swallowing towns and people, especially the young. His magic fails to arrest it, but he crosses to the other side and says a veil now separates him from others. The giants’ hands he summons appear severed and manacled, then become a child’s mittens joined by their drawstring. Beth interprets the dream as his coming seven-year absence [MTM B2:68-69].","citations":["MTM B2:68-69"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Giants & Titans","Duffey","Dreams"]},{"name":"Chastaigne, Joseph","definition":"Proprietor of the Imperial Bar or Royal on Basse-Terre, jokingly called Sloppy Joe’s by Finnegan and his three companions. The place is fastidious, green-fronted, breezy, and scented with rum and lime; the narrator likens its faint tinkling to both ice in a glass and the music of the spheres [D:235]. After Finnegan’s companions leave, Chastaigne sees the same restlessness in him and predicts that he too will leave within a month, on the next boat. He and Angela Cosquin are Finnegan’s remaining close friends there [D:266].","citations":["D:235","D:266"],"tags":["Caribbean","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Chastellain","definition":"One of the named would-be leaders of the non-splendid, old-line humans resisting the Royal Pop transformation. Their leadership fails as some join the splendid people and others die disintegrating into trash; Chastellain’s individual fate is not specified [MTM B11:335].","citations":["MTM B11:335"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Chastity","definition":"Virtue praised in a passage attributed to St. Francis de Sales as the lily among virtues and as integrity opposed to corruption. The narrator connects chastity of mind with the highest intelligence and says Duffey sometimes fails in practice but never redefines corruption as virtue [MTM B1:27]. Duffey condemns a war on chastity alongside wars on poverty and obedience [MTM B6:193]. In <em>Promantia</em>’s account of Casey’s past lives, Prince Casimir’s lifelong vow of chastity frustrates his father’s succession plans [PG:536].","citations":["MTM B1:27","MTM B6:193","PG:536"],"tags":["Duffey","Theology","Saints","Identity"]},{"name":"Chattering (little idiot)","definition":"Corporal Robert Casper’s instruction in Don Lewis’s patrol story: keep the apparently Japanese soldier talking while Casper gets behind him. The fugitive repeatedly eludes their attempts to surround him [D:44-45].","citations":["D:44-45"],"tags":["Soldiers","Pacific Theater"]},{"name":"Cheap-Shot Artist, The (also Old Clootie)","definition":"The Devil as the Cheap-Shot Artist and father of all cheap-shot artists. X identifies him as the power behind Finnegan and Dotty’s reported deaths [MTM B8:238]. Soon afterward Duffey encounters him as the Majesty at a meeting of the New Catholic Press Guild and calls him Old Clootie [MTM B8:239-243]. Entering the Devil’s mind, Duffey hears him glory in cheap shots, hatred, slander, destruction, and the negation of creation [MTM B8:242]. Clootie is a Scots name associated with the Devil. Duffey later uses it to unsettle the adversary in their struggle over his creative powers [MTM B10:303-304].","citations":["MTM B8:238","MTM B8:239-243","MTM B8:242","MTM B10:303-304"],"tags":["The Devil","Duffey","Cosmic Struggle","Creation","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Checkbook","definition":"Gertrude Brannagan proposes to fetch her checkbook to help Eva obtain Kate’s Neanderthal after the men’s magically manufactured money proves impractical [MTM B13:415]. Sebastian Hilton pays Lily Koch a hundred dollars in cash for a statuette because he has left his checkbook at school [MTM B1:28-29].","citations":["MTM B13:415","MTM B1:28-29"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Visual Arts","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Cheek","definition":"Finnegan reports having a Z cut into his cheek after his jukebox quarrel [A:264]. Duffey lightly nicks Bagby’s cheek while shaving him, to keep his new acquaintance from getting too far ahead in their verbal contest [MTM B2:45].","citations":["A:264","MTM B2:45"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey"]},{"name":"Cheese","definition":"Duffey eats cheese with black bread and beer at the Dublin [MTM B2:39], then buys ten pounds as stock for his own establishment [MTM B2:41]. Mary Catherine serves blue cheese to Finnegan [A:247], and Letitia includes cheese in the meal she sets out for Giulio [MTM B3:106]. The Harvesters’ Dinner at the Colony Club also includes cheeses [MTM B3:94]. “Cheese Factory” is among the words on milk-can covers that young Duffey remembers or obtains from other minds [MTM B1:17]. Gautier eats a pound of cheese daily and makes two pounds to barter [A:196]. Honeybucket calls his proposed plankton-and-livestock venture the Ancient Syrian Cheese Company, although the narrator reveals that he has not tried the plan and does not know what plankton looks like [D:219].","citations":["MTM B2:39","MTM B2:41","A:247","MTM B3:106","MTM B3:94","MTM B1:17","A:196","D:219"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan","Food & Dining","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Cheese Factory","definition":"Words on milk-can covers among the iron-lettered household inscriptions that young Duffey knows by observation or by borrowing other people’s memories [MTM B1:17].","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Duffey","Language & Words","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Chef (from Martinique)","definition":"Unnamed chef from Martinique at Howland Jerome Howland’s luxurious Club Royal in Chicago [A:246].","citations":["A:246"],"tags":["Caribbean","Food & Dining","Clubs & Societies","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Chef (High)","definition":"Olga Sanchez’s unnamed husband, described as a beautiful Mexican man, who becomes high chef of the Rounders’ Club main dining room [MTM B2:74].","citations":["MTM B2:74"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","Food & Dining","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Chemical Unbalance","definition":"One of the possible explanations Elena lists while asking Finnegan why he drinks. He answers her whole series with nine yesses and a no, then attributes his drinking to its pleasure and his excess to an evil streak; he does not identify which proposed reason he rejects [A:274].","citations":["A:274"],"tags":["Finnegan","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Chemicals","definition":"Among the materials Leonard Archive says the settlement can selectively draw from water through varied electrical voltages, frequencies, and electrode arrangements. His list also includes fish, crustaceans, animals, plants, minerals, salts, alcohols, petroleum, and sugars. Honeybucket Kincaid demonstrates the equipment while preparing the visitors’ meal [MTM B13:417-418].","citations":["MTM B13:417-418"],"tags":["Fishing","Food & Dining","Creation"]},{"name":"Chemist","definition":"Zabotski’s former occupation. He is a retired chemist and experimenter who supplies Duffey with the large glass-lined distillation pot that becomes the communal soup crock [A:132-133] [MTM B6:195]. The later description calls him a smelly man in a smelly trade, then specifies that his offensiveness is to the eye rather than the nose [MTM B11:306].","citations":["A:132-133","MTM B6:195","MTM B11:306"],"tags":["Occupations","Duffey"]},{"name":"Cherry bomb","definition":"Simile for the heart bursting as Duffey wrestles Morpheus, panics at extinction, and collapses toward the single-celled remnant of their diminishing world [GD:500].","citations":["GD:500"],"tags":["Duffey","Death & Mortality","Dreams"]},{"name":"Cherson","definition":"Name X says the Goths used for the Crimean peninsula, the Tauric or Euxine Chersonese, in his account of the Devil’s imprisonment [MTM B6:204]. See Chersonese (Euxine/Tauric).","citations":["MTM B6:204"],"tags":["The Devil"]},{"name":"Chersonese (Euxine/Tauric)","definition":"Crimean peninsula in the accounts of the Devil’s thousand-year imprisonment. X calls it the Tauric or Euxine Chersonese and says Christians brought the prisoner from Aachen in 946 or 947, delivered him first to Muslim authorities, and then to devil-worshipping guards. In his account it was divided between Kiev and Tmutarakan and had been called the Prison, Phylake or Carcer, long before this confinement [MTM B6:204-205]. Henri’s account dates the transfer from Aachen to 946 [MTM B5:180]. Teresa says the ancient <em>Argo</em> sailed past the Devil’s preordained prison on its way to Colchis [MTM B7:234].","citations":["MTM B6:204-205","MTM B5:180","MTM B7:234"],"tags":["The Devil","Ships","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Chest","definition":"Patrick Stranahan, not Henry, has the chest and shoulders of a heavyweight wrestler [A:102]. The thunder colt tears open the chest of Deutero-Finnegan, the Finnegan effigy, in the Opera House spectacle [MTM B11:351]. Duffey sees the Devil’s tetragrammaton cut into Sebastian Hilton’s chest in a transport, but on examining the body finds plague sores forming the same design, not knife wounds [MTM B7:226]. In the container sense, Papa Devil brings an ice chest to the Padre Island beach party [D:34] [D:40]; treasure chests marked with suggestive pirates’ initials feature on Angela’s father’s forged maps [D:259-260].","citations":["A:102","MTM B11:351","MTM B7:226","D:34","D:40","D:259-260"],"tags":["The Devil","Finnegan","Visual Arts","Death & Mortality","Ships"]},{"name":"Chestnut","definition":"Color of Dotty’s memorable hair [A:105-106], and one of the color terms used for the women Johnny Duckwalk and Don Barnaby pursue on Basse-Terre [D:236]. Chestnut Street names the St. Louis district of fleshpots frequented by Bagby [MTM B2:62].","citations":["A:105-106","D:236","MTM B2:62"],"tags":["Women Characters","Caribbean","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Chestnut Street","definition":"St. Louis street whose fleshpots Bagby frequents. The same portrait describes his brawling throughout the city [MTM B2:62].","citations":["MTM B2:62"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Combat"]},{"name":"Chewing Tobacco","definition":"Product advertised by the company sponsoring the limerick contest in Marie’s Shakes Pearson story. Her uncle repeatedly begins tobacco verses on bark, only to abandon them for whisky and find them eaten by squirrels when he returns [A:25].","citations":["A:25"],"tags":["Family","Literature","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Chiang","definition":"Chiang Kai-shek, the Chinese Nationalist leader. In his polemic against the <em>Bark</em>, Casey condemns its support for Chiang and calls him discredited and possibly the most evil man alive [A:130].","citations":["A:130"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Journalism"]},{"name":"Chiara","definition":"One of the three children, with Rafaello and Theresa Anna, whom Theresa remembers from her twelve-year other life with Finnegan. She has Masses said for them while uncertain whether that life was a dream; the narrator explicitly distinguishes these children from those she has with Vincent [A:203-204]. A later account names Chiara, Rafaello, and Theresa Anna as Vincent and Teresa Stranahan’s three younger children, born many years after their older ones [MTM B12:385].","citations":["A:203-204","MTM B12:385"],"tags":["Family","Dreams","Time & Eternity","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Chicago","definition":"Major home and working city for Duffey and his circle. One account includes Chicago among the cities where he lived with kindred between five and twelve; his 1925 return is a night-train journey from Omaha [MTM B1:7] [MTM B3:77]. He joins Gabriel Szymansky’s business, adding books and art to its paired antique and pawn shops [MTM B3:81-82]. North Chicago’s golden age lasts from 1925 to 1935, followed by an electrum age to about 1946; its circle includes the Kochs, Hiltons, Szymanskys, artists such as Groben, and the young Carruthers, Rattigan, and Stones [MTM B3:98-99]. The city is the setting of the Leader’s appearance and the portfolio blackmail against Duffey [MTM B4:116] [MTM B4:139]. The <em>Crock</em> belongs to this Chicago world, which also lies behind Duffey’s warning about Hugo Stone and Stein [A:40-41]. Finnegan plans to visit Casey there [A:120], receives Chicago friends’ mail at Palermo [D:114-115], and returns to the city during his later human-life interval [D:272]. Duffey leaves Chicago for St. Louis in 1946 and does not return from that trip [MTM B5:149]; later he and X do visit Chicago by traveling backward in time after their Cuban search for Finnegan [MTM B12:376]. Margaret Stone recalls her Chicago childhood through her father’s unread Cook County history [MTM B11:308].","citations":["MTM B1:7","MTM B3:77","MTM B3:81-82","MTM B3:98-99","MTM B4:116","MTM B4:139","A:40-41","A:120","D:114-115","D:272","MTM B5:149","MTM B12:376","MTM B11:308"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan","Family","Journalism","History & Memory","Money & Trade","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Chicago Herald and Examiner","definition":"Newspaper publishing Elena O’Higgins’s modest November 14 report on the mule transported thirty feet through the air beneath the Pont du Sable Traffic Trestle. Its photograph shows the airborne mule without giant hands, and its witnesses disagree about the means of transport [MTM B4:132].","citations":["MTM B4:132"],"tags":["Journalism","Giants & Titans"]},{"name":"Chicago style jazz","definition":"Jazz style favored in the <em>Crock</em>’s short survey of new recordings [A:41].","citations":["A:41"],"tags":["Music","Journalism"]},{"name":"Chicago Tribune","definition":"Newspaper publishing Tony Apostolo’s November 14 report on the transported mule. It includes accounts from numerous witnesses and all three photographs exchanged among the reporters, including the image showing giant hands. The witnesses offer conflicting explanations, though most describe giant hands lifting the mule [MTM B4:132-133].","citations":["MTM B4:132-133"],"tags":["Giants & Titans","Journalism"]},{"name":"Chicago-Hot Jazz","definition":"Music Rollo McSorley says can still be heard only on Fleshpot Row. The narrator mocks his musical judgment, concedes some truth to the location claim, and describes the row’s version as emphasizing the style’s faults [MTM B4:126].","citations":["MTM B4:126"],"tags":["Music","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Chicle","definition":"Crop grown in the fictional country San Simeon, listed with henequen among the industries that do not account for its exceptional wealth, which lies in its possession of gold [MB:558].","citations":["MB:558"],"tags":["Fictional Places","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Child-Hero, The","definition":"One of the Royal Pop People whom Duffey meets in New Orleans. Although his designation sounds like a type, the narrator insists that he and the other named figures are unique, enduring persons [MTM B11:322]. He disparages Duffey’s historical apparatus and questions the reality of the classical past [MTM B11:326-327]. In the new morning park he affirms its reality, identifies his people as newly awakened children of the thunder and mountain, and justifies the destruction of those judged insufficiently splendid [MTM B11:331-334]. Salvation Sally reports that she and the Child Hero clear a building’s third floor of its inhabitants [MTM B11:338]. He joins the dinner at Girardeau’s and then performs in the Cretan Bull Drama at the Decatur Street Opera House, where Zabotski is killed [MTM B11:344] [MTM B11:349].","citations":["MTM B11:322","MTM B11:326-327","MTM B11:331-334","MTM B11:338","MTM B11:344","MTM B11:349"],"tags":["New Orleans","Minor Characters","Theater","Apocalyptic","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Childhood (in deep fimbul-winter)","definition":"Finnegan’s image of his people’s primordial life in the deep fimbul-winter. In his ancestral outcry, he says they invented the inventions of childhood and asks how they have since been cast as monsters [D:213].","citations":["D:213"],"tags":["Finnegan","Time & Eternity","Identity"]},{"name":"Childhood of astronomy","definition":"The narrator’s name for the elementary knowledge Hans gains by learning to calculate the positions of familiar stars. The Professor’s theories about the universe follow this practical lesson [A:214].","citations":["A:214"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Childhood of the soul","definition":"The Professor’s term for the present limits of human perception. He argues that sight reveals only the reverse of Heaven’s tapestry: people already inhabit the spiritual universe and eternity, but cannot yet perceive them fully [A:214].","citations":["A:214"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","Heaven & Hell"]},{"name":"Childrens' Gin Fund","definition":"Mock charitable fund to which Dotty says Marie should give her money instead of paying Salvation Sally’s passage to America. The suggestion appears in Dotty’s comic letter complaining about Sally’s arrival [A:147].","citations":["A:147"],"tags":["Women Characters","Alcohol"]},{"name":"China Lobby","definition":"Political alignment Casey attributes to the <em>Bark</em> in his hostile review. He calls the magazine straight China Lobby and attacks its support for Chiang and opposition to the Communist movement in China [A:130].","citations":["A:130"],"tags":["Journalism"]},{"name":"China Shop","definition":"Mary Louise’s comparison for Bagby’s eagerness to buy anything, after he pays a thousand dollars for Elroy Redheart’s chambered-heart artwork: a kid in a China Shop [MTM B7:224].","citations":["MTM B7:224"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"China Straits","definition":"Straits traversed by the returning voyagers after skirting the d’Entrecasteau Islands and Trobriands in <em>Archipelago</em> [A:36]. Biloxi Brannagan has not only sailed through them but dived there for old wrecks [D:203].","citations":["A:36","D:203"],"tags":["Sailing","Seas & Oceans","Shipwrecks"]},{"name":"Chinese girl (Angela Ching)","definition":"See <em>Ching, Angela</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Chinese period","definition":"Hans’s name for the phase in which Chinese ballads and Heine’s influence mingle with a Rhineland sadness, German roofs, Chinese houses, river junks, and love. His unpublished Li Tai Po ballad introduces the account [A:221-223].","citations":["A:221-223"],"tags":["Literature","Music","Germany"]},{"name":"Chinese style","definition":"The manner in which Hans proposes to decorate his flat. Jimmy suggests rugs on the walls and a red-and-black color scheme [A:229].","citations":["A:229"],"tags":["Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Ching, Angela","definition":"Slim Chinese girl among Duffey’s ladies who gather at his Rounders’ Club in St. Louis and kiss him goodbye before his departure [MTM B2:70].","citations":["MTM B2:70"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Ching-Ling Charley's Doss House","definition":"One of the fictitious sponsors advertised on Stein’s G.I. radio station, the Anopheles Network, housed in one of his two tents. The spoof promises genteel surroundings and an atmosphere one can cut with a knife [A:46].","citations":["A:46"],"tags":["Soldiers","Journalism"]},{"name":"Chiodo","definition":"Italian for nail, used in Theresa’s saying to Finnegan that a chiodo in the heart never killed. She speaks of loving Finnegan while expecting happiness with Vincent [A:107].","citations":["A:107"],"tags":["Italy","Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Chipmunks","definition":"One of Finnegan’s first guesses about the small animals around him as he wakes before dawn. He gradually recognizes rats, his own dried blood, and the foul dump in which he has slept [A:177].","citations":["A:177"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Chith","definition":"Destination beyond mountains in the voyage poem opening Finnegan’s chapter, whose subtitle identifies him with Iason and the Other People: he will go over the mountains to Chith [A:171].","citations":["A:171"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Chiv","definition":"Knife word that Casey’s notebook identifies as Romani, using the term Gypsy in its list of language facts [A:164].","citations":["A:164"],"tags":["Weapons","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Chloros","definition":"Greek color word for a pale or yellowish green. Hans considers and rejects it as a description of Marie’s green eyes, reasoning that nobody would have eyes of that shade [A:24].","citations":["A:24"],"tags":["Greece","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Chocolate","definition":"Among the provisions Finnegan unloads at Anastasia Grandmother’s house on Naxos [D:126]. Casey includes a chocolate soldier among the eminent figures who echo the propagandists’ chosen language [A:185-186]. Chocolate is also one of the color terms for the Basse-Terre women pursued by Don Barnaby and Johnny Duckwalk [D:236]. In Elroy Redheart’s chambered-heart artwork, a dead girl’s own red chocolate heart lies among the candies beside her, removed from her opened breast [MTM B7:221].","citations":["D:126","A:185-186","D:236","MTM B7:221"],"tags":["Finnegan","Greece","Caribbean","Women Characters","Food & Dining","Visual Arts","Journalism"]},{"name":"Chocolate Soldier","definition":"One of the ostensibly independent eminent figures who echo the phrases of Casey’s propagandists. Casey lists him with a physicist, wit, widow, educator, and statesman [A:185-186].","citations":["A:185-186"],"tags":["Soldiers","Journalism"]},{"name":"Chodorlahomor","definition":"King of Elam or Susiana, described in Duffey’s narrative as ruler of a Devildom east of the Tigris and leader of the three crooked kings Amraphel, Arioch, and Thadal. Duffey recognizes those three names in the <em>Flame Man</em> comic strip as the Slant-Faced Men, the magicless anti-magi; their allegiance to Chodorlahomor is supplied outside the strip. Duffey remembers blessing Abraham after his victory over the four kings [MTM B7:227-228].","citations":["MTM B7:227-228"],"tags":["Duffey","Old Testament","Magic & Sorcery","Literature"]},{"name":"Chok-chok-chok-kachoom","definition":"One of the animal sounds in the immense rhapsody heard the night before Zabotski, Wife Waldo, children, and animals depart in his giant ark-like contraption [MTM B9:285].","citations":["MTM B9:285"],"tags":["Ships","Music","Creation"]},{"name":"Chort","definition":"Devil-name in Zabotski’s account of Slavic folk-comic tales and the Devil’s release from an underground prison [MTM B6:191]. In X’s family history, Chortovitch means son of the Devil and has been euphemized on a gravestone to Bogovitch, son of God [D:277].","citations":["MTM B6:191","D:277"],"tags":["The Devil","Eastern Europe","Family","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Chortovitch, Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch Papadiabolous","definition":"The elder Devil whose history X reconstructs from the neglected grave marked I.N.C.G.P. Bogovitch, which Finnegan locates. X interprets Bogovitch as a euphemism for Chortovitch, son of the Devil [D:276-278]. In his account the man is born near Krakow in 1830 to the Irish governess Katie Noonan, is baptized Columkill, and receives Gregorovitch from his adopter Gregory. X believes the father was literally the Devil, as the parish record reportedly says, while admitting the alternative that Gregory fathered him; Katie affectionately calls him Ifreann [D:278-279]. X describes him as a murderer, participant in the Paris Commune, disciple of Kropotkin, and old-line communist who sires twins at seventy, simultaneously but to different mothers on different continents and in different centuries [D:280-281]. X says a document conferring dangerous power is buried with the father [D:290].","citations":["D:276-278","D:278-279","D:280-281","D:290"],"tags":["The Devil","Ireland","Eastern Europe","Finnegan","Devils (Characters)","Family","Identity","Crime"]},{"name":"Chorus","definition":"Refrain or collective voice: the cicadas surrounding Finnegan and Anastasia on the Naxos mountaintop [D:132]; the Gadarene Swine Song sung by Sebastian, Countess Margaret, Letitia, and Duffey, with Giulio Solli howling its basso accompaniment [MTM B3:107]; and the short counter-ode preceding Duffey’s curtain speech in Cleo Mahoney’s play [MTM B10:297].","citations":["D:132","MTM B3:107","MTM B10:297"],"tags":["Music","Theater","Literature","Greece"]},{"name":"Chosen (the)","definition":"The privileged company invoked by the poem opening Stranahan’s chapter, whose speaker chooses the life of the Fiver, grappling ghosts and cozening coneys with his companions [A:91].","citations":["A:91"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Literature","Argonautica"]},{"name":"Chosky Bottoms, The","definition":"Elusive local bottoms discussed by Finnegan and his country friends after a night with coon dogs. They disagree whether they have just been there; Old Pop Potter treats the name as a general spook place always beyond the next ridge. Its legends include a man-killing panther, swamp ghost, masked riders, a Northern Platoon lost in quicksand, ancestral coons and fish, Bloody Mulligans, and eighteen-foot bull snakes. Finnegan compares its elusive character with the St. Kitts he hopes to find, rather than placing the bottoms near that island [D:273-274].","citations":["D:273-274"],"tags":["Folklore","Ghosts & Spirits","Finnegan","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Christ-heads (Danish-bread)","definition":"The Danish-bread Christ is one of the Seven Christs Duffey brings back from his lost years: a head shaped from mixed wheat and rye dough, baked and varnished. The narrator says such heads are baked in parts of Denmark for Corpus Christi, and marvels at this particular example’s artistic power and preservation [MTM B3:103].","citations":["MTM B3:103"],"tags":["Duffey","Visual Arts","Liturgy"]},{"name":"Christian Chinese","definition":"Description of Ignatius Ti, the ward boy who translates the parakeet Amoy’s stories. His family has lived in the islands for centuries [A:79].","citations":["A:79"],"tags":["Family","Minor Characters","Language & Words","Islands"]},{"name":"Christian mermaids","definition":"Mermaids who, according to Anastasia’s account, lived on a small island between Patmos and Lipsos in the second century. Their chapel of Our Lady of the Dolphins still survives in poor condition [D:34-35].","citations":["D:34-35"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Islands","Greece"]},{"name":"Christmas","definition":"One of the four annual occasions on which Finnegan writes to Dotty while in the army [A:176]. Mary Catherine’s Irish whisky has remained unopened since Christmas when she brings it out for Finnegan [A:249]. The <em>Brunhilde</em> party spends eight or nine days at Port of Spain over Christmas and New Year’s [D:85]. Duffey remembers spending a Christmas vacation at Sebastian Hilton’s wealthy Chicago home [MTM B3:81].","citations":["A:176","A:249","D:85","MTM B3:81"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Ships","Ports & Harbors","Alcohol","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Christopher (brother of Henry)","definition":"One of Henry Francis Salvatore’s brothers, alongside Eustace, Hilaire, Joseph, Louis, Mark, and Simeon [A:122].","citations":["A:122"],"tags":["Family","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Christopher (Saint)","definition":"Earlier heading that treated an island name as an account of the saint. See <em>St. Kitts (St. Christopher)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Islands","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Christopher Columbus (c. 1451--1506)","definition":"Italian navigator (1451-1506) whose four Spanish-sponsored Atlantic voyages began in 1492 as a search for a westward route to Asia. His Caribbean landfalls inaugurated sustained European contact and colonization, with extensive transfers of organisms, goods, and people known as the Columbian Exchange. His colonial government and the enslavement of Indigenous people belong to the contested legacy of those voyages. In <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>, discovery by Columbus is one of the narrator’s playful requirements for an island paradise [D:237]; Ignacio similarly calls St. Christopher holy as a Columbus landfall [A:272].","citations":["D:237","A:272"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Sailing","Italy","Spain","Caribbean","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Chromatic Beauty","definition":"Beauty coupled with the continuity of evil in Casey’s childhood theory of personalized temptations. His erotic imaginary world contains treacherous, beautiful women from history and legend, compared with St. Anthony’s temptations [A:157].","citations":["A:157"],"tags":["Sin & Redemption","Dreams"]},{"name":"Chrome","definition":"Part of the rich interior of the outwardly dingy Cadillac Bar, where white-jacketed bar-boys preside amid chrome and crystal [D:58]. In Elroy Redheart’s chambered-heart artwork, chrome babes, cars, and domiciles furnish the affluent half of the second red room [MTM B7:222].","citations":["D:58","MTM B7:222"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Chrome babes","definition":"Figures among the brash, affluent scenes in the second red room of Elroy Redheart’s chambered-heart artwork, alongside chrome cars and chrome domiciles [MTM B7:222].","citations":["MTM B7:222"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Chromosomes","definition":"In the turbaned speaker’s account of the Royal Pop Historians’ techniques, an ancestral cicada had two more chromosomes and, coincidentally, two more notes in its song than modern descendants. He claims both the old song and the missing chromosomes can be recovered, the latter duplicated by advanced techniques [MTM B11:323-325].","citations":["MTM B11:323-325"],"tags":["History & Memory","Music"]},{"name":"Chronicker, S.J., Thomas J.","definition":"Jesuit contributor to the <em>Crock</em>, whose article in the issue described in <em>Archipelago</em> is on Diabolism in the World Today [A:41]. Listed as Thomas Chroniker S.J. among the ecclesial or vineyard people of Duffey’s Chicago golden age [MTM B3:99], and as Thos. J. Chronicker, S.J. among the magazine’s contributors during Duffey’s wartime editorship [MTM B4:138].","citations":["A:41","MTM B3:99","MTM B4:138"],"tags":["Religious Orders","Duffey","Minor Characters","Journalism"]},{"name":"Chrysostom (Papadiabolous I alias)","definition":"Alias of Gregory McIfreann Papadiabolous, the Asiatic twin or Papadiabolous I born in Smyrna, according to X. His other names include Sanditen, Protagoras, and Hochstruwelpieter-Goltz. X distinguishes him from the Irish twin Gregory McColum, Papadiabolous II, whose aliases include Noonan and Flaherty [D:285].","citations":["D:285"],"tags":["The Devil","Devils (Characters)","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Chrysostom (Saint)","definition":"St. John Chrysostom, whom Finnegan tentatively names, with Jerome as an alternative, when discussing Le Marin’s theory of an island Terrestrial Paradise. Finnegan thinks such authorities sought Paradise in the Near East, not in Le Marin’s Antilles [D:234].","citations":["D:234"],"tags":["Saints","Patristics","Heaven & Hell","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Chthonic Goddesses","definition":"The goddesses invoked in the portrait of Mary Louise Byrne’s strength and bovine quality. The narrator presents her as the royal bovine or sacred cow through which such goddesses pass, as well as an embodied part of Duffey’s own soul [MTM B2:55].","citations":["MTM B2:55"],"tags":["Women Characters","Other Mythologies","Duffey"]},{"name":"Church (building/institution)","definition":"Both a religious institution and a place of worship. Sally calls churches traps in her argument with Hans and Marie [A:8]. Particular buildings include Sydney’s Annunciation Church, where Hans and Marie marry [A:31]; the unnamed small Naxos church where Finnegan receives confession and attends Mass [D:119-120]; and the unnamed church opposite his encounter with Tommy Katt [D:232]. Duffey visits the winged stone church of St. Malachy in Chicago while seeking his future wife [MTM B3:87-88]. Teresa’s and the Stranahans’ shared parish hosts her wedding to Vincent [MTM B5:183]; in her memories of another life or dream she also knows the church where her children with Finnegan were baptized [A:204]. X locates Ifreann Columkill’s baptismal record, naming the Devil as his father, at St. Elmo’s near Krakow [D:279]. A priest from St. Katherine’s blesses the communal pot [A:133] [MTM B6:195]. St. Michael’s is being unstructured on the Great Day [GD:496], while Elena describes St. Cristina’s Chicago parish using Casey’s substituted music for special liturgy [PG:525]. In <em>How Many Miles to Babylon</em>, Hackenschmidt declares the Thing dead after a ritual destroying the Ekklesia’s straw-man Peter [MB:568].","citations":["A:8","A:31","D:119-120","D:232","MTM B3:87-88","MTM B5:183","A:204","D:279","A:133","MTM B6:195","GD:496","PG:525","MB:568"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Ecclesiastical","Liturgy","Ekklesia","Finnegan","Duffey"]},{"name":"Church of St. Elmo","definition":"Parish nearest Krakow in X’s reconstructed family history. He says its record names the Devil as father of Katie Noonan’s child, who is baptized Columkill [D:279].","citations":["D:279"],"tags":["Catholic Church","The Devil","Eastern Europe","Family","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Church of St. Katherine","definition":"Church supplying the priest whom Duffey brings to bless the great communal soup pot [A:133] [MTM B6:195].","citations":["A:133","MTM B6:195"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Duffey","Prayer & Devotion"]},{"name":"Church of St. Malachy","definition":"Chicago stone church two blocks north of Duffey’s hotel, with three spread-winged archangels on its south front and nine large angels on each east and west side. Its ruddy, winged turrets catch the morning sun while Duffey searches the arriving worshippers for his moth, his future wife [MTM B3:87-88]. Letitia makes arrangements there for their wedding [MTM B3:92]; they visit it while preparing their home and marriage [MTM B3:96], and the subsequent account confirms that they marry [MTM B3:98].","citations":["MTM B3:87-88","MTM B3:92","MTM B3:96","MTM B3:98"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Sacraments","Angels & Demons","Duffey","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Church of St. Michael","definition":"Church being dismantled through faith in the Great Day episode. After leaving the Pelican, Duffey visits its damaged stone Michael at what was the entrance; the statue says that the living water has gone and that no Mass or even morning remains [GD:496].","citations":["GD:496"],"tags":["Angels & Demons","Duffey","Metaphysics","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Church of St. Philomena","definition":"Earlier heading that misclassified the school as a church. See <em>St. Philomena's</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Church of the Annunciation (Sydney)","definition":"Sydney church where John Gottfried Schultz, called Hans, marries Marie Anna Monaghan on Monday, February 15, 1943. Hans lacks army permission, so the wedding is temporarily secret and none of their friends attends; twenty or thirty of Marie’s relatives are present [A:31].","citations":["A:31"],"tags":["Australia","World War II","Sacraments","Family"]},{"name":"Churchy people","definition":"The ecclesial or vineyard members of Duffey’s Chicago golden-age circle: Thomas Chroniker S.J.; Tim and Gale Tuthill; Sister Mary Cornelia (Sullivan) Foster and Alma Ruch; Dan and Nan Donovan; Sister Mary Aurora (Rittenhouse); Martin and Katherine Redwine; Frantz and Elaine St. Clair. The narrator says these vineyard workers keep the world turning [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Churn","definition":"Household vessel bearing the iron-lettered names Sturgis, Curtis Improved, or Star Barrel in Duffey’s remembered or mind-borrowed childhood observations [MTM B1:17]. The narrator later compares the ten-year-old Casey, after his attempted debut as world Leader, to a churn in which butter of a particular flavor is forming [MTM B4:118].","citations":["MTM B1:17","MTM B4:118"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Churning Creek","definition":"Descriptive phrase, not a stated place-name, for the creek Henry and Casey climb up to before swimming in a deep hole below a waterfall [A:42].","citations":["A:42"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cicadas","definition":"Insects whose chorus surrounds Finnegan and Anastasia on the moonlit Naxos mountaintop while their donkey grazes below [D:132]. A turbaned Royal Pop Historians speaker claims that an ancestral cicada had two additional chromosomes and, coincidentally, two extra notes in its song, both accessible to their reconstruction techniques [MTM B11:324-325].","citations":["D:132","MTM B11:324-325"],"tags":["Greece","Finnegan","Music","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Cid, The","definition":"One of the enchanted or trance-sleeping leaders X lists, with Barbarossa, Alaric, Brian Boru, and Arthur, whose subjects await their awakening or release. X contrasts their sleep with the Devil’s wakefulness in prison [MTM B6:204-205].","citations":["MTM B6:204-205"],"tags":["Other Mythologies","Dreams","The Devil"]},{"name":"Cigar","definition":"A recurrent character prop. Stein smokes while typing beneath his publisher’s placard [A:120]; in the Great Day episode he wishes to be buried with a lit cigar and claims his lengthening stogie is made of faith-tobacco [GD:493-494]. Le Marin includes a still-smoking cigar in the standard derelict-ship story he applies to the <em>Sarcophagus</em> [D:243]. The dead hack from the <em>Seabach</em> has a mauled cigar in his mouth [D:135] [D:147]. Hillary Hilton trades in distressed cigars [A:253]. After buying his St. Louis premises, Duffey smokes half a cigar and later gives it to Giulio [MTM B2:40] [MTM B2:42]; he accidentally knocks Charley Murray’s cigar from the balloon before releasing its hydrogen [MTM B2:66]. X smokes one of the Mogul’s cigars while awaiting him in his apartment [MB:570].","citations":["A:120","GD:493-494","D:243","D:135","D:147","A:253","MTM B2:40","MTM B2:42","MTM B2:66","MB:570"],"tags":["Duffey","Money & Trade","Ships","Crew & Sailors","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Cigar Store Christ","definition":"One of Duffey’s Seven Christs, a St. Kitts statue resembling a cigar-store Indian. Its right-hand fingers form a cup or cone for cigars; it has the conventional coloring and a bare head, but Christ’s face. The narrator says its Black artist knew the face without recognizing its owner [MTM B3:103].","citations":["MTM B3:103"],"tags":["Duffey","Visual Arts","Caribbean","New Testament"]},{"name":"Cigar Store Indian","definition":"Four life-size figures Finnegan carves while staying with Casey, doing novelty work for money. The narrator praises their authenticity, humor, and burlesque touch. A rich man buys them for a hundred dollars each. Years later Hillary Hilton owns one, which he refuses to sell because of his love for Finnegan; the other three are untraced [A:183].","citations":["A:183"],"tags":["Finnegan","Visual Arts","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Cincinnati","definition":"One of the gracious American cities Patrick Stranahan recommends knowing [A:110]. In Finnegan’s confused rhapsody of places and times, some of the bars he seems to be visiting are, he thinks, really in Cincinnati [A:200].","citations":["A:110","A:200"],"tags":["Finnegan","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Cinders","definition":"What remains of Chicago in Casey’s ninth imaginary world, a revolutionary fantasy in which workers topple buildings and start a fire greater than the original Chicago fire [A:160].","citations":["A:160"],"tags":["Dreams","Combat"]},{"name":"Circle","definition":"A grouping of companions or a pattern of association. The soldiers pass their last bottle around a circle in the grass [A:69]; Patrick Stranahan’s household welcomes Monica’s and the daughters’ many social circles [A:104]. The account <em>How Many Miles to Babylon</em> is attributed to a member of Finnegan’s old outer circle [MTM B12:368], while Charlotte says Casey’s Antichrist title carries little prestige with the real inner-circle people [MTM B12:379]. Bagby’s letters after death describe circles differentiated by intensity and transfiguration rather than by inner and outer position [MTM B8:248-249]. Stein notes the stir caused by the Devil’s release in esoteric circles, while varying his opinion of whether that released figure is the Devil or a decoy [MTM B10:291].","citations":["A:69","A:104","MTM B12:368","MTM B12:379","MTM B8:248-249","MTM B10:291"],"tags":["Family","The Devil","The Dead","Metaphysics","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Circuit","definition":"Green’s metaphor for a contingent universe held in existence by God’s continual pressure on a momentary-contact switch, in the Ward Fourteen conversation [A:76]. In the performing sense, X says he played the circuits as the Great X-Capo [A:137]. Duffey visits his circuit of midwestern cities for a year or more after the missing years, starting and selling three businesses [MTM B3:77]. Anastasia says Seaworthy and his company make circuits to check their apparatus and men in different towns, calling the business deep and murderous [D:39].","citations":["A:76","A:137","MTM B3:77","D:39"],"tags":["Theology","Duffey","Crime","Theater"]},{"name":"Circuits (Great X-Capo played)","definition":"Performance circuits X says he toured as the Great X-Capo. He invokes that stage career to explain his ease in opening a locked box [A:137].","citations":["A:137"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Theater"]},{"name":"Circus","definition":"A source of images and performances: circus girl acrobats populate Casey’s erotic dream world [A:157]; Professor Kirol has performed in European and American circuses [A:215-216]; and Marie Courtois’s strength and excitement are compared with a circus woman and a circus [D:37]. Monica calls Finnegan’s behavior with Mary Schaeffer either a scandal or a circus [A:105]. Circuses also figure among the popular arts the narrator says are deliberately degraded after the Devil’s release [MTM B6:207].","citations":["A:157","A:215-216","D:37","A:105","MTM B6:207"],"tags":["Women Characters","Theater","Carnival","The Devil"]},{"name":"Circus Girl Acrobats","definition":"Among the women from history, legend, and performance who populate Casey’s childhood erotic imaginary world, described as treacherous and beautiful temptations [A:157].","citations":["A:157"],"tags":["Women Characters","Carnival","Dreams"]},{"name":"Cistern hand-pump","definition":"Water pump at Duffey’s newly purchased St. Louis horse barn, working after a little priming [MTM B2:41]. He uses it while preparing a bath for his first customer, before arranging a city water supply [MTM B2:42].","citations":["MTM B2:41","MTM B2:42"],"tags":["Duffey","Occupations"]},{"name":"Cisterns, Deep","definition":"Ancestral depths invoked in Finnegan’s litany about his primordial people. His question about the earth’s deep cisterns stands between questions about making blood from elemental materials and remembering childhood’s blue ice [D:213].","citations":["D:213"],"tags":["Finnegan","History & Memory","Creation"]},{"name":"Citadel of sanity","definition":"Finnegan’s description of the Brannagans’ hospitable home. His thanksgiving for this refuge follows praise of Gertrude’s extravagant cooking and the joke that her southern kitchen has escaped fried chicken and cornbread [D:208].","citations":["D:208"],"tags":["Finnegan","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Citizen","definition":"A political or civic identity: X says Ifreann claimed Krakow citizenship throughout his life, although he left below citizenship age [D:280]. Carmelo’s travel papers make Duffey the Irish Free State citizen Mike Duffey for the Cuban journey [MTM B12:369]. Widow Waldo reports that the state inspector relies on pressure from neighbors and fellow citizens against the part of Zabotski’s structure on his own lot [MTM B9:274]. An excerpt attributed to Leo XIII’s <em>The Condition of Labor</em> speaks of industrious, modest men winning their fellow citizens’ sympathy [MTM B5:176].","citations":["D:280","MTM B12:369","MTM B9:274","MTM B5:176"],"tags":["Ireland","Duffey","Identity","Social Class"]},{"name":"City Commission","definition":"Municipal body to which a reporter says Zabotski complained about flooding on Dumaine Street, severe enough to float an ocean-going ship past his house. Zabotski concedes that the remark sounds like his own and that sufficient rain could carry his giant contraption to Lake Borgne [MTM B9:289].","citations":["MTM B9:289"],"tags":["Ships","New Orleans"]},{"name":"City Hall","definition":"Chicago municipal building visited by Duffey and Letitia while preparing their home and marriage, in a round of visits also including St. Malachy’s, an insurer, a bank, and a lawyer [MTM B3:96].","citations":["MTM B3:96"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Civil litigation","definition":"One of the popular arts the narrator includes in the sweeping loss of style and class following the Devil’s release [MTM B6:207].","citations":["MTM B6:207"],"tags":["The Devil","Social Class"]},{"name":"Civil War, American","definition":"American conflict of 1861-1865, beginning with the bombardment of Fort Sumter on April 12, 1861. Lee’s April 9, 1865 surrender at Appomattox was a decisive ending, though other Confederate surrenders followed. The novel associates Prisoner John’s twenty-year captivity in an attic cell with Civil War days [MTM B1:13]. Private Gregory says he lost his papers during the war and used that loss to reduce his recorded age [A:77].","citations":["MTM B1:13","A:77"],"tags":["History & Memory","Soldiers","Duffey"]},{"name":"Civilized person","definition":"Trinali’s joking name for herself when she insists that only barbarians dine by electric light. Her electricity has been cut off for nonpayment, so she brings candles and would rather nobody else use electricity either [A:228].","citations":["A:228"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Identity"]},{"name":"Claim check","definition":"Ticket X steals from Doll’s bag while she shops, enabling him to retrieve the suitcase of money from its locker. He substitutes the matching suitcase packed with banded newspaper pieces and lays a trail for the pursuers to find that decoy [D:178].","citations":["D:178"],"tags":["Crime","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Clancys (Family)","definition":"One of the families an older relative names among Duffey’s kin in an account of his childhood among Iowa farms and towns and several cities. Duffey disputes the genealogy, suggesting that she remembers his twin instead [MTM B1:7-8].","citations":["MTM B1:7-8"],"tags":["Duffey","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Clang-for-service bell","definition":"Bell Bagby proposes for Duffey’s establishment, so customers can call him back while he works in Bagby’s adjoining cabinet shop. A boarded doorway between the buildings can be reopened for that arrangement [MTM B2:46].","citations":["MTM B2:46"],"tags":["Duffey","Occupations"]},{"name":"Clarence","definition":"Place whose beaches appear with those of Mona and Baile in the projected voyage of the poem opening Finnegan’s chapter [A:171].","citations":["A:171"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Clarence (Sulphur-Bottom Sullivan)","definition":"Baptismal name of Sulphur-Bottom Sullivan, and the only name his parents used for him. The narrator calls the nickname’s origin remarkable but declines to tell it [D:217].","citations":["D:217"],"tags":["Identity","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Clarenden","definition":"Alias X assigns to Gregory McColum Papadiabolous, the Irish twin or Papadiabolous II. His other listed names are Noonan, Flaherty, Christy, Cobbett, Malory, Ingraham, and Arbuthnot [D:285].","citations":["D:285"],"tags":["The Devil","Devils (Characters)","Identity"]},{"name":"Clarity","definition":"Lucidity that can be revealing or deceptive. Finnegan calls his conviction that he is the world’s only person full clarity during his chaparral ordeal [A:205]; after burying Don Lewis, a little clarity makes him recognize that Papa Devil was not the killer [D:112]. Hans asks Marie to turn his incoherent but revealing insights into clarity [MTM B6:186]. The magus recovers his identity as Melchisedech as his clarity increases [MTM B13:402], yet the poisoned spring later makes him see unreal things with deceptive clarity before he gains the special perception of dying [MTM B13:441]. “To Trinali with Clarity” is a projected ode-title in Trinali’s fantasy of a significant early death [A:232].","citations":["A:205","D:112","MTM B6:186","MTM B13:402","MTM B13:441","A:232"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Literature","Identity","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Claxton, John","definition":"Artist whose slate-slab paintings Duffey adds to his New Orleans art-dealing stock [MTM B6:199].","citations":["MTM B6:199"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Cleavage","definition":"Rock property mentioned in Casey’s notebook claim that rocks can be left- or right-handed according to their formation and type of cleavage [A:164].","citations":["A:164"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Clement","definition":"Clement of Rome, recognizable among Linus and Cletus aboard the sacred ship in Henry’s vision [A:45]. One of the Apostolic Fathers and traditionally the fourth bishop of Rome, he led the Roman church in the late first century; chronological lists variously give about 88-97 or 92-99. Early church tradition connects him with the Apostles, and one tradition makes Peter his consecrator. His <em>First Letter to the Corinthians</em>, usually placed around 96, seeks to resolve a dispute within that church and is a very early Christian witness to communal unity and order. Later tradition relates his exile and martyrdom by drowning with an anchor; these are hagiographical reports rather than securely established biographical details. His Roman Catholic feast is November 23.","citations":["A:45"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Saints","Popes","Patristics","Church History","Literature"]},{"name":"Clement (Hal)","definition":"Author of <em>Left of Africa</em>, listed as number 1 in Manuscript Press’s Lost Manuscripts series in the publisher’s notice at the end of this edition of <em>Archipelago</em>; <em>Archipelago</em> itself is number 2 [A:286].","citations":["A:286"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Cleopatra","definition":"One of the exalted names Hilary Hilton says reincarnation claimants choose. He uses the analogy to ridicule Casey’s claim of exchanging souls with the Devil; he does not say Casey claims to have been Cleopatra [PG:531].","citations":["PG:531"],"tags":["Identity","Social Class"]},{"name":"Cleveland Bays","definition":"Horses drawing Sebastian Hilton’s hired carriage for the school dance, praised by the narrator as unsurpassed in style. The liveried driver, post-boy’s horn, and circuitous ride with the girls briefly relieve the boys’ dread of formal pairing [MTM B1:32].","citations":["MTM B1:32"],"tags":["Social Class"]},{"name":"Cliff people","definition":"People Hans reaches by climbing the apparently inaccessible cliffs of the island. He eats with them and learns their language; their food includes taro, goop, cuscus or coconut possum, birds, and meat whose peculiar taste prompts his teasing hints of cannibalism [A:58]. Finnegan is afraid to meet them, and the narrator explains that they look exactly like him: great noses, chinlessness, a deceptive shambling gait, and sudden speed and strength. Whatever remnant they represent, Finnegan belongs to it too [A:59].","citations":["A:58","A:59"],"tags":["Islands","Finnegan","Food & Dining","Language & Words","Identity"]},{"name":"Climax","definition":"A culmination whose possibility the narratives repeatedly question. The noises at Yokipoki’s grave build to a climax before Finnegan discovers the rats [D:47]. Doll demands a climax to the conflict with the older people, but X insists their method is endless gnawing and undermining, not climaxes [D:292]. Finnegan’s response to Dotty resembles the culmination of longing and also sheer terror [A:92]; even the final shooting is denied the name of crisis because it changes nobody’s attitude [A:282]. Other uses include the jungle raid’s climax [A:54], Endymeon’s search for a climactic line for his fourth stanza [A:238], the ironic description of America’s entry into the world war as a climax of political unease [MTM B4:138], and the Thunder Colt Game as the opera’s climax piece [MTM B11:350].","citations":["D:47","D:292","A:92","A:282","A:54","A:238","MTM B4:138","MTM B11:350"],"tags":["Women Characters","Finnegan","Theater","Literature","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Climbing","definition":"A recurrent practical skill and dream action. Jungle roots can serve as ladders [A:36], and Hans climbs cliffs to reach the cliff people [A:58]; his ascents through arrows and pistol fire, up an immense iron ladder, and into a giant spider’s web are explicitly dreams [A:61]. At school Rattigan climbs like a monkey, Sebastian like a squirrel, Duffey competently with giant hands available for support, and Charley Murray in terror [MTM B1:25]. Duffey also escapes his attic by a vine-covered rope [MTM B1:13] and, drawing on his rigging experience, climbs above the stage to inspect the Majesty’s apparent height [MTM B8:240]. Finnegan scales six stories of a Gothic hotel using its ornamentation; his recent upper-story burglaries have sought evidence rather than profit [D:162]. Little Alcohol handles climbing and high windows for his burglary partnership [D:218].","citations":["A:36","A:58","A:61","MTM B1:25","MTM B1:13","MTM B8:240","D:162","D:218"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","Finnegan","Duffey","Theater","Crime","Occupations","Dreams"]},{"name":"Clio","definition":"Greek Muse of History. In Finnegan’s dream she carries a trumpet and clepsydra, joining the other muses in an investiture he resists because accepting it means lifelong work [A:181]. The narrator later quotes a prediction attributed to Nostradamus in which the new Pope redirects Clio’s road, applying it to Pope Finnegan I [MB:598].","citations":["A:181","MB:598"],"tags":["Finnegan","History & Memory","Dreams","Popes"]},{"name":"Clock (Argo as dating)","definition":"One of the <em>Argo</em>’s functions according to Duffey. An artifact touched to the talking oak in its wheel elicits its year of origin in the appropriate era and annals. When confused, the wood instead calls out coordinates that may have no relation to the object. The log also records the last nine thousand years and the next sixty-seven, though interpreting it requires interior illumination and later futures belong to another log [MTM B13:411].","citations":["MTM B13:411"],"tags":["Ships","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Clootie, Old","definition":"Scots name for the Devil, associated with cloven hooves. Duffey uses its familiar, belittling tone toward the Majesty while exploring the giant’s trashy mind at the New Catholic Press Guild assembly [MTM B8:239-243]. He uses it again while accusing the devil confronting him in the closed place of being unable to create; that figure alternates between claiming authority and speaking as an agent for a client [MTM B10:303].","citations":["MTM B8:239-243","MTM B10:303"],"tags":["The Devil","Duffey","Creation","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Clothes","definition":"Garments repeatedly used to mark role and identity. The well-fitted clothes of the opening Asimov epigraph disguise a hypothetical Neanderthal, while Seaworthy first appears shabby [D:9]. Finnegan buys a satchel, clothes and shoes with Seaworthy’s money [D:17], and later looks like a Montenegran prince in evening dress [D:100]. For the swim to Paros, he and Manuel tow their clothes and shoes in leather sacks tied to floats [D:151]. On Basse-Terre he gives old clothes to a friend without understanding the plan; a similar-looking seaman is then found murdered in them [D:267]. Other appearances include the Bavarian Club girls’ peasant costumes [MTM B2:47], Elena’s refusal to undress while playing concertina [A:273], the hired Cuban sleeper’s work clothes beside his nude body [MTM B12:373], and old travel clothes beside Count Finnegan in his painting [MB:551]. On the Great Day, clothed Duffey is treated as conspicuous and improper to look at [GD:496].","citations":["D:9","D:17","D:100","D:151","D:267","MTM B2:47","A:273","MTM B12:373","MB:551","GD:496"],"tags":["Caribbean","Finnegan","Duffey","Women Characters","Visual Arts","Identity","Social Class","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Clotilda","definition":"Third of the women’s names assigned to the four companions’ daily bottles in Paradise, after Agatha and Bathildes. On the unusually heavy drinking night described, they have continued through Clotilda, Delphina, Etheldreda, Faustina and Georgina to Hildegarde [D:243].","citations":["D:243"],"tags":["Alcohol","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Cloudburst","definition":"Sudden storm announced by Bandicoot as it arrives. Its flood carries Zabotski’s ark, its human passengers and its animals along the street toward Lake Borgne [MTM B9:287].","citations":["MTM B9:287"],"tags":["Ships","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Cloudy Mountains","definition":"Mountains in New Guinea whose peaks Biloxi Brannagan has climbed and whose streams and dips he has panned for gold, part of the catalogue showing his travels exceeding Finnegan’s [D:203].","citations":["D:203"],"tags":["Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Clown","definition":"A recurring comic role, image and insult. Howland plays at being Finnegan’s valet [A:182]; the <em>Seabach</em>’s black crewman has clownish moments beneath his undersized pearl-gray derby [D:135]; and X arrives looking both happy and wistful [A:136] [MTM B6:200]. The Fleshpot Row comedian’s tortured face forms a clown’s flesh mask [MTM B4:127], while nightclub comedian Otto Glotglutz is reduced to tears by Hiram III’s retort [MB:564]. Trinali makes clown figures from cork and raffia [A:228], and Finnegan’s verse dismisses Omar as a clown [A:276]. Finnegan applies the insult to Don Barnaby while wondering whether he is the other man he remembers [D:249], and sees through X’s performance as a comical drunk [D:174]. The narrator finally calls Pope Finnegan I a big-nosed clown as well as a wanderer and master artist [MB:598].","citations":["A:182","D:135","A:136","MTM B6:200","MTM B4:127","MB:564","A:228","A:276","D:249","D:174","MB:598"],"tags":["Finnegan","Popes","Theater","Visual Arts","Identity"]},{"name":"Club","definition":"Social, professional and entertainment associations recur throughout the books. Duffey’s Rounders’ Club combines restaurant, bar, residence, transport hire and games, with automatic Steeplechase Club membership for patrons [MTM B2:49]; he joins, or in another version founds, the World-Wide Argonauts Argo Balloon Club [MTM B2:63]. The Sydney Plaza houses seamen’s and other clubs [A:3], Stein lectures to service clubs [A:19], a Malayan boy names an Indonesian Club [A:88], and Maurine jokingly calls her family’s men the Rod and Gun Club [A:103]. The Pelican has an unofficial Glee Club [MTM B7:226]; Mary Catherine belongs to three book clubs [A:249]; and Kirol works in nightclub entertainment [A:216]. Finnegan drinks with Seaworthy and meets Papa Devil in a club [D:13-15], takes Doll to a supper club [D:173], and later seeks X and Doll in the club opposite the graveyard [D:275]. Named establishments include the Bavarian Club [MTM B2:47], Chicago’s Colony Club [MTM B3:93], and the Beacon Club in Duffey’s waterfront catalogue [MTM B13:407]. Charlotte calls the circle of extraordinary acquaintances her Prodigious Peoples’ Club [MTM B8:260].","citations":["MTM B2:49","MTM B2:63","A:3","A:19","A:88","A:103","MTM B7:226","A:249","A:216","D:13-15","D:173","D:275","MTM B2:47","MTM B3:93","MTM B13:407","MTM B8:260"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","Bars & Taverns","Finnegan","Duffey","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Club Royal","definition":"Howland Jerome Howland’s plush establishment, catering to patrons whose hard childhoods have fostered an appetite for conspicuous luxury. It serves Danish beer and Hungarian Tokay, employs a chef from Martinique, and has an orchestra playing old classic jazz [A:246].","citations":["A:246"],"tags":["Alcohol","Music","Clubs & Societies","Social Class"]},{"name":"Coach Horses (Big)","definition":"Horses in the childhood livery stable recalled by Duffey, one of them distinguished by a notched ear. The detail belongs to his unusually persistent memory of ordinary surroundings [MTM B1:16].","citations":["MTM B1:16"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Coachman","definition":"Liveried driver of Sebastian Hilton’s hired carriage for the school dance, praised for having as much style as the carriage and its Cleveland Bays [MTM B1:32].","citations":["MTM B1:32"],"tags":["Occupations","Minor Characters","Social Class"]},{"name":"Coal (Black)","definition":"A color comparison and a fuel. The narrator calls the Council Bluffs park squirrels coal black and claims they occur nowhere else; this is the novel’s assertion [MTM B1:9]. Finnegan also lists coal among fuels whose different hazes obscure the sky’s true color [D:127]. Charcoal briquettes, a distinct fuel, appear during the soldiers’ island routine [A:31].","citations":["MTM B1:9","D:127","A:31"],"tags":["Language & Words","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Coast Guard Cutters","definition":"Vessels sent against the mysterious bell-ringers near San Simeon. Newly appearing shoals block the cutters; fog then frustrates men approaching in shallow-draft rowboats. The Sea Bell’s ship moves through deep water that shoals as soon as it passes [MB:560].","citations":["MB:560"],"tags":["Ships","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Cobber","definition":"Australian informal term for a friend. Finnegan uses it in his exaggerated imitation of an Australian while trying to pass as an Aussie in the drinking-club episode [A:13].","citations":["A:13"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","Identity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cobbett","definition":"William Cobbett (1763-1835), the radical journalist and historical writer whose works Patrick Stranahan places with Livy as a sufficient reading of history [A:109]. Separately, Cobbett is one of the aliases X lists for Gregory McColum Papadiabolous, the Irish twin or Papadiabolous II [D:285].","citations":["A:109","D:285"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Historical Figures","History & Memory","Devils (Characters)","Identity"]},{"name":"Cobbles","definition":"Paving stones. The warning that even the cobbles have ears belongs to the joke about disguising the visiting Americans as Australians [A:11]. At the Royal Pop History meeting, the turbaned speaker claims ironstone cobbles succeeded slates and shales in Decatur Street’s layered record [MTM B11:329]. Near the Naxos landing, six dead men, including the <em>Seabach</em> crew and Joe Cross, lie on wet cobbles [D:147].","citations":["A:11","MTM B11:329","D:147"],"tags":["Language & Words","New Orleans","Greece","The Dead","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Cocktail Parties","definition":"Part of Casey’s offered career for Finnegan as a celebrated proletarian painter: wealthy patrons, cocktail parties and house visits in exchange for occasional manifestos [A:183-184].","citations":["A:183-184"],"tags":["Finnegan","Visual Arts","Social Class"]},{"name":"Coconut","definition":"Image of the world as something Finnegan means to crack open, with or without Seaworthy’s help [D:18-19]. In Count Finnegan’s commentary, a Lever Brothers coconut plantation supplies a punning measure of Casey’s nuttiness [PG:544].","citations":["D:18-19","PG:544"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Coconut Possum","definition":"Animal heard squawking above Henry and Casey as they descend through the jungle [A:44]. Coconut possum is also among the meats Hans eats with the cliff people [A:58].","citations":["A:44","A:58"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Islands"]},{"name":"Code","definition":"Secret notation or a rule of conduct. The <em>Seabach</em>’s log includes cryptic numerical jottings [D:135]; X claims he can break any code in the papers Finnegan might have examined [D:188], asks a telephone contact about a missing code book, and supplies whistle signals for his companions [D:184]. Count Finnegan and Cardinal Hedayat receive coded assignments in the eschatological drama [MB:554], while coded bells communicate with dolphins and porpoises [MB:575]. Charlotte says Casey violates the confidence man’s code by exploiting the helpless [MTM B12:379]. In Casey’s political attack on the <em>Bark</em>, free elections are called unguided elections in code [A:130]; Trinali’s code forbids deliberately planning the picturesque early death she imagines [A:232].","citations":["D:135","D:188","D:184","MB:554","MB:575","MTM B12:379","A:130","A:232"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words","Identity","Crime"]},{"name":"Coffee House (by the harbor)","definition":"Harborside coffee house visited by Hans Schultz and Marie Monaghan after the day’s outings. They linger over coffee laced with brandy, talking about peoples, religion and marriage [A:7].","citations":["A:7"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Food & Dining","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Coffee House (Ghost)","definition":"The Ghost Coffee Shop, a New Orleans café run by Anthony Ghost, whose father came from the Netherlands. Its customers include dock workers, seamen, night people and schoolchildren, among them the Ursuline students discussing <em>Seven Roads</em> with Duffey [MTM B10:293]. A preceding account of literal ghosts gathering for one hour before and after dawn is explicitly retracted by the narrator, except for the existence of Count Finnegan’s painting of ghostly customers [MTM B10:292].","citations":["MTM B10:293","MTM B10:292"],"tags":["New Orleans","Ghosts & Spirits","Food & Dining","Duffey"]},{"name":"Coffee making and consumption","definition":"A frequent accompaniment to conversation and ordinary hospitality. Hans and Marie linger over brandy-laced coffee while Hans credits Clement VIII with giving the drink Christian sanction [A:7]. Finnegan, Anastasia, Marie, Papa Devil and Joe Cross have coffee ashore at a small establishment [D:67], and Doll includes it in the graveyard picnic [D:284]. Duffey discusses <em>Seven Roads</em> with the schoolgirls at the Ghost Coffee Shop [MTM B10:293]. Coffee becomes uncanny when Charlotte serves it without cups on the Great Day [GD:484], while the still-warm coffee on the supposedly derelict <em>Sarcophagus</em> belongs to Castleman’s false report as related by Le Marin [D:245]. A man Don Lewis asks for help dismisses five hundred dollars as insufficient coffee money [D:108]. Coffee bushes belong to Duffey’s remembered domain as the Boy King [MTM B1:17], and coffee making is included among the everyday arts said to be degraded after the Devil’s release [MTM B6:207].","citations":["A:7","D:67","D:284","MTM B10:293","GD:484","D:245","D:108","MTM B1:17","MTM B6:207"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Finnegan","Duffey","Ships","Alcohol","The Devil"]},{"name":"Coffin","definition":"Container for death, survival and jokes about both. Le Marin says the <em>Sarcophagus</em> was coffin-shaped [D:244]. Finnegan reports burying Papa Devil without a coffin on top of another man [D:153]. The <em>Argo</em> carries more coffins than would be needed for all its Masters [MTM B13:449]; Duffey’s stripped bones rest in one until God tells him to resume his work [MTM B13:443]. Zabotski supplies an unused coffin for Letitia, who had admired its class [MTM B8:253]. Show Boat jokingly hopes Dotty’s funeral will be their next reunion and says Dotty should look pretty in a coffin [A:262-263]. Trinali’s ode about herself in her coffin is buried with her [A:240].","citations":["D:244","D:153","MTM B13:449","MTM B13:443","MTM B8:253","A:262-263","A:240"],"tags":["Ships","Duffey","Devils (Characters)","Women Characters","Literature","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Cognomen","definition":"Finnegan’s word for the name by which he introduces himself to Seaworthy while unable to remember his proper name [D:11]. X later describes the many names accumulated by the Papadiabolous twins [D:285].","citations":["D:11","D:285"],"tags":["Finnegan","Devils (Characters)","Identity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cognoscenti","definition":"One of X’s overlapping names for the floating intellectual world, together with intelligentsia and gnostics. He says this group was disproportionately represented among the invited witnesses at the Devil’s release [MTM B6:205].","citations":["MTM B6:205"],"tags":["The Devil","Language & Words","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Coil","definition":"The snake coiled in Justinian’s skull in a “Promontory Goats” monologue supplies the image for judgments attributed to a snake rather than to the dead speaker himself [PG:545]. The Sidewinder, Nick, is likewise described as coiling laterally and striking as he falls wounded in the final shooting [A:282].","citations":["PG:545","A:282"],"tags":["Identity","Death & Mortality","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Coin parlor","definition":"One of the Kasmir Street businesses where, according to Newton Prescott, every coin parlor once had at least one Casey Machine, an achronological eavesdropping device [CM:503].","citations":["CM:503"],"tags":["Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Coin-bending","definition":"Strongman feat in Hans Schultz’s carnival repertoire. The account says it requires angry determination and disregard for pain, comparing it with bending horseshoes and specially tempered steel bars [A:219].","citations":["A:219"],"tags":["Carnival","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Colchis","definition":"Black Sea kingdom associated with the Argonauts’ quest for the Golden Fleece. The <em>Argo</em> sails under its flag [MTM B12:386], and its logs record the finding of the Fleece as a major early triumph [MTM B13:421]. Combings from that Fleece furnish Duffey’s golden mask, scarf and gauntlets after his reduction to bones [MTM B13:443]. In the Royal Pop Historians’ account, destroying the Black Sea is intended both to erase the Devil’s prison and to weaken Colchis as a base of the <em>Argo</em> forces [MTM B11:336-337].","citations":["MTM B12:386","MTM B13:421","MTM B13:443","MTM B11:336-337"],"tags":["Argonautica","Ships","Duffey","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Colfax, Jenny","definition":"One of the theatrical friends in Duffey and Letitia’s Chicago circle, named together with Duke Colfax [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Theater"]},{"name":"Collagen contractility","definition":"One item in the elaborate physiological list used by <em>The Eighteenth Book of Strange Encounters</em> to claim that Duffey is more than four thousand years old, despite the apparent fifty-five-year age of his body. The fictional report groups it with Howship’s lacunar frequency, thrombocyte-shaped remnants and other tests, then quotes Duffey’s explanation that a person may be older than his body [MTM B8:250].","citations":["MTM B8:250"],"tags":["Duffey","Language & Words","Identity"]},{"name":"Collins, Emmet (Emma)","definition":"Emma Collins, a young woman, celebrated impersonator and protégée of X, who appears as Emmet Collins of Boston, the double of Cardinal Merry of Cork [MB:579] [MB:598]. She talks her way past the bounty-men by pretending to inspect their operation for Track and Total [MB:595-596]. Her extraordinary laughter after Pope Finnegan laments the lack of women’s roles exposes the disguise, and John Giwa demands X’s explanation [MB:597-598].","citations":["MB:579","MB:598","MB:595-596","MB:597-598"],"tags":["Women Characters","Theater","Identity"]},{"name":"Colmar","definition":"Alsatian city in modern France where Duffey experiences a restaurant scene borrowed from Sebastian Hilton’s memory shortly before his thirteenth birthday. He eats soupe aux grenouilles and Tournedos au Poivre Vert at the Rotisserie Schillinger; the narrator only conjectures that he is staying at the Hotel du Champ de Mars. The account notes that Colmar was not formally French at that remembered time, though it calls the place France [MTM B1:35].","citations":["MTM B1:35"],"tags":["France","Duffey","Food & Dining","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Colonial","definition":"A historical style and a form of collective life. Angela says her father could imitate the old handwriting of Englishmen, Spaniards, Frenchmen and Colonials on his forged maps [D:260]. Brannagan orders a volvox colonial as fruit in the future settlement [MTM B13:418]. A separate grotesque catalogue of proposed murders imagines rapidly spreading colonial microbes [MB:581].","citations":["D:260","MTM B13:418","MB:581"],"tags":["Language & Words","Food & Dining","Crime"]},{"name":"Colony Club, The","definition":"Chicago club whose sunlit rooms, gaming, loaded sideboards, abundant beef dinners and Canadian whisky embody its splendid vulgarity and fast money [MTM B3:93]. Duffey visits with Lily and Letitia Koch; the Mullens group, except the newly arrived Manolo, is known there as the Cavendishes. Charlotte describes her stolen fortune and the mind-alarm that has exposed the disposable Mullens name [MTM B3:93]. Sebastian joins the Harvesters’ Dinner and discusses Duffey’s coming marriage; the Girl Countess later plays bridge with Charlotte, Gloria and Lily [MTM B3:94-95].","citations":["MTM B3:93","MTM B3:94-95"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Alcohol","Food & Dining","Money & Trade","Clubs & Societies","Social Class"]},{"name":"Color","definition":"A pervasive means of describing bodies, atmosphere and art. Anastasia’s olive complexion shifts between green and gold, and her eyes between black and purple [D:32]; Marie’s coloring is vividly red-blond [D:57], Seaworthy recovers his granite color after turning ashen [D:15], and Papa Devil has a ghoulish purple hue [D:30]. Angela appears in dazzling color [D:259]. Finnegan explains differences in sky color through fuel smoke [D:127], while X’s eight-toned car surrounds him with a seeming rainbow [D:178-179]. Duffey has sorrel hair and fire-blue eyes [MTM B1:6]; Beth Keegan has black hair and ivory skin [MTM B2:56]; and the frozen woman’s veins, eyelids and flesh between the toes are sky blue [MTM B13:398]. The Finnegan effigy’s destruction releases scarlet, crimson, sulphur, saffron and flame-colored blood, converging on garish orange [MTM B11:351].","citations":["D:32","D:57","D:15","D:30","D:259","D:127","D:178-179","MTM B1:6","MTM B2:56","MTM B13:398","MTM B11:351"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Women Characters","Visual Arts","Identity"]},{"name":"Columkill","definition":"Baptismal name of Katie Noonan’s son, called Ifreann by his mother and given the patronym Gregorovitch after Gregory, his adoptive father. X reports that the parish register lists the Devil as father and favors that explanation, while acknowledging Gregory as another possibility [D:279].","citations":["D:279"],"tags":["Sacraments","Devils (Characters)","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Combat Books (Special)","definition":"Books from which Sebastian Hilton knows fighting tricks. He uses them while theatrically provoking and humiliating larger schoolboys, casting them as bullies before the witnesses [MTM B1:23].","citations":["MTM B1:23"],"tags":["Combat","Literature"]},{"name":"Combat Buffalos","definition":"Amphibious armed vehicles in the war-invasion half of Elroy Redheart’s second red room, emerging from landing craft with combat alligators amid artificial thunder and lightning [MTM B7:222].","citations":["MTM B7:222"],"tags":["Combat","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Combings, Golden","definition":"Threads taken from the Great Golden Fleece of Colchis and made by Casey Gorshok into a mask, scarf and gauntlets for Duffey’s exposed bones. They enable his continued appearance as an <em>Argo</em> Master after God instructs him to resume service despite being dead [MTM B13:443].","citations":["MTM B13:443"],"tags":["Argonautica","Duffey","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Combo","definition":"A small performing ensemble. Examples include Duffey’s Rounders’ String Band [MTM B2:52], the Monumental Jazz musicians at Good Guy’s [MTM B7:220], and the combo that plays The King Shall Ride and the Gadarene Swine Song during Duffey’s reunion with his creations at Rounders’ [MTM B5:158]. Kirol forms combos as part of his changing carnival arrangements [A:215]. The Brimstone Combo belongs to Trinali’s poem about a saloon in the imagined groves of Gomorrah [A:235].","citations":["MTM B2:52","MTM B7:220","MTM B5:158","A:215","A:235"],"tags":["Music","Theater","Clubs & Societies","Duffey","Literature"]},{"name":"Comedian","definition":"See <em>Clown</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Theater","Identity"]},{"name":"Comedy","definition":"A mode ranging from comic entertainment to the shape of human and sacred events. X compares Finnegan’s narrow escapes from his pursuers to an old comedy [D:177]. Musical comedy, nightclub comedy and two-reel comedy making figure among the popular arts said to be trashed after the Devil’s release [MTM B6:207-209]. Showboat regards Duffey as her maker and scenarist in the human comedy [MTM B5:181]. Goldfarb’s Alley is the family television comedy interrupted by Duffey’s projected confrontation with Casey [MTM B12:380]. Pope Finnegan calls the conclave an Eschatological Comedy, joking that its sole defect is the absence of women’s roles, immediately before Emma Collins’s disguise is exposed [MB:597-598].","citations":["D:177","MTM B6:207-209","MTM B5:181","MTM B12:380","MB:597-598"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Popes","Theater","Literature"]},{"name":"Comic Books","definition":"Alternative to a newspaper proposed by the young newspaper worker whom Duffey confronts on the Great Day. Since everything is new, the worker argues, there can be no news; old comic-book reruns may replace the paper [GD:487].","citations":["GD:487"],"tags":["Duffey","Literature","Journalism"]},{"name":"Comic Magazines","definition":"Finnegan recalls reading an abundant collection of old comic magazines, with a bottle for company, while off duty from an engine-room job on an unnamed ship. He expected an Atlantic port but disembarked in San Francisco [A:145]. Beth Keegan also draws much of her playful dialogue from comic magazines rather than plays [MTM B2:60].","citations":["A:145","MTM B2:60"],"tags":["Ships","Finnegan","Literature","Theater"]},{"name":"Comic Mask","definition":"Finnegan’s description of his face after identifying himself with the boy in the lost-face story. He says he has a comic mask for a face, a monicker for a name and only a shadow’s shadow, expressing his sense of being a changeling without a secure identity [A:56].","citations":["A:56"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature","Identity"]},{"name":"Comic Monsters","definition":"Bagby’s description of the Slant-Faced Men’s treatment in pulp-magazine letter departments, where they are made into comic monsters like BEMs and HLPs. Dotty identifies three comic strips using them; she would find them funny if she did not know they were real [MTM B7:225].","citations":["MTM B7:225"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Literature"]},{"name":"Commission","definition":"A sales fee, an artistic order or a public body. Duffey and Rattigan sell fellow pupils’ goods on commission, largely as an accommodation [MTM B1:26]. Trinali earns five dollars in rent credit for finding Hans as a tenant [A:227], while Finnegan receives a dealer’s standing order for all the Orange Period paintings he can produce [A:191]. Honeybucket faces the Corporation Commission over his proposed stock issue [D:218]. A reporter asks Zabotski about his complaint to the City Commission concerning street flooding [MTM B9:289].","citations":["MTM B1:26","A:227","A:191","D:218","MTM B9:289"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Finnegan","Duffey","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Commission Salesman","definition":"Finnegan’s first white-collar employment, selling a dog-housebreaking preparation door to door in Buffalo. He initially sells only two or three bottles a day, earning seventy-five cents per bottle, while McGregor supplies exhausting demonstrations [A:189]. He becomes successful by marketing the preparation to practical jokers, selling three thousand bottles in a week before retiring from the business [A:190].","citations":["A:189","A:190"],"tags":["Finnegan","Occupations","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Committee","definition":"Collective machinery for social action, sometimes satirical. The Moral Sanctions Committee for the Removal of Eye-Sores and Abominations confronts Zabotski about his growing structure [MTM B9:276-277]. The <em>Union Sheet</em>’s fellows seem to appear only in committees [MTM B7:213]. Manuel instead calls himself a committee of one, charged with infiltrating the apparatus that makes devils, because he trusts only his own incorruptibility [D:56].","citations":["MTM B9:276-277","MTM B7:213","D:56"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","The Devil","Journalism"]},{"name":"Commodore (Bar)","definition":"Sydney drinking place visited while Hans rapidly trains for the beer contest, and revisited on the celebratory pub crawl after he defeats Bushmaster [A:18] [A:23].","citations":["A:18","A:23"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Australia","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Common Law (Obscure bit)","definition":"Finnegan’s claimed legal justification for temporarily appropriating George Brubaker’s car: the Lien of Extreme Need. He invokes it after the girl driving him realizes the car may not be his [D:168].","citations":["D:168"],"tags":["Finnegan","Crime","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Common Sense","definition":"The narrator’s joking explanation for Stein’s quick mastery of the Dominion Rules for beer-drinking contests: little in them supposedly falls outside common law and common sense [A:19].","citations":["A:19"],"tags":["Alcohol","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Communications Shack","definition":"The <em>Sarcophagus</em>’s radio shack, seized first when the replacement dock rats take over the ship in Le Marin’s account. Finnegan makes it an analogy for the world’s takeover, saying its communications shack was also captured first [D:244].","citations":["D:244"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Ships","Identity","Crime"]},{"name":"Communism","definition":"Atheistic Communism is one of the seven contemporary heads Bagby assigns to the old enemy [MTM B5:169]. In Show Boat’s letter, Vincent argues that their circle talks too much about Communism while neglecting the secular-liberal threat [A:263]. Casey’s editorial attacks the <em>Bark</em> for identifying China’s supposedly progressive forces with Western Communism [A:130].","citations":["MTM B5:169","A:263","A:130"],"tags":["Cosmic Struggle","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Communist","definition":"Political label with unstable personal associations. Casey calls Stein, under the name Hugo Stone, a card-carrying Communist [A:129]. <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> preserves conflicting accounts of whether Stein and Hugo are the same person; Casey says he heard of Stein’s Chicago Communist identity but knew him only later in the army [MTM B4:112]. X describes Ifreann as an old-line Communist and gives the movement a fiercely hostile characterization [D:281]. Duffey’s Hungarian tavern-sign Christ comes from the brief Communist period of Béla Kun, but the picture itself shows a hearty Christ drinking beer, not Christ identified as a Communist [MTM B3:103].","citations":["A:129","MTM B4:112","D:281","MTM B3:103"],"tags":["Identity","Devils (Characters)","Visual Arts","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Communist Thing","definition":"Henry’s father Lawrence calls Communism one shell in the old Antagonist’s shell game and warns that it may be a diversion. Henry answers that, among the many holes admitting water to the boat, it is presently one of the largest [A:125-126].","citations":["A:125-126"],"tags":["Cosmic Struggle","Family"]},{"name":"Community Buildings","definition":"City-park buildings among the venues for Duffey and Letitia’s children’s puppet shows. Their other sites include fire stations, lodge halls, children’s homes, hospitals, libraries, auditoriums and schools; Letitia makes and manipulates the puppets and supplies their voices [MTM B4:133].","citations":["MTM B4:133"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Women Characters","Theater"]},{"name":"Companion","definition":"A fellow participant whose identity may itself be uncertain. Vincent is Finnegan’s companion at the opening of Archipelago [A:1]. Teresa describes taking an Oread’s part with two other Oreads, and Finnegan’s ghostly participation in her marriage to Vincent [MTM B6:188]. Papa Devil calls Dopey Finnegan’s fetch or double [D:121]; a leftfooted killer and an unnamed companion follow Finnegan after his departure [D:193]. On the <em>Argo</em>, the newly recognized old companion of Duffey and Brannagan is Kasmir Gorshok [MTM B13:426]. The ship carries effigies of respected companions as well as Masters [MTM B13:449]. Count Finnegan arrives with Gilberto Levine and O’Brien and Herman Hercules; the text leaves open whether these are doubles of dead cardinals or the cardinals disguised as their own doubles [MTM B13:450-453].","citations":["A:1","MTM B6:188","D:121","D:193","MTM B13:426","MTM B13:449","MTM B13:450-453"],"tags":["Ships","Finnegan","Duffey","Argonauts (Characters)","Identity"]},{"name":"Company","definition":"A group of associates or a business concern. The Devil is Dead announces Papadiabolous and his company [D:6], and the <em>Brunhilde</em>’s company includes the Devil [D:31]. The Old Wooden Ship’s resident community is called the Company of Fifty [D:215-216] [D:225]; Duffey names the Devil’s invited adherents the Company of One Thousand [MTM B6:208]. Artists contribute to Duffey and his company’s rebuilding project [MTM B6:199]. Commercial examples include the Acme and Rock Island names on remembered pump handles [MTM B1:17], the chewing-tobacco firm in Marie’s Shakes Pearson anecdote [A:25], Duffey and Letitia’s insurer [MTM B3:96], and the consortium buying Duffey’s school businesses [MTM B2:38]. Honeybucket calls his untried plankton enterprise the Ancient Syrian Cheese Company [D:218-219]. The edition’s copyright page also calls its publisher a Hachette UK company [D:296].","citations":["D:6","D:31","D:215-216","D:225","MTM B6:208","MTM B6:199","MTM B1:17","A:25","MTM B3:96","MTM B2:38","D:218-219","D:296"],"tags":["The Devil","Ships","Finnegan","Duffey","Bars & Taverns","Money & Trade","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Company of Fifty, The","definition":"The resident regulars of the Old Wooden Ship, whose roster ought to number fifty although the narrator admits forgetting one. They include McGivern, four women called Dotty, the five so-called Swedes and many other individual characters. Frequent visitors such as Finnegan, Le Marin and Dry Gulch Cavaldos are explicitly distinguished from these residents [D:215-216]. Finnegan immerses himself in the company for reassurance in the reality of humanity; the account admits that Susie Kutz’s favorable reports color its portrayal [D:225].","citations":["D:215-216","D:225"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Identity"]},{"name":"Company of One Thousand","definition":"Duffey’s name for the permitted witnesses to the Devil’s release near Yalta, whom he regards as adherents transformed by the encounter [MTM B6:208]. X says exactly one thousand people held permits and that his own, issued under an old assumed identity, was number 982 [MTM B6:204-205].","citations":["MTM B6:208","MTM B6:204-205"],"tags":["The Devil","Identity","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Compass","definition":"The <em>Argo</em>’s compass needle bends toward something below decks as the hidden Man of Mystery’s presence asserts itself; an effigy seaman attributes the distortion to personal magnetism [MTM B13:461]. After the Antichrist reaches the Plains of Megiddo, every compass needle in the world points there as the world’s new center [MTM B13:462].","citations":["MTM B13:461","MTM B13:462"],"tags":["Apocalyptic","Ships","Sailing"]},{"name":"Compassion","definition":"Concern for another’s suffering, sometimes ambiguous or perilously excessive. Seaworthy professes it for Finnegan’s bleeding feet [D:11], and Manuel claims Marie has it for him [D:56]. Charley Murray feels it for the larger boys Sebastian intends to defeat [MTM B1:22]. Papa Piccone’s compassionate acceptance of a mouse instead of a dinosaur may be directed toward the mouse or toward embarrassed Duffey [MTM B5:170]. Casey nearly equals Hans in compassion but conceals it better [A:169]; the “Promontory Goats” introduction suspects his extreme compassion, especially the wish to redeem the Devil, has become sinful [PG:518]. Kasmir Gorshok’s pity for body lice motivates the creation of artificial people as hosts [PG:535]. A modest coffee place is called compassionate [D:67], while the speculative Goat Contingency leads its perpetual debt-payers toward the hell named The End of Compassion [MTM B10:299].","citations":["D:11","D:56","MTM B1:22","MTM B5:170","A:169","PG:518","PG:535","D:67","MTM B10:299"],"tags":["Sin & Redemption","Heaven & Hell","Finnegan","Duffey","Creation"]},{"name":"Compulsion","definition":"An overriding urge. Finnegan leaves Dotty under a compulsion to seek without finding [A:176]. Duffey feels compelled to find his future wife in Chicago before knowing who she is [MTM B3:82]; the sought moth is finally identified with Letitia [MTM B3:80] [MTM B3:90]. Cleo’s explanation of the Goat Contingency describes compulsively trading the worthy for the worthless [MTM B10:295]; Casey’s pursuit of the Devil’s redemption is already an obsession in his schooldays [PG:518].","citations":["A:176","MTM B3:82","MTM B3:80","MTM B3:90","MTM B10:295","PG:518"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Women Characters","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Computer","definition":"A device invoked in Duffey’s proposed test of Cardinal Artemis’s flayed skin against the marked skin of the sleeping Gilberto. He suggests flaying the sleeper and running both skins through their computer; the passage does not show the proposal carried out. X and Duffey remain uncertain which man is the original and which the double [MTM B13:451-452].","citations":["MTM B13:451-452"],"tags":["Duffey","Identity"]},{"name":"Con man","definition":"A swindler or trickster. Henry toasts Sammy as a con man after Sammy borrows the distracted shoeshine boys’ box to earn whisky money, with Finnegan supplying the musical distraction [A:67-68]. Angela’s father supplies confidence men with fabricated treasure maps and matching half-maps designed for elaborate swindles [D:260-261].","citations":["A:67-68","D:260-261"],"tags":["Crime","Finnegan","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Conceit","definition":"Vanity or an imagined construction. Amoy the parakeet is conceited enough to call himself beautiful despite the narrator’s contrary judgment [A:79]. Finnegan attributes conceit to the fugue in his letter about music’s unkindness [A:263]. Elwin Elkheart dismisses the <em>Argo</em> and divine interventions as Duffey’s conceits [MTM B13:402]. Anastasia reports that Marie regards the category of lady as a bourgeois conceit, while insisting on her own claim to it [D:33].","citations":["A:79","A:263","MTM B13:402","D:33"],"tags":["Language & Words","Duffey","Women Characters","Music"]},{"name":"Concentration","definition":"Duffey’s intense mental concentration, or prayer, before seven exhausted mice arrive to perform in the puppet show at Gurdon S. Hubbard Elementary School. The teacher opens the doors and Letitia plays banjo during the wait [MTM B4:136].","citations":["MTM B4:136"],"tags":["Prayer & Devotion","Duffey","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Concertina","definition":"Instrument Finnegan plays in bars and streets, provoking Ignacio’s objection to his lack of dignity [A:272]. Theresa says playing it has spread her fingers, along with her work with puppets and tricks [A:108].","citations":["A:272","A:108"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Music","Theater"]},{"name":"Conch","definition":"Shell whose intricate shape supplies a comparison for Trinali’s chosen driftwood, destined to become a carved lamp [A:233]. In Count Finnegan’s commentary, the shape of a conch can move Casey to tears, illustrating the sensitivity that remains alongside his dangerous commitments [PG:544].","citations":["A:233","PG:544"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Literature","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Conches","definition":"Creatures in Bayougoula Park’s suddenly present fountain, alongside turtles, frogs, whistling blue fish, carp, oysters and dangerous alligators [MTM B11:331].","citations":["MTM B11:331"],"tags":["New Orleans","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Conclave","definition":"An assembly, royal, personal or ecclesiastical. Duffey claims attendance at Kings’ Conclaves with Solomon, Saul, Ptolemy, Chandragupta, Nebuchadnezzar, Hsien, Barbarossa, Stephen of Hungary and Conrad II [MTM B5:166]. He suspects his talismanic creations of arranging their St. Louis gathering independently of him [MTM B5:150-152]. Cardinal Henri reads a prophetic reference to the future conclave and rejects its assumption that he will already be dead, proposing to attend as his double’s double [MTM B12:387-388]. In <em>More Than Melchisedech</em>’s later events, the supposed Last Conclave at Babylonia Bagascia assembles to dismantle the Ekklesia, with Monsignor X as servitor [MB:561] [MB:566] [MB:569]. X later serves the thirteen at Mooring-Stone, where the ostensibly surviving doubles include true cardinals and elect Pope Finnegan I [MB:596-598].","citations":["MTM B5:166","MTM B5:150-152","MTM B12:387-388","MB:561","MB:566","MB:569","MB:596-598"],"tags":["Popes","Ecclesiastical","Prophecy & Visions","Finnegan","Duffey","Ekklesia","Identity"]},{"name":"Concordia","definition":"Concordia, one of the institutions where Duffey takes courses during the summer of 1915, alongside St. Louis University, Washington University, a pharmacy school, and a music school. The novel does not give its full institutional name [MTM B2:49].","citations":["MTM B2:49"],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Concordia University","definition":"Earlier heading supplied University, which the novel does not give. See <em>Concordia</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Concrete (Ready-mix)","definition":"Tim Stranahan’s ready-mix business, in which he offers Finnegan a job. Dotty jokes that the work may consist of modeling concrete overcoats, prompting Finnegan to doubt Tim’s fondness for him [A:100].","citations":["A:100"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Finnegan","Occupations"]},{"name":"Condensors","definition":"Devices in which Newton Prescott claims to have trapped more than two hundred postmortem electrical discharges, each occurring about thirty minutes after clinical death. In Josephine McSorely’s purported report, he proposes using one charge in every Casey Machine and interprets part of the data as coming from beyond death [CM:507].","citations":["CM:507"],"tags":["Death & Mortality","Literature"]},{"name":"Confederate","definition":"The ringleader boy’s supposed helper in finding the Sleeping Man’s cave on the Marianao Coast. He demands two additional gold pieces from Duffey for this confederate, then returns with a cave containing a plainly recent Cuban stand-in instead of Finnegan [MTM B12:373].","citations":["MTM B12:373"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Money & Trade","Crime"]},{"name":"Conference","definition":"A private consultation or a formal political meeting. Finnegan, Vincent, Tom and Freddy withdraw to confer when Bushmaster nearly spoils the Australian-disguise hoax [A:12]. Sammy and Finnegan confer before their shoeshine con [A:67]. In X’s account of the Devil’s imprisonment, the principals of the Yalta Conference consult the prisoner daily or hourly [MTM B6:204].","citations":["A:12","A:67","MTM B6:204"],"tags":["The Devil","Finnegan","Identity","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Confession","definition":"Finnegan confesses his drinking, sins and fear of an alien kindred within him to the Capuchin on Naxos; the priest first rejects ordinary blame-shifting, then accepts the possibility of another kindred and grants absolution [D:119]. Duffey tells his blackmailer that the genuinely guilty acts in the compromising photographs have already been confessed and absolved [MTM B4:140]. Charlotte, anticipating danger in her current theft, tells Duffey she needs confession for exploiting widows and orphans, carnality and badger games before they enter St. Malachy’s [MTM B3:87].","citations":["D:119","MTM B4:140","MTM B3:87"],"tags":["Sacraments","Finnegan","Duffey","Women Characters","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Confidence and gamine friends","definition":"Duffey and Letitia’s Chicago associates described as the better grade of confidence people: Charlotte Garfield, Gideon Sedgewick, Mary Regina Toast, Ralph Kirby, Ira and Rebecca Spain, Victor Ryan, Homer and Evangeline Durban, and Fred and Helen Batavia [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters","Crime"]},{"name":"Confidence people","definition":"Swindlers represented among the roughly five hundred confidence people and thieves attending the Royal Rogues’ and Graceful Swindlers’ Ninth Annual Convention and Confidence-Men’s Congress at the Royal Orleans Hotel. Charlotte attends as a visitor [MTM B7:216]. Anastasia tells Manuel that spies and confidence men ordinarily pass themselves off as traveling salesmen [D:55].","citations":["MTM B7:216","D:55"],"tags":["Crime","Clubs & Societies","Identity"]},{"name":"Confirmation Name","definition":"According to Casey, Melchisedech is Duffey’s confirmation name, taken when he was confirmed at forty; his given name in that account is Michael [A:42].","citations":["A:42"],"tags":["Sacraments","Duffey","Identity"]},{"name":"Confraternity of the Ribbers","definition":"The community of practical jokers to whom Finnegan endears himself with his Buffalo dog-preparation trick, even while he and McGregor are banned from taverns. Their enthusiasm leads him to sell the product as a joke device [A:190].","citations":["A:190"],"tags":["Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Confusion","definition":"Uncertainty about identity, memory and events. The apparently simple facts of Finnegan’s life are said to contain amazing confusion, as others remember them differently [A:174]. Finnegan recalls burying Papadiabolous through a mental blind, while X questions how that dead man could have sailed again [D:153]. Zabotski’s Moral Sanction visitors remember both yesterday and eight years ago as the time of their earlier complaint [MTM B9:277]. The changed New Orleans morning contains a pleasant confusion of new arrivals and an unpleasant confusion of displaced creatures [MTM B11:314]. Askandan defines his trade as bringing confusion out of chaos, between excessive simplicity and intolerable disorder [A:257].","citations":["A:174","D:153","MTM B9:277","MTM B11:314","A:257"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Identity","Time & Eternity","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Conger, Herbert","definition":"One of the sporty people in Duffey and Letitia’s Chicago circle [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Conglomerate of driftwood","definition":"The mass of cast-up wood on the rough shore that Trinali calls the Elephants’ Graveyard. Her imagery makes its largest pieces the bones of mammoths, behemoths and Leviathan [A:232].","citations":["A:232"],"tags":["Women Characters","Literature"]},{"name":"Congress","definition":"A public or professional assembly. Bagby proposes that the U.S. Congress imitate Teras councils by including living and dead members [MTM B5:165]. Charlotte attends a Confidence-Men’s Congress in New Orleans [MTM B7:216], and George Koran lists the Student Repudiation Congress among the Majesty’s engagements [MTM B8:243]. A team from the Consenting Adults’ World Congress participates in the grotesque consumption of Paul XI’s remains during the Voltaire Festival [MB:565].","citations":["MTM B5:165","MTM B7:216","MTM B8:243","MB:565"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","The Dead","The Devil","Popes"]},{"name":"Congruence","definition":"A fitting together of events or patterns. Duffey anticipates a seemingly impossible convergence of his creations [MTM B5:150-151] and tells a distressed woman, whom he sends back toward reconciliation, not to wonder too much about the congruence of events [MTM B4:144]. In “Promontory Goats,” molecular congruencies figure among the physical traces a speaker believes invading spirits can read in the brain, while the mind itself remains inaccessible [PG:539].","citations":["MTM B5:150-151","MTM B4:144","PG:539"],"tags":["Duffey","Creation","Identity"]},{"name":"Conjurer's trick","definition":"Image for Anastasia’s extraordinary presence as she cleans and shaves Finnegan at the bar. Reality seems too pale a word for someone both fleshly and translucent; the narration calls her a conjurer’s trick, but not a cheap one [D:13].","citations":["D:13"],"tags":["Women Characters","Finnegan","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Conner, Mike","definition":"Golfer named with Peggy Conner among Duffey and Letitia’s Chicago sporty friends [MTM B3:99]. Both later attend Hilary Hilton’s gathering for Duffey [MTM B12:383].","citations":["MTM B3:99","MTM B12:383"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Conner, Peggy","definition":"One of Duffey’s Chicago friends, named with Mike Conner among the golfers [MTM B3:99]. She and Mike also attend Hilary Hilton’s later party [MTM B12:383]. See also Munster, Peggy.","citations":["MTM B3:99","MTM B12:383"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Duffey"]},{"name":"Consanguinity","definition":"Blood kinship linking the goat-man’s seven youngest kids, three sets of twins and one single birth from four young sister goats. Their affection and family relationship accompany their shared ambition to climb the fallen sycamore [A:194].","citations":["A:194"],"tags":["Family","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Conscience","definition":"Moral unease or restraint. Finnegan’s troubled conscience accompanies the dreams in which he resists the muses [A:181]. The purported <em>Underground Eagle</em> report says Casey repeatedly washes his hands and conscience of the Casey Machine affair, while another participant predicts he will nevertheless buy one [CM:507]. Johnny Duckwalk, seemingly without conscience, is unexpectedly seized by repentance and resolves to confess his theft [D:264]. Hans Schultz creates a consensus of conscience in an unnamed field of business where it had almost vanished [MTM B8:247].","citations":["A:181","CM:507","D:264","MTM B8:247"],"tags":["Sin & Redemption","Finnegan","Money & Trade","Dreams"]},{"name":"Consensus","definition":"Shared judgment or participation, which the books repeatedly distinguish from secure truth. Letitia calls apparently objective reality a consensus of private projections [MTM B3:96]; Zabotski’s neighbors unanimously find him quarrelsome, while the narrator insists they are all mistaken [MTM B9:269]. Hans creates a consensus of conscience in business [MTM B8:247]. X says the Liberal Consensus is prominent at the Devil’s release [MTM B6:205], and the later world’s consensus hatreds surround Hiram III after he refuses to abolish his nation [MB:563]. “Promontory Goats” describes groups in Hell choosing increasingly cruel shared tortures [PG:532-533]. Finnegan’s resurrection painting produces agreement among his friends that he is alive [MB:550]. Brannagan says withdrawing from the shimmering future can help break the consensus that makes it likely [MTM B13:470].","citations":["MTM B3:96","MTM B9:269","MTM B8:247","MTM B6:205","MB:563","PG:532-533","MB:550","MTM B13:470"],"tags":["The Devil","Finnegan","Duffey","Metaphysics","Heaven & Hell","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Consenting Adults' World Congress","definition":"Group whose winning team of fifty devours Paul XI’s entrails in a basilica after they have been used to strangle Hiram III during the Voltaire Festival [MB:565].","citations":["MB:565"],"tags":["Popes","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Conservatism (universe is inherently)","definition":"In a recurring vision, a great mathematician explains that the universe is inherently conservative: complete alterations occur only at intervals and require grave reason. His lecture connects a new alteration to the appearance of a chicken-hearted devil [PG:542].","citations":["PG:542"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Prophecy & Visions","The Devil"]},{"name":"Consignment","definition":"A shipment of goods awaiting realization or sale. Joseph the Haussa diamond smuggler seeks a trustworthy partner and says realizing on a consignment can take years [D:94]. Hillary Hilton’s distressed-goods ventures include spoiled bananas and consignments of wormy sesame seed [A:254]. Eva uses the word figuratively for the tall stories she must prepare if she takes over the Neanderthal [MTM B13:408].","citations":["D:94","A:254","MTM B13:408"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Consort, Passionate","definition":"Mary Louise Byrne’s description of herself in relation to Duffey. She calls herself both his sister “in some respect” and his passionate consort, while referring to another shared life that they keep below the threshold [MTM B2:60].","citations":["MTM B2:60"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Consortium","definition":"A group acting together. Duffey sells his boarding-school businesses to a consortium of other boys [MTM B2:38]. Beth Keegan and a consortium of friends contrive her dazzling voice, walk and theatrical persona; the narration extends this performance into the imagery of an invented woman or living statue [MTM B2:57].","citations":["MTM B2:38","MTM B2:57"],"tags":["Duffey","Money & Trade","Women Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Conspiracies","definition":"Secret plots that, in Papa Devil’s account, succeed partly by persuading people that no conspiracies exist [D:87]. The Loosed Devil repeats this instruction to his followers [MTM B8:242]. X describes pursuing the Conspiracy despite ridicule and reports a disputed encounter with the Devil [A:139-140]; Dotty mocks the investigators’ credulity when they accept a clipping announcing X’s death [A:141]. Breaking up secret plots and conspiracies is among the <em>Argo</em>’s purposes [MTM B13:405].","citations":["D:87","MTM B8:242","A:139-140","A:141","MTM B13:405"],"tags":["The Devil","Ships","Crime"]},{"name":"Conspiracy, The","definition":"The hostile movement X says he pursues beyond its apparent defeat [A:139]. In his account, Ifreann joined it when its evil was still undiluted by nonsense or Slavic imperialism [D:281]. By the later graveyard encounter, X says Seaworthy’s group has declined to its fringes, though it can still obtain funds [D:289].","citations":["A:139","D:281","D:289"],"tags":["The Devil","Clubs & Societies","Eastern Europe"]},{"name":"Conspirators, Alien","definition":"X’s designation for the group containing the original Papadiabolous, whom he calls its biggest member. He describes the group as well financed and unusually free to act, with Seaworthy as executive officer [D:155].","citations":["D:155"],"tags":["The Devil","Devils (Characters)","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Constant Gnawing","definition":"X’s description of the long struggle of the “other people”: gradual undercutting and inflaming rather than a final climax. Doll disputes his prediction and insists that the conflict must be brought to an end [D:292].","citations":["D:292"],"tags":["Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Constantine, Saint","definition":"Constantine the Great, Roman emperor from 306 to 337 and the first Christian emperor, recognized as a saint in the Eastern Church. Henry recognizes Constantine among the figures aboard the <em>Navicula Petri</em> in his dream [A:45]. With Licinius he issued the Edict of Milan in 313, granting religious liberty. He summoned the Council of Nicaea in 325, which affirmed the Son’s full divinity, and founded the new imperial capital Constantinople on the site of Byzantium, dedicated in 330.","citations":["A:45"],"tags":["Saints","Historical Figures","Church History","Ships","Dreams"]},{"name":"Constellations of the Oceans","definition":"Finnegan’s poetic description of ocean islands as constellations that prefigure the skies, spoken to Hans while reflecting on Xavier and the great archipelagos [A:35].","citations":["A:35"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Islands","Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Constitution","definition":"The American constitutional document whose authority the <em>Bark</em> supports. In Casey’s hostile <em>Crock</em> editorial, the magazine is criticized for placing undue value on a document he regards as worthy but transitory, parts of which might survive in future constitutions [A:128] [A:130].","citations":["A:128","A:130"],"tags":["Journalism","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Constitutionalist","definition":"Casey’s label for the <em>Bark</em>’s domestic political position in his hostile <em>Crock</em> editorial, where he criticizes its attachment to the Constitution [A:128] [A:130].","citations":["A:128","A:130"],"tags":["Journalism"]},{"name":"Construction Job","definition":"A building project on which Finnegan spends a week with Hans in a shack, probably in the summer of 1950. Hans’s off-hours recollections supply much of the account of his early life [A:207].","citations":["A:207"],"tags":["Finnegan","Occupations","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Contact","definition":"A connection through which people exchange information or gain access. Manuel hopes Marie may become his contact in his investigation of the devils [D:56]. Papa says Don Lewis contacted him from a distance but did not recognize him on arrival [D:120]. X cites contacts who reported Papadiabolous I’s surprise, after death, at learning he had a twin [D:286]. Finnegan parks outside a police station because X says this will make pursuers pause to develop contacts inside [D:198]. X later makes contact with John Mogul of Track and Total [MB:570]. The word also describes Duffey and Bagby’s many affinities [MTM B5:154], and the glancing contact of Casey and Stein from which the Kasmir-Stein combination sparks [MTM B5:183].","citations":["D:56","D:120","D:286","D:198","MB:570","MTM B5:154","MTM B5:183"],"tags":["The Devil","Duffey","Identity","Crime"]},{"name":"Contadino","definition":"Italian for a farmer or peasant. The term describes Giulio Solli when Finnegan realizes that Mary McCracken must have loved the old man to have married him [A:245].","citations":["A:245"],"tags":["Italy","Finnegan","Family","Social Class","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Containment Policy","definition":"Casey’s proposed way of holding the Thing in check. He questions whether containment can work, but doubts it can be defeated in any other way; he contrasts this patient struggle with Henry’s attempt to settle everything in one stroke [A:155].","citations":["A:155"],"tags":["Cosmic Struggle","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Contemplation","definition":"Duffey’s examination of the urn containing his own ashes while he eats and drinks with X at his New Orleans art shop [MTM B1:6].","citations":["MTM B1:6"],"tags":["Duffey","Death & Mortality","Paradox"]},{"name":"Contes Drolatiques, Les","definition":"Balzac’s French stories, a favorite of Gautier the goat-man, who reads them aloud to his goats. He calls the book filthy and Gothic, doubts whether he should read it to the young goats, but says Balzac appeals to goats and to the goat in man [A:196] [A:198].","citations":["A:196","A:198"],"tags":["Literature","France"]},{"name":"Context","definition":"The frame in which an identity or event makes sense. Finnegan’s shipboard dreams ask whether his discontinuous lives and the “other human race” can belong to one person [D:91]; letters from old friends seem addressed to another Finnegan in another context [D:115], and his memory of bending over Papa Devil seems detached from its context [D:83]. Father McGuigan accuses Patrick Stranahan of using a false context and unlawful metaphor when he discusses creating people; Duffey answers that his own creations are literal [MTM B5:166]. Duffey’s commentary examines Casey in <em>Argo</em>, medieval, secular and eschatological contexts [PG:534-535]. Rebeka discusses transformation activators in dreams [MTM B10:294]; the Seven Lost Years provide the context of the <em>Argo</em>’s fourteenth voyage and Duffey’s anomalous returns to ordinary life [MTM B10:300] [MTM B13:445]. Stein’s later account similarly places Finnegan between several worlds [MB:548-549].","citations":["D:91","D:115","D:83","MTM B5:166","PG:534-535","MTM B10:294","MTM B10:300","MTM B13:445","MB:548-549"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Identity","Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","Dreams","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Continent, Southern","definition":"The sunny land beyond an Antarctic ice ring in Casey’s fifth imaginary world. His fantasy requires a larger, melon-shaped earth; he imagines the southern continent as a possible Atlantis, to which he would carry modern techniques and from which he would bring back the Higher Ethic [A:158].","citations":["A:158"],"tags":["Dreams","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Continental Shelf","definition":"Undersea location of the Green Castles in Trinali’s fanciful account of a piece of driftwood’s travels [A:232-233].","citations":["A:232-233"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Contingency","definition":"A possible or conditionally realized world. Rafelson describes Duffey’s contingent creations approaching a fulfillment that could make them real retrospectively [MTM B4:146-147]; Patrick Stranahan describes talismanic creation through equations with as many as thirty billion interdependent integers [MTM B5:165-166]. Seven prominent alternatives are presented for Duffey: the Great Day’s dissolution of boundaries; the doubtful Goat Contingency involving Casey’s attempted soul trade and cosmic debt; the Petrine spy story; the <em>Argo</em>’s fourteenth voyage; the Thunder Colt world presentation; confrontation with the Loosed Devil; and a garden encounter with God [MTM B10:298-301]. These alternatives are not all mutually exclusive, and the <em>Argo</em> voyage is said to be certain. Cleo’s theatrical forecast of the seventh contingency introduces a rowdy bird carrying a warning that anything that can go wrong will [MTM B10:296-297]. Stein’s account of Finnegan likewise involves several worlds and disputed deaths [MB:548-549].","citations":["MTM B4:146-147","MTM B5:165-166","MTM B10:298-301","MTM B10:296-297","MB:548-549"],"tags":["Apocalyptic","The Devil","Finnegan","Duffey","Creation","Time & Eternity","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Contingent","definition":"In Casey’s discussion with Duffey, contingent philosophy includes problems Casey says can be expressed only mathematically. Duffey disagrees, maintaining that every problem can be stated in straightforward words [MTM B5:150].","citations":["MTM B5:150"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Language & Words","Duffey"]},{"name":"Continuous-Flow Federal Funding","definition":"One of the continuing subsidies Newton Prescott seeks for the Casey Machine. Alongside Counter-Flow and Impacted Entertainment Area funding, he argues for public payments because pay-per-use machines favor the rich. He obtains guarantees in perpetuity and funding for his own group to provide perpetual care for each machine site as a shrine [CM:503] [CM:512-513].","citations":["CM:503","CM:512-513"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Social Class"]},{"name":"Contract","definition":"A formal agreement, ranging from Duffey’s purchase of a St. Louis horse barn, with contract, bill of sale and deed [MTM B2:40], to the murder contracts against thirteen cardinals. The Final Conclave gives Track and Total the job [MB:569]. Its head John Mogul has distributed the targets among separate contractors; X asks for the entire job, but Mogul offers successive assignments conditional on performance, followed by proposed verbal agreements [MB:571-573].","citations":["MTM B2:40","MB:569","MB:571-573"],"tags":["Duffey","Money & Trade","Crime","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Contradiction, Principle of Non-","definition":"The philosophical principle which an epigraph attributed to Ignace Wolff says is thwarted by bodies; the same passage declares bodies to negate the principle of sufficient reason. It is credited to Gedanken von Leibern [D:67].","citations":["D:67"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Paradox"]},{"name":"Contraption","definition":"Zabotski’s enormous construction, intruding from his property onto his neighbors’ land [MTM B9:270-271]. Widow Waldo identifies its overlapping prototypes as ship, castle, anti-time machine and the old woman’s shoe [MTM B9:275]. It carries Waldo, children and animals away in the cloudburst, reaching Lake Borgne [MTM B9:287], and is later called a floating boat palace [MTM B10:290]. The watertight boat reappears when Duffey returns from the Fifth Contingency [MTM B12:356].","citations":["MTM B9:270-271","MTM B9:275","MTM B9:287","MTM B10:290","MTM B12:356"],"tags":["Ships","Time & Eternity","Old Testament","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Convention","definition":"A professional gathering, as in the Royal Rogues’ and Graceful Swindlers’ Ninth Annual Convention and Confidence-Men’s Congress, attended by Charlotte at the Royal Orleans Hotel [MTM B7:216], or the Society for Creative History’s New Orleans meeting [MTM B11:307]. The word also denotes the conventions of ordinary life: after his first death, Finnegan feels freed from the narrative constraints described as Aristotle’s Unities and Plutarch’s Heroics [D:161].","citations":["MTM B7:216","MTM B11:307","D:161"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","History & Memory","Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Conversation of ordinance","definition":"The gunfire Finnegan hears while still descending the hill on Naxos: rifles, BARs, fifty-caliber machine guns, and a heavier report that might be a forty-five-millimeter weapon. Not knowing what is happening below makes this “conversation” maddening [D:143-144].","citations":["D:143-144"],"tags":["Greece","Islands","Finnegan","Weapons"]},{"name":"Coogan, Kester","definition":"Artist whose work Duffey offers for sale early in his New Orleans enterprise, among the artists whose ideas contribute to his rebuilding project [MTM B6:199].","citations":["MTM B6:199"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Minor Characters","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Coon Dogs","definition":"Hunting dogs with which Finnegan and his country friends have spent the night before their early-morning conversation about his coming graveyard meeting [D:273].","citations":["D:273"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Coon Hunting","definition":"One of the popular arts of everyday life said to lose its class and style in the massive degradation of the arts following the Devil’s release [MTM B6:207].","citations":["MTM B6:207"],"tags":["The Devil"]},{"name":"Coons","definition":"Raccoons among the creatures around Hans’s childhood nursery grounds [A:208]. In Finnegan’s account of the elusive Chosky Bottoms, the grandfather of all big coons belongs to that legendary place. After a hunt, Finnegan has shot a boar coon estimated at sixty pounds and boasts it would be small where he comes from; his companions reject the boast [D:274].","citations":["A:208","D:274"],"tags":["Finnegan","Folklore"]},{"name":"Cooperative, The","definition":"A term in the <em>Crock</em> article title “The Consumer, the Cooperative, and the Coefficient of Cost Constancy,” identified as written in Duffey’s style among the magazine’s largely Duffey-written contents [A:41].","citations":["A:41"],"tags":["Duffey","Journalism"]},{"name":"Copenhagen","definition":"One end of the range of ports in which Henry Salvatore is said to have sampled the vices of a merchant seaman, “from Copenhagen to Aruba” [A:124].","citations":["A:124"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Ports & Harbors"]},{"name":"Copper Box (Angela Cosquin sa Caisse)","definition":"Angela’s personal savings box, inscribed “Angela Cosquin sa Caisse,” meaning Angela Cosquin, her cash box. After returning salvaged treasure to the <em>St. Marc</em> and <em>San gre del Cristo</em> wrecks, she places her own money in this copper box and hides it beside the hoard. She subsequently adds one saved gold sovereign each month [D:262].","citations":["D:262"],"tags":["Shipwrecks","Money & Trade","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Copper-iron nail","definition":"The large, sharp nail of old Macedonian workmanship fastening the Royal Pop People’s parchment notice to Duffey’s door. He initially thinks it brass or bronze, then identifies it as copper-iron [MTM B11:312-313].","citations":["MTM B11:312-313"],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Coq Bleu","definition":"One of the nightspots visited by Hans and his bohemian friends during the evening that ends with the group dispersing at dawn [A:239].","citations":["A:239"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Coracle","definition":"A small rounded boat. A coracle appears on one side of the red-gold coin Biloxi Brannagan produces to identify himself aboard the <em>Argo</em>; the other side bears the Celtic Cross of Christ, and the coin carries the name Naomh Brandon [MTM B13:425].","citations":["MTM B13:425"],"tags":["Saints","Ships","Money & Trade","Identity"]},{"name":"Coracle Boat","definition":"See <em>Coracle</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Saints","Ships","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Coral","definition":"Reef-forming material produced by hard corals’ calcium-carbonate skeletons. Hans describes islands passed on the journey to Luzon as coral and limestone [A:62]. Finnegan’s Sergeant goes out from coral reefs with the Malayan boy before their underwater journey [A:89]. Coral beaches form the invasion setting in the second chamber of Elroy Redheart’s art construction, with combat buffalos, alligators and other amphibious vehicles emerging from landing craft [MTM B7:221-222].","citations":["A:62","A:89","MTM B7:221-222"],"tags":["Islands","Seas & Oceans","Combat","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Coral Sea","definition":"One of the seas included in the catalogue of the <em>Argo</em>’s voyages, remembered by Melchisedech as seas he too has sailed [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Cord","definition":"A string fastens Phoebe Anne Ankerson’s ownership tag just inside the handle of the suitcase stolen by Finnegan and Doll [D:172]. Dangling cords also accompany the brain removed in Finnegan’s tale of an analyst who dissects a person without locating his essence [MTM B5:181-182].","citations":["D:172","MTM B5:181-182"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Coriander Seeds","definition":"Seeds Amoy is said to have stolen during his previous life as a blackbird, after millet and rice and before his work for jewel thieves, in Ignatius Ti’s expansive translation of the bird’s story [A:80].","citations":["A:80"],"tags":["Crime","Folklore","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cork (Material)","definition":"Material Trinali uses with bottles and raffia to make dolls, marionette figures, Mexicans, donkeys, clowns and prizefighters [A:228]. She dyes corks eight standard colors in a dishpan, building her stock while making the dishes taste strange [A:229]. A bottle cork also ends her wine poem as an image of the difficulty of keeping the god imprisoned in the bottle [A:231-232].","citations":["A:228","A:229","A:231-232"],"tags":["Women Characters","Visual Arts","Literature","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Cork (Terrance Cardinal Merry of)","definition":"Cardinal of Cork named Terrence Cardinal Merry in the list of John Mogul’s targets and their doubles. His listed double is Emmet Collins of Boston [MB:579], later revealed as the young woman and impersonator Emma Collins, a protégée of X [MB:598].","citations":["MB:579","MB:598"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ireland","Identity"]},{"name":"Cork, Ireland","definition":"Irish city in which Loy says there is a Duffey’s tavern [A:2], and one of the places Marie has considered looking for a husband [A:7]. X identifies Cork as the birthplace of Gregory McColum, Papadiabolous II, contrasting him with his Smyrna-born twin Gregory McIfreann, Papadiabolous I [D:285].","citations":["A:2","A:7","D:285"],"tags":["Ireland","Bars & Taverns","Devils (Characters)","Identity"]},{"name":"Cork, Terry","definition":"Seaman and journalist, one of the Forty Scribbling Seamen celebrated in Dotty’s song. He writes “The Plimsoll Line” for the <em>Bark</em> and about half the Seaman’s Paper during his stays of a few months in town. Other Terrys, including Anderson, Jamil, Renier and Bannon, take over when he leaves [MTM B7:213].","citations":["MTM B7:213"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Minor Characters","Journalism","Music"]},{"name":"Cork-cutting Machine","definition":"Machine in Portugal that is set running after Cardinal Artemis blesses it, seizes him and removes his skin in one piece; the victim dies almost immediately. The account leaves unresolved the related confusion between Artemis and his double Gilberto, and between their skins [MB:591].","citations":["MB:591"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Crime","Death & Mortality","Identity"]},{"name":"Corn Beer","definition":"Drink made by Gautier the goat-man, alongside berry wine and potato whisky [A:196-197].","citations":["A:196-197"],"tags":["Alcohol","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Corn Country Cantos","definition":"One of the nine projected volumes into which Endymeon Ellenbogen says he has classified his still uncollected and unpublished poems [A:237].","citations":["A:237"],"tags":["Literature","Literary Figures"]},{"name":"Corn-dog making","definition":"One of the minor arts said to lose class and style during the degradation of everyday life associated with the Devil’s release [MTM B6:207-208].","citations":["MTM B6:207-208"],"tags":["The Devil","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Cornelia, Sister Mary Foster","definition":"Earlier heading misplaced the given and family names and omitted Sullivan. See <em>Foster, Sister Mary Cornelia (Sullivan)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Corpse (Unaccountable)","definition":"The male body Seaworthy cannot dispose of in his dream of an Indian city. Attempts to bury it, abandon it in the gutter or push it through windows fail; it finally grapples with him and asks who will bury him [D:72-73]. Finnegan subsequently recognizes a real burial behind the dream and recalls helping Seaworthy dig the grave after Seaworthy struck the still-moving victim with a shovel [D:74-75].","citations":["D:72-73","D:74-75"],"tags":["Finnegan","Dreams","Death & Mortality","Crime"]},{"name":"Corpus Christi Day","definition":"Feast for which, according to the novel, Christ-head loaves are baked in parts of Denmark. Duffey’s Danish-bread Christ, made of wheat and rye flour, baked and varnished, is one of the extraordinary Christs in his collection [MTM B3:103].","citations":["MTM B3:103"],"tags":["Liturgy","Food & Dining","Visual Arts","Duffey"]},{"name":"Corpusculization","definition":"One of the jargon-like words spoken by the satirical holy figure of the demiurge Teilhard during the Great Day service, with McLuhan and McGonigal beside him and the Unfaithful Assembled responding [GD:497].","citations":["GD:497"],"tags":["Language & Words","Theology","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Corriander Seeds","definition":"Corriander is the spelling printed in the novel. See <em>Coriander Seeds</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Crime","Folklore","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Corridor","definition":"Passages whose doors Duffey orders opened at Hubbard Elementary School so mice can reach the puppet show; the teacher has the doors opened [MTM B4:135-136]. Metaphorical gusty corridors also describe the Gadarene Swine Song as Bagby plays it on a horn at Good Guy’s [MTM B7:220].","citations":["MTM B4:135-136","MTM B7:220"],"tags":["Duffey","Music"]},{"name":"Corruption","definition":"The opposite of integrity in the chastity passage introducing Duffey’s schoolboy circle. Duffey sometimes suffers corruption but never redefines it as a virtue [MTM B1:27]. He later warns Casey that infiltrators intend to corrupt the <em>Crock</em> [A:40]. Finnegan considers a corpse as a possible dream symbol of violation or corruption before recognizing its literal referent [D:74]. Showboat recognizes coordinated crowd corruption through the three-spot device [MTM B7:231]. In the Resurrection painting, Finnegan’s flesh undergoes corruption and transfiguration simultaneously [MB:551].","citations":["MTM B1:27","A:40","D:74","MTM B7:231","MB:551"],"tags":["Sin & Redemption","Finnegan","Duffey","Journalism","Visual Arts","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Cortina","definition":"Name associated with the language-learning manuals on Duffey’s shelves, which help Casey pursue his ambition to learn all languages [A:159]. Cortina published self-study language courses, including Spanish in Twenty Lessons.","citations":["A:159"],"tags":["Duffey","Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Coryphaena Fish (dolphin fish)","definition":"Horn-playing fish that raise their heads above the waves after Finnegan, Gilberto and Herman Hercules board the <em>Argo</em>. Their shell horns have bright brass stops and frets; the music signals that a present or future pope is aboard [MTM B13:450-451]. When the three disembark at the Ringing Rocks, the fish remain beside that shore [MTM B13:453]. Coryphaena is also the zoological name of the dolphinfish genus, given supernatural behavior here.","citations":["MTM B13:450-451","MTM B13:453"],"tags":["Popes","Miracles","Ships","Seas & Oceans","Finnegan","Music"]},{"name":"Coscuin, Dana","definition":"Voyager who takes the <em>Argo</em> around the Horn while the ship bears the name <em>Catherine</em> [MTM B13:405].","citations":["MTM B13:405"],"tags":["Ships","Crew & Sailors","Sailing"]},{"name":"Cosenza (or Consenza)","definition":"Cosenza is a city in Calabria. The claimed connection making it Papa Devil’s place of origin remains unverified. The cited scene concerns Anastasia’s burial and Marie’s dying [D:157]; X’s later account gives Smyrna and Cork as the birthplaces of the two Papadiabolous figures [D:285].","citations":["D:157","D:285"],"tags":["Italy","Devils (Characters)"]},{"name":"Cosmic Balancing","definition":"Unverified headword for the proposed idea that a good creature requires an evil counterpart. The supplied passage is Private Gregory’s account of changing identities and his long military service, and does not state this theory [A:77].","citations":["A:77"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Cosmic Entity","definition":"Unverified designation supposedly applied by X to Papadiabolous. The supplied passage instead presents Finnegan confronting Papa Devil over Don Lewis’s body and contains no such description by X [D:111].","citations":["D:111"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)"]},{"name":"Cosmic shift","definition":"Unverified phrase allegedly describing Casey’s role as a point man. The supplied passage concerns the <em>Argo</em> company’s failed recognition of X, and does not contain Casey’s alleged claim or Brannagan’s dismissal in favor of paganism [MTM B13:434].","citations":["MTM B13:434"],"tags":["Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Cosmos","definition":"The universe, figured as the web in which the suns are caught in the possible confrontation between Duffey and the Devil [MTM B10:301]. Green claims to demonstrate its dependence on him by dimming Ward Fourteen’s lights, while Gregory points out that the compressor explains the effect [A:76]. Rafelson speaks of human relations to the exocosmos in his account of Duffey [MTM B4:148]. In the Great Day ending, Morpheus claims the whole cosmos was the dream of a single-celled creature and is collapsing back into that origin [GD:500]. The Promantia’s related image is of ships and oceans of middle space [D:6].","citations":["MTM B10:301","A:76","MTM B4:148","GD:500","D:6"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Duffey","Creation","Dreams","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Cosquin, Angela","definition":"Barmaid and factotum of Finnegan’s island Paradise, employed by Joseph Chastaigne at the Imperial Bar, or whatever its name may be [D:258-259] [D:266]. Angela is vividly dressed, musical, talkative and remarkably strong: she carries two hundred pounds on her head and can launch a six-man boat alone. Her ancestry includes French, Carib and English forebears, noblemen and slaves [D:259]. Her father, a cartographer and antiquarian, becomes a maker of convincing false treasure maps for confidence men [D:259-261]. The nearby <em>St. Marc</em> and <em>San gre del Cristo</em> wrecks are real, despite her denial; she dives to their treasure, returns what she has brought up when its use attracts suspicion, and hides her own savings beside it in an inscribed copper box, adding a sovereign monthly [D:262]. Johnny Duckwalk secretly steals each added sovereign [D:263]. Angela herself draws accurate maps of Finnegan’s seven land-buried stashes, marked S X S and robado por C F, and refuses to sell them. She first calls him Count Finnegan [D:263]. When he leaves, she offers him the wrecks’ gold, embraces him, and joins his pledge to protect the poor; he promises to return but never does [D:268]. Archipelago recalls Angela and the wrecks [A:188], and Finnegan later recognizes Elena as both herself and Angela [A:278].","citations":["D:258-259","D:266","D:259","D:259-261","D:262","D:263","D:268","A:188","A:278"],"tags":["Caribbean","Finnegan","Women Characters","Bars & Taverns","Shipwrecks","Money & Trade","Family","Music","Identity"]},{"name":"Costerman's Whaler's Inn","definition":"Costerman’s Whalers’ Inn, one of the waterfront establishments recalled in Melchisedech’s catalogue of maritime places aboard the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:407].","citations":["MTM B13:407"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Duffey","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Cotillion dancing","definition":"One of the lively arts of ordinary life said to lose class and style during the degradation following the Devil’s release [MTM B6:207].","citations":["MTM B6:207"],"tags":["The Devil","Music"]},{"name":"Count Finnegan","definition":"Finnegan’s title, first given him by Angela Cosquin in her account of the seven hidden treasures [D:263] and used at their farewell [D:268]. He describes his scrimshaw pieces as recognizable to seamen as Count Finnegan’s work [MTM B5:173]. Duffey says X reports that Finnegan was secretly named a papal count, while acknowledging X’s unreliability [MTM B12:367]. A Latin scroll identifies the risen figure in Finnegan’s Resurrection painting as the Papal Count [MB:551]. He is the central figure of the Petrine spy-story contingency [MTM B10:300] and the listed double of Joseph Cardinal Hedayat [MB:579].","citations":["D:263","D:268","MTM B5:173","MTM B12:367","MB:551","MTM B10:300","MB:579"],"tags":["Finnegan","Visual Arts","Social Class","Identity","Resurrection","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Count of Monte Cristo","definition":"One of the compelling French tales Gautier the goat-man reads to his goats during winter evenings, alongside Tartarin of Tarascon and Gil Blas [A:198].","citations":["A:198"],"tags":["Literature","France"]},{"name":"Count von Vinger","definition":"Earlier heading normalized the source’s printed County to Count. See <em>County von Vinger</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Crime","Identity","Folklore"]},{"name":"Counterfeit Creature","definition":"The false replacements in Le Marin’s story of the <em>Sarcophagus</em>: dock rats are signed on in place of vanished seamen and look exactly like them. Le Marin says he too was replaced, yet continues the tale in the first person. Finnegan compares the takeover to a world in which every person has been supplanted [D:244].","citations":["D:244"],"tags":["Ships","Crew & Sailors","Identity","Crime"]},{"name":"Counterpart Funds","definition":"Payments of Current and Future Contingency kinds sought by Newton Prescott to offset the Casey Machine’s greater availability to rich users than to poor ones. He reports obtaining continuing guarantees [CM:513].","citations":["CM:513"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Social Class"]},{"name":"Countess (Girl Countess), The","definition":"Sebastian Hilton’s companion, introduced to Duffey at the Colony Club as the Girl Countess. Duffey recognizes her from his earlier reading of Sebastian’s mind. She is playing bridge as Lily Koch’s partner against Charlotte and Gloria Mullens [MTM B3:95]. See Countess Margaret, The (Margaret Hochfelsen).","citations":["MTM B3:95"],"tags":["Women Characters","Clubs & Societies","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Countess Margaret, The (Margaret Hochfelsen)","definition":"Margaret Hochfelsen, the “ever-young countess,” is Sebastian Hilton’s companion and a friend of Duffey and Letitia in their Chicago golden age [MTM B3:98]. When Sebastian introduces the Girl Countess, Duffey recognizes her from his earlier reading of Sebastian’s mind [MTM B3:95]. Letitia claims to have projected her into the Chicago scene; the narrator later calls her an Animation without settling who made her [MTM B3:96] [MTM B7:215].<br><br>She identifies Giulio as a Teras and promises to help the son who has inherited his difficult nature [MTM B3:107]. She and Sebastian marry after decades together; he is killed a month later [MTM B7:216].<br><br>After Letitia’s funeral in New Orleans, the Countess invites Duffey to marry her and live in Transylvania. She claims that nineteen long generations of her family are still alive and that enormous wolves serve and protect them. Duffey repeatedly challenges her stories; he postpones her proposal for fifty years, and she promises to ask again [MTM B8:254-256].<br><br>The later narrative links her with the Royal Pop People through its warning that she will become one of them and grow younger [MTM B12:383]. This shifting identity should be distinguished from the role of the Countess played by Becky Stein in <em>Seven Roads</em> [MTM B12:352].","citations":["MTM B3:98","MTM B3:95","MTM B3:96","MTM B7:215","MTM B3:107","MTM B7:216","MTM B8:254-256","MTM B12:383","MTM B12:352"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Duffey","Eastern Europe","Metaphysics","Identity","Family"]},{"name":"County von Vinger","definition":"Alias, printed “County von Vinger,” of Ali ben Taife, also called Lord Peter Petrof and, in Rio, Sinhor Dedos or Mr. Fingers. In Ignatius Ti’s account, this thief-master kills Amoy when the bird refuses to surrender a swallowed blue diamond [A:81].","citations":["A:81"],"tags":["Crime","Identity","Folklore"]},{"name":"Courage","definition":"Bushmaster’s backers praise his bulldog courage during the losing beer contest, prompting Finnegan’s joke about his forebears [A:23]. X calls pursuit of the supposedly defeated Conspiracy the most courageous act, yet admits he cannot face his ironic wife after his long absence [A:139-141]. Duffey retains strong personal courage despite his other failings [MTM B4:110]. Le Marin, emboldened when he encounters Castleman in a bar, then leaves the dangerous man alone rather than let courage carry him too far [D:245-246].","citations":["A:23","A:139-141","MTM B4:110","D:245-246"],"tags":["Duffey","Crime","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Courier fonts","definition":"Unverified claim that Casey preferred Courier type for the <em>Crock</em>. The supplied page concerns Duffey’s encounter with a distressed woman and the magazine’s stockholders, with no typeface preference stated [MTM B4:144].","citations":["MTM B4:144"],"tags":["Journalism"]},{"name":"Courtois, Marie (aka the Golden Ogress)","definition":"Seaworthy’s seagoing secretary on the <em>Brunhilde</em>. Finnegan first remembers her as the Golden Ogress who collected his information for the ship’s register; her beauty and aura of power make the transaction feel like a mortgage on his soul [D:32]. Golden-skinned, red-haired, exceptionally strong and about a foot taller than the small Anastasia, she revels in her physical power on the beach and in the surf [D:33] [D:37]. Anastasia denounces her as evil, calls her brainless and skylla in their quarrelling, and accuses her of madness; Marie answers with political diagnoses of Anastasia. Despite this, the cabin-mates are described as good friends whose feud sharpens their wits [D:33] [D:69-70]. In a possibly waking ocean dream Marie identifies herself with Demeter and Persephone and claims earthly reincarnation is the only immortality; Finnegan rejects her claim [D:91-92]. She kills Don Lewis by throttling him and breaking his neck, then describes the act with ecstatic delight. Finnegan and Joe Cross plan retaliation, but Papa Devil intervenes [D:110-113]. Manuel says she insisted on Anastasia’s killing [D:148]. Finnegan later reports that Marie shot Anastasia and was herself shot by Joe, who was then shot in turn. He perceives her dying aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em>, enjoying it and expecting a favored place beyond death [D:157].","citations":["D:32","D:33","D:37","D:69-70","D:91-92","D:110-113","D:148","D:157"],"tags":["Women Characters","Giants & Titans","Devils (Characters)","Ships","Crime","Immortality","Greek Mythology","Dreams","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Courtship (Romantic)","definition":"One of the popular arts said to lose class and style in the degradation of everyday life following the Devil’s release [MTM B6:207].","citations":["MTM B6:207"],"tags":["The Devil"]},{"name":"Covenant","definition":"A binding pact, appearing in opposed forms. In the sixth contingency the Adversary presses Duffey to accept a fatally defective covenant; the dramatic forecast warns that acceptance would unmake everyone [MTM B10:296] [MTM B10:301]. Finnegan later describes the saving Covenant as an intrusion that has changed time, space and the form of everything, and that the killers seek to extinguish [MB:587]. An ancestral dolphin knows this Pact and accepts that it was made with humans rather than dolphins, although hostile humans will try to nullify it [MB:594].","citations":["MTM B10:296","MTM B10:301","MB:587","MB:594"],"tags":["The Devil","Duffey","Metaphysics","Ekklesia"]},{"name":"Covenanter","definition":"Unverified epithet allegedly applied to Henry by Papa Devil. The cited page concerns Aunt Gertrude’s cooking and X’s coded telephone call to Finnegan, and does not contain that exchange [D:208].","citations":["D:208"],"tags":["The Devil"]},{"name":"Covenanters","definition":"Unverified designation supposedly applied to sour members of the <em>Brunhilde</em> crew. The supplied passage is Finnegan and Seaworthy’s conversation on the sidewalk before their voyage, and contains no such designation [D:11].","citations":["D:11"],"tags":["Ships","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Cow (Sacred)","definition":"Image applied to Mary Louise Byrne: her strength and bovine quality are described as royal and sacred, a phase through which the narrative says all chthonic goddesses pass [MTM B2:55].","citations":["MTM B2:55"],"tags":["Women Characters","Duffey","Other Mythologies"]},{"name":"Cow County Roundup","definition":"Unverified title of a supposed rodeo publication to which a friend of Gregory contributes. The supplied passage is Gregory’s account of his non-aging and military service, and contains no such publication or contributor [A:77].","citations":["A:77"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"Coward (self-described)","definition":"Don Lewis’s evasive self-characterization in his story of the three vanished soldiers: one was a coward, and he will not identify which was himself [D:44]. His later slip identifies him with the reluctant PFC Lorrigan, who hung back from the killing [D:45]. The narrator says he has twice proclaimed his cowardice, while stressing the dangerous resistance and covert communications he has organized aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em> [D:105].","citations":["D:44","D:45","D:105"],"tags":["Identity","Crime","Ships"]},{"name":"Cowboys","definition":"Tommy Cow-Town Borger and Jake-Leg Lewis, the salt-water cowboys who ride cattle boats rather than horses. As live cattle shipments become rare, they spend long intervals between voyages, still wearing boots, spurs, chaps and gauntlets [D:222].","citations":["D:222"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Ships","Occupations"]},{"name":"Cowboys (Mexican)","definition":"Unverified entry. The earlier glossary gives Vaqueros and cites Archipelago, page 176. That page concerns Finnegan and Dotty, and no supporting Mexican-cowboy passage has been located in the supplied texts.","citations":[],"tags":["Occupations"]},{"name":"Cowles Buggy Whip, The","definition":"One of the whips recalled by the turbaned Royal Pop historian while giving a Decatur Street flagstone a voice for its memories of horse traffic [MTM B11:329].","citations":["MTM B11:329"],"tags":["New Orleans","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Cows","definition":"Animals in the phrase heard during Duffey’s dream, “the years that the cows have eaten.” Beth Keegan associates it with seven sheaves of grain and expects a seven-year absence [MTM B2:68-69]. The imagery suggests a blend of Pharaoh’s cows and grain in Genesis 41:26-27 with the consumed years of Joel 2:25. A “honey old cow” sometimes supplies Duffey’s soup kitchen [A:134]; cowbells mingle with sheep bells during Finnegan and Anastasia’s Naxos vigil [D:131].","citations":["MTM B2:68-69","A:134","D:131"],"tags":["Scripture","Duffey","Food & Dining","Dreams"]},{"name":"Crab cooties","definition":"Parasites among the creatures attacking Duffey in the dung heap after he turns the forbidden handle and falls from the Presence’s green meadows. He finally recognizes that the Presence remains with him even there [MTM B12:392-393].","citations":["MTM B12:392-393"],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Crabbing","definition":"One of the lively arts of everyday life said to lose class and style in the massive degradation following the Devil’s release [MTM B6:207].","citations":["MTM B6:207"],"tags":["The Devil","Occupations"]},{"name":"Crack","definition":"A sound, opening or fracture used in several scenes. Papa Devil’s threat over Don Lewis’s grave bursts like thunder [D:110]; Le Marin asks Finnegan to crack another bottle [D:243]. Finnegan jokingly says Theresa’s baptism cracked the font, and she counters that any of several witches might have done it [A:108]. Bagby likens Duffey to a sidewalk crack treated as a fetish mark by fates and principalities [MTM B10:302]. The nearly closed door between Finnegan and Dotty figures their dwindling relationship [A:106-107]. Casey imagines Henry aging into a priest whose thunder has cracks, a prospect Finnegan rejects [A:155]. A crack in Eva’s ice pillar frees her jaw to speak [MTM B13:399].","citations":["D:110","D:243","A:108","MTM B10:302","A:106-107","A:155","MTM B13:399"],"tags":["Language & Words","Finnegan","Duffey","Alcohol","Identity"]},{"name":"Crackerjacks","definition":"Boxed snack from which Aunt Mary Ellen Hart says Duffey obtained his first talisman. She cannot explain its subsequent growth or the origin of the similar talismans he makes [MTM B1:18].","citations":["MTM B1:18"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey"]},{"name":"Crafts","definition":"Duffey excels at crafts, especially carpentry and machining, before quickly learning art techniques and bronze casting [MTM B2:53]. Gautier the goat-man is likewise called a master of all crafts, with tools, a loom and a wheel, making nets, seines, lines and traps [A:196].","citations":["MTM B2:53","A:196"],"tags":["Duffey","Occupations","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Crags","definition":"The limestone heights of central San Simeon where the hurricane bird nests before flying over the Ocean of Mexico. Accounts differ on whether there is one recurrent bird or several newly hatched each year [MB:557-558].","citations":["MB:557-558"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Other Mythologies"]},{"name":"Crane, Leander","definition":"One of the theatrical people in Duffey’s Chicago circle [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Theater"]},{"name":"Crankiness","definition":"One possible explanation for the inner kabouters or manifest goblins. Absalom Stein says he and Casey exchanged one each, and argues that the effects support their being distinct immaterial persons rather than merely traits [PG:537-538].","citations":["PG:537-538"],"tags":["Identity","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Crash year","definition":"The future year Duffey visits when Mary Virginia and the others perceive him as a ghost already dead for months. He withdraws from that year and puts it back into the future, returning to the task of writing his <em>Bark</em> review [MTM B12:360-361].","citations":["MTM B12:360-361"],"tags":["Duffey","Time & Eternity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Creation (First)","definition":"The measure of the old-fashioned rebuilding urged in Henri Salvatore’s letter to Duffey: construction should begin from the original foundation and be as old as the first Creation, while its wine and vessels remain new [MTM B5:179-180].","citations":["MTM B5:179-180"],"tags":["Creation","Duffey"]},{"name":"Creature (Single-celled)","definition":"The origin and last remnant of the cosmos in Morpheus’s account of a diminishing dream. He says one cell imagined a second self and dreamed the worlds; as the dream contracts, he and Duffey wrestle over which will be the surviving cell [GD:500].","citations":["GD:500"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Dreams","Creation","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Credit","definition":"Lazarus Reilly extends beer credit and other help to guests at the Old Wooden Ship [D:216]. Duffey admits that his credit, like his lodging facilities, is limited when welcoming the Royal Pop People [MTM B11:312]. Trinali receives five dollars of rent credit for finding Hans’s apartment [A:227]. In the sense of belief, Brannagan credits only diamonds he has personally held [D:207], while the Devil loses credit through premature predictions of complete victory [MTM B8:245-246].","citations":["D:216","MTM B11:312","A:227","D:207","MTM B8:245-246"],"tags":["Money & Trade","The Devil","Duffey","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Creed","definition":"A body of belief. Casey reports Duffey’s charge that the new liberal permits only a creed secretly devoted to ancient tyranny [A:42]. The hostile review of Royce Rollins’s The Return of the Neanderthal calls that fictional book an insane creed [D:240].","citations":["A:42","D:240"],"tags":["Literature","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Creek","definition":"Finnegan and Howland wade westward- or southwestward-flowing creeks, then camp in a creek bend to cook rabbits and fish [A:178]. Henry and Casey climb to a churning creek and swim in its waterfall-cut pool [A:42]. Finnegan fishes intermingling streams and creeks in Gautier’s New Jersey swamp camp [A:195-196]. In New Guinea, closely spaced foamy creeks and steep bluffs illustrate Casey’s scenic-density idea [A:36].","citations":["A:178","A:42","A:195-196","A:36"],"tags":["Finnegan","Fishing"]},{"name":"Creepers","definition":"Trailing growth from aromatic bushes above the clean sand in which Finnegan digs Don Lewis’s grave [D:110]. Earlier, rocks and creepers are personified as keeping out of Papa Devil’s way when he descends from the ruined wall where Finnegan and Anastasia watch the port [D:70].","citations":["D:110","D:70"],"tags":["Finnegan","Devils (Characters)"]},{"name":"Creighton","definition":"One possible school in the deliberately variable account of Duffey’s boarding-school years. The narrator suggests Creighton in Omaha, recalls a high school as well as a college there, and says his single remembered experience in fact took place at two or more schools [MTM B1:24].","citations":["MTM B1:24"],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Cremated","definition":"Reduced to ashes, as Duffey describes his own remains when X returns the cigar urn to him [MTM B6:203]. The later account distinguishes the slant-faced killers who strip the flesh from his bones from the slack-faced men who burn that flesh and put its perpetually hot ashes in a canister formerly belonging to the King of Spain [MTM B13:442].","citations":["MTM B6:203","MTM B13:442"],"tags":["Duffey","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Crescent","definition":"One of the seven Iowa places competing as Duffey’s birthplace in one of his possible childhoods [MTM B1:7].","citations":["MTM B1:7"],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Cretan Bull","definition":"Bull in the Cretan Bull Drama staged at the Decatur Street Opera House. Young performers leap over its horns, while human victims, including Zabotski, are given to the bulls to kill [MTM B11:349].","citations":["MTM B11:349"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Theater","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Crete","definition":"One of the places Finnegan guesses might send the boat Anastasia awaits during the Naxos vigil; she does not confirm the guess [D:131]. Anastasia’s description evokes statuettes from Cretan cenotaphs [D:32], and her mermaid history says early Cretan mermaids were captives from Naxos, not a separate variety [D:35]. Casey’s erotic dream-world includes Cretan girl bullfighters [A:157]. Beth Keegan is compared with living ivory statuary, Etruscan and Cretan in its ancient freshness [MTM B2:60].","citations":["D:131","D:32","D:35","A:157","MTM B2:60"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Islands","Greece","Women Characters","Dreams","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Crime","definition":"The Professor tells Hans that anyone who tolerates dullness commits a crime, in his sermon on thought and the life of the mind [A:212]. Seaworthy similarly exaggerates his displeasure at being served Texas beer into a crime deserving punishment and penitence; the beer is the only kind available cold [D:12].","citations":["A:212","D:12"],"tags":["Crime","Alcohol","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Crimea","definition":"The Black Sea peninsula where the narrative places the Devil’s deep, iron-doored prison. He is released on the last Saturday of May 1946, though the narrator also raises the possibility of a recurring release in 1846 [MTM B5:180] [MTM B5:184]. Duffey hears the release treated comically on the radio [MTM B6:190]. X’s claimed eyewitness account names the Tauric and Euxine Chersonese and alleges that the Yalta Conference principals consulted the imprisoned Devil [MTM B6:204].","citations":["MTM B5:180","MTM B5:184","MTM B6:190","MTM B6:204"],"tags":["The Devil","Seas & Oceans","Duffey","Fictional Places","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Crimean dungeon","definition":"The Devil’s thousand-year prison, dated 946-1946 in Absalom Stein’s Notes on the Finnegan Cycle [MTM B6:185]. The narrative places the release in May 1946 but also asks whether it recurs from a release a century earlier [MTM B5:184].","citations":["MTM B6:185","MTM B5:184"],"tags":["The Devil","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Crimson Blood","definition":"One of the colors in the richly mingled blood spilled when the thunder colt kills the Deutero-Finnegan effigy at the Decatur Street Opera House [MTM B11:351].","citations":["MTM B11:351"],"tags":["New Orleans","Finnegan","Theater"]},{"name":"Crissie Verse","definition":"Crissie Cristofero’s prophetic couplets across the seven scenes of the poster for Cleo Mahoney’s <em>Seven Roads</em>. The girls discuss each contingency through its verse, although Crissie does not necessarily understand her own prophecy [MTM B10:293-296]. Becky Stein later calls her bitter couplet about Margaret Stone’s altered funeral liturgy a Crissie verse [MTM B12:354].","citations":["MTM B10:293-296","MTM B12:354"],"tags":["Literature","Theater","Death & Mortality","Prophecy & Visions","Liturgy"]},{"name":"Cristobal Colon","definition":"Christopher Columbus, named Cristobal Colon by Ignacio, who calls St. Kitts or St. Christopher one of his landfalls [A:272]. In The Devil is Dead, discovery by Columbus is one of the narrator’s playful requirements for the island Paradise reached at Basse-Terre [D:235] [D:237].","citations":["A:272","D:235","D:237"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Cristofero, Crissie","definition":"Ursuline Academy student, painter, and author of the prophetic couplets on the seven-scene poster for Cleo Mahoney’s <em>Seven Roads</em>. When rain nearly destroys her original water-soluble painting, she struggles to reconstruct it in oils [MTM B10:293]. Rebeka Stein calls her a sibyl or pythoness, observing that Crissie does not understand her own prophetic verses [MTM B10:295]. She later asks Duffey to perform magic during the play’s intermission [MTM B12:354].","citations":["MTM B10:293","MTM B10:295","MTM B12:354"],"tags":["Women Characters","Visual Arts","Theater","Prophecy & Visions","Duffey"]},{"name":"Cro Magnon man","definition":"Prehistoric humanity invoked in Casey’s school notebook, which speculates about Cro-Magnon skin color using a racial slur [A:162-163]. The Devil is Dead’s chapter-opening speculation describes Cro-Magnon man emerging from violent absorption of a kindred people, outwardly dominant but carrying a deep recessive inheritance [D:283].","citations":["A:162-163","D:283"],"tags":["History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Crock (2) (Zabotski's Pot)","definition":"Zabotski’s thousand-gallon, glass-lined vessel, formerly used for distillation experiments. Duffey turns it into the continually boiling soup pot for the poor, with a wall temporarily removed to bring it indoors and a priest blessing it [A:132-133]. <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> makes the Great Pot part of the project of rebuilding the world [MTM B6:195].","citations":["A:132-133","MTM B6:195"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Crock (3) (old butter-crock)","definition":"Nostalgic object in Endymeon Ellenbogen’s catalogue of kinds of popular songs he writes, alongside remembered mothers and bee trees [A:237-238].","citations":["A:237-238"],"tags":["Music","Family","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Crock (4) (Casey the)","definition":"Casey the Crock is Kasmir Gorshok, Casey Szymansky’s medieval manifestation as a ninth-century scholar and necromancer; his Argonaut identity is Peleus [A:39] [PG:534-535]. <em>Archipelago</em>’s Chapter Six is titled “Casey the Crock, or the Losing of Peleus” [A:151].","citations":["A:39","PG:534-535","A:151"],"tags":["Identity","Magic & Sorcery","Argonauts (Characters)"]},{"name":"Crock (5) (pottery)","definition":"The pot image behind the <em>Crock</em> magazine’s name: its cover carries d’Allesandro’s elaborate engraving of an old chamber pot, below a black Gothic title [A:40-41]. The connection is a visual pun; the passage does not state a separate etymology for the magazine’s name.","citations":["A:40-41"],"tags":["Journalism","Visual Arts","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Crock, The","definition":"Casey Szymansky’s magazine, published in successive college versions and later on the small press in Duffey’s Chicago bookstore [MTM B4:119]. Duffey runs it after Casey joins the army in 1942, attracting talented contributors and resisting the peculiar people’s efforts to take it over [MTM B4:138]. In Archipelago, his letter describes blackmail, assault and a warning about Casey’s stock; Casey identifies Stein as the threatened infiltrator [A:39-40]. In <em>More Than Melchisedech</em>, a group gains control through a new proxy from Casey. About a year later Casey returns, confirms the changed orientation, resumes the editorship and permanently excludes Duffey [MTM B4:144-145]. Casey associates his change with a soul exchange, which Stein later describes as an exchange of their older souls [MTM B4:145] [MTM B5:169]. Duffey’s admirers remember the earlier <em>Crock</em> as an intelligent, humorous cult sheet [MTM B5:158].\n\nThe Archipelago issue is a twenty-page tabloid with d’Allesandro’s chamber-pot engraving, a black Gothic title and the subscript Formerly the C of S [A:40-41]. Its contents include Schrade’s sonnet, Ethyl Ellenberger’s ode, Demetrio Glauch’s ballad, and articles by Thos. J. Chronicker, S.J., Christopher Tompkinson and Tom (Big Bear) Rogers. Mary Frances Rattigan writes “Books Abroad” as Polly Polyglot. Duffey supplies most of the issue; “Moles at the Grass Roots,” Fables in Fabianism, and The Consumer, the Cooperative, and the Coefficient of Cost Constancy are described as being in his style. Regular sections include “Urbe et Orbe,” “Judgement Day” reviews, recording notes favoring Chicago jazz, and a pro-and-con letters forum. Four Groben woodcuts and On the John musings with a shadowed Thinker drawing complete the description [A:41].\n\nAfter the <em>Bark</em> appears, Casey uses the <em>Crock</em> to attack its editors and aims; the publications address the same influential readership [A:128-130]. Later Show Boat reports that Casey’s creditors have taken his press and building but cannot use the <em>Crock</em>’s name, and that he is again at odds with the group [A:262-263].","citations":["MTM B4:119","MTM B4:138","A:39-40","MTM B4:144-145","MTM B4:145","MTM B5:169","MTM B5:158","A:40-41","A:41","A:128-130","A:262-263"],"tags":["Duffey","Journalism","Literature","Visual Arts","Music","Money & Trade","Identity"]},{"name":"Crocked","definition":"Drunk: Theresa warns Finnegan against getting crocked before supper [A:108], while Elena later deplores his habitual drunkenness [A:274]. Trinali and Jimmy are crocked within an hour of drinking with Hans [A:226]. Duffey retains his small-scale magic whether crocked or uncrocked [MTM B5:170]. Finnegan also calls himself the Irish crock during his St. Patrick’s celebrations [D:189].","citations":["A:108","A:274","A:226","MTM B5:170","D:189"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Finnegan","Duffey","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Cromwell, Oliver (1599--1658)","definition":"Oliver Cromwell (1599-1658), Parliamentary commander in the British Civil Wars and Lord Protector of England, Scotland and Ireland from 1653 until his death. His military victories and Puritan reforms were followed by a constitutionally powerful executive and broader toleration of many Protestant faiths; his Irish campaign included the massacres at Drogheda and Wexford. The glossary’s literary connection remains unverified: the supplied pages concern Papa Piccone’s Oscar Oliver Omygosh story and the <em>Argo</em>’s visit to Oliver Greenflag and his companions [MTM B5:167-168] [MTM B13:417-418].","citations":["MTM B5:167-168","MTM B13:417-418"],"tags":["Historical Figures","History & Memory","Ireland"]},{"name":"Cronies","definition":"Seaworthy’s close associates William Gerecke and Peter Wirt share his and Papa Devil’s mystery. They enter the bar with those two and Captain Gonof while Finnegan and Anastasia are dismissed [D:30]. Seaworthy’s cronies visit offices in foreign ports [D:103] and wait with the old netman aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em> during the Naxos ambush, experienced fighters despite their inconspicuousness [D:136]. X later asks what Finnegan learned from Seaworthy and his cronies [D:188]. Elsewhere, Patrick Stranahan’s visitors include K.C.s and cronies [A:104], and Finnegan’s port-town friends report his heavy drinking before the <em>Brunhilde</em> sails [D:27].","citations":["D:30","D:103","D:136","D:188","A:104","D:27"],"tags":["Ships","Crew & Sailors","Finnegan","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Crook","definition":"Doll calls Finnegan a crook after receiving a fifty-dollar note during their escape in a borrowed-by-force car [D:168]. The related adjective describes the three kings identified with the Slant-Faced Men [MTM B7:227], the Professor’s path of false knowledge [A:212], and the Devil’s preference for crooked lines and a tongue able to enter hidden recesses [MTM B8:242].","citations":["D:168","MTM B7:227","A:212","MTM B8:242"],"tags":["The Devil","Finnegan","Crime","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Crooked Tongue","definition":"The Devil’s own image for deceit’s reach: in the speech Duffey overhears within his mind, the Devil recommends a crooked tongue because it can penetrate recesses inaccessible to a straight one [MTM B8:242].","citations":["MTM B8:242"],"tags":["The Devil","Duffey","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Crooked Way","definition":"The Professor associates the crooked way with the one forbidden tree, the pseudodendron of false knowledge [A:212]. In X’s account of the Devil’s release, the Devil proposes to preach a scenic, crooked way alongside defamation and destruction [MTM B6:206].","citations":["A:212","MTM B6:206"],"tags":["The Devil","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Crooks (Family)","definition":"One of the families listed by an older relative as Duffey’s extensive kindred. Duffey disputes the account and the authenticity of those supposed relatives [MTM B1:7].","citations":["MTM B1:7"],"tags":["Duffey","Family"]},{"name":"Cross (Celtic)","definition":"The Celtic Cross of Christ on one side of Biloxi Brannagan’s red-gold identity coin, with a coracle on the reverse and the name Naomh Brandon. He produces it during the <em>Argo</em> company’s attempt to recognize a visitor [MTM B13:425].","citations":["MTM B13:425"],"tags":["Ships","Ireland","Money & Trade","Identity"]},{"name":"Cross (Greek)","definition":"One of the devices on the <em>Navicula Petri</em> in Henry’s dream, alongside a lamb, fish and six-pointed star [A:44].","citations":["A:44"],"tags":["Ships","Dreams","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Cross (Holy)","definition":"Relic said in the <em>Argo</em>’s quest history to have been recovered by the <em>Argo</em> Masters exactly a century after Saladin dragged it away at his horse’s tail [MTM B13:421].","citations":["MTM B13:421"],"tags":["Ships","Argonautica","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Cross (Iron)","definition":"One of the decorations Casey wears in his youthful appearance as the Leader, before Letitia intercepts him on the way to mount the white horse [MTM B4:117].","citations":["MTM B4:117"],"tags":["Identity"]},{"name":"Cross (Red)","definition":"Organization invoked by a witness trying to explain the obscure international ARGO group with which Duffey supposedly worked from a ship during his seven missing years [MTM B2:72].","citations":["MTM B2:72"],"tags":["Ships","Clubs & Societies","Duffey"]},{"name":"Cross (St. Andrew's)","definition":"One of the types of cross turned into a spinning, fire-lettered torture device in the grotesque resurgence of crucifixion described amid the world’s murder entertainments [MB:582].","citations":["MB:582"],"tags":["Crime","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Cross (St. Peter's)","definition":"One of the types of cross turned into a spinning, fire-lettered torture device in the grotesque resurgence of crucifixion described amid the world’s murder entertainments [MB:582].","citations":["MB:582"],"tags":["Crime","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Cross Purposes","definition":"The conflict of intentions Finnegan wants to avoid with Anastasia, warning that he may have to play his own hand and is frightened by his vision of her dead [D:70].","citations":["D:70"],"tags":["Finnegan","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Cross, Joe","definition":"<em>Brunhilde</em> crewman, cabin-mate of Finnegan and Don Lewis, and initially one of the narrator’s good people: compromised but a true prince. He has burr-short red hair and later a red beard around his youthful face [D:30-31] [D:100]. At the beach he breaks water and whistles at Finnegan and Anastasia like a comic fish [D:38]. Experience in a Baltimore raw bar equips him to help prepare the seafood supper [D:40]. During the Mexican landing he accompanies Manuel, Papa Devil, Finnegan and the women but remains with the oar boat [D:57].\n\nDon’s covert arrangements bring Joe aboard as part of a loose resistance [D:105], yet Joe’s closeness to Marie makes Don doubt him [D:106]. In Tangier on the day Don is killed, the usually friendly Joe is armed and hostile to Finnegan [D:101-102]. After discovering Marie’s guilt, Finnegan trusts Joe again; they set out to kill her, but Papa Devil disarms Joe and stops them [D:113].\n\nJoe is later found dead on the Naxos landing with Papa Devil and five olive-shirted raiders [D:147]. Manuel says Joe tried to play both sides, opposed Anastasia’s killing, and was murdered aboard ship before his body was dragged ashore [D:148]. Finnegan later reports the shooting sequence: Marie killed Anastasia, Joe shot Marie, and Joe was himself shot [D:157].","citations":["D:30-31","D:100","D:38","D:40","D:57","D:105","D:106","D:101-102","D:113","D:147","D:148","D:157"],"tags":["Ships","Crew & Sailors","Finnegan","Food & Dining","Crime","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Cross, St. John of","definition":"See <em>St. John of the Cross</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Saints","Religious Orders","Theology","Mysticism","Spain","Finnegan","Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Cross-index","definition":"X’s secret reference system for those present at the Devil’s release, including extra names because many attendees use aliases. He offers to bring the list if the <em>Bark</em> will publish it, but Dotty refuses [MTM B6:206].","citations":["MTM B6:206"],"tags":["The Devil","Journalism","Identity"]},{"name":"Crossroads","definition":"Duffey’s early independence after completing high school at fifteen is called a crossroads of life [MTM B2:38]. At the St. Louis gathering, Patrick Stranahan imagines a crossroads sign pointing toward nowhere, the easy house, the crossbar hotel and the charnel house, with old, fresh and placental blood running down it: an ominous crux in the discussion of the Devil’s release [MTM B5:164].","citations":["MTM B2:38","MTM B5:164"],"tags":["Duffey","Prophecy & Visions","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Crotalus, Niccolo (aka Nick the Rattle)","definition":"Earlier heading substituted the snake genus Crotalus for the character’s name. See <em>Niccolo Crotolus (Nick the Rattle, The Sidewinder)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Finnegan","Crime","Weapons","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Crow","definition":"Bird image used for Salvation Sally’s clattering voice [A:8-9] and in Dotty’s description of Sally as a crow [A:147]. The <em>Brunhilde</em> is called an old crow of a ship [D:21], and Morris Poor insults Countess Margaret as a skinny crow during the mule episode [MTM B4:131]. Actual crows gather at the driftwood graveyard [A:233] and in clouds around Hans’s childhood nursery grounds [A:208].","citations":["A:8-9","A:147","D:21","MTM B4:131","A:233","A:208"],"tags":["Women Characters","Ships","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Crowd, The","definition":"Name for the Ekklesia or Assembly whose leaders declare its dismantling at the Last Conclave [MB:561]. In ordinary uses, Finnegan arranges acquaintances as the apparent audience for his art show [A:191], calls Casey the soft-headed member of their circle [A:185], and joins the onlookers ordered away from the Naxos dead [D:147]. Don Lewis wonders whether a crowd will protect him or make killing him easier [D:106]. A Pattenite polemicist attacks the infidelity crowd’s use of antiquity evidence [MTM B9:283], while Tony Apostolo calls Duffey’s reporter-filled party a pressy crowd [MTM B4:130].","citations":["MB:561","A:191","A:185","D:147","D:106","MTM B9:283","MTM B4:130"],"tags":["Ekklesia","Finnegan","Journalism","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Crown","definition":"The King’s Crown of Salem is one face of Duffey’s gold identity coin [MTM B13:425]. X brings the Triple Crown, or a double of it, to the Mooring-Rock conclave [MB:596]; a dead man wearing that crown also appears in Crissie’s third-contingency verse [MTM B10:295]. Casey’s Leader regalia includes the Crown of St. Stephen among its decorations [MTM B4:117]. In Finnegan’s ode to Loy Larkin, her pinkish hair is a tipsy crown [A:32].","citations":["MTM B13:425","MB:596","MTM B10:295","MTM B4:117","A:32"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Money & Trade","Social Class","Popes"]},{"name":"Crown of St. Stephen","definition":"One of the decorations in Casey’s assorted regalia when he plans to appear as the Leader on the white horse. Letitia intercepts him before his planned entrance [MTM B4:117]. See also <em>King Stephen of Hungary</em>.","citations":["MTM B4:117"],"tags":["Identity","Social Class"]},{"name":"Cruikshank, Rosemary Gate","definition":"A Gate among the Princes of the Crowd at the Last Conclave. Immediately after Stockton Stone Crocker is shot for voting for Cardinal Hedayat, Rosemary votes for the effigy or Straw-Man as the armed Hackenschmidt commands [MB:567].","citations":["MB:567"],"tags":["Ekklesia","Crime","Social Class"]},{"name":"Cruise","definition":"Dotty calls her funded journey to visit Finnegan in Havana a cruise [A:277]. The <em>Brunhilde</em>’s earlier voyage is recalled through Finnegan’s disputed deaths at several of its stopping points in Stein’s opening account in How Many Miles to Babylon [MB:548].","citations":["A:277","MB:548"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Finnegan","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Crusader Godfrey of the Gate","definition":"One of the notable passengers named in the <em>Argo</em>’s history, alongside St. Paul and Mark Twain [MTM B13:404].","citations":["MTM B13:404"],"tags":["Ships","Argonautica"]},{"name":"Crushed Louse","definition":"The quipping publisher’s expression for the nitty-gritty in <em>The Answer</em>, specifically the announced time and place of the Leader’s coming appearance in Henry Horner Park [MTM B4:116].","citations":["MTM B4:116"],"tags":["Journalism","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Crust (Earth's)","definition":"Sulem says that the crust of the world is thin even for people who possess a tujuan, and fears that Finnegan, who lacks one, may step through it and fall forever [A:60].","citations":["A:60"],"tags":["Finnegan","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Crutches","definition":"X’s metaphor for the support of the Church in his letter to Absalom Stein. The women have that support; X says he has it only a little and Stein not at all, so they must “hobble without that crutch” [A:265-266].","citations":["A:265-266"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Crypt (Burial)","definition":"In <em>The Resurrection of Count Finnegan</em>, the burial crypt appears as an ocean cave beneath a rock shelf [MB:550]. In the third contingency outlined in Book X, a dead man elected to high office is ordered out of his crypt by a powerful voice [MTM B10:295].","citations":["MB:550","MTM B10:295"],"tags":["Finnegan","The Dead","Visual Arts","Resurrection","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Crystal","definition":"The Cadillac Bar is furnished with chrome and crystal [D:58]. In Trinali’s ode, a crystal wineglass resembles the transparent sphere of Fifth Heaven [A:231]. A Royal Pop speaker describes crystals as records of events in different temporal directions, and crystal balls as spheres tuned to the direction usually called the future [MTM B11:330].","citations":["D:58","A:231","MTM B11:330"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Heaven & Hell","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Crystal Palace","definition":"One of the places where Finnegan washes dishes while waiting to sail from the unnamed Texas seaport [D:10] [D:11] [D:19].","citations":["D:10","D:11","D:19"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Finnegan","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Cuba","definition":"Setting of Finnegan's last encounter with Dotty and Niccolo in <em>Archipelago</em>. At the opening of the final chapter he mistakes Havana for a transformed New Orleans; the telephone booth contains a Havana directory [A:269]. Dotty travels there after receiving a lawyer's letter, and she and Niccolo exchange shots on the beach, wounding Finnegan as well as one another [A:277] [A:281-282].<br><br>In <em>More Than Melchisedech</em>, Dotty's departure to meet Finnegan is recalled with Cuba given as a possible destination [MTM B8:237]. The Petrine Spy Story contingency locates their deaths on the Marianao Coast and treats those deaths as part of a larger masquerade [MTM B10:300]. Duffey later travels to Havana with Carmelo Mendoza, whom he discovers to be X, to investigate Finnegan's disappearance; Horace Pie joins them [MTM B12:367] [MTM B12:371]. They find the sea cave shown in <em>The Resurrection of Count Finnegan</em>. Duffey enters its previous day and meets Finnegan there [MTM B12:374].","citations":["A:269","A:277","A:281-282","MTM B8:237","MTM B10:300","MTM B12:367","MTM B12:371","MTM B12:374"],"tags":["Caribbean","Finnegan","Duffey","Death & Mortality","Resurrection"]},{"name":"Cuban Boat","definition":"The boat Duffey and Carmelo Mendoza take from New Orleans to Cuba to investigate Finnegan’s reported death. They travel as working seamen under the names Mike Duffey and Karl Metz [MTM B12:367] [MTM B12:369].","citations":["MTM B12:367","MTM B12:369"],"tags":["Ships","New Orleans","Caribbean","Finnegan","Duffey","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Cuban Man (Portly)","definition":"The man whom boys present to Duffey, X, and Horace Pie as the “Sleeping Man” in a Cuban coastal cave. He lies naked on a rock shelf beside his clothes, but is an impostor rather than Finnegan [MTM B12:372] [MTM B12:373].","citations":["MTM B12:372","MTM B12:373"],"tags":["Caribbean","Minor Characters","Finnegan","Duffey"]},{"name":"Cubist","definition":"Jimmy defines an Orthoclast as a Cubist who paints things along their supposed natural cleavages to show matter inside out [A:230]. On the <em>Argo</em>, shattered mirrors divide each viewer into many reflections, and the world itself subsequently appears cubistic [MTM B13:461].","citations":["A:230","MTM B13:461"],"tags":["Ships","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Cubits","definition":"Measures invoked when the narrator considers the uncertain dimensions of the <em>Argo</em>. He gives their range as nine to thirty-nine inches and mentions differing dimensions in the Vulgate, Septuagint, and Hebrew accounts [MTM B13:403].","citations":["MTM B13:403"],"tags":["Ships","Scripture"]},{"name":"Cuckoos","definition":"Ignatius Ti’s term for the inmates of Ward Fourteen while telling them about midnight, ghosts, and the dead [A:75] [A:85] [A:86].","citations":["A:75","A:85","A:86"],"tags":["Language & Words","Ghosts & Spirits"]},{"name":"Cucumbers","definition":"Henri Salvatore tells Duffey to go to New Orleans and grow superior cucumbers in an asphalt garden for the Greater Glory [MTM B5:184]. Finnegan boasts that he can ask in Russian for cucumbers instead of an unwanted dish [D:190].","citations":["MTM B5:184","D:190"],"tags":["New Orleans","Finnegan","Duffey","Food & Dining","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cudgel","definition":"Duffey reports that the people trying to take over the <em>Crock</em> have cudgelled him, breaking his nose and right hand [A:40].","citations":["A:40"],"tags":["Duffey","Combat","Journalism"]},{"name":"Cult","definition":"The <em>Crock</em> is a “cult sheet” among Duffey’s special people [MTM B5:158]. Mary Catherine calls Casey a cult figure earning more than thirty thousand dollars a week, while Elwin K. Elkheart dismisses belief in the <em>Argo</em> as the latest cult [MTM B12:377] [MTM B13:402]. In a review quoted in <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>, Charles O. A. Harrington warns that the theory of prehistoric human returnees might start a cult [D:242]. The word also appears in the “cultist edifices” contrasted with the Plaza and in the mistaken cultic identification of Finnegan’s Heliogabalus amulet [A:3] [A:61]. In “Promontory Goats,” Margaret Stone describes a childhood cultus of serving an hour for a soul in Purgatory; Bascom Bagby describes prayers for the Poor Souls arriving instead as further burdens for them [PG:540] [PG:541] [PG:546].","citations":["MTM B5:158","MTM B12:377","MTM B13:402","D:242","A:3","A:61","PG:540","PG:541","PG:546"],"tags":["Prayer & Devotion","Heaven & Hell","Magic & Sorcery","Ships","Journalism","Finnegan","Duffey"]},{"name":"Culture","definition":"Finnegan is willing to accept Brannagan’s tale of Prester John living amid a static culture in Madagascar [D:206]. Cyrus Roundhead claims that the Macedonians invented the history and arts of ancient Greece to give themselves a culture [MTM B11:337]. The “maize culture” of San Simeon means cultivation of maize [MB:557].","citations":["D:206","MTM B11:337","MB:557"],"tags":["Africa","Greece","Finnegan","History & Memory","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Cultus (Goat)","definition":"Unverified entry. The earlier glossary attributes a goat cultus to Biloxi Brannagan and cites <em>Archipelago</em>, page 25. That page does not support the attribution. The separate reference to a “different cultus” concerns Finnegan’s amulet Heliogabalus [A:61].","citations":["A:61"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cumana","definition":"Cumaná, a city in Venezuela. A port on the <em>Brunhilde</em>’s itinerary, listed after Willemsted and Caracas and before Port of Spain [D:85].","citations":["D:85"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","Caribbean"]},{"name":"cummings (1894--1962), e. e.","definition":"Earlier heading placed the initials after the life dates and used inconsistent name capitalization. See <em>Cummings, E. E. (1894–1962)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Historical Figures","Literary Figures","Literature","Visual Arts","Duffey","World War I"]},{"name":"Cummings, E. E. (1894–1962)","definition":"Edward Estlin Cummings (1894–1962), American poet, painter, and playwright, known for experiments with poetic form and language. His collections include <em>Tulips and Chimneys</em> (1923) and <em>95 Poems</em> (1958). Duffey claims to have driven ambulances with him in France, but the narrator questions Duffey’s account and suggests that he may have lived scenes taken from Sebastian Hilton’s mind [MTM B2:72].","citations":["MTM B2:72"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Literary Figures","Literature","Visual Arts","Duffey","World War I"]},{"name":"Cup","definition":"Finnegan’s island-creation story makes the Aegean islands fragments of a smashed electrum cup [A:35]. He uses a cup of water to baptize his Heliogabalus amulet, and later sings of glory drowned in a cup [A:61] [A:276]. The notebook calls the wheat’s chaff-cup the glume [A:164]. Marie Courtois first finds Finnegan “in his cups”; Mrs. Mullens supplies paper cups for drinking Duffey’s whisky [D:32] [MTM B3:78]. On the Great Day, Duffy discovers that Charlotte’s customers, including himself, are drinking coffee without cups [GD:484].","citations":["A:35","A:61","A:276","A:164","D:32","MTM B3:78","GD:484"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Folklore","Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Alcohol","Food & Dining","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cups and Wineskins","definition":"The vessels whose remaining wine is secretly drunk by the tippling Apostle in the story Show Boat repeats from her father. He cannot remember whether the Apostle was Thomas, Bartholomew, or Jude [A:262].","citations":["A:262"],"tags":["New Testament","Alcohol","Folklore"]},{"name":"Cur dogs","definition":"As the Royal Pop party destroys the supposed pseudo-persons, the victims come to resemble “cur dogs and sick spirits.” Mary Virginia cannot tell whether some are dogs, snakes, or people, and questions their destruction [MTM B11:338].","citations":["MTM B11:338"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Women Characters","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Curator","definition":"Conrad Stone Hackenschmidt’s designation for Monsignor X, the sole attendant serving the 666 Princes at the Last Conclave [MB:566].","citations":["MB:566"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia"]},{"name":"Cures","definition":"The Boy King places his golden hands on blind or lame patients so that a physician can cure them [MTM B1:18]. Duffey objects to proposed “cures” of Holy Poverty, distinguishing poverty from the degradations wrongly called by its name [MTM B6:194]. Finnegan cures Mary Catherine’s hiccups by standing on her stomach [A:113] [A:248]. Don Barnaby seeks a joy-pill dispenser for his hangover [D:249]. At Weinsburg, Duffey cures a brilliant young man’s stuttering, despite the man’s wish to retain the affliction as a source of anger [MTM B13:430-431].","citations":["MTM B1:18","MTM B6:194","A:113","A:248","D:249","MTM B13:430-431"],"tags":["Miracles","Finnegan","Duffey","Women Characters","Alcohol","Social Class"]},{"name":"Curia (Roman)","definition":"“Reactionary Members of the Curia” are among the targets of the Devil’s public speech as “The Majesty,” alongside Pharisees, Legalists, Rigidists, and Insensate Hierarchies [MTM B8:243].","citations":["MTM B8:243"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","The Devil"]},{"name":"Curiosity","definition":"Finnegan explores the older burial beneath the grave he is digging out of curiosity; later he wonders why Papa Devil shows none about Finnegan’s knowledge of his true face [D:76] [D:86]. X briefly asks Doll for her life story, and elsewhere admits his persistent curiosity about his own and Finnegan’s origins [D:183] [A:265-266]. Duffey’s “disoriented curiosities” draw him toward making images even in the Presence [MTM B12:390-391]. John Mogul says that unsatisfied curiosity about X’s purposes eats him up [MB:573]. A curious goat is the lone witness near Don Lewis’s body; “curious” also means strange in the description of the gothic hotel Finnegan climbs [D:110] [D:162].","citations":["D:76","D:86","D:183","A:265-266","MTM B12:390-391","MB:573","D:110","D:162"],"tags":["The Devil","Finnegan","Duffey","Death & Mortality","Identity"]},{"name":"Current","definition":"Duffey says the current favored his eight-thousand-mile swim when he emerges at St. Louis [MTM B13:470]. Papadiabolous describes winds and currents carrying peoples westward across the Atlantic and making the return difficult [D:89]. A cold current chills Finnegan as he recalls the corpse he buried [D:76]. Electrical current occurs in the notebook’s definition of power factor and in Finnegan’s later display of knowledge [A:163] [D:190].","citations":["MTM B13:470","D:89","D:76","A:163","D:190"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Finnegan","Duffey","Devils (Characters)","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Curse","definition":"An epigraph attributed to Belloc asks whether anyone is under a curse forbidding morning ale [A:16]. The Brannagan home is said to escape the “curse” of southern fried chicken and corn bread [D:208]. Zabotski commands Blackstone’s sycamore to wither, while denying that he can command a man’s death [MTM B9:270]. The apparently ageless soldier calls the doctor investigating his age “cursed” [A:78].","citations":["A:16","D:208","MTM B9:270","A:78"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Food & Dining","Literature","Death & Mortality","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Curtain","definition":"Night falls over the closed bars like a curtain [A:10]. Patric associates a rising theatre curtain with renewed hope for a worthy play [A:110]. A manuscript descendant of the <em>Crock</em> circulates behind the Iron Curtain [A:168]. Duffey borrows Charley Murray’s “Lace Curtain Irish elegance,” and Widow Waldo hangs actual lace curtains in Zabotski’s contraption [MTM B1:26] [MTM B9:278]. At the Decatur Street Opera House, lightning and thunder destroy the stage curtain instead of raising it [MTM B11:349].","citations":["A:10","A:110","A:168","MTM B1:26","MTM B9:278","MTM B11:349"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Theater","Journalism","Ireland"]},{"name":"Curtis Improved","definition":"One of the names in iron lettering on churns remembered by the young Duffey, whose recollections include details taken from other people’s minds [MTM B1:17].","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Curvature of block","definition":"The bend in the block that makes Zabotski’s and Bryan Blackstone’s properties adjoin at the rear, although Homer Hoose’s house lies between theirs along the street [MTM B9:269].","citations":["MTM B9:269"],"tags":["Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Cuscus","definition":"A marsupial, also called the coconut possum, eaten by the cliff people whom Hans visits [A:58]. Sulem describes a person’s tujuan as directions written on vellum made from its skin and rolled into a tiny packet [A:60].","citations":["A:58","A:60"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Folklore","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Custodian of the Ashes","definition":"The role X claims when he and Kasmir Gorshok recover Duffey’s remains at Gur’yev in Tartary. X intends to return the ashes to Duffey when he meets him alive again [MTM B13:442].","citations":["MTM B13:442"],"tags":["Duffey","Death & Mortality","Resurrection"]},{"name":"Cutch","definition":"Destination of a Dutchman’s cargo in the voyage poem opening the Finnegan chapter; the Dutchman sails from the Gulf of Oman [A:172].","citations":["A:172"],"tags":["Sailing","Maritime Trade","Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Cutie-pie","definition":"Unverified entry. The earlier glossary attributes “Cutie-pie” to Gregory McIfreann Papadiabolous as a name for Nick the Rattle, but the cited page describes the shooting of Nick, Dotty, and Finnegan without that expression [A:282].","citations":["A:282"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cutter","definition":"Coast guard vessels sent against the ringing Sea-Bell. Newly appearing shoals keep them from reaching it [MB:560].","citations":["MB:560"],"tags":["Ships","Ekklesia"]},{"name":"Cyclone Cellar","definition":"The bar run by Cyclone Samenoff where Anna Louise sings and buys exclusive rights to one of Endymeon Ellenbogen’s songs [A:237-238].","citations":["A:237-238"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Music","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Cyclones (Sky-high)","definition":"The whirlwinds surrounding Duffey after his fall into the dung heap. Their lightning and thunder become an occasion for his recognition that he remains in the Presence [MTM B12:392-393].","citations":["MTM B12:392-393"],"tags":["Duffey","Theology"]},{"name":"Cyclops","definition":"“Land of the Cyclops” is the title of Chapter Eight of <em>Archipelago</em> [A:189]. Its Texas episode calls the state the dread land of a race of one-eyed or squint-eyed tall men [A:201].","citations":["A:189","A:201"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Giants & Titans","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Cygnus","definition":"Unverified entry. The earlier glossary says that Finnegan uses Cygnus as a direction marker, but its citation concerns Henri’s instructions to Duffey and the journey to New Orleans [MTM B5:184].","citations":["MTM B5:184"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Cypress","definition":"Trinali collects cypress knees in a tidewater swamp for her craftwork [A:229] [A:236]. Hans earlier sees her figuratively as made of driftwood, with cypress knees of her own [A:234]. Anastasia’s head is compared to that of a small cypress-wood Diana Artemis at Delos [D:133].","citations":["A:229","A:236","A:234","D:133"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Women Characters","Greek Mythology"]},{"name":"d'Alesandro","definition":"Engraver in Duffey’s Chicago circle, called d’Allesandro in <em>Archipelago</em> and d’Alesandro in <em>More Than Melchisedech</em>. His chamber-pot illustration supplies the <em>Crock</em> cover, and Casey names him among Duffey’s four prospective recruits to the Catholic Center Party of America [A:40] [A:42]. He attends the gathering where Morris Poor challenges Duffey’s sanity and contributes engravings to the <em>Crock</em> under Duffey [MTM B4:128] [MTM B4:138]. A commentator of the same name in “Promontory Goats” identifies himself as a leading mathematician and describes Casey’s mathematical jokes in his classroom; the passage does not establish that this is the engraver [PG:526].","citations":["A:40","A:42","MTM B4:128","MTM B4:138","PG:526"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Duffey","Minor Characters","Visual Arts","Journalism"]},{"name":"d'Allesandro","definition":"Spelling used in <em>Archipelago</em> for the engraver. See <em>d'Alesandro</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Catholic Church","Duffey","Minor Characters","Visual Arts","Journalism"]},{"name":"Daemon(s)","definition":"In an epigraph attributed to Galen’s <em>Techne Iatrike</em>, daemons possibly from worlds beyond the moon beget Tityroi or Satyroi on earthly women. Their children are fertile, but the next generation is sterile, so the daemons must return each century to renew the race. The epigraph also reports a daemon fathering children on thirteen women in one night at Calchedon in Bithyni [D:160-161]. Finnegan calls the spirit that guides his painting a daemon or devil and describes painting the spirit itself [D:253].","citations":["D:160-161","D:253"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","The Devil","Finnegan","Visual Arts","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Dago","definition":"An ethnic slur used for Finnegan in Doppio di Pinne’s note and in the description of his brother Jake’s contempt for him [D:51] [A:174]. Finnegan uses it when recalling his first impression of Theresa Piccone’s photograph and when wrongly assuming a Greek Capuchin must be Italian [A:108] [D:118]. Giulio calls the son who receives Duffey’s talisman “the dago son” [MTM B3:107]. Hecklers use the slur against Showboat during her street preaching [MTM B7:231].","citations":["D:51","A:174","A:108","D:118","MTM B3:107","MTM B7:231"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Family","Language & Words","Italy"]},{"name":"Dago house-wife","definition":"An unnamed St. Louis housewife known to the group. The narrator says that Mary sometimes drinks coffee with her in her kitchen; Dotty asks why anyone should doubt it [A:148].","citations":["A:148"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Prayer & Devotion"]},{"name":"Dakar","definition":"A Senegalese port visited by the <em>Brunhilde</em> on its northward voyage after Freetown. The itinerary places Dakar between Bathurst and St. Louis [D:95-96].","citations":["D:95-96"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","Africa"]},{"name":"Dakota","definition":"The Stranahans arrange annual pheasant-hunting trips to Dakota with Hans and Henry, along with trips for Gulf marlin and Minnesota deer, “God and circumstances permitting” [A:103].","citations":["A:103"],"tags":["Family","Fishing"]},{"name":"Dali, Salvador","definition":"Salvador Dalí (1904-1989), Spanish surrealist painter. Duffey recognizes landscapes by Dalí, Doré, and Hieronymus Bosch within the Devil’s mind [MTM B8:241]. Clarence Schrade later says that Casey’s drawings burlesque the hellscapes of Dalí, Doré, Bosch, and Finnegan [PG:522]. Dalí studied at the San Fernando Royal Academy of Fine Arts in Madrid and was finally expelled in 1926. His paintings combine precise technique with dream imagery, as in <em>The Persistence of Memory</em> (1931). Influenced by Freud, he developed the paranoiac-critical method to explore unstable perceptions and double images. He also worked in sculpture, film, and writing, collaborating with Luis Buñuel and Alfred Hitchcock, and developed photographic projects with Philippe Halsman. The surrealists attempted to expel him in 1934; the Gala-Salvador Dalí Foundation dates his expulsion to 1939. He lived in the United States during the Second World War and returned to Spain in 1948. King Juan Carlos I made him Marquis of Dalí de Púbol in 1982. After Gala’s death in 1982 he moved to Púbol. Following a fire there in 1984, he moved to the Galatea Tower in Figueres, where he remained until his death.","citations":["MTM B8:241","PG:522"],"tags":["The Devil","Spain","Duffey","Historical Figures","Visual Arts","Dreams"]},{"name":"Dallas","definition":"City on the Devil's speaking itinerary. George Koran reports that “The Majesty” will give five talks there to “fleece the sheepy rich” [MTM B8:243] [MTM B8:244].","citations":["MTM B8:243","MTM B8:244"],"tags":["The Devil","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Damascus","definition":"Henri Salvatore calls St. Louis Duffey’s Damascus: a place where he has had the opportunity for blindness and recovery before going on to his life’s work in New Orleans [MTM B5:184].","citations":["MTM B5:184"],"tags":["Duffey","New Testament","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Dame Bagby","definition":"Henri Cardinal Salvatore’s name for Duffey’s sister Mary Louise, earlier called Mary Louise Byrne and married to Bascom Bagby [MTM B2:55] [MTM B5:155]. The Cardinal reports her wish to bury Duffey in the family plot near Bagby in St. Louis; the narrator also calls her Dame Bagby [MTM B12:384-385].","citations":["MTM B2:55","MTM B5:155","MTM B12:384-385"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Family","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Dane","definition":"See <em>Danish-bread Christ</em>, one of the seven Christ images Duffey collects during his lost years [MTM B3:103].","citations":["MTM B3:103"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Danish Beer","definition":"One of Patrick Stranahan’s four sufficient brews, alongside an American beer, a Bohemian beer, and Guinness [A:110]. Finnegan and his companions also drink Danish beer on Basse-Terre, where it costs more than the island rum [D:236].","citations":["A:110","D:236"],"tags":["Caribbean","Alcohol","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Danish-bread Christ","definition":"One of the Seven Christs Duffey assembles after his lost years: a Christ-head formed from mixed wheat and rye dough, baked and varnished. The narrator connects it with Danish Corpus Christi breadmaking [MTM B3:103].","citations":["MTM B3:103"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining","Visual Arts","Liturgy"]},{"name":"Danovitz, Rolo","definition":"The spoiled-brat Antichrist of a contemporary cycle of quasi-fiction, whom Hilary Hilton identifies with Casey Szymansky in conversation with Duffey. Hilton says he spent several million dollars establishing Casey’s identity and believes Casey demands sacrifices from others while falsely claiming to sacrifice himself. These are Hilton’s assertions [MTM B12:378].","citations":["MTM B12:378"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Antichrist","Identity","Literature"]},{"name":"Dante Alighieri","definition":"Dante Alighieri (1265-1321), Italian poet and author of the <em>Divine Comedy</em>, whose three parts, <em>Inferno</em>, <em>Purgatorio</em>, and <em>Paradiso</em>, recount a journey through Hell, Purgatory, and Heaven. Virgil and Beatrice guide Dante, and Bernard of Clairvaux becomes his final guide. His other works include the <em>Vita nuova</em> and <em>Monarchia</em>. Hans says that Finnegan will quote Dante until his listener’s ears fall off [A:9]. Finnegan proposes to “pull a Dante” by painting his enemies dead and rotten, comparing this with placing enemies in Hell [D:61]. Patrick Stranahan dismisses Dante as a “rimester” when Papa Piccone objects to his omission from Patrick’s approved poets [A:111].","citations":["A:9","D:61","A:111"],"tags":["Theology","Heaven & Hell","Italy","Finnegan","Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Danvers, Dotty","definition":"A regular at the Old Wooden Ship [D:215]. Like Dotty Hulme, she has been married to Buffalo Chips Dugan; like Little Dotty Nesbitt, she has married one of two brothers in Sugarland, Texas. These overlapping marriages help explain why people confuse the women [D:220].","citations":["D:215","D:220"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Family"]},{"name":"Darkness","definition":"Finnegan weeps in the dark from weariness and loneliness [A:179]. A scriptural excerpt in <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> names the “shapers of darkness” among the powers opposed to human beings [MTM B5:175]. In the television apparition of Casey’s head, its rolled-back eyes can see only its interior darkness [MTM B12:381-382]. The authorial speaker in the Postscript also describes darkness and despondency when another agency appears to write with his hand [PS:475].","citations":["A:179","MTM B5:175","MTM B12:381-382","PS:475"],"tags":["Finnegan","Scripture","Theology","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Darnley, Robert","definition":"A local historical scholar associated with Dillard, present among the historians crowding Duffey’s premises during the Royal Pop episode [MTM B11:322].","citations":["MTM B11:322"],"tags":["Minor Characters","History & Memory","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Darts","definition":"One of four family names invoked by a Pattenite in his attack on the claimed evidence for early humanity in Africa, alongside Brooms, Oakleys, and Leakys [MTM B9:283].","citations":["MTM B9:283"],"tags":["Africa","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Darwin, Charles","definition":"Charles Darwin, English naturalist, invoked in the badge “Darwin or Death” worn by an armed Orthodoxer who challenges Zabotski’s account of origins [MTM B9:286].","citations":["MTM B9:286"],"tags":["Historical Figures","History & Memory","Combat"]},{"name":"Day Star","definition":"A primordial point of reference in Henri Salvatore’s letter to Duffey: the wine and bottles in which they will share were already new when the Day Star was made [MTM B5:179-180].","citations":["MTM B5:179-180"],"tags":["Alcohol","Creation","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Daytona","definition":"An occasional destination on the drives taken by Finnegan, Doll, and X from their Florida lake retreat [D:181] [D:183].","citations":["D:181","D:183"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Dead Man","definition":"Anastasia asks Finnegan to paint her dead so that she can see how she looks [D:61]. Later, the <em>Argo</em> sails with the bones of the “merry dead man,” Melchisedech Duffey, at the helm [MTM B13:444].","citations":["D:61","MTM B13:444"],"tags":["Sailing","Finnegan","Duffey","Women Characters","The Dead","Visual Arts","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Dead Sea","definition":"The imagined setting of Trinali’s poem “To Trinali in the Old Wood Graveyard on the Dead Sea.” It associates the driftwood shore with Lot, Gomorrah, salt, fire, and divine wrath, and ends with the burning Dead Sea and its Devil Birds [A:235-236].","citations":["A:235-236"],"tags":["Old Testament","Seas & Oceans","Literature","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Death","definition":"A recurrent, often non-final condition in the <em>Argo</em> narratives. The <em>Promantia</em> says that dying is not always serious the first time and introduces people unwilling to stay dead [D:6]. Private Gregory describes successive lives, while his fellow patient the sergeant claims to live several simultaneously [A:79]; Duffey remembers three childhoods occupying the same years [MTM B1:6]. In the psychiatric ward, Green, who identifies himself as God, says that the universe depends on God's momentary attention and would vanish if He nodded; Gregory points out that Green's demonstration coincides with the compressor dimming the lights [A:76]. The postscript calls <em>Argo</em>'s apparent endings a continuing surge of multiplicities, rather than actual endings [PS:479].\n\nIgnatius Ti explains midnight as the hour when people choose to die in company and a hole opens in the veil through which old ghosts can return. His account includes hospital pranks with supposed corpses, his people's practice of seating corpses at wakes and burials, and a murdered husband who returns to his wife's bed. The narration then lets loose ghosts into the ward [A:85-87]. An epigraph attributed to Ignace Wolff makes bodies the obstacle to non-contradiction and sufficient reason [D:67]. Seaworthy's dream expresses the embarrassment and inescapable stickiness of possessing a corpse [D:72]. Papa Devil's living disguise and dead face are different: Finnegan paints the concealed face before others see it on the corpse [D:63] [D:86] [D:146].\n\nDuffey's dying is a prolonged sequence. Cardinal Salvatore speaks of his friends' worn-out mortal coils and of Duffey's departure to a greater thing [MTM B12:385]. His seizure and sudden leave-taking occur among friends [MTM B12:388]. His garden vision is explicitly an anteroom, with the last second still unfinished [MTM B12:389]. After he turns the forbidden handle, he falls into a dung heap, crying that he is falling forever; as much as fifty seconds of his last moment may still remain. He eventually recognizes God's presence in the torment and whirlwind too [MTM B12:392-393]. Earlier he tells X that remaining within two weeks of death deepens his piety [MTM B12:376].\n\nIn “The Casey Machine,” a pastor tells a sinner that everyone will know everything, with edification and joy for the saved and fiery regret for the damned. Whether complete knowledge follows particular or general judgment is uncertain, but eternity has no time differential [CM:504]. The printed quotation attributed to the Baltimore Catechism describes the just rising glorious and immortal, shining like the sun, swift as thought, able to penetrate matter, beautiful, painless and changeless, while the damned also rise to eternal punishment. The narrator's fascination with undead, dead and resurrected bodies develops alongside the Casey Condition [CM:511].\n\nBagby's letters describe a purgatorial life free of the tyrannies of hours and places: hard but not torturous work, ample leisure, no sleep, stronger passions disciplined again and again, and refining relationships with kindred, aliens, angels and the damned. His letters expressly question whether any damnation is irrevocable. Particularity and knowledge grow; Earth-hours are appointments for contemplating Earth. He criticizes neglected mathematical, economic and theological fields, describes thatched huts and circles of increasing intensity, and distinguishes contentment from a growing, kinetic, mustard-seed happiness [MTM B8:248-249]. Cardinal Salvatore locates him on Purgatory's north slope [MTM B12:384]. The Ghost Coffee Shop initially appears as a refuge for spirits purging the horror of haunting, but the narrator immediately retracts this account, except for Finnegan's picture, and identifies Anthony Ghost's ordinary coffee shop [MTM B10:292-293].\n\nHell receives several qualified accounts. In “Promontory Goats,” it was once pleasant and isolated, and some parts still provide natural happiness without the Beatific Vision. Most inhabitants join groups whose consensual pleasures become ever more extreme torture. They can withdraw but choose the pleasure of tormenting others even at the cost of their own agony. The same passage calls substitution for such souls irrational yet a greater sacrifice [PG:532-533]. The sinner Lorenzo, rather than an authoritative narrator, welcomes endless wallowing and gloating despite his pastor's warning [CM:504-505]. Clarence Schrade describes Casey's drawing of the Iron Meadows of Hell [PG:522-523], while the prose following Casey's poem identifies the troll beneath the bridge as the Devil [PG:523]. Finnegan associates the dying Marie Courtois with Persephone's recurrent visits to Hell [D:157]. Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni says that Heaven, Hell and Purgatory are already here, the visible universe being the reverse of Heaven's tapestry [A:211] [A:214]. Duffey's claim that Heaven contains traitors, sycophants and socialists belongs to his comic argument about salvation and jazz [A:117]. The Terrestrial Paradise is offered as an attainable earthly place, with exact coordinates in the Antilles [D:6] [D:234]; <em>Archipelago</em> also says Finnegan knew its location [A:188].\n\nDuffey's apparent inability to end coexists with his deaths. The narrative and Stein's notes distinguish his ending from an end to his existence and fear disintegration into several strands [MTM B10:290-291]. His own ashes sit before him in an old royal urn [MTM B1:6]. One account says he is killed, stripped of flesh and cremated at Our'yev [MTM B13:436]; the fuller account has the slant-faced killers strip his flesh in the amoral desert, followed by slack-faced burners, before his bones and ashes appear in Gur'yev. His spirit lingers, and X takes custody of the ashes in a cigar canister formerly belonging to the King of Spain [MTM B13:442]. After three days in an <em>Argo</em> coffin, his bones answer God and resume duty as Pilot and Master [MTM B13:443]. He sails as a merry skeleton, yet can regain his former flesh in adventures falling earlier than the Laughing Prince episode, while the ashes persist [MTM B13:444]. Pseudo-Melchisedech subsequently tells him that he has lived the lost years seven times and died seven deaths; three led to Hell and four did not [MTM B13:465].\n\nFinnegan's death quest is named through his fleece, thanato rather than mallion. Stein's account juxtaposes reports of his deaths in the Philippines, on the <em>Brunhilde</em>, at Tangier, Naxos, Galveston and the Marianao Coast with evidence of survival and Finnegan's differing recollections [MB:548]. A seaman resembling him is actually killed in his discarded clothes [D:267]. He remembers Papa Devil killing him during one of several incompatible first encounters [D:83]. After Anastasia's death he says he is dead too; X answers that he has undergone such a death and continued on a lesser level [D:156]. The narrator calls this Finnegan's first death and subsequent existence a shadow life [D:158]; Finnegan later finds entertainment in this life after death in Paradise [D:237]. Doll's poem predicts that he will soon be dead as the Devil [D:294]. Finnegan's belief that X had died is a separate matter: X admits fabricating and circulating the obituary as a joke [D:152-153].\n\nAccounts of Casey also remain plural. X writes that he was dead and lives again [A:266]. Tony Apostolo reports his belief that he is the world's scapegoat [PG:525-526]; Hilary Hilton reads a divine sign making him the instrument of many people's salvation and damnation [PG:531-532]. John Schultz believes Casey died to escape his overwhelming followers, then reports the stolen body and the resurrection plaque beside the open grave [PG:541-543]. Bagby places Casey with him in Purgatory and denies that he is in Hell, while admitting the slight possibility that his visitor is a pseudo or effigy [PG:546]. Stein attributes Casey's wish to substitute for a damned soul largely to genuine compassion and describes their supposed soul exchange as a trade of kabouters [PG:537-538].\n\nThe original Papadiabolous is killed by Seaworthy and buried with Finnegan's help on top of an earlier occupant [D:75-76]. Seaworthy believes the man has returned, but Finnegan knows that the new Papadiabolous is another person [D:83-85]. X identifies the surrogate as the Irish policeman Noonan or Flaherty [D:154-155]. His death at Naxos reveals his true face; Finnegan then declares both the original and surrogate finally dead, a claim about these men rather than the extinction of every Devil [D:146] [D:150]. Anastasia is shot and falls from the breakwater [D:147]. Finnegan identifies Marie as her killer and Joe Cross as Marie's subsequent shooter; X thinks Seaworthy arranged Anastasia's lavish funeral [D:157]. Don Lewis bears the mark of death, is hunted through Tangier and killed by Marie, whose strength breaks his neck; Finnegan buries him in clean sand [D:100] [D:105-113].\n\nDeaths of worlds and institutions extend the motif. The Great Day contingency unstructures things and obliterates boundaries and persons [MTM B10:298-299]. Its closing dream belongs to Duffey and Morpheus, not the grieving Finnegan: waking and sleeping become impossible, the cosmos diminishes, and a single creature swallows itself before Morpheus calls the apparent end a beginning [GD:500-501]. Cleo's fifth future includes replacement of humanity and slaughter [MTM B10:296]. Hugh de Turenne reports the Black Sea's obliteration and the intended erasure of its memory [MTM B11:336]. Mary Virginia reports Dominican preachers predicting the world's end that week [MTM B11:310]. Hackenschmidt declares the Ekklesia dead, orders the bells destroyed lest they awaken it, and commissions the killing of the thirteen absent princes [MB:568-569]. John Mogul's suggestion that dead men cast no shadows is explicitly contradicted by the narrator: history and the surviving doubles refute it [MB:588].\n\nThe grave marked I.N.C.G.P. Bogovitch becomes the watched rendezvous of Finnegan, X and Doll [D:275-277]. It contains the earlier remains and Papadiabolous; Doll drops her poem through the ground's recurrent split to the bottom [D:76] [D:293-294]. Niccolo Crotolo explains two kinds of assassination contract: the piccola vendetta kills a victim possibly in grace, while the more expensive grande aims at death in a state of damnation. He says Finnegan's employer finally specified only the piccola [A:279-280].","citations":["D:6","A:79","MTM B1:6","A:76","PS:479","A:85-87","D:67","D:72","D:63","D:86","D:146","MTM B12:385","MTM B12:388","MTM B12:389","MTM B12:392-393","MTM B12:376","CM:504","CM:511","MTM B8:248-249","MTM B12:384","MTM B10:292-293","PG:532-533","CM:504-505","PG:522-523","PG:523","D:157","A:211","A:214","A:117","D:234","A:188","MTM B10:290-291","MTM B13:436","MTM B13:442","MTM B13:443","MTM B13:444","MTM B13:465","MB:548","D:267","D:83","D:156","D:158","D:237","D:294","D:152-153","A:266","PG:525-526","PG:531-532","PG:541-543","PG:546","PG:537-538","D:75-76","D:83-85","D:154-155","D:150","D:147","D:100","D:105-113","MTM B10:298-299","GD:500-501","MTM B10:296","MTM B11:336","MTM B11:310","MB:568-569","MB:588","D:275-277","D:76","D:293-294","A:279-280"],"tags":["Death & Mortality","Resurrection","Immortality","Ghosts & Spirits","The Dead","Heaven & Hell","Theology","Time & Eternity","Metaphysics","Paradox","Dreams","Identity","History & Memory","Duffey","Finnegan","Devils (Characters)","The Devil","Ekklesia","Apocalyptic","Ships","Crime","Literature","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Decadence","definition":"A condition of decline invoked in contrasting settings. X reports representatives of the decadent and goatish arts at the Devil's release in the Crimea [MTM B6:205]. Le Marin calls the Old Wooden Ship's people decadent, fragmented and episodical; Finnegan nevertheless chooses their humanity over the strong patterns of his former companions [D:215].","citations":["MTM B6:205","D:215"],"tags":["The Devil","Bars & Taverns","Literature","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Decatur Street","definition":"Street in the changing New Orleans of the Royal Pop episode. The narrator says it now exists where Magazine Street, or perhaps Peters Street, had been, although residents do not remember it; the Decatur Street Opera House likewise has newly acquired its apparent history [MTM B11:346]. A turbaned speaker claims that an old flagstone has survived successive road surfaces and remembers horses, carriages, streetcars, automobiles, and every two-legged and four-legged walker [MTM B11:329-330]. Its remembered whips include the Cowles Buggy Whip, Jacksonville Drover, S.R. and Co's Australian, Western Mule Skinner, Milford Quirt, Hodson's Superior Horse Whip, San Antonio, Fancy, Never-Break Dog Whip and Elko, accompanied by wagoners' curses, rattling whipple trees and chains [MTM B11:329]. The opera house gives its name to the Thunder Colt Complex, or Presentation of the World [MTM B10:300].","citations":["MTM B11:346","MTM B11:329-330","MTM B11:329","MTM B10:300"],"tags":["New Orleans","Fictional Places","Theater","History & Memory","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Decatur Street Opera House","definition":"Opera house in New Orleans whose advertisement appears in Teresa Showboat Piccone Stranahan's edition of <em>Show-Bill</em>. Dotty says it does not exist; Teresa calls this its first appearance and explains that it supplies a needed place for eschatological dramas [MTM B7:230]. It gives its name to the Thunder Colt Complex, the Presentation of the World [MTM B10:300]. Later it stands as a memorable old opera house despite residents' inability to remember it [MTM B11:346]. Its curtain is destroyed by lightning and simultaneous thunder instead of rising, an effect unique in the town. The Cretan Bull Drama is accompanied by the Bull Waltz [MTM B11:349]. The Fire-Drake Frolic follows, with Fritz's Fandango in Three Flames; the climax is the Thunder Colt Game, orchestrated to Thunder Torus music [MTM B11:350].","citations":["MTM B7:230","MTM B10:300","MTM B11:346","MTM B11:349","MTM B11:350"],"tags":["New Orleans","Fictional Places","Theater","Music","Apocalyptic","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Deer","definition":"Deer hunting is among the lively arts said to have been corrupted after the Devil's release [MTM B6:207]. Finnegan recalls that other fathers made swamp-deer calls for their sons, whereas Giulio made calls for panthers, alligators and stranger creatures [MTM B5:174]. The goat-man kills a deer once a year [A:197]. In his transport concerning his people's ancestry, Finnegan calls the giant stag one of their animal brothers [D:212-213]. Brannagan includes Deer Island among the islands visible from his chair [D:204].","citations":["MTM B6:207","MTM B5:174","A:197","D:212-213","D:204"],"tags":["Finnegan","Family","Islands","Giants & Titans"]},{"name":"Deer Island","definition":"Island off Biloxi, Mississippi; one of four islands Brannagan says he can see from his chair [D:204].","citations":["D:204"],"tags":["Islands"]},{"name":"Delancy, Doll","definition":"Dolores Delancy, called Doll; Finnegan’s companion after the <em>Brunhilde</em> voyage [D:171]. He meets her in New York during his flight with Seaworthy’s money. She says she is locked out, freezing, and starving, and denies that she is hustling. He makes her drive a stolen car to Newark under the name Mrs. George Brubaker [D:166-169]. She helps him manage the suitcase and evade pursuit on the journey to Baltimore [D:169-172]. She has a broad face and bright gray eyes, and the narrator compares her placidity to a sleeping volcano. She says she has walked out of an unbearable former life [D:173].\n\nShe welcomes X into their company, calling him “the cutest little man,” and travels south with him and Finnegan [D:174] [D:178]. She receives a share of the stolen money; in the Florida lake country the three discuss their identities and plot Seaworthy’s murder [D:180]. She catches fish by hand, goes drinking with Finnegan, and repeatedly tries to tell the two men her story, but neither listens [D:181-184]. X suspects that her hearing is poor [D:185-186]. When they separate, she leaves in the little Road Runner [D:193].\n\nAt their later graveyard meeting, Doll hears X’s account of the Devil’s family [D:275-279]. Against X and Finnegan, she insists that ordinary people can help save the world, that the conflict must have a climax, and that she has long been engaged in saving the world herself. She also questions whether her first meeting with Finnegan was accidental [D:290-292]. She drops her poem <em>Diabolique</em> into the split in the grave [D:293].\n\nIn <em>More Than Melchisedech</em>, Dotty Yekouris reports X’s identification of Doll with herself and describes Doll as resembling her “with the brains knocked out.” This is a reported identification across Finnegan’s worlds [MTM B6:188-189].","citations":["D:171","D:166-169","D:169-172","D:173","D:174","D:178","D:180","D:181-184","D:185-186","D:193","D:275-279","D:290-292","D:293","MTM B6:188-189"],"tags":["Women Characters","Finnegan","Literature","Crime","Identity"]},{"name":"Delay","definition":"One of the names X gives the ship on which Finnegan sailed, alongside <em>World</em> and <em>Argo</em>. X also says Finnegan imagined most of the voyage’s details [A:187].","citations":["A:187"],"tags":["Ships","Identity"]},{"name":"Delos","definition":"Greek island. In the narrator's comparison of Anastasia to Diana Artemis, a small cypress-wood image of that goddess is said to have stood on a temple step at Delos [D:133].","citations":["D:133"],"tags":["Islands","Greece","Greek Mythology","Visual Arts","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Delphina","definition":"One of the women's names assigned to the daily bottles drunk by Finnegan, Charles Le Marin, Don Barnaby and Johnny Duckwalk in Paradise. Delphina follows Clotilda and precedes Etheldreda in their alphabetical sequence [D:236] [D:243].","citations":["D:236","D:243"],"tags":["Alcohol","Finnegan","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Delta","definition":"Appears in two separate contexts. Three young men saw the hands off the tower clock on the Great Delta Insurance Company Building during the Great Day [GD:483]. X uses the word in a cryptic telephone message to Finnegan: the goats are staked near the delta while hunters wait for the tiger [D:208].","citations":["GD:483","D:208"],"tags":["Language & Words","Apocalyptic","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Deluge","definition":"Noah's Flood. Hinnegan claims that the Milesians were in Ireland before it; the schoolboy narrator answers that knowing this would require Noah to have brought two Irishmen aboard the ark [A:164-165].","citations":["A:164-165"],"tags":["Old Testament","Ireland","Folklore"]},{"name":"Demeter","definition":"Greek goddess of agriculture. In one of Finnegan's uncertain waking dreams, Marie Courtois says she is both Demeter and Demeter's daughter Persephone, returning from Hell to Earth in her own reincarnation [D:91]. Marie also links the pietà form and the thirteenth Station of the Cross to a supposed earlier group of Demeter and Pontos on the Aventine [D:92].","citations":["D:91","D:92"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Underworld","Women Characters","Family","Dreams"]},{"name":"Demetriades, Anastasia","definition":"Barmaid at the club visited by Finnegan and Seaworthy, and later aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em>. She washes and shaves Finnegan, tells him they have met before, and prompts his exclamation “Anastasia, the resurrection!” [D:13] [D:16] [D:22]. Small and wiry, with a changing appearance, she has an olive and gold complexion sometimes touched with sea-green; her eyes appear black or purple [D:20] [D:32]. Her speech reaches Finnegan’s mind before its sound begins [D:20]. The narrative calls her a water-witch and associates her true form with the ship [D:31].\n\nShe is of a mermaid family of Naxos, though the Demetriades have produced no fishtail for six generations. A powerful swimmer, she supplies the account of mermaid appearance, habitation, history, and decline; the account describes mermaids as close-cropped and boyish, and Anastasia herself is boyish in figure [D:34-37]. She and Marie Courtois share a cabin, feud, and remain friends despite Anastasia’s jealousy. Anastasia calls Marie “skylla” and puts “crotin oil” in her martini; Finnegan calls Anastasia “Little Miss Resurrection” [D:33]. Her jealousy intensifies when Finnegan admires Marie at the Padre Island beach party [D:34] [D:37-38]. Anastasia notices the waves’ apparent writing but explicitly says she cannot read Chaldee; Finnegan claims that he can [D:38].\n\nShe identifies Papadiabolous as Papa Devil and says he seems nicer than on an earlier visit [D:23-24]. She brings Finnegan’s luggage aboard for the departure [D:27]. Walking inland with him on Padre Island, she suspects Don Lewis is one kind of spy and herself another, and warns that Seaworthy and the Devil own the ship’s people [D:38-40]. Later she calls the <em>Brunhilde</em> a plague ship, admits she deliberately joined its dangerous business, and calls Finnegan her brother [D:69-70]. Finnegan paints her dead as the wife of Sindbad’s fourth voyage, having first seen her death in his imagination [D:61-62].\n\nOn Naxos she visits her grandmother with Finnegan and takes him to a mountain vigil, bringing a reflecting lantern with which she intends to signal a boat [D:124-127] [D:131]. When he wakes before dawn, she has gone [D:140-141]. He subsequently sees her body laid on a sofa, still smiling; she has been shot once and pulled dead from the water below the breakwater. An Orthodox priest has given her the last rites and asks about her Roman rosary, which Finnegan says he gave her [D:147]. Finnegan later says Marie shot her, and X thinks Seaworthy arranged her lavish funeral [D:157]. Finnegan recalls her promise that they will be together again, then doubts whether his people have that promise [D:149]. A voice coming from the sleeping Don Barnaby says it owed Anastasia fifty dollars when it died; the narration specifies that the voice is not Barnaby’s [D:238].","citations":["D:13","D:16","D:22","D:20","D:32","D:31","D:34-37","D:33","D:34","D:37-38","D:38","D:23-24","D:27","D:38-40","D:69-70","D:61-62","D:124-127","D:131","D:140-141","D:147","D:157","D:149","D:238"],"tags":["Women Characters","Nymphs & Mermaids","Crew & Sailors","Greece","Finnegan","Death & Mortality","Resurrection"]},{"name":"Demetrio","definition":"Proprietor of a fly-specked Mexican bar visited by Finnegan, Marie Courtois and Anastasia after they leave the Cadillac Bar [D:57-58]. A gray-headed old bandit, he claims wide worldly experience through travel to Alice, Eagle Pass, Uvalde, Beeville, San Angelo, Weiniar and Port Isabel, and work milking goats, keeping bees and in a pickle factory. His lined face resembles both an old Roman and an old Aztec, like a face on a coin [D:58]. Finnegan makes a fine portrait in ink on a poster; the following page calls it Demetrio's own head hanging behind the bar, although the portrait sentence itself names Manuel [D:58-59]. Demetrio gives Finnegan a belt and each girl a silver ring, all his own handiwork [D:59].","citations":["D:57-58","D:58","D:58-59","D:59"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","Visual Arts","Occupations"]},{"name":"Demetrio Glauch","definition":"Literary friend in Duffey's North Chicago golden age [MTM B3:98]. He rejects Fleshpot Row and is mocked by the women at supper; Duffey's initial refusal to leave with him creates lasting doubt between them [MTM B4:126-127]. He is present when Morris Poor challenges Duffey's sanity and magical powers at Duffey's place [MTM B4:128]. He contributes to <em>The Crock</em>, appearing in a contributor list alongside Hermione Groben [MTM B4:138]; an issue described in <em>Archipelago</em> contains his ballad, without a supplied title [A:41]. His signed introduction frames the assessment and collection of Casey's work in “Promontory Goats” [PG:519]. In a later commentary he says Casey's associates stole the body and recalls a <em>Crock</em> advertisement offering dead bodies for special rites [PG:546].","citations":["MTM B3:98","MTM B4:126-127","MTM B4:128","MTM B4:138","A:41","PG:519","PG:546"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Duffey","Literary Figures","Literature","Journalism","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Demiurge(s)","definition":"Creative beings or powers. The narrator allows as one authentic perspective that God creates through demiurges, giants and magi, without making that the sole account of creation [MTM B3:76-77]. Rosemary Riorden tells Finnegan that his father is Aeson, a demiurge, although he knows him as Giulio [A:89]. During the Great Day, a satiric transfiguration at St. Michael's puts the demiurge Teilhard on the altar with McLuhan and McGonigal, blessing the Unfaithful Assembled [GD:497]. In Duffey's television over-picture during <em>Goldfarb's Alley</em>, four black giants of demiurgic appearance carry Casey as a head-only person [MTM B12:381].","citations":["MTM B3:76-77","A:89","GD:497","MTM B12:381"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Creation","Argonautica","Giants & Titans","Finnegan","Duffey","Theater"]},{"name":"Demons","definition":"Hilary Hilton says Casey professes to sacrifice himself for humanity and especially demons, whom he calls the most unfortunate and abused creatures; Hilton simultaneously denies that Casey has ever actually sacrificed himself [MTM B12:378]. The epigraph attributed to Galen in <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> describes daemons begetting Tityroi or Satyroi on earthly women. Those satyr offspring, rather than the daemons, are the race with the stated generational fertility limits [D:160-161].","citations":["MTM B12:378","D:160-161"],"tags":["Angels & Demons","Monsters & Creatures","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Dempster's Three-Spring Handy Wagon","definition":"Vehicle in the turbaned Royal Pop speaker's catalogue of memories supposedly held by a Decatur Street flagstone. Despite being called a wagon, it carried swells and dudes to the opera [MTM B11:329].","citations":["MTM B11:329"],"tags":["New Orleans","History & Memory","Theater"]},{"name":"Den","definition":"Shelter or lair in several figurative uses. John Mogul recites a verse about eleven fuzzy cubs hiding in a den, representing his remaining primary targets; the next beheading is aimed at Ignatius Cardinal Ti [MB:578]. Finnegan's perch on a hotel merlon is described as a gargoyle's den or nest [D:162]. In Paradise he calls the place a den and vows to stay, though Sloppy Joe predicts his departure [D:266].","citations":["MB:578","D:162","D:266"],"tags":["Finnegan","Caribbean","Literature","Crime"]},{"name":"Denmark","definition":"Denmark is associated with the Danish-bread Christ among Duffey's Seven Christs: the narrator says that some parts of the country bake Christ-heads for Corpus Christi Day [MTM B3:103]. Finnegan and his three companions also drink good Danish beer in Basse-Terre, at a higher price than the local rum [D:236].","citations":["MTM B3:103","D:236"],"tags":["Duffey","Visual Arts","Alcohol","Food & Dining","Caribbean"]},{"name":"DePolis","definition":"Cook aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em>, also written De Polis. At the Padre Island beach party he leaves the cooking to the others, disliking both beach parties and anyone else cooking [D:34] [D:40]. Anastasia later watches him put garlic in the stew while Manuel prays, joking that both are gainfully employed because prayer and garlic can change the world [D:68].","citations":["D:34","D:40","D:68"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Ships","Food & Dining","Occupations","Prayer & Devotion"]},{"name":"Derringer","definition":"One of the weapons Doll suggests assigning by lot when she, X and Finnegan discuss dispersing, alongside a rifle and a pop pistol [D:185].","citations":["D:185"],"tags":["Weapons","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Descalced","definition":"Barefoot religious priest who accompanies Papa Devil up the old walls at a Mexican stop, possibly Paraiso or Campeche [D:67] [D:70]. Both move noiselessly, the priest having the natural advantage of bare feet. He calls the Devil's aura exceptionally evil but also says that each has been having fun with the other and that he is less naive than he seems [D:70-71]. Finnegan and Anastasia give him the last drink; he blesses them with his eyes and walks down to the boat with them [D:71].","citations":["D:67","D:70","D:70-71","D:71"],"tags":["Religious Orders","Minor Characters","Devils (Characters)","Ships"]},{"name":"Desert","definition":"The desert of Cades appears in Melchisedech's line during his exchange of psalm-prayers with Finnegan [MTM B12:374-375]. In Tartary, Duffey encounters an imperfect, hallucinatory desert superimposed on industrial and agricultural country, with unnatural heat, intense thirst, misplaced plants and rocks, and badly realized skeletons [MTM B13:439]. He drinks the Laughing Prince's poisoned spring; the slant-faced killers strip the flesh from his bones before the poison kills him. The amoral desert then fades and his remains appear at Gur'yev [MTM B13:440-442]. The opening poem of the Finnegan chapter in <em>Archipelago</em> places a burning desert at Yenbo and Lith [A:171].","citations":["MTM B12:374-375","MTM B13:439","MTM B13:440-442","A:171"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan","Scripture","Prayer & Devotion","Dreams","Death & Mortality","Literature"]},{"name":"Deutero-Finnegan","definition":"Stein's name for a young painter resembling Finnegan in appearance and painting. Although accepted as splendid and noetic, he voluntarily puts his hands into the human ashes at the Decatur Street Opera House, where they kindle flame [MTM B11:347]. The Thunder Colt kills this Finnegan effigy during the presentation [MTM B11:351]. After returning from the Fifth Contingency, Duffey identifies the dead painter as Jacob Soule and realizes that he has brought this death back with him [MTM B12:355].","citations":["MTM B11:347","MTM B11:351","MTM B12:355"],"tags":["New Orleans","Finnegan","Visual Arts","Theater","Identity","Death & Mortality","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Devil (Papadiabolous I; The Devil Himself; Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch Papadiabolous Chortovitch; Bogovitch; Son of the Devil; brother of Gregory McColum/Papadiabolous II; Gregory McIfreann Papadiabolous; Sanditen; Chrysostom; Protagoras; Hochstruwelpieter-Goltz)","definition":"This inherited heading combines the elder Ifreann with his son Gregory McIfreann. See <em>Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch</em> for the father. See <em>Papadiabolous (I) (Gregory McIfreann Papadiabolous, Papadiabolous I)</em> for the Asiatic son killed by Seaworthy. See <em>Papadiabolous (II) (Gregory McColum Papadiabolous, Papadiabolous II, Papa Devil, Papa D, Noonan)</em> for the Irish son who impersonates his murdered brother. These are three distinct people in X’s account [D:277-281] [D:285-289].","citations":["D:277-281","D:285-289"],"tags":["The Devil","Devils (Characters)","Family","Identity","Crime","Death & Mortality","Ships","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Devil (Papadiabolous II; Gregory McColum Papadiabolous; Noonan; Flaherty; Christy; Clarenden; Cobbett; Malory; Ingraham; John Gregory; N; the Irish cop; The Tiger)","definition":"Gregory McColum Papadiabolous, the Irish son in X's genealogy, born in Cork to the same father as Gregory McIfreann, Papadiabolous I. X calls them twins while specifying different mothers and birthplaces. His aliases include Noonan, Flaherty, Christy, Clarenden, Cobbett, Malory, Ingraham and Arbuthnot; John Gregory can designate either brother [D:285]. X knows him as N, the undercover Irish cop [D:287]. He is a large and powerful man, comparatively good, whose earlier work as gentleman burglar, contrabandist, swindler and forger equips him for police work. The brothers share build, coloring and skull structure, but their natural fleshed faces differ [D:285-286].\n\nHe assumes his murdered brother's place the next morning and carries the impersonation through the voyage [D:288-289]. The frightening presence in the club is already this apparent return from death [D:15]; later Finnegan challenges the mask and asks how he can deceive long-standing associates so completely [D:87-88]. Papa answers questions with evasions, calling himself lamb-like when accused of killing Dopey [D:89]. He initially suspects Finnegan of Don Lewis's murder, but recognizes that Finnegan lacks the necessary strength [D:110-111]. At Naxos he is shot repeatedly and bludgeoned, his pockets emptied or cut out and his shoes removed. Finnegan recognizes the true face [D:146]. X identifies that face as Noonan or Flaherty and calls him an extraordinary mimic and a valuable ally [D:154-155]. X later attributes the killing to Seaworthy, who failed to recognize the unmasked man as his shipboard Papa Devil [D:289].","citations":["D:285","D:287","D:285-286","D:288-289","D:15","D:87-88","D:89","D:110-111","D:146","D:154-155","D:289"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Identity","Family","Ireland","Crew & Sailors","Ships","Crime","Finnegan","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Devil's Tetragrammaton","definition":"The design Duffey reports seeing cut onto Sebastian Hilton’s chest by the Three Slant-Faced Men in a transport or detached experience. When Duffey later examines Sebastian’s body, he finds that Sebastian died of plague and that the same design is formed by plague sores, not knife cuts. Duffey uses the discrepancy while discussing the Slant-Faced Men’s winders and the records they play [MTM B7:226].","citations":["MTM B7:226"],"tags":["The Devil","Magic & Sorcery","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Diabolicus II, Papa","definition":"Unattested variant heading in the earlier glossary. See <em>Papadiabolous (II) (Gregory McColum Papadiabolous, Papadiabolous II, Papa Devil, Papa D, Noonan)</em> for the Irish policeman Gregory McColum [D:285].","citations":["D:285"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Identity"]},{"name":"Diana Artemis","definition":"Diana, equated with the Greek Artemis, goddess of the hunt and associated with the moon. The narrator calls Diana Artemis a bringer of light when comparing Anastasia's head with a painted marble image reportedly seen by Pausanius at Messene in the sanctuary of Asclepius, or with a cypress-wood image on a temple step at Delos [D:133].","citations":["D:133"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Greece","Women Characters","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Dillard, Dandy Dan Dillard","definition":"Prizefighter who faces Duffey in the third preliminary bout on a Monday night bill, a four-round contest [MTM B2:50]. Duffey experiences it as an unmerciful beating equivalent to four hours of Hell, yet learns that he won the first three rounds widely and was well ahead in the fourth. The fight is stopped because Dillard is out on his feet and in danger, while Duffey is unmarked; Duffey's memory of being beaten then fades. Dillard is left a livid hulk. The experience draws Duffey into boxing, particularly promotion, which he takes up within six months [MTM B2:51].","citations":["MTM B2:50","MTM B2:51"],"tags":["Duffey","Combat","Occupations","Paradox"]},{"name":"Dimbeau, Cyril","definition":"French scientist who, with his brother Cyrus, recovers the Sword and Scabbard of Saint Sécaire from beneath the seabed at the Nine Mile Depth [MTM B13:422]. He dismisses Duffey and Brannagan as extinct sea creatures and encourages testing Duffey’s warning about the sword. When Cyrus draws it an inch and ten assistants fall into deep sleep, Cyril urges him to draw it completely, despite Duffey’s warning that this would end the world [MTM B13:423]. Brannagan seals the sword in its scabbard again; Duffey drops it back into the sea. Brannagan’s Forgetfulness Mesh leaves the bathysphere party unable to remember the episode [MTM B13:424].","citations":["MTM B13:422","MTM B13:423","MTM B13:424"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Science & Technology","Family","Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Sea"]},{"name":"Dimbeau, Cyrus","definition":"French scientist who, with his brother Cyril, recovers the Sword and Scabbard of Saint Sécaire from beneath the seabed at the Nine Mile Depth [MTM B13:422]. Ignoring Duffey’s warning, he draws the sword one inch from its scabbard, and the ten assistants restraining Duffey and Brannagan fall into deep sleep. Cyril urges him to draw it completely, although Duffey has warned that this would end the world [MTM B13:423]. Brannagan seals the sword in its scabbard again, and Duffey drops it back into the sea. Cyrus proposes recovering it once more, but Brannagan’s Forgetfulness Mesh prevents the bathysphere party from remembering the episode [MTM B13:424].","citations":["MTM B13:422","MTM B13:423","MTM B13:424"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Science & Technology","Family","Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Sea"]},{"name":"Dinah Joyce","definition":"One of the college and university people in Duffey's North Chicago golden-age circle, listed with David Joyce [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Diocletian","definition":"Roman emperor, reigning AD 284-305. The narrator lists him among people whose lives supposedly include seven hidden or dark years, alongside Caesar, Boëthus and others [MTM B2:72]. Diocletian is also one of Gautier's goats, counted among the clever animals that understand Finnegan when he talks to them [A:196] [A:199-200].","citations":["MTM B2:72","A:196","A:199-200"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Minor Characters","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Dionysus","definition":"Greek god of wine and ecstatic intoxication, one of the mythological identities assigned to Finnegan alongside Ulysses and the Terras or Arracht identity [A:39]. Finnegan's early description compares his loose strength and frightening speed to Dionysus before puffiness from indulgence; the lightness and grace in the same sentence belong to the separate Mercury comparison [A:6].","citations":["A:39","A:6"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Finnegan","Alcohol","Identity"]},{"name":"Dirty Five, The","definition":"Mystic brotherhood of Henry F. Salvatore, also Euphemus; John G. Schultz, Hans or Orpheus; Vincent J. Stranahan, Meleager; Kasmir W. Szymansky, Casey or Peleus; and John A. Solli, Finnegan or Iason. The narration gives them earlier lives and multiple mythological identities, describes their shared knowledge and ambitions, and says amnesia separates them from earlier greatness [A:38-39]. Henry is the fifth member and the fat Frenchman who refuses the Australian furlough; Casey is called the fourth [A:18] [A:38].\n\nCasey's interest in languages leads to his acceptance by John Schultz and entry into the group [A:159]. Loy's letter asks for the Dirty Fiver whom she and Margaret have not met, meaning Casey [A:27]. Casey later tells Finnegan private dreams he would not tell the others [A:161]. Hans, Henry and Finnegan solemnly shake hands after their companions fall asleep, as though forming a mystic trio within the Five [A:69]. It is Dotty who enjoys her introduction to the order during the St. Louis visit [A:176].\n\nA banner in Henry's dream echoes the group's high motto, <em>Ubicumque fuerit corpus illic congregabuntur et aquilas</em>, concerning the eagles gathering at the body [A:44]. The poem opening Vincent's chapter remembers their sea-kingdom and sailing the ship anciently called <em>Argo</em> [A:91]. Finnegan later belongs with Le Marin, Don Barnaby and Johnny Duckwalk to a dark counterpart of the Dirty Five [A:188]. In <em>More Than Melchisedech</em>, Duffey describes his wider set of Animated Marvels as involving both human and Teras elements [MTM B5:169-170].","citations":["A:38-39","A:18","A:38","A:159","A:27","A:161","A:69","A:176","A:44","A:91","A:188","MTM B5:169-170"],"tags":["Mysticism","Argonautica","Argonauts (Characters)","Clubs & Societies","Identity","History & Memory","Dreams","Language & Words","Ships"]},{"name":"Disembodied Brain","definition":"Name Aga Gonof reportedly uses for Charlotte Garfield, according to Charlotte's account to Duffey [MTM B3:84].","citations":["MTM B3:84"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Doekvalk (aka Johnny Duckwalk)","definition":"Spelling of Johnny Duckwalk’s name on his ship’s papers. See <em>Duckwalk, Johnny (aka Doekvalk)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Minor Characters","Caribbean","Alcohol","Crime","Sin & Redemption","Identity","Finnegan","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Doki, Joseph Cardinal","definition":"Cardinal of Douala, one of the thirteen men listed by X as John Mogul's contracted assassination targets [MB:571]. His double is John Giwa of Anecho [MB:579]. When Giwa arrives at Mooring-Rock, Doki is expressly described as dead [MB:596].","citations":["MB:571","MB:579","MB:596"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia","Minor Characters","Crime","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Dolphin (talking)","definition":"An ancestral dolphin who accompanies the yacht carrying Bolo Manolo, Douglas McAfee and Lloyd Cardigan-Pembroke toward Mooring-Stone. The men communicate with him using coded apparatus lowered overboard. He knows the Covenant and says dolphins expected it to be made with them but accepted its being made with humans instead. His fellows are towing their own bell, said to be the world's largest, toward the mooring place [MB:594]. He corrects Manolo's belief that Blood-of-Martyrs Grass is extinct, reporting its new growth on the stone and the rising water temperature and land. He then arranges the Dolphin Bell, whose underwater sound the men feel an hour later [MB:595].","citations":["MB:594","MB:595"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Ships","History & Memory","Ekklesia","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Don Juan","definition":"Figure in Hans Schultz's account of dark doubles. He says Don Juan arranged for a shoddy double to suffer Hell in his place, but was himself the shoddy double of a more elegant sinner and was trapped into suffering for that man [MTM B6:186].","citations":["MTM B6:186"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Folklore","Identity","Literature"]},{"name":"Don Lewis","definition":"Crewman aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em>, sharing quarters with Finnegan and Joe Cross; dark, handsome and called the ship's most handsome man [D:30-31]. His campfire story strongly identifies him with PFC Timothy Lorrigan of Boston, although he initially withholds which of three missing soldiers he was and then tries to retract his revealing first-person slip [D:44-45]. He tells how he buried the man he calls Yokipoki, mistook rats disturbing the body for a return from death, and subsequently became a furtive man under another name [D:43-48].\n\nThe narrative calls him a coward playing a daring game: he secretly organizes resistance aboard the ship, helps bring in Joe Cross, Manuel, Anastasia and Finnegan, and helps arrange the apparent resurrection of the Devil without understanding every part of it [D:105]. He is hunted through Tangier and killed by Marie Courtois, whose strength enables her to throttle him and break his neck. Finnegan finds the robbed body at the bottom of the tell and buries it in clean sand under creepers [D:105-113]. Don Barnaby, a different later companion, disconcertingly seems to carry some of Don Lewis's memories [D:265].","citations":["D:30-31","D:44-45","D:43-48","D:105","D:105-113","D:265"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Ships","Finnegan","Crime","Death & Mortality","Identity","The Dead"]},{"name":"Don Vincent de Ollos","definition":"One of the earlier identities assigned to Vincent Stranahan, listed with Mercurius and Austin [A:39].","citations":["A:39"],"tags":["Folklore","Identity"]},{"name":"Donovan, Dan / Nan","definition":"Dan and Nan Donovan, counted among the ecclesial or churchy people in Duffey's North Chicago golden-age circle [MTM B3:99]. Both are listed among the company at Duffey's establishment when Morris Poor challenges his magical powers, leading to the demonstration with the airborne mule [MTM B4:128-132].","citations":["MTM B3:99","MTM B4:128-132"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Duffey","Magic & Sorcery","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Donovan, Dry Dennis","definition":"Marie Monaghan's uncle Dry Dennis Donovan, called Dryden because he is always dry [A:28].","citations":["A:28"],"tags":["Family","Minor Characters","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Doofey","definition":"Mr. X's name for Melchisedech Duffey, used during his introductory telephone call and their conversations about the boxes and the conspiracy [A:135-136] [A:139]. X also uses it when presenting the boxes and urn in <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> [MTM B6:201].","citations":["A:135-136","A:139","MTM B6:201"],"tags":["Duffey","Identity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Doppio di Pinne (Dopey the Seaman; Finnegan's fetch)","definition":"Seaman known as Dopey, intended to replace Finnegan by assuming his papers and person after Finnegan's death, but apparently killed instead [D:51]. He occupies Finnegan's bunk for the preceding two days [D:63]. Joe Cross reports Papa Devil entering the cabin, Dopey's screams ending abruptly, and the room then containing only Papa and a small dribble of blood, with no possible exit for Dopey [D:64]. Finnegan and Dopey resemble one another in size, shape and coloring, especially when seen sideways [D:65]. Dopey's writings express both ambition and fear that he, rather than Finnegan, will be trapped and killed; Finnegan continues to feel his invisible presence in the bunk [D:78].\n\nPapa Devil calls him Finnegan's double or fetch but denies killing him, even suggesting that Finnegan may be Dopey [D:89]. Papa later says that Dopey shares the strange blood, is a sometime killer, and was summoned by Seaworthy to kill Papadiabolous; Seaworthy mistook Finnegan for him. Papa leaves open whether he or Finnegan killed Dopey, or whether the fetch was absorbed into Finnegan [D:121-122]. Finnegan subsequently shaves Dopey's face in his mirror; the dazed face says it is not dead [D:187]. Patricia also tells Dotty that Dopey is her brother and the same person as Finnegan, who sometimes became Doppio in childhood [A:174]. Finnegan later thinks of his fetch as having died in his place, either aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em> at Papadiabolous's hands or later on Galveston Island in Seaworthy's trap [A:278].","citations":["D:51","D:63","D:64","D:65","D:78","D:89","D:121-122","D:187","A:174","A:278"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Ships","Finnegan","Crime","Death & Mortality","Identity","Ghosts & Spirits","The Dead"]},{"name":"Dordogne the Talking Oak","definition":"The Talking Oak associated with the <em>Argo</em>. A piece in the ship's wheel recounts the ship's history when questioned [MTM B13:404]. Teresa Piccone says the talismans were pieces of the <em>Argo</em>'s gold filigree laid over pieces of Dordogne, with the gold coming from the Great Fleece. She explains their function as awakening and recollection, restoring the company to knowledge of themselves and one another [MTM B5:178-179]. When the Masters pretend not to recognize X, he asks them to consult the talking oak in the wheel, confident that the ship will know him [MTM B13:434].","citations":["MTM B13:404","MTM B5:178-179","MTM B13:434"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Argonautica","Ships","Duffey","Minor Characters","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Dorn, Enos","definition":"One of the sporty people in Duffey's North Chicago golden-age circle [MTM B3:99]. He is also among the old friends attending Duffey's later party with Casey of the Zodiac [MTM B12:383].","citations":["MTM B3:99","MTM B12:383"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Dorotea","definition":"Name used for Dotty, Dorothy Yekouris, when Elena writes to her by interpreting Finnegan's song of desolation, the empty island and his longing for her arrival [A:275-276]. The eleventh chapter of <em>Archipelago</em> is titled “Crotolo or Dorotea” [A:269]; Crotolo belongs to the family name claimed by the killer Niccolo, rather than being another name for Dotty [A:279]. In <em>More Than Melchisedech</em>, a Cuban informant calls the woman who had the sleeping Finnegan placed in a sea-door tomb La Dorotea. He says she remained three weeks, died from the effects of a gunshot wound, and was buried in the San Francisco cemetery because her medallion identified her with the Third Order of St. Francis [MTM B12:372].","citations":["A:275-276","A:269","A:279","MTM B12:372"],"tags":["Women Characters","Finnegan","Music","Identity","Death & Mortality","Religious Orders"]},{"name":"Dorothy","definition":"Red-headed town girl present with Virginia at the picnic held at the Bay Hotel [A:29].","citations":["A:29"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Dotty","definition":"Nickname of Dorothy Mary Yekouris, Finnegan's beloved from New Orleans [A:92] [A:95]. She is also addressed as Dot by Patricia, Finnegan's sister, in the passage excerpted as an epigraph in <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> [A:174-175] [D:18-19]. She works with Duffey on the Pelican Press papers and <em>The Bark</em> [MTM B7:212] [MTM B7:215].","citations":["A:92","A:95","A:174-175","D:18-19","MTM B7:212","MTM B7:215"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Women Characters","New Orleans","Journalism","Identity"]},{"name":"Doucet, Therese","definition":"Casting director of <em>Seven Roads</em>, the girls' play about the Patriarch Melchisedech's seven possible futures. She accepts Duffey for the role when he offers to play Melchisedech; Cleo Mahoney supplies the script as its playwright [MTM B9:265]. Therese is among the Ursuline Academy girls whom Duffey meets at the Ghost Coffee Shop before the planned performance in the school auditorium [MTM B10:293].","citations":["MTM B9:265","MTM B10:293"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters","Theater","Time & Eternity","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Drexel, Monica","definition":"Sometimes Charley Murray's girl, one of the two Monicas at Duffey's farewell supper at Meinkmuellers. The other is Charley's sister Monica Murray. Duffey confuses them because their names and appearances are alike, apparently giving a talisman first to the wrong Monica before the other recognizes it as hers [MTM B2:69]. A girl called Monica also accompanies Charley at Beth Keegan's balloon dinner party [MTM B2:65].","citations":["MTM B2:69","MTM B2:65"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters","Magic & Sorcery","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Duckhunter, Crissie","definition":"George Duckhunter's wife in “The Casey Machine.” She calls the machines unspeakably vile and wants George and Newton Prescott to stop dealing with them even if they return [CM:503]. Distressed by their continuing wealth, she wishes they could shut off the money and become poor and honest; George replies that they were never either [CM:504].","citations":["CM:503","CM:504"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Money & Trade","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Duckhunter, George","definition":"Crissie Duckhunter's husband and an associate of Newton Prescott in “The Casey Machine.” He calls the machines gold mines and argues that if their vileness cannot be stopped, money can at least be made from it [CM:503]. He says that he and Crissie were never poor or honest, that he cannot shut off the continuing millions, and that they are being paid as guardians of some kind. He cannot clearly remember the circumstances and believes Crissie and Prescott suffer the same gaps [CM:504].","citations":["CM:503","CM:504"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Money & Trade","Sin & Redemption","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Duckwalk, Johnny (aka Doekvalk)","definition":"Johnny Duckwalk, whose ship's papers spell the name Doekvalk. He is one of the four companions in Basse-Terre Paradise with Finnegan, Le Marin and Don Barnaby, and a champion drinker [D:236]. The narrator calls him a prince of a fellow and excuses his daytime sleep by his activity at night [D:238]. He accuses Le Marin of passing off a counterfeit <em>Marie Celeste</em> story [D:246]. He also has a mean streak: each month he steals the gold sovereign Angela Cosquin has just deposited underwater, leaving the other treasure alone [D:263].\n\nHis subsequent sickness of repentance makes him leave Paradise to confess a previously unnoticed appropriation of money and serve an expected six to ten years. He later writes from prison urging the others to leave the island [D:264-265]. The confessed offence is not identified as his thefts from Angela. He belongs with Finnegan, Le Marin and Don Barnaby to the dark counterpart of the Dirty Five [A:188].","citations":["D:236","D:238","D:246","D:263","D:264-265","A:188"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Caribbean","Alcohol","Crime","Sin & Redemption","Identity","Finnegan","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Duffey, Letitia (Koch)","definition":"Melchisedech Duffey's wife, Lily Koch's younger sister and the daughter of August and Elinore Koch [MTM B3:92] [MTM B3:98]. Duffey recognizes his sought-for joyful moth, <em>tinea letitia</em>, in her. She is Lily's partner at Koch's Galleries, retaining her half-interest after marriage; she has already anticipated their home and made its arrangements before Duffey recognizes her [MTM B3:91-92]. Charlotte calls her partly Duffey's creation through the rib-shaped talisman, a bonus outside his twelve prime creations [MTM B3:93]. Letitia also claims creative power herself, telling Lily that she projected the Countess into Chicago and treating reality as subjective projections [MTM B3:96].\n\nShe has abundant hair between blonde, scarlet and walnut, a ruddy complexion, a high-comedy look, sea-green-to-blue eyes and a full-bodied build; she is taller than Duffey [MTM B3:92]. Her fine scanning eye and haunting voice make her reading compelling, and her recorded time-and-temperature advertisements move listeners [MTM B4:114]. Her prescience helps her understand the Mullens murders and Charlotte's escape [MTM B3:97-98]. She and Duffey are central figures and, in a sense, co-creators of the North Chicago golden age [MTM B3:98]. The narrator says that they design their neighborhood, events, life scenarios and people, and calls Letitia one of Duffey’s creations, with an even stronger gift for creating people and their surroundings [MTM B3:100].\n\nIn New Orleans she arrives with their best possessions and enough cash to sustain their work [MTM B6:198]. The narrator describes her later beauty as proto-legendary, her welcome and affection illuminating the establishments [MTM B7:211]. After Dotty's disappearance she edits <em>The Bark</em>, maintaining it from private funds while other Pelican Press accounts decline [MTM B8:238-239]. Her sudden death leaves Duffey distraught; after protesting to a burning bush, he grudgingly accepts its explanation [MTM B8:253]. Though the death had been foreknown for years, its suddenness overwhelms him [MTM B8:254].","citations":["MTM B3:92","MTM B3:98","MTM B3:91-92","MTM B3:93","MTM B3:96","MTM B4:114","MTM B3:97-98","MTM B3:100","MTM B6:198","MTM B7:211","MTM B8:238-239","MTM B8:253","MTM B8:254"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Family","New Orleans","Journalism","Visual Arts","Prophecy & Visions","Creation","Death & Mortality","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Duffey, Melchisedech","definition":"<p><strong>Identity and appearance.</strong> Magus, art dealer, writer and one of the Masters of the <em>Argo</em>. He identifies himself as the Boy King and King of Salem [MTM B1:15] [MTM B5:166] [MTM B13:402]. He has sorrel hair, fire-blue eyes, a solid but not oversized body, and a mouth that may begin grinning before his eyes. He habitually bangs his hands together and addresses people and even his own clay creatures with promises of immediate action [MTM B1:6]. He begins growing a beard at fifteen to look older [MTM B2:38]; in New Orleans he keeps it white and bright and wears an artist’s tie and beret for his trade [A:143]. His voice is Boston Irish with a touch of stage Yiddish. <em>Archipelago</em> calls him a patriarch without seed, a prophet without honor and a high-sounding brawler whose apparently universal knowledge is shared by others in the company [A:97]. The later narrator describes his uncertain age, wilfulness and spaciousness, and the unexplained restraints upon him [MTM B11:312]. His names include Michael, according to a relative whose account he disputes; Mikey; Melky; Mike Duffey on his seaman’s papers; and Melchisedech Himself [MTM B1:7] [MTM B1:9] [MTM B1:30] [MTM B12:369] [MTM B13:402]. Casey gives another modern account: Michael is his given name, and Melchisedech his confirmation name, received when confirmed at forty [A:42]. In the postscript Duffey signs himself the fifty-ninth King of Salem and says that he has always known of fifty-eight predecessors [PS:478]. He also passes through cycles of renewed youth; Stein accuses him of whitening his hair and beard to conceal their return to black [MTM B11:317-319].</p><p><strong>Childhoods and kindred.</strong> Duffey remembers simultaneous twentieth-century childhoods in Iowa, St. Louis and Boston, behind which stands an older, continuing life as the Boy King [MTM B1:6-7] [MTM B1:15-18]. Seven Iowa places dispute his birthplace: Minden, Underwood, Beebee Town, Neola, Crescent, Avoca and Union Township. A relative says that his mother died when he was five, that he lived with kindred until twelve, and that he then entered boarding schools. These kindred include the Duffeys, Kellys, Byrnes, McGuires, Crooks, Bagbys, Haleys, Healeys, Haydens, Kanes, Whites, Hughes, Kennedys, Thompsons and Clancys. Duffey disputes both this genealogy and the name Michael, claiming to have been born without parents when already five years old [MTM B1:7-8]. One proposed explanation of the overlapping childhoods is his appropriation of his brother’s and sister’s experiences. The St. Louis childhood is associated particularly with the sister’s life, and an analyst concludes that Duffey was himself a girl during part of his childhood [MTM B1:15-16]. These explanations do not cancel the narrative’s account of the ancient Boy King, whose tents, flocks, gardens and powers are presented as more substantial than his modern surroundings [MTM B1:17-18].</p><p><strong>Schooling and formation.</strong> Sent to boarding school at twelve, he spends four high-school years in more than one institution. The uncertain locations include Creighton, Loras, Rockhurst and a boys’ school near St. Louis University; the narrator says that at least two of these places are involved [MTM B1:20] [MTM B1:24]. Charley Murray, John Rattigan and Sebastian Hilton are his principal companions. A skilled carpenter, machinist and maker of molds and patterns, he makes Murray’s conjuring props, using his giants when ordinary tools cannot suffice [MTM B1:21-23]. He trades with Rattigan and appropriates his business judgment, Murray’s good nature and honesty, and Sebastian’s intelligence and taste [MTM B1:25-26]. An older priest accepts his account of being a magus and warns him against pride. Lily Koch’s art sales, and his reading of her mind and Sebastian’s, establish his lifelong vocation as an art expert [MTM B1:28-29].</p><p><strong>Powers and their limits.</strong> His gifts include producing gold, entering other minds, moving within time, commanding invisible giants and forming persons through talismans; the last is initially called his greatest gift [MTM B1:5]. He appropriates the perceptions and memories of other children, sometimes knowing households he has never visited [MTM B1:16-17]. At Lake Manawa he believes that good divers return from the water as evil replacements; his mental effort to keep one underwater ends in a drowning, described as his first killing [MTM B1:8-9]. As Boy King he can remain invisible beneath an urchin disguise, summon giants, heal and animate birds [MTM B1:18]. His gold appears as sparks or coins as well as bars and engraved objects [MTM B1:5] [MTM B2:38-39]. His first major loss is the facility for making money, which never fully returns. This coincides with damage to the Pont du Sable Traffic Trestle, a bridge whose failing supports become an image of his own losses; he nevertheless retains substantial wealth [MTM B4:109-110]. The gifts can diminish, be forgotten or fail to achieve what he expects. His encounter with the art giants teaches him that he will more often recognize great art than make it [MTM B2:54]. His prescience and his belief that he causes persons to exist are closely connected; he later loses the effective bestowal of talismans for a period of his life [MTM B4:120]. In another episode he returns to yesterday to meet Finnegan in the burial cave [MTM B12:374].</p><p><strong>St. Louis.</strong> At fifteen, on the last day of May 1915, Duffey sets out on his own after selling his school businesses [MTM B2:38]. He establishes the Rounders’ Club, with food, drink, lodging, transport, music, games and imported goods, and studies at several institutions while finding more hours of activity than an ordinary day permits [MTM B2:49]. Charley Murray becomes half-owner and his business partner [MTM B2:53]. Duffey boxes, wins seven of his first nine fights, and turns to promoting fights; he also becomes the band’s banjo player [MTM B2:50-54]. Bascom Bagby is his friend, business associate and quasi-brother. Mary Louise says that Bagby shares their person; Duffey later calls him his dark object [MTM B2:61-62] [MTM B2:73]. Mary Louise Byrne is Duffey’s sister or stepsister, and their relationship includes an erotic attachment. She introduces Beth Keegan as a substitute beloved [MTM B2:54-56]. When Duffey returns from his lost years, Mary Louise has married Bagby [MTM B2:73]. Duffey joins, or in another version founds, the World-Wide Argonauts Argo Balloon Club. His balloon becomes the <em>Argo Twelve and a Half</em> after a powerful visitor explains that the actual twelfth voyage is still active and the balloon may serve as its pinnace. Bagby later reports ghostly excursions in it, which Duffey accepts [MTM B2:63-64] [MTM B2:66].</p><p><strong>Marriage, Chicago and performance.</strong> A compelling moth-call brings Duffey from Omaha to Chicago in 1925. Charlotte Garfield, an adult confidence woman with a child’s appearance, helps him find the woman who will become his wife; he uses his mind-reading powers to assist her search for Aga Gonof’s hidden stash [MTM B3:77-80] [MTM B3:84] [MTM B3:88-89]. Duffey marries Letitia Koch, Lily Koch’s sister, whose formation involved the special rib-shaped talisman he had given Lily in their schooldays [MTM B1:29-30] [MTM B3:90-93]. Their rooms and shops become the heart of a near-north-side Chicago golden age. They design its people, settings and events together, with Letitia said to possess the gift even more strongly [MTM B3:100]. His Priceless Item Room houses seven representations of Christ collected and sent home during the lost years, joined by an eighth brought by August Koch [MTM B3:103-104]. Duffey and Letitia give puppet shows: Letitia works made puppets, while Duffey calls out local mice and directs them as costumed actors, accompanying the performances with his banjo [MTM B4:133-134]. His supposedly last public magic act, dated November 13, 1935, is the transport of a mule through the air; witnesses and photographs offer conflicting accounts of the giant hands involved [MTM B4:127-132]. Dr. Morris Poor challenges his powers and calls him insane [MTM B4:128]. Dr. Saul Rafelson analyzes him without declaring him cured. Rafelson’s catalogue includes divine or golden childhood, mastery of giants, kingship, successive and parallel lives, inner monsters and shadows, the power to confer life, and the belief that the world depends on him. The doctor nevertheless acknowledges extraordinary facts about Duffey and the persons said to be his creations [MTM B4:146-147].</p><p><strong>Casey and the <em>Crock</em>.</strong> Duffey is a part-time partner of Casey’s father and a principal influence on Casey’s education. Casey learns more from his bookshelves than at school, and learns Yiddish, Hebrew and Irish from him. Before entering the army, Casey moves his college <em>Crock</em> to a little press in the back room of Duffey’s bookstore [A:156] [A:159] [A:167-168]. The narrator calls Duffey closer to Casey than Casey’s father, though he seems to shrug off Casey’s later hostile editorial [A:131]. Duffey edits the <em>Crock</em> while Casey serves in the army; after its takeover and Casey’s return he is excluded from it [MTM B4:138] [MTM B4:145]. The attempted takeover exposes compromising photographs of his conduct with adult women and with girls from the age of eight or ten. He disputes some appearances, calls one photograph an entrapment, and says that guilty acts have been confessed and absolved; he refuses the blackmailers’ demand for control [MTM B4:139-141]. In his wartime letter to Casey he reports that the pressure temporarily drove his wife away and left him with a broken nose and right hand, and warns Casey against gambling any stock [A:40]. The Chicago double Mike Melchiades, whose marriage suffers from acts he denies committing, insists that Duffey is only his spinoff. Duffey suspects that this may be true, and helps the double’s wife return to him while knowing that Letitia is leaving their own home for a time [MTM B4:141-144]. Casey describes Duffey as the sole member of the Catholic Center Party of America, a Centrist who favors small government and opposes what he sees as collectivist infiltration; his index lists several thousand alleged infiltrators in Catholic lay groups [A:42-43]. He writes most of the issue described in <em>Archipelago</em>, including material identifiable as his in “Moles at the Grass Roots,” “Fables in Fabianism” and “The Consumer, the Cooperative, and the Coefficient of Cost Constancy.” That issue also contains the “Urbe et Orbe” notes, the “Judgement Day” review section, recording reviews favoring Chicago jazz and a readers’ letter forum [A:41]. His <em>Razzle Daz</em> comic strip uses Finnegan, Hans and Show Boat among its models [MTM B5:159]. Casey’s <em>Answer</em> is printed on Duffey’s press, but Duffey is initially unaware of its authorship and publication [MTM B4:113-118]. </p><p><strong>New Orleans, commerce and charity.</strong> He comes to New Orleans to put Henry’s ideas into practice. Commercial printing is to support the crusading journal, with work on it continuing after the paid working day. Mrs. Duffey sells their Chicago home and household goods and brings eighteen hundred of his books; the narrator says that he commits all he has [A:117-119]. In New Orleans Duffey helps establish the Pelican Press to publish the <em>Bark</em>, operates the Walk-In Art Bijou and a pawnshop, and makes and plays recorder flutes [MTM B6:190] [MTM B6:199] [MTM B7:211]. With Stein and Gabrielovitch he starts the <em>Investigator</em>; he writes for both journals and sells books, antiques and works by the Studios and Pirates’ Alley painters. His safe holds cash, valuable art, including authentic Van Ghis, and the Infiltrates file [A:143]. He supplies the cards on which X adds information to that file [A:137]. A poem in <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> is attributed to Melchisedech Duffy’s <em>The Alien Awakens</em> [D:197]. He insists upon a flophouse and turns Zabotski’s thousand-gallon crock into a soup service, installing gas burners, obtaining a blessing from St. Katherine’s and raising money for barley; Dotty pays the gas and water bills. <em>Archipelago</em> gives an initial cost of one cent a bowl, eventually about three hundred meals daily with continuous staffing, and a retrospective duration of at least a quarter-century [A:126] [A:132-134]. <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> instead describes growth from six recipients to about six hundred after a week and then several thousand. Medical students’ apparent planting of human bones gives the pot its name, Cannibal Duffey’s Irish Restaurant [MTM B6:197]. About the mid-1950s his Bijou receives <em>The Resurrection of Count Finnegan</em>, with Finnegan and Joseph Hedayat pictured decades ahead and looking identical. He sells it to Hilary Hilton for twenty thousand dollars and continues displaying it at Hilton’s request [MB:550-554].</p><p><strong>The talismanic company.</strong> Duffey maintains that he literally helped form twelve persons, with others outside or partly within that count [MTM B5:150-151]. Henri Salvatore summons him to St. Louis for the last Saturday in May, promising the assignment for the rest of his life. There he encounters the brilliant company whose members know his <em>Crock</em> more readily than his kingship [MTM B5:152] [MTM B5:157-158]. The list announced as twelve names Finnegan (John Solli), Hans (John) Schultz, Henri Salvatore, Vincent Stranahan, Casey Szymansky, Dotty Yekouris, Mary Monaghan Schultz, Mary Virginia Schaeffer, Teresa Piccone, Absalom Stein and Mr. X: eleven names. Mary Catherine Carruthers is separately identified as another of the creations [MTM B5:158] [MTM B5:161-162]. Finnegan and Teresa are its two central creations [MTM B5:157] [MTM B5:162]; the narrator distinguishes Finnegan as the greatest from Teresa as the finest [MTM B5:182]. Duffey says he put unusual amounts of Teras blood into his people; Finnegan’s claim to be hollow troubles him [MTM B5:170-172].</p><p><strong>Creation, awakening and faith.</strong> The talismans are associated with the <em>Argo</em>’s gold filigree, the Talking Oak and the Golden Fleece [MTM B5:178]. Marie says that Duffey collects the companions and gives them settings and sparkle rather than making them; he disputes her account [MTM B5:159]. Teresa tells Duffey that he awakened the company rather than created it, calling its members back into bodies to oppose the released Devil [MTM B7:233-234]. Stein sometimes reverses the dependence entirely, proposing that they made Duffey into the magus who thinks he made them [MTM B10:304]. The <em>Argo</em> Masters are also described as an interdependent society whose members act upon one another [MTM B13:446]. Duffey nevertheless insists to the Devil that he can perform primary creation [MTM B10:303]. His artistic work includes perhaps ten thousand figures in clay and baked bread dough [MTM B7:228-229]. In his commentary on Casey he recalls Peleus as a good, brave companion on the <em>Argo</em>, and offers another explanation of his twentieth-century Animations: they serve much the same purpose as Gorshok’s artificial hosts for body lice. His creatures doubt the account, and he says he has forgotten any other purpose [PG:534-536]. A separately unlabelled passage among Casey’s collected writings proposes that Duffey’s talismans implanted the remembered ancient lives as educational devices [PG:545]. He seeks to join art, law, divine worship, kindness and charity in daily life [MTM B7:228-229]. He rejects ordinary mediocrity while insisting upon humility [A:143]. His subordination to Henry is nevertheless described as a punishment for pride: he must serve one of his own satellites [MTM B8:245]. X calls him a “toy patriarch,” fitted for crusading rather than secret work, who must find one greater than Melchisedech [A:265-266].</p><p><strong>The Seven Lost Years.</strong> Duffey disappears for seven years and returns in 1923, wandering among his old cities before returning to St. Louis. Eleven primary talisman children are born during his absence [MTM B2:71-74]. His claims of wartime army and ambulance service are uncertain; the narrator suggests that some experiences may have been taken from Sebastian Hilton, while witnesses also recall Duffey working with an organization named ARGO [MTM B2:72-73]. The <em>Argo</em> travels behind this interval, but its seven years need not follow consecutively in ordinary time [MTM B2:71] [MTM B13:406]. Absalom Stein's account places his death in Neustria and says that X later returns his ashes in a Spanish king’s old receptacle [MTM B6:185]. Duffey can keep these ashes beside him while alive in New Orleans [MTM B1:6]. His account of his ancient life explicitly rejects reincarnation, though he allows that his bodily history may include successive bodies or the renovation of one body [MTM B8:250]. He later remembers his modern lifetime as a sixty-year leave from the Holy Ship, beginning with his landing in 1923; he hopes to repeat it, while Cardinal Henri warns that repeated returns delay his arrival at the other shore [MTM B12:387].</p><p><strong>Contingencies and rival explanations.</strong> Stein considers both identification with the biblical Melchisedech and an unusual sharing of the ancient king’s memories; he also calls Duffey the man who does not have an ending. Mary Virginia conjectures that Duffey comes from a discarded creation. These are the speakers’ proposed explanations [MTM B10:291-292]. The same Stein passage alternately treats the released Devil as a minor decoy and as the Devil himself [MTM B10:291]. Seven principal contingencies confront Duffey when his life overruns the present and fractures [MTM B10:298-301]. He refuses to represent mankind in his meeting with the Devil, rejects the Devil’s demand for his services and faces the claimed mortgage upon his soul and body [MTM B10:301-303]. The seventh contingency presents an encounter with the invisible God and a possibility of personal disaster [MTM B10:301].</p><p><strong>Bereavement and continuing bonds.</strong> Bagby’s weekly letters continue for many years after his funeral, describing an existence outside ordinary time; Duffey rereads and savors them throughout each week [MTM B8:237] [MTM B8:248-249]. When Letitia dies suddenly, Duffey first refuses to accept it, then speaks privately with a burning Elephant Ear bush and grudgingly accepts the divine will [MTM B8:253-254]. Countess Margaret proposes marriage and a long life in Transylvania, and Lily offers marriage and renewed companionship. He postpones the Countess’s offer and tells Lily that he needs everyone [MTM B8:254-257].</p><p><strong>Later journeys and trials.</strong> In his account for <em>House and Home Happenings</em>, Duffey describes studying time with Zabotski and visiting the Pristine World for three or five years, returning in two days [MTM B9:267] [MTM B9:272-273] [MTM B9:284]. Duffey answers the ritual summons of the Royal Pop People, written on parchment in squid ink and nailed to his door [MTM B11:311-313]. In the fifth contingency he is seduced by the splendid people, abandons suffering friends, and only belatedly tears himself free by reversing his movement through time. Bagby condemns his pride and cowardice and demands that he recover his strength in the wilderness [MTM B12:353] [MTM B12:356-357]. After returning from the fifth contingency he fears that he has damaged time and discovers that Margaret Stone’s death has entered his own world [MTM B12:354]. In the crash year Mary Virginia takes him for a ghost; his withdrawal returns that year to the future [MTM B12:361]. He contributes magic to the intermission of the girls’ play and later travels with Carmelo Mendoza. Horace Pie's reference to Mr. Eggs alerts him that the disguised companion is X, though he subsequently claims that he always knows his own handiwork [MTM B12:366] [MTM B12:369-372]. At Hilary Hilton’s farewell party he compels Casey’s presence by projecting Adam Scanlon’s portrait of his head onto television [MTM B12:380-383]. At the end of Book Twelve, he turns the forbidden handle, falls from the green country with shattered bones into a dung heap, where vermin, dung rats, buzzards and blood snakes attack him, and discovers that God remains present in the tempest [MTM B12:392-393]. From the dung, broken objects and his own blood he joyfully begins making another company of Animated Marvels, aided by lightning. He chooses to sail again while keeping this work as his eventual delight; the last minute of his life is still unfinished [MTM B12:394-395].</p><p><strong>Death and the <em>Argo</em>.</strong> The three slant-faced attackers have stalked him since he was about six; a kit-fox helps him survive a childhood knifing. After the <em>Crock</em> assault he kills one of them, but their number is repeatedly restored [MTM B1:12] [MTM B1:19-20] [MTM B5:150] [MTM B7:225-227]. He identifies his priesthood with an obscure oriental rite that permits marriage [MTM B13:413-414]. As an <em>Argo</em> Master, Duffey cures a brilliant young man’s stutter at Weinsburg on the Nechar [MTM B13:430]. Three slant-faced killers strip the flesh from his bones and kill him; three slack-faced attendants burn the stripped flesh, sinew and viscera. His bones and ashes appear in Gur’yev, where X and Kasmir Gorshok recover them. X keeps the ashes, and the bones are taken aboard the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:442-443]. God tells him that his duties do not require a fleshed body. Casey makes a golden mask, scarf and gauntlets from the Fleece, and Duffey resumes his work as pilot [MTM B13:443-444]. When he moves into earlier portions of his life, he has flesh again while the ashes remain: the text calls this the Ambiguity of the Flesh. His Chicago and New Orleans lives consequently take on the character of incursions into the past [MTM B13:444-446]. With Brannagan and Gorshok he also tries to prevent the Antichrist from boarding the ship, holding that harmful prophecies must be resisted [MTM B13:454]. In the shimmering future their resistance fails: Casey appears as the Antichristus, the ship takes him to Habonim, and Duffey, Brannagan and Gunboat Smith remain undeceived but strongly affected. They fade out of that future without knowing whether another attempt is possible; Duffey has regained flesh [MTM B13:461-463]. His “Promontory Goats” commentary likewise foresees disputing Casey yet giving him passage against his will. He says that he has returned from the future and beyond death but is forbidden to disclose more [PG:537]. In <em>How Many Miles to Babylon</em>, the fleshless, golden-masked pilot carries Finnegan, Gilberto Levine-and-O’Brien and Herman Hercules toward Mooring-Stone, and later addresses the conclave that elects Finnegan pope [MB:589-590] [MB:597].</p><p><strong>Returns and the appended story.</strong> At twenty-three Duffey comes ashore from the Sea of Lost Years and meets a young double who tells him he has lived the lost years seven times and died seven different deaths, three ending in damnation. Duffey suggests that God employs magi to explore possible endings; the double disputes this and, after Duffey has left, admits uncertainty about whether he serves God or the Devil [MTM B13:465-466]. Another return places Duffey in St. Louis in 1923, where the child Teresa offers to give him a valid birth in the future, believing that one demonstrably real life will establish the reality of the others [MTM B13:470-473]. In the appended “Great Day in the Morning,” spelled Duffy, he encounters a world losing its containers and distinctions. Its catastrophe enacts the conditions of his own bitterly comic <em>Bark</em> article from a year before. Horrified that the world has taken the joke seriously, he appeals to laughter and considers another article to undo the result [GD:491-493]. He insists upon a substantial universe, talks with the damaged statue of St. Michael, and wrestles with Morpheus as they shrink into a self-swallowing single cell. Morpheus calls this a return to the beginning; the final verses then enact obliteration [GD:481-501]. Lafferty’s postscript describes these apparent endings as cross-sections of continuing lives on multiple tracks [PS:479-480]. Finnegan also names him among the friends of his normal life in <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> [D:90].</p>","citations":["MTM B1:15","MTM B5:166","MTM B13:402","MTM B1:6","MTM B2:38","A:143","A:97","MTM B11:312","MTM B1:7","MTM B1:9","MTM B1:30","MTM B12:369","A:42","PS:478","MTM B11:317-319","MTM B1:6-7","MTM B1:15-18","MTM B1:7-8","MTM B1:15-16","MTM B1:17-18","MTM B1:20","MTM B1:24","MTM B1:21-23","MTM B1:25-26","MTM B1:28-29","MTM B1:5","MTM B1:16-17","MTM B1:8-9","MTM B1:18","MTM B2:38-39","MTM B4:109-110","MTM B2:54","MTM B4:120","MTM B12:374","MTM B2:49","MTM B2:53","MTM B2:50-54","MTM B2:61-62","MTM B2:73","MTM B2:54-56","MTM B2:63-64","MTM B2:66","MTM B3:77-80","MTM B3:84","MTM B3:88-89","MTM B1:29-30","MTM B3:90-93","MTM B3:100","MTM B3:103-104","MTM B4:133-134","MTM B4:127-132","MTM B4:128","MTM B4:146-147","A:156","A:159","A:167-168","A:131","MTM B4:138","MTM B4:145","MTM B4:139-141","A:40","MTM B4:141-144","A:42-43","A:41","MTM B5:159","MTM B4:113-118","A:117-119","MTM B6:190","MTM B6:199","MTM B7:211","A:137","D:197","A:126","A:132-134","MTM B6:197","MB:550-554","MTM B5:150-151","MTM B5:152","MTM B5:157-158","MTM B5:158","MTM B5:161-162","MTM B5:157","MTM B5:162","MTM B5:182","MTM B5:170-172","MTM B5:178","MTM B7:233-234","MTM B10:304","MTM B13:446","MTM B10:303","MTM B7:228-229","PG:534-536","PG:545","MTM B8:245","A:265-266","MTM B2:71-74","MTM B2:72-73","MTM B2:71","MTM B13:406","MTM B6:185","MTM B8:250","MTM B12:387","MTM B10:291-292","MTM B10:291","MTM B10:298-301","MTM B10:301-303","MTM B10:301","MTM B8:237","MTM B8:248-249","MTM B8:253-254","MTM B8:254-257","MTM B9:267","MTM B9:272-273","MTM B9:284","MTM B11:311-313","MTM B12:353","MTM B12:356-357","MTM B12:354","MTM B12:361","MTM B12:366","MTM B12:369-372","MTM B12:380-383","MTM B12:392-393","MTM B12:394-395","MTM B1:12","MTM B1:19-20","MTM B5:150","MTM B7:225-227","MTM B13:413-414","MTM B13:430","MTM B13:442-443","MTM B13:443-444","MTM B13:444-446","MTM B13:454","MTM B13:461-463","PG:537","MB:589-590","MB:597","MTM B13:465-466","MTM B13:470-473","GD:491-493","GD:481-501","PS:479-480","D:90"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Argonauts (Characters)","Duffey","Argonautica","New Orleans","Visual Arts","Journalism","Music","Family","Identity","Creation","Time & Eternity","Death & Mortality","Theology"]},{"name":"Dugan, Delbert","definition":"Name into which John Rattigan is said to turn for a year or so during Duffey's boarding-school years, as Sebastian Hilton turns into Martin Troyat, preserving the group of four boys. Duffey and Charley Murray remain together throughout; Sebastian returns for the final year after attending school in Europe [MTM B1:24].","citations":["MTM B1:24"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Duke and Jenny Colfax","definition":"Theatrical people listed together among Duffey's North Chicago golden-age friends [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Theater"]},{"name":"Duke Colfax","definition":"Theatrical person listed with Jenny Colfax among Duffey's North Chicago golden-age friends [MTM B3:99]. See also <em>Duke and Jenny Colfax</em>.","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Theater"]},{"name":"Duke of Moule, The (Don Barnaby)","definition":"Don Barnaby, duke by nickname, one of Finnegan's three companions in the Basse-Terre Paradise with Charles Le Marin and Johnny Duckwalk [D:236]. A voice unlike his own comes from him while he sleeps, asking Finnegan to repay Anastasia fifty dollars owed at death [D:238]. He tells the company a story beginning with the varieties of joy pills [D:248]. After Duckwalk leaves, Barnaby also departs, promising to return, though Angela doubts him. He seems to carry Don Lewis's memories while being someone else; Angela reports his claim that he has not been that other Don for several years [D:265]. He belongs to the dark counterpart of the Dirty Five [A:188].","citations":["D:236","D:238","D:248","D:265","A:188"],"tags":["Caribbean","Finnegan","Minor Characters","Identity","Clubs & Societies","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Dutch Duquesne","definition":"A snapping-turtle-looking member of the Old Wooden Ship circle. He says he makes a hundred dollars nightly in tips as a waiter at the Balinese Room or another fashionable pier establishment, then loses the money at games. Mary, who works at the clubs, says he is actually a kitchen boy cleaning fish and shucking oysters, receives no tips and never has or loses more than five dollars [D:221].","citations":["D:221"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Occupations","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Dutch Eckel (Little)","definition":"See <em>Eckel, Little Dutch</em> [D:221] [MTM B13:458].","citations":["D:221","MTM B13:458"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Occupations","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Dutchman","definition":"Name used for Hans Schultz by Marie Monaghan when he quotes verse [A:9]. James or Jimmy Fitzjames also calls him Dutch and Dutchman, beginning with a request for a quarter to buy a book in New York [A:224-225].","citations":["A:9","A:224-225"],"tags":["Language & Words","Literature","Identity"]},{"name":"Dürer, Albrecht (1471--1528)","definition":"German Renaissance painter, printmaker and theorist whose work brought Northern European detail into dialogue with Italian concerns for proportion and perspective. His <em>Apocalypse</em> is a woodcut series; <em>Melencolia I</em> is an engraving. His theoretical publications treat measurement and geometry, fortification and human proportion. Patrick Stranahan includes Dürer with Watteau and Hogarth in his deliberately restricted list of painters worth owning, saying that one minor original and a dozen good copies are enough for an honest man's budget [A:109].","citations":["A:109"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Visual Arts","Germany","Italy"]},{"name":"Eabhraioch","definition":"Address used by Duffey to Zabotski during their bantering exchange in what Duffey calls the holy language: \"Have a care, Eabhraioch\" [MTM B11:320]. It apparently represents Irish <em>Eabhrach</em>, a Hebrew or Jew, though the novel's spelling differs from that dictionary form.","citations":["MTM B11:320"],"tags":["Ireland","Duffey","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Eads bridge (St. Louis)","definition":"Bridge across the Mississippi at St. Louis. Duffey, in what the narrator calls his fifth or sixth youth, walks from the river shore below it onto a low boat that is the <em>Argo</em> in disguise [MTM B2:71]. His eventual return from the Sea of Lost Years brings him to a pier below the bridge in 1923 [MTM B13:470], where the fishing boy identifies himself as ten-year-old Philip Stranahan [MTM B13:471]. The bridge marks both embarkation and return in Duffey's changing chronology.","citations":["MTM B2:71","MTM B13:470","MTM B13:471"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","Seas & Oceans","Duffey","Time & Eternity","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Earth","definition":"World whose history and workings receive several competing accounts. In Duffey's account of Zabotski and the Fundamentalers, Zabotski's shortened Pristine History resembles their chronology [MTM B9:278-280]. Zabotski says the Pristine and Everyday-or-Molasses worlds occupy the same space, but the Pristine World proceeds faster and excludes duplicate or non-valid people and situations [MTM B9:279]. Newton Prescott, speaking for an electronic community of minds, claims that earthly magnetism binds Earth to something beyond the solar system and contains buried memories and mysteries [CM:510]. Absalom Stein's notes date the Devil's release from his Crimean dungeon to 1946, ending a supposed thousand-year good era [MTM B6:185]. Hans Schultz invokes Coriolis as the great Earth force while explaining the direction of Australian horse races [A:4].","citations":["MTM B9:278-280","MTM B9:279","CM:510","MTM B6:185","A:4"],"tags":["Metaphysics","History & Memory","The Devil","Duffey"]},{"name":"Earth Creature","definition":"Teresa Piccone Stranahan's earthly aspect: the narrator imagines green moss growing in the corners of her eyes because she is an Earth Creature. The same passage gives her star-sparkle all over because she is also a Heavenly creature [MTM B5:180].","citations":["MTM B5:180"],"tags":["Women Characters","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Earth Hours","definition":"Appointments outside time described in one of Bascom Bagby's <em>Letters After I Am Dead</em>. During them the dead contemplate Earth and review its happenings with greater maturity and attention to particulars [MTM B8:248-249].","citations":["MTM B8:248-249"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Earthly one","definition":"The Terrestrial Paradise to which Finnegan and his friends travel, distinguished from the Heavenly Paradise. Its surviving snakes foreshadow their expulsion; the place is identified with Basse-Terre [D:234-235].","citations":["D:234-235"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Islands","Finnegan"]},{"name":"East","definition":"Direction taken by the <em>Argo</em> when its sea begins to change: unusually, it runs eastward with the wind and waves [MTM B13:459]. Other uses place Chodorlahomor's Elam or Susiana east of the Tigris [MTM B7:228], describe the Ohio River joining the Mississippi from the east [MTM B2:65], and locate the recovery of Duffey's bones and ashes at Gur'yev, called the East Mouth of the Ural [MTM B13:442].","citations":["MTM B13:459","MTM B7:228","MTM B2:65","MTM B13:442"],"tags":["Ships","Seas & Oceans","Duffey","Middle East"]},{"name":"East (the)","definition":"See <em>East</em> for directional uses, including the Ohio River's approach to the Mississippi [MTM B2:65]. For X's account of the Devil's Crimean prison, see <em>Euxine Chersonese</em> [MTM B6:204].","citations":["MTM B2:65","MTM B6:204"],"tags":["The Devil","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"East China Sea","definition":"Sea off eastern China, west of Japan's Kyushu and Ryukyu islands. It is among the many seas the narrator says Duffey and the <em>Argo</em> have sailed [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans","Islands"]},{"name":"East Coast (Adriatic)","definition":"Eastern Adriatic shore associated with Illyria. In a letter to Marie, Hans remembers the <em>Argo</em> sailing westward from Illyria repeatedly, though sometimes the Hadriaticus has no far shore and sometimes its western shore is not Italy [MTM B6:186].","citations":["MTM B6:186"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","History & Memory"]},{"name":"East Mouth of the River Ural","definition":"Location assigned to Our'yev in a summary that says Duffey was killed there, stripped of his flesh and cremated [MTM B13:436]. The fuller account places the killing and burning in the Prince's amoral desert. When that setting fades, the bones and smoking flesh-ashes appear in a street in Gur'yev at the East Mouth of the Ural, where X and Kasmir Gorshok identify them. The ashes are in a cigar canister once owned by the King of Spain; X takes custody of them [MTM B13:442].","citations":["MTM B13:436","MTM B13:442"],"tags":["Spain","Duffey","The Dead","Death & Mortality","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Easter","definition":"Christian feast celebrating Christ's resurrection. X makes his peace with the Church each Easter, only to become agnostic again by midsummer and atheist by autumn [A:138]. On Passion Sunday at Naxos, Finnegan seeks confession as part of making his Easter Duty [D:118-119].","citations":["A:138","D:118-119"],"tags":["New Testament","Liturgy","Resurrection","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Easter Duty","definition":"Catholic obligation of annual Communion, ordinarily fulfilled during the Easter season, associated with the separate obligation of yearly confession. Finnegan explicitly wants to make his Easter Duty on Passion Sunday at Naxos. His inadequate Greek makes finding a confessor difficult, but a Greek Capuchin who speaks Italian hears him [D:118-119].","citations":["D:118-119"],"tags":["Sacraments","Liturgy","Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Catholic Church","Religious Orders"]},{"name":"Eating and Drinking","definition":"Ordinary activities made exceptional for Finnegan and Anastasia Demetriades by their setting outside her grandmother's little farmhouse, on a blue mountain slope of Naxos [D:124-127].","citations":["D:124-127"],"tags":["Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Women Characters","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Eckel, Little Dutch","definition":"Engineer at a Texas City refinery and a regular resident of the Old Wooden Ship [D:216], in Galveston, Texas [MTM B13:458]. He stays because its people housed him when he was poor. Called the prince of the place, he spends more money there than any other patron [D:221].","citations":["D:216","MTM B13:458","D:221"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Money & Trade","Occupations"]},{"name":"Ecstatics","definition":"People whom the narrator, alongside several saints, credits with brief bilocation. Their few minutes of being in two places at once are contrasted with the twelve-year difficulty posed by Finnegan's and Theresa's overlapping histories [A:202].","citations":["A:202"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Mysticism","Saints","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Ectocarpus (Hashed) Collins","definition":"Cocktail requested by Duffey from Honeybucket Kincaid, along with pond-scum bread and sea-lice soup [MTM B13:417]. Kincaid's apparatus uses frequencies to gather organisms and substances from water; it collects ectocarpus and alcohol for the drinks [MTM B13:418]. The name refers to <em>Ectocarpus</em>, a genus of filamentous brown algae.","citations":["MTM B13:417","MTM B13:418"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Eden","definition":"Garden of the first human pair in Genesis 2-3. The narrator's worm-in-the-apple discussion invokes Eden and original sin [D:264]. The Basse-Terre Paradise is also called Eden when Don Barnaby becomes restless and leaves it [D:265].","citations":["D:264","D:265"],"tags":["Old Testament","Creation","Sin & Redemption","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Edgewater, Bernice","definition":"One of the money-and-commerce people, or simply rich people, in Duffey's North Chicago golden-age circle. She is listed with Julian Edgewater [MTM B3:100].","citations":["MTM B3:100"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters","Money & Trade","Social Class"]},{"name":"Edgewater, Julian","definition":"One of the money-and-commerce people, or simply rich people, in Duffey's North Chicago golden-age circle. He is listed with Bernice Edgewater [MTM B3:100].","citations":["MTM B3:100"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Money & Trade","Social Class"]},{"name":"Edith Summerfield","definition":"Member of St. Louis high society and, with Cyrus Summerfield, a commentator on it for both the <em>Globe</em> and the <em>Post Dispatch</em>. The young couple are full of glitter, and Edith is described as a sort of cousin to Beth Keegan. They attend Beth's formal dinner in Duffey's balloon gondola two miles high [MTM B2:65] and give the event elegant treatment in the press [MTM B2:66].","citations":["MTM B2:65","MTM B2:66"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Food & Dining","Journalism","Family","Social Class"]},{"name":"Editor","definition":"Role taken by Duffey when Casey joins the army in April 1942 and leaves <em>The Crock</em> in his care [MTM B4:138]. Dotty Yekouris edits <em>The Bark</em> [MTM B7:213], which she runs with Duffey, Absalom Stein and Mary Virginia Schaeffer [MTM B7:215]. Letitia Duffey becomes its new editor after the disappearances from their circle [MTM B8:238].","citations":["MTM B4:138","MTM B7:213","MTM B7:215","MTM B8:238"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Journalism","Occupations"]},{"name":"Edward Cardinal Leviathan","definition":"Cardinal of Edinburgh named among John Mogul's contracted targets [MB:571]. His double or shadow-man is Douglas McAfee of London [MB:579].","citations":["MB:571","MB:579"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia","Minor Characters","Crime","Identity"]},{"name":"Edward Ranwick","definition":"Art-school acquaintance of Duffey in St. Louis, one of the young men who accompanies him, Mary Louise Byrne and Beth Keegan on outings. At the Bavarian Club he compares the sturdy blond girls to brewery horses. His own work attracts some attention at the art school, but Duffey calls it skinny art and finds something lacking [MTM B2:58].","citations":["MTM B2:58"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Visual Arts","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Effigy","definition":"Representation that can take a person's place. The <em>Argo</em>'s effigies work as seamen while their Masters are absent; a returning Master dons his effigy, and the Argonauts' creative alterations to absent companions' effigies help form their personalities [MTM B13:449]. In the forced Ekklesia election, the Straw-Man is elected Cap-Stone and then burned, producing red smoke, bloody rain and a stream of blood [MB:567-568]. X distinguishes the Devil's former effigy flesh from the real flesh released from imprisonment [MTM B6:205]. In Letitia's scrapbook, an unidentified speaker calls political opponents effigies to justify even their literal killing [MTM B8:251-252].","citations":["MTM B13:449","MB:567-568","MTM B6:205","MTM B8:251-252"],"tags":["Identity","Ships","The Devil","Ekklesia","Death & Mortality","Crime"]},{"name":"Effigy Seamen","definition":"Working substitutes for absent <em>Argo</em> Masters. Duffey initially considers three monkey-like or wraithlike seamen of his own shaping, but there are many more aboard [MTM B13:402-403] [MTM B13:405]. They are subsequently explained as materialized, programmed memories or place tokens left by the old crewmen, including Duffey and Biloxi Brannagan, to preserve their places and perform useful work [MTM B13:415-416]. Returning Masters don their effigies, and the Argonauts' alterations to absent companions' effigies help build their personalities [MTM B13:449].","citations":["MTM B13:402-403","MTM B13:405","MTM B13:415-416","MTM B13:449"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Ships","Crew & Sailors","Duffey","Identity"]},{"name":"Efram","definition":"One of the twenty Stone boys in four families of first cousins living near Duffey's Chicago bookstore, and Myron's twin. Margaret Stone says she is their first cousin once removed and Absalom Stein their second cousin. In her account, Hugo is a joke name used by several boys, most often the twins together, whom Duffey mistakes for one fast-moving child; she denies that Absalom visited the shop. Duffey insists instead that Hugo was Absalom, a person he himself made [MTM B4:112].","citations":["MTM B4:112"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Egg","definition":"Container or image of emergence in several episodes. The children at Widow Waldo's orphanage say their green-clay animals come from green-clay eggs and can have life breathed into them [MTM B9:285]. In the Thunder Colt Game, a colt still within its egg is fed a human victim; lightning breaks the egg and awakens consciousness, followed by thunder awakening its thunder dimension. Cyrus Roundhead calls the colt's awakening a symbol of the Royal Pop People's own emergence into consciousness [MTM B11:350]. In an earlier comic image, the tangled account of Duffey's borrowed girlhood quacks, lays an addled egg and expires under an analyst's scrutiny [MTM B1:16]; Mary Louise Byrne is later identified as his sister-person [MTM B2:55].","citations":["MTM B9:285","MTM B11:350","MTM B1:16","MTM B2:55"],"tags":["Creation","Identity","Monsters & Creatures","Duffey","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Ego","definition":"One of the terms the <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> Postscript draws from C. G. Jung's <em>Aion</em>, alongside Self, Shadow, Anima and Animus. The Postscript counts these as five persons within a person and extends the idea into physically visible multiplicity [PS:477].","citations":["PS:477"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Identity","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Egypt","definition":"Ancient kingdom invoked by Duffey when he calls himself and Mary Louise a royal family. He says Egypt's kings married their sisters, while adding that he has never been sure Mary Louise is his real sister [MTM B2:55].","citations":["MTM B2:55"],"tags":["Africa","Duffey","Family","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Eighteenth Book of Strange Encounters, The","definition":"Purported source of a mock medical report arguing that New Orleans art dealer Melchisedech Duffey is more than four thousand years old. Its impressive list of indices and tests is said to give that age consistently, although his nonessential body material seems only fifty-five years old. Duffey suggests either that he has carried his essential pattern through several bodies or that one body has been repeatedly renovated, and rejects the label reincarnation. The extract is attributed to the editors of the Sixteenth and Seventeenth <em>Books of Strange Encounters</em> [MTM B8:250].","citations":["MTM B8:250"],"tags":["Duffey","Literature","Identity","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Ekklesia","definition":"The Assembly or Crowd, the ancient ecclesiastical institution whose termination is declared at the supposedly last Conclave in Babylonia Bagascia. Its Princes, styled Gates or Stones, intend to dismantle it [MB:561-562]. Paul the Eleventh, its Cap-Stone, has been decapitated before the Conclave, and the destruction of his still-speaking head is treated as the institution's end [MB:562-563]. The coerced election and burning of a Straw-Man Cap-Stone is meant to complete that suppression [MB:567-568]. The story nevertheless culminates in another conclave at Mooring Rock and the election of Pope Finnegan the First [MB:597-598].","citations":["MB:561-562","MB:562-563","MB:567-568","MB:597-598"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia","Popes","Apocalyptic","Resurrection","Finnegan"]},{"name":"El Quds","definition":"Arabic name for Jerusalem, meaning the holy place. In Cyrus Roundhead's revision of history, El Quds is instead one of several ruined cities near an abortive Turkish real-estate project supposedly named Jerusalem; he denies that a real Jerusalem ever existed [MTM B11:337].","citations":["MTM B11:337"],"tags":["Middle East","History & Memory","Language & Words"]},{"name":"El-Nahr, Kantara fok","definition":"Writer whom X credits with the eight-thousand-page novel <em>El-Kamar</em>. According to X, Kantara fok El-Nahr begins the tenth page from its end with the declaration that he will begin his story; X says the writer is a slow starter, not a humorist, and adopts the same declaration for his own account [D:284].","citations":["D:284"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Elagabalus (Heliogabalus)","definition":"Roman emperor, born Varius Avitus Bassianus around 203-204 and ruling from 218 to 222. A young priest of the Syrian deity Elagabal, he promoted that cult at Rome, became notorious in hostile accounts of his religious and personal conduct, and was killed by the Praetorian Guard. In <em>Archipelago</em>, Finnegan names Sulem's reptilian protective figure Heliogabalus, baptizes it, and wears it beside his Miraculous Medal, joking that he has made the old Roman a Christian [A:61].","citations":["A:61"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Italy","Monsters & Creatures","Finnegan","Identity","Sacraments"]},{"name":"Elam","definition":"Kingdom called Elam or Susiana, a Devildom east of the Tigris, in the account identifying the Three Slant-Faced Men. Their ruler is Chodorlahomor; the three crooked kings Amraphel, Arioch and Thadal are his followers [MTM B7:227-228]. Duffey recalls Abraham opposing the four kings with 318 men and himself blessing Abraham [MTM B7:228].","citations":["MTM B7:227-228","MTM B7:228"],"tags":["Old Testament","The Devil","Middle East","History & Memory","Duffey"]},{"name":"Electrum","definition":"Alloy of gold and silver, occurring naturally and also made artificially. The mixture supplies the image for Duffey's North Chicago electrum age, following the pure golden years of 1925-1935 and lasting until about 1946 [MTM B3:98]. Finnegan also tells a creation legend in which a broken electrum cup becomes the white, gold and silver islands of the Aegean [A:35].","citations":["MTM B3:98","A:35"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Islands","Greece","Creation","Duffey","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Electrum age","definition":"The mixed gold-and-silver phase of Duffey and Letitia's North Chicago golden age, running from the end of the pure gold years in 1935 until about 1946. It has some minor disasters, unlike the preceding 1925-1935 golden period [MTM B3:98].","citations":["MTM B3:98"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Eleemosynary Institutions (State Inspector of)","definition":"State official who writes and telephones Widow Waldo about Zabotski's large structure. Waldo tells Zabotski it must be removed from the grounds of the Golden Childrens Home and Haven Orphanage; the part on his own lot may be beyond the state's power to remove, though neighbors are pressing him about it [MTM B9:274].","citations":["MTM B9:274"],"tags":["Occupations","Minor Characters","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Elegant Riverboat Deck","definition":"Games area at Duffey's Rounders' Club in St. Louis. The club's advertisement names the deck and lists racquets, whist, poker and horseshoes; it also offers tuxedos for hire [MTM B2:49]. A picture of Severed Giant Hands hangs above the doorway leading to the deck [MTM B5:156].","citations":["MTM B2:49","MTM B5:156"],"tags":["Duffey","Clubs & Societies","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Elena (Girl who wrote to Dotty Yekouris)","definition":"Girl who, with the lawyer Ignacio, writes to Dotty Yekouris that Finnegan is coming apart and invites her to join him, possibly in Cuba [MTM B8:237-238]. In <em>Archipelago</em> Elena sells Finnegan his concertina, accompanies him, and summons Ignacio after a fight [A:273]. She tends Finnegan's injuries and translates his songs of loneliness into a letter to Dotty or Dorotea [A:274-276]. Looking over his drawings, Finnegan later recognizes Elena as both herself and Angela Cosquin [A:278].","citations":["MTM B8:237-238","A:273","A:274-276","A:278"],"tags":["Caribbean","Finnegan","Women Characters","Music","Identity"]},{"name":"Elena O'Higgins","definition":"Journalist and member of Duffey's North Chicago bookish circle [MTM B3:98], among the women he loves [MTM B4:111]. She calls a photographer to witness the mule-moving challenge [MTM B4:130] and writes the measured <em>Chicago Herald and Examiner</em> report on November 14, following the feat of November 13, 1935. Her report acknowledges conflicting testimony; its photograph shows the airborne mule without visible giant hands [MTM B4:127] [MTM B4:132]. She attends Duffey's later party with Casey [MTM B12:383]. Her commentary on Casey objects to his sale of consecrated hosts and recounts his destruction and replacement of sacred organ music [PG:524-525].","citations":["MTM B3:98","MTM B4:111","MTM B4:130","MTM B4:127","MTM B4:132","MTM B12:383","PG:524-525"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Literature","Journalism","Sacraments","Music"]},{"name":"Elkheart, Elwin K.","definition":"Secretary-General of WSMA, an affiliate of WSMAASRTFM. In a quoted skeptical statement, he refuses belief in either Divine Intervention or a ship sailing under its patent, and calls the <em>Argo</em> and such interventions conceits of Duffey the mountebank [MTM B13:402].","citations":["MTM B13:402"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Duffey","Minor Characters","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Elko","definition":"Whip named in the turbaned speaker's nostalgic catalogue at the Royal Pop gathering. He imagines a Decatur Street stone remembering its sound along with the Cowles Buggy Whip, Jacksonville Drover and other whips of the carriage era [MTM B11:329].","citations":["MTM B11:329"],"tags":["History & Memory","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Ellasar","definition":"Kingdom of Arioch in the novel's identification of the Three Slant-Faced Men with three crooked kings. The narration gives Ellasar, Pontus or Lower Babylonia as alternative designations [MTM B7:227].","citations":["MTM B7:227"],"tags":["Middle East","Old Testament","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Ellenberger, Ethyl","definition":"One of the young girls who begins visiting Duffey's Chicago bookstore at eight or nine. She and Mary Frances Rattigan usually come together; Duffey wrestles with them on the old black sofa, in an episode whose assurances of hearty fun are qualified by the narrator's warnings [MTM B4:111]. Ethyl contributes to <em>The Crock</em> [MTM B4:138], including an ode in the issue described in <em>Archipelago</em> [A:41]. At Duffey's later party she attends with her husband John Ryan [MTM B12:383].","citations":["MTM B4:111","MTM B4:138","A:41","MTM B12:383"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters","Literature","Journalism"]},{"name":"Ellenbogen, Elmer Eggleton / Endymeon","definition":"The character says he legally changed his former first name Elmer. See <em>Ellenbogen, Endymeon</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Literary Figures","Music","Literature","Visual Arts","Money & Trade","Identity"]},{"name":"Ellenbogen, Endymeon","definition":"Endymeon Ellenbogen, a member of Hans Schultz's New York Bohemian circle, introduced at Lupido's as a genius [A:236]. His former name is Elmer Eggleton Ellenbogen; he says he legally changed his first name and spent much of his inheritance subsidizing three published novels, <em>Dawn Country</em>, <em>Shenandoah Saga</em> and <em>The Man from Minneapolis</em>. His unpublished poems are planned as nine collections, including “Sonnets and Succotash.” He claims translations of the <em>Greek Anthology</em>, the <em>Spanish Golden Book</em>, Horace and Petrarch, and plans the <em>Elder Edda</em> once he learns Icelandic, maintaining that translation does not diminish one's creative gift [A:237].\n\nHe earns small sums writing popular songs and playing piano; Sally and Helen are types of songs in his catalogue, rather than identified song titles [A:238]. He also tells of carving a marble group of nereids too large to remove through his room's doors or windows, then cutting it up and distributing the figures. The previous sculptor left a book on teleportation [A:239].","citations":["A:236","A:237","A:238","A:239"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Music","Literature","Visual Arts","Money & Trade","Identity"]},{"name":"Ellis, Havelock","definition":"English physician, writer and early sex researcher (1859-1939), known for <em>Studies in the Psychology of Sex</em>, including his work with John Addington Symonds on homosexuality, and for advocacy of sex education and women's rights. In the <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> Postscript he matters especially as the proponent of what it calls the Law of Intellectual Constancy: the idea that apparently ordinary people possess powers comparable to celebrated geniuses [PS:476]. The Postscript treats this as a basis for its account of universal genius and overflowing, multiple personality [PS:477].","citations":["PS:476","PS:477"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Literary Figures","Literature","Identity","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Elmer Eggleton Ellenbogen","definition":"Former full name of Endymeon Ellenbogen. He introduces himself as Elmer Eggleton Ellenbogen, then explains that he legally changed his first name [A:236-237]. See Ellenbogen, Elmer Eggleton / Endymeon.","citations":["A:236-237"],"tags":["Identity"]},{"name":"Elysian Field Avenue","definition":"Elysian Field Avenue, one of the New Orleans streets through which ghosts supposedly slip after a night's haunting. The narrator immediately retracts this account as a lie, except for Finnegan's painting of the ghosts in the bar; the next page gives the ordinary history of Anthony Ghost's coffee shop [MTM B10:292-293].","citations":["MTM B10:292-293"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Embulking pellets","definition":"Imaginary murder device discussed in the Murder Contest associated with John Mogul's killings: pellets placed in food supposedly make the victim grow exponentially until exploding [MB:582-583].","citations":["MB:582-583"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Crime"]},{"name":"Emery paper (Blue)","definition":"Abrasive paper used as the image for Patrick Stranahan's rough face, which is likened to blue emery paper [A:102].","citations":["A:102"],"tags":[]},{"name":"Emery, Art","definition":"Crewman of the <em>Brunhilde</em>, hostile to Finnegan after his joke on Art's name and prettiness. At the ship's bar gathering, Art goes to inform on the company [D:22]. He is listed among the five crewmen [D:30] and grouped by the narrator with Harry Scott and Chris McAbney as the wrong sort of people; this later passage assigns the first day's informing to Chris [D:31]. Anastasia calls Art the Devil's former catamount, now rejected [D:68]. Much later Finnegan sees him on a street corner and knocks him flat in sudden hatred, learning that the <em>Brunhilde</em> has reached port [D:230].","citations":["D:22","D:30","D:31","D:68","D:230"],"tags":["The Devil","Ships","Crew & Sailors","Finnegan","Crime"]},{"name":"Emily (Henry Salvatore's aunt)","definition":"Emily is one of Henry Francis Salvatore’s two aunts in the neighborhood of his Morgan City family; the other is Marietta [A:122-123].","citations":["A:122-123"],"tags":["Louisiana","Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Emperor","definition":"Imperial title used in several different contexts. A proud former fire horse, dressed for the appearance of the leader in Henry Horner Park, holds its head like a Roman Emperor. The same episode describes a German Blue Max among missing decorations as bearing the old Emperor Maximilian's seal [MTM B4:116]. Finnegan is shriven on the feast of St. Henry Emperor while preparing for a possible death [A:277].","citations":["MTM B4:116","A:277"],"tags":["Saints","Germany","Italy","Identity"]},{"name":"Emperor Henry (of Neustria)","definition":"Imperial identity of Henri Salvatore, once the Argonaut Euphemus, later Emperor Henry of Neustria, and now ruler of Invisible Neustria. After the Devil's release, his small personal fiefdoms are set against the Devil and avert or postpone the world's destruction for decades; Duffey is made subordinate to this creation of his own [MTM B8:245]. Mary Virginia Schaeffer was his betrothed before he gave her up for God [MTM B7:214]. The name Neustria recalls the western kingdom of the Franks, transformed here into an invisible realm.","citations":["MTM B8:245","MTM B7:214"],"tags":["Argonautica","Argonauts (Characters)","Cosmic Struggle","History & Memory","Identity","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Empire (Roman)","definition":"Historical setting invoked in the passage attributed to G. K. Chesterton's <em>The Everlasting Man</em>: the Creator's entry into the ordinary life of the Roman Empire is presented as the startling uniqueness of the Incarnation [MTM B5:176]. Herman Hercules also remembers Rome in the imperial centuries as one of the crowded cities whose spectacles drew long-lived people like himself [MB:585].","citations":["MTM B5:176","MB:585"],"tags":["Italy","New Testament","Theology","History & Memory","Immortality"]},{"name":"Enac (sons of)","definition":"Biblical giants, the sons of Anak or Enac, reported by the scouts in Canaan. The Numbers epigraph to the Angela Cosquin chapter combines the sight of these giants with the people's fearful weeping and the counter-exhortation that the inhabitants can be eaten like bread [D:258].","citations":["D:258"],"tags":["Giants & Titans","Old Testament","Scripture"]},{"name":"End of Compassion, The","definition":"Name of the lower, more painful hell in the Goat Contingency contemplated by Duffey: compassionate scapegoats such as Casey would bear the world's accumulated debts forever after trading away everything. The narration calls this a very doubtful possible outcome, while leaving Casey's attempted soul-trade with the Devil uncertain [MTM B10:299].","citations":["MTM B10:299"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Duffey","The Devil","Time & Eternity","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"End of Time","definition":"A problem raised by Duffey’s approaching end and his identification with the biblical Melchisedech, who has “neither beginning of days nor end of life” in the passage heading Book Six [MTM B6:185]. Duffey takes the Ursuline girls’ seven-arrowed sign to signify either his own death or the end of time itself. Cleo Mahoney’s play asks what becomes of a man who cannot end when his life or the world reaches its end [MTM B9:264] [MTM B9:265]. At the beginning of Book Ten, the narrator distinguishes Duffey’s impending ending from the possible end of the world. Duffey can no longer enter a valid future along the normal track. The passage connects this obstruction with the Seven Lost Years and his distrust of the seven futures offered him [MTM B10:290].","citations":["MTM B6:185","MTM B9:264","MTM B9:265","MTM B10:290"],"tags":["Duffey","Time & Eternity","Death & Mortality","Immortality","Scripture"]},{"name":"Endoderm","definition":"In biology, the innermost embryonic germ layer. The narrative instead uses the word for the inside of Cardinal Artemis's skin, where Finnegan secretly imprints a great painting by radiation. After the Cardinal's flaying, Finnegan is uncertain whether the painted skin belongs to the trophy held by Mogul or is being worn by the double Gilberto Levine-and-O'Brien [MB:591].","citations":["MB:591"],"tags":["Finnegan","Visual Arts","Identity"]},{"name":"Engineer","definition":"Unnamed professional who tells Duffey that the support knocked out from the Pont du Sable Traffic Trestle is ornamental, not weight-bearing [MTM B3:104-105]. The narrator adds that it had carried weight for Duffey himself, preserving the bridge's personal significance [MTM B3:105].","citations":["MTM B3:104-105","MTM B3:105"],"tags":["Duffey","Occupations","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"England","definition":"Country named in the <em>Argo</em>'s remembered history: King Richard of England recaptures the ship from the Saracens, and Robert of Namur later sails it as the <em>Salle du Roi</em> for another English king [MTM B13:404]. Cecil Octavian, double of Cardinal Brokenbolt, is from England [MB:579]. The English Bulldog chosen to help guard the <em>Argo</em> is Gunboat Smith, recruited at the Old Wooden Ship in Galveston, Texas [MTM B13:458].","citations":["MTM B13:404","MB:579","MTM B13:458"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Duffey","Identity"]},{"name":"English","definition":"Nationality or ancestry in Ralph Rolfe's English paternal descent [MTM B13:427], and a breed designation in the English Bulldog Gunboat Smith, recruited to protect the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:458]. In the Martyr-Bell's story, English raiders attack Ste. Genevieve in 1594 [MB:576]. The codenamed Father Brown also invokes Old English when explaining that drench could mean drown [MTM B13:457].","citations":["MTM B13:427","MTM B13:458","MB:576","MTM B13:457"],"tags":["Family","Language & Words","Ships"]},{"name":"Englishman","definition":"English raiders are the persecutors in the Martyr-Bell's origin story. In 1594 they murder thirty-nine Christians on Ste. Genevieve who refuse to renounce their faith; their ship, the <em>Lord Cramner</em>, is then wrecked and its crew drowned. Cannon recovered from it are made into the bell, which rings for four hundred years whenever Englishmen or other enemies of the faith approach [MB:576].","citations":["MB:576"],"tags":["Combat","Ships","Death & Mortality","Catholic Church","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Enniscorthy and Mary Sweeny","definition":"Musical friends in Duffey's North Chicago golden-age circle. The musical group ranges across opera, ragtime, Chicago-hot music and string bands, and includes composers, players and stagers [MTM B3:99]. The later party list names Enniscorthy and Mary Margaret Sweeny [MTM B12:383].","citations":["MTM B3:99","MTM B12:383"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters","Music"]},{"name":"Enniscorthy Sweeny","definition":"Musical friend of Duffey's Chicago circle [MTM B3:99], also called Ennis and described as ruddy-faced and a near-genius. After an apparent ending of <em>More Than Melchisedech</em>, the narrative quotes his comparison of an event to a box viewed from four sides: past, present, alternative present and future. Each perspective belongs to the event and should be retained when another view appears; the quotation introduces the turn toward an alternate ending [MTM B13:467].","citations":["MTM B3:99","MTM B13:467"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Time & Eternity","History & Memory","Paradox"]},{"name":"Entrance Person","definition":"Designation the Better Life League gives Zabotski. In his written account, Duffey affirms that the League is right about this: Zabotski is a strong and peculiar Entrance Person, although Zabotski himself thinks little of the League [MTM B9:268].","citations":["MTM B9:268"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Entrepreneur","definition":"Role exemplified by Duffey's interconnected businesses. He buys and refits a St. Louis livery stable as a residence, shop, lodging house, bar and entertainment establishment [MTM B2:40-41], developed into Rounders' Club [MTM B2:49]. He also promotes his own boxing bills [MTM B2:51]. In New Orleans his Walk-In Art Bijou [MTM B1:6] and pawn shop share the building where he helps found and fit out the Pelican Press to publish <em>The Bark</em> and other work. The narrative connects this practical labor with rebuilding the world [MTM B6:190].","citations":["MTM B2:40-41","MTM B2:49","MTM B2:51","MTM B1:6","MTM B6:190"],"tags":["Duffey","Money & Trade","Occupations","Journalism","Clubs & Societies","Creation"]},{"name":"Envy","definition":"One element in Stein's overflowing look at the company, mixed with amusement, compassion, contempt and proprietorship [A:30]. Finnegan is also described as the envy of men on the street while walking with Anastasia and Marie Courtois [D:57-58]. Lily Koch jokingly envies those whose way of life lets them commit murders [MTM B3:97].","citations":["A:30","D:57-58","MTM B3:97"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Ephphatha","definition":"Aramaic command meaning be opened, spoken by Jesus in Mark 7:34. Duffey uses it to release the stutter of a brilliant but unnamed young man at Weinsburg on the Nechar [MTM B13:430]. The man protests that frustration fuels the destructive work he wants to accomplish; Duffey refuses to restore the impediment. Casey lingers behind, and the narration raises the possibility of someone giving the stutter back without confirming that it happens [MTM B13:431].","citations":["MTM B13:430","MTM B13:431"],"tags":["New Testament","Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Miracles"]},{"name":"Ephrata (Chicago)","definition":"Biblical-sounding designation attached to Mary Catherine Carruthers in the list of Duffey's Animated Marvels: \"But Thou, Chicago Ephrata, out of Thee shall come\" [MTM B5:183]. It adapts Micah 5:2's prophecy of a ruler coming from Bethlehem Ephrata, substituting Chicago and leaving the sentence unfinished.","citations":["MTM B5:183"],"tags":["Old Testament","Women Characters","Prophecy & Visions","Scripture"]},{"name":"Epic","definition":"Heroic literary form invoked in three different registers. A pre-pre-Homeric epic passage appears among the underlayers of the old goatskin parchment Duffey examines [MTM B11:327]. Finnegan's order of a hundred oysters prompts the question of which epic hero ate such a meal [MTM B5:172], followed by Duffey's recollection of Fingal or Finn McCool eating oysters larger than wagon wheels [MTM B5:173-174]. Marie also calls her uncle Shakes Pearson's repeatedly interrupted tobacco-contest limericks an epic, as squirrels eat the bark he writes on [A:25-26].","citations":["MTM B11:327","MTM B5:172","MTM B5:173-174","A:25-26"],"tags":["Finnegan","Food & Dining","Literature","Folklore"]},{"name":"Epirus","definition":"Ancient region on the northwestern side of the Greek world. Cyrus Roundhead incorporates it into his invented geography of Greece as one of five concentric rings around Hellas, alongside Aetolia, Thessaly, Macedonia and Thrace [MTM B11:337].","citations":["MTM B11:337"],"tags":["Greece","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Episodes of the Argo","definition":"Lafferty collection listed as a 1990 title in the bibliography at the front of <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> [D:4]. Published by United Mythologies Press, the chapbook contains Argo material, including part of the conclusion of the <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> sequence.","citations":["D:4"],"tags":["Literature","Argonautica"]},{"name":"Epistle (Third, to the Kids in St. Louis)","definition":"Writing attributed to Margaret Stone, excerpted among the documents surrounding the St. Louis gathering. Margaret describes her extraordinary body heat, private flight and command over tempests, then contrasts her weak hands with the responsibility for caring for the world for one hour out of every twenty-four [MTM B5:177-178].","citations":["MTM B5:177-178"],"tags":["Women Characters","Miracles","Prophecy & Visions","Literature"]},{"name":"Erculo, Martino Cardinal","definition":"Cardinal of Milan, paired with the double Herman Hercules in the list of the thirteen cardinals pursued by John Mogul [MB:579]. Hercules calls Erculo his primary, though later narration leaves uncertain whether the supposed double might be the Cardinal in disguise [MB:584] [MB:588].","citations":["MB:579","MB:584","MB:588"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Identity","Crime"]},{"name":"Eros","definition":"Greek word for love, especially passionate or sexual love. Eros is one of the metaphorical flowers, with Philos and Agape, bursting through New Orleans's broken pavement in the underground sunshine. The flowers are red, yellow and purple; the passage specifically assigns hot purple to Agape [MTM B7:211].","citations":["MTM B7:211"],"tags":["New Orleans","Greece","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Error","definition":"In the passage attributed to E. I. Watkin's <em>The Catholic Center</em>, Catholicism's exclusion of religious error follows from its claim to include the whole of religious truth [MTM B5:177]. Mary Virginia Schaeffer uses a different image in her Duffey Book: nine supposedly discarded but still living worlds would be anthologies of errors, failures and imperfect conceptions. She presents this as a fancy based on legends, not scriptural authority, and wonders whether Duffey came from one of them [MTM B10:291-292].","citations":["MTM B5:177","MTM B10:291-292"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Theology","Creation","Duffey","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Euphemus","definition":"Henri Salvatore's Argonaut identity. Henri himself asks whether he was Euphemus for nothing when Teresa summons him to explain the company's past [MTM B7:232]. Teresa identifies Henry as Euphemus and says that his ocean-father taught him to walk on water, though he seldom does so now [MTM B7:234].","citations":["MTM B7:232","MTM B7:234"],"tags":["Argonautica","Argonauts (Characters)","Identity","Miracles"]},{"name":"Euxine Chersonese","definition":"Name that X says the Romans used for the peninsula otherwise called the Tauric Chersonese, Krym, or Crimea. In his account the Devil was imprisoned there in 946 or 947 [MTM B6:204]. Stein's notes give the dungeon's millennium as 946-1946 and date the Devil's release to 1946 [MTM B6:185].","citations":["MTM B6:204","MTM B6:185"],"tags":["The Devil","Eastern Europe","Heaven & Hell","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Evil","definition":"Casey calls the Problem of Evil the only problem in the world and doubts that any of his companions' ways of confronting it will work [A:154]. Demetrio Glauch describes his particular lifelong obsession as the possible final redemption of the Devil [PG:518-519]. Evil also appears in the displaced spirits after the neighborhood's destruction [MTM B11:314], the former owners of the <em>Brunhilde</em> [MTM B13:404], and the Argonauts' continuing struggle, complicated by the Laughing Prince's claimed Third Way [MTM B13:437]. Duffey calls the cured stutterer an evil genius turned good or complacent [MTM B13:431]. Bagby's posthumous letter, by contrast, denies that passions themselves are evil, comparing ungoverned passions to unbroken horses [MTM B8:248]. Duffey's meeting with the Loosed Devil belongs to the sixth proposed contingency of the world [MTM B10:301-302].","citations":["A:154","PG:518-519","MTM B11:314","MTM B13:404","MTM B13:437","MTM B13:431","MTM B8:248","MTM B10:301-302"],"tags":["Theology","The Devil","Cosmic Struggle","Sin & Redemption","Ghosts & Spirits"]},{"name":"Evil Eye","definition":"An unconfirmed attribution of a malevolent gaze to Casey Szymansky.","citations":[],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Evil Prince","definition":"One of the princely adversaries named with the Purple Prince, Mocking Prince, and Laughing Prince. The narrative stresses the adversaries' courage, force of personality, and ability to defeat or kill <em>Argo</em> Masters; it singles out the Laughing Prince of Tartary as possibly the strongest and trickiest [MTM B13:437].","citations":["MTM B13:437"],"tags":["Argonautica","Cosmic Struggle","Combat"]},{"name":"Evil Spirit","definition":"Evil spirits are among the displaced beings haunting the sites of the neighborhood's destruction, deprived of their places and forms [MTM B11:314]. During the effort to bar the Man of Mystery from the <em>Argo</em>, the expert called Professor Van Dusen warns that blind spirits can enter through blind fireplaces and then assume almost any form; he recommends guarding each fireplace with a firedrake [MTM B13:455-456].","citations":["MTM B11:314","MTM B13:455-456"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Magic & Sorcery","Shapeshifting","Ships"]},{"name":"Ewaglouwshkoul (Eva)","definition":"The woman Duffey and the <em>Argo</em>’s crew release from the ice after forty thousand years. She identifies herself as Ewaglouwshkoul; Duffey calls her Little Eva, and she accepts “Neanderthal Eve” as a possible nickname. She says she is not the first woman of her tribe but perhaps the last, and hopes to find surviving men of her own people and have children [MTM B13:399-401]. The narration later calls her a Neo-Neanderthal [MTM B13:407].\n\nShe asks to be taken to Biloxi, where Duffey is already bound, and plans to acquire a waterfront establishment to meet seamen of her blood. Her father ran the Old Stone Ship in her ancient world [MTM B13:401]. She wants to buy Kate’s Neanderthal Bar and rename it Eva’s Neanderthal Bar [MTM B13:407]. When she needs a $14,000 down payment, Duffey produces gold pieces and Brannagan produces banknotes with identical serial numbers. Gertrude offers to fetch her checkbook and arrange matters [MTM B13:414-415].","citations":["MTM B13:399-401","MTM B13:407","MTM B13:414-415"],"tags":["Women Characters","Monsters & Creatures","Argo","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Ewe","definition":"One of the animals supplying the Boy King Melchisedech's milk. The account first calls the product of camel, ass, ewe, gazelle, cow, and Indian buffalo his silk, then explains that it was milk in actuality [MTM B1:17-18].","citations":["MTM B1:17-18"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Exocosmos","definition":"The surrounding world with which Zabotski has what Duffey calls non-mutual arrangements. The immediate example is the Better Life League, which recognizes Zabotski as an Entrance Person although Zabotski thinks little of the League [MTM B9:268].","citations":["MTM B9:268"],"tags":["Language & Words","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Exorcism","definition":"A religious rite seeking liberation from demonic power. In Finnegan's account of painting a devil, Father Le Faye, S.J., performs a short exorcism and orders the painting burned and its ashes swept away [D:254-255].","citations":["D:254-255"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Catholic Church","Liturgy","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Exorcist","definition":"Father Le Faye, S.J., is described as an exorcist of some fame in Finnegan's account. He arrives without being summoned, recognizes the devil in Finnegan's painting, performs a short exorcism, and directs that the picture be burned [D:254-255].","citations":["D:254-255"],"tags":["Religious Orders","Catholic Church","Visual Arts","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Exotic","definition":"One of the exuberant adjectives applied to Dotty Yekouris in a description borrowed from a nursery plant catalogue, which the narrator still judges inadequate to her [MTM B7:213].","citations":["MTM B7:213"],"tags":["Women Characters","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Expedition","definition":"A Cummings-clan member of the Fundamentalers tells Zabotski that his group has three expeditions searching the mountains of Armenia for Noah's Ark. Zabotski claims that the true High Armenia cannot be securely identified with the modern country or the mountain now called Ararat [MTM B9:280].","citations":["MTM B9:280"],"tags":["Old Testament","Ships","Middle East"]},{"name":"Experience","definition":"Casey's erotic experiences in the second of his twelve imaginary worlds, begun before he was eight. They involve women from history and legend, including queens, nymphs, amazons, gladiators, bullfighters, and acrobats, and lead to his theory of personalized temptations [A:156-157].","citations":["A:156-157"],"tags":["Dreams","Women Characters","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Experiment","definition":"In Elena O'Higgins's commentary, Casey is criticized for publishing an advertisement in the <em>Crock</em> offering supposedly genuine consecrated hosts for a dollar each, postpaid, for fun, experiment, or special rites. The advertisement claims that a defrocked priest supplies them and that prodigies sometimes occur; Elena says it ran in at least three issues but does not know the response [PG:524].","citations":["PG:524"],"tags":["Journalism","Sacraments","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Expert","definition":"Duffey becomes an art expert by ransacking the minds of Sebastian Hilton, Lily Koch, and adult customers at Lily's sales [MTM B1:29]. As a stamp and postmark expert and dealer, he examines the otherwhere stamps on Bagby's extraordinary letters [MTM B7:219]. Later the <em>Argo</em> Masters consult experts in detection to prevent the Man of Mystery from boarding; some are fictional, some are real persons behind disguises, and the names Philo Vance, Father Brown, Doctor Thorndyke, Max Carrados, and Professor Van Dusen may be code names [MTM B13:454-455].","citations":["MTM B1:29","MTM B7:219","MTM B13:454-455"],"tags":["Duffey","Occupations","Visual Arts","Literature","Ships"]},{"name":"Explanation","definition":"Duffey seeks to understand the seven-arrowed sign and the school play that appear to predict his possible endings. He consults Stein, who jokes, and then seeks the absent Zabotski [MTM B9:264-266]. Zabotski explains the extraordinary fertility of his Lake Borgne flats as God's blessing on a just man chosen for a special role [MTM B12:362].","citations":["MTM B9:264-266","MTM B12:362"],"tags":["Duffey","Theater","Prophecy & Visions","Miracles"]},{"name":"Explorer","definition":"Casey's role in his fifth imaginary world. He supposes that the Earth is half again as large as generally believed, with elongated poles and an Antarctic ring of ice surrounding a sunny southern continent, probably a migrated Atlantis. As its explorer he would carry modern techniques there and bring a Higher Ethic back [A:158].","citations":["A:158"],"tags":["Dreams","Identity","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Extinction","definition":"Melchisedech's panic while wrestling Morpheus as the dreamed cosmos contracts toward a single surviving cell; the narrator distinguishes it from the lesser fear of damnation [GD:500]. Earlier, Stein fears that forgotten people will pass into oblivion, becoming people who never existed, unless someone remembers them; he imagines them bawling against extinction [MTM B11:315].","citations":["GD:500","MTM B11:315"],"tags":["Duffey","Death & Mortality","Dreams","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Extradigitalism","definition":"Having extra digits, or polydactyly. John Mogul has five fingers and one thumb on each hand, a condition said to be common in his family. He raises all twelve digits to indicate that twelve men remain under his killing contracts [MB:574].","citations":["MB:574"],"tags":["Family","Crime"]},{"name":"Extravaganza","definition":"The show Duffey, Letitia, and Lily see at Morgenstein's Comedy Music Box on Randolph Street, after supper at Lily's. On leaving, they hear the news of the Mullens double murder [MTM B3:97].","citations":["MTM B3:97"],"tags":["Duffey","Theater","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Eye","definition":"Duffey has fire-blue eyes [MTM B1:6]; Letitia's are described as between sea-green and Melchisedech blue [MTM B3:92]. The woman whom Duffey releases from the ice has blue eyes and identifies herself as Ewaglouwshkoul, Little Eva or the Neanderthal Eve [MTM B13:399-400]. In his later bony form Melchisedech has no eyes, although some faculty of sensing remains and is impeded by his golden mask [MTM B13:444].","citations":["MTM B1:6","MTM B3:92","MTM B13:399-400","MTM B13:444"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Eye-Sores","definition":"The Moral Sanctions Committee for the Removal of Eye-Sores and Abominations objects to Zabotski's ever-growing backyard construction. Its spokeswoman complains that eight years have passed since his supposed promise to act; Zabotski replies that he promised not to act and that people fail to listen closely [MTM B9:276].","citations":["MTM B9:276"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","Ships"]},{"name":"Eyebrows","definition":"Bascom Bagby has beetling brows when he first visits Duffey's premises for a shave and haircut [MTM B2:43]. Each of Patrick Stranahan's eyebrows is said to contain more hair than most men have on their heads [A:102].","citations":["MTM B2:43","A:102"],"tags":[]},{"name":"Eyelids","definition":"The eyes of the head-only image of Casey Szymansky are sewn open, forcing it to look despite its refusal. Duffey animates the image from Adam Scanlon's hostile painting and projects it into the Goldfarb's Alley comedy broadcast, where four giants carry the head and the spectacle terrifies performers and viewers [MTM B12:381].","citations":["MTM B12:381"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Theater","Magic & Sorcery","Giants & Titans"]},{"name":"Ezekiel","definition":"Hebrew prophet active in the early sixth century BC. The novel calls Blessed Ezekiel a very early <em>Argo</em> Master and invokes his promise of opened graves and return to the land, from Ezekiel 37:12, while considering futures that must yield up past and present [MTM B13:447].","citations":["MTM B13:447"],"tags":["Old Testament","Argonauts (Characters)","Prophecy & Visions","Resurrection"]},{"name":"Face, Finnegan's","definition":"Earlier heading named only Finnegan although the verified catalogue also treats the faces of other characters. See <em>Faces</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","Visual Arts","Monsters & Creatures","Devils (Characters)","Women Characters","Dreams","The Dead","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Faces","definition":"Finnegan's first reflected face has a banana nose, restless cheek and temple muscles, a moving mouth, and reddened eyes [A:1]. His apparent grace gives way at close range to a gargoyle's comic mask and bugle nose [A:6]; washing cannot remove the grotesqueness of his face [D:13]. At Las Palmas he looks like a Montenegran prince, noble of nose [D:100]. On Naxos his gargoyle head contrasts with Anastasia's Diana-like head [D:133]. The later painting of Count Finnegan gives the banana nose nobility and distinction, while preserving humor, pain, and torture in the eyes [MB:551]. Joe Cross finds Finnegan disturbingly like Dopey when glimpsed from the side, though less alike face-on [D:65]. While shaving, Finnegan sees and shaves Dopey's distinct face in the mirror; after he smashes the glass, Dopey remains visible in a fragment, his eyes closed in torturous sleep [D:187-188].\n\nOther faces form a related catalogue. Saxon Seaworthy's lined, granite face has a depth that the word dull cannot encompass [D:11]. It turns ashen and ancient when Papa Devil arrives, then regains its controlled expression [D:15]; it is tired granite during Don Lewis's story [D:43] and stony-faced during Seaworthy's own dream account [D:72]. Papa Devil has a purplish complexion and a reddish fringe around his nearly bald head [D:23], becoming more ghoulish after an admitted killing [D:30]. Finnegan discovers that the purple pumpkin-like head conceals another face and paints what lies beneath [D:62-63]. He paints this face again in a death scene [D:86], challenges Papa Devil about his living mask [D:88], and later recognizes the slain man on Naxos by that reconstructed face [D:146]. His sketch enables X to identify the impersonator as an Irish policeman called Noonan or Flaherty [D:154]. Earlier, Papa Devil speaks through a distorted face while threatening to bury Finnegan beside Don Lewis [D:111].\n\nAnastasia appears transparent, translucent, and transcendent [D:13]; her face is comical in direct view but beautiful in memory or anticipation, with an olive complexion shifting between green and gold [D:32]. Finnegan praises her green complexion while she is seasick [D:28]. Her head is compared with statues of Diana Artemis at Messene and Delos [D:133]. Marie Courtois's beauty is described through her golden coloring, red-gold hair, strength, and stately form [D:37]. Don Lewis has a fevered handsomeness [D:100] and later tries unsuccessfully to hide his identity beneath a hood [D:102]. Demetrio's lined face recalls both an old Roman and an Aztec, like a face on an ancient coin; the following account of Finnegan's ink portrait on the back of a poster calls the sitter Manuel [D:58]. On the same page the street-food seller who joins Finnegan's joke has a face like laughing leather [D:58]. The old teras in Finnegan's sleep has a face of gloomy granite [D:140]. The man from whom Don Lewis seeks concealment twists his face in apparent spasm, then reveals that he is laughing at the small payment offered [D:107-108]. Brannagan has the clear eyes and powerful forearms of an old seaman [MTM B13:409]. In Don Lewis's story the man later called Yokipoki, probably not his real name, first appears as a ragged little man [D:44] [D:46].","citations":["A:1","A:6","D:13","D:100","D:133","MB:551","D:65","D:187-188","D:11","D:15","D:43","D:72","D:23","D:30","D:62-63","D:86","D:88","D:146","D:154","D:111","D:32","D:28","D:37","D:102","D:58","D:140","D:107-108","MTM B13:409","D:44","D:46"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","Visual Arts","Monsters & Creatures","Devils (Characters)","Women Characters","Dreams","The Dead","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Faggots","definition":"Bundles of sticks used as fuel. On Naxos, Finnegan includes them among the fuels whose smoke, he says, gives different lands and islands their distinctive skies; he imagines electric, solar, or atomic heat restoring an older sky color [D:127].","citations":["D:127"],"tags":["Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Falo","definition":"One of the two girls building an American-style cabaret in the jungle on Luzon when Vincent and Finnegan arrive [A:63-64]. Falo and Nauti offer Four Feathers and Five Roses whisky; Falo brings water and firmly refuses Vincent's attempt to take her by the waist [A:64]. The cabaret has bananas to eat. Falo repeatedly wins Robber Cassino because she has used the same old cards since childhood and knows them all [A:65]. Both girls say goodbye one time, explaining that goodbye all the time would mean their guests were never coming back [A:66].","citations":["A:63-64","A:64","A:65","A:66"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","Women Characters","Alcohol","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Falsario","definition":"Spanish for forger or counterfeiter. Finnegan supplies the word when Papa Devil, ostensibly seeking a notary on the Mexican side of the port, cannot make his actual need understood. They are promptly directed to a forger, where Papa Devil and Manuel dismiss Finnegan and the two women [D:57].","citations":["D:57"],"tags":["Language & Words","Crime","Ports & Harbors","Finnegan","Devils (Characters)"]},{"name":"Falsetto Chorus","definition":"An unconfirmed chorus associated in the earlier glossary with a plaint and the phrase Triumphant grew a taint.","citations":[],"tags":["Music"]},{"name":"Family","definition":"The novels give family histories alongside more uncertain kinships and claims of shared or magically created personality.\n\nPatrick J. Stranahan, a successful attorney, and Monica Murry Stranahan have four sons, Philip, Hugh, Timothy Navisius, and Vincent J., and two daughters, Norma and Maurine [A:94].\n\nThe Koch family comprises August and Elinore and their daughters Lily and Letitia, who marries Duffey [MTM B3:98]. Letitia's hair mingles blonde, scarlet, and walnut tones; she is ruddy, full-bodied, and comical in expression, with eyes between sea-green and Melchisedech blue. She is younger than Lily and taller than Duffey [MTM B3:92].\n\nMary Virginia Schaeffer belongs to the family owning Red Dog Motor Freight, which serves Galveston, Houston, Morgan City, New Orleans, Baton Rouge, Shreveport, Port Arthur, Orange, and Beaumont [MTM B6:198].\n\nLawrence Salvatore and Mary Genevieve Hebert are parents of Henry Francis, Christopher, Eustace, Hilaire, Joseph, Louis, Mark, Simeon, Angela, Lucy, and Rita. Nearby uncles include Alphonse, Charles, Fulgence, George, and Matthew Salvatore, and Anthony, Edward, Gregory, Julien, and Nicole Hebert; nearby aunts are Emily and Marietta [A:122-123]. Henry is the fat, mean, precociously learned boy whom Lawrence finds already educated early in his thirteenth year [A:123]; his Argonaut identity is Euphemus [A:38].\n\nMary Louise Byrne is called Duffey's sister and, with some uncertainty, his stepsister [MTM B2:54-55]. Their erotic attachment is described through patriarchal sister-wife imagery, while Mary Louise is also presented as an exteriorized part of Duffey's personality [MTM B2:55-56]. She later marries Bascom Bagby, making him Duffey's brother-in-law [MTM B5:155]. Bagby is also taken for Duffey's brother and described as part of their shared person. His black hair and whiskers and swarthy skin contrast with Duffey's coloring, while their large hands, swinging shoulders, and stride resemble one another [MTM B2:61]. Duffey's wife in Chicago is Letitia [MTM B3:98].\n\nThe Angelino clan is described as mainly Italian and German, with Bohemian, Mexican, Irish Tinker, and Romany ancestry. Elena Angelino, who works with trained bears, insists that her great-grandfather was a bear; the Professor says that he knew him and agrees [A:218].\n\nGiulio Solli and Mary McCracken are Finnegan's parents [A:244-245]. The military roll gives his name as John A. Solli [A:71]. His brother Giacomo, called Jake, drives taxis, and his sister Patricia is an acrobatic dancer. Patricia also calls Doppio di Pinne her brother and the same person as Finnegan, while admitting that the family never understood the alternation [A:174]. Finnegan's later meeting with the shabby, sorrowful Giulio takes place after his father's death and ends in a recognition of paternal love [A:244-245].\n\nAnastasia Demetriades has two uncles who run a restaurant, where she has worked as cashier [D:40]. She and Finnegan visit the family farm on Naxos, where her grandmother Anastasia, called Gyiagyia, remains after hardship has emptied the household [D:124-126].\n\nIn X's account, Katie Noonan bears Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch near Krakow in 1830. X believes his father to be the Devil and cites the parish register, while acknowledging but rejecting Gregory, Katie's employer and the child's adoptive father, as a possible biological father [D:278-279]. Ifreann later fathers the twins Gregory McIfreann, Papadiabolous I, born in Smyrna, and Gregory McColum, Papadiabolous II, born in Cork, to different mothers at the turn of the century [D:281] [D:285]. X explains that names in this family move among parents, children, and grandchildren, with Papadiabolous and Chortovitch recurring as auxiliary names [D:278].","citations":["A:94","MTM B3:98","MTM B3:92","MTM B6:198","A:122-123","A:123","A:38","MTM B2:54-55","MTM B2:55-56","MTM B5:155","MTM B2:61","A:218","A:244-245","A:71","A:174","D:40","D:124-126","D:278-279","D:281","D:285","D:278"],"tags":["Family","Identity","Duffey","Finnegan","Women Characters","Devils (Characters)","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Fancy, The","definition":"One of the whips named by a speaker at the Royal Pop gathering while describing the memories preserved in a Decatur Street stone. It appears alongside the Milford Quirt, San Antonio, Never-Break Dog Whip, and Elko in a nostalgic catalogue of vanished street traffic [MTM B11:329].","citations":["MTM B11:329"],"tags":["History & Memory","Occupations"]},{"name":"Fandango in Three Flames","definition":"Fritz's music accompanying the Fire-Drake Frolic during the opera presentation, in which further human victims are killed [MTM B11:350].","citations":["MTM B11:350"],"tags":["Music","Theater","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Fanged Fish, The (bar)","definition":"Waterfront establishment at Ogopo in Duffey's catalogue of remembered ports and drinking places, named with the Benevolent Shark at Maule [MTM B13:407].","citations":["MTM B13:407"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Duffey","Bars & Taverns","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Fare delle capriole","definition":"Italian phrase for performing capers or leaps. Finnegan uses it while explaining a goat joke: to cut capers can mean dancing and playing tricks, while his other gloss, <em>castrare becchi</em>, makes it mean castrating he-goats. The goat-man criticizes the pun and discusses related French and Spanish forms [A:199].","citations":["A:199"],"tags":["Language & Words","Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Farer","definition":"A traveler in Vincent Stranahan's poem written on the back of an insurance policy: a Farer will find a Theresa. The poem turns from roaming to life in the town on the river [A:92].","citations":["A:92"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"Farmer","definition":"Farmers appear in Hans Schultz's comic hypnotism act, where fat farmers become bathing beauties [A:219], and in Howland's jingle, where a farmer ploughs up the five gophers' house [A:179]. Marie Monahan's story about her uncle Shakes Pearson includes a proposed limerick about an old tobacco-growing farmer; the farmer is a figure in the verse, while Shakes is the trembling, whisky-drinking author [A:25].","citations":["A:219","A:179","A:25"],"tags":["Occupations","Theater","Literature"]},{"name":"Farmer's Canopy-Top Surrey, the","definition":"Earlier heading changed the source’s plural possessive Farmers’ to singular Farmer’s. See <em>Farmers’ Canopy-Top Surrey</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["History & Memory"]},{"name":"Farmer's Daughter, The (club)","definition":"One of the places on Chicago's Fleshpot Row that Shirley Israel recommends to Duffey as the haunts of all the real people. Her list includes Herm's, Curley Q, Seven Steps Down, Blow Brass, and Dung Heap [MTM B4:126].","citations":["MTM B4:126"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Farmers’ Canopy-Top Surrey","definition":"Horse vehicle in the nostalgic catalogue recited by the Royal Pop lecturer who describes the memories of a Decatur Street stone. The Farmers' Canopy-Top Surrey is listed with the Fulton Road Cart, Imperial Carriage, and other vehicles that once passed over the stone [MTM B11:329].","citations":["MTM B11:329"],"tags":["History & Memory"]},{"name":"Farmhouse","definition":"A farmhouse on the uphill road to Anastasia's grandmother on Naxos. Finnegan, Anastasia, and their donkey receive water at its well; the woman invites them to sit with <em>Kathisate</em>, but they have no time to stay [D:124]. The donkey is named Papapaleologus [D:125].","citations":["D:124","D:125"],"tags":["Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Farrell, Father","definition":"Father Walter Farrell, co-author with Martin J. Healy of <em>My Way of Life, the Summa Simplified for Everyone</em>. A passage credited to Farrel and Healy in the novel's collection of documents concerns the choices of heaven and hell made from moment to moment [MTM B5:177]. In the later part of <em>Archipelago</em>, Finnegan reads Father Farrell's books on the <em>Summa</em> and St. John of the Cross while attending Mass and preparing for possible death [A:277].","citations":["MTM B5:177","A:277"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Theology","Finnegan","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Fascist","definition":"An accusation used by several speakers. In Letitia's scrapbook Father Blevins calls his bishop a clerical-fascist and denies his right to have views [MTM B8:251]. Rollo McSorley spits the word at Tony Apostolo when they bet on Duffey's power to move a mule [MTM B4:129]. Marie Courtois attributes neo-fascistic indoctrination to Anastasia [D:33]. Casey's <em>Crock</em> attack calls Gabrielovitch a poisoner of the Fascist persuasion [A:129], and Casey tells Stein to denounce the Australian brigadier as a fascist fink [A:54]. Finnegan reports being called fascist, fink, scab, and fruit by the music patrons whom he has provoked with a silent jukebox record [A:264].","citations":["MTM B8:251","MTM B4:129","D:33","A:129","A:54","A:264"],"tags":["Language & Words","Journalism","Soldiers","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Fascist Fink","definition":"The accusation Casey tells Stein to make against the Australian brigadier, during the scheme exploiting Stein's mistaken belief that Tania, the stage persona of lance corporal Tommy Trouncer, is a woman whom the brigadier has brought onto the island [A:51-53]. Casey urges Stein to say that the brigadier had him beaten for his views and that a raid will expose moral turpitude. The raid produces an international military scandal and damages Stein and his American officer allies [A:54].","citations":["A:51-53","A:54"],"tags":["Australia","Soldiers","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Father (Duffey's)","definition":"Duffey denies having a father when Bagby says that he believes he knew him [MTM B2:43]. The opening account applies the biblical description of Melchisedech as without father, mother, or genealogy to Duffey, while acknowledging confusion over how those specifications fit him [MTM B1:6].","citations":["MTM B2:43","MTM B1:6"],"tags":["Duffey","Family","Identity","Old Testament"]},{"name":"Father (Finnegan's)","definition":"Giulio Solli, the Monster, who calls himself a Teras. He tells Duffey that he held Duffey's talisman to his wife's belly and placed it in the hands of his Italian son at birth [MTM B3:107]; the talisman had been given to Giulio in St. Louis [MTM B4:120]. Duffey identifies Finnegan as Giulio's son and says Giulio has been dead for at least ten years, while Bagby insists that he has recently met him [MTM B5:165]. In <em>Archipelago</em>, Finnegan meets his shabby, sorrowful father after death and takes him for beer near the station. Giulio has a scraggly moustache, root-like hands, and a deepening silence. Finnegan attacks him as a failed dockworker and old peasant, then recognizes, when Giulio weeps and leaves, that his father loved the family and did everything he could [A:244-245]. <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> returns to this encounter and Finnegan's realization that Giulio belonged to a monstrous apparatus and died as the Monster Forgotten [D:228-229].","citations":["MTM B3:107","MTM B4:120","MTM B5:165","A:244-245","D:228-229"],"tags":["Family","Finnegan","Duffey","Monsters & Creatures","The Dead","Magic & Sorcery","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Father (Henri Salvatore's)","definition":"Lawrence Salvatore, father of Henry Francis Salvatore [A:122]. Tired of disputes with Henry's teachers, he examines the boy and discovers that he is already educated; this happens early in Henry's thirteenth year [A:123]. After the war, Lawrence questions his son's haste to enter seminary and discusses Henry's fear for the Church [A:124-125].","citations":["A:122","A:123","A:124-125"],"tags":["Louisiana","Family","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Father (Theresa Piccone's)","definition":"Gaetano Piccone, called Papa Piccone, father of Theresa Antoinette Piccone and proprietor of the Star and Garter Theatre [A:94] [MTM B5:163]. He is flamboyant, enjoys gambling, and flourishes hundred-dollar bills [A:95]. He claims to have accidentally made his unsuccessful burlesque character Oliver Oscar Omygosh into a real man; Bagby remembers the man but disputes Papa's claim to have created him [MTM B5:167-168]. Teresa is Beth Keegan's goddaughter [MTM B5:162]. Patrick Stranahan has represented Papa in disputes; the two become friends and argumentative companions, drinking whisky at the Stranahans' and wine at the Piccones'. Papa secretly burlesques Patrick onstage as Hallohan J. O'Hoolihan [A:104]. Before Theresa's wedding, Monica reports Papa's plan to replace the organist with accordion playing in the aisles, which Maurine calls a joke. Maurine instead anticipates his bringing three barrels of wine and jugs to the reception so that, unlike the hosts at Cana, he will not be remembered for running out [A:105].","citations":["A:94","MTM B5:163","A:95","MTM B5:167-168","MTM B5:162","A:104","A:105"],"tags":["Family","Theater","Alcohol","Creation","Liturgy","New Testament"]},{"name":"Father Baumgartner","definition":"Priest involved in Hans Schultz's schooling at St. Joseph's in St. Gallen, Wisconsin [A:207-208]. John William Schultz quarrels with him over the elementary curriculum and demands Greek, Aquinas, and Albert for the children. Father Baumgartner replies that Hans is only nine, that he himself does not know Greek, and that English literacy should come first [A:208-209].","citations":["A:207-208","A:208-209"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Family","Theology"]},{"name":"Father of Lies","definition":"The Devil, whose reputation makes X unsure how to interpret his statements about which people belong to his party [A:139]. In the account of the Fiefdoms' struggle, the claim that morals have entirely disappeared is named as one of the Father of Lies' falsehoods; the narrator insists that morality continues to resist assault [MTM B8:246].","citations":["A:139","MTM B8:246"],"tags":["The Devil","Cosmic Struggle","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Father Time","definition":"The personification of time, conventionally an old man with a scythe. The goat-man resembles Father Time because he carries a scythe, but he uses it practically to cut wild grass and store winter fodder for his goats [A:194]. His name is Gautier [A:196].","citations":["A:194","A:196"],"tags":["Time & Eternity","Folklore"]},{"name":"Fatted Calf","definition":"In her letter to Finnegan, Loy Larkin says that Papa neglected her garden but killed the fatted calf for his returning prodigal daughter [A:27]. The phrase adapts the celebration of the returning son in Luke 15:23-24.","citations":["A:27"],"tags":["New Testament","Family","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Faulte des Boemes","definition":"The fault or heresy of the Bohemians, from Villon's “Ballade des menus propos.” Marie Monaghan quotes the line in the novel's spelling, \"Je congois le faulte des Boemes,\" in an in-person fashion. Salvation Sally claims the gift of tongues, interprets the phrase as the heresy of Bohemia, and asks who the heretics really are [A:8]. The glossary to Pierre Jannet's edition of Villon identifies the allusion with the followers of Jan Hus and Jerome of Prague.","citations":["A:8"],"tags":["Theology","Eastern Europe","Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Faust","definition":"Legendary figure who bargains away his soul to the Devil. Hans Schultz is identified with Dr. Faustus, Apollo, and Aquinas Redevivus [A:39], and later described as simultaneously Faust and Apollo [A:169]. In Teresa Piccone's account of the Argonauts, Hans is Orpheus and is manifested at a later time as Faust [MTM B7:234]. Private Gregory uses Faust's moment of decision as the model for his theory of possessed men: an obscure beginning, a hiatus and bargain, then power and acclaim won by the devils within them [A:84].","citations":["A:39","A:169","MTM B7:234","A:84"],"tags":["Folklore","The Devil","Identity","Literature"]},{"name":"Faustina","definition":"The sixth in the sequence of ladies' names given to the daily bottles shared by Finnegan, Charles Le Marin, Don Barnaby, and Johnny Duckwalk in their Basse-Terre paradise [D:235-236]. Faustina follows Etheldreda and precedes Georgina; on this unusually heavy drinking day the Four reach Hildegarde, the eighth bottle [D:243].","citations":["D:235-236","D:243"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Caribbean","Finnegan","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Feast of the Apparition","definition":"The feast Marie Monaghan names when announcing her engagement to Hans Schultz at the Lotus Eaters in Sydney. She calls the day the second Thursday of February and says she will never forget it [A:27-28]. In the story's 1943 chronology this is February 11, matching the feast of Our Lady of Lourdes, which commemorates the first apparition there.","citations":["A:27-28"],"tags":["Australia","Liturgy","History & Memory"]},{"name":"February 11, 1943","definition":"Date of Loy Larkin's letter to Finnegan from Cambeltown, New South Wales, inviting him and his friends to meet her and Margaret the following morning [A:27].","citations":["A:27"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","World War II"]},{"name":"February 15, 1943","definition":"Wedding date of John Gottfried Schultz, called Hans, and Marie Anna Monaghan at Annunciation Church in Sydney, Australia. Hans has no army permission to marry, so the wedding is kept secret for a while; the twenty or thirty guests are all Marie's relatives [A:31].","citations":["A:31"],"tags":["Australia","World War II","Family"]},{"name":"February 18th, 1943","definition":"Date Hans Schultz and the other boys returning from the Australian furlough report to the Troop Movement Office and are sent back to New Guinea [A:31]. Henry Salvatore had refused that furlough and had stayed behind [A:38].","citations":["A:31","A:38"],"tags":["World War II","Australia","Pacific Theater"]},{"name":"Federal Agrandisement","definition":"Casey Szymansky's explanation of the subject of Duffey's political analogies. Duffey objects that liberals who once wanted minimal government now want maximal government, a Leviathan that consumes the other whales [A:42].","citations":["A:42"],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Federal Buildings (New)","definition":"Potential sites for Finnegan's murals in Casey's proposal for gaining money and commissions through liberal political patronage. Party sanction would bring commissions worth thousands of dollars, including work in new federal buildings [A:183-184].","citations":["A:183-184"],"tags":["Finnegan","Visual Arts","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Federal Funding (Continuous-Flow)","definition":"Federal support obtained by Newton Prescott for the Casey Machine. Arguing that paid access favors the rich, he seeks Continuous-Flow Funding, Compensatory Counter-Flow Payments, and Impacted Entertainment Area Funding. He also secures shrine status for each machine's site, with Perpetual Care paid through Perpetual Guardian Payments to his own group; several forms of payment are guaranteed in perpetuity [CM:503] [CM:513]. His underlying concern is to preserve something of the machine against anticipated forgetting [CM:512-513].","citations":["CM:503","CM:513","CM:512-513"],"tags":["Money & Trade","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Fetch","definition":"A double or wraith. Duffey has shadows, fetches, and doubles, described as often fleshed, or at least believed by him to be so [MTM B4:141]. The Postscript describes the Irish hero-giant Fingal accompanied by nine black-cloaked doubles while he wears white [PS:478].\n\nDoppio di Pinne, called Dopey, is Finnegan's double. Papadiabolous II questions whether Dopey ever lived and whether Finnegan killed him [D:89]. He later says that Dopey was a seaman and sometime killer of the strange blood, whom Seaworthy summoned to kill Papadiabolous, but remains unsure how close the duality was [D:121]. He offers the further possibility that Dopey disappeared and was absorbed into Finnegan [D:122]. <em>Archipelago</em> calls Dopey Finnegan's fetch who died in his place, either in the <em>Brunhilde</em>'s cabin at Papadiabolous's hands or later in Seaworthy's Galveston trap [A:278].\n\nThe fictional review of Royce Rollins's <em>The Return of the Neanderthal: The Alien Within</em> attributes to Rollins the theory that doubles and fetches are ordinary manifestations of his interior aliens [D:240-242].","citations":["MTM B4:141","PS:478","D:89","D:121","D:122","A:278","D:240-242"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Giants & Titans","Ireland","Finnegan","Duffey","Devils (Characters)","Death & Mortality","Identity"]},{"name":"Fetish","definition":"A term associated with marks, symbolic objects, and rituals. Seaworthy bears a scar or tattoo inside his left wrist and claims to see the same mark beneath Finnegan's skin [D:10]. Finnegan perceives another, less literal mark in Seaworthy, indicating to him that the man has killed [D:16-17]. After the Naxos shooting, Finnegan's wrist seems to star like cracked glass and bleed beneath the skin before becoming the mark [D:145]. X later calls it the sign of the octopus; Finnegan dates it to the shooting and wonders whether he too belongs to the alien flesh, a category X still questions [D:156].\n\nBagby calls Duffey a fetish mark that fates and principalities touch or avoid in choosing their courses [MTM B10:302]. Rebeka Stein calls the absurd or incongruous device that triggers a dreamlike transformation a fetish transformation activator, exemplified by the potholes leading to alternate futures in Cleo Mahoney's play [MTM B10:293-294]. At the Final Conclave, Stone Hackenschmidt calls burning the Straw-Man Cap-Stone a fetish-ritual that has got out of hand when it produces enormous quantities of blood [MB:568].","citations":["D:10","D:16-17","D:145","D:156","MTM B10:302","MTM B10:293-294","MB:568"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Metaphysics","Ekklesia","Identity"]},{"name":"Fetish mark","definition":"Bagby's description of Duffey in a letter: like a sidewalk crack or roadside post, he is something that fates and principalities touch or avoid and perhaps use to determine their courses. Bagby insists that being such a fetish is not lowly, since even the grand powers cannot ignore him [MTM B10:302].","citations":["MTM B10:302"],"tags":["Duffey","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Fetish transformation activator","definition":"Rebeka Stein's name for the usually ridiculous or incongruous object that activates a transformation in a dream or detached experience. She praises the potholes in Cleo Mahoney's <em>Seven Roads</em>: stepping into one would bring Duffey into one of seven alternate scenes or futures, sometimes without his noticing the transition [MTM B10:293-294].","citations":["MTM B10:293-294"],"tags":["Theater","Time & Eternity","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Fetish-ritual","definition":"Stone Hackenschmidt's name for burning the Straw-Man Cap-Stone at the Final Conclave. The effigy, soaked in a lamb's blood, produces red smoke, blood-red rain, and a flood far beyond what ten thousand lambs could supply; Hackenschmidt remarks that the fetish-ritual has got out of hand [MB:568].","citations":["MB:568"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia"]},{"name":"Fiddlers' Greens","definition":"Duffey's name for the green places of amusement he hopes suit their green years. He asks whether the clubs are Fiddlers' Greens or Thelemes; Shirley Israel dismisses those as old-fashioned beside the fashionable establishments of Fleshpot Row [MTM B4:126].","citations":["MTM B4:126"],"tags":["Duffey","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Field, Elysian","definition":"Earlier inverted heading omitted Avenue; the source uses singular Field. See <em>Elysian Field Avenue</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Fiery Furnace","definition":"The ordeal of Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego in Daniel 3. In his longer, unsent letter to Duffey, Henri Salvatore credits Margaret Stone with reminding them to remain aflame, then adds the nightly self-examination question, \"Did I spend sufficient time in the Fiery Furnace today?\" [MTM B5:179-180].","citations":["MTM B5:179-180"],"tags":["Theology","Old Testament","Scripture"]},{"name":"Fifth Avenue (New York)","definition":"Manhattan street invoked in the opening epigraph attributed to Asimov: a shaved, neatly dressed Neanderthal could probably walk along New York's Fifth Avenue without attracting much notice [D:9].","citations":["D:9"],"tags":["Literature","Identity"]},{"name":"Fifth Heaven","definition":"Heavenly sphere used in Trinali Peterson's wine-glass poem. The half-globe of crystal resembles the transparent sphere of Fifth Heaven; its fragile stem supports that heaven's dome, and the wine holds the mystery of death and resurrection [A:231].","citations":["A:231"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Alcohol","Death & Mortality","Resurrection","Literature"]},{"name":"Fifty (Company of)","definition":"The resident regulars of the Old Wooden Ship tavern. The narrator lists forty-nine names and cries that one has been forgotten from the company of fifty [D:215-216]. Finnegan immerses himself in their company to recover assurance of humanity's reality [D:225]. The forgotten regular, Hildegarde Katt, is remembered in the following chapter [D:229].","citations":["D:215-216","D:225","D:229"],"tags":["Finnegan","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Figs","definition":"Fruit in the Boy King Duffey's groves, described as more real than Iowa's apple trees or Missouri's plum and peach trees [MTM B1:17]. Zabotski calls the sycamore the American Fig and uses it as a disparaging comparison for Bryan Blackstone [MTM B9:270]. The description of Naxos includes figs alongside olives, lemons, grapes, and honey [D:125]. In Ti's account of Amoy's earlier life as a bird working for thieves, Amoy steals figs and olives after progressing through millet, rice, coriander, and dates [A:80].","citations":["MTM B1:17","MTM B9:270","D:125","A:80"],"tags":["Islands","Greece","Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Figures","definition":"Representations, puppets, and persons treated as constructed figures. In Elroy Redheart's third red room, most figures are cheap Hong Kong plastic castings resembling Cracker Jack prizes; their arranged scenes depict feverish wealth, a hectic man, and a beautiful young woman [MTM B7:223]. The woman is shown giggling and sticking pins into the heart she holds in her hands [MTM B7:224].\n\nCharlotte Mullens says she chooses empty, pliable flesh-and-blood people, usually actors, and makes them compliant figures and extensions of her aura. Duffey wonders whether her sister and son are projections or ventriloquist's figures; Charlotte insists that her method contains nothing beyond nature [MTM B3:83].\n\nOther figures include the laughing, turbaned Christ from Turkish Anatolia, described as carved a thousand years earlier and freshly repainted within fifty years [MTM B3:104]; Teilhard's demiurgic apparition on St. Michael's altar, with McLuhan and McGonigal beside him [GD:497]; and Casey as a cult figure whom Mary Catherine Carruthers says earns more than thirty thousand dollars a week [MTM B12:377].\n\nTheresa Piccone writes that she works a ventriloquist's figures and that dummies prefer that name [A:49]. Trinali Peterson makes figures from discarded bottles, corks, and raffia, including Dresden figures, marionettes, Mexicans, donkeys, clowns, and prize-fighters [A:228].","citations":["MTM B7:223","MTM B7:224","MTM B3:83","MTM B3:104","GD:497","MTM B12:377","A:49","A:228"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Theater","Women Characters","Creation","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Filicudi","definition":"One of the Aeolian Islands off Sicily. In Anastasia's account of mermaid history, medieval mermaids lived off Sicily, especially around Salina and Filicudi [D:34-35].","citations":["D:34-35"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Islands","Italy"]},{"name":"Final Beatitude","definition":"The strong promise associated with the Fourteenth Voyage of the <em>Argo</em>, the fourth of the seven contingent futures. This voyage concerns ongoing beatitudes, the completed quest, and the enduring finding of the Golden Fleece; unlike the other contingencies, it is said to be certain to happen [MTM B10:300].","citations":["MTM B10:300"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Argonautica","Theology"]},{"name":"Final Conclave","definition":"The declared last assembly of the Ekklesia, or Crowd, held in Babylonia Bagascia to confirm the institution's dismantling [MB:561-563]. Paul XI, the Cap-Stone, is decapitated the day before it opens; the final evening's entertainment is the Voltaire Festival [MB:562-565]. Monsignor X serves the assembly. Hackenschmidt threatens to kill him for failing to anticipate a demand for a submachine gun, but X denies that he will be killed and produces a BAR instead [MB:566-567].","citations":["MB:561-563","MB:562-565","MB:566-567"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Fingal the Hero","definition":"Irish hero-giant invoked when Duffey meets Finnegan. Duffey recalls Fingal, or Finn McCool in another version, eating a hundred oysters each larger than a wagon wheel, and wonders whether the young man is an incognito hero or giant [MTM B5:173-174]. The Postscript describes Fingal in white sheep-wool attended by nine doubles in black sheep-wool [PS:478].","citations":["MTM B5:173-174","PS:478"],"tags":["Folklore","Giants & Titans","Ireland"]},{"name":"Finn McCool","definition":"Fionn mac Cumhaill, legendary leader of the Fianna, whose adventures are set in a third-century heroic age. Duffey calls Finnegan the original Finn McCool [MTM B5:169], and recalls the hero, also named Fingal in another version, eating a hundred oysters larger than wagon wheels [MTM B5:173-174]. Absalom Stein's notes report that Lafferty rejects a Joycean derivation of Finnegan and associates him with earlier Irish legend, implying identities with Finn McCool, Fingal, and Cú Chulainn [MB:549-550].","citations":["MTM B5:169","MTM B5:173-174","MB:549-550"],"tags":["Folklore","Ireland","Finnegan","Literary Figures"]},{"name":"Finnegan (John A. Solli)","definition":"Giovanni, or John, A. Solli, called Finnegan, born June 1, 1919, in New Orleans to Giulio Solli and Mary McCracken; his brother is Giacomo and his sister Patricia [A:173] [MTM B3:76]. Vincent explains Finnegan as his nickname [A:2]. Beyond the birth and family facts, the narrator warns that accounts of his life disagree [A:174-175].\n\nFinnegan is dark and lean, with a long banana nose, restless facial muscles, and a mouth continually in motion [A:1]. His movement suggests extraordinary grace, strength, and speed, while his face is a gargoyle's comic mask and his speech an unmatched mixture of New Orleans, waterfront Irish, Italian, and Cajun elements [A:6]. He is a painter, seaman, and wanderer [A:175] [D:114], a former steeplejack and accomplished burglar who conducts upper-story break-ins to obtain evidence [D:162], and a former boxer [D:172-173]. He works a nine-hour shift on the New Orleans docks before moving into the Pelican Press print shop [A:116]. He also markets smuggled diamonds for Joseph, whom he meets in Freetown, sending him drafts over the following years [D:93-95].\n\nHe belongs to the Dirty Five with Vincent Stranahan, Hans Schultz, Henry Salvatore, and Casey Szymansky, brought together in the army [A:38-39] [MTM B5:171]. He is Iason in their Argonaut identities [A:39]. In Duffey's account of his twelve talismanic creations, Finnegan is the central creation and the salt of their lives [MTM B5:161] [MTM B5:174]. Giulio, who calls himself a Teras, describes giving Duffey's talisman to his son at birth [MTM B3:107].\n\nFinnegan repeatedly experiences amnesia when passing between his upper and lower lives, sometimes losing months of memory and even his name [D:11-12] [D:18] [D:50]. He wonders what connects his successive returns from oblivion and whether he belongs to another human race [D:91]. His sense of alienness alternates with recognition of kinship, especially with Anastasia [D:20]. At confession he describes memories of belonging to the Devil's kindred and an old animal rising within him; the priest absolves his whole person, in all its aspects [D:119-120]. Anastasia's grandmother places him among the violent people of the other blood [D:128]; Anastasia calls both herself and him teras and says they try to make amends [D:139]. Papadiabolous II includes the three of them among the Non-people [D:121]. These identities do not settle all the competing accounts of Finnegan's existence.\n\nHis clairvoyance includes perceiving Seaworthy's killer's mark, claiming to read the waves' Chaldee writing, and reading the concealed contents of Dopey's letter [D:16-17] [D:38] [D:50-51]. He reconstructs and paints Papadiabolous II's true face before seeing it exposed in death [D:63]. He speaks effective Spanish and Italian and can imitate almost any voice [D:57] [D:114]. He uses a knife in his attack on Gerecke and is an excellent rifle shot [D:164-165] [D:197].\n\nFinnegan paints the <em>Brunhilde</em>'s barroom with scenes of Sindbad's voyages, including the ship's people as the dead; he tells Anastasia that when he paints them dead, they are dead [D:59] [D:61]. He works on a large Yellow Period painting while helping Duffey establish the Pelican Press in New Orleans [MTM B6:190]. In Manhattan, under the name Gregory van Ghi, he successfully sells Orange Period paintings and obtains a standing dealer's commission [A:191]. His account of studying under Van Ghi in Florence is challenged by Patricia, who says Van Ghi is an invented name; the narrator of his Florence devil-painting tale also denies its stated circumstances [A:175] [D:252-256].\n\nThe painting <em>The Resurrection of Count Finnegan</em> arrives at Duffey's New Orleans gallery after reports of Finnegan's death. It shows a much older Finnegan rising from a sea cave and repairing his decayed flesh; his banana nose has gained nobility, while his humor, pain, and physical energy remain recognizable [MB:550-551]. Its other half depicts Joseph Cardinal Hedayat of Antioch, aged into an appearance identical to the pictured Finnegan, though the two men do not yet look identical in ordinary life [MB:552]. Hilton purchases the painting for twenty thousand dollars and leaves it displayed in Duffey's gallery [MB:553]. Later, Finnegan paints the thirteen <em>Deaths of the Cardinals</em> on the <em>Argo</em>'s Bread and Wine Room bulkheads. The narrator celebrates their power while raising the problem of whether Hedayat, his accomplished painter-double, could have made them [MTM B13:452].\n\nFinnegan sails on the <em>Brunhilde</em> as a crewman and shares a cabin with Don Lewis and Joe Cross, both counted among his friends [D:27] [D:30-31]. He resists claims that Seaworthy and the Devil own him [D:40]. He has helped Seaworthy bury the man whom X later identifies as Papadiabolous I, Gregory McIfreann [D:75-76] [D:153] [D:285]. The apparently resurrected Papa frightens him, but he needles and challenges him [D:22] [D:30]. He later understands that the Papa he knows is a replacement and ally: X identifies the dead man's face as the Irish policeman Noonan or Flaherty, and subsequently explains that he is Gregory McColum, Papadiabolous II [D:113] [D:154] [D:285-286].\n\nAnastasia is his beloved companion and declares herself his sister in a sense he does not yet understand [D:20] [D:69]. Finnegan is initially drawn to Marie Courtois's beauty and strength [D:37], but recoils from her ecstatic admission of killing Don Lewis. He and Joe Cross attempt to return and kill her, until Papa intervenes [D:112-113]. Finnegan buries Don Lewis in the sand near Tangier [D:110-112] [A:188]. At Naxos he rushes down the mountain after hearing gunfire, only to find Papadiabolous II and Anastasia already dead [D:140-147]. He escapes into hiding with Manuel [D:148-149]. With Mr. X, an acquaintance from both his lives, he tries to understand what happened [D:152-154]. Anastasia's loss becomes his first death in the narrative's figurative sense, and he dedicates the remaining shadow of his life to robbing and killing Seaworthy [D:156-158].\n\nIn New York he climbs into the conspirators' meeting, kills Gerecke while intending to kill Seaworthy, and steals their immensely valuable suitcase [D:162-166]. He escapes with Doll Delancy, who becomes his companion; Mr. X subsequently joins them in Baltimore and takes charge of their protection [D:167] [D:171-174] [D:177]. Finnegan later immerses himself in the Old Wooden Ship's Company of Fifty to recover assurance of humanity's reality [D:225]. Before leaving that town, he attacks the Pirate Hotel, kills two guards and another person, but hears Seaworthy's laugh behind him [D:231]. He reaches the Basse-Terre paradise with Le Marin, Don Barnaby, and Johnny Duckwalk [D:233-236]. Eventually he returns for a watched graveyard rendezvous with Doll and X, calling Seaworthy his odd uncle as he approaches their final confrontation [D:275-276].\n\nFinnegan's deaths remain disputed. Stein records incompatible accounts of his death or survival in the Philippines, the <em>Brunhilde</em>, Tangier, Naxos, Galveston, and the Marianao Coast near Havana, and believes he is still alive [MB:548-550]. In Cuba he is called the Sleeping Man; witnesses disagree about whether he has already left his sea-cave tomb [MTM B12:370-372]. He later appears on the <em>Argo</em>, where he and Biloxi Brannagan exchange banter and where he is explicitly named as Hedayat's double; Herman Hercules is the double of Erculo [MTM B13:453]. The list of pursued doubles confirms those pairings [MB:579]. At the conclave on the Mooring-Rock, at least one of the supposed doubles is believed to be a surviving true cardinal, preserving the succession [MB:596]. Finnegan abstains while the other twelve vote for him, and X crowns him Pope Finnegan the First [MB:597].\n\nThe closing words about the day the Devil is dead belong to Doll's poem <em>Diabolique</em>, which also foretells Finnegan's approaching death [D:293-294].","citations":["A:173","MTM B3:76","A:2","A:174-175","A:1","A:6","A:175","D:114","D:162","D:172-173","A:116","D:93-95","A:38-39","MTM B5:171","A:39","MTM B5:161","MTM B5:174","MTM B3:107","D:11-12","D:18","D:50","D:91","D:20","D:119-120","D:128","D:139","D:121","D:16-17","D:38","D:50-51","D:63","D:57","D:164-165","D:197","D:59","D:61","MTM B6:190","A:191","D:252-256","MB:550-551","MB:552","MB:553","MTM B13:452","D:27","D:30-31","D:40","D:75-76","D:153","D:285","D:22","D:30","D:113","D:154","D:285-286","D:69","D:37","D:112-113","D:110-112","A:188","D:140-147","D:148-149","D:152-154","D:156-158","D:162-166","D:167","D:171-174","D:177","D:225","D:231","D:233-236","D:275-276","MB:548-550","MTM B12:370-372","MTM B13:453","MB:579","MB:596","MB:597","D:293-294"],"tags":["Popes","Argonauts (Characters)","Ships","Sailing","Crew & Sailors","New Orleans","Finnegan","Duffey","Devils (Characters)","Monsters & Creatures","Combat","Visual Arts","Crime","Money & Trade","Occupations","Family","Metaphysics","Identity","Death & Mortality","Resurrection","Creation","Ghosts & Spirits"]},{"name":"Finnegan Cycle","definition":"Body of legend studied by Absalom Stein, who says he has not determined its exact relation to the <em>Argo</em> legend [MTM B6:188]. His uncertainty is repeated as the opening epigraph of “How Many Miles to Babylon” [MB:550].","citations":["MTM B6:188","MB:550"],"tags":["Folklore","Literature","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Finnegan Diamonds","definition":"Diamonds marketed by Finnegan through his partnership with Joseph the Haussa. He leaves Freetown with a sack of stones and sends Joseph drafts as he sells them over the following years [D:94-95]. Hillary Hilton says he has handled Finnegan diamonds [A:255]. Duffey later tells the Devil that he already wears them and that all Finnegan’s friends do likewise [MTM B10:303].","citations":["D:94-95","A:255","MTM B10:303"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Money & Trade","Africa"]},{"name":"Finnegan O'Hannigan McGillicuddy","definition":"Name Finnegan gives Howland Jerome Howland during his wanderings. When Howland doubts it, Finnegan admits that he has just invented most of it, though everyone calls him Finnegan [A:178].","citations":["A:178"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Finnegan Production","definition":"Finnegan's proposed intervention to have Hillary Hilton reclaim his wife Mary Jean and displace Casey Szymansky, giving the friends another chance to straighten Casey out [A:252]. Finnegan promises that his part in the production will be funny and require a couple of gadgets [A:253].","citations":["A:252","A:253"],"tags":["Finnegan","Family"]},{"name":"Finnegan Visitation","definition":"Teresa Piccone Stranahan’s phrase in a private letter for an experience of Finnegan’s ghostly presence. She says he inhabits his friends, and reports that Stein mistook his first such experience, on Passion Sunday afternoon in 1948, for a visitation at Finnegan’s death [MTM B6:188].","citations":["MTM B6:188"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Finnegan","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Finnerty, Vincent","definition":"Member of the rich money-and-commerce circle around Duffey and Letitia during Chicago's golden age [MTM B3:100]. He is one of the people described as mentalists about money who foresee the 1929 financial collapse [MTM B4:110].","citations":["MTM B3:100","MTM B4:110"],"tags":["Duffey","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Fire","definition":"Fire appears as practical heat, destructive force, and sacred sign. Finnegan tells of an open fire in the north woods beside which a young hobo aged overnight [D:14]. Papa Devil builds a beach campfire after the Padre Island meal; its flickering light later picks out the cliffs of Seaworthy's granite-like face [D:34] [D:40] [D:48]. Finnegan and Howland make a pit fire to cook rabbits [A:178], while the Australian picnic party sings around charcoal briquettes in a large plate supplied by a hotel girl [A:31]. Hans says he painted carnival pictures of Zippo the Fire-Eater [A:230].\n\nFinnegan tells Doll that his kind set people on fire when they dislike them [D:182], and later broods on burning cities and the possibility of hellfire consuming the earth [D:256]. After his attack on Gerecke, he escapes down iron fire-ladders [D:165]. A noble white horse intended for Casey's Leader rally had been bought from a fire department giving up its horses [MTM B4:117].\n\nHenri Salvatore's letter credits Margaret Stone with the injunction to remain aflame [MTM B5:179-180]. At the Opera House, Margaret produces holy-ghost fire about her tongue, ignites human ashes in her mouth, and pours orange flame over herself without apparent burning [MTM B11:348]. The Fire-Drake Frolic kills further human victims to Fritz's <em>Fandango in Three Flames</em> [MTM B11:350].\n\nAfter Duffey is killed, three slack-faced men burn his flesh, sinews, and viscera into perpetually hot ashes, leaving his bones separate [MTM B13:442]. The Straw-Man Cap-Stone is burned in the stove at the Final Conclave, producing extraordinary blood and red smoke [MB:568].","citations":["D:14","D:34","D:40","D:48","A:178","A:31","A:230","D:182","D:256","D:165","MTM B4:117","MTM B5:179-180","MTM B11:348","MTM B11:350","MTM B13:442","MB:568"],"tags":["Miracles","Ghosts & Spirits","Finnegan","Duffey","Ekklesia","Food & Dining","Theater","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Fire Drake Frolic, The","definition":"A sequence in the Decatur Street Opera House's blood entertainment, in which further human victims are killed to the accompaniment of Fritz's <em>Fandango in Three Flames</em> [MTM B11:350].","citations":["MTM B11:350"],"tags":["Theater","Monsters & Creatures","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Fire Drakes","definition":"See <em>Firedrakes</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Ships","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Fire-Eater (Zippo the)","definition":"Zippo the Fire-Eater, a carnival performer whose picture Hans Schultz includes among the many act pictures he says he painted for carnivals [A:230].","citations":["A:230"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Carnival"]},{"name":"Firedrakes","definition":"Fabulous creatures appearing among the discarded remnants of the old world. Margaret Stone reports that a half-man, half-fire-drake bites a woman; when examined, it is only rubber, though the woman is diagnosed with infectious draconitis [MTM B11:309]. The Pop Historians' safari also attacks a building in which human remnants, morphic dragons, and papier-mâché fire-lizards have barricaded themselves [MTM B11:338]. Kasmir Gorshok says he can summon firedrakes to the <em>Argo</em>. The Professor recommends a firedrake at each fireplace, since blind spirits may enter through such neglected openings, to help exclude the Man of Mystery [MTM B13:456]. After the security arrangements are made, the firedrakes patrol constantly [MTM B13:458].","citations":["MTM B11:309","MTM B11:338","MTM B13:456","MTM B13:458"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Ships","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Firenze","definition":"Florence, Italy, the setting of Finnegan's story about studying painting under Van Ghi [D:252]. In his tale, he paints a devil whom Father Le Faye recognizes and exorcises, and whom Van Ghi calls an old repeater appearing in many works of art; both advise burning the picture [D:253-255]. The narrator then says Finnegan did paint a devil, but not under the circumstances he has just described [D:256].","citations":["D:252","D:253-255","D:256"],"tags":["The Devil","Italy","Finnegan","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Firm, The","definition":"The Stranahan family law firm, proposed as a place where Vincent could learn law alongside extension courses instead of attending college [A:103]. His father Patrick is a successful attorney [A:94].","citations":["A:103","A:94"],"tags":["Family","Occupations"]},{"name":"First Day (Creation)","definition":"The creation imagery of <em>Archipelago</em>'s opening: the fresh morning evokes the making of the world, while beer is available because barley and hops have already been made on the third day. Finnegan, cast as the first man, drinks the first beer [A:1].","citations":["A:1"],"tags":["Alcohol","Creation","Finnegan"]},{"name":"First Day (Finnegan)","definition":"First day of Finnegan's fighting binge, when he says he won thirteen consecutive fights before losing one [D:191-192]. He separately describes the railroad man who broke his nose and disproved his boast that he could take two punches to give one; the passage does not identify that man as the opponent in the first loss [D:192].","citations":["D:191-192","D:192"],"tags":["Finnegan","Combat"]},{"name":"First Death of Finnegan","definition":"The death named by the narrator after Anastasia’s killing and Finnegan’s vow to devote his remaining “shadow life” to hunting Seaworthy [D:157-158]. The narrator distinguishes it from a later physical death on another island, while saying that this first death has a finality of its own [D:158]. The following chapter describes the shadow life as freeing Finnegan from the unities and the heroic conventions [D:161]. Doll later recalls his saying that their adventures are what he does after he is dead [D:173].","citations":["D:157-158","D:158","D:161","D:173"],"tags":["Finnegan","Death & Mortality","Identity"]},{"name":"First Executive","definition":"Title under which Bandicoot Blackstone says his son John will run the family businesses while Bandicoot remains chairman of their boards. This succession is discussed while Bryan Blackstone is dying and expected not to survive the day [MTM B9:288].","citations":["MTM B9:288"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Family","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"First Peter 5, 8-9","definition":"Biblical exhortation to vigilance against the Devil, pictured as a prowling lion, and to resistance in faith. A version of its opening appears among <em>More Than Melchisedech</em>'s collected documents, pointedly credited to \"Dotty O'Toole. Sonnet\" [MTM B5:175].","citations":["MTM B5:175"],"tags":["New Testament","Scripture","The Devil","Literature"]},{"name":"First Speaker, The","definition":"The first lecturer at the Royal Pop Historians' sessions in Duffey's buildings. He speaks in a voice compared to a thunder colt's and defines the present as the all-embracing moment [MTM B11:323]. His gaudy turban carries a sparkling gem or eye that makes his visible face seem false. He presents history as everything in that moment, subject to the historians' continuing reconstruction and revision [MTM B11:324].","citations":["MTM B11:323","MTM B11:324"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Metaphysics","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Firster","definition":"Term associated with Peter in the earlier glossary’s account of the “Paean.” The earlier glossary cites the opening “Paean” of <em>Archipelago</em>, page ix. That preliminary page is absent from the supplied copy, so the reading remains unverified.","citations":[],"tags":["Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Fish","definition":"Fish furnish meals, marvelous sea life, and figurative language. Rough fish are continually added to the Big Pot [MTM B6:196]. In futuristic Louisiana, Honeybucket Kincaid's tuned electrodes gather nine-pound alligator gar and fourteen-pound Blooper Fish for Biloxi and Duffey's meal [MTM B13:416-417]. Whistling blue fish occupy Bayougoula Park's uncanny fountain [MTM B11:331]. Duffey worries that Casey Gorshok's devil-fish mascot will recover the last scrap of the dangerous logic paper [MTM B13:430].\n\nThe Fanged Fish is a remembered waterfront establishment at Ogopo [MTM B13:407]. The <em>Argo</em> knows migrating seas that travel with their fish [MTM B13:405]; its talking oak recalls St. Brandon's encounter with the great fish Jascoyne [MTM B13:404]. Coryphaena Fish rise above the waves blowing shell horns after Finnegan and his companions board, a salute associated with a present or future pope aboard the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:450-451]. Sea creatures are said to enjoy the sunken bells of ships and cities [MB:575].\n\nHenri Salvatore says some <em>Argo</em> mariners have fishy flesh [MTM B12:386]. Mary Louise calls Bagby's inexplicable letters as fishy as the Gascinade River [MTM B7:219]. Eva fears that paying for her bar with old fifty-dollar gold pieces will look fishy [MTM B13:414]. In his commentary on Casey, Finnegan calls him fishy as the Grand Banks and says he attracts weak-fish opinions [PG:543-544].","citations":["MTM B6:196","MTM B13:416-417","MTM B11:331","MTM B13:430","MTM B13:407","MTM B13:405","MTM B13:404","MTM B13:450-451","MB:575","MTM B12:386","MTM B7:219","MTM B13:414","PG:543-544"],"tags":["Popes","Ships","Seas & Oceans","Fishing","Louisiana","Finnegan","Duffey","Food & Dining","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Fish Market","definition":"Maurine Stranahan's teasing name for her family's Club Room, where mounted fish and gun cases display the male Stranahans' sporting interests. She also calls the men the Rod and Gun Club and says her live-bait fishing shows up their fly fishing [A:103].","citations":["A:103"],"tags":["Fishing","Family"]},{"name":"Fish Spears","definition":"Two- and three-tined implements that Hans Schultz cites while disputing the saying that fingers were made before forks. He distinguishes ancient fish spears from untined stabbers used as eating forks; his claim that utensils predate fingers is part of the conversation's conceit [A:4].","citations":["A:4"],"tags":["Fishing","Food & Dining","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Fishtail people","definition":"The mermaid people from whom Anastasia Demetriades claims descent on Naxos [D:115]. In her account the fishtail is disappearing: most surviving mermaids are now two-footed, and her own family has produced no fishtail for six generations [D:36].","citations":["D:115","D:36"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Islands","Greece","Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Fishy","definition":"Mary Louise Bagby's description of the unexplained correspondence between Bagby and Duffey: \"as fishy as the Gascinade River.\" She objects that Bagby never writes letters; he answers that these belong to another context outside daily time [MTM B7:219].","citations":["MTM B7:219"],"tags":["Language & Words","Time & Eternity","Family"]},{"name":"Fitzjames, James","definition":"Young scholar Hans Schultz meets among New York's secondhand bookshops. Fitzjames begs a quarter for <em>Jaufre and Brunissende</em>; Hans buys him the book, follows him to a park to read, and shares giant bagels and a quart of beer [A:224]. Fitzjames says he sleeps in friends' hallways and on their sofas, calls himself his circle's most intelligent and least charming member, and describes the group as the new leaven [A:225]. He remarks that fish and old bread are the food of poverty [A:234]. He and Hans are the only Romans at Trinali Peterson's burial and find the service inadequate [A:240].","citations":["A:224","A:225","A:234","A:240"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Alcohol","Food & Dining","Literature","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Five Dollar Gold Piece","definition":"Gold coin Duffey produces by snapping his fingers and gives to Gretchen Sisler beside a park [MTM B2:38-39]. Later he rubs his hands together and pours seven United States five-dollar gold pieces onto a library table. Patrick Stranahan observes that they were minted about ten years earlier and suggests that Duffey could have carried them beforehand; Duffey insists that he performs magic rather than tricks [MTM B5:167].","citations":["MTM B2:38-39","MTM B5:167"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Five Foot Shelf","definition":"Absalom Stein's five-foot shelf of old humor magazines, from which he reads jokes on the Anopheles Network, his G.I. radio station [A:46].","citations":["A:46"],"tags":["Journalism","Literature","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Five Roses","definition":"One of the mild whiskies offered at Falo and Nauti's newly opening jungle cabaret on Luzon. Finnegan and Vincent are its first customers, and Falo brings water to accompany the Five Roses [A:63-64].","citations":["A:63-64"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Flagship of Abd-Aliah","definition":"An identity of the <em>Argo</em> in the history told by the talking oak in its wheel. After serving as the <em>Anthony Ship</em> at Actium, it becomes Abd-Aliah of the Sea's flagship and carries his beautiful daughter, who has a tail. Abd-Aliah later sells it to Sindbad of El-Basrah [MTM B13:404].","citations":["MTM B13:404"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Ships","Identity"]},{"name":"Flaherty","definition":"Alias of Gregory McColum Papadiabolous II, the Irish cop who also uses Noonan and takes his brother Papadiabolous I's place [D:285] [D:289]. X recognizes him from Finnegan's sketch as the valuable ally killed on the Naxos landing [D:154]. Finnegan has already found his shot and bludgeoned body there [D:146].","citations":["D:285","D:289","D:154","D:146"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Ireland","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Flame Man (comic strip)","definition":"Comic strip in which Dotty Yekouris says the Slant-Faced Men have appeared [MTM B7:225]. Duffey later finds their names, Amraphel, Arioch, and Thadal, given almost casually in the strip, where the three are depicted as stooges [MTM B7:227].","citations":["MTM B7:225","MTM B7:227"],"tags":["Literature","Identity"]},{"name":"Flap-jacking","definition":"One of the minor popular arts listed among the lively practices of daily life that are endangered, degraded, or disappear during the massive trashing of the arts after the Devil's release [MTM B6:207-208].","citations":["MTM B6:207-208"],"tags":["Food & Dining","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Flash girl","definition":"Fashionable female companion in Casey Szymansky's tenth imagined world, the world of money, prestige, and social standing. He wants a flash car and a flash girl, choosing whichever of Evelyn, Mary Jean, or Mary Carruthers grows up prettiest [A:160].","citations":["A:160"],"tags":["Dreams","Women Characters","Social Class"]},{"name":"Flat","definition":"An editorial description of Finnegan's living quarters in the old print shop taken over for the Pelican Press: cots and a cook stove stand at the back of a cluttered shop-like building [A:116]. The separate phrase \"fell flat\" describes Duffey stumbling over Crissie Cristofero's future sign on Dumaine Street [MTM B12:358].","citations":["A:116","MTM B12:358"],"tags":["New Orleans","Finnegan","Duffey"]},{"name":"Flats, Jackass","definition":"One of the regions named in Don Lewis's tale of three American soldiers lost on Willy Jones Island after Santee and Casper kill the elusive man called Yokipoki. Despite its name, Jackass Flats is the most precipitous country the narrator has seen [D:44-46].","citations":["D:44-46"],"tags":["Islands","Soldiers","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Flaying of Cardinal Artemis","definition":"One of the thirteen paintings in the <em>Deaths of the Cardinals</em> series on the <em>Argo</em>'s Bread and Wine Room bulkheads, showing Cardinal Artemis's execution. It is singled out among the surpassing works attributed to Count Finnegan, although the narrator also raises the possibility of his painter-double Cardinal Hedayat [MTM B13:452].","citations":["MTM B13:452"],"tags":["Finnegan","Visual Arts","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Fleece Itself","definition":"The Great Golden Fleece of Colchis, whose combings Casey Gorshok makes into a golden mask, neck scarf, and gauntlets for the fleshless Melchisedech Duffey [MTM B13:443]. Despite their sacred material and their maker's mastery, the garments sometimes impair Duffey's sensing, touch, and impulses [MTM B13:444].","citations":["MTM B13:443","MTM B13:444"],"tags":["Argonautica","Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Ships"]},{"name":"Fleece, Golden","definition":"The Golden Fleece of Colchis, object of Jason and the Argonauts' quest. Stranahan's poem recalls the friends sailing the ancient <em>Argo</em> in pursuit of golden cargo [A:91], and Rosemary tells the Sergeant that he will find the fleece without recognizing his success [A:89]. The <em>Argo</em>'s logs record the fleece's discovery as a brilliant early triumph [MTM B13:421]. Its finding abides forever in the Fourteenth Voyage's \"Quest Accomplished\" motif [MTM B10:300]. Threads combed from it protect a magus on an icy cliff from freezing, falling, and death [MTM B13:397-399]; later Casey makes its golden fabric into Duffey's mask, scarf, and gauntlets [MTM B13:443].","citations":["A:91","A:89","MTM B13:421","MTM B10:300","MTM B13:397-399","MTM B13:443"],"tags":["Argonautica","Greek Mythology","Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Ships"]},{"name":"Flesh Weed Eater","definition":"An agency of destruction named by the child hero among the Royal Pop People. Even the flesh weed eater cannot dispose of certain bright, strong, crookedly talented human weeds; those must be destroyed by thunder colts [MTM B11:334-335].","citations":["MTM B11:334-335"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Flesh Weeds","definition":"The child hero's contemptuous term for the human remnants whom the Royal Pop People are eager to eliminate while determining who qualifies as a thunder person [MTM B11:334].","citations":["MTM B11:334"],"tags":["Social Class","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Fleshpot Row","definition":"Chicago entertainment row including Herm's, Curley Q, Seven Steps Down, Blow Brass, The Farmer's Daughter, and Dung Heap, frequented by Duffey and his circle. Rollo McSorley recommends it as the remaining home of Chicago-Hot Jazz, while the narrator confirms the music but condemns its worst tendencies. Letitia and Demetrio object to the establishments' atmosphere [MTM B4:125-126].","citations":["MTM B4:125-126"],"tags":["Duffey","Bars & Taverns","Music"]},{"name":"Fleshpots","definition":"Places of indulgence: Bagby frequents the fleshpots of Chestnut Street [MTM B2:62]. Rollo and Josephine McSorley urge Duffey toward gaudy establishments on Randolph Street, defending fleshpots if they serve Irish stew; Letitia finds their atmosphere stifling [MTM B4:125].","citations":["MTM B2:62","MTM B4:125"],"tags":["Duffey","Bars & Taverns","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Flies","definition":"Duffey pictures a few perceptive people as flies who notice when the molasses trapping them hardens into amber, while most recognize only that something is wrong [MTM B9:268]. In his commentary on Casey's possible eschatological role, he calls premature antichrists flies buzzing about the Lord of the Flies [PG:534-535].","citations":["MTM B9:268","PG:534-535"],"tags":["Apocalyptic","Duffey","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Flintskin","definition":"Insult aimed at Duffey by the leader of three young men after Duffey knocks them down. The speaker couples \"old flintskin\" with \"old muleskin\" and \"old camelskin,\" continuing the Great Day encounter's imagery of old skins that must be discarded [GD:482-483].","citations":["GD:482-483"],"tags":["Duffey","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Floating palaces","definition":"Hillary Hilton's description of three garbage scows that he says he has refurbished and sold to an Arabian Gulf potentate [A:255].","citations":["A:255"],"tags":["Ships","Money & Trade","Middle East"]},{"name":"Floating restaurant","definition":"Unidentified restaurant supposedly associated with John Mogul; the proposed reference at <em>Archipelago</em>, page 122, does not establish this establishment or ownership.","citations":[],"tags":["Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Flood","definition":"The biblical deluge of Genesis 6-9. In Casey's school notes, Hinnegan places the Milesians in Ireland before it, prompting Casey's joke about Noah carrying two Irishmen on the ark [A:164-165]. Jimmy Hansen calls the Bushmaster-Schultz drinking contest the greatest event since the flood dried [A:19]. A Pattenite reader dates the Floodtide Catastrophes, sudden mountain formation, and Noah's flotation to about 2800 BC in Zabotski's <em>Pristine History of the World</em>, comparing this with Fundamentaler chronology [MTM B9:278]. Zabotski says mountains rose beneath the ark and insists that his own approaching flood will be the original, earlier floods merely practice [MTM B9:281] [MTM B9:287].","citations":["A:164-165","A:19","MTM B9:278","MTM B9:281","MTM B9:287"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Ships","Ireland","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Floodtide Catastrophes","definition":"Catastrophes dated to about 2800 BC in Zabotski's <em>Pristine History of the World</em>, according to a Pattenite reader who finds its chronology nearly identical to that of the Fundamentalers. The same cluster includes the instantaneous rise of the Alpine-Himalayan System and the flotation of Noah's barge. Zabotski calls the agreement possibly accidental [MTM B9:278].","citations":["MTM B9:278"],"tags":["Old Testament","Ships","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Flores Sea","definition":"One of the seas in the catalogue of waters sailed by the <em>Argo</em> and Melchisedech Duffey, listed between the Java and Bali seas [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Florida","definition":"State where Finnegan, Doll, and X stay in lake country, fish, and drink at a shady bait house [D:180-182]. They drive to Orlando, Kissimee, Pine Castle, Apopka, and sometimes Daytona [D:183]. Finnegan later reports that the leftfooted killer has been asking for him in Winter Garden [D:193]. In Le Marin's sea story, Florida is where he comes ashore after hiding for three days aboard the seized <em>Sarcophagus</em> [D:245].","citations":["D:180-182","D:183","D:193","D:245"],"tags":["Finnegan","Fishing","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Flour","definition":"Wheat and rye flour form the Danish-bread Christ-head in Duffey's art collection; the narrative associates such loaves with Corpus Christi Day in Denmark [MTM B3:103]. Flour is also among the provisions Finnegan unloads from Papapaleologus's panniers at Anastasia Grandmother's house [D:126].","citations":["MTM B3:103","D:126"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Visual Arts","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Flower Growing","definition":"One of the popular arts listed among the lively practices degraded by the deformed counterparts that emerge after the Devil's release [MTM B6:207].","citations":["MTM B6:207"],"tags":["History & Memory"]},{"name":"Flowers","definition":"The underground sunshine of New Orleans bursts through gaps in the pavement like exploding red, yellow, and purple flowers named Philos, Eros, and Agape. Hot purple Agape is the theme flower of the hundred-and-twenty-month late summer shared by Duffey and Letitia [MTM B7:211]. Earlier, the chubby young woman at St. Malachy's takes a red rose; she is subsequently identified as Letitia Koch [MTM B3:88-91].","citations":["MTM B7:211","MTM B3:88-91"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Women Characters","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Flute (Magic)","definition":"The Magic Flute, one of the celebrated objects in the catalogue of the <em>Argo</em>'s discoveries, alongside the Ring of the Nebelungs and the Philosopher's Stone [MTM B13:421-422].","citations":["MTM B13:421-422"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Ships","Music"]},{"name":"Flute (Recorder)","definition":"Old-style recorder flute Duffey builds from walnut and Philippine mahogany, with brass stop-keys and filigree, while helping establish the Pelican Press [MTM B6:190]. Letitia prefers it to his banjo because he cannot sing while playing it [MTM B7:226]. After her death, he plays the flute through a difficult hour of paperwork [MTM B8:254]. He also performs on recorder and banjo during the intermission of <em>Seven Roads</em> at the Ursuline Academy [MTM B12:366].","citations":["MTM B6:190","MTM B7:226","MTM B8:254","MTM B12:366"],"tags":["Duffey","Music","Theater","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Flute bands","definition":"Musical ensembles named among the popular arts whose style and vitality are degraded in the massive trashing of the arts [MTM B6:207]. Duffey had hoped to form a flute band as an occasional alternative to his neighborhood string band [MTM B6:190].","citations":["MTM B6:207","MTM B6:190"],"tags":["Music","Duffey","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Fly Fishing","definition":"One of the popular arts included among the lively practices being degraded in the trashing of the arts [MTM B6:207].","citations":["MTM B6:207"],"tags":["Fishing","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Flying mule","definition":"Mule Duffey transports by summoned giant hands at the Pont du Sable Traffic Trestle on November 13, 1935, after Morris Poor and Rollo McSorley challenge him [MTM B4:127] [MTM B4:130-132]. The largest of three mules moves thirty feet across a half-street to a traffic island. Witnesses and newspaper photographs offer varying accounts, though the narrator affirms that giant hands performed the feat [MTM B4:131-133]. The initial claim that this is Duffey's last public magic is immediately corrected by recalling his puppet shows with Letitia [MTM B4:133].","citations":["MTM B4:127","MTM B4:130-132","MTM B4:131-133","MTM B4:133"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Giants & Titans","Duffey","Journalism"]},{"name":"Flying Rings","definition":"Apparatus used with the turning pole in Hans Schultz's agility training. His physical education also includes twice-daily runs on the mile-long track through his father John William's nursery at St. Gallen [A:208].","citations":["A:208"],"tags":["Family"]},{"name":"Flying Serpent","definition":"Name of the <em>Argo</em> when Willy Jones sails it in the Moluccas, according to the ship's history told by the talking oak [MTM B13:404-405].","citations":["MTM B13:404-405"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Identity"]},{"name":"Flying Steinmetz","definition":"One of Absalom Stein's self-named mixed drinks at the Bay Hotel picnic. He prepares the assortment with Gilbeys gin and ice, though no separate recipe for the Flying Steinmetz is supplied [A:29-30].","citations":["A:29-30"],"tags":["Alcohol"]},{"name":"Foetus (Quick)","definition":"One of the \"quick foetuses\" cast into the Moloch Furnace during the festival at Babylonia Bagascia. It speaks from the furnace, identifies itself as Little Hugh of Lincoln, and is met with laughter [MB:566]. <em>Foetus</em> is a British spelling of <em>fetus</em>.","citations":["MB:566"],"tags":["Apocalyptic","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Fog-Horn Symphony","definition":"Ninth title in Endymeon Ellenbogen's proposed nine-volume classification of his poetry, which he says remains uncollected and unpublished [A:237].","citations":["A:237"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"Fogazzaro","definition":"False name Biloxi Brannagan gives when Finnegan first joins him and Gertrude in their kitchen garden. Finnegan immediately disbelieves it, and Gertrude corrects Biloxi aloud before he supplies Brannagan [D:199].","citations":["D:199"],"tags":["Identity","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Font (Holy Water)","definition":"The chipped holy-water font on the wall is one of Finnegan's intensely remembered details of his uncertain twelve-year life with Theresa [A:204]. In an earlier exchange, he teases her that the baptismal font cracked when she was baptized; she answers that any of a dozen witches might have cracked it [A:108].","citations":["A:204","A:108"],"tags":["Sacraments","Finnegan","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Fonts (Printing)","definition":"The old and damaged printing fonts Stein notices in a supposed newspaper clipping about X. Dotty identifies the obsolete printing as the Pelican Press's own work and concludes that X printed the clipping there before sending it back to entertain them [A:142].","citations":["A:142"],"tags":["Journalism"]},{"name":"Foolish Colts","definition":"Duffey's comparison for most of the world, which stands in an imagined middle ground although, in his account of Casey's ideological conversion, there is no position between the sides. The discussion concerns Casey's Party commitments and the intended use of the <em>Crock</em> as a Liberal Catholic vehicle for propaganda [A:98].","citations":["A:98"],"tags":["Duffey","Catholic Church","Journalism"]},{"name":"Foot-painter","definition":"Profession of Marie Monaghan's uncle Shoeless Veronese van Rijn. She says he works in watercolors, oils, and charcoal with his feet, travels with carnivals, and may be the world's finest foot-painter [A:28].","citations":["A:28"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Carnival","Occupations","Family"]},{"name":"Football","definition":"Sport in Casey's imagined record books, where he becomes a phenomenal running back while also playing professional baseball [A:156]. Tom \"Big Bear\" Rogers contributes “The Floating Screen: The Next Important Development in Football” to the <em>Crock</em> [A:41]. Howland has played neither high-school football nor basketball because he never attended high school [A:180]. Football and six-man football appear in the catalogue of threatened popular arts [MTM B6:207]. Zabotski explains his Pristine World by compressing a football game from three hours of clock time to one hour of whistle time, twelve minutes with the ball in play, and roughly three minutes of essential action [MTM B9:279].","citations":["A:156","A:41","A:180","MTM B6:207","MTM B9:279"],"tags":["Dreams","Journalism","Time & Eternity","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Foraminifera Julep","definition":"Earlier heading normalized the printed spelling Julip to Julep. See <em>Foraminifera Julip</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Louisiana","Alcohol","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Foraminifera Julip","definition":"Drink Biloxi Brannagan orders from Honeybucket Kincaid in futuristic rural Louisiana, printed as \"Foraminifera Julip\" [MTM B13:416-418]. Kincaid prepares the drinks using the scene's frequency-based gathering of substances from water, including alcohol; the text supplies no complete recipe [MTM B13:418].","citations":["MTM B13:416-418","MTM B13:418"],"tags":["Louisiana","Alcohol","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Forca, Freddy Tin-Horn","definition":"One of the fifty resident regulars at the Old Wooden Ship, listed between Tippio and Sulphur-Bottom Sullivan [D:215-216]. The narrator declines to tell his story but says Tin-Horn describes the kind of sport he is [D:218]. The nickname suggests a pretentious gambler, without establishing his stakes or any particular exploit.","citations":["D:215-216","D:218"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Ford Alexandrine","definition":"The proposed “Ford Alexandrine,” whose next-year model is suggested as a classy chassis for the Casey Machine. The suggestion occurs among advice from the machine’s promoters without an individual speaker being named [CM:508-509].","citations":["CM:508-509"],"tags":["Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Forebears","definition":"Ancestors. Angela Cosquin's mixed island ancestry includes French, Carib, and English people, with both noblemen and slaves among her forebears [D:259]. During the drinking contest, Bushmaster's supporters praise the bulldog courage of his forebears, prompting Finnegan's pun that some of them are bulldogs [A:23].","citations":["D:259","A:23"],"tags":["Family","Social Class"]},{"name":"Forebrain","definition":"The seat of ordinary human consciousness in the fictional cranial theory quoted from Carmody Overlark's <em>Studies in the Imagination</em>. The epigraph contrasts modern humans' developed forebrains with Neanderthals' allegedly larger back-brains, assigning the latter unconscious and paranormal powers [D:270-271]. It credits the other people with humanity's imagination and ghostliness while the modern contribution is forebrain consciousness [D:272].","citations":["D:270-271","D:272"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Metaphysics","Literature"]},{"name":"Forgery","definition":"Papadiabolous II practices forgery before entering police work, according to X [D:286]. X himself sends notes in Papadiabolous's handwriting to keep the gang believing he lives and to lure Seaworthy into his trap [D:289]. Gilberto Levine-and-O'Brien fears that marks on the skin he wears are imitations of his own; he refuses Finnegan's offer to inspect them [MB:590-591]. Beth Keegan uses forgery figuratively when interpreting Duffey's dream, suggesting that people he treats as artistic creations may prove false [MTM B2:67-68]. A Royal Pop lecturer likewise calls planchette and ouija dull forgeries of genuine talking tables [MTM B11:330].","citations":["D:286","D:289","MB:590-591","MTM B2:67-68","MTM B11:330"],"tags":["Crime","Identity","Dreams","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Forgetfulness Mesh","definition":"Brannagan's means of erasing the scientists' memory after he and Duffey defeat their attempt to draw the Sword of Saint Sécaire. Brannagan rewelds sword and scabbard with hot flux; Duffey then pretends to drop them accidentally, returning them to Nine Mile Depth. The Forgetfulness Mesh prevents the Dimbeau brothers and their fellow scientist from recalling the episode and trying again [MTM B13:423-424].","citations":["MTM B13:423-424"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Weapons","Duffey","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Fork","definition":"Duffey's dowser's fork, cut from a red peach tree, detects bloodshed and murder in an object's history; for the particular parchment under investigation he instead chooses violet light [MTM B11:326]. At dinner, Hans jokingly argues that forks precede fingers because ancient fish spears and untined eating stabbers survive while fingers do not [A:4-5]. Finnegan claims Italians invented forks to retrieve olives from bottles [A:5], and elsewhere says he used shovel and pitchfork in his carnival duties with horses [A:220].","citations":["MTM B11:326","A:4-5","A:5","A:220"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Fishing","Finnegan","Duffey","Carnival"]},{"name":"Forma rotunda et globosa","definition":"Latin for \"round and ball-shaped form,\" used in Finnegan's phrase <em>in forma rotunda et globosa</em> for Nada Patkaniowska as Brynhilde, the Northern Valkyrie [A:193]. Her imposing physique and habit of carrying Finnegan on her shoulder supply the joke [A:192].","citations":["A:193","A:192"],"tags":["Language & Words","Women Characters","Other Mythologies"]},{"name":"Formaldehyde","definition":"Substance Casey calls \"formaldehide\" in item XVI of his school notebook, where he claims that Bakelite is made from it and gum arabic [A:163]. His formula is inaccurate: Bakelite is a phenol-formaldehyde resin.","citations":["A:163"],"tags":["History & Memory"]},{"name":"Formerly the C of S","definition":"Subscript beneath the black Gothic title of the <em>Crock</em>, marking its earlier identity as the <em>C of S</em> and its clandestine beginnings [A:41].","citations":["A:41"],"tags":["Journalism"]},{"name":"Formulas, All the","definition":"Casey Szymansky and Hinnegan's ambitious notebook project: they have begun a book in which they intend to write every formula in the world [A:163].","citations":["A:163"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"Fornicational Intercourse","definition":"Father Blevins's phrase in a clipping preserved in Letitia's scrapbook. He claims that college girls need firsthand sexual experience as part of total education, boasts that he supplies it as an expert, and says he makes participation as compulsory as he can [MTM B8:251].","citations":["MTM B8:251"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Fortress Christianity","definition":"Casey's third imagined world, with a religious setting though the narrator questions its religious substance. In the Fortress Minor version he belongs to a clever, courageous persecuted minority in the early Roman Empire or Recusancy England, amid secret names, priest rooms, prisons, and torture. Fortress Major places the faithful in a besieged city, with enemies climbing like giant spiders and tower bells tolling. His attachment to persecuted-minority identity later contributes to his joining the Party [A:157].","citations":["A:157"],"tags":["Dreams","Catholic Church","Identity"]},{"name":"Fortress Major","definition":"The besieged-city form of Casey's Fortress Christianity fantasy: all the faithful are inside, enemies swarm the walls like giant spiders, and the tower bells toll for hours [A:157].","citations":["A:157"],"tags":["Dreams","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Fortress Minor","definition":"The early Roman or Recusancy England version of Casey's Fortress Christianity fantasy, featuring a persecuted minority's secret movements and names, priest rooms, prisons, and torture [A:157].","citations":["A:157"],"tags":["Dreams","Catholic Church","Identity"]},{"name":"Foster, Sister Mary Cornelia (Sullivan)","definition":"Sister Mary Cornelia (Sullivan) Foster, named among the ecclesial or vineyard people in Duffey and Letitia’s Chicago circle [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Four Feathers","definition":"One of the mild whiskies offered, alongside Five Roses, at Falo and Nauti's jungle cabaret [A:64].","citations":["A:64"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Four Waters of Paradise","definition":"Waters of the future paradise on which Finnegan says he may next sail aboard the <em>Argo</em>. While bound for the Ringing Rocks and Mooring Stone refuge, he says he may never set foot on the ship again until that voyage [MTM B13:453].","citations":["MTM B13:453"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Ships","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Fourth Pleistocene Ice Age","definition":"Vincent's ice-age reference in his speculation that the universe might have been created ten minutes ago with its apparent past already installed: fossils, sediments, partly run-down atomic clocks, and an earth already partway down the slope from the fourth Pleistocene ice age [A:56]. Modern stratigraphy dates the Pleistocene-Holocene boundary to about 11,700 years ago; the quoted fourth ice age is not itself a formal epoch.","citations":["A:56"],"tags":["Creation","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Fox fire","definition":"The glow produced by luminous fungi in decaying wood. Henry and Casey encounter fox-fire in the jungle, where dead wood and rotten leaves glow like coals [A:42]. Duffey later imagines a secular world's impoverishment through swamps lacking fox fire and other brilliant natural phenomena [MTM B13:460].","citations":["A:42","MTM B13:460"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Duffey"]},{"name":"Fox Hunting","definition":"Charley Murray plays fox-hunting tunes on the post-boy's horn during the carriage ride with Duffey, Sebastian Hilton, Rattigan, Lily Koch, and three other girls [MTM B1:31-32]. The phrase also describes Finnegan's savage attack on Seaworthy's group before sailing for the island paradise: he kills two guards, climbs the Pirate Hotel, strikes down his supposed principal target, and escapes wounded under fire. Seaworthy's laugh reveals that he has again survived [D:231] [D:233-235].","citations":["MTM B1:31-32","D:231","D:233-235"],"tags":["Music","Combat","Finnegan","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Francine","definition":"Woman described as a pretty, dark girl Finnegan dates after meeting her in a Chicago bar. She arranges to obtain a kitchen job for Howland at her hotel and says she may marry him if he proves steady [A:181-182]. She and Howland remain close to Finnegan, who often stays with the Howlands in later years [A:182]. When Howland runs the luxurious Club Royal, Francine worries about their spending and hopes to draw him away gradually; she promises that Finnegan will always have a room with them [A:246-247]. He includes her among the friends whose faces he sketches [A:278].","citations":["A:181-182","A:182","A:246-247","A:278"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Family","Occupations"]},{"name":"Francis","definition":"Name shared by distinct figures. Francis O'Brien is among the women who kiss Duffey goodbye at the Rounders' Club [MTM B2:70]. A quotation on chastity attributed to St. Francis de Sales opens section 5 of Book One [MTM B1:27]. Henry sees a Francis, alongside Anthony and other saints, aboard the dream ship <em>Navicula Petri</em> [A:44-45]. In the Cuban account, La Dorotea's Third Order of St. Francis medallion explains her burial in the San Francisco cemetery [MTM B12:372].","citations":["MTM B2:70","MTM B1:27","A:44-45","MTM B12:372"],"tags":["Saints","Religious Orders","Dreams","Minor Characters","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Freddy Castle","definition":"Australian soldier who, with Tom Shire, befriends the visiting Americans at the racetrack. Freddy is smaller than the large, fair Tom, has a rusty tinge, and is presented as unmistakably Australian [A:4-5]. When Finnegan and Vincent phone the barracks for help finding late beer, Freddy's rusty voice joins Tom's elaborate instructions [A:10]. The two Australians disguise them in unsuitable winter tunics and hats for a supposed forbidden visit to the wet canteen; Freddy signals Bushmaster to maintain the hoax [A:11-12]. Freddy and Tom judge the Hans-Bushmaster drinking contest [A:20], join the subsequent pub crawl [A:23], and attend the Bay Hotel picnic [A:29]. Finnegan later sketches Freddy's face among those of his friends [A:278].","citations":["A:4-5","A:10","A:11-12","A:20","A:23","A:29","A:278"],"tags":["Australia","Soldiers","Finnegan","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Free Martin","definition":"Free Martin is the two-word form used in Casey’s notebook. See <em>Freemartin</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Freemartin","definition":"Casey's spelling of <em>freemartin</em>, a sterile female calf associated with being a male calf's twin. In item XXIV of his notebook he records that heifers born twin to bulls are nearly always sterile and credits Hinnegan with teaching him about farm matters [A:164].","citations":["A:164"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Freetown","definition":"Sierra Leone port reached by the <em>Brunhilde</em> on January 26 after leaving Natal on January 18 [D:89]. Finnegan resumes drinking there and meets Joseph, a Haussa diamond smuggler seeking a reliable overseas partner [D:93-94]. He leaves with a heavy sack of stones and subsequently markets diamonds from it and two further sacks, sending drafts to Freetown [D:95]. On Naxos, he recalls having sinned since Freetown and seeks confession for his Easter Duty [D:118]. Don Barnaby also names Freetown among the places visited within his extraordinary joy-pill garden experience [D:251].","citations":["D:89","D:93-94","D:95","D:118","D:251"],"tags":["Africa","Ports & Harbors","Maritime Trade","Finnegan","Crime","Sacraments"]},{"name":"Freezing Rain","definition":"Weather during Finnegan's escape with Seaworthy's stolen suitcase: the freezing rain turns to noisy sleet while he seeks shelter and encounters the girl who will accompany him [D:166].","citations":["D:166"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Freight train","definition":"Vincent's proposed escape on the night before his wedding: he asks Finnegan to catch a freight train and go away with him. Finnegan answers that Vincent can leave while he stays to marry Show Boat [A:111].","citations":["A:111"],"tags":["Finnegan","Family"]},{"name":"French Szymansky","definition":"Imagined future designation for Balzac in Casey's youthful literary fantasy: Casey would become an American Balzac so much greater that Balzac would be remembered as the French Szymansky [A:157-158].","citations":["A:157-158"],"tags":["Literature","Identity","Creation"]},{"name":"Frenchman (Dead)","definition":"Dead Frenchman, one of the varieties of joy pill Don Barnaby lists alongside Haiti hoodoo, High Harry, Deep Romany, and Black Rooster [D:248].","citations":["D:248"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Frenchman, Fat","definition":"Henri Salvatore, the Fat Frenchman from the Swamps, one of Duffey's twelve talismanic creations [MTM B5:161]. His swamps are Louisiana's [MTM B13:416], and he is also the Emperor Henry of Neustria, charged with rebuilding the world [MTM B5:183]. He belongs to the Seven Pillars of Righteousness, the original trustees and directors of the <em>Bark</em> [MTM B7:214].","citations":["MTM B5:161","MTM B13:416","MTM B5:183","MTM B7:214"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Louisiana","Duffey","Journalism","Creation"]},{"name":"Freud, Sigmund","definition":"Sigmund Freud (1856-1939), Vienna-based neurologist and founder of psychoanalysis. Beth Keegan declares herself Freudian and asks Duffey and the others for dreams to interpret [MTM B2:66-68].","citations":["MTM B2:66-68"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Duffey","Dreams"]},{"name":"Freudian","definition":"Pertaining to Freud's psychoanalysis. Beth Keegan adopts the label and reads Duffey's dream of Paradise Birds becoming clay pigeons as doubt about his powers and creations [MTM B2:66-68]. Finnegan finds equal sagacity in Freudian literature and Gypsy Dream Books, then discovers that his corpse memory is literal rather than a symbolic dream [D:74]. The narrator jokes that Freud did not know underwater swimming as a trigger for recalled dreams or suppressed incidents, linking Finnegan's diving to his recollection [D:71].","citations":["MTM B2:66-68","D:74","D:71"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Literature","Dreams"]},{"name":"Friar Tuck","definition":"Companion of Robin Hood in the legendary tradition; one of Henry Salvatore's mythological identities, alongside Pantegruel and the Giant of the Beanstalk [A:39].","citations":["A:39"],"tags":["Folklore","Giants & Titans","Argonauts (Characters)"]},{"name":"Friday","definition":"One of the Answer's three publication days, with Monday and Wednesday [MTM B4:113]. Its Friday issue announces the Leader's appearance that evening at Henry Horner Park [MTM B4:116]. Pelican Press's <em>Jazz Magazine</em> also comes out on Fridays [MTM B7:212]. Finnegan teases Theresa Piccone that a witch's cabalistic fingernail marks appear only on Fridays; she answers that she will wear gloves [A:108]. In Private Gregory's account of early anti-Christian horror stories, Christ's tomb is sealed Friday night before his Sunday resurrection [A:85].","citations":["MTM B4:113","MTM B4:116","MTM B7:212","A:108","A:85"],"tags":["New Testament","Journalism","Magic & Sorcery","Women Characters","Resurrection"]},{"name":"Frieda","definition":"One of the women living at the Old Wooden Ship who are called “salt water widows.” She appears in the roster of its resident regulars [D:216] [D:221].","citations":["D:216","D:221"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Friesian","definition":"Relating to Friesland or its people and language; also spelled Frisian. In item XX of Casey's notebook, Dutch Friesian is called the language nearest English, illustrated by a rhyme about good butter and good cheese [A:164]. The poem opening Finnegan's chapter worries that the Argonauts tore their sheets and became mired in Friesian bogs; it offers an imagined question, not a settled account of their voyage [A:171-172].","citations":["A:164","A:171-172"],"tags":["Argonautica","Finnegan","Language & Words","Literature"]},{"name":"Frogs","definition":"Bull frogs inhabit the tumbling, noisy fountain in Bayougoula Park [MTM B11:331]. While staying with the goat-man Gautier in a New Jersey swamp, Finnegan borrows his spot lantern after dark and spears a sackful of frogs [A:195-196].","citations":["MTM B11:331","A:195-196"],"tags":["Finnegan","Fishing"]},{"name":"Fundamentalers","definition":"Advocates of a short biblical chronology who visit Zabotski and Duffey to discuss their histories. The visitors include Pattenite and Hatch adherents, a follower of the Rev. Patrick O’Connell, and a Cummings-clan member [MTM B9:277] [MTM B9:278] [MTM B9:280]. A Pattenite finds Zabotski’s chronology close to theirs: first life between 20,000 and 10,000 BC, the sixth-day creation of land, animals, and man between 10,000 and 2,800 BC, and the Floodtide Catastrophes about 2,800 BC [MTM B9:278]. Zabotski calls them half correct but insists that their time scale must be understood in relation to the Pristine World; similar dates do not establish agreement about how history works [MTM B9:274] [MTM B9:279] [MTM B9:280]. The Cummings-clan visitor treats the Ark’s survival in High Armenia, guarded by angels, as a test of faith and reports three expeditions searching for it [MTM B9:280]. The visitors finally declare that Duffey and Zabotski are against them because they do not share their teachings [MTM B9:284]. Later news reports nineteen unarmed Fundamentalers killed in a “shootout” with Orthodoxer police [MTM B9:288].","citations":["MTM B9:277","MTM B9:278","MTM B9:280","MTM B9:274","MTM B9:279","MTM B9:284","MTM B9:288"],"tags":["Theology","Old Testament","History & Memory","Creation","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Fundamentalist Drunkards","definition":"Group provided with special Stones among the enlarged representation of the Ekklesia, whose Princes assemble for its Last Conclave in Babylonia Bagascia [MB:561-562].","citations":["MB:561-562"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Furtive Man","definition":"Recurring designation for hidden and endangered people. On Padre Island, Don Lewis sees a furtive man and tells of another, Yokipoki, whom he buried on Willy Jones Island; after that experience Lewis himself became furtive [D:43-48]. Having returned under a different name and no longer feeling his own master, he asks whether such singular people are forgotten by God [D:48]. The narrator includes Finnegan among the furtive men and recalls the man who swam out to the <em>Brunhilde</em> [D:41] [D:50]. The swimmer proves to be Manuel, a stowaway and self-described spy. Finnegan suspects a prior acquaintance between Manuel and Papa Devil after seeing a fleeting exchange between them [D:54].","citations":["D:43-48","D:48","D:41","D:50","D:54"],"tags":["Identity","Finnegan","Ships","Islands","Theology","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Gabrielovitch, Draja","definition":"Journalist who works for all the Pelican Press sheets, including the <em>Bark</em>, and also for the Slavic-language press, work the narrator says will get him killed [MTM B7:212]. He is one of the Seven Pillars of Righteousness, the <em>Bark</em>'s founding trustees and directors, with Henri Salvatore, Melchisedech Duffey, Absalom Stein, Dotty Yekouris, Hans Schultz, and John Solli [MTM B7:214].","citations":["MTM B7:212","MTM B7:214"],"tags":["Journalism","Minor Characters","Occupations"]},{"name":"Gadarene Swine","definition":"Bagby's name for the monster Giulio, embodied in the cruel, comic Gadarene Swine Song that he whistles after him. Bagby predicts that Giulio will be pursued until he drowns, though Duffey calls him a good man [MTM B2:48]. Giulio later adopts the name when describing the devil that afflicts him, consumes his soul, and returns with seven worse devils after being driven out [MTM B3:106]. The name evokes the Gospel accounts in Matthew 8, Mark 5, and Luke 8 in which expelled demons enter swine that rush into the water and drown.","citations":["MTM B2:48","MTM B3:106"],"tags":["New Testament","Angels & Demons","Monsters & Creatures","Music","Duffey"]},{"name":"Gadgets","definition":"A couple of unspecified devices Finnegan proposes adding to his plan with Hilary Hilton. The intended production is explicit: Hilton is to expel Casey again and reclaim Mary Jean, giving Casey's friends another chance to straighten him out [A:252-253].","citations":["A:252-253"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Gaetano I--VI (Mad Kings)","definition":"The first six Gaetanos of San Simeon's Balbo dynasty follow a recurring succession: at thirty-nine the prince goes mad and returns as Mad King, his father becomes Mad King Emeritus, and his grandfather dies [MB:558-559]. Gaetano VI orders that no bells be rung along the North Coast, on pain of death [MB:560].","citations":["MB:558-559","MB:560"],"tags":["Social Class","Family","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Gaetano Polytech","definition":"School in Balbo City attended by the eleven-year-old Saucerite who visits the <em>Argo</em>. He hopes to go to the University of Miami the following season if he can obtain a chess scholarship [MB:592].","citations":["MB:592"],"tags":["Fictional Places","Minor Characters","Ships"]},{"name":"Gaetano VII (Mad King)","definition":"The prince expected to become San Simeon's seventh Mad King. At thirty-nine, instead of following his predecessors into madness and returning home, he becomes a high churchman and stays away. His father consequently remains the reigning Mad King, while his grandfather, the Mad King Emeritus, cannot be relieved and die [MB:559].","citations":["MB:559"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Family","Social Class","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Galapagos Islands","definition":"The supposed source of turtles for Aunt Gertrude's chowder: Brannagan says he has them specially flown in from Galapagos. The narrator immediately calls this a lie and identifies local fishermen as their actual source [D:208].","citations":["D:208"],"tags":["Islands","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Galen","definition":"Greek physician of the Roman imperial period, traditionally dated 129 to about 216 CE. Finnegan credits him with estimating the moon's size by a goat-sucker's supposed flight behind it, an extravagant account of twelve wingbeats and a moon as large as a ship [D:132]. Chapter Eleven of <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> opens with a passage attributed to Galen's <em>Techne Iatrike</em> about Daemons begetting satyrs on women; the narrator then pointedly notes that Galen's teacher was named Satyrus [D:160-161].","citations":["D:132","D:160-161"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Literature","Monsters & Creatures","Finnegan","Greece"]},{"name":"Galensprach","definition":"Earlier heading omitted a letter from the form printed in the source. See <em>Galgensprach</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Language & Words","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Galgensprach","definition":"Galgensprach in the source: X's term for a final grand opportunity to speak. Describing his perilous clandestine life, he expects to die suddenly, anonymously, and without even that last moment [A:138-139].","citations":["A:138-139"],"tags":["Language & Words","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Galilean","definition":"One of Margaret Stone's overlapping identities: the narrator calls her a Galilean and a close kinswoman of Jesus Christ as part of an extravagant genealogy in which she is all ethnics in one [MTM B7:212].","citations":["MTM B7:212"],"tags":["New Testament","Identity","Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Galilee, Sea of","definition":"The Gospel setting invoked in Cardinal Brokenbolt's epitaph: the Lord walking by the Sea of Galilee sees two brothers [MB:589]. The wording recalls Matthew 4:18, the calling of Simon Peter and Andrew.","citations":["MB:589"],"tags":["Scripture","New Testament","Family"]},{"name":"Gall bladder","definition":"An ingredient Don Barnaby says Madame Le Mourcheor omits from her Juju Joy Pills. He claims that an alligator, being reptilian, has no gall bladder [D:249]. This is part of his extravagant account, not accurate anatomy: alligators do have gallbladders.","citations":["D:249"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Galleon","definition":"The form partly resembled by <em>Navicula Petri</em>, the boat Henry Salvatore sees in his dream: it looks like a galleon, yet is quite other [A:44].","citations":["A:44"],"tags":["Ships","Dreams","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Galley","definition":"The ship's kitchen where Casey works while the other Dirty Five sleep on deck during the memorable Talaud Islands voyage. After midnight he joins them and names their ship <em>Argo</em> for that night [A:63]. On Duffey's later <em>Argo</em>, some of his mysterious, partly self-created seamen serve in the galley [MTM B13:402-403].","citations":["A:63","MTM B13:402-403"],"tags":["Ships","Crew & Sailors","Occupations","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Galley Slave","definition":"Playful description of Casey Szymansky, who works in the galley while his companions sleep on deck. After midnight, the galley slave comes up and declares the vessel to be the ship named <em>Argo</em>, for that night at least [A:63].","citations":["A:63"],"tags":["Ships","Occupations"]},{"name":"Galveston","definition":"Texas port from which Henry Salvatore signs onto cotton boats, cattle boats, tankers, and sulphur boats [A:124]. Mary Virginia Schaeffer is from Galveston [MTM B5:157], and it is included in the birthplaces of the six companions listed after the first five talismanic people [MTM B3:76]. Her family's Red Dog Motor Freight runs from Galveston and Houston toward Morgan City and New Orleans, with other stops in Louisiana and Texas. Joe Smith begins there the triptych of Dotty O'Toole that Finnegan later completes [MTM B6:198]. The Old Wooden Ship Tavern in Galveston is where Gunboat Smith serves as Bulldog-in-Residence before joining the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:458].","citations":["A:124","MTM B5:157","MTM B3:76","MTM B6:198","MTM B13:458"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Ships","Women Characters","Bars & Taverns","Visual Arts","Maritime Trade"]},{"name":"Galveston Island","definition":"Site of one disputed death of Finnegan. Stein says that Finnegan did not die at Saxon X Seaworthy's hand on Galveston Island, although Doll Delancy found a body on West Beach and swore it was his [MB:548]. Archipelago offers Galveston as one possible place where Doppio di Pinne died in Finnegan's stead; the alternative is the <em>Brunhilde</em>'s cabin, at Papadiabolous's hands [A:278].","citations":["MB:548","A:278"],"tags":["Islands","Finnegan","Women Characters","Death & Mortality","Identity"]},{"name":"Game warden","definition":"Royal official in Duffey's dream as the Boy King. Summoned to stop men shooting his newly made Birds of Paradise, the warden finds that the fallen birds have become clay pigeons. He nevertheless shoots and kills the men, then threatens to shoot Duffey with his next arrows if called out on another clay-pigeon chase [MTM B2:67].","citations":["MTM B2:67"],"tags":["Duffey","Dreams","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Games","definition":"Recreations, deceptions, performances, and contests recur across the cycle. The Rounders' Club advertises games on its Elegant Riverboat Deck, with racquets, whist, poker, and horseshoes [MTM B2:49]. Duffey and his school friends play Kelly pool and rotation in a private house's attic billiard room while escaping a formal dance [MTM B1:32-33]. Charlotte Mullens begins with solitaire, plays two-handed cards with Duffey, and then includes Gloria in three-handed games with kisses for stakes [MTM B3:78]. Charlotte and Gloria, also called the Cavendishes, live by card-sharping and play bridge against Lily Koch and the Girl Countess [MTM B3:95]. Charlotte hears that Sebastian Hilton is rich and fancies himself a gamer, making him an attractive target [MTM B3:94].\n\nCharlotte describes her and Gloria's sexual blackmail schemes while walking with Duffey toward St. Malachy's Church [MTM B3:87]. A compromising photograph of Duffey with Shirley Israel is described as a badger-game entrapment [MTM B4:139-140]. Duffey calls Hilary Hilton and Mary Jean's treatment of Casey a reverse badger game: Casey may have sought death at Hilary's hands but received painful derision [PG:536]. Margaret Stone likens Charlotte's moral peril to Nine Dollar Dog, a game whose outcome becomes unalterable nine plays before its end [MTM B7:217].\n\nThe Thunder Colt Game is the climax of the Decatur Street Opera House's bloody performance, accompanied by Thunder Torus music; the colt kills a Finnegan effigy and Margaret Stone [MTM B11:349-351]. In a cosmic contest, Duffey wrestles Morpheus over which will remain as the last dreaming cell, and the narrator calls cosmic wrestling the game of timeless Greek gods [GD:500]. Zabotski compares the Pristine World to the essential action of a football game, stripped of repetition and empty time [MTM B9:279]. A young Saucerite hopes to obtain a chess scholarship to the University of Miami [MB:592]. Game of Hearts is among the titles Dotty gives for the Open Heart Novels [MTM B7:224].","citations":["MTM B2:49","MTM B1:32-33","MTM B3:78","MTM B3:95","MTM B3:94","MTM B3:87","MTM B4:139-140","PG:536","MTM B7:217","MTM B11:349-351","GD:500","MTM B9:279","MB:592","MTM B7:224"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Cosmic Struggle","Duffey","Women Characters","Literature","Theater","Money & Trade","Clubs & Societies","Crime","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Gannet","definition":"Bird that has remained on the <em>Brunhilde</em>'s main stack for days. Papa Devil identifies it as a gannet, and Finnegan reports Manuel's interpretation that someone else will die. Papa replies that Manuel knows of another coming death, but that a bird did not tell him [D:120].","citations":["D:120"],"tags":["Ships","Finnegan","Devils (Characters)","Death & Mortality","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Gans, Joe","definition":"American boxer, 1874-1910. During his fighting binge, Finnegan boasts that he went ninety rounds with Joe Gans and challenges local men to a fast twenty rounds [D:191].","citations":["D:191"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Finnegan","Combat"]},{"name":"Gaping Nothingness","definition":"Phrase in X's angry letter to Absalom Stein. After describing the world's coming desolation as its becoming trivial and narrow, X invokes everlasting phoniness and gaping nothingness upon Stein for reading the Teilhard bogus without laughing, and urges him to renounce this prostitution of the mind [A:265-266].","citations":["A:265-266"],"tags":["Language & Words","Theology"]},{"name":"Gar","definition":"Alligator gar, a large freshwater fish. In the Louisiana swamps, Honeybucket Kincaid adjusts voltage and frequency and uses underwater electrodes to summon the nine-pound gar from about a mile around. From perhaps three thousand he selects one for Brannagan's meal, eviscerates it, takes three pounds of tender flank meat requested for grilling, and puts the gar into a high-frequency oven [MTM B13:416-417].","citations":["MTM B13:416-417"],"tags":["Fishing","Louisiana","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Gar-fish","definition":"Francine's comparison for Finnegan's drinking: she asks whether he still drinks like a gar-fish. He says his friends have stopped asking; she refuses to join that silence [A:247].","citations":["A:247"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Garbage Scow","definition":"Vessel from which X says he was once dropped, trussed and weighted, during his clandestine adventures [A:138]. Hilary Hilton also boasts of refurbishing three garbage scows and selling them to an Arabian Gulf potentate, transformed into floating palaces [A:255].","citations":["A:138","A:255"],"tags":["Ships","Maritime Trade","Middle East"]},{"name":"Garden","definition":"A place of remembered belonging as well as an ordinary cultivated space. At Archipelago's opening, Finnegan looks up from his beer to find that the Garden has vanished and he is in the World [A:1]. Later he likens the Green Islands to the Garden, but the Malayan boy insists that they are not it and that he must go home [A:88]. The companions sleeping in high grass at the jungle's edge offer a related pastoral image [A:69]. Show Boat, in her letter to Dotty, identifies the Garden's serpent with the many-armed political monster she and Vincent oppose [A:262-263]. In the seventh contingency, Duffey converses wordlessly with the invisible God in an afternoon garden, where an offered solution still carries a possible personal disaster [MTM B10:301].\n\nOther gardens have concrete settings. Loy Larkin complains that the garden she planted in November is all weeds [A:27]. Young Duffey escapes the slant-faced boys through the squash and corn rows at his aunt's house [MTM B1:12-13]. He envisages his old stable yard as a beer garden, courtyard cafe, and open-air market [MTM B2:47]. Henri sends him to New Orleans to grow cucumbers for the Greater Glory in an asphalt garden [MTM B5:184]. The priest at St. Malachy's has a rose garden [MTM B3:87]. Duffey and Brannagan meet in the latter's private beer garden in Biloxi [MTM B13:408-409]. Joseph invites Finnegan to drink gin in a garden at Freetown [D:93]. Don Barnaby describes Madame Le Mourcheor's small interior garden expanding, after his joy pill, into a boundless world of changing people, marriage, and family [D:249-250].","citations":["A:1","A:88","A:69","A:262-263","MTM B10:301","A:27","MTM B1:12-13","MTM B2:47","MTM B5:184","MTM B3:87","MTM B13:408-409","D:93","D:249-250"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","New Orleans","Dreams","Family","Bars & Taverns","Food & Dining","Theology"]},{"name":"Garfield, Charlotte","definition":"Adult confidence woman who continues to look like a nine-year-old girl, moving between a child's manner and an experienced woman's voice. Duffey meets her on the night train from Omaha to Chicago in 1925. Calling herself Charlotte Mullens, she plays cards with him and Gloria, with kisses as stakes [MTM B3:77-78]. She claims to be thirty-eight, older than her father and older than her mother, and rejects Gloria's suggestion that Duffey is a father image: he is her sweetheart. Gloria's role shifts from mother to sister; Charlotte identifies the third member of their party, Manolo, as her son [MTM B3:80-81]. She later explains that she recruits pliable flesh-and-blood people, usually actors, and makes them compliant figures of her own aura [MTM B3:83].\n\nCharlotte is a strong psychic who reads Duffey's mind and communicates with him out of physical presence [MTM B3:81] [MTM B3:83-84]. She enlists his help to find Aga Gonof's hidden stash, claiming a small share through her former husband's dealings and proposing to take the remainder as interest. Duffey obtains the key pattern; Charlotte has the key made. In a shared experience she calls a psychosomatic trance, Duffey seizes the coded location and she arranges the information into the bank and deposit-box numbers. She says she has used such trances in mentalist acts [MTM B3:84-86]. She promises to find Duffey's elusive 'moth' in return, directs him to St. Malachy's, and knows the identity of the woman he will marry, Letitia Koch [MTM B3:86-88] [MTM B3:91]. While walking to the church for confession, she describes blackmail schemes conducted with Gloria and exploiting her own childish appearance [MTM B3:87-88].\n\nAt the Colony Club, where the troupe is known as the Cavendishes, Charlotte announces that she has stolen and reburied the loot and is now a millionaire. A mental alarm has compromised the disposable name Mullens [MTM B3:93]. The troupe also earns its living through cardsharping [MTM B3:95]. Gloria and Manolo are murdered at their hotel, echoing their deaths in Duffey's trance. Letitia says Charlotte escaped to her prepared hiding place; Charlotte had named St. Angela Orba Orphanage, where two sisters know she is an adult [MTM B3:85-86] [MTM B3:97].\n\nAs Charlotte Garfield she later attends the Royal Rogues' and Graceful Swindlers' convention at New Orleans's Royal Orleans Hotel [MTM B7:216]. She now claims to be about two hundred years old, admits her earlier age was a lie, and says her Mendacious Midget Doll bears her true age. Margaret Stone calls her Midge and Baby Face while warning that her chosen game may already have decided her fate [MTM B7:217]. Calling herself Charlotte Carrollton, she advertises for a new professional family under the name Darnley. Separately from recruiting actors, she claims she can bear full-grown nineteen-year-old sons, but declines to explain how. She says she changes families every three or four years, proposes a Patriarch and Angelic Child confidence scheme with Duffey, and eventually engages the Darnleys [MTM B8:259-261].\n\nShe remains an old associate of Duffey, criticizes Casey's exploitation of vulnerable people, and says she herself has a foot in several worlds [MTM B12:379-380]. Charlotte Garfield and her son Michael attend Hilary Hilton's party at Randal's [MTM B12:380] [MTM B12:383].","citations":["MTM B3:77-78","MTM B3:80-81","MTM B3:83","MTM B3:81","MTM B3:83-84","MTM B3:84-86","MTM B3:86-88","MTM B3:91","MTM B3:87-88","MTM B3:93","MTM B3:95","MTM B3:85-86","MTM B3:97","MTM B7:216","MTM B7:217","MTM B8:259-261","MTM B12:379-380","MTM B12:380","MTM B12:383"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Crime","Family","Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","New Orleans","Identity","Magic & Sorcery","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Gargoyles","definition":"A recurrent grotesque image associated with Finnegan: his face is a comic mask with a bugle nose [A:6], and he calls himself a gargoyle while Anastasia recalls a story about one with a good heart [D:39]. During his pursuit of Seaworthy he perches six stories above the street like a living gargoyle [D:162]. The four senior gargoyles he observes are Seaworthy, Gerecke, Wirt, and Orestes Gonof [D:163]. Duffey also includes Gargoyles among the Teras variants whose blood he puts in unusually large amounts into his creations [MTM B5:169-170]. A Chesterton epigraph imagines monsters as gargoyles belonging to a definite cathedral, whose architecture and consecration the writer affirms [MTM B5:177].","citations":["A:6","D:39","D:162","D:163","MTM B5:169-170","MTM B5:177"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Finnegan","Duffey","Literature","Creation","Identity"]},{"name":"Garlic","definition":"De Polis puts garlic into the <em>Brunhilde</em>'s stew as Anastasia jokes that prayer and garlic can change the world's nature [D:68]. Theresa Piccone reports cooking three days of specialties at Monica Stranahan's house and leaving a lingering garlic smell. Monica compares garlic to sin and adapts the Gospel warning of woe to whoever brings it [A:48]. The Old Wooden Ship also stands at the end of a garlic block [D:215].","citations":["D:68","A:48","D:215"],"tags":["Sin & Redemption","Scripture","Ships","Women Characters","Bars & Taverns","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Garlic block","definition":"Pungent neighborhood description for the block whose last building is the Old Wooden Ship [D:215]. The tavern is explicitly placed in Galveston in <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> [MTM B13:458].","citations":["D:215","MTM B13:458"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Garment, Seamless","definition":"The Seamless Garment of Christ, listed among the <em>Argo</em> Masters' findings alongside such relics and treasures as Roland's Horn and the Holy Shroud [MTM B13:422].","citations":["MTM B13:422"],"tags":["New Testament","Argonautica","Ships"]},{"name":"Garnets","definition":"Stones singled out by the Royal Pop lecturer, with spars and other imperfect crystals, as especially good at recording events in every direction. Hard prismatic crystals are said to focus more particularly on the direction ordinarily called the future [MTM B11:330].","citations":["MTM B11:330"],"tags":["History & Memory","Prophecy & Visions","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Garrard, Ouida (Cotton-Picker)","definition":"Cotton Picker, a Cajun woman among the resident regulars of the Old Wooden Ship [D:216], the tavern later explicitly located in Galveston [MTM B13:458]. She goes around with Catherine Cadensus, Vivian Gilligan, and Mercedes Morrero, a quartet the narrator calls exceptionally pretty. She enjoys strangers taking her dancing and riding and makes them feel welcome [D:224].","citations":["D:216","MTM B13:458","D:224"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Gas","definition":"Fuel for the burners under Duffey's enormous soup crock [A:133] [MTM B6:195]. At his St. Louis horse-barn business he has gas lighting with replaceable mantles, a gas cook stove, and plans for a gas heater to supply hot water [MTM B2:40-42]. Gas lighting is cut off at night in the boys' school rooms, so his after-curfew store operates by candles or kerosene [MTM B1:26].\n\nHydrogen lifts Duffey's balloon [MTM B2:63]. Charley Murray proposes compressed-gas or kerosene lights, but Duffey favors electric lighting after reading of fatal explosions on other balloons [MTM B2:64-65]. After the candle-lit dinner aloft, he extinguishes the candles and knocks Murray's cigar overboard before opening a gate valve to release hydrogen and descend [MTM B2:66]. Separately, Finnegan jokes that a jury trial for his disturbance would have sent him to the gas chamber [A:264].","citations":["A:133","MTM B6:195","MTM B2:40-42","MTM B1:26","MTM B2:63","MTM B2:64-65","MTM B2:66","A:264"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan","Food & Dining","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Gas Chamber","definition":"The punishment Finnegan imagines he would have received if he had asked for a jury trial after being arrested for disturbing the peace. He writes that the judge would have imposed ten years if empowered to do so, then escalates the joke to the gas chamber [A:264].","citations":["A:264"],"tags":["Finnegan","Crime","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Gascinade River","definition":"Spelling used in Mary Louise's remark that Bagby's supposedly unwritten letters are as fishy as the Gascinade River [MTM B7:219]. It apparently points to Missouri's Gasconade River, though whether the variant spelling is deliberate is uncertain.","citations":["MTM B7:219"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Gasoline","definition":"Fuel in the Dirty Five's improvised lanterns, beer bottles fitted with tightly twisted rag wicks [A:39]. Duffey also equips his balloon with a gasoline engine and propeller, but these affect its speed by only about five miles an hour [MTM B2:64].","citations":["A:39","MTM B2:64"],"tags":["Duffey","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Gate of Hell","definition":"Earlier singular heading referred to the plural portae inferi in the cited banner. See <em>Gates of Hell</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["New Testament","Heaven & Hell","Scripture","Ecclesiastical","Ships","Dreams","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Gate Valve","definition":"Valve Duffey opens to release hydrogen and bring his balloon down after the dinner party aloft. Before opening it he blows out the candles and knocks Charley Murray's cigar overboard [MTM B2:66].","citations":["MTM B2:66"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Gates of Hell","definition":"The gates of hell, portae inferi, in the banner Portae inferi non prevalebunt on <em>Navicula Petri</em> in Henry Salvatore's dream. It forms a sequence with Tu es Petrus and tibi dabo claves regni [A:44], echoing Matthew 16:18-19 and the promise that the gates of hell will not prevail against the Church.","citations":["A:44"],"tags":["New Testament","Heaven & Hell","Scripture","Ecclesiastical","Ships","Dreams","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Gates of Labor / Media / Pentecostals / Roarers / Teilhardians / Gays / Hot Brains / Levelers","definition":"Constituencies assigned new Princes, Gates, or Stones in the attempt to give the Ekklesia a sufficiently broad base to survive. The list includes Labor, Media, Pentecostals, Roarers, Teilhardians, Gays, Hot Brains, and Levelers. Media groups receive thirteen Stones and Labor sixteen. Instrumentalists have separate representatives, including five Gates for electric guitarists [MB:561-562].","citations":["MB:561-562"],"tags":["Ekklesia","Ecclesiastical","Music","Social Class"]},{"name":"Gates, The","definition":"Popular title, alongside Stones, for the Princes of the Ekklesia, explained through Cardines, hinges or swingers [MB:561]. Six hundred sixty-six attend the Last Conclave in Babylonia Bagascia, with thirteen Princes absent [MB:566]. Under Hackenschmidt's armed coercion, six hundred sixty vote for the Straw-Man and six for Joseph Cardinal Hedayat; the six dissenting votes are then counted for the effigy [MB:567]. Before the conclave, several Gates break open the decapitated Paul the Eleventh's skull and take his brains to fry with onions and eat [MB:562-563].","citations":["MB:561","MB:566","MB:567","MB:562-563"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia","Social Class","Crime"]},{"name":"Gautier (Goat-man)","definition":"An old French goat-herder whom Finnegan meets in New Jersey's back country. He lives with about forty goats in barns, shacks, caves, and summer camps, mowing grass for their winter feed [A:194-195]. He came from southern France in 1908 with six goats, was driven out of New York with them, and after decades of travel now remains in Jersey. His name Gautier is derisively pronounced Goat-Ear because of his goats and hairy ears; he prefers goat-man [A:196].\n\nHe is a self-sufficient, one-man Gypsy camp with three carts, each drawn by four goats, tools including a loom and wheel, and skill in making nets, seines, lines, and traps. He eats and trades goat cheese, makes berry jelly and wine, corn beer and potato whisky [A:196-197], and eats boiled wheat with milk and honey, acorns, and dried goat meat chopped with fat and honey for storage [A:197]. His syrinx and bagpipes account for much of the supposed ghost music of the swamps. He sometimes occupies Harlow House and may himself account for its ghost, though whether the house is truly haunted remains uncertain. The claim that ghosts can be seen without fear is made about goats [A:197].\n\nAt Harlow House he reads French books aloud to the goats, including Balzac's Contes Drolatiques, Stendhal, Daudet's Tartarin, Lesage's Gil Blas, and Dumas's Count of Monte Cristo. After they sleep, he reads Old American Blood and Thunder romances privately [A:198-199]. Finnegan first gains the goats' friendship by helping Luin, the one youngster unable to join the others climbing a fallen sycamore. The seven youngest have weekday names: Bownee, Luin, Mairt, Keadin, Ordin, Haoine, and Sahirn [A:194-195]. Finnegan's fancy that Gautier resembles an aged Pan remains a fancy [A:194]. He finds the goat-man a spoofer and a little wild, enjoys staying about a week, but refuses the offered inheritance and leaves [A:199-200].","citations":["A:194-195","A:196","A:196-197","A:197","A:198-199","A:194","A:199-200"],"tags":["France","Finnegan","Ghosts & Spirits","Fishing","Food & Dining","Music","Occupations","Literature","Greek Mythology","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Gawk, Golden","definition":"X's mocking image of intellectual idol-worship in his letter rebuking Stein for taking Teilhard seriously. He tells Stein to renounce the adoration of the golden gawk [A:266]. The phrase may recall the golden calf of Exodus 32, but that particular allusion is not explicit.","citations":["A:266"],"tags":["Old Testament","Language & Words","Theology"]},{"name":"Gayety","definition":"Gaiety in Finnegan's “Antipodal Ode to Loy Larkin”: he praises the joy she has drawn from life alongside the southern roil and rant [A:32].","citations":["A:32"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Gays","definition":"One of the constituencies represented by Gates in the expanded Ekklesia before the Last Conclave at Babylonia Bagascia [MB:562] [MB:566].","citations":["MB:562","MB:566"],"tags":["Ekklesia","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Gazelle","definition":"One of the animals furnishing the Boy King Melchisedech with what is called silk but is identified as milk in actuality, along with the camel, ass, ewe, cow, and India buffalo. This belongs to the more genuine childhood behind his temporal lives [MTM B1:17-18].","citations":["MTM B1:17-18"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Gdansk","definition":"Baltic port near the mouth of the Vistula, where the disguised Kasmir Gorshok joins Duffey and Brannagan aboard the <em>Argo</em>. They initially fail to recognize him; his identification reveals Casey Szymansky, a true <em>Argo</em> Master [MTM B13:424-426].","citations":["MTM B13:424-426"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","Seas & Oceans","Argonautica","Identity"]},{"name":"Gedanken von Leibern","definition":"Gedanken von Leibern, the work credited to Ignace Wolff beneath the epigraph to Chapter Five, The Unaccountable Corpse, of The Devil is Dead. The passage makes bodies the exception to non-contradiction and denies sufficient reason for their existence [D:67].","citations":["D:67"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Paradox","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Gedanken von Liebern","definition":"Earlier heading reversed letters in the source’s title Gedanken von Leibern. See <em>Gedanken von Leibern</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Paradox","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Gelding","definition":"Castrated male horse. While planning his sea-stallion paintings, Finnegan imagines a stallion’s lunging gallop as a breaker and declares a sea gelding impossible [D:60].","citations":["D:60"],"tags":["Finnegan","Visual Arts","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Gems","definition":"Jewels in several literal and figurative settings. Ignatius Ti's account of Amoy's former life as a thieving bird includes a swallowed blue diamond, star sapphires, and opals [A:80-81]. Hilary Hilton says he has traded Finnegan diamonds [A:255], while Duffey says Finnegan and Joseph distribute diamonds to Finnegan's friends [MTM B10:303]. Primary documents are compared to treasures of gold and gems brought into daylight [MTM B6:185]. The Royal Pop lecturer wears a turban with a sparkling gem or eye [MTM B11:324]; he says two dozen cheap gems inside the Decatur Street Opera House preserve its complete history [MTM B11:328].","citations":["A:80-81","A:255","MTM B10:303","MTM B6:185","MTM B11:324","MTM B11:328"],"tags":["Finnegan","Money & Trade","New Orleans","Theater","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Gene pool","definition":"In conversation with Gertrude Brannagan about Eva, Duffey says that every few centuries another of her people is released from cold storage to contribute to the gene pool. He predicts no disturbance until their proportion reaches about one in ten thousand and qualifies their supposed unfallen state as a popular legend they themselves believe [MTM B13:410].","citations":["MTM B13:410"],"tags":["Duffey","Theology","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"General Judgment","definition":"The final judgment of humanity. In the theological discussion surrounding the Casey Machine, a soggy sinner asks whether everyone will know all human thoughts and deeds after the Particular or General Judgment. His pastor says everyone will know, but is unsure at which judgment full knowledge arrives; he distinguishes the saved person’s joy from the damned person’s suffering [CM:504].","citations":["CM:504"],"tags":["Theology","Sin & Redemption","Time & Eternity","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Genesis","definition":"First book of the Bible. In the discussion of Casey and evil, the narrator invokes Genesis for the human heart’s evil from youth, an allusion to Genesis 8:21 [A:154]. Creation and the Flood also shape the Fundamentalers’ chronology when they compare it with Zabotski’s Pristine History [MTM B9:278-280].","citations":["A:154","MTM B9:278-280"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Creation","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Genesis 14:18","definition":"The verse about Melchisedech, king of Salem, bringing out bread and wine supplies the epigraph to Book Two, “Late Boyhood of a Magus” [MTM B2:38]. It links Duffey’s name to the biblical king and priest and introduces the bread-and-wine imagery associated with him.","citations":["MTM B2:38"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Duffey","Food & Dining","Literature"]},{"name":"Genesis 6:4","definition":"The verse about giants, divine sons, human daughters, and ancient men of renown is the epigraph to Chapter Fourteen, Company of Fifty, of The Devil is Dead. Finnegan immediately answers with the mighty men of the present and asks why his remembered short, stooped earth-people have been remembered as giants [D:212].","citations":["D:212"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Giants & Titans","Finnegan","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Geni in the Bottle","definition":"Image in Trinali’s wine-making poem. After describing wine as a divine birth and resurrection, she identifies the Geni in the Bottle with a God imprisoned by the dry desert people who hate that God [A:231].","citations":["A:231"],"tags":["Alcohol","Literature","Theology","Resurrection"]},{"name":"Genius","definition":"Intellectual or creative power in several registers. Letitia’s question about the block’s confounded geniuses points to Casey, whose genius is then directly affirmed [MTM B4:115] [MTM B4:118]. His television projections depend on both electronic genius and psychic magnitude [MTM B12:380]. Brannagan calls Finnegan an impelled genius [MTM B13:453], and Enniscorthy Sweeny is called a near-genius [MTM B13:467]. The Balbo dynasty retains its genius even as its members become mad [MB:558]. Duffey tells the cured stutterer that removing his frustration turns an evil genius into a good or at least complacent one [MTM B13:431]. The postscript’s argument about Havelock Ellis leads to the assertion that all humanity consists of glowing geniuses [PS:477].","citations":["MTM B4:115","MTM B4:118","MTM B12:380","MTM B13:453","MTM B13:467","MB:558","MTM B13:431","PS:477"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan","Metaphysics","Creation","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Gentleman burglar","definition":"One of the former occupations X attributes to Papadiabolous II, Gregory McColum Papadiabolous, who also uses the name Noonan. Before police work he was a gentleman burglar, contrabandist, swindler, and forger; X says that training later proved useful [D:285-286].","citations":["D:285-286"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Crime","Occupations","Identity"]},{"name":"Gentleman Rounder","definition":"Magazine for which Absalom Stein has agreed to write reviews after returning from the Devildom. His daughter Becky describes its contempt for ordinary people and worries over his new sense of superiority [MTM B12:355]. Separately, Duffey's Rounders' Club advertises itself to the Gentlemen Rounders of the World [MTM B2:49].","citations":["MTM B12:355","MTM B2:49"],"tags":["Literature","Duffey","Clubs & Societies","Social Class"]},{"name":"Gentlemen","definition":"A recurring form of address and social description. John Rattigan calls time on the boys' school sales with 'Time, gentlemen,' and Lily Koch uses the corresponding address to ladies and gentlemen [MTM B1:27] [MTM B1:29]. Duffey advertises a quality gentlemen's bar and club; eight shabby drinking gentlemen soon occupy a loafers' bench [MTM B2:41] [MTM B2:44]. Bagby's tall tale fills the former horse-barn flophouse with five hundred and fifty men sleeping on hooks [MTM B2:44-45]. Finnegan remembers Buffalo's droll gentlemen and ladies [A:191]. In the Louisiana swamps, Duffey and Brannagan meet three hospitable gentlemen: Leonard Archive, Oliver Greenflag, and Harry A. Honeybucket Kincaid [MTM B13:417].","citations":["MTM B1:27","MTM B1:29","MTM B2:41","MTM B2:44","MTM B2:44-45","A:191","MTM B13:417"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","Clubs & Societies","Louisiana","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Geography","definition":"Something exceeded by Finnegan’s account of Chosky Bottoms, a general spook-place always over the next ridge, and by his similarly flexible use of St. Kitts [D:274]. Margaret Stone reports that the Black Sea has been obliterated and that the Royal Pop People call this the end of the old geography [MTM B11:308].","citations":["D:274","MTM B11:308"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Ghosts & Spirits","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Geometry","definition":"Subject that John William Schultz says may be deferred, along with algebra, in his proposal to compress elementary schooling into a week's evenings before real education begins [A:208-209]. During the Royal Pop gathering, the Geometry of Shining Space names the calculation through which the visitors' presence expands Duffey's buildings [MTM B11:321]. Geometry and the art of words are then said to have changed to suit the new conditions [MTM B11:323].","citations":["A:208-209","MTM B11:321","MTM B11:323"],"tags":["Duffey","Family","Language & Words","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Gerecke, William","definition":"One of Saxon Seaworthy's two cronies, with Peter Wirt, in the <em>Brunhilde</em>'s ruling circle [D:30]. Finnegan senses in him the same disturbing power associated with Seaworthy, Papa Devil, and the other peculiar people [D:50]. After the deaths of both Papadiabolouses, X names Gerecke among the four remaining rulers [D:155]. At the later meeting Finnegan spies upon, Gerecke is one of four senior gargoyles and is described as wiry as a spider [D:163]. Finnegan intends to stab Seaworthy, but kills Gerecke in the dark instead [D:164-165].","citations":["D:30","D:50","D:155","D:163","D:164-165"],"tags":["Ships","Devils (Characters)","Finnegan","Combat","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"German","definition":"Language and background associated with Hans Schultz and his German father John William, who teaches him German and Latin [A:208]. Hans receives a German letter from his father [A:39]; Casey learns German and Polish from his own father [A:156] [A:159]. X travels as the supposed West German Karl Metz and is later said to be one-quarter German through his mother's family [MTM B12:369] [MTM B12:371-372].\n\nGerman lunches belong to Duffey and his school friends' outings [MTM B1:25]. The Bavarian Club has Germanish blonde entertainers, including Helen Platner [MTM B2:58], and German women attend St. Malachy's in Chicago [MTM B3:88]. Duffey's stolen medal stock includes what he calls the German Blue Max [MTM B4:115-116]. Hilary Hilton trades German clay [A:255]. Hackenschmidt addresses the conclave in a bright German-silver voice [MB:567].","citations":["A:208","A:39","A:156","A:159","MTM B12:369","MTM B12:371-372","MTM B1:25","MTM B2:58","MTM B3:88","MTM B4:115-116","A:255","MB:567"],"tags":["Germany","Duffey","Family","Identity","Language & Words","Food & Dining","Clubs & Societies","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"German clay","definition":"Commodity Hilary Hilton includes in his wide-ranging trade, alongside such goods as uncased Swiss movements, tropical birds, and private libraries [A:255].","citations":["A:255"],"tags":["Germany","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Germanies, The","definition":"Designation for German lands: Conrad Stone Hackenschmidt comes from Weinsburg in the Germanies [MB:566], while Duffey includes Conrad the Second of the Germanies among the kings whose conclaves he remembers attending [MTM B5:166].","citations":["MB:566","MTM B5:166"],"tags":["Germany","History & Memory","Duffey"]},{"name":"Ghost","definition":"An apparition, double, surviving presence, or figure of estrangement. Finnegan feels like a defective ghost searching for his misplaced soul [A:56]; he later calls X his antipodal ghost [D:209]. Private Gregory identifies ghosts with devils in the psychiatric ward's storytelling [A:84-87]. Gautier's pipes account for ghost music in the swamps, and he may himself be the supposed ghost of Harlow House; the claim of seeing ghosts concerns his goats [A:197]. Archipelago's fantastical account of Black Papua includes a nightly ghost life and a waterfall that disappears after the morning rain [A:36-37].\n\nMary Louise Byrne is apparition-like, and Beth Keegan's living-statue quality prompts the observation that such stories are ghost stories [MTM B2:55] [MTM B2:57]. Teresa describes Finnegan's ghostly visits and inhabiting of people [MTM B6:188]; accounts of his reported deaths leave his survival disputed [MB:548-549]. Mary Virginia encounters Duffey as a ghost after his death in the crash year, while young Teresa sees him in an alternate ending and suggests he can be a young ghost [MTM B12:359-361] [MTM B13:472-473].\n\nOther uses retain their narrative qualifications. Le Marin tells a disputed story of the deserted <em>Sarcophagus</em> and his own disappearance [D:243-246]. Soldiers unable to silence the Sea-Bell and Martyr-Bell near San Simeon call them ghost bells [MB:560] [MB:576]. The apparent supernatural clientele of the Ghost Bar is immediately retracted in favor of Anthony Ghost's coffee shop, though Finnegan's painting remains [MTM B10:292-293]. The epigraph credited to Carmody Overlark connects ghostliness with the other people's inherited mental powers [D:270-272]. In the postscript, Lafferty calls his continuing work A Ghost Story and links the phrase to everyone's capacity to split into ghostly aspects; high spirits can make such multiplicity visible [PS:475] [PS:477-478].","citations":["A:56","D:209","A:84-87","A:197","A:36-37","MTM B2:55","MTM B2:57","MTM B6:188","MB:548-549","MTM B12:359-361","MTM B13:472-473","D:243-246","MB:560","MB:576","MTM B10:292-293","D:270-272","PS:475","PS:477-478"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Finnegan","Duffey","New Orleans","Literature","Identity","Death & Mortality","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Ghost Bar","definition":"Name used in the initially supernatural description of the Ghost Coffee Shop in New Orleans. Ghosts supposedly meet before and after dawn to exchange haunting stories, with a Finnegan painting of their gathering on the wall. The narrator then retracts the whole account except the painting and substitutes the account of Anthony Ghost's ordinary clientele [MTM B10:292-293].","citations":["MTM B10:292-293"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","New Orleans","Finnegan","Visual Arts","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Ghost Coffee Shop, The","definition":"New Orleans coffee shop run by Anthony Ghost, whose father came from the Netherlands. Its morning customers include dock workers, seamen, wandering Quarter people, night people compared to ghosts, and schoolchildren. Ursuline Academy day students come for breakfast after early mass at the Cathedral. Duffey, a customer for thirty years, talks there with Crissie Cristofero, Therese Doucet, and Cleo Mahoney about <em>Seven Roads</em>; Rebeka Stein sometimes joins the girls [MTM B10:293]. The preceding account of literal ghosts is retracted, but the large Finnegan painting of their gathering remains [MTM B10:292].","citations":["MTM B10:293","MTM B10:292"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Theater","Visual Arts","Ghosts & Spirits","Occupations"]},{"name":"Ghost, Anthony","definition":"Proprietor of the Ghost Coffee Shop in New Orleans, whose father came from the Netherlands. His customers include dock workers, seamen, night people and schoolchildren; Duffey has been coming there for thirty years [MTM B10:293]. During the discussion of <em>Seven Roads</em> and the potholes that lead into alternate futures, Anthony tells Duffey that he will step into a pothole if one is nearby [MTM B10:294].","citations":["MTM B10:293","MTM B10:294"],"tags":["Minor Characters","New Orleans","Food & Dining","Duffey"]},{"name":"Ghostliness","definition":"The books' recurring uncertainty about apparitions, doubles, bodily identity, and the division between living and dead. The postscript calls Lafferty's continuing work A Ghost Story because people can split into aspects without knowing which is the self and which the ghost [PS:475]. It treats visible multiplicity as common but commonly suppressed in memory, illustrating the idea with Fingal's nine black-cloaked fetches accompanying his white-cloaked self and an alleged beheaded double of Charles I [PS:477-478]. These are the postscript's accounts. Mary Virginia calls ghosts the essence of ambiguity while addressing Duffey, whom she says has been dead for months; he fades from her view and withdraws from the crash year [MTM B12:359-361].\n\nThe psychiatric-ward stories distinguish several explanations. Private Gregory identifies ghosts with devils and calls possession hypnotism by an immortal will, with great men serving as inhabited shells [A:84-85]. Howell locates the horror in the familiar dead returning and says Irish wake stories temper it with humor. Gregory attributes early fears of revenants to anti-Christian stories of the risen Christ [A:85]. Ignatius Ti explains midnight as a temporary opening in the veil through which the dead may return; he describes fear of corpses and drunken medical pranksters, then tells of a wife who unknowingly sleeps beside her murdered husband's ghost. After his departure, the narrator allows loose ghosts into the ward and the boys' thoughts [A:85-87].\n\nFinnegan's ghostliness includes his sense of being a defective ghost seeking a soul [A:56] and of looking out from a stranger's body [A:92]. Vincent calls Theresa a literal ghost and compares her with Finnegan [A:112]. Teresa later writes that Finnegan inhabits Vincent and herself, that a visitation frightened Stein, and that person and ghost can coexist. She claims the same talent and identifies herself as Anemotrephes in a detached experience rather than a dream [MTM B6:188]. Dotty calls Finnegan her ghost-lover, explicitly distinguishes this from Teresa's intimacy with him, and says Teresa and Finnegan were never lovers [MTM B6:188-189]. Stein's accounts question the repeated reports of Finnegan's death [MTM B6:187] [MB:548-549]. Sally and Duffey see a young painter as a possible younger ghost or fetch of him [MTM B11:333]. After Anastasia's death, Finnegan says he too is dead; he later calls X his antipodal ghost [D:156] [D:209].\n\nDopey leaves a presence Finnegan senses in his bunk [D:64] [D:78]. Papadiabolous II offers the possibility that Dopey was Finnegan's fetch and was absorbed into him; he describes himself as the close double of another [D:121-122]. The apparent return of the buried Papa Devil terrifies Seaworthy [D:84], but X's later account distinguishes two Papadiabolouses [D:285-286]. Finnegan's dream encounters with the Oreads and an old Teras belong to the same uncertain border of person, kindred, and apparition [D:137-140]. Le Marin's tale of the deserted <em>Sarcophagus</em> instead explains the disappearing crew through doubles, piracy, and murder, and is challenged as a recycled <em>Mary Celeste</em> story [D:243-246].\n\nDuffey's alternate ending places him beyond ordinary sight until five-year-old Teresa Piccone recognizes a ghost who has either died or not yet been born. She also reports talking to Vincent, dead before her birth. She proposes keeping in touch by playing ghost, suggests that Duffey can be young again, and offers to give him a future birth [MTM B13:472-473]. Mary Louise Byrne's resemblance to an apparition and Beth Keegan's living-statue effect offer other personal forms of ghostliness [MTM B2:55-57]. Newton Prescott describes himself as Casey's orange-gloved ghostly splinter, subject to shifting years and retained as Casey's eyes and ears. The apartment viewers glimpse his hands, while the rental lady sees and speaks with him; he connects her ability to the Casey Machine [CM:515-516]. The <em>Argo</em>'s mechanical crew are expressly place tokens or materialized residues of absent sailors, rather than wraiths or effigies [MTM B13:415].\n\nPlaces and events also carry uncertain ghostliness. Archipelago gives Black Papua a nightly life among the dead [A:37]. Gautier makes the swamp's ghost music; the haunting of Harlow House remains uncertain, and apparent ghost-seeing belongs to his goats [A:197]. Chosky Bottoms is a movable spook-place [D:274]. The supernatural description of New Orleans ghosts arriving along Ste. Ann, Dumaine, St. Philip, Ursulines, Decatur, Chartres, Bourbon, Frenchman, and Elysian Field is retracted in favor of Anthony Ghost's coffee shop and ordinary night people; the Finnegan painting survives the retraction [MTM B10:292-293]. The Royal Pop upheaval dislodges evil spirits and uncreations [MTM B11:314], while Duffey and Stein discuss phenomena that unhappen and become unremembered [MTM B11:339-340]. The Thinking Machine proposes guarding blind fireplaces against blind spirits [MTM B13:455-456]. Casey's school notebook identifies human attackers behind one alleged poltergeist encounter without treating that explanation as disproof of all poltergeists [A:164].\n\nCorporeal remains keep these questions concrete. The Devil is Dead announces people unwilling to stay dead [D:6]. Its first remembered encounter stands across from the graveyard, while Finnegan later recalls first meeting Seaworthy inside it and helping put Papa Devil over an older burial [D:10] [D:75-76]. The later graveyard rendezvous with X and Doll locates the grave marked I.N.C.G.P. Bogovitch [D:275-277]. X takes custody of Duffey's ashes, expecting to return them to him alive [MTM B13:442]. Demetrio Glauch reports a <em>Crock</em> advertisement selling dead bodies as emptied temples [PG:546]; John Mogul describes his own killing business as the work of a knacker and euthanasiast [MB:572-573]. These uses of bodies accompany the ghost theme without themselves proving an apparition.","citations":["PS:475","PS:477-478","MTM B12:359-361","A:84-85","A:85","A:85-87","A:56","A:92","A:112","MTM B6:188","MTM B6:188-189","MTM B6:187","MB:548-549","MTM B11:333","D:156","D:209","D:64","D:78","D:121-122","D:84","D:285-286","D:137-140","D:243-246","MTM B13:472-473","MTM B2:55-57","CM:515-516","MTM B13:415","A:37","A:197","D:274","MTM B10:292-293","MTM B11:314","MTM B11:339-340","MTM B13:455-456","A:164","D:6","D:10","D:75-76","D:275-277","MTM B13:442","PG:546","MB:572-573"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Finnegan","Duffey","Women Characters","The Dead","Metaphysics","Identity","Death & Mortality","Resurrection","Dreams","Literature","New Orleans","Ships","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Ghostly Thing","definition":"Unlocated phrase in the working glossary, associated there with a paean sung by Sergeant Murphy's subjects. Its source and proposed connection with the Holy Ghost remain unverified.","citations":[],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits"]},{"name":"Giant Hands","definition":"The hands of Duffey's giants, which can be visible although their owners are invisible. As a boy he commands a giant hand to pull a church bell rope, and three companions see it [MTM B1:15]. His giants break rocks and release springs and rivers; their hands also make exceptionally intricate conjuring props [MTM B1:18] [MTM B1:21]. Moving a mountain is a hypothetical possibility when Morris Poor challenges him [MTM B4:129]. Giant hands then lift a mule across a traffic island, seen by some witnesses and sensed by all. The photographs differ: one shows the airborne mule without hands, another shows hands with possible double-exposure blurs [MTM B4:131-132].\n\nIn Duffey's dream the hands arrive severed at the forearms, bloodied, powerless, and manacled; they then resolve into a little boy's mittens joined by a drawstring [MTM B2:68]. A picture of Severed Giant Hands hangs above the doorway to the Rounders' Club's Elegant Riverboat Deck. Before his own creations, Duffey feels comparably deprived of creative power [MTM B5:156].","citations":["MTM B1:15","MTM B1:18","MTM B1:21","MTM B4:129","MTM B4:131-132","MTM B2:68","MTM B5:156"],"tags":["Giants & Titans","Duffey","Clubs & Societies","Dreams","Creation"]},{"name":"Giant(s)","definition":"Duffey commands visible and invisible giants to perform miracles, including breaking rocks, releasing water, ringing a bell, and lifting a mule [MTM B1:5] [MTM B1:15] [MTM B1:18] [MTM B4:131-132]. His ebony or faber-giants help manufacture difficult props; more external art giants guide him toward works by others that surpass excellence [MTM B1:21] [MTM B2:54]. Dandy Dan Dillard seems to rout the giants during Duffey's apparent beating, but Duffey discovers that he has won the fight unmarked and that Dan is the injured fighter [MTM B2:51]. Four black demiurgic giants carry Casey's head-only person in Duffey's television projection from an Adam Scanlon painting; the head is explicitly not shown as severed [MTM B12:381].\n\nOther uses differ in kind. Genesis 6:4 supplies the giants epigraph [D:212]; Patrick Stranahan laments the Church's closed age of giants [A:110]. Papa is called a giant [D:110], while the Majesty is an evil, illusory giant whose mind Duffey enters [MTM B8:241] [MTM B8:243]. A little boy's account makes the Wiper-Person a cloud-high giant who erases clouds and memories [CM:513]. Henry's mythological identities include the Giant of the Beanstalk [A:39]; Marie is the Golden Giantess [D:33]. Giant creatures include the spider in Hans's climbing dream [A:61], squid in Trinali's fanciful account of driftwood [A:233], and the stag in Finnegan's transport into ancestral memory [D:213]. Finnegan's island-creation story invokes great sea turtles [A:35], and San Simeon's legends include the giant huracan bird [MB:557-558].","citations":["MTM B1:5","MTM B1:15","MTM B1:18","MTM B4:131-132","MTM B1:21","MTM B2:54","MTM B2:51","MTM B12:381","D:212","A:110","D:110","MTM B8:241","MTM B8:243","CM:513","A:39","D:33","A:61","A:233","D:213","A:35","MB:557-558"],"tags":["Old Testament","Miracles","Scripture","Monsters & Creatures","Giants & Titans","Duffey","Devils (Characters)","Dreams","Creation"]},{"name":"Giwa, John","definition":"John Giwa of Anecho, the thirteenth double or shadow-man and the replacement for dead Cardinal Doki of Douala. He flies to Marseille, cuts twelve fingers and thumbs from John Mogul's hands, skewers Mogul's tongue, then travels through Miami and parachutes onto Mooring Rock to join the other twelve conclave members. The narrator calls him violent and holy. He says their inner revelation assures them that at least one member is a true cardinal, enough for the conclave's legitimacy [MB:596]. He blesses the risen land as Babylonia Sancta and asks the dead Melchisedech to speak living words. X calls the vote and crowns Finnegan; Giwa greets him with “Reign in grace, Pope Finnegan the First” [MB:597]. When the impersonator of Cardinal Mercy is revealed as a woman, Giwa demands an explanation from X [MB:598].","citations":["MB:596","MB:597","MB:598"],"tags":["Popes","Ecclesiastical","Africa","Finnegan","Ekklesia","Crime"]},{"name":"Glauch, Demetrio","definition":"Writer and contributor to the <em>Crock</em> under Duffey; a wartime issue includes his ballad [A:41] [MTM B4:138]. Casey names him among Duffey's prospects for the Catholic Center Party of America [A:42]. He denounces the Curley Q and the establishments of Fleshpot Row on Randolph Street as a stifling bawdy house and leaves. Duffey's reluctance to leave with him creates lasting disappointment between them [MTM B4:125-127]. He is present when Morris Poor challenges Duffey's magic and later attends Hilary Hilton's party [MTM B4:128] [MTM B12:383]. Glauch signs the opening assessment of Casey in “Promontory Goats,” dated All Souls Day, Chicago, 1984 [PG:518-519]. In a later commentary he reports that Casey's own supporters or associates have stolen the body, recalling a <em>Crock</em> advertisement for corpses [PG:546].","citations":["A:41","MTM B4:138","A:42","MTM B4:125-127","MTM B4:128","MTM B12:383","PG:518-519","PG:546"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Duffey","Literary Figures","Bars & Taverns","Literature","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Globigerina glacé","definition":"Fantastical salad ordered by Duffey during the <em>Argo</em> Masters' visit to Honeybucket Kincaid's swamp establishment. He also orders chlamydomonas with kelp syrup, pond-scum bread, sea-lice soup, and a Hashed-Ectocarpus Collins [MTM B13:417]. The name borrows Globigerina, a real genus of foraminifera. Kincaid's electrical equipment selects and prepares the fish in this meal, but the text does not specify how the salad is made [MTM B13:417].","citations":["MTM B13:417"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Glotglutz, Otto","definition":"Nightclub comedian nicknamed Glotzie and described as a sick comedian. Hiram III's unexpected answering quip reduces him to tears, while shocked onlookers cry “Shame! Shame!” [MB:564].","citations":["MB:564"],"tags":["Theater","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"God","definition":"The characters’ accounts of God range from comic claims to questions about creation, salvation, and the fate of particular people. In Ward Fourteen, Green says that God sustains the universe from moment to moment, identifies himself as God, and demonstrates his power by dimming the lights; Gregory points out that a compressor has just been switched on [A:76]. Sulem says that God has forgotten to give Finnegan a <em>tujuan</em>, or direction in life [A:60]. Askandan, by contrast, assures Finnegan that he has a soul and that God is in him [A:258].<br><br>Patrick Stranahan says their faith forbids them to regard anyone as doomed [A:102]. Henry answers the practical problem of rebuilding the world by saying that God gave them each other [A:126]. The account of Finnegan and Theresa’s uncertain twelve years together ends in gratitude for the richness of that life [A:204]. Don Lewis asks whether God knows the singulars who seem to have been forgotten [D:48].<br><br>The seventh contingency brings Duffey into a garden with the Invisible God, who affirms the uniqueness of every person [MTM B10:301] [MTM B12:389]. Later, God comes aboard the <em>Argo</em> and tells Duffey’s bones that death has not released him from his work as pilot and master [MTM B13:443].<br><br>Other references include the discussion of holy wealth and holy poverty [MTM B4:125], Theresa’s blessing of the wine [A:108], and Seaworthy’s comic threat of divine punishment for bad beer [D:12]. X’s explanation of “Bogovitch” as “son of God” describes a euphemism on a gravestone, not a literal statement of the buried Devil’s divine parentage [D:277-278].","citations":["A:76","A:60","A:258","A:102","A:126","A:204","D:48","MTM B10:301","MTM B12:389","MTM B13:443","MTM B4:125","A:108","D:12","D:277-278"],"tags":["Theology","Creation","Metaphysics","Sin & Redemption","Finnegan","Duffey"]},{"name":"God's spy (Zophiel)","definition":"Unverified heading in the working glossary for a supposed speculation by Duffey about a divine spy or faculty called Zophiel. No supporting passage has been located in the three core books.","citations":[],"tags":["Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Goldbeater, Clement","definition":"Name credited with an Enniscorthy Chronicle passage used as the epigraph to Chapter Ten, “Down with the Dead Men,” in <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>. Its children play at being the Other Kind of People while remaining indistinguishable from ordinary people, introducing the chapter's uncertainty over identity [D:143].","citations":["D:143"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Identity"]},{"name":"Goldchild, Harry","definition":"Wealthy benefactor who had the Golden Children's Home and Haven Orphanage built and endowed several decades before the Zabotski episode. The narrator calls him a bloated plutocrat [MTM B9:280].","citations":["MTM B9:280"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Golden Children's Home and Haven Orphanage","definition":"Orphanage behind Zabotski's building site, founded and endowed decades earlier by Harry Goldchild and distinguished by a golden roof [MTM B9:271] [MTM B9:280]. His expanding contraption encroaches on its grounds, and the state inspector orders that part removed [MTM B9:274]. Widow Waldo serves the orphans, who assemble green-clay animals capable of moving, though the early ones fall apart [MTM B9:275]. Children carry these animals into the contraption; Waldo, children, and animals depart with Zabotski during the flood, and his craft subsequently reaches his lot on Lake Borgne [MTM B9:284] [MTM B9:287].","citations":["MTM B9:271","MTM B9:280","MTM B9:274","MTM B9:275","MTM B9:284","MTM B9:287"],"tags":["Ships","Money & Trade","Clubs & Societies","Creation"]},{"name":"Golden Ogress, The (Marie Courtois)","definition":"Finnegan's name for Marie Courtois, Seaworthy's seagoing secretary, whom he first remembers as the Golden Ogress in an apparent dream while she collects his details for the ship's register [D:32]. She is also the Golden Giantess, a formidable red-gold-haired woman whose strength and beauty are described in primordial terms. Anastasia, her roommate, alternates rivalry and quarrelling with friendship; she calls Marie “la giganta dorada” when Marie rows with exceptional power [D:33] [D:37] [D:57].\n\nIn an ocean dream of uncertain bodily reality, Marie identifies herself as both Demeter and Persephone, annually returning from Hell. She argues with Finnegan about immortality, boasts of blood on her hands, and encloses him in her hair in an ambiguously presented seduction [D:91-92]. Finnegan later recognizes her as Don Lewis's murderer; she revels in the killing, and the narration attributes her ability to do it to her extraordinary strength [D:112-113].","citations":["D:32","D:33","D:37","D:57","D:91-92","D:112-113"],"tags":["Giants & Titans","Ships","Finnegan","Women Characters","Crime","Dreams"]},{"name":"Goldfarb's Alley","definition":"Suppertime family television comedy playing at Randal's during Hilary Hilton's gathering for Duffey and his old friends. Duffey superimposes an animated version of Adam Scanlon's painting: four black giants carry Casey as a screaming head-only person, explicitly not a severed head [MTM B12:380-381]. Viewers initially mistake the violence for the show's usual comedy, then react with terror or rush into the streets to rescue their cult hero. Even the comedy's pictured characters flee the screen [MTM B12:381].","citations":["MTM B12:380-381","MTM B12:381"],"tags":["Duffey","Theater","Clubs & Societies","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Golgi bodies","definition":"Cellular organelles associated with processing and sorting proteins and lipids. A passage attributed to the Eighteenth Book of Strange Encounters includes secretion of Golgi bodies around the lipid vesicule among the alleged tests proving Duffey more than four thousand years old, although his body's material is said to be only fifty-five years old [MTM B8:250]. The real biological term is incorporated into the fictional report's extravagant catalogue of age measurements.","citations":["MTM B8:250"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Gompertz function analogies","definition":"One of the alleged age measurements in the Eighteenth Book of Strange Encounters report that Duffey is more than four thousand years old. It appears beside Howship's lacunar frequency and numerous other technical-sounding measurements [MTM B8:250]. The phrase borrows Gompertz's name from mathematical work on human mortality, but the fictional report gives no procedure or explanation of its supposed analogies.","citations":["MTM B8:250"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory","Death & Mortality","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Gonof, Aga","definition":"Father of Orestes Gonof. Charlotte says he owns boats carrying liquor from Canada to Chicago, Detroit, and Cleveland, and that one of her husbands dealt with him. He has deposited the stash she seeks; she claims five percent belongs to her and proposes taking the rest as interest. Duffey says it did not rightfully belong to either her or the depositor, who is about to leave confinement [MTM B3:83-84]. Duffey mentally reconstructs the safety-deposit key, and Charlotte goes to have it made [MTM B3:84].","citations":["MTM B3:83-84","MTM B3:84"],"tags":["Duffey","Family","Crime","Ships","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Gonof, Orestes (Captain Gonof)","definition":"Captain of the <em>Brunhilde</em> and a member of Seaworthy's inner circle, though he can seem an almost forgotten figure [D:30] [D:100]. He takes part in the pursuit of Don Lewis in Tangier, but Don thinks he is not following seriously [D:108]. His reaction on approaching a Greek island is said to reveal his origin [D:115]. After both the true and false Papadiabolous have died, he joins Seaworthy, Gerecke, and Wirt in the ruling four. X calls him exceptionally shrewd and evil; Finnegan says Gonof means thief and is not Greek [D:155]. At the later meeting watched by Finnegan, he is the largest and most menacing of the four senior gargoyles [D:163].","citations":["D:30","D:100","D:108","D:115","D:155","D:163"],"tags":["Ships","Crew & Sailors","Greece","Africa","Devils (Characters)","Crime"]},{"name":"Good Guy's, The","definition":"New Orleans jazz club visited by Duffey, Bagby, Mary Louise, and Salvation Sally. Duffey ranks its music alongside the Rounders' Club's. Its band plays Monumental Jazz, prompting his reflections on classical jazz's aging and diminished inventiveness. Bagby takes a horn and plays the Gadarene Swine Song, and the band joins in [MTM B7:220].","citations":["MTM B7:220"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Music","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Gordon, Harold (Shrimp Boat)","definition":"Harold “Shrimp Boat” Gordon, a regular at the Old Wooden Ship in Galveston [D:215] [MTM B13:458]. He, Tom, and Barney are commercial fishermen who seldom fish: after assessing sea, sky, and weather, they often decide to stay at the Ship and drink [D:220].","citations":["D:215","MTM B13:458","D:220"],"tags":["Ships","Fishing","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Occupations"]},{"name":"Gorgios","definition":"The Sergeant's term for non-Romanies or outsiders, humorously defined as everyone outside the Dirty Five. He says the group taught the Jojos Polish, which the Jojos thought was English and the Gorgios mistook for a Malayan dialect [A:73].","citations":["A:73"],"tags":["Language & Words","Soldiers","Eastern Europe"]},{"name":"Gospel","definition":"The Christian good news. Henry Salvatore says that the poor need the Gospel preached to them and condemns mouths unable to name Holy Poverty [A:125]. Margaret Stone's urgent street preaching concerns the Resurrection and Real Presence, including people on Dauphin Street at midnight [A:144]. Duffey examines a palimpsest containing a first-century copy, or possible original, of the Fourth Gospel, with notes in pagona shorthand; the child-hero then calls it the original [MTM B11:327]. The Fourth Gospel is John, conventionally dated in its final form to about AD 90-100. Tony Apostolo imagines an angel bringing a Fifth Gospel as a hypothetical news story he would already have covered, not an actual new gospel or angelic visitation [PG:525].","citations":["A:125","A:144","MTM B11:327","PG:525"],"tags":["New Testament","Angels & Demons","Journalism","Literature"]},{"name":"Gothic","definition":"A word used across architectural, typographical, literary, and historical imagery. <em>The Crock</em>'s title is printed in black Gothic [A:41]. Gautier calls Balzac's <em>Les Contes Drolatiques</em> Gothic when defending his readings to the goats [A:198]. Finnegan climbs a Gothic hotel and appears as its living gargoyle; Gertrude Brannagan's ample, curly, parapetted appearance becomes Living Gothic [D:162] [D:200]. Countess Margaret mentions, then rejects, the theory that her people are a fragment of a migrating Gothic nation [MTM B8:255]. Duffey's ordeal unfolds under a Gothic sky that he recognizes as a Finnegan sky [MTM B12:393]. An unlabelled speaker in “Promontory Goats” remembers Justinian's skull amid an empire disappearing into Gothic fog [PG:545]. Historically, Gothic names a medieval architectural style prominent from the twelfth through sixteenth centuries and a later literary tradition of terror and the uncanny flourishing in the late eighteenth and nineteenth centuries.","citations":["A:41","A:198","D:162","D:200","MTM B8:255","MTM B12:393","PG:545"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature","Visual Arts","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Goths","definition":"Germanic people associated with the invasions of Roman territories in late antiquity. In X's account of the Devil's Crimean imprisonment, the Goths supposedly call the peninsula Cherson [MTM B6:204]. Countess Margaret also mentions and rejects the idea that her people broke off from a migrating Gothic nation [MTM B8:255].","citations":["MTM B6:204","MTM B8:255"],"tags":["History & Memory","Eastern Europe"]},{"name":"Gourd","definition":"Container from which the Sergeant and a Malayan boy drink jungle juice during his noon-dream of leaving the psychiatric ward and revisiting old companions [A:88] [A:90].","citations":["A:88","A:90"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","Soldiers","Alcohol","Dreams"]},{"name":"Governess","definition":"Katie Noonan's occupation in a landed Polish household. X says that Irish governesses commonly taught English to such families and were valued for lower wages, Catholic instruction, and manners. Katie worked in Gregory's home, left some months before the birth of Ifreann Columkill, and returned several weeks afterward [D:279].","citations":["D:279"],"tags":["Ireland","Eastern Europe","Family","Occupations"]},{"name":"Governor","definition":"In Sulem’s explanation, the liver is the governor of the body and one proposed location of the tujuan, the direction assigned to a life; he distinguishes this from the semangat or soul [A:60]. The governor of San Simeon’s northern province receives Mad King Gaetano VI’s prohibition of bell-ringing and sends forces against the newly sounding Sea-Bell [MB:560].","citations":["A:60","MB:560"],"tags":["Social Class","Metaphysics","Music"]},{"name":"Goyim","definition":"Term for Gentiles or non-Jews. Stein welcomes the appearance of noble noses among the goyim [A:127]. Thadal, one of the Three Slant-Faced Men identified as crooked kings, is called King of the Nations or of the Goyim [MTM B7:227].","citations":["A:127","MTM B7:227"],"tags":["Language & Words","Old Testament","Identity"]},{"name":"Grace","definition":"Divine favor or a state opposed to damnation, with other uses as charm and delight. Finnegan’s return to drink at Freetown on January 26 is a fall from grace, accompanied by his joke about falling from a woman named Grace [D:93]. Casey says his own fall from grace preceded his doubt [A:153]. Grace and pleasure belong to Finnegan and Theresa’s other life or dream [A:204]; Elena later asks whether he is dangerously fallen from grace [A:274]. Niccolo distinguishes killing a victim in a possible state of grace from killing to secure damnation [A:280].\n\nDuffey says he has walked in grace three days out of four throughout his life [MTM B12:390]. Margaret Stone invokes grace for the unwilling souls of the masked hecklers [MTM B7:232]; later, her promise that none of her acquaintances will be lost raises the disputed question of the Devil’s salvation [MTM B13:447]. In separate uses, Lily Koch calls part of New Orleans’s architectural mixture Place de Grace French [MTM B7:215], and Grace Plunkett is named with Jerome among Duffey’s university friends [MTM B3:99].","citations":["D:93","A:153","A:204","A:274","A:280","MTM B12:390","MTM B7:232","MTM B13:447","MTM B7:215","MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Theology","Sin & Redemption","The Devil","Finnegan","Duffey","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Gradual","definition":"Liturgical text heard at Vincent and Theresa’s wedding Mass. Vincent finds its images of a fruitful wife and children like olive plants comical and points them out to Theresa, called Show Boat [A:113].","citations":["A:113"],"tags":["Liturgy","Scripture","Family"]},{"name":"Graduate","definition":"Duffey promises to return next year when Maurine Stranahan graduates; she is then finishing eleventh grade [A:100]. In the narrator’s recurring vision, graduate students watch a composite great mathematician demonstrate changes to the universe [PG:542].","citations":["A:100","PG:542"],"tags":["Prophecy & Visions","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Grail, Holy","definition":"The Sancgreal, an object of medieval Arthurian romance whose literary tradition begins in the late twelfth century. In the <em>Argo</em>’s legendary quest history, the Masters recover the Holy Grail that had been stolen from Glastonbury [MTM B13:422].","citations":["MTM B13:422"],"tags":["Folklore","Ships","Argonautica","Literature"]},{"name":"Grain","definition":"Millet and rice stolen by Amoy in Ignatius Ti’s account of the bird’s earlier life [A:80], and weevily grain that Askandanakandrian claims special skill in rehabilitating [A:257]. Beth interprets Duffey’s lost years as seven sheaves of grain [MTM B2:69]. Poisonous fruit and grain form an image of the dangerously pleasurable harvests of his New Orleans golden age [MTM B7:210]. X recommends taking his information on Infiltrates with a grain of salt [A:137], while Casey’s notebook praises the taller grain of the Great Lakes region in his imaginative comparison with the Mediterranean [A:166].","citations":["A:80","A:257","MTM B2:69","MTM B7:210","A:137","A:166"],"tags":["Duffey","Money & Trade","Dreams","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Grand Banks","definition":"Fishing grounds off Newfoundland, used in the narrator’s description of Casey as fishy as the Grand Banks themselves [PG:544].","citations":["PG:544"],"tags":["Fishing","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Grand Canary","definition":"Gran Canaria, one of the Canary Islands, where the <em>Brunhilde</em> party stays two days [D:96]. Finnegan reads reports of upheavals following the ship’s recent ports of call, and Don Lewis tells him that they have been setting those fires [D:98-99]. At a luxurious dinner Papa Devil completes his resemblance to Count von Baummenbauch by inserting a monocle; the glittering company includes Don Lewis already marked for death [D:99-100]. Archipelago recalls Finnegan drinking brandy there and learning about mermaids from one of them [A:188].","citations":["D:96","D:98-99","D:99-100","A:188"],"tags":["Islands","Finnegan","The Devil","Nymphs & Mermaids","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Grand Concourse","definition":"Approach feeding the newly present Decatur Street in the Royal Pop People’s altered New Orleans. A thousand horse carriages pass through it on their way to the opera without crowding [MTM B11:346].","citations":["MTM B11:346"],"tags":["New Orleans","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Grand Manner","definition":"Earlier glossary label for Marie Courtois’s sumptuous dress on the Mexican shore. The source describes her as dressed in the “high manner,” drawing the crowd’s admiration [D:57].","citations":["D:57"],"tags":["Women Characters","Social Class"]},{"name":"Grandes, Los","definition":"Seaworthy and his associates Wirt and Gerecke, the powerful conspirators aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em> [D:109]. They go ashore with two seamen at a port whose identity is uncertain [D:67]. Anastasia watches Chris McAbney fill his pipe while he waits for them [D:68]. Don Lewis fears that they may arrange his murder through local men; he also lists Captain Gonof separately among possible killers [D:106] [D:109].","citations":["D:109","D:67","D:68","D:106"],"tags":["Ships","Crew & Sailors","Crime"]},{"name":"Grandfather","definition":"Ancestry is literal, invented and unstable. Sulem makes the small protective amulet called the Grandfather of Finnegan [A:59-61]. Elena Angelino says her great-grandfather was a bear, a claim supported by the Professor but doubted by Hans [A:218]. X considers a manifestation of the Devil his own grandfather and the great-grandfather of Finnegan and Anastasia [D:279]; he also describes family names passing between generations, even from grandson to grandfather [D:278]. In Cuba, he speaks in the manner of his German maternal grandfather [MTM B12:372].\n\nPhilip Stranahan mistakes the older and younger appearances of Duffey for grandfather and grandson; Duffey says they are one [MTM B13:473]. San Simeon’s dynastic cycle requires the prince’s grandfather, the previous Mad King Emeritus, to die when the generations advance, but Gaetano’s failure to return delays the succession [MB:559]. Finnegan drunkenly styles himself grandfather of all the saltshakers [D:189].","citations":["A:59-61","A:218","D:279","D:278","MTM B12:372","MTM B13:473","MB:559","D:189"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Finnegan","Duffey","Family","Identity","The Devil"]},{"name":"Grandfather of Finnegan","definition":"Very small protective amulet carved by Sulem, who believes Finnegan lacks the direction or tujuan needed to guide his life and hopes to trick the ruin awaiting him. It resembles a lizard and a toad, with steady eyes and loathsome integrity. Finnegan baptizes it Heliogabalus and wears it on a chain with his Miraculous Medal. The narrator says it stays with him to the end and is then mistaken for an object of another cult [A:59-61].","citations":["A:59-61"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Finnegan","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Grandson","definition":"In X’s account of the Gregorovitch family’s confusing names, a father may even be named for his grandson [D:278].","citations":["D:278"],"tags":["Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Granite","definition":"Recurring image for Saxon Seaworthy’s aged, hard face: old granite, temporarily ashen at Papa Devil’s appearance, tired by the campfire, then relaxed and lit like old cliffs [D:11] [D:15] [D:22] [D:43] [D:48]. Finnegan later calls him a granite-faced fox [D:158] [D:165]. The old teras in Finnegan’s dream also has a gloomy granite face [D:140]. In Trinali’s poem, granite mountains are remnants of a statue’s fingers from an older world [A:227].","citations":["D:11","D:15","D:22","D:43","D:48","D:158","D:165","D:140","A:227"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Dreams","Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Grape Cuttings","definition":"In Ignacio’s account of Joseph of Maintz’s journey to the moon, grape cuttings accompany wheat, bread, wine, books and an altar stone so that the priest can continue celebrating Mass [A:271].","citations":["A:271"],"tags":["Folklore","Liturgy","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Grapes","definition":"Fruit grown on Naxos [D:125]. Trinali’s wine-making poem specifies a quart of water for every quart of grapes [A:231]. Duffey wishes Charlotte Garfield sons like clusters of green grapes around an old arbor [MTM B8:260].","citations":["D:125","A:231","MTM B8:260"],"tags":["Greece","Alcohol","Literature","Family"]},{"name":"Grapevines","definition":"Among the authentic possessions of Melchisedech’s ancient life as the Boy King, set against the less substantial childhoods of his modern life [MTM B1:17].","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Duffey","Identity","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Grass","definition":"Gautier cuts wild grass for his goats’ winter fodder; at their swamp camp Finnegan catches fish among grass roots standing in water [A:194-195]. The Dirty Five and Sammy sleep in high grass after riding the white horses [A:68-69], and Papapaleologus grazes below Finnegan and Anastasia on Naxos [D:132]. The word also appears in the <em>Crock</em> article title “Moles at the Grass Roots” [A:41] and in the comparison of Finnegan’s attack on the Pirate Hotel to a tiger entering a grass-hut village [D:231].\n\nGrass greens Duffey’s remembered ancient country [MTM B1:6]; reed grass feeds the great beasts at Zabotski Flats [MTM B12:362]. The <em>Argo</em> appears to sail through a sea of grass along a weed-grown Louisiana canal [MTM B13:416]. Blood-of-Martyrs Grass reappears on the rising Mooring-Stone after its supposed extinction [MB:595].","citations":["A:194-195","A:68-69","D:132","A:41","D:231","MTM B1:6","MTM B12:362","MTM B13:416","MB:595"],"tags":["Fishing","Finnegan","Duffey","Ships","Ekklesia"]},{"name":"Grass Roots (Moles at the)","definition":"“Moles at the Grass Roots,” an article in the <em>Crock</em> described as written in Duffey’s style [A:41].","citations":["A:41"],"tags":["Duffey","Journalism"]},{"name":"Grasshoppers","definition":"An ancient grasshopper and its virus illustrate the Royal Pop History speaker’s claim that recorded stone patinas can reconstruct the past in minute detail. The speaker says the virus can be analyzed and prescribed for, though medication cannot yet be sent back to the insect [MTM B11:325].","citations":["MTM B11:325"],"tags":["History & Memory","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Gratz, Efram","definition":"Efram Gate Gratz, one of the Princes of the Crowd at the Last Conclave. He voices their total animosity toward the Ekklesia, saying they have come to bury Peter and intend to kill him, desecrate his grave and prevent a recurrence of the institution [MB:561].","citations":["MB:561"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia","Social Class","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Grave","definition":"Burial places recur as sites of confused identity and return. The Promantia distinguishes an unnamed man buried twice in two graves from another who died twice [D:6]. Finnegan offers to deepen Seaworthy’s grave and helps bury Papa Devil atop an older body [D:75-76] [D:153]. The headstone later reads I.N.C.G.P. Bogovitch [D:277]; the grave holds Ifreann Noonan Columkill and Gregory McIfreann [D:284]. X explains that Seaworthy killed Papadiabolous I while reopening his father’s grave for valuable information [D:288]. He calls the sought document a key, plans to recover it [D:290], and Doll drops her Diabolique verses through the split that has persisted since the second burial [D:293].\n\nFinnegan plans to paint Anastasia among the dead in a common grave [D:61], and later buries Don Lewis in a shallow sandy grave [D:111]. Gregory describes possession as hypnotism from beyond the grave [A:84]. A grave without a stone appears among the closing obliterative verses of the Great Day [GD:501].","citations":["D:6","D:75-76","D:153","D:277","D:284","D:288","D:290","D:293","D:61","D:111","A:84","GD:501"],"tags":["The Devil","Finnegan","The Dead","Visual Arts","Literature","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Grave Register","definition":"Burial register that X believes incomplete for earlier years while searching for the Devil’s grave. Despite inspecting more than two hundred graves, he needs Finnegan to lead him to the neglected marker [D:276].","citations":["D:276"],"tags":["The Devil","History & Memory","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Graveyard","definition":"The burial ground across the boulevard from the bar where Finnegan sits with Seaworthy [D:10]. Finnegan recalls meeting him there at night during Papa Devil’s burial [D:75-76]. He later returns to the club and crosses to meet Doll and X in the graveyard, where he identifies the Devil’s grave [D:275-277]. X’s remark that doubles do not grow even on graveyard yews belongs to his earlier explanation of the apparent resurrection [D:154].\n\nTrinali calls her driftwood beach the Elephants’ Graveyard [A:232] and makes it the setting of To Trinali in the Old Wood Graveyard on the Dead Sea [A:235]. At the Great Day newspaper office, a staff member suggests that their undated publication might be a graveyard-shift paper [GD:487].","citations":["D:10","D:75-76","D:275-277","D:154","A:232","A:235","GD:487"],"tags":["Finnegan","The Devil","Death & Mortality","Literature","Journalism"]},{"name":"Gravity","definition":"Figurative attraction and weight: Henri Salvatore is the center of gravity of any room he enters [MTM B5:161]; Duffey’s immense but light-moving creations seem to have come from other gravities and worlds [MTM B5:156-157]; and a Texas town draws the wandering Finnegan into its vortex [A:201].","citations":["MTM B5:161","MTM B5:156-157","A:201"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Gray","definition":"A recurring color: gray in Brannagan’s sandy hair [D:199], Doll’s eyes [D:173], Mary Virginia’s hair [MTM B11:318], the Naxos raider’s derby [D:147], and the St. Louis squirrels of Duffey’s childhood [MTM B1:17]. X’s nested containers include a gray box [A:136].\n\nBagby’s mental St. Louis consists of black, white and gray sketches [MTM B2:62]; Count Finnegan’s Ghost Bar painting uses ghost grays [MTM B10:292]; and the terrible sky above the fallen Duffey is gray-orange [MTM B12:393]. The <em>Argo</em>’s moral landscape distinguishes good gray from bad gray [MTM B13:437]. Hans’s meditation on Marie’s green eyes rejects the claim that vair denotes gray [A:24].","citations":["D:199","D:173","MTM B11:318","D:147","MTM B1:17","A:136","MTM B2:62","MTM B10:292","MTM B12:393","MTM B13:437","A:24"],"tags":["Women Characters","Visual Arts","Metaphysics","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Gray Box","definition":"Container inside the black box X brings to Duffey; inside it is a brown box. The nested boxes have been stolen from Casey and belong to the <em>Crock</em> [A:136].","citations":["A:136"],"tags":["Journalism","Crime"]},{"name":"Great Day Contingency, The","definition":"The first of seven possible futures singled out in Book X from an indefinite number of contingencies. It abolishes boundaries and thereby persons. Duffey first imagined it as a joke, but the world’s misplaced faith makes his invention a trap for its inventor. Its theme is the clock stopped at “twelf” and the snake swallowing itself [MTM B10:298-299]. The seven alternatives are not all mutually exclusive. The Great Day is distinct from the fifth, the Thunder Colt Complex or Decatur Street Opera House Presentation [MTM B10:300-301].<br><br>The appended story “Great Day in the Morning” develops the same premise. Melchisedech, spelled Duffy in that story, finds his bitterly comic article being fulfilled as an emancipation from walls, clothes, skins, minds, and personal distinction. He calls for laughter to undo the disaster, but Mary Virginia and Salvation Sally reject it as incompatible with the new condition [GD:492-495]. The inhabitants merge into fleshy spheres while the sun remains suspended in its dawning [GD:496] [GD:498-499]. Duffy and Morpheus finally contract into a single-celled creature swallowing itself. Morpheus calls this a repeated beginning; the narrator points to an inaccessible hint of another outcome in Duffy’s old article and still finds persons and verses left to dispose of [GD:500-501].","citations":["MTM B10:298-299","MTM B10:300-301","GD:492-495","GD:496","GD:498-499","GD:500-501"],"tags":["Duffey","Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","Identity","Apocalyptic","Dreams"]},{"name":"Great Day Rime","definition":"Verses of the Great Day that seem to hang everywhere in the air, both tongueless and soundless. Their nameless day interrupts the ordinary weekly sequence in Duffey’s thoughts as he reaches the Pelican Press [GD:490].","citations":["GD:490"],"tags":["Literature","Apocalyptic","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Great Delta Insurance Company Building, The","definition":"Building whose tower clock is mutilated at the onset of the Great Day. Three young men saw both clock hands down to stubs; one falls and dies while descending. Duffey interprets the handless clock as a foretold sign of the turning-over and stopped time, while resisting the conclusion that the Great Thing has arrived [GD:483].","citations":["GD:483"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Death & Mortality","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Great Devil Himself","definition":"The ultimate Devil, whose relation to the Devil-Released-From-Prison remains disputed. Stein sometimes regards the released devil as a minor decoy permitting the Devil Himself to work unnoticed, but on other days believes they are the same [MTM B10:291]. Margaret Stone says the devil she has met is covered by her promise of salvation but is not the Great Devil Himself, whom she has not known; Duffey counters that prevailing opinion identifies him as the Great Devil [MTM B13:447].","citations":["MTM B10:291","MTM B13:447"],"tags":["The Devil","Theology","Identity"]},{"name":"Great Horse Carriages of the Past","definition":"Catalogue evoked by the Royal Pop History speaker from the memories of a Decatur Street flagstone: Acme Top and Acme Open, Southern Beauty, Fulton Road Cart, Livery Special, Farmers’ Canopy-Top Surrey, Johnson’s Jump-Seat Buggy, Imperial Carriage, Dempster’s Three-Spring Handy Wagon, Drexel’s Eight-Horse Dray, Pontiac’s Special Milk Wagon, Hallock’s Grocery Cart, Sears Famous and Road Runner [MTM B11:329]. The nostalgic display is undercut by the Countess’s admission that the new people are stealing human memories and claiming them as their own [MTM B11:329].","citations":["MTM B11:329"],"tags":["New Orleans","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Great Lakes","definition":"The American Mediterranean in item XXXIX of Casey’s school notebook. He imagines their surrounding regions as more abundant counterparts of Africa, Greece, Spain, Illyria and Italy, with Chicago at the center [A:166].","citations":["A:166"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Literature"]},{"name":"Great Mogul Diamond","definition":"Famous Indian diamond discovered in the seventeenth century. It is among the prizes found in the <em>Argo</em>’s legendary history [MTM B13:422].","citations":["MTM B13:422"],"tags":["Ships","Argonautica","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Great Pot Itself","definition":"The thousand-gallon, glass-lined pot supplied by Zabotski for Duffey’s free soup project. Blessed by a priest from St. Katherine’s, it is to boil forever, receiving meat, fish and vegetables from people or secretly from God. Duffey fears that a lack of true Holy Poverty leaves nobody willing to receive its slumgullion, imperiling the project of rebuilding the world [MTM B6:194-195].","citations":["MTM B6:194-195"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining","Prayer & Devotion"]},{"name":"Great Ship Itself","definition":"The larger <em>Argo</em> of earlier times. One opinion holds that the present, smaller <em>Argo</em> is only its ship’s boat or pinnacle, although the present vessel retains the great ship’s relics and identifications [MTM B13:403].","citations":["MTM B13:403"],"tags":["Ships","Argonautica","Identity"]},{"name":"Great X-Capo, The","definition":"X’s former stage name as a magician and escape artist. He recalls being billed by this name on the circuits while boasting that he can open small locks with mittens on [A:137]. He later brings his old magician’s cloak to the graveyard meeting [D:276].","citations":["A:137","D:276"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Theater","Identity"]},{"name":"Greater Archipelago (The)","definition":"The Land of the Animated Marvels, also called the Archipelago: a scattered recuperative island for the <em>Argo</em> whose people live chiefly in Chicago, St. Louis and New Orleans. They provide provisions, healing and virtues, and some are themselves <em>Argo</em> Masters. Its relation to the ship in time and space remains imperfectly understood [MTM B13:448].","citations":["MTM B13:448"],"tags":["Ships","Islands","Argonautica","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Greater Chicago Phone Directory","definition":"Directory Casey brings uninvited onto the Castle Theatre stage during Goat Boy. Reading seven names from it in an ecstatic voice creates an overwhelming audience response and wins his bet, demonstrating his power to spellbind crowds [PG:543].","citations":["PG:543"],"tags":["Theater","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Greater Neustria","definition":"Stein’s name for the thousand-year domain of Christendom into which he imagines Hitler raiding backward from the future. His Notes on the Finnegan Cycle link this Neustria or Latter Neustria with the Devil’s imprisonment from 946 to 1946 [MTM B6:185]. The name adapts historical Neustria, the western Frankish kingdom of the Merovingian period, to a much broader fictional chronology.","citations":["MTM B6:185"],"tags":["World War II","History & Memory","Fictional Places","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Greatheart, Mona","definition":"Artist among Duffey’s Chicago friends, listed with Cassius Greatheart in the golden-age circle of 1925-1935 and its later electrum phase to about 1946 [MTM B3:98]. She repeatedly models the visiting Monster Giulio in clay [MTM B3:106]. Duffey includes her among the women he loves [MTM B4:111], and compromising photographs later show him playing the funny lover with her [MTM B4:139].","citations":["MTM B3:98","MTM B3:106","MTM B4:111","MTM B4:139"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Greb, Harry","definition":"American boxer (1894-1926). During his fighting binge, Finnegan boasts that he has beaten Harry Greb and therefore can beat the local challengers [D:191].","citations":["D:191"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Finnegan","Combat"]},{"name":"Greece","definition":"Region in which the <em>Brunhilde</em>’s voyage brings Finnegan into the familiar world of his companions. At Zakinthos, Seaworthy and Gonof’s Greek origins emerge; at Naxos, the party reaches Anastasia’s home and the island associated with Ariadne [D:115]. Ancient and modern Greece mingle in Finnegan and Anastasia’s moonlit conversation, including their extravagant story of a bird flying behind the moon [D:132].<br><br>On Naxos, Finnegan confesses to a Greek Capuchin who speaks Italian [D:118-119]. After Anastasia’s death, an Orthodox priest says that he gave her the last rites [D:147-148]. Greece also appears among the countries where Ifreann lived, according to X’s reconstruction of his life [D:280]. See also Greek.","citations":["D:115","D:132","D:118-119","D:147-148","D:280"],"tags":["Greece","Islands","Finnegan","Greek Mythology"]},{"name":"Greece (Ancient)","definition":"Ancient cultural world invoked both playfully and polemically. Finnegan’s Naxos conversation leads to the narrator’s joke that the Greeks invented the moon [D:132], and Cosmopolis is called a Greek idea as the ship enters Greek waters [D:115]. Cyrus Roundhead, in his campaign to erase human history, claims that Greece consisted of Hellas and five concentric rings and that Macedonians invented heroic Greece and an imaginary Athens; John the Greek challenges him from personal memory [MTM B11:337].","citations":["D:132","D:115","MTM B11:337"],"tags":["Greece","History & Memory","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Greek","definition":"Language and cultural inheritance invoked in several distinct settings. Hans’s father demands Greek instruction for the fourth-grade pupils, but the priest replies that he does not know the language [A:209]. Hans himself puzzles over Greek words for the color of Marie’s eyes [A:24].<br><br>Finnegan’s Romaic is inadequate for confession on Naxos. The Greek Capuchin reproaches him for knowing too little Greek and argues that Italians should have retained it as their language [D:118-119]. Finnegan later has trouble following the Orthodox priest’s remarks after Anastasia’s death [D:147-148]. X explains Anastasia’s use of <em>adelphos</em> through what he calls its Old Greek sense of cousin as well as brother [D:279].<br><br>Other uses include the Greek Cross among the devices on Henry’s dream ship [A:44], the Greek name of a successor to Casey’s <em>Crock</em> [A:168], Teresa’s remarks about Greek performers at the Star and Garter [MTM B6:188], the qualified suggestion of Greek ancestry in Margaret Stone’s portrait [MTM B11:310], and Morpheus among the Greek gods in “Great Day in the Morning” [GD:500]. See also Greece.","citations":["A:209","A:24","D:118-119","D:147-148","D:279","A:44","A:168","MTM B6:188","MTM B11:310","GD:500"],"tags":["Greece","Language & Words","Greek Mythology"]},{"name":"Greek Anthology","definition":"Collection of Greek epigrams transmitted through successive compilations, notably the Palatine manuscript of the tenth century and Maximus Planudes’s related anthology around 1300. Endymeon Ellenbogen boasts that he has translated the <em>Greek Anthology</em> among his other works [A:237].","citations":["A:237"],"tags":["Greece","Literature"]},{"name":"Greek Cross","definition":"One of the symbolic devices on the <em>Navicula Petri</em> in Henry’s dream, alongside the Lamb, Fish and Six-Pointed Star [A:44].","citations":["A:44"],"tags":["Ships","Catholic Church","Dreams"]},{"name":"Greek Island","definition":"Naxos, where Finnegan and Anastasia eat outdoors at Anastasia Grandmother’s mountain farm, discussing the island’s blue haze and the changing colors of skies [D:125] [D:127].","citations":["D:125","D:127"],"tags":["Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Women Characters","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Greek, John the","definition":"Owner of John the Greek’s Famous French Restaurant. At the Royal Pop History sessions in New Orleans, he challenges Cyrus Roundhead’s denial of Athens by saying that he lived there until he was thirteen [MTM B11:336-337]. His remembered life directly resists the attempt to abolish human history.","citations":["MTM B11:336-337"],"tags":["Greece","New Orleans","History & Memory","Identity","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Greely Pack Saddles","definition":"Pack saddles named by one drinker as the explanation for the padded leather straps in Duffey’s old horse barn. He says the former livery proprietor rented these superior saddles with horses and mules for excursions into the hills and woods. Another drinker gives a different explanation for the straps [MTM B2:45].","citations":["MTM B2:45"],"tags":["Duffey","Occupations","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Green","definition":"Color used for eyes, complexions, landscapes, money, and changing life. Hans’s meditation on Marie’s green eyes connects Greek color terms, Romany, French, sacred images, and witches [A:24]. Anastasia has a sea-green touch in her complexion [D:20], and Finnegan also teases her about turning green with seasickness [D:28]. Sebastian Hilton’s complexion is pale greenish [MTM B1:22].<br><br>Finnegan describes islands as green and fiber-colored in his account of their creation [A:35]. Trinali speaks of the Green Castles and the Green Death in her stories of driftwood [A:233], and of the Green Groves of Gomorrah in a poem [A:235].<br><br>Green also belongs to Seaworthy’s money [D:18], Hillary Hilton’s stock of paint [A:250], the Green Mule drink [MTM B4:132], and the stain of money in a young admirer’s ideal of the rich beach bum [MTM B5:173]. Widow Waldo describes the children’s green-clay animals [MTM B9:275]. A Royal Pop Historian uses “green dust of life” in an account of making history [MTM B11:324]. The green bay tree appears on the Pope’s shield in the closing verses of “How Many Miles to Babylon” [MB:598]. See also Green (Private).","citations":["A:24","D:20","D:28","MTM B1:22","A:35","A:233","A:235","D:18","A:250","MTM B4:132","MTM B5:173","MTM B9:275","MTM B11:324","MB:598"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Language & Words","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Green (Private)","definition":"Talkative soldier in Ward Fourteen. In the Sergeant’s assessment, Green is mistaken on fewer than six points, which nevertheless hold together logically [A:75-76]. He says the contingent universe depends on God’s moment-by-moment attention and identifies himself as God. When the lights dim as promised, Gregory points out that a ward-boy has started the compressor; Green replies that he created both and can use them [A:76-77]. He prompts Gregory’s account of his troubles [A:78] and has left the ward by the later scene [A:88].","citations":["A:75-76","A:76-77","A:78","A:88"],"tags":["Soldiers","Metaphysics","Identity"]},{"name":"Green Bay Tree","definition":"Heraldic tree on Pope Finnegan’s shield in the novel’s verse attributed to Nostradamus [MB:598]. The wording recalls Psalm37:35 in the King James Bible, where a flourishing green bay tree pictures the wicked in power; that possible scriptural echo sits uneasily beside the poem’s joyful welcome of Finnegan.","citations":["MB:598"],"tags":["Popes","Old Testament","Prophecy & Visions","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Green Castles","definition":"Underwater places beneath the Continental Shelf visited by Trinali’s stogy log, which she nevertheless judges uninspired. This is the first log she describes, before the more promising convoluted piece [A:232-233].","citations":["A:232-233"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Fictional Places","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Green Death","definition":"The death into which Trinali says a convoluted piece of driftwood descended before returning as a talkative, reminiscent ghost. In her fanciful account, the log has learned from its sea encounters and will become a leering lamp under her tools [A:233].","citations":["A:233"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Death & Mortality","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Green Eye Shade","definition":"John Rattigan’s trademark at the boys’ boarding-school sales. He wears it even though their candlelit or kerosene-lit room is too dim to require one, and removes it last when undressing for bed [MTM B1:26-27]. Lily Koch instead uses snap-on celluloid cuffs or gauntlets as her merchant’s trademark [MTM B1:29].","citations":["MTM B1:26-27","MTM B1:29"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Occupations"]},{"name":"Green Islands","definition":"Title of Chapter Two of <em>Archipelago</em>. Finnegan recounts a creation legend in which a woven mat falls into the sea, turtles tear it into pieces, and these root themselves as green and fiber-colored islands, with Guinea at the stitched center [A:35]. Later, a Malayan boy tells the Sergeant that he has been in the Green Islands long enough and must go home; this visit proves to be part of the Sergeant’s noonday dream in the ward [A:88-90].","citations":["A:35","A:88-90"],"tags":["Islands","Other Mythologies","Soldiers","Dreams"]},{"name":"Green Ladies","definition":"Drinks Margaret Stone says everyone in the New Orleans Quarter has been drinking all week: like peppermint schnapps, except that absinthe replaces the schnapps. Mary Virginia asks about them while questioning Margaret’s account of the city’s extraordinary overnight changes [MTM B11:309].","citations":["MTM B11:309"],"tags":["Alcohol","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Green Mule, The","definition":"Rollo McSorley’s name for the potent new drink Shirley Israel serves at Duffey’s party [MTM B4:129-130]. In his facetious Daily News account, titled “I was kicked by a flying mule,” he blames the Green Mule for his condition and admits losing his bet and failing to beat the man he challenged. The narrative instead shows Sebastian Hilton knocking down Rollo and Morris Poor while Duffey moves the actual mule through the air [MTM B4:131-132].","citations":["MTM B4:129-130","MTM B4:131-132"],"tags":["Alcohol","Journalism","Duffey"]},{"name":"Green Tree","definition":"Bar visited by Hans Schultz, Vincent, Finnegan, Tom Shire, Freddy Castle and the newly arrived Casey while Hans prepares for his beer-drinking contest with Joe Bushmaster [A:17-18].","citations":["A:17-18"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Finnegan","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Green-clay animals","definition":"Animals the children of the Golden Childrens Home and Haven Orphanage prepare for Zabotski’s vessel. Early examples fall apart after running about [MTM B9:275]. The children carry later ones aboard while they kick and move weakly [MTM B9:284]. Duffey suggests baking them to hold their heads and legs together, but the children say that would kill them: they come from green-clay eggs and can still be breathed into. Before the voyage, their hooting and bellowing become a great rhapsody of living animal sounds [MTM B9:285].","citations":["MTM B9:275","MTM B9:284","MTM B9:285"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Creation","Duffey","Ships"]},{"name":"Greenberry Wine","definition":"One of the drinks Aunt Gertrude has in her lavishly hospitable household, alongside huckleberry wine and perry, during Finnegan’s stay with the Brannagans [D:208].","citations":["D:208"],"tags":["Alcohol","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Greenery","definition":"Vegetation ranging from everyday shade to transformed landscapes. It cuts the glare outside the bar Finnegan and his friends call Sloppy Joe’s [D:235]; appears in bales beside Duffey’s premises as vanished buildings give way to something newly forming [MTM B11:313-314]; and grows tall and lush on Zabotski Flats [MTM B12:362]. Hans’s unwritten aerie poem has golden-touched greenery [A:210], while the speaker of Duffey’s <em>The Alien Awakens</em> seeks greenery beyond the smouldering plain as an escape from horror [D:196-197].","citations":["D:235","MTM B11:313-314","MTM B12:362","A:210","D:196-197"],"tags":["Literature","New Orleans","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Greenflag, Oliver","definition":"One of the three hospitable men who receive Duffey and Biloxi Brannagan in the Louisiana water country, with Leonard Archive and Harry A. “Honeybucket” Kincaid [MTM B13:417]. Honeybucket operates their frequency apparatus, and Leonard explains its ability to gather organisms and materials from water [MTM B13:417-418]. Greenflag acknowledges that Puff Fish present a moral problem and individual vileness, but doubts that access can be prevented once frequency modulation and braiding are understood. He compares the signals to dippers that can draw any desired thing from water, land or sky [MTM B13:418-419].","citations":["MTM B13:417","MTM B13:417-418","MTM B13:418-419"],"tags":["Fishing","Louisiana","Duffey","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Gregorian chant","definition":"The reported reference to Gregorian chant in Don Barnaby’s garden vision has not been located. The cited passage describes changing identities, sleep, travel and the effects of Madame Le Mourcheor’s pill, without the alleged list of musical and theological figures [D:250-252].","citations":["D:250-252"],"tags":["Music","Liturgy"]},{"name":"Gregorio (Nicholas Cardinal)","definition":"Cardinal of Messina, named among the thirteen men targeted by John Mogul’s assassination contracts. His double is Arnoldo Rugutini of New York City [MB:571] [MB:578-579].","citations":["MB:571","MB:578-579"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia","Identity","Crime"]},{"name":"Gregorovitch","definition":"Patronym given to Katie Noonan’s son Columkill from Gregory, the man who adopts him. X gives the son’s fuller name as Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch Papadiabolous Chortovitch, dates his birth near Krakow to 1830, and argues that his natural father was the Devil [D:278-279].","citations":["D:278-279"],"tags":["The Devil","Family","Identity","Eastern Europe"]},{"name":"Gregory (adopter of Columkill)","definition":"Man in the landed Polish household where Katie Noonan serves as governess. He adopts her son Columkill, who receives the patronym Gregorovitch. X calls Gregory kind and acknowledges, but rejects, the possibility that he was also the boy’s natural father [D:279].","citations":["D:279"],"tags":["Eastern Europe","Minor Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Gregory (Private)","definition":"Large, handsome soldier in Ward Fourteen, with a purplish, pumpkin-shaped skull. He says that he never ages and must periodically change identity [A:77] [A:84]. His account stretches through centuries at sea and earlier irregular service to more than a hundred continuous years as a U.S. Army sergeant. Lost papers during the Indian War and Civil War let him reduce his recorded age [A:77].\n\nIn 1907, he says, he went AWOL, presented himself as twenty, cultivated political patrons and obtained a Military Academy appointment. He became an Army History specialist, archivist and teacher, remaining an officer for nearly forty years. When an army doctor proposed studying his failure to age, he escaped again [A:78]. As a young Australian private, he was detained after his fingerprints matched those of the vanished American colonel. His unpublished study of subversion within military intelligence also angered the people it exposed [A:79].\n\nHe describes possession as the hypnotic action of immortal wills and says influential men may be shells inhabited by devils [A:84-85]. Claiming personal recollection of Roman times, he explains how enemies of early Christianity made the resurrected Christ a frightening figure for children; these are anti-Christian stories about Christ, rather than stories about the Antichrist [A:85]. He remains in the ward after the Sergeant leaves, fearing confinement forever [A:90]. Finnegan later identifies him with Papadiabolis the Devil, while Dotty’s unmailed letter calls him one of the Papadiabouloi without specifying which [A:278] [MTM B6:188-189].","citations":["A:77","A:84","A:78","A:79","A:84-85","A:85","A:90","A:278","MTM B6:188-189"],"tags":["Soldiers","Immortality","Australia","Ghosts & Spirits","The Devil","Identity","History & Memory","New Testament"]},{"name":"Gregory Hall","definition":"Residential hall at St. Bonaventure’s whose boys dominate the school paper, <em>The Towers</em>. Its high towers appear on the masthead; the rival manuscript <em>Chambers</em> answers them with drawings of chamber pots [A:167].","citations":["A:167"],"tags":["Journalism","Literature"]},{"name":"Gregory McColum Papadiabolous II","definition":"See Papadiabolous (II) (Gregory McColum Papadiabolous, Papadiabolous II, Papa Devil, Papa D, Noonan). X gives Gregory McColum as the Irish brother’s name [D:285].","citations":["D:285"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Identity","Ireland"]},{"name":"Gregory McIfreann Papadiabolous I","definition":"See Papadiabolous (I) (Gregory McIfreann Papadiabolous, Papadiabolous I). X gives Gregory McIfreann as the Asiatic brother’s name [D:285].","citations":["D:285"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Identity","The Devil"]},{"name":"Gregory van Ghi","definition":"See Van Ghi. Gregory van Ghi is the artist’s name Finnegan has printed on his cards in New York [A:191].","citations":["A:191"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Gregory, John","definition":"Name either Papadiabolous brother may use. X explains that Gregory McIfreann and Gregory McColum both adopt forms of John as a working first name, including Ivan, Sean, Jan, Ian, Jean, Giovanni, Juan, Johannes and Giannes. This practice complicates his effort to disentangle their identities [D:285].","citations":["D:285"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Identity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Gremium Matris","definition":"Latin for the mother’s lap or bosom. In an ocean dream whose bodily reality Finnegan cannot determine, Marie Courtois identifies herself with Demeter and Persephone [D:91]. She lays Finnegan’s head in her lap, binds him there with her hair, and equates the gremium matris with both her lap and the Earth. Her discourse connects this grouping with the Pietà; the maternal image carries a threatening element through the binding and her pleasure in bloodshed [D:92].","citations":["D:91","D:92"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Greek Mythology","Dreams","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Grenade man","definition":"Unnamed older enemy waiting on the shore above the fissured cave to complete an earlier attempt to murder the reawakened Count Finnegan. He has known Finnegan for more than thirty years; two riflemen offshore and an old enemy shark also wait for the Count [MB:554-555]. The man throws three grenades, then a depth charge, and slips into the water after it. The riflemen shoot at a man, or men, flung up by the explosion, while the narrative leaves open Finnegan’s possible escape beneath the shark and launch. The scene is on the Mariano Coast near Havana [MB:556-557].","citations":["MB:554-555","MB:556-557"],"tags":["Finnegan","Combat","Weapons","Resurrection","Ships"]},{"name":"Grenadines","definition":"Caribbean island group. In Angela’s account of her father’s forged shipwreck-treasure maps, the Grenadines are one of seven false or shoal passages through the Antilles where he imagines wrecks would accumulate [D:260].","citations":["D:260"],"tags":["Islands","Caribbean","Shipwrecks"]},{"name":"Grenouilles, soupe aux","definition":"Frog soup, eaten in Duffey’s vividly appropriated memory of Colmar in Alsace just before his thirteenth birthday. He orders in polished French at the Rotisserie Schillinger and also eats Tournedos au Poivre Vert. The scene was stolen from Sebastian Hilton’s mind, but its detailed flavors become part of Duffey’s own memory [MTM B1:35].","citations":["MTM B1:35"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining","History & Memory","Identity","France"]},{"name":"Gretchen Sisler","definition":"See Sisler, Gretchen. She introduces herself to Duffey by this name when he gives her a gold coin [MTM B2:39].","citations":["MTM B2:39"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Grimaldi","definition":"One of the early human types invoked in the novel’s accounts of the “other people.” Harrington’s review reports the suggestion that the supposed Neanderthal returnees might instead be Grimaldi [D:242]. Overlark’s epigraph includes Grimaldi among the types said to possess the enlarged back-brain [D:272].","citations":["D:242","D:272"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Literature"]},{"name":"Grimm's Law","definition":"The Germanic consonant shift formulated by Jacob Grimm in 1822, describing systematic changes from Proto-Indo-European stops to their Proto-Germanic counterparts. In item XIII of his school notebook, Casey applies the name to a compressed list of correspondences: Latin K or hard C with German or English H, F with B, P with F, and Greek G with Russian ZH or German or English K [A:163]. These are Casey’s notebook formulations; the historical law describes changes from the languages’ common ancestor.","citations":["A:163"],"tags":["Language & Words","Germany"]},{"name":"Grindstones","definition":"Household tools bearing such iron-lettered names as Schofields and Auto Ball Bearing. Their names belong to the store of noticed objects and words the young Duffey appropriates, often from other people’s minds [MTM B1:17].","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Occupations","History & Memory","Duffey","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Grip Machine","definition":"Strength-testing device at the Fun House out on the Lake. In her Third Epistle to the Kids in St. Louis, Margaret Stone says she can grip only thirty-seven pounds, compared with Dotty Yekouris’s hundred and ninety. She contrasts her physically weak hands with the extraordinary gifts and an hour’s daily care of the world entrusted to them [MTM B5:178].","citations":["MTM B5:178"],"tags":["Women Characters","Miracles"]},{"name":"Grisly (Most)","definition":"Howell’s description of the dead man as the familiar become horrible. A corpse returning to life is, for him, the supreme ghost-story terror, requiring humor to make it bearable, as in Irish wake stories [A:85].","citations":["A:85"],"tags":["The Dead","Death & Mortality","Ghosts & Spirits","Folklore"]},{"name":"Groben, Hierome","definition":"Artist in Duffey’s Chicago circle, named among the golden-age friends [MTM B3:98]. He makes Doré-like engravings of the visiting Monster Giulio [MTM B3:106] and supplies four woodcuts to <em>The Crock</em> [A:41]. He is present when Morris Poor challenges Duffey’s powers [MTM B4:128], and later appears as Groben of Chicago among the artists associated with Duffey’s rebuilding of the world [MTM B6:198].","citations":["MTM B3:98","MTM B3:106","A:41","MTM B4:128","MTM B6:198"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Visual Arts","Journalism"]},{"name":"Groll's Trolls","definition":"Name for people whom Eva regards as the inferior rivals of her own people in running waterfront establishments. She claims that her people served better food and drink, played better music, and told better stories; she nevertheless calls all the groups cousins [MTM B13:408].","citations":["MTM B13:408"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Food & Dining","Music","Family"]},{"name":"Grouse","definition":"Birds among the small creatures along the mile-long track through the nursery where Hans Schultz runs morning and evening [A:208].","citations":["A:208"],"tags":[]},{"name":"Groves","definition":"The Boy King’s groves of figs, dates, olives, apricots, and pomegranates belong to the childhood presented as more real than Melchisedech’s American childhood [MTM B1:17]. Trinali’s poem asks what became of the “Green Groves of Gomorrah” and imagines their trees surviving, salted and fire-marked, in the Elephants’ Graveyard [A:235].","citations":["MTM B1:17","A:235"],"tags":["Old Testament","Literature","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Grubby","definition":"The Professor calls the pursuit of What rather than Why the “grubby thing” confused with learning [A:220]. Duffey thinks of Hugo Stone as Absalom Stein’s “grubby pupa form” [MTM B5:160]. Zabotski calls sycamore fruit “grubby and trashy” while comparing Bryan Blackstone to the tree [MTM B9:270].","citations":["A:220","MTM B5:160","MTM B9:270"],"tags":["Language & Words","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Grundy County","definition":"County in Illinois where Hillary Hilton buys a bridge while Finnegan accompanies him on his salvage business. Hilton plans to cut it up for a profit [A:253-254]. The identification follows the Chicago setting of this episode [A:246] [A:249].","citations":["A:253-254","A:246","A:249"],"tags":["Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Guadeloupe","definition":"Basse-Terre is an island within the Guadeloupe archipelago, rather than an alternative name for the entire archipelago. Basse-Terre in the Antilles is the earthly “Paradise” shared by Finnegan, Charles Le Marin, Don Barnaby, and Johnny Duckwalk [D:233] [D:235-237]. The narrator distinguishes it from Basseterre on St. Kitts, presents a French-speaking island society and a volcano named Soufriere, and describes their rum drinking, hammocks, swimming, fishing, poker, and seafaring stories. The haven remains vulnerable to the “snakes” that eventually drive them out [D:235-237]. In a separate account of treasure maps, Barnaby lists the Guadelupe passage among seven true passages through the Antilles [D:260].","citations":["D:233","D:235-237","D:260"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Islands","Caribbean","Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Sailing"]},{"name":"Guadeloupe, Antilles (aka Basse-Terre)","definition":"Earlier heading that treated Guadeloupe and Basse-Terre as interchangeable names. See <em>Guadeloupe</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Islands","Caribbean","Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Sailing"]},{"name":"Guaiacum","definition":"One of the substances Duffey suggests when asked to identify the odor of the clipping announcing X’s death. Dotty identifies the smell as rosin and recognizes that X printed the clipping himself [A:142].","citations":["A:142"],"tags":["Death & Mortality","Journalism"]},{"name":"Guanche","definition":"Member of the indigenous people of Tenerife in the Canary Islands. The waiter serving brandy on Grand Canary is described as “a Guanche from before the time of Spain” [D:99-101].","citations":["D:99-101"],"tags":["Islands","Minor Characters"]}];
